window.FRENCH_PRACTICE_CONTENT = {"chapters":[{"number":0,"slug":"chapter-00-getting-started","title":"Getting Started — Pronunciation and Survival French","documents":[{"id":"chapter-00-getting-started/README.md","title":"Chapter 0: Getting Started — Pronunciation and Survival French","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-0-getting-started-pronunciation-and-survival-french\">Chapter 0: Getting Started — Pronunciation and Survival French</h1>\n<p><strong>Start here if you have never studied French.</strong></p>\n<p>Chapters 1-16 teach grammar, and from the very first page they ask you to read French examples aloud. That instruction is useless if nobody has shown you how French letters sound. This chapter fills that gap.</p>\n<p>It also gives you enough real French to survive a first conversation, so you are speaking from day one rather than after Chapter 6.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>The alphabet, the five accent marks, and how to spell your name aloud.</li>\n<li>Which letters are silent, and why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> sound different.</li>\n<li>The vowel sounds English does not have, especially <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code>.</li>\n<li>The four nasal vowels.</li>\n<li>The consonants that trap English speakers: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ch</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gn</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu</code>, soft <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">g</code>.</li>\n<li>Elision (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'habite</code>), liaison (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code>), and French rhythm.</li>\n<li>Greetings, politeness, and when to say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n<li>Numbers 0-20 and a full self-introduction.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-in-this-chapter\">What is in this chapter</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>File</th>\n<th>Use it for</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code></span></td>\n<td>The eight teaching units. Work through these in order.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">survival-phrases.md</code></span></td>\n<td>A phrasebook of ready-made sentences for real situations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">glossary.md</code></span></td>\n<td>Plain-English definitions of every grammar term used in Chapters 0-16.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code></span></td>\n<td>Closed-book practice.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code></span></td>\n<td>Corrections with the reason behind each one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code></span></td>\n<td>A 7-day schedule and a readiness test for Chapter 1.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"best-learning-order\">Best learning order</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit at a time in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Say every French example aloud.</strong> On the website, tap the French text to hear it, then imitate it immediately.</li>\n<li>Cover the French and write it from memory.</li>\n<li>Pick five phrases from <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">survival-phrases.md</code></span> and use them the same day.</li>\n<li>Look up any unfamiliar grammar word in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">glossary.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Complete <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code></span> with no notes visible.</li>\n<li>Correct with <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code></span>, writing down the <strong>rule</strong> behind each error, not just the right answer.</li>\n<li>Follow <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code></span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"why-pronunciation-comes-before-grammar\">Why pronunciation comes before grammar</h2>\n<p>Three practical reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>You cannot memorize what you cannot say.</strong> Vocabulary learned only by eye fades quickly and does not transfer to listening.</li>\n<li><strong>French spelling hides sound.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlent</code> has seven letters and sounds like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code>. Without this chapter, later verb tables look arbitrary.</li>\n<li><strong>Agreement is audible.</strong> The difference between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code> is a pronunciation fact. Chapter 1's grammar becomes much easier once you can hear it.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"how-long-this-takes\">How long this takes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pace</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Light</td>\n<td>1 week (one unit per day)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard</td>\n<td>3-4 days (two units per day)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intensive</td>\n<td>1 long session, then daily review while doing Chapter 1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Do not aim for a perfect accent before moving on. Aim for a confident first attempt at any written word.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-target\">Mastery target</h2>\n<p>Move to Chapter 1 when you can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>read an unfamiliar French sentence aloud without freezing;</li>\n<li>say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> so a listener could tell them apart;</li>\n<li>greet someone, introduce yourself, and order something in a café from memory;</li>\n<li>explain what elision and liaison are, in your own words;</li>\n<li>count from 0 to 20 without hesitating.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You do <strong>not</strong> need a native accent. You need to be understood, and to stop guessing.</p>","search":"# chapter 0: getting started — pronunciation and survival french **start here if you have never studied french.** chapters 1-16 teach grammar, and from the very first page they ask you to read french examples aloud. that instruction is useless if nobody has shown you how french letters sound. this chapter fills that gap. it also gives you enough real french to survive a first conversation, so you are speaking from day one rather than after chapter 6. ## what you will learn 1. the alphabet, the five accent marks, and how to spell your name aloud. 2. which letters are silent, and why `petit` and `petite` sound different. 3. the vowel sounds english does not have, especially `u` versus `ou`. 4. the four nasal vowels. 5. the consonants that trap english speakers: `r`, `j`, `ch`, `gn`, `qu`, soft `c` and `g`. 6. elision (`j'habite`), liaison (`les amis`), and french rhythm. 7. greetings, politeness, and when to say `tu` or `vous`. 8. numbers 0-20 and a full self-introduction. ## what is in this chapter | file | use it for | |---|---| | [`lessons.md`](lessons.md) | the eight teaching units. work through these in order. | | [`survival-phrases.md`](survival-phrases.md) | a phrasebook of ready-made sentences for real situations. | | [`glossary.md`](glossary.md) | plain-english definitions of every grammar term used in chapters 0-16. | | [`exercises.md`](exercises.md) | closed-book practice. | | [`answer-key.md`](answer-key.md) | corrections with the reason behind each one. | | [`review-plan.md`](review-plan.md) | a 7-day schedule and a readiness test for chapter 1. | ## best learning order 1. read one unit at a time in [`lessons.md`](lessons.md). 2. **say every french example aloud.** on the website, tap the french text to hear it, then imitate it immediately. 3. cover the french and write it from memory. 4. pick five phrases from [`survival-phrases.md`](survival-phrases.md) and use them the same day. 5. look up any unfamiliar grammar word in [`glossary.md`](glossary.md). 6. complete [`exercises.md`](exercises.md) with no notes visible. 7. correct with [`answer-key.md`](answer-key.md), writing down the **rule** behind each error, not just the right answer. 8. follow [`review-plan.md`](review-plan.md). ## why pronunciation comes before grammar three practical reasons: 1. **you cannot memorize what you cannot say.** vocabulary learned only by eye fades quickly and does not transfer to listening. 2. **french spelling hides sound.** `parlent` has seven letters and sounds like `parle`. without this chapter, later verb tables look arbitrary. 3. **agreement is audible.** the difference between `petit`/`petite` and `bon`/`bonne` is a pronunciation fact. chapter 1's grammar becomes much easier once you can hear it. ## how long this takes | pace | time | |---|---| | light | 1 week (one unit per day) | | standard | 3-4 days (two units per day) | | intensive | 1 long session, then daily review while doing chapter 1 | do not aim for a perfect accent before moving on. aim for a confident first attempt at any written word. ## mastery target move to chapter 1 when you can: - read an unfamiliar french sentence aloud without freezing; - say `tu` and `tout` so a listener could tell them apart; - greet someone, introduce yourself, and order something in a café from memory; - explain what elision and liaison are, in your own words; - count from 0 to 20 without hesitating. you do **not** need a native accent. you need to be understood, and to stop guessing. "},{"id":"chapter-00-getting-started/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 0 Lessons: Sounds, Spelling, and Your First French","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-0-lessons-sounds-spelling-and-your-first-french\">Chapter 0 Lessons: Sounds, Spelling, and Your First French</h1>\n<p>This chapter assumes you have never studied French. Before you learn grammar, you need to be able to <strong>look at a French word and make a reasonable sound come out of your mouth</strong>. That single skill makes every later chapter easier, because from Chapter 1 onward you will be asked to read examples aloud.</p>\n<p>Nothing here requires memorizing rules perfectly. Your goal is recognition and a confident first attempt.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>name the letters of the French alphabet and the five accent marks;</li>\n<li>predict which letters at the end of a word are silent;</li>\n<li>produce the main French vowel sounds, including the ones English does not have;</li>\n<li>recognize and produce the four nasal vowels;</li>\n<li>pronounce the consonants that behave differently from English;</li>\n<li>apply elision (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>) and hear liaison between words;</li>\n<li>greet someone, say please and thank you, and choose between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>;</li>\n<li>count from 0 to 20 and give your name, age, and language level.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>None. This is the true starting point of the course.</p>\n<p>You do <strong>not</strong> need to know any grammar words yet. If a term such as <em>noun</em>, <em>vowel</em>, or <em>syllable</em> is unfamiliar, open <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">glossary.md</code></span> in this chapter, which explains every grammar word used in the whole course in plain English.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Pronunciation is a physical skill, not a fact to memorize. Read silently once, then <strong>say every example out loud</strong>. On the website, tap any French text to hear it spoken, then imitate it immediately. Copy the rhythm before worrying about individual sounds.</p>\n<p>If a phrase goes past too quickly, drag <strong>Speaking speed</strong> in the sidebar down to about 60% and listen again: French runs its words together, and slowing it down is often the only way to hear where one ends and the next begins. Select the phrase and press <strong>Slower</strong> to hear it at half speed without changing your setting. Then put the speed back up to 90-100% and imitate it there. Practising only at slow speed teaches you to speak in a way no French person does, so treat slow playback as a magnifying glass, not a comfort zone.</p>\n<p>Work through one unit per session. Return to this chapter briefly whenever a later chapter introduces words you cannot pronounce.</p>\n<p>Every practice exercise in this course has a <strong>Show answers</strong> button folded underneath it, with a short explanation of the rule behind each answer. Leave it closed until you have committed to an answer out loud. Reading a model answer feels like learning, but the effort of producing your own one first is what actually makes it stick, and a mistake you make and then correct is worth more than a correct answer you only recognised.</p>\n<blockquote>A useful loop for every example: <strong>listen → imitate immediately → compare → repeat twice</strong></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-the-alphabet-and-the-accent-marks\">Unit 1: The Alphabet and the Accent Marks</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French uses the same 26 letters as English, but the <strong>letter names</strong> differ, and five accent marks change either the sound or the meaning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-alphabet\">The alphabet</h3>\n<p>Say each letter aloud. The third column is an approximate English respelling, not an exact sound.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Letter</th>\n<th>French name</th>\n<th>Sounds roughly like</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code></td>\n<td>ah</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">b</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bé</code></td>\n<td>bay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cé</code></td>\n<td>say</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dé</code></td>\n<td>day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code></td>\n<td>uh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">effe</code></td>\n<td>eff</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">g</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gé</code></td>\n<td>zhay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ache</code></td>\n<td>ash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i</code></td>\n<td>ee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ji</code></td>\n<td>zhee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">k</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ka</code></td>\n<td>kah</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n<td>ell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">emme</code></td>\n<td>em</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">enne</code></td>\n<td>en</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code></td>\n<td>oh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">p</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pé</code></td>\n<td>pay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">q</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu</code></td>\n<td>kü</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">erre</code></td>\n<td>air (throaty)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">esse</code></td>\n<td>ess</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">té</code></td>\n<td>tay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code></td>\n<td>ü</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">v</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vé</code></td>\n<td>vay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">w</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">double vé</code></td>\n<td>doo-bluh-vay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">x</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ixe</code></td>\n<td>eeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i grec</code></td>\n<td>ee-grek</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">z</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">zède</code></td>\n<td>zed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the trap: French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">g</code> sounds like English \"j,\" and French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j</code> sounds like the \"s\" in <em>measure</em>. English speakers reverse these constantly when spelling aloud.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-five-accent-marks\">The five accent marks</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Accent</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">é</code></td>\n<td>accent aigu</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">café</code></td>\n<td>Makes a closed \"ay\" sound.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">è</code></td>\n<td>accent grave</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mère</code></td>\n<td>Makes an open \"eh\" sound.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ê</code></td>\n<td>accent circonflexe</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fête</code></td>\n<td>Usually \"eh\"; often marks a lost letter s.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ç</code></td>\n<td>cédille</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code></td>\n<td>Forces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> to sound like \"s\" before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ë</code></td>\n<td>tréma</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Noël</code></td>\n<td>Separates two vowels so each is pronounced.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two important points for beginners:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>An accent is part of the spelling, not decoration.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> means \"or\" and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> means \"where.\" Leaving out an accent can create a different word.</li>\n<li><strong>The grave accent on <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">là</code> does not change the sound.</strong> It only distinguishes words that would otherwise look identical: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code> (has) versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> (to/at).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Without looking above:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Which accent turns <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> into an \"s\" sound?</li>\n<li>Which two letters do English speakers most often swap when spelling aloud in French?</li>\n<li>What is the difference in meaning between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>The cédille</strong> — in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code>, the mark under <strong>c</strong> makes it sound like <strong>s</strong> before <strong>a</strong>, <strong>o</strong>, or <strong>u</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">g</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j</code></strong> — French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">g</code> is named with a sound like English <strong>j</strong>, while French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j</code> has the sound in English <em>measure</em>. English speakers often reverse them.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> means <strong>or</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> means <strong>where</strong>. The grave accent does not change the sound here, but it changes the word.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the production task, answers vary. Spell your own name with French letter names, slowly first and then at normal speed. Mark yourself wrong only if you use English letter names, skip a letter, or cannot repeat the same spelling twice.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h3>\n<p>Spell your own first name aloud using French letter names. Repeat it twice at normal speed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<p>Read each line aloud twice, then cover it and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment ça s'écrit ?</code> — How is that spelled?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça s'écrit M-A-R-I-E.</code> — It is spelled M-A-R-I-E.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite à Paris.</code> — I live in Paris.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où est le café ?</code> — Where is the café?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est une fête française.</code> — It is a French celebration.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Use these as the model lines to check your memory copy. The goal is exact spelling, accents, apostrophes, and punctuation.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment ça s'écrit ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code> needs the cedilla because <strong>c</strong> before <strong>a</strong> would otherwise sound hard. The apostrophe in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'écrit</code> is required before the vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça s'écrit M-A-R-I-E.</code> — the same cedilla and elision appear again. The capital letters are being named, so the spelling matters more than a grammar rule here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite à Paris.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before the silent <strong>h</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habite</code>. The accent in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> marks the word meaning to/at.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où est le café ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> needs the accent because without it, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> means or. French leaves a space before the question mark.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est une fête française.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>, and both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fête</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">française</code> need their accents.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-silent-letters\">Unit 2: Silent Letters</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French writes more letters than it pronounces. Most words <strong>end in a silent letter</strong>. This is the single biggest shock for English speakers, and getting used to it early prevents months of over-pronouncing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rule-1-most-final-consonants-are-silent\">Rule 1: Most final consonants are silent</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> — small (you do not say the final t)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code> — big (no final d)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — you (no final s)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup</code> — a lot (no final p)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"rule-2-the-letters-in-careful-are-usually-pronounced-at-the-end\">Rule 2: The letters in CaReFuL are usually pronounced at the end</h3>\n<p>The English word <strong>CaReFuL</strong> is a memory aid for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code> — with (c is pronounced)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code> — hello (r is pronounced)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code> — nine (f is pronounced)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avril</code> — April (l is pronounced)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is a helpful tendency, not a law. Common exceptions include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanc</code> (white) and the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb ending, where the r is silent: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> — to speak.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rule-3-a-final-e-is-silent\">Rule 3: A final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> is silent</h3>\n<p>A final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> is not pronounced, but it <strong>makes the consonant before it pronounced</strong>. This is why gender agreement is audible:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Masculine</th>\n<th>Feminine</th>\n<th>What you hear</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code></td>\n<td>\"puh-TEE\" vs \"puh-TEET\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code></td>\n<td>\"grahn\" vs \"grahnd\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">française</code></td>\n<td>\"frahn-SAY\" vs \"frahn-SEZ\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>This matters enormously from Chapter 1, where adjectives change for gender. The written <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> is silent, yet it is exactly what makes the difference hearable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rule-4-the-verb-ending-ent-is-silent\">Rule 4: The verb ending <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code> is silent</h3>\n<p>In the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code> form of a verb, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code> is completely silent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parle</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlent</code> sound <strong>identical</strong> — he speaks / they speak</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You will meet this in Chapter 3. Do not try to hear a difference that does not exist; the subject word tells you the number.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rule-5-h-is-always-silent\">Rule 5: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code> is always silent</h3>\n<p>French never pronounces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hôtel</code> — the hotel</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'heure</code> — the hour</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are two kinds of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code> in spelling. A \"mute h\" behaves like a vowel and allows <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'homme</code> — the man). An \"aspirated h\" blocks it (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le héros</code> — the hero). Both are silent; only the linking behavior differs. Learn the few aspirated-h words as you meet them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Which letters do you pronounce in each word?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">salut</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chat</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">salut</code> — pronounce the vowel sound, but not the final <strong>t</strong>. Most final consonants are silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code> — pronounce the final <strong>r</strong>. <strong>R</strong> is one of the CaReFuL letters that is often heard at the end.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chat</code> — do not pronounce the final <strong>t</strong>. The <strong>ch</strong> sound is <strong>sh</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code> — pronounce the <strong>v</strong> before the final silent <strong>e</strong>. A final <strong>e</strong> is silent, but it makes the consonant before it heard.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code> — pronounce the final <strong>f</strong>. <strong>F</strong> is one of the CaReFuL letters.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The rule is not that final letters are always silent; the rule is that most final consonants are silent unless a common pattern, such as CaReFuL or a final <strong>e</strong>, wakes them up.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h3>\n<p>Say these pairs aloud and exaggerate the difference: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américain</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américaine</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut, ça va ?</code> — Hi, how's it going?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est petit, mais elle est petite.</code> — He is small, but she is small.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants parlent avec le professeur.</code> — The students speak with the teacher.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'hôtel est ouvert à neuf heures.</code> — The hotel opens at nine o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Beaucoup de gens habitent ici.</code> — A lot of people live here.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Use these as the model lines for the memory copy.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut, ça va ?</code> — the final <strong>t</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">salut</code> is silent, and French leaves a space before the question mark.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est petit, mais elle est petite.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> has a silent final <strong>t</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> pronounces that <strong>t</strong> because of the final silent <strong>e</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants parlent avec le professeur.</code> — <strong>-ent</strong> on <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlent</code> is silent, but final <strong>c</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code> is pronounced.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'hôtel est ouvert à neuf heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before the silent <strong>h</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code> usually keeps its final <strong>f</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Beaucoup de gens habitent ici.</code> — the final <strong>p</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup</code> and final <strong>s</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gens</code> are silent.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you pronounced a silent final consonant that the sentence is meant to train.</p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h4>\n<p>Rewrite sentence 2 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigué</code>, then with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code>. Say both versions aloud and listen for the feminine consonant.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut, ça va ?</code> — the final <strong>t</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">salut</code> is silent. Keep the cedilla in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code>; it forces the soft sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est petit, mais elle est petite.</code> — the final consonant is silent in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> but pronounced in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> because the final silent <strong>e</strong> wakes up the <strong>t</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants parlent avec le professeur.</code> — the plural ending -ent on <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlent</code> is silent. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code> keeps its final <strong>c</strong> because <strong>c</strong> is one of the CaReFuL letters.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'hôtel est ouvert à neuf heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before the silent <strong>h</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code> usually pronounces its final <strong>f</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Beaucoup de gens habitent ici.</code> — the final <strong>p</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup</code> and the final <strong>s</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gens</code> are silent. Do not add an English-style final consonant.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est fatigué, mais elle est fatiguée.</code> — the feminine spelling adds <strong>-e</strong>, though in this word the spoken difference is small.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est content, mais elle est contente.</code> — the final <strong>t</strong> is silent in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code> but heard in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contente</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-vowel-sounds\">Unit 3: Vowel Sounds</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French vowels are <strong>pure and short</strong>. English vowels glide: the English \"o\" in <em>go</em> slides toward \"oo.\" French vowels hold one steady position from start to finish. Keep your mouth still while the vowel lasts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"single-vowels\">Single vowels</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Spelling</th>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code></td>\n<td>ah</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code></td>\n<td>the table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">î</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></td>\n<td>ee</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code></td>\n<td>he</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code></td>\n<td>oh</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mot</code></td>\n<td>the word</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">û</code></td>\n<td>ü (see below)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td>you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> (unstressed)</td>\n<td>uh</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code></td>\n<td>the</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">é</code></td>\n<td>ay (closed, no glide)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le café</code></td>\n<td>the coffee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">è</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ê</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ei</code></td>\n<td>eh (open)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mère</code></td>\n<td>the mother</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-two-sounds-english-speakers-must-learn\">The two sounds English speakers must learn</h3>\n<p><strong>1. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code>.</strong> These are different sounds, and confusing them changes words.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> is the easy one: it is English \"oo\" as in <em>food</em>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> — we</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> does not exist in English. To make it: say \"ee,\" hold your tongue exactly where it is, then round your lips as if to whistle. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> — you</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practice this minimal pair slowly, ten times: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dessus</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dessous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rue</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">roue</code>.</p>\n<p>If you say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> with an \"oo\" sound, you are saying a different word. This pair is worth real practice time.</p>\n<p><strong>2. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eu</code>.</strong> Say \"ay\" as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">é</code>, then round your lips without moving your tongue.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code> — two</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je peux</code> — I can</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"vowel-combinations\">Vowel combinations</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Spelling</th>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code></td>\n<td>oo</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code></td>\n<td>hello</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eau</code></td>\n<td>oh</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'eau</code></td>\n<td>the water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oi</code></td>\n<td>wah</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code></td>\n<td>me</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ei</code></td>\n<td>eh</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code></td>\n<td>French</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">œu</code></td>\n<td>uh (rounded)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code></td>\n<td>two</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ille</code></td>\n<td>ee-yuh</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la famille</code></td>\n<td>the family</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Say each pair aloud and state which word has the \"oo\" sound:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sourd</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> — the <strong>oo</strong> sound is in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> uses the rounded-lip French <strong>u</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sourd</code> — the <strong>oo</strong> sound is in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sourd</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code> uses <strong>u</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — the <strong>oo</strong> sound is in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code> uses <strong>u</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the recording task, answers vary. A good recording makes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> clearly different: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> starts from an <strong>ee</strong> tongue position with rounded lips, while <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> is the easier <strong>oo</strong> sound.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h3>\n<p>Record yourself saying <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut, tu vas où ?</code> — Hi, where are you going? Listen back and check that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> sound clearly different.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour, vous allez bien ?</code> — Hello, are you well?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as deux frères ?</code> — Do you have two brothers?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais de l'eau, s'il vous plaît.</code> — I would like some water, please.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, j'habite dans une petite rue.</code> — Me, I live on a small street.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La famille française arrive aujourd'hui.</code> — The French family arrives today.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour, vous allez bien ?</code> — the <strong>ou</strong> sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> is the easy <strong>oo</strong> sound, not the French <strong>u</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as deux frères ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> uses the rounded-lip French <strong>u</strong>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code> uses the rounded <strong>eu</strong> sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais de l'eau, s'il vous plaît.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'eau</code> is required because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eau</code> begins with a vowel sound. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code> is the polite fixed phrase here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, j'habite dans une petite rue.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before the silent <strong>h</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habite</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rue</code> contains French <strong>u</strong>, not <strong>ou</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La famille française arrive aujourd'hui.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">française</code> needs the cedilla and the final silent <strong>e</strong>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aujourd'hui</code> keeps its apostrophe.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-nasal-vowels\">Unit 4: Nasal Vowels</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French has vowels pronounced partly <strong>through the nose</strong>. They appear when a vowel is followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code> <strong>and no vowel comes after that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code></strong>.</p>\n<p>The key point: in a nasal vowel, <strong>you do not pronounce the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code> as a consonant</strong>. The letter is an instruction to send the vowel through your nose, then it disappears.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-four-nasal-vowels\">The four nasal vowels</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Spelling</th>\n<th>Rough sound</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">an</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">am</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">em</code></td>\n<td>\"ahn\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le temps</code></td>\n<td>big, time/weather</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">om</code></td>\n<td>\"ohn\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nom</code></td>\n<td>good, the name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">in</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">im</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ain</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ein</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">yn</code></td>\n<td>\"anh\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vin</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le pain</code></td>\n<td>wine, bread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">um</code></td>\n<td>\"uhn\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le parfum</code></td>\n<td>a/one, perfume</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Many speakers today merge <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> into <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">in</code>. As a beginner you may safely do the same; you will still be understood.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-the-vowel-is-not-nasal\">When the vowel is NOT nasal</h3>\n<p>If a vowel follows the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code>, or the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code> is doubled, the sound is normal and you pronounce the consonant:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Nasal</th>\n<th>Not nasal</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> — a/one</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> — a/one (fem.)</td>\n<td>A vowel follows the n.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code> — good</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code> — good (fem.)</td>\n<td>The n is doubled.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américain</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américaine</code></td>\n<td>A silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> follows, so the n is pronounced.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>This is the pronunciation half of gender agreement. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américain</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américaine</code> sound clearly different, and that difference is one of the most audible signals of gender in speech.</p>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Nasal or not nasal?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">important</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italien</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italienne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">important</code> — nasal. The ending has a vowel followed by <strong>n</strong> with no vowel pronounced after it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code> — not nasal. A vowel follows the <strong>n</strong>, so the <strong>n</strong> is pronounced normally.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italien</code> — nasal. The ending <strong>ien</strong> is nasal here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italienne</code> — not nasal. The doubled <strong>n</strong> is pronounced, so the vowel is no longer nasal.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien</code> — nasal. Both parts of the word contain nasal vowel spellings.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the production pairs, answers vary. The rule to listen for is that the feminine form often stops the nasal vowel: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code> is nasal, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code> pronounces the <strong>n</strong>.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h3>\n<p>Say each masculine/feminine pair aloud five times: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un grand pain, s'il vous plaît.</code> — One large loaf of bread, please.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour, comment vous appelez-vous ?</code> — Hello, what is your name?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est canadien et elle est canadienne.</code> — He is Canadian and she is Canadian.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Combien de temps ?</code> — How much time?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un bon restaurant italien.</code> — It is a good Italian restaurant.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un grand pain, s'il vous plaît.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pain</code> all contain nasal vowels. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code>, the final <strong>d</strong> is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour, comment vous appelez-vous ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comment</code> has a nasal vowel and a silent final <strong>t</strong>. The formal question repeats <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est canadien et elle est canadienne.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code> is nasal at the end; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> is not nasal because the <strong>n</strong> is doubled and pronounced.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Combien de temps ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien</code> contains two nasal vowel spellings. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">temps</code> has a nasal vowel and silent final letters.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un bon restaurant italien.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code> is nasal, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">restaurant</code> has a nasal ending. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italien</code> is nasal because nothing follows the <strong>n</strong>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-consonants-that-behave-differently\">Unit 5: Consonants That Behave Differently</h2>\n<h3 id=\"the-french-r\">The French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code></h3>\n<p>The French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code> is made <strong>in the back of the throat</strong>, not with the tip of the tongue. Start from the sound at the end of the Scottish <em>loch</em> or a soft gargle, then soften it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code> — hello</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">merci</code> — thank you</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paris</code> — Paris</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An English <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code> will be understood. Aim for improvement over time rather than perfection now.</p>\n<h3 id=\"soft-and-hard-c-and-g\">Soft and hard <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">g</code></h3>\n<p>Before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, both letters go \"soft.\" Elsewhere they are \"hard.\"</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Letter</th>\n<th>Before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></th>\n<th>Elsewhere</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code></td>\n<td>\"s\": <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le cinéma</code></td>\n<td>\"k\": <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le café</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">g</code></td>\n<td>\"zh\": <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la girafe</code></td>\n<td>\"g\" as in <em>go</em>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le gâteau</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The cedilla <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ç</code> forces the soft sound where it would otherwise be hard: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code> — French.</p>\n<h3 id=\"other-key-consonants\">Other key consonants</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Spelling</th>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ch</code></td>\n<td>\"sh\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le chat</code></td>\n<td>the cat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j</code></td>\n<td>\"zh\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td>I</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu</code></td>\n<td>\"k\" only</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code></td>\n<td>who</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gn</code></td>\n<td>\"ny\" as in <em>canyon</em></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la montagne</code></td>\n<td>the mountain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">th</code></td>\n<td>plain \"t\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le thé</code></td>\n<td>the tea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> between two vowels</td>\n<td>\"z\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code></td>\n<td>the house</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ss</code></td>\n<td>\"s\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poisson</code></td>\n<td>the fish</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>That last pair matters: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poisson</code> is the fish, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poison</code> is poison. One <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> between vowels sounds like \"z.\"</p>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Say each word aloud, then state the sound of the highlighted letter:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le garçon</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la chaise</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la cuisine</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code> — the <strong>ç</strong> sounds like <strong>s</strong>. The cédille keeps <strong>c</strong> soft before <strong>a</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le garçon</code> — the <strong>ç</strong> again sounds like <strong>s</strong>, this time before <strong>o</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la chaise</code> — <strong>ch</strong> sounds like <strong>sh</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> — <strong>qu</strong> sounds like <strong>k</strong> only; the <strong>u</strong> is not a separate vowel.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la cuisine</code> — the <strong>c</strong> sounds like <strong>k</strong> because it comes before <strong>u</strong>, not before <strong>e</strong>, <strong>i</strong>, or <strong>y</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The habit to build is to look at the next letter. With <strong>c</strong> and <strong>g</strong>, the following vowel often tells you whether the sound is hard or soft.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup !</code> — Thank you very much!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le garçon mange du poisson.</code> — The boy is eating fish.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un thé, s'il vous plaît.</code> — I would like a tea, please.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui est-ce ?</code> — Who is it?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La cuisine de la maison est grande.</code> — The kitchen of the house is large.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup !</code> — the final <strong>p</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup</code> is silent. French leaves a space before the exclamation mark.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le garçon mange du poisson.</code> — the cedilla in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">garçon</code> keeps <strong>c</strong> soft before <strong>o</strong>. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">poisson</code>, the doubled <strong>ss</strong> gives an <strong>s</strong> sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un thé, s'il vous plaît.</code> — <strong>th</strong> is pronounced like a plain <strong>t</strong> in French. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code> keeps its apostrophe and accent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui est-ce ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu</code> makes only a <strong>k</strong> sound; the <strong>u</strong> is not pronounced separately. French spacing before the question mark still applies.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La cuisine de la maison est grande.</code> — the <strong>s</strong> between vowels in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code> sounds like <strong>z</strong>. The final <strong>e</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code> makes the <strong>d</strong> audible.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-6-elision-liaison-and-rhythm\">Unit 6: Elision, Liaison, and Rhythm</h2>\n<h3 id=\"elision-dropping-a-vowel\">Elision: dropping a vowel</h3>\n<p>When a short word ending in a vowel meets a word starting with a vowel sound, the vowel is dropped and replaced by an apostrophe. This is <strong>required</strong>, not optional.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Words</th>\n<th>Becomes</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ami</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'ami</code></td>\n<td>the friend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habite</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'habite</code></td>\n<td>I live</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'est</code></td>\n<td>is not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eau</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'eau</code></td>\n<td>of water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il</code></td>\n<td>that he</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Remember that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code> is silent, so a word starting with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code> usually counts as starting with a vowel: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hôtel</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'habite</code>.</p>\n<p>You will apply elision in every chapter from Chapter 1 onward.</p>\n<h3 id=\"liaison-linking-words-together\">Liaison: linking words together</h3>\n<p>Normally a final consonant is silent. But when the next word begins with a vowel sound, that consonant often <strong>wakes up and links</strong> to it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Written</th>\n<th>Sounds like</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code></td>\n<td>\"lay-z-ami\"</td>\n<td>the friends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous êtes</code></td>\n<td>\"vou-z-et\"</td>\n<td>you are</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un grand arbre</code></td>\n<td>\"un grahn-t-arbre\"</td>\n<td>a big tree</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon ami</code></td>\n<td>\"mon-n-ami\"</td>\n<td>my friend</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Sound changes in liaison: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">x</code> become \"z,\" and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d</code> becomes \"t.\"</p>\n<p>Liaison is often the <strong>only</strong> way to hear that something is plural, because the plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> is otherwise silent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le ami</code> is impossible, but compare <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'ami</code> (one friend) with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code> (several friends, linked with a \"z\")</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not force liaison everywhere. Learn it first in fixed high-frequency phrases such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les enfants</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'est un</code>. One firm rule: <strong>never link after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code></strong> (and). <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un livre et un stylo</code> has no link after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rhythm-and-stress\">Rhythm and stress</h3>\n<p>English stresses one syllable hard inside a word. French does not. French gives <strong>roughly equal weight to every syllable</strong>, with a slight lengthening on the <strong>last syllable of a group of words</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>English: <em>IM-por-tant</em></li>\n<li>French: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">important</code> — three even syllables, with a small push on the last</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Try <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un restaurant italien.</code> — It is an Italian restaurant. Say it as one smooth group with no strong internal stress. Sounding French depends more on this even rhythm than on any single consonant.</p>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aime</code> correctly.</li>\n<li>Write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> around the verb.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons</code>, what sound links the two words?</li>\n<li>After which small word is liaison forbidden?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aime</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound. Elision is written with an apostrophe.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'a pas</code> — the full sentence could be <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'a pas de café.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before the vowel sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons</code>, the final <strong>s</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> links as a <strong>z</strong> sound.</li>\n<li>Liaison is forbidden after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un homme et une femme</code>, do not link from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code> into <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The rule difference: elision changes the spelling, while liaison changes the sound between two written words.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<p>Say each sentence as one smooth group, then write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime les enfants.</code> — I like children.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous avez un grand appartement.</code> — You have a large apartment.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons à l'école.</code> — We are going to school.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce n'est pas un problème.</code> — It is not a problem.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un homme et une femme arrivent.</code> — A man and a woman arrive.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime les enfants.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aime</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les enfants</code> may link with a <strong>z</strong> sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous avez un grand appartement.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez</code> links with <strong>z</strong>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand appartement</code> links with a <strong>t</strong> sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons à l'école.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons</code> links with <strong>z</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la école</code> must become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'école</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce n'est pas un problème.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> stays after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un homme et une femme arrivent.</code> — link in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un homme</code> and from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">femme</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivent</code>; never link after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check both spelling and sound. A sentence can be spelled correctly but still lose the rhythm if you pronounce each word separately.</p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"notice-the-pattern\">Notice the pattern</h4>\n<p>In sentence 5, mark where you link and where you must not. There is a link in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un homme</code> and in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une femme arrivent</code>, but none after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime les enfants.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aime</code>. In careful speech, the <strong>s</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> links to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">enfants</code> with a <strong>z</strong> sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous avez un grand appartement.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez</code> links with a <strong>z</strong> sound. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand appartement</code> links with a <strong>t</strong> sound because final <strong>d</strong> changes to <strong>t</strong> in liaison.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons à l'école.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons</code> links with a <strong>z</strong> sound, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la école</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'école</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce n'est pas un problème.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>. Do not drop <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> in standard written French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un homme et une femme arrivent.</code> — link in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un homme</code> and between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">femme</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivent</code>; do not link after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>. The word <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">homme</code> begins with a silent <strong>h</strong>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-7-greetings-politeness-and-tu-or-vous\">Unit 7: Greetings, Politeness, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French politeness is more explicit than English politeness. Adding <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code> before a request is not optional decoration; leaving it out sounds abrupt to French ears.</p>\n<blockquote><strong>The single most useful habit in this course:</strong> begin every interaction with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> (or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonsoir</code> in the evening) before you say anything else.</blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"essential-greetings\">Essential greetings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>When to use</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code></td>\n<td>Hello / Good morning</td>\n<td>Any time until evening. Always safe.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonsoir</code></td>\n<td>Good evening</td>\n<td>From roughly 6 p.m.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut</code></td>\n<td>Hi / Bye</td>\n<td>Informal only, with friends.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir</code></td>\n<td>Goodbye</td>\n<td>Any situation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne journée</code></td>\n<td>Have a good day</td>\n<td>When leaving.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À bientôt</code></td>\n<td>See you soon</td>\n<td>Informal farewell.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"please-thank-you-sorry\">Please, thank you, sorry</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">S'il vous plaît</code></td>\n<td>Please</td>\n<td>Formal or to a group.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">S'il te plaît</code></td>\n<td>Please</td>\n<td>Informal, one person you know.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci</code></td>\n<td>Thank you</td>\n<td>Universal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup</code></td>\n<td>Thank you very much</td>\n<td>Warmer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De rien</code></td>\n<td>You're welcome</td>\n<td>Literally \"of nothing.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Excusez-moi</code></td>\n<td>Excuse me</td>\n<td>To get attention or apologize.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pardon</code></td>\n<td>Sorry / Excuse me</td>\n<td>Short, for bumping into someone.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"choosing-tu-or-vous\">Choosing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></h3>\n<p>This choice exists in every French sentence you address to someone, so decide it early.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></th>\n<th>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a close friend</td>\n<td>someone you have just met</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a family member</td>\n<td>a shop assistant, waiter, official</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a child</td>\n<td>someone noticeably older</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a classmate of similar age</td>\n<td><strong>any group of people</strong>, always</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>When in doubt, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</strong> An unnecessary <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> sounds polite. An unwanted <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> sounds rude. A French speaker will invite you to switch by saying <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On peut se tutoyer ?</code> — Shall we use \"tu\" with each other?</p>\n<p>Note that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> is also the plural \"you.\" Even with close friends, once there are two of them, you must use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"asking-how-someone-is\">Asking how someone is</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>Register</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça va ?</code></td>\n<td>How's it going?</td>\n<td>Informal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment ça va ?</code></td>\n<td>How are you?</td>\n<td>Neutral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment allez-vous ?</code></td>\n<td>How are you?</td>\n<td>Formal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça va bien, merci. Et vous ?</code></td>\n<td>I'm well, thanks. And you?</td>\n<td>Polite reply</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça va, et toi ?</code></td>\n<td>I'm fine, and you?</td>\n<td>Informal reply</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Always return the question. Answering <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça va</code> and stopping sounds like you are ending the conversation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour madame, comment allez-vous ?</code> — Hello ma'am, how are you?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut ! Ça va ?</code> — Hi! How's it going?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un café, s'il vous plaît.</code> — A coffee, please.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup. — De rien.</code> — Thank you very much. — You're welcome.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Excusez-moi, où est la gare ?</code> — Excuse me, where is the train station?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée !</code> — Goodbye, have a good day!</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour madame, comment allez-vous ?</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> with a stranger or in a formal situation.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut ! Ça va ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">salut</code> is informal, so it belongs with friends, family, or peers.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un café, s'il vous plaît.</code> — the request is polite because it includes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code>; in real life, say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> first.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup. — De rien.</code> — this is the normal thank-you and reply pair.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Excusez-moi, où est la gare ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> needs the accent to mean where, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">excusez-moi</code> uses the formal or plural form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée !</code> — this is a standard daytime farewell; French leaves a space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">!</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For any greeting line, mark yourself wrong if the social register is wrong even when the words are grammatical.</p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h4>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1 and 3 in the informal register (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> forms) and sentence 2 in the formal register.</p>\n<h4 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h4>\n<p>Write the exact four lines you would say when entering a bakery: greeting, request, thanks, farewell.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour madame, comment allez-vous ?</code> — this is formal because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> is used with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">madame</code>. Use it with a stranger or someone you do not know well.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut ! Ça va ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">salut</code> is informal, so it fits friends and family, not a shop or a first meeting.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un café, s'il vous plaît.</code> — the request is polite because it includes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code>. In a real shop, say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> first.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup. — De rien.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de rien</code> is a standard answer to thanks. Do not translate the English words one by one.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Excusez-moi, où est la gare ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">excusez-moi</code> uses the formal or plural form. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> needs the accent to mean where.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée !</code> — this is the normal polite leaving formula during the day.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Informal version of sentence 1: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut, comment vas-tu ?</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> for one familiar person.</li>\n<li>Informal version of sentence 3: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un café, s'il te plaît.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> for one familiar person.</li>\n<li>Formal version of sentence 2: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour ! Comment allez-vous ?</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> and a neutral greeting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bakery model:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un croissant, s'il vous plaît.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Your exact request may differ, but it must open with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> and include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-8-numbers-0-to-20-and-introducing-yourself\">Unit 8: Numbers 0 to 20 and Introducing Yourself</h2>\n<h3 id=\"numbers-0-to-20\">Numbers 0 to 20</h3>\n<p>Chapter 4 covers the full number system. These twenty are enough to give your age, a price, and a phone number.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>French</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">zéro</code></td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">onze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">douze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code></td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">treize</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code></td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatorze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre</code></td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quinze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinq</code></td>\n<td>16</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">seize</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six</code></td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-sept</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sept</code></td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-huit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit</code></td>\n<td>19</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code></td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Pronunciation warnings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The final consonant of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinq</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix</code> is pronounced when the number stands alone, but often changes before a noun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six</code> alone ends in \"s,\" yet <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six livres</code> drops it and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six ans</code> links as \"z.\"</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sept</code> keeps its \"t\" but drops the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">p</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt</code> has a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t</code> when alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not over-study this now. Say the numbers in order daily until they are automatic.</p>\n<h3 id=\"introducing-yourself\">Introducing yourself</h3>\n<p>These sentences use grammar from Chapters 1-4. Learn them now as <strong>fixed chunks</strong>; the rules behind them arrive later.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'appelle Marie.</code></td>\n<td>My name is Marie.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment vous appelez-vous ?</code></td>\n<td>What is your name? (formal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment tu t'appelles ?</code></td>\n<td>What is your name? (informal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Enchanté.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Enchantée.</code></td>\n<td>Nice to meet you. (m. / f.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code></td>\n<td>I am twenty years old.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis américain.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américaine.</code></td>\n<td>I am American. (m. / f.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite à Toronto.</code></td>\n<td>I live in Toronto.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle un peu français.</code></td>\n<td>I speak a little French.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two things to notice now and understand later:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>French uses <strong>have</strong>, not <strong>be</strong>, for age: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans</code> literally means \"I have twenty years.\" Chapter 2 explains this.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Enchanté</code> adds an <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> if you are female. That is adjective agreement, taught in Chapter 1.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"survival-phrases-when-you-are-stuck\">Survival phrases when you are stuck</h3>\n<p>These four sentences will keep almost any beginner conversation alive:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne comprends pas.</code> — I don't understand.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pouvez-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ?</code> — Can you repeat, please?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît.</code> — More slowly, please.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment dit-on \"book\" en français ?</code> — How do you say \"book\" in French?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A fuller list is in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">survival-phrases.md</code></span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h3>\n<p>Write and memorize a five-line self-introduction: name, age, nationality, city, and language level. Say it aloud without reading.</p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pronouncing final consonants.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> ends in a silent t. Remember CaReFuL for the usual exceptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Saying <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> with an \"oo\" sound.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> are different words. Practice the rounded-lip <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> separately.</li>\n<li><strong>Pronouncing the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> in a nasal vowel.</strong> In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code>, the n is an instruction for the vowel, not a consonant you say.</li>\n<li><strong>Treating accents as optional.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> (or) and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> (where) are different words; write the accent.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting elision.</strong> Write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'habite</code>, never <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je habite</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stressing one syllable hard.</strong> French syllables are even. Uneven stress is the most common reason a learner sounds foreign even with correct sounds.</li>\n<li><strong>Starting a request without <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code>.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> first, every time.</li>\n<li><strong>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> with a stranger.</strong> Default to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> until invited to switch.</li>\n<li><strong>Assuming <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlent</code> sounds different from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parle</code>.</strong> The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code> ending is silent; only the subject reveals number.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-arriving-at-a-caf\">Mini-dialogue: arriving at a café</h2>\n<p>Read the dialogue in French first. Then use the English to check your understanding.</p>\n<p><strong>Serveur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour madame, vous désirez ?</code> — Hello ma'am, what would you like?</p>\n<p><strong>Claire :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour. Un café et un croissant, s'il vous plaît.</code> — Hello. A coffee and a croissant, please.</p>\n<p><strong>Serveur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Très bien. C'est tout ?</code> — Very good. Is that all?</p>\n<p><strong>Claire :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, c'est tout. Ça fait combien ?</code> — Yes, that's all. How much is it?</p>\n<p><strong>Serveur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Six euros, s'il vous plaît.</code> — Six euros, please.</p>\n<p><strong>Claire :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pardon, je ne comprends pas. Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît ?</code> — Sorry, I don't understand. More slowly, please?</p>\n<p><strong>Serveur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Six euros.</code> — Six euros.</p>\n<p><strong>Claire :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ah, merci beaucoup ! Au revoir.</code> — Ah, thank you very much! Goodbye.</p>\n<p><strong>Serveur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée !</code> — Goodbye, have a good day!</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find every use of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code> and explain why the formal form is correct here.</li>\n<li>Find two silent final consonants in the dialogue.</li>\n<li>Find one nasal vowel in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six euros</code>.</li>\n<li>Where does liaison occur in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six euros</code>? What sound links them?</li>\n<li>Rewrite Claire's first line as if she were speaking to a close friend.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code> appears in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un café et un croissant, s'il vous plaît.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Six euros, s'il vous plaît.</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît ?</code> The formal form is correct because Claire and the server are strangers in a service interaction.</li>\n<li>Two silent final consonants: final <strong>t</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> and final <strong>t</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lentement</code>. Another clear one is final <strong>p</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">merci beaucoup</code>. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six euros</code>, the final written <strong>x</strong> is not silent because it links to the next word.</li>\n<li>Nasal vowels: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code> has the nasal <strong>on</strong> in the first syllable. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien</code> has two, the <strong>om</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">com-</code> and the <strong>ien</strong> at the end. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six euros</code> has none — the useful sound there is liaison, not nasality.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six euros</code>, the final <strong>x</strong> links to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">euros</code> with a <strong>z</strong> sound: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six euros</code>.</li>\n<li>Close-friend version: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut. Un café et un croissant, s'il te plaît.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> because Claire is now speaking to one familiar person.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>One existing prompt is a little misleading: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six euros</code> is mainly a liaison example, not a nasal-vowel example.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-mon-premier-jour\">Connected reading: mon premier jour</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour ! Je m'appelle Ana. J'ai dix-neuf ans et je suis canadienne. J'habite à Montréal, dans une petite rue près de l'université. Je parle un peu français, mais je ne comprends pas toujours les gens rapides. Le matin, je dis bonjour à mes voisins, et le soir je dis bonsoir. Quand je ne comprends pas, je demande : « Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît. » Les gens sont patients, alors je progresse un peu chaque jour.</code></p>\n<p>— Hello! My name is Ana. I am nineteen years old and I am Canadian. I live in Montreal, on a small street near the university. I speak a little French, but I do not always understand fast people. In the morning, I say hello to my neighbors, and in the evening I say good evening. When I do not understand, I ask: \"More slowly, please.\" People are patient, so I make a little progress every day.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the text aloud twice, focusing on even syllable rhythm.</li>\n<li>Find three words with a nasal vowel.</li>\n<li>Find two examples of elision.</li>\n<li>Find the two numbers and say them aloud.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the first three sentences about yourself, changing the name, age, nationality, and city.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Rhythm task: answers vary, but you should hear even syllables, not one heavy English-style stress inside each word.</li>\n<li>Three nasal-vowel words include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ans</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montréal</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gens</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">patients</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lentement</code>. Some of these stop being nasal in related feminine forms, so listen carefully.</li>\n<li>Two examples of elision: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m'appelle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'université</code>.</li>\n<li>The numbers are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf</code> and the idea of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un peu</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf</code> is nineteen; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> is one or a.</li>\n<li>Model rewrite: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour ! Je m'appelle Sam. J'ai vingt ans et je suis américain. J'habite à Boston, dans une petite rue près de l'université.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own version, keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai</code> for age and make the nationality adjective agree with the person speaking.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"sound-traps-at-a-glance\">Sound traps at a glance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Trap</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Final consonants</td>\n<td>Usually silent, except CaReFuL (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l</code>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n<td>Silent, but makes the previous consonant heard.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code> on a verb</td>\n<td>Completely silent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code></td>\n<td>Always silent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> vs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> = rounded \"ee\"; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> = \"oo.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vowel + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code></td>\n<td>Nasal, unless a vowel follows or the n is doubled.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> between vowels</td>\n<td>Sounds like \"z.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ch</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gn</code></td>\n<td>\"sh\" / \"zh\" / \"k\" / \"ny.\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"politeness-at-a-glance\">Politeness at a glance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Say</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Entering anywhere</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Making a request</td>\n<td>add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stranger, older person, any group</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Friend, family, child</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leaving</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-habits\">Core habits</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Say every French example aloud; never read only with your eyes.</li>\n<li>Imitate the rhythm of a whole phrase before fixing single sounds.</li>\n<li>Learn greetings and politeness as fixed chunks, not word by word.</li>\n<li>Accept that spelling and sound differ; trust the rules over the letters.</li>\n<li>Return to this chapter whenever a new word resists pronunciation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Record yourself (30-60 seconds) doing the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>greet a stranger politely and ask how they are;</li>\n<li>introduce yourself with name, age, nationality, and city;</li>\n<li>order two items in a café and ask the price;</li>\n<li>say you do not understand and ask the person to slow down;</li>\n<li>thank them and say goodbye.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>After recording:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>listen back once for <strong>rhythm</strong> only, checking for even syllables;</li>\n<li>listen again for the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> distinction;</li>\n<li>listen a third time for silent final consonants you accidentally pronounced;</li>\n<li>write the whole script from memory;</li>\n<li>re-record only the lines you corrected.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When you can do this comfortably, you are ready for Chapter 1.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary, but your script should sound like a polite beginner who can survive a first café interaction. Model script:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour monsieur, comment allez-vous ? Je m'appelle Ana. J'ai dix-neuf ans. Je suis canadienne et j'habite à Montréal. Je parle un peu français. Un café et un croissant, s'il vous plaît. Ça fait combien ? Pardon, je ne comprends pas. Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît ? Merci beaucoup. Au revoir, bonne journée !</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: it greets a stranger with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>; it gives name, age, nationality, city, and level; it orders two café items and asks the price; it says the learner does not understand and asks for slower speech; it ends with thanks and goodbye.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>you use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> with the stranger;</li>\n<li>you say <strong>Je suis dix-neuf ans</strong> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai dix-neuf ans</code>;</li>\n<li>you omit <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code> from the request;</li>\n<li>you pronounce silent final consonants in words such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ans</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup</code>;</li>\n<li>you leave out required apostrophes in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 0 lessons: sounds, spelling, and your first french this chapter assumes you have never studied french. before you learn grammar, you need to be able to **look at a french word and make a reasonable sound come out of your mouth**. that single skill makes every later chapter easier, because from chapter 1 onward you will be asked to read examples aloud. nothing here requires memorizing rules perfectly. your goal is recognition and a confident first attempt. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. name the letters of the french alphabet and the five accent marks; 2. predict which letters at the end of a word are silent; 3. produce the main french vowel sounds, including the ones english does not have; 4. recognize and produce the four nasal vowels; 5. pronounce the consonants that behave differently from english; 6. apply elision (`le` -> `l'`) and hear liaison between words; 7. greet someone, say please and thank you, and choose between `tu` and `vous`; 8. count from 0 to 20 and give your name, age, and language level. ## prerequisite quick check none. this is the true starting point of the course. you do **not** need to know any grammar words yet. if a term such as *noun*, *vowel*, or *syllable* is unfamiliar, open [`glossary.md`](glossary.md) in this chapter, which explains every grammar word used in the whole course in plain english. ## how to study this chapter pronunciation is a physical skill, not a fact to memorize. read silently once, then **say every example out loud**. on the website, tap any french text to hear it spoken, then imitate it immediately. copy the rhythm before worrying about individual sounds. if a phrase goes past too quickly, drag **speaking speed** in the sidebar down to about 60% and listen again: french runs its words together, and slowing it down is often the only way to hear where one ends and the next begins. select the phrase and press **slower** to hear it at half speed without changing your setting. then put the speed back up to 90-100% and imitate it there. practising only at slow speed teaches you to speak in a way no french person does, so treat slow playback as a magnifying glass, not a comfort zone. work through one unit per session. return to this chapter briefly whenever a later chapter introduces words you cannot pronounce. every practice exercise in this course has a **show answers** button folded underneath it, with a short explanation of the rule behind each answer. leave it closed until you have committed to an answer out loud. reading a model answer feels like learning, but the effort of producing your own one first is what actually makes it stick, and a mistake you make and then correct is worth more than a correct answer you only recognised. > a useful loop for every example: **listen -> imitate immediately -> compare -> repeat twice** ## unit 1: the alphabet and the accent marks ### core idea french uses the same 26 letters as english, but the **letter names** differ, and five accent marks change either the sound or the meaning. ### the alphabet say each letter aloud. the third column is an approximate english respelling, not an exact sound. | letter | french name | sounds roughly like | |---|---|---| | `a` | `a` | ah | | `b` | `bé` | bay | | `c` | `cé` | say | | `d` | `dé` | day | | `e` | `e` | uh | | `f` | `effe` | eff | | `g` | `gé` | zhay | | `h` | `ache` | ash | | `i` | `i` | ee | | `j` | `ji` | zhee | | `k` | `ka` | kah | | `l` | `elle` | ell | | `m` | `emme` | em | | `n` | `enne` | en | | `o` | `o` | oh | | `p` | `pé` | pay | | `q` | `qu` | kü | | `r` | `erre` | air (throaty) | | `s` | `esse` | ess | | `t` | `té` | tay | | `u` | `u` | ü | | `v` | `vé` | vay | | `w` | `double vé` | doo-bluh-vay | | `x` | `ixe` | eeks | | `y` | `i grec` | ee-grek | | `z` | `zède` | zed | notice the trap: french `g` sounds like english \"j,\" and french `j` sounds like the \"s\" in *measure*. english speakers reverse these constantly when spelling aloud. ### the five accent marks | accent | name | example | what it does | |---|---|---|---| | `é` | accent aigu | `café` | makes a closed \"ay\" sound. | | `è` | accent grave | `mère` | makes an open \"eh\" sound. | | `ê` | accent circonflexe | `fête` | usually \"eh\"; often marks a lost letter s. | | `ç` | cédille | `français` | forces `c` to sound like \"s\" before `a`, `o`, `u`. | | `ë` | tréma | `noël` | separates two vowels so each is pronounced. | two important points for beginners: 1. **an accent is part of the spelling, not decoration.** `ou` means \"or\" and `où` means \"where.\" leaving out an accent can create a different word. 2. **the grave accent on `à`, `où`, `là` does not change the sound.** it only distinguishes words that would otherwise look identical: `a` (has) versus `à` (to/at). ### retrieval check without looking above: 1. which accent turns `c` into an \"s\" sound? 2. which two letters do english speakers most often swap when spelling aloud in french? 3. what is the difference in meaning between `ou` and `où`? 1. **the cédille** — in `ça` and `français`, the mark under **c** makes it sound like **s** before **a**, **o**, or **u**. 2. **`g` and `j`** — french `g` is named with a sound like english **j**, while french `j` has the sound in english *measure*. english speakers often reverse them. 3. `ou` means **or**; `où` means **where**. the grave accent does not change the sound here, but it changes the word. for the production task, answers vary. spell your own name with french letter names, slowly first and then at normal speed. mark yourself wrong only if you use english letter names, skip a letter, or cannot repeat the same spelling twice. ### production task spell your own first name aloud using french letter names. repeat it twice at normal speed. ### sentence usage practice read each line aloud twice, then cover it and write it from memory. 1. `comment ça s'écrit ?` — how is that spelled? 2. `ça s'écrit m-a-r-i-e.` — it is spelled m-a-r-i-e. 3. `j'habite à paris.` — i live in paris. 4. `où est le café ?` — where is the café? 5. `c'est une fête française.` — it is a french celebration. use these as the model lines to check your memory copy. the goal is exact spelling, accents, apostrophes, and punctuation. 1. `comment ça s'écrit ?` — `ça` needs the cedilla because **c** before **a** would otherwise sound hard. the apostrophe in `s'écrit` is required before the vowel sound. 2. `ça s'écrit m-a-r-i-e.` — the same cedilla and elision appear again. the capital letters are being named, so the spelling matters more than a grammar rule here. 3. `j'habite à paris.` — `je` becomes `j'` before the silent **h** of `habite`. the accent in `à` marks the word meaning to/at. 4. `où est le café ?` — `où` needs the accent because without it, `ou` means or. french leaves a space before the question mark. 5. `c'est une fête française.` — `ce` becomes `c'` before `est`, and both `fête` and `française` need their accents. ## unit 2: silent letters ### core idea french writes more letters than it pronounces. most words **end in a silent letter**. this is the single biggest shock for english speakers, and getting used to it early prevents months of over-pronouncing. ### rule 1: most final consonants are silent - `petit` — small (you do not say the final t) - `grand` — big (no final d) - `vous` — you (no final s) - `beaucoup` — a lot (no final p) ### rule 2: the letters in careful are usually pronounced at the end the english word **careful** is a memory aid for `c`, `r`, `f`, `l`: - `avec` — with (c is pronounced) - `bonjour` — hello (r is pronounced) - `neuf` — nine (f is pronounced) - `avril` — april (l is pronounced) this is a helpful tendency, not a law. common exceptions include `blanc` (white) and the `-er` verb ending, where the r is silent: `parler` — to speak. ### rule 3: a final `-e` is silent a final `-e` is not pronounced, but it **makes the consonant before it pronounced**. this is why gender agreement is audible: | masculine | feminine | what you hear | |---|---|---| | `petit` | `petite` | \"puh-tee\" vs \"puh-teet\" | | `grand` | `grande` | \"grahn\" vs \"grahnd\" | | `français` | `française` | \"frahn-say\" vs \"frahn-sez\" | this matters enormously from chapter 1, where adjectives change for gender. the written `-e` is silent, yet it is exactly what makes the difference hearable. ### rule 4: the verb ending `-ent` is silent in the `ils`/`elles` form of a verb, `-ent` is completely silent: - `il parle` and `ils parlent` sound **identical** — he speaks / they speak you will meet this in chapter 3. do not try to hear a difference that does not exist; the subject word tells you the number. ### rule 5: `h` is always silent french never pronounces `h`: - `l'hôtel` — the hotel - `l'heure` — the hour there are two kinds of `h` in spelling. a \"mute h\" behaves like a vowel and allows `l'` (`l'homme` — the man). an \"aspirated h\" blocks it (`le héros` — the hero). both are silent; only the linking behavior differs. learn the few aspirated-h words as you meet them. ### retrieval check which letters do you pronounce in each word? 1. `salut` 2. `bonjour` 3. `chat` 4. `sportive` 5. `neuf` 1. `salut` — pronounce the vowel sound, but not the final **t**. most final consonants are silent. 2. `bonjour` — pronounce the final **r**. **r** is one of the careful letters that is often heard at the end. 3. `chat` — do not pronounce the final **t**. the **ch** sound is **sh**. 4. `sportive` — pronounce the **v** before the final silent **e**. a final **e** is silent, but it makes the consonant before it heard. 5. `neuf` — pronounce the final **f**. **f** is one of the careful letters. the rule is not that final letters are always silent; the rule is that most final consonants are silent unless a common pattern, such as careful or a final **e**, wakes them up. ### production task say these pairs aloud and exaggerate the difference: `petit`/`petite`, `grand`/`grande`, `américain`/`américaine`. ### sentence usage practice 1. `salut, ça va ?` — hi, how's it going? 2. `il est petit, mais elle est petite.` — he is small, but she is small. 3. `les étudiants parlent avec le professeur.` — the students speak with the teacher. 4. `l'hôtel est ouvert à neuf heures.` — the hotel opens at nine o'clock. 5. `beaucoup de gens habitent ici.` — a lot of people live here. use these as the model lines for the memory copy. 1. `salut, ça va ?` — the final **t** in `salut` is silent, and french leaves a space before the question mark. 2. `il est petit, mais elle est petite.` — `petit` has a silent final **t**; `petite` pronounces that **t** because of the final silent **e**. 3. `les étudiants parlent avec le professeur.` — **-ent** on `parlent` is silent, but final **c** in `avec` is pronounced. 4. `l'hôtel est ouvert à neuf heures.` — `le` becomes `l'` before the silent **h** of `hôtel`; `neuf` usually keeps its final **f**. 5. `beaucoup de gens habitent ici.` — the final **p** of `beaucoup` and final **s** of `gens` are silent. mark yourself wrong if you pronounced a silent final consonant that the sentence is meant to train. #### change the sentence rewrite sentence 2 with `fatigué`, then with `content`. say both versions aloud and listen for the feminine consonant. model memory check: 1. `salut, ça va ?` — the final **t** in `salut` is silent. keep the cedilla in `ça`; it forces the soft sound. 2. `il est petit, mais elle est petite.` — the final consonant is silent in `petit` but pronounced in `petite` because the final silent **e** wakes up the **t**. 3. `les étudiants parlent avec le professeur.` — the plural ending -ent on `parlent` is silent. `avec` keeps its final **c** because **c** is one of the careful letters. 4. `l'hôtel est ouvert à neuf heures.` — `le` becomes `l'` before the silent **h** of `hôtel`. `neuf` usually pronounces its final **f**. 5. `beaucoup de gens habitent ici.` — the final **p** of `beaucoup` and the final **s** of `gens` are silent. do not add an english-style final consonant. change the sentence: - `il est fatigué, mais elle est fatiguée.` — the feminine spelling adds **-e**, though in this word the spoken difference is small. - `il est content, mais elle est contente.` — the final **t** is silent in `content` but heard in `contente`. ## unit 3: vowel sounds ### core idea french vowels are **pure and short**. english vowels glide: the english \"o\" in *go* slides toward \"oo.\" french vowels hold one steady position from start to finish. keep your mouth still while the vowel lasts. ### single vowels | spelling | sound | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `a`, `à` | ah | `la table` | the table | | `i`, `î`, `y` | ee | `il` | he | | `o` | oh | `le mot` | the word | | `u`, `û` | ü (see below) | `tu` | you | | `e` (unstressed) | uh | `le` | the | | `é` | ay (closed, no glide) | `le café` | the coffee | | `è`, `ê`, `ai`, `ei` | eh (open) | `la mère` | the mother | ### the two sounds english speakers must learn **1. `u` versus `ou`.** these are different sounds, and confusing them changes words. - `ou` is the easy one: it is english \"oo\" as in *food*. `nous` — we - `u` does not exist in english. to make it: say \"ee,\" hold your tongue exactly where it is, then round your lips as if to whistle. `tu` — you practice this minimal pair slowly, ten times: `tu` / `tout`, `dessus` / `dessous`, `rue` / `roue`. if you say `tu` with an \"oo\" sound, you are saying a different word. this pair is worth real practice time. **2. `eu`.** say \"ay\" as in `é`, then round your lips without moving your tongue. - `deux` — two - `je peux` — i can ### vowel combinations | spelling | sound | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `ou` | oo | `bonjour` | hello | | `au`, `eau` | oh | `l'eau` | the water | | `oi` | wah | `moi` | me | | `ai`, `ei` | eh | `français` | french | | `eu`, `œu` | uh (rounded) | `deux` | two | | `ille` | ee-yuh | `la famille` | the family | ### retrieval check say each pair aloud and state which word has the \"oo\" sound: 1. `tu` / `tout` 2. `sur` / `sourd` 3. `vu` / `vous` 1. `tu` / `tout` — the **oo** sound is in `tout`. `tu` uses the rounded-lip french **u**. 2. `sur` / `sourd` — the **oo** sound is in `sourd`. `sur` uses **u**. 3. `vu` / `vous` — the **oo** sound is in `vous`. `vu` uses **u**. for the recording task, answers vary. a good recording makes `tu` and `où` clearly different: `tu` starts from an **ee** tongue position with rounded lips, while `où` is the easier **oo** sound. ### production task record yourself saying `salut, tu vas où ?` — hi, where are you going? listen back and check that `tu` and `où` sound clearly different. ### sentence usage practice 1. `bonjour, vous allez bien ?` — hello, are you well? 2. `tu as deux frères ?` — do you have two brothers? 3. `je voudrais de l'eau, s'il vous plaît.` — i would like some water, please. 4. `moi, j'habite dans une petite rue.` — me, i live on a small street. 5. `la famille française arrive aujourd'hui.` — the french family arrives today. model memory check: 1. `bonjour, vous allez bien ?` — the **ou** sound in `bonjour` and `vous` is the easy **oo** sound, not the french **u** of `tu`. 2. `tu as deux frères ?` — `tu` uses the rounded-lip french **u**. `deux` uses the rounded **eu** sound. 3. `je voudrais de l'eau, s'il vous plaît.` — `de l'eau` is required because `eau` begins with a vowel sound. `s'il vous plaît` is the polite fixed phrase here. 4. `moi, j'habite dans une petite rue.` — `je` becomes `j'` before the silent **h** of `habite`. `rue` contains french **u**, not **ou**. 5. `la famille française arrive aujourd'hui.` — `française` needs the cedilla and the final silent **e**. `aujourd'hui` keeps its apostrophe. ## unit 4: nasal vowels ### core idea french has vowels pronounced partly **through the nose**. they appear when a vowel is followed by `n` or `m` **and no vowel comes after that `n`/`m`**. the key point: in a nasal vowel, **you do not pronounce the `n` or `m` as a consonant**. the letter is an instruction to send the vowel through your nose, then it disappears. ### the four nasal vowels | spelling | rough sound | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `an`, `am`, `en`, `em` | \"ahn\" | `grand`, `le temps` | big, time/weather | | `on`, `om` | \"ohn\" | `bon`, `le nom` | good, the name | | `in`, `im`, `ain`, `ein`, `yn` | \"anh\" | `le vin`, `le pain` | wine, bread | | `un`, `um` | \"uhn\" | `un`, `le parfum` | a/one, perfume | many speakers today merge `un` into `in`. as a beginner you may safely do the same; you will still be understood. ### when the vowel is not nasal if a vowel follows the `n`/`m`, or the `n`/`m` is doubled, the sound is normal and you pronounce the consonant: | nasal | not nasal | why | |---|---|---| | `un` — a/one | `une` — a/one (fem.) | a vowel follows the n. | | `bon` — good | `bonne` — good (fem.) | the n is doubled. | | `américain` | `américaine` | a silent `-e` follows, so the n is pronounced. | this is the pronunciation half of gender agreement. `bon`/`bonne` and `américain`/`américaine` sound clearly different, and that difference is one of the most audible signals of gender in speech. ### retrieval check nasal or not nasal? 1. `important` 2. `téléphone` 3. `italien` 4. `italienne` 5. `combien` 1. `important` — nasal. the ending has a vowel followed by **n** with no vowel pronounced after it. 2. `téléphone` — not nasal. a vowel follows the **n**, so the **n** is pronounced normally. 3. `italien` — nasal. the ending **ien** is nasal here. 4. `italienne` — not nasal. the doubled **n** is pronounced, so the vowel is no longer nasal. 5. `combien` — nasal. both parts of the word contain nasal vowel spellings. for the production pairs, answers vary. the rule to listen for is that the feminine form often stops the nasal vowel: `bon` is nasal, but `bonne` pronounces the **n**. ### production task say each masculine/feminine pair aloud five times: `un`/`une`, `bon`/`bonne`, `canadien`/`canadienne`. ### sentence usage practice 1. `un grand pain, s'il vous plaît.` — one large loaf of bread, please. 2. `bonjour, comment vous appelez-vous ?` — hello, what is your name? 3. `il est canadien et elle est canadienne.` — he is canadian and she is canadian. 4. `combien de temps ?` — how much time? 5. `c'est un bon restaurant italien.` — it is a good italian restaurant. model memory check: 1. `un grand pain, s'il vous plaît.` — `un`, `grand`, and `pain` all contain nasal vowels. in `grand`, the final **d** is silent. 2. `bonjour, comment vous appelez-vous ?` — `comment` has a nasal vowel and a silent final **t**. the formal question repeats `vous` after the verb. 3. `il est canadien et elle est canadienne.` — `canadien` is nasal at the end; `canadienne` is not nasal because the **n** is doubled and pronounced. 4. `combien de temps ?` — `combien` contains two nasal vowel spellings. `temps` has a nasal vowel and silent final letters. 5. `c'est un bon restaurant italien.` — `bon` is nasal, and `restaurant` has a nasal ending. `italien` is nasal because nothing follows the **n**. ## unit 5: consonants that behave differently ### the french `r` the french `r` is made **in the back of the throat**, not with the tip of the tongue. start from the sound at the end of the scottish *loch* or a soft gargle, then soften it. - `bonjour` — hello - `merci` — thank you - `paris` — paris an english `r` will be understood. aim for improvement over time rather than perfection now. ### soft and hard `c` and `g` before `e`, `i`, `y`, both letters go \"soft.\" elsewhere they are \"hard.\" | letter | before `e`, `i`, `y` | elsewhere | |---|---|---| | `c` | \"s\": `le cinéma` | \"k\": `le café` | | `g` | \"zh\": `la girafe` | \"g\" as in *go*: `le gâteau` | the cedilla `ç` forces the soft sound where it would otherwise be hard: `français` — french. ### other key consonants | spelling | sound | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `ch` | \"sh\" | `le chat` | the cat | | `j` | \"zh\" | `je` | i | | `qu` | \"k\" only | `qui` | who | | `gn` | \"ny\" as in *canyon* | `la montagne` | the mountain | | `th` | plain \"t\" | `le thé` | the tea | | `s` between two vowels | \"z\" | `la maison` | the house | | `ss` | \"s\" | `le poisson` | the fish | that last pair matters: `le poisson` is the fish, `le poison` is poison. one `s` between vowels sounds like \"z.\" ### retrieval check say each word aloud, then state the sound of the highlighted letter: 1. `ça` 2. `le garçon` 3. `la chaise` 4. `qui` 5. `la cuisine` 1. `ça` — the **ç** sounds like **s**. the cédille keeps **c** soft before **a**. 2. `le garçon` — the **ç** again sounds like **s**, this time before **o**. 3. `la chaise` — **ch** sounds like **sh**. 4. `qui` — **qu** sounds like **k** only; the **u** is not a separate vowel. 5. `la cuisine` — the **c** sounds like **k** because it comes before **u**, not before **e**, **i**, or **y**. the habit to build is to look at the next letter. with **c** and **g**, the following vowel often tells you whether the sound is hard or soft. ### sentence usage practice 1. `merci beaucoup !` — thank you very much! 2. `le garçon mange du poisson.` — the boy is eating fish. 3. `je voudrais un thé, s'il vous plaît.` — i would like a tea, please. 4. `qui est-ce ?` — who is it? 5. `la cuisine de la maison est grande.` — the kitchen of the house is large. model memory check: 1. `merci beaucoup !` — the final **p** of `beaucoup` is silent. french leaves a space before the exclamation mark. 2. `le garçon mange du poisson.` — the cedilla in `garçon` keeps **c** soft before **o**. in `poisson`, the doubled **ss** gives an **s** sound. 3. `je voudrais un thé, s'il vous plaît.` — **th** is pronounced like a plain **t** in french. `s'il vous plaît` keeps its apostrophe and accent. 4. `qui est-ce ?` — `qu` makes only a **k** sound; the **u** is not pronounced separately. french spacing before the question mark still applies. 5. `la cuisine de la maison est grande.` — the **s** between vowels in `maison` sounds like **z**. the final **e** in `grande` makes the **d** audible. ## unit 6: elision, liaison, and rhythm ### elision: dropping a vowel when a short word ending in a vowel meets a word starting with a vowel sound, the vowel is dropped and replaced by an apostrophe. this is **required**, not optional. | words | becomes | english | |---|---|---| | `le` + `ami` | `l'ami` | the friend | | `je` + `habite` | `j'habite` | i live | | `ne` + `est` | `n'est` | is not | | `de` + `eau` | `d'eau` | of water | | `que` + `il` | `qu'il` | that he | remember that `h` is silent, so a word starting with `h` usually counts as starting with a vowel: `l'hôtel`, `j'habite`. you will apply elision in every chapter from chapter 1 onward. ### liaison: linking words together normally a final consonant is silent. but when the next word begins with a vowel sound, that consonant often **wakes up and links** to it. | written | sounds like | english | |---|---|---| | `les amis` | \"lay-z-ami\" | the friends | | `vous êtes` | \"vou-z-et\" | you are | | `un grand arbre` | \"un grahn-t-arbre\" | a big tree | | `mon ami` | \"mon-n-ami\" | my friend | sound changes in liaison: `s` and `x` become \"z,\" and `d` becomes \"t.\" liaison is often the **only** way to hear that something is plural, because the plural `-s` is otherwise silent: - `le ami` is impossible, but compare `l'ami` (one friend) with `les amis` (several friends, linked with a \"z\") do not force liaison everywhere. learn it first in fixed high-frequency phrases such as `vous avez`, `nous avons`, `les enfants`, `c'est un`. one firm rule: **never link after `et`** (and). `un livre et un stylo` has no link after `et`. ### rhythm and stress english stresses one syllable hard inside a word. french does not. french gives **roughly equal weight to every syllable**, with a slight lengthening on the **last syllable of a group of words**. - english: *im-por-tant* - french: `important` — three even syllables, with a small push on the last try `c'est un restaurant italien.` — it is an italian restaurant. say it as one smooth group with no strong internal stress. sounding french depends more on this even rhythm than on any single consonant. ### retrieval check 1. write `je` + `aime` correctly. 2. write `ne` + `a` + `pas` around the verb. 3. in `nous avons`, what sound links the two words? 4. after which small word is liaison forbidden? 1. `j'aime` — `je` becomes `j'` before a vowel sound. elision is written with an apostrophe. 2. `n'a pas` — the full sentence could be `il n'a pas de café.` `ne` becomes `n'` before the vowel sound in `a`. 3. in `nous avons`, the final **s** of `nous` links as a **z** sound. 4. liaison is forbidden after `et`. in `un homme et une femme`, do not link from `et` into `une`. the rule difference: elision changes the spelling, while liaison changes the sound between two written words. ### sentence usage practice say each sentence as one smooth group, then write it from memory. 1. `j'aime les enfants.` — i like children. 2. `vous avez un grand appartement.` — you have a large apartment. 3. `nous allons à l'école.` — we are going to school. 4. `ce n'est pas un problème.` — it is not a problem. 5. `un homme et une femme arrivent.` — a man and a woman arrive. model memory check: 1. `j'aime les enfants.` — `je` becomes `j'` before `aime`; `les enfants` may link with a **z** sound. 2. `vous avez un grand appartement.` — `vous avez` links with **z**, and `grand appartement` links with a **t** sound. 3. `nous allons à l'école.` — `nous allons` links with **z**; `la école` must become `l'école`. 4. `ce n'est pas un problème.` — `ne` becomes `n'` before `est`, and `pas` stays after the verb. 5. `un homme et une femme arrivent.` — link in `un homme` and from `femme` to `arrivent`; never link after `et`. check both spelling and sound. a sentence can be spelled correctly but still lose the rhythm if you pronounce each word separately. #### notice the pattern in sentence 5, mark where you link and where you must not. there is a link in `un homme` and in `une femme arrivent`, but none after `et`. model memory check: 1. `j'aime les enfants.` — `je` becomes `j'` before `aime`. in careful speech, the **s** of `les` links to `enfants` with a **z** sound. 2. `vous avez un grand appartement.` — `vous avez` links with a **z** sound. `grand appartement` links with a **t** sound because final **d** changes to **t** in liaison. 3. `nous allons à l'école.` — `nous allons` links with a **z** sound, and `la école` becomes `l'école`. 4. `ce n'est pas un problème.` — `ne` becomes `n'` before `est`. do not drop `pas` in standard written french. 5. `un homme et une femme arrivent.` — link in `un homme` and between `femme` and `arrivent`; do not link after `et`. the word `homme` begins with a silent **h**. ## unit 7: greetings, politeness, and `tu` or `vous` ### core idea french politeness is more explicit than english politeness. adding `bonjour` before a request is not optional decoration; leaving it out sounds abrupt to french ears. > **the single most useful habit in this course:** begin every interaction with `bonjour` (or `bonsoir` in the evening) before you say anything else. ### essential greetings | french | english | when to use | |---|---|---| | `bonjour` | hello / good morning | any time until evening. always safe. | | `bonsoir` | good evening | from roughly 6 p.m. | | `salut` | hi / bye | informal only, with friends. | | `au revoir` | goodbye | any situation. | | `bonne journée` | have a good day | when leaving. | | `à bientôt` | see you soon | informal farewell. | ### please, thank you, sorry | french | english | note | |---|---|---| | `s'il vous plaît` | please | formal or to a group. | | `s'il te plaît` | please | informal, one person you know. | | `merci` | thank you | universal. | | `merci beaucoup` | thank you very much | warmer. | | `de rien` | you're welcome | literally \"of nothing.\" | | `excusez-moi` | excuse me | to get attention or apologize. | | `pardon` | sorry / excuse me | short, for bumping into someone. | ### choosing `tu` or `vous` this choice exists in every french sentence you address to someone, so decide it early. | use `tu` | use `vous` | |---|---| | a close friend | someone you have just met | | a family member | a shop assistant, waiter, official | | a child | someone noticeably older | | a classmate of similar age | **any group of people**, always | **when in doubt, use `vous`.** an unnecessary `vous` sounds polite. an unwanted `tu` sounds rude. a french speaker will invite you to switch by saying `on peut se tutoyer ?` — shall we use \"tu\" with each other? note that `vous` is also the plural \"you.\" even with close friends, once there are two of them, you must use `vous`. ### asking how someone is | french | english | register | |---|---|---| | `ça va ?` | how's it going? | informal | | `comment ça va ?` | how are you? | neutral | | `comment allez-vous ?` | how are you? | formal | | `ça va bien, merci. et vous ?` | i'm well, thanks. and you? | polite reply | | `ça va, et toi ?` | i'm fine, and you? | informal reply | always return the question. answering `ça va` and stopping sounds like you are ending the conversation. ### sentence usage practice 1. `bonjour madame, comment allez-vous ?` — hello ma'am, how are you? 2. `salut ! ça va ?` — hi! how's it going? 3. `un café, s'il vous plaît.` — a coffee, please. 4. `merci beaucoup. — de rien.` — thank you very much. — you're welcome. 5. `excusez-moi, où est la gare ?` — excuse me, where is the train station? 6. `au revoir, bonne journée !` — goodbye, have a good day! model memory check: 1. `bonjour madame, comment allez-vous ?` — use `vous` with a stranger or in a formal situation. 2. `salut ! ça va ?` — `salut` is informal, so it belongs with friends, family, or peers. 3. `un café, s'il vous plaît.` — the request is polite because it includes `s'il vous plaît`; in real life, say `bonjour` first. 4. `merci beaucoup. — de rien.` — this is the normal thank-you and reply pair. 5. `excusez-moi, où est la gare ?` — `où` needs the accent to mean where, and `excusez-moi` uses the formal or plural form. 6. `au revoir, bonne journée !` — this is a standard daytime farewell; french leaves a space before `!`. for any greeting line, mark yourself wrong if the social register is wrong even when the words are grammatical. #### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1 and 3 in the informal register (`tu` forms) and sentence 2 in the formal register. #### make it personal write the exact four lines you would say when entering a bakery: greeting, request, thanks, farewell. model memory check: 1. `bonjour madame, comment allez-vous ?` — this is formal because `vous` is used with `madame`. use it with a stranger or someone you do not know well. 2. `salut ! ça va ?` — `salut` is informal, so it fits friends and family, not a shop or a first meeting. 3. `un café, s'il vous plaît.` — the request is polite because it includes `s'il vous plaît`. in a real shop, say `bonjour` first. 4. `merci beaucoup. — de rien.` — `de rien` is a standard answer to thanks. do not translate the english words one by one. 5. `excusez-moi, où est la gare ?` — `excusez-moi` uses the formal or plural form. `où` needs the accent to mean where. 6. `au revoir, bonne journée !` — this is the normal polite leaving formula during the day. change the sentence: - informal version of sentence 1: `salut, comment vas-tu ?` — use `tu` for one familiar person. - informal version of sentence 3: `un café, s'il te plaît.` — `te` replaces `vous` for one familiar person. - formal version of sentence 2: `bonjour ! comment allez-vous ?` — use `vous` and a neutral greeting. bakery model: 1. `bonjour.` 2. `un croissant, s'il vous plaît.` 3. `merci beaucoup.` 4. `au revoir, bonne journée !` your exact request may differ, but it must open with `bonjour` and include `s'il vous plaît`. ## unit 8: numbers 0 to 20 and introducing yourself ### numbers 0 to 20 chapter 4 covers the full number system. these twenty are enough to give your age, a price, and a phone number. | # | french | # | french | |---:|---|---:|---| | 0 | `zéro` | 11 | `onze` | | 1 | `un` | 12 | `douze` | | 2 | `deux` | 13 | `treize` | | 3 | `trois` | 14 | `quatorze` | | 4 | `quatre` | 15 | `quinze` | | 5 | `cinq` | 16 | `seize` | | 6 | `six` | 17 | `dix-sept` | | 7 | `sept` | 18 | `dix-huit` | | 8 | `huit` | 19 | `dix-neuf` | | 9 | `neuf` | 20 | `vingt` | pronunciation warnings: - the final consonant of `cinq`, `six`, `huit`, `dix` is pronounced when the number stands alone, but often changes before a noun: `six` alone ends in \"s,\" yet `six livres` drops it and `six ans` links as \"z.\" - `sept` keeps its \"t\" but drops the `p`. - `vingt` has a silent `t` when alone. do not over-study this now. say the numbers in order daily until they are automatic. ### introducing yourself these sentences use grammar from chapters 1-4. learn them now as **fixed chunks**; the rules behind them arrive later. | french | english | |---|---| | `je m'appelle marie.` | my name is marie. | | `comment vous appelez-vous ?` | what is your name? (formal) | | `comment tu t'appelles ?` | what is your name? (informal) | | `enchanté.` / `enchantée.` | nice to meet you. (m. / f.) | | `j'ai vingt ans.` | i am twenty years old. | | `je suis américain.` / `américaine.` | i am american. (m. / f.) | | `j'habite à toronto.` | i live in toronto. | | `je parle un peu français.` | i speak a little french. | two things to notice now and understand later: 1. french uses **have**, not **be**, for age: `j'ai vingt ans` literally means \"i have twenty years.\" chapter 2 explains this. 2. `enchanté` adds an `-e` if you are female. that is adjective agreement, taught in chapter 1. ### survival phrases when you are stuck these four sentences will keep almost any beginner conversation alive: - `je ne comprends pas.` — i don't understand. - `pouvez-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ?` — can you repeat, please? - `plus lentement, s'il vous plaît.` — more slowly, please. - `comment dit-on \"book\" en français ?` — how do you say \"book\" in french? a fuller list is in [`survival-phrases.md`](survival-phrases.md). ### production task write and memorize a five-line self-introduction: name, age, nationality, city, and language level. say it aloud without reading. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. **pronouncing final consonants.** `petit` ends in a silent t. remember careful for the usual exceptions. 2. **saying `tu` with an \"oo\" sound.** `tu` and `tout` are different words. practice the rounded-lip `u` separately. 3. **pronouncing the `n` in a nasal vowel.** in `bon`, the n is an instruction for the vowel, not a consonant you say. 4. **treating accents as optional.** `ou` (or) and `où` (where) are different words; write the accent. 5. **forgetting elision.** write `j'habite`, never `je habite`. 6. **stressing one syllable hard.** french syllables are even. uneven stress is the most common reason a learner sounds foreign even with correct sounds. 7. **starting a request without `bonjour`.** say `bonjour` first, every time. 8. **using `tu` with a stranger.** default to `vous` until invited to switch. 9. **assuming `ils parlent` sounds different from `il parle`.** the `-ent` ending is silent; only the subject reveals number. ## mini-dialogue: arriving at a café read the dialogue in french first. then use the english to check your understanding. **serveur :** `bonjour madame, vous désirez ?` — hello ma'am, what would you like? **claire :** `bonjour. un café et un croissant, s'il vous plaît.` — hello. a coffee and a croissant, please. **serveur :** `très bien. c'est tout ?` — very good. is that all? **claire :** `oui, c'est tout. ça fait combien ?` — yes, that's all. how much is it? **serveur :** `six euros, s'il vous plaît.` — six euros, please. **claire :** `pardon, je ne comprends pas. plus lentement, s'il vous plaît ?` — sorry, i don't understand. more slowly, please? **serveur :** `six euros.` — six euros. **claire :** `ah, merci beaucoup ! au revoir.` — ah, thank you very much! goodbye. **serveur :** `au revoir, bonne journée !` — goodbye, have a good day! ### guided analysis 1. find every use of `s'il vous plaît` and explain why the formal form is correct here. 2. find two silent final consonants in the dialogue. 3. find one nasal vowel in `bonjour`, `combien`, and `six euros`. 4. where does liaison occur in `six euros`? what sound links them? 5. rewrite claire's first line as if she were speaking to a close friend. 1. `s'il vous plaît` appears in `un café et un croissant, s'il vous plaît.`, `six euros, s'il vous plaît.`, and `plus lentement, s'il vous plaît ?` the formal form is correct because claire and the server are strangers in a service interaction. 2. two silent final consonants: final **t** in `tout` and final **t** in `lentement`. another clear one is final **p** in `beaucoup` from `merci beaucoup`. in `six euros`, the final written **x** is not silent because it links to the next word. 3. nasal vowels: `bonjour` has the nasal **on** in the first syllable. `combien` has two, the **om** of `com-` and the **ien** at the end. `six euros` has none — the useful sound there is liaison, not nasality. 4. in `six euros`, the final **x** links to `euros` with a **z** sound: `six euros`. 5. close-friend version: `salut. un café et un croissant, s'il te plaît.` — `vous` changes to `te` because claire is now speaking to one familiar person. one existing prompt is a little misleading: `six euros` is mainly a liaison example, not a nasal-vowel example. ## connected reading: mon premier jour `bonjour ! je m'appelle ana. j'ai dix-neuf ans et je suis canadienne. j'habite à montréal, dans une petite rue près de l'université. je parle un peu français, mais je ne comprends pas toujours les gens rapides. le matin, je dis bonjour à mes voisins, et le soir je dis bonsoir. quand je ne comprends pas, je demande : « plus lentement, s'il vous plaît. » les gens sont patients, alors je progresse un peu chaque jour.` — hello! my name is ana. i am nineteen years old and i am canadian. i live in montreal, on a small street near the university. i speak a little french, but i do not always understand fast people. in the morning, i say hello to my neighbors, and in the evening i say good evening. when i do not understand, i ask: \"more slowly, please.\" people are patient, so i make a little progress every day. ### reading practice 1. read the text aloud twice, focusing on even syllable rhythm. 2. find three words with a nasal vowel. 3. find two examples of elision. 4. find the two numbers and say them aloud. 5. rewrite the first three sentences about yourself, changing the name, age, nationality, and city. 1. rhythm task: answers vary, but you should hear even syllables, not one heavy english-style stress inside each word. 2. three nasal-vowel words include `bonjour`, `ans`, `canadienne`, `montréal`, `dans`, `comprends`, `gens`, `patients`, and `lentement`. some of these stop being nasal in related feminine forms, so listen carefully. 3. two examples of elision: `m'appelle`, `j'ai`, `j'habite`, and `l'université`. 4. the numbers are `dix-neuf` and the idea of `un` in `un peu`. `dix-neuf` is nineteen; `un` is one or a. 5. model rewrite: `bonjour ! je m'appelle sam. j'ai vingt ans et je suis américain. j'habite à boston, dans une petite rue près de l'université.` for your own version, keep `j'ai` for age and make the nationality adjective agree with the person speaking. ## chapter reference sheet ### sound traps at a glance | trap | rule | |---|---| | final consonants | usually silent, except careful (`c`, `r`, `f`, `l`). | | final `-e` | silent, but makes the previous consonant heard. | | `-ent` on a verb | completely silent. | | `h` | always silent. | | `u` vs `ou` | `u` = rounded \"ee\"; `ou` = \"oo.\" | | vowel + `n`/`m` | nasal, unless a vowel follows or the n is doubled. | | `s` between vowels | sounds like \"z.\" | | `ch` / `j` / `qu` / `gn` | \"sh\" / \"zh\" / \"k\" / \"ny.\" | ### politeness at a glance | situation | say | |---|---| | entering anywhere | `bonjour` | | making a request | add `s'il vous plaît` | | stranger, older person, any group | `vous` | | friend, family, child | `tu` | | leaving | `au revoir, bonne journée` | ### core habits - say every french example aloud; never read only with your eyes. - imitate the rhythm of a whole phrase before fixing single sounds. - learn greetings and politeness as fixed chunks, not word by word. - accept that spelling and sound differ; trust the rules over the letters. - return to this chapter whenever a new word resists pronunciation. ## final production assignment record yourself (30-60 seconds) doing the following: 1. greet a stranger politely and ask how they are; 2. introduce yourself with name, age, nationality, and city; 3. order two items in a café and ask the price; 4. say you do not understand and ask the person to slow down; 5. thank them and say goodbye. after recording: 1. listen back once for **rhythm** only, checking for even syllables; 2. listen again for the `u` versus `ou` distinction; 3. listen a third time for silent final consonants you accidentally pronounced; 4. write the whole script from memory; 5. re-record only the lines you corrected. when you can do this comfortably, you are ready for chapter 1. answers vary, but your script should sound like a polite beginner who can survive a first café interaction. model script: `bonjour monsieur, comment allez-vous ? je m'appelle ana. j'ai dix-neuf ans. je suis canadienne et j'habite à montréal. je parle un peu français. un café et un croissant, s'il vous plaît. ça fait combien ? pardon, je ne comprends pas. plus lentement, s'il vous plaît ? merci beaucoup. au revoir, bonne journée !` how it satisfies the brief: it greets a stranger with `bonjour` and `vous`; it gives name, age, nationality, city, and level; it orders two café items and asks the price; it says the learner does not understand and asks for slower speech; it ends with thanks and goodbye. mark yourself wrong if: - you use `tu` with the stranger; - you say **je suis dix-neuf ans** instead of `j'ai dix-neuf ans`; - you omit `s'il vous plaît` from the request; - you pronounce silent final consonants in words such as `vous`, `ans`, or `beaucoup`; - you leave out required apostrophes in `j'ai`, `j'habite`, or `s'il`. "},{"id":"chapter-00-getting-started/survival-phrases.md","title":"Survival Phrases: Your First 100 French Sentences","type":"Phrasebook","html":"<h1 id=\"survival-phrases-your-first-100-french-sentences\">Survival Phrases: Your First 100 French Sentences</h1>\n<p>This is a phrasebook, not a grammar lesson. Every sentence here is a <strong>fixed chunk</strong> you can use today without understanding how it is built. The grammar behind each one arrives in Chapters 1-16.</p>\n<p>Tap any French phrase on the website to hear it. Say it aloud immediately after. If it goes by too fast, slow <strong>Speaking speed</strong> down in the sidebar, or select the phrase and press <strong>Slower</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-use-this-page\">How to use this page</h2>\n<p>Do not memorize all of it. Choose <strong>five phrases per day</strong>, say each one ten times aloud, and use them in a real or imagined situation the same day. A phrase you have used once is worth more than ten you have only read.</p>\n<p>Star the phrases you actually need. A learner in a French-speaking city needs the café and directions sections first; a learner in a classroom needs the classroom section first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-greetings-and-goodbyes\">1. Greetings and goodbyes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code></td>\n<td>Hello / Good morning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonsoir</code></td>\n<td>Good evening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut</code></td>\n<td>Hi / Bye (informal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir</code></td>\n<td>Goodbye</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne journée</code></td>\n<td>Have a good day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne soirée</code></td>\n<td>Have a good evening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À bientôt</code></td>\n<td>See you soon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À demain</code></td>\n<td>See you tomorrow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne nuit</code></td>\n<td>Good night (only when going to bed)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Careful: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne nuit</code> is not a greeting. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonsoir</code> when you arrive in the evening and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne nuit</code> only when someone is going to sleep.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-politeness\">2. Politeness</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">S'il vous plaît</code></td>\n<td>Please (formal / plural)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">S'il te plaît</code></td>\n<td>Please (informal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci</code></td>\n<td>Thank you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup</code></td>\n<td>Thank you very much</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De rien</code></td>\n<td>You're welcome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vous en prie</code></td>\n<td>You're welcome (formal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Excusez-moi</code></td>\n<td>Excuse me (formal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pardon</code></td>\n<td>Sorry / Excuse me</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Désolé</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Désolée</code></td>\n<td>Sorry (m. / f.)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-are-you\">3. How are you?</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça va ?</code></td>\n<td>How's it going?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment ça va ?</code></td>\n<td>How are you?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment allez-vous ?</code></td>\n<td>How are you? (formal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça va bien, merci.</code></td>\n<td>I'm well, thank you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça va, et toi ?</code></td>\n<td>I'm fine, and you? (informal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et vous ?</code></td>\n<td>And you? (formal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pas mal.</code></td>\n<td>Not bad.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça ne va pas très bien.</code></td>\n<td>I'm not doing very well.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Always return the question with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et toi ?</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et vous ?</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-introducing-yourself\">4. Introducing yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'appelle ___.</code></td>\n<td>My name is ___.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment vous appelez-vous ?</code></td>\n<td>What is your name? (formal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment tu t'appelles ?</code></td>\n<td>What is your name? (informal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Enchanté.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Enchantée.</code></td>\n<td>Nice to meet you. (m. / f.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai ___ ans.</code></td>\n<td>I am ___ years old.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis américain(e) / canadien(ne) / anglais(e).</code></td>\n<td>I am American / Canadian / English.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite à ___.</code></td>\n<td>I live in ___.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens de ___.</code></td>\n<td>I come from ___.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis étudiant(e).</code></td>\n<td>I am a student.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille dans ___.</code></td>\n<td>I work in ___.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"5-when-you-don-t-understand\">5. When you don't understand</h2>\n<p>These are the most important phrases in this file. Learn them first.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne comprends pas.</code></td>\n<td>I don't understand.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne sais pas.</code></td>\n<td>I don't know.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pouvez-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ?</code></td>\n<td>Can you repeat, please?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>More slowly, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment dit-on ___ en français ?</code></td>\n<td>How do you say ___ in French?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qu'est-ce que ça veut dire ?</code></td>\n<td>What does that mean?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment ça s'écrit ?</code></td>\n<td>How is that spelled?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous anglais ?</code></td>\n<td>Do you speak English?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle un peu français.</code></td>\n<td>I speak a little French.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'apprends le français.</code></td>\n<td>I am learning French.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A useful habit: instead of switching to English, say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne comprends pas</code> and wait. Most people will simply rephrase more simply.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-in-a-caf-or-restaurant\">6. In a café or restaurant</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Une table pour deux, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>A table for two, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La carte, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>The menu, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais ___.</code></td>\n<td>I would like ___.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un café, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>A coffee, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Une carafe d'eau, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>A jug of tap water, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est tout, merci.</code></td>\n<td>That's all, thank you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça fait combien ?</code></td>\n<td>How much is it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'addition, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>The bill, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux payer par carte ?</code></td>\n<td>Can I pay by card?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'était délicieux.</code></td>\n<td>It was delicious.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais</code> (I would like) is far more polite than <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux</code> (I want). Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais</code> for every request.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-shopping\">7. Shopping</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Combien ça coûte ?</code></td>\n<td>How much does it cost?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est trop cher.</code></td>\n<td>That's too expensive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je cherche ___.</code></td>\n<td>I'm looking for ___.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous avez ___ ?</code></td>\n<td>Do you have ___?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je regarde, merci.</code></td>\n<td>I'm just looking, thanks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le prends.</code></td>\n<td>I'll take it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous avez une autre taille ?</code></td>\n<td>Do you have another size?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"8-directions-and-travel\">8. Directions and travel</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où est ___ ?</code></td>\n<td>Where is ___?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où sont les toilettes ?</code></td>\n<td>Where are the toilets?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je cherche la gare.</code></td>\n<td>I'm looking for the train station.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est loin ?</code></td>\n<td>Is it far?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est près d'ici ?</code></td>\n<td>Is it near here?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À gauche</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À droite</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tout droit</code></td>\n<td>Left / Right / Straight ahead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis perdu(e).</code></td>\n<td>I am lost.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un billet pour ___, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>A ticket to ___, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quelle heure part le train ?</code></td>\n<td>What time does the train leave?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Note the trap: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout droit</code> means \"straight ahead,\" while <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à droite</code> means \"to the right.\"</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-classroom-and-study-phrases\">9. Classroom and study phrases</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai une question.</code></td>\n<td>I have a question.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pouvez-vous m'aider ?</code></td>\n<td>Can you help me?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment ?</code></td>\n<td>Sorry, what?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Encore une fois, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>One more time, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est correct ?</code></td>\n<td>Is that correct?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas sûr(e).</code></td>\n<td>I'm not sure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qu'est-ce que c'est ?</code></td>\n<td>What is it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que je peux ___ ?</code></td>\n<td>May I ___?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"10-emergencies\">10. Emergencies</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au secours !</code></td>\n<td>Help!</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Appelez un médecin !</code></td>\n<td>Call a doctor!</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai besoin d'aide.</code></td>\n<td>I need help.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis malade.</code></td>\n<td>I am sick.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où est la pharmacie ?</code></td>\n<td>Where is the pharmacy?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a eu un accident.</code></td>\n<td>There has been an accident.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"11-small-words-that-do-a-lot-of-work\">11. Small words that do a lot of work</h2>\n<p>These connectors make short sentences sound natural. Sprinkle them into everything you say.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">non</code></td>\n<td>yes / no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code></td>\n<td>and</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mais</code></td>\n<td>but</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aussi</code></td>\n<td>also</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">alors</code></td>\n<td>so / then</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parce que</code></td>\n<td>because</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un peu</code></td>\n<td>a little</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup</code></td>\n<td>a lot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">très</code></td>\n<td>very</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maintenant</code></td>\n<td>now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aujourd'hui</code></td>\n<td>today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">demain</code></td>\n<td>tomorrow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peut-être</code></td>\n<td>maybe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien sûr</code></td>\n<td>of course</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'accord</code></td>\n<td>okay / agreed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"12-two-ready-made-conversations\">12. Two ready-made conversations</h2>\n<p>Memorize these end to end. They cover most first encounters.</p>\n<h3 id=\"meeting-someone\">Meeting someone</h3>\n<p><strong>A :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour ! Je m'appelle Ana. Et vous ?</code> — Hello! My name is Ana. And you?</p>\n<p><strong>B :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour Ana, je m'appelle Paul. Enchanté.</code> — Hello Ana, my name is Paul. Nice to meet you.</p>\n<p><strong>A :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Enchantée. Vous habitez ici ?</code> — Nice to meet you. Do you live here?</p>\n<p><strong>B :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, j'habite à Lyon. Et vous ?</code> — Yes, I live in Lyon. And you?</p>\n<p><strong>A :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis canadienne, mais j'habite à Paris maintenant. Je parle un peu français.</code> — I am Canadian, but I live in Paris now. I speak a little French.</p>\n<p><strong>B :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous parlez très bien !</code> — You speak very well!</p>\n<h3 id=\"ordering-a-drink\">Ordering a drink</h3>\n<p><strong>A :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour, un café et une eau, s'il vous plaît.</code> — Hello, a coffee and a water, please.</p>\n<p><strong>B :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bien sûr. C'est tout ?</code> — Of course. Is that all?</p>\n<p><strong>A :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, c'est tout. Ça fait combien ?</code> — Yes, that's all. How much is it?</p>\n<p><strong>B :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quatre euros cinquante.</code> — Four euros fifty.</p>\n<p><strong>A :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pardon, je ne comprends pas. Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît ?</code> — Sorry, I don't understand. More slowly, please?</p>\n<p><strong>B :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quatre euros cinquante.</code> — Four euros fifty.</p>\n<p><strong>A :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup. Au revoir !</code> — Thank you very much. Goodbye!</p>\n<h2 id=\"practice-plan\">Practice plan</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>Task</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Sections 1-3. Greet five people aloud, out loud, at home.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Section 4. Record your self-introduction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Section 5. Memorize all ten \"I don't understand\" phrases.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Section 6 or 8, whichever you will use first.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Section 11. Rewrite five earlier phrases adding a connector.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Both conversations in section 12, from memory.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Cover the English column and say each starred phrase from memory.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A phrase counts as learned only when it comes out <strong>without an English step in the middle</strong>.</p>","search":"# survival phrases: your first 100 french sentences this is a phrasebook, not a grammar lesson. every sentence here is a **fixed chunk** you can use today without understanding how it is built. the grammar behind each one arrives in chapters 1-16. tap any french phrase on the website to hear it. say it aloud immediately after. if it goes by too fast, slow **speaking speed** down in the sidebar, or select the phrase and press **slower**. ## how to use this page do not memorize all of it. choose **five phrases per day**, say each one ten times aloud, and use them in a real or imagined situation the same day. a phrase you have used once is worth more than ten you have only read. star the phrases you actually need. a learner in a french-speaking city needs the café and directions sections first; a learner in a classroom needs the classroom section first. ## 1. greetings and goodbyes | french | english | |---|---| | `bonjour` | hello / good morning | | `bonsoir` | good evening | | `salut` | hi / bye (informal) | | `au revoir` | goodbye | | `bonne journée` | have a good day | | `bonne soirée` | have a good evening | | `à bientôt` | see you soon | | `à demain` | see you tomorrow | | `bonne nuit` | good night (only when going to bed) | careful: `bonne nuit` is not a greeting. use `bonsoir` when you arrive in the evening and `bonne nuit` only when someone is going to sleep. ## 2. politeness | french | english | |---|---| | `s'il vous plaît` | please (formal / plural) | | `s'il te plaît` | please (informal) | | `merci` | thank you | | `merci beaucoup` | thank you very much | | `de rien` | you're welcome | | `je vous en prie` | you're welcome (formal) | | `excusez-moi` | excuse me (formal) | | `pardon` | sorry / excuse me | | `désolé` / `désolée` | sorry (m. / f.) | ## 3. how are you? | french | english | |---|---| | `ça va ?` | how's it going? | | `comment ça va ?` | how are you? | | `comment allez-vous ?` | how are you? (formal) | | `ça va bien, merci.` | i'm well, thank you. | | `ça va, et toi ?` | i'm fine, and you? (informal) | | `et vous ?` | and you? (formal) | | `pas mal.` | not bad. | | `ça ne va pas très bien.` | i'm not doing very well. | always return the question with `et toi ?` or `et vous ?`. ## 4. introducing yourself | french | english | |---|---| | `je m'appelle ___.` | my name is ___. | | `comment vous appelez-vous ?` | what is your name? (formal) | | `comment tu t'appelles ?` | what is your name? (informal) | | `enchanté.` / `enchantée.` | nice to meet you. (m. / f.) | | `j'ai ___ ans.` | i am ___ years old. | | `je suis américain(e) / canadien(ne) / anglais(e).` | i am american / canadian / english. | | `j'habite à ___.` | i live in ___. | | `je viens de ___.` | i come from ___. | | `je suis étudiant(e).` | i am a student. | | `je travaille dans ___.` | i work in ___. | ## 5. when you don't understand these are the most important phrases in this file. learn them first. | french | english | |---|---| | `je ne comprends pas.` | i don't understand. | | `je ne sais pas.` | i don't know. | | `pouvez-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ?` | can you repeat, please? | | `plus lentement, s'il vous plaît.` | more slowly, please. | | `comment dit-on ___ en français ?` | how do you say ___ in french? | | `qu'est-ce que ça veut dire ?` | what does that mean? | | `comment ça s'écrit ?` | how is that spelled? | | `parlez-vous anglais ?` | do you speak english? | | `je parle un peu français.` | i speak a little french. | | `j'apprends le français.` | i am learning french. | a useful habit: instead of switching to english, say `je ne comprends pas` and wait. most people will simply rephrase more simply. ## 6. in a café or restaurant | french | english | |---|---| | `une table pour deux, s'il vous plaît.` | a table for two, please. | | `la carte, s'il vous plaît.` | the menu, please. | | `je voudrais ___.` | i would like ___. | | `un café, s'il vous plaît.` | a coffee, please. | | `une carafe d'eau, s'il vous plaît.` | a jug of tap water, please. | | `c'est tout, merci.` | that's all, thank you. | | `ça fait combien ?` | how much is it? | | `l'addition, s'il vous plaît.` | the bill, please. | | `je peux payer par carte ?` | can i pay by card? | | `c'était délicieux.` | it was delicious. | `je voudrais` (i would like) is far more polite than `je veux` (i want). use `je voudrais` for every request. ## 7. shopping | french | english | |---|---| | `combien ça coûte ?` | how much does it cost? | | `c'est trop cher.` | that's too expensive. | | `je cherche ___.` | i'm looking for ___. | | `vous avez ___ ?` | do you have ___? | | `je regarde, merci.` | i'm just looking, thanks. | | `je le prends.` | i'll take it. | | `est-ce que vous avez une autre taille ?` | do you have another size? | ## 8. directions and travel | french | english | |---|---| | `où est ___ ?` | where is ___? | | `où sont les toilettes ?` | where are the toilets? | | `je cherche la gare.` | i'm looking for the train station. | | `c'est loin ?` | is it far? | | `c'est près d'ici ?` | is it near here? | | `à gauche` / `à droite` / `tout droit` | left / right / straight ahead | | `je suis perdu(e).` | i am lost. | | `un billet pour ___, s'il vous plaît.` | a ticket to ___, please. | | `à quelle heure part le train ?` | what time does the train leave? | note the trap: `tout droit` means \"straight ahead,\" while `à droite` means \"to the right.\" ## 9. classroom and study phrases | french | english | |---|---| | `j'ai une question.` | i have a question. | | `pouvez-vous m'aider ?` | can you help me? | | `comment ?` | sorry, what? | | `encore une fois, s'il vous plaît.` | one more time, please. | | `c'est correct ?` | is that correct? | | `je ne suis pas sûr(e).` | i'm not sure. | | `qu'est-ce que c'est ?` | what is it? | | `est-ce que je peux ___ ?` | may i ___? | ## 10. emergencies | french | english | |---|---| | `au secours !` | help! | | `appelez un médecin !` | call a doctor! | | `j'ai besoin d'aide.` | i need help. | | `je suis malade.` | i am sick. | | `où est la pharmacie ?` | where is the pharmacy? | | `il y a eu un accident.` | there has been an accident. | ## 11. small words that do a lot of work these connectors make short sentences sound natural. sprinkle them into everything you say. | french | english | |---|---| | `oui` / `non` | yes / no | | `et` | and | | `mais` | but | | `aussi` | also | | `alors` | so / then | | `parce que` | because | | `un peu` | a little | | `beaucoup` | a lot | | `très` | very | | `maintenant` | now | | `aujourd'hui` | today | | `demain` | tomorrow | | `peut-être` | maybe | | `bien sûr` | of course | | `d'accord` | okay / agreed | ## 12. two ready-made conversations memorize these end to end. they cover most first encounters. ### meeting someone **a :** `bonjour ! je m'appelle ana. et vous ?` — hello! my name is ana. and you? **b :** `bonjour ana, je m'appelle paul. enchanté.` — hello ana, my name is paul. nice to meet you. **a :** `enchantée. vous habitez ici ?` — nice to meet you. do you live here? **b :** `oui, j'habite à lyon. et vous ?` — yes, i live in lyon. and you? **a :** `je suis canadienne, mais j'habite à paris maintenant. je parle un peu français.` — i am canadian, but i live in paris now. i speak a little french. **b :** `vous parlez très bien !` — you speak very well! ### ordering a drink **a :** `bonjour, un café et une eau, s'il vous plaît.` — hello, a coffee and a water, please. **b :** `bien sûr. c'est tout ?` — of course. is that all? **a :** `oui, c'est tout. ça fait combien ?` — yes, that's all. how much is it? **b :** `quatre euros cinquante.` — four euros fifty. **a :** `pardon, je ne comprends pas. plus lentement, s'il vous plaît ?` — sorry, i don't understand. more slowly, please? **b :** `quatre euros cinquante.` — four euros fifty. **a :** `merci beaucoup. au revoir !` — thank you very much. goodbye! ## practice plan | day | task | |---|---| | 1 | sections 1-3. greet five people aloud, out loud, at home. | | 2 | section 4. record your self-introduction. | | 3 | section 5. memorize all ten \"i don't understand\" phrases. | | 4 | section 6 or 8, whichever you will use first. | | 5 | section 11. rewrite five earlier phrases adding a connector. | | 6 | both conversations in section 12, from memory. | | 7 | cover the english column and say each starred phrase from memory. | a phrase counts as learned only when it comes out **without an english step in the middle**. "},{"id":"chapter-00-getting-started/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 0 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-0-exercises\">Chapter 0 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these with the lessons closed. Put a question mark beside any answer you guessed, and check those first in the answer key.</p>\n<p>Sections A-J have definite answers. Sections K-L are productive tasks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-silent-letters\">A. Silent letters</h2>\n<p>Is the <strong>final consonant</strong> pronounced? Answer yes or no, then say the word aloud.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avril</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Pronounced?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Most final consonants are silent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>c</strong> is one of the CaReFuL letters.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>r</strong> is one of the CaReFuL letters.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Final <strong>s</strong> is normally silent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>f</strong> is one of the CaReFuL letters.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>The infinitive ending <strong>-er</strong> is a common exception; the final <strong>r</strong> is not pronounced.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avril</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>l</strong> is one of the CaReFuL letters.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Final <strong>d</strong> is silent, and the vowel before it is nasal.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The trap is trusting the spelling too much. In French, a written final consonant is often only a spelling clue, not a sound to pronounce.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-u-versus-ou\">B. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code></h2>\n<p>In each pair, which word contains the \"oo\" sound?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rue</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">roue</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sourd</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Say both words in each pair aloud before answering.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>The <strong>oo</strong> sound is in the second word each time.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> has the French rounded <strong>u</strong> sound; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> has <strong>ou</strong>, the easy <strong>oo</strong> sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">roue</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rue</code> has <strong>u</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">roue</code> has <strong>ou</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code> has <strong>u</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> has <strong>ou</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sourd</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code> has <strong>u</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sourd</code> has <strong>ou</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you cannot hear the difference yet, mark the item for sound practice rather than treating it as a vocabulary error. The mouth position is the rule: French <strong>u</strong> starts from an <strong>ee</strong> tongue position with rounded lips.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-nasal-vowels\">C. Nasal vowels</h2>\n<p>Does the word contain a nasal vowel? Answer nasal or not nasal, and give the reason.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américain</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américaine</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code></td>\n<td>Nasal</td>\n<td>A vowel is followed by <strong>n</strong> with no vowel after it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code></td>\n<td>Not nasal</td>\n<td>The <strong>n</strong> is doubled, so it is pronounced as a consonant.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américain</code></td>\n<td>Nasal</td>\n<td>The ending <strong>ain</strong> is nasal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américaine</code></td>\n<td>Not nasal</td>\n<td>A silent <strong>-e</strong> follows, so the <strong>n</strong> is pronounced.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code></td>\n<td>Not nasal</td>\n<td>A vowel follows the <strong>n</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien</code></td>\n<td>Nasal twice</td>\n<td>Both <strong>om</strong> and <strong>ien</strong> are nasal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></td>\n<td>Nasal</td>\n<td>A vowel is followed by <strong>n</strong> with nothing after it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></td>\n<td>Not nasal</td>\n<td>A vowel sound follows, so the <strong>n</strong> is pronounced.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Items 1/2, 3/4, and 7/8 show why gender can change pronunciation. The feminine form often stops the vowel from being nasal.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-elision\">D. Elision</h2>\n<p>Combine the words correctly.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">école</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> loses its vowel before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>. Elision is required, not optional.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hôtel</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code> is silent, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code> behaves like it begins with a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'est pas</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> stays after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'eau</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eau</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'école</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">école</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The trap is leaving both vowels written out. Forms such as <strong>je ai</strong> or <strong>la école</strong> are not slow French; they are wrong spelling.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-liaison\">E. Liaison</h2>\n<p>Mark where two words link, and give the <strong>linking sound</strong>. If there is no liaison, say so and explain why.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un grand arbre</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon ami</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un livre et un stylo</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>Liaison?</th>\n<th>Linking sound</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>s</strong> becomes a <strong>z</strong> sound.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>s</strong> becomes a <strong>z</strong> sound.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un grand arbre</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>d</strong> becomes a <strong>t</strong> sound.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon ami</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>n</strong> links into the vowel.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un livre et un stylo</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Liaison is never made after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">stylo</code> also begins with a consonant.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Final <strong>s</strong> becomes a <strong>z</strong> sound.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Do not wake up every silent consonant. Liaison happens in specific environments, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code> is the first hard no to memorize.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-consonant-sounds\">F. Consonant sounds</h2>\n<p>Give the sound of the letter or letters in bold type.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code> — the <strong>ç</strong></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le garçon</code> — the <strong>ç</strong></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la chaise</code> — the <strong>ch</strong></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> — the <strong>qu</strong></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la montagne</code> — the <strong>gn</strong></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code> — the <strong>s</strong></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poisson</code> — the <strong>ss</strong></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> — the <strong>j</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code> — the <strong>ç</strong> sounds like <strong>s</strong>; the cedilla forces the soft sound before <strong>a</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le garçon</code> — the <strong>ç</strong> again sounds like <strong>s</strong>, this time before <strong>o</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la chaise</code> — <strong>ch</strong> sounds like <strong>sh</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> — <strong>qu</strong> sounds like <strong>k</strong> only; the <strong>u</strong> is not a separate sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la montagne</code> — <strong>gn</strong> sounds like <strong>ny</strong>, as in canyon.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code> — single <strong>s</strong> between vowels sounds like <strong>z</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poisson</code> — doubled <strong>ss</strong> sounds like <strong>s</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> — <strong>j</strong> sounds like the <strong>s</strong> in measure.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The important minimal pair is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poisson</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poison</code>: doubled <strong>ss</strong> keeps the <strong>s</strong> sound, but one <strong>s</strong> between vowels becomes <strong>z</strong>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"g-tu-or-vous\">G. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>?</h2>\n<p>Choose the correct form of address.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Your closest friend.</li>\n<li>A waiter you have just met.</li>\n<li>Two children together.</li>\n<li>Your grandmother.</li>\n<li>A new manager at your workplace.</li>\n<li>Your younger brother.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> — one close friend takes the familiar form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — a waiter you have just met takes polite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — any group takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, even a group of children.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> — a family member normally takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — a new manager is a person in authority whom you do not know well.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> — a younger brother is family and one person.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Item 3 is the common trap. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> is both formal singular and ordinary plural, so two people always require <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"h-what-would-you-say\">H. What would you say?</h2>\n<p>Give one appropriate French sentence for each situation.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You walk into a bakery at ten in the morning.</li>\n<li>You want to order a coffee politely.</li>\n<li>Someone has just thanked you.</li>\n<li>You did not understand what a person said.</li>\n<li>You want the person to speak more slowly.</li>\n<li>You are leaving a shop at midday.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary, but each response must be polite and appropriate to the situation.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour.</code> — in a bakery, greet first. Starting directly with the order sounds abrupt.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un café, s'il vous plaît.</code> — the request needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.</code> is also correct.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De rien.</code> — a standard reply to thanks. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vous en prie.</code> is also correct but more formal.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne comprends pas.</code> — this directly says you did not understand. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pardon ?</code> is also usable.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît.</code> — a clear survival phrase for asking someone to slow down.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée !</code> — a normal daytime farewell in a shop.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if the French is understandable but socially wrong, such as ordering without <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> or asking a stranger with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"i-numbers\">I. Numbers</h2>\n<p>Write each number in French.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>3</li>\n<li>8</li>\n<li>12</li>\n<li>15</li>\n<li>17</li>\n<li>20</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code> — memorize the small numbers as complete sound chunks.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit</code> — the final consonant is pronounced when the number stands alone.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">douze</code> — do not confuse it with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code>; they are different words.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quinze</code> — the <strong>qu</strong> makes a <strong>k</strong> sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-sept</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sept</code> keeps the <strong>t</strong> sound but not the <strong>p</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt</code> — the final <strong>t</strong> is silent when the word stands alone.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The spelling trap is that numbers do not all pronounce final letters the same way. Learn each number aloud, not just visually.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"j-find-and-fix-the-error\">J. Find and fix the error</h2>\n<p>Each sentence contains one mistake a beginner commonly makes. Rewrite it correctly and name the rule.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je habite à Paris.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis vingt ans.</code></li>\n<li>You arrive at a friend's home at 8 p.m. and say: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne nuit !</code></li>\n<li>In a café you say: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux un café.</code></li>\n<li>You ask a stranger on the street: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu parles anglais ?</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite à Paris.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> must become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before the silent <strong>h</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habite</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code> — French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> for age, literally to have years. Chapter 2 explains the rule fully.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonsoir !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne nuit</code> is for going to bed, not arriving at someone's home in the evening.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux</code> sounds blunt in a café; soften the request and add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous anglais ?</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous parlez anglais ?</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> with a stranger. The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> version is too familiar here.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For each correction, name the rule you fixed. A corrected sentence without the rule is only half learned.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"k-production\">K. Production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a five-line self-introduction: name, age, nationality, city, and language level. Memorize it and say it aloud without reading.</li>\n<li>Write the exact four lines you would say in a bakery: greeting, request, thanks, farewell.</li>\n<li>Choose five phrases from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">survival-phrases.md</code> that you personally will need this week. Write them out and use each one aloud today.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>These are productive tasks, so answers vary. Use these models and criteria to check yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Self-introduction model:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'appelle Ana.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai dix-neuf ans.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis canadienne et j'habite à Montréal.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle un peu français.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>A correct self-introduction includes name, age with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai</code>, nationality with agreement, city with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite à</code>, and language level.</p>\n<p><strong>Bakery script model:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un croissant, s'il vous plaît.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci beaucoup.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>It is wrong if it does not open with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> or if the request omits <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code>.</p>\n<p>For your five survival phrases, answers vary. Choose phrases you can actually use this week, copy accents and apostrophes exactly, and say each one aloud until it is a usable chunk rather than a word-by-word translation.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"l-dictation-and-oral-recall\">L. Dictation and oral recall</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Dictation.</strong> Use text-to-speech on the website to hear six sentences from the \"Sentence usage practice\" sections of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in two passes: first the sounds you missed, then the silent letters and accents you left out.</li>\n<li><strong>Read aloud.</strong> Read the \"Connected reading: mon premier jour\" passage aloud twice. Record the second attempt and check it for even syllable rhythm.</li>\n<li><strong>Oral recall.</strong> Explain aloud, in simple English, and then give one French example from memory:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>which final consonants are usually pronounced;</li>\n<li>what makes a vowel nasal;</li>\n<li>the difference between elision and liaison;</li>\n<li>when to use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>These tasks do not have one hidden script, but they do have clear standards.</p>\n<p><strong>Dictation:</strong> score each sentence in three passes: words, accents, and silent letters you wrote but did not pronounce. The silent-letter pass matters because French spelling often records information your ear cannot hear.</p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:</strong> judge rhythm first. Even syllable rhythm is more important at this stage than a perfect French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Oral recall model answers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Final consonants:</strong> most are silent, but <strong>c</strong>, <strong>r</strong>, <strong>f</strong>, and <strong>l</strong> are often pronounced. The infinitive ending <strong>-er</strong> is a common exception. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Nasal vowels:</strong> a vowel followed by <strong>n</strong> or <strong>m</strong> is nasal when no vowel follows and the consonant is not doubled. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Elision versus liaison:</strong> elision removes a vowel and writes an apostrophe, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai</code>; liaison wakes up a silent final consonant before a vowel sound, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>:</strong> use it with strangers, older people, people in authority, and any group. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment allez-vous ?</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you can state the rule but cannot give a French example, redo the recall tomorrow. Production is the goal.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 0 exercises complete these with the lessons closed. put a question mark beside any answer you guessed, and check those first in the answer key. sections a-j have definite answers. sections k-l are productive tasks. ## a. silent letters is the **final consonant** pronounced? answer yes or no, then say the word aloud. 1. `petit` 2. `avec` 3. `bonjour` 4. `vous` 5. `neuf` 6. `parler` 7. `avril` 8. `grand` | # | word | pronounced? | why | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `petit` | no | most final consonants are silent. | | 2 | `avec` | yes | final **c** is one of the careful letters. | | 3 | `bonjour` | yes | final **r** is one of the careful letters. | | 4 | `vous` | no | final **s** is normally silent. | | 5 | `neuf` | yes | final **f** is one of the careful letters. | | 6 | `parler` | no | the infinitive ending **-er** is a common exception; the final **r** is not pronounced. | | 7 | `avril` | yes | final **l** is one of the careful letters. | | 8 | `grand` | no | final **d** is silent, and the vowel before it is nasal. | the trap is trusting the spelling too much. in french, a written final consonant is often only a spelling clue, not a sound to pronounce. ## b. `u` versus `ou` in each pair, which word contains the \"oo\" sound? 1. `tu` / `tout` 2. `rue` / `roue` 3. `vu` / `vous` 4. `sur` / `sourd` say both words in each pair aloud before answering. the **oo** sound is in the second word each time. 1. `tout` — `tu` has the french rounded **u** sound; `tout` has **ou**, the easy **oo** sound. 2. `roue` — `rue` has **u**; `roue` has **ou**. 3. `vous` — `vu` has **u**; `vous` has **ou**. 4. `sourd` — `sur` has **u**; `sourd` has **ou**. if you cannot hear the difference yet, mark the item for sound practice rather than treating it as a vocabulary error. the mouth position is the rule: french **u** starts from an **ee** tongue position with rounded lips. ## c. nasal vowels does the word contain a nasal vowel? answer nasal or not nasal, and give the reason. 1. `bon` 2. `bonne` 3. `américain` 4. `américaine` 5. `téléphone` 6. `combien` 7. `un` 8. `une` | # | word | answer | why | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `bon` | nasal | a vowel is followed by **n** with no vowel after it. | | 2 | `bonne` | not nasal | the **n** is doubled, so it is pronounced as a consonant. | | 3 | `américain` | nasal | the ending **ain** is nasal. | | 4 | `américaine` | not nasal | a silent **-e** follows, so the **n** is pronounced. | | 5 | `téléphone` | not nasal | a vowel follows the **n**. | | 6 | `combien` | nasal twice | both **om** and **ien** are nasal. | | 7 | `un` | nasal | a vowel is followed by **n** with nothing after it. | | 8 | `une` | not nasal | a vowel sound follows, so the **n** is pronounced. | items 1/2, 3/4, and 7/8 show why gender can change pronunciation. the feminine form often stops the vowel from being nasal. ## d. elision combine the words correctly. 1. `je` + `ai` 2. `le` + `hôtel` 3. `ne` + `est` + `pas` 4. `de` + `eau` 5. `que` + `elle` 6. `la` + `école` 1. `j'ai` — `je` loses its vowel before `ai`. elision is required, not optional. 2. `l'hôtel` — `h` is silent, so `hôtel` behaves like it begins with a vowel sound. 3. `n'est pas` — `ne` becomes `n'` before `est`; `pas` stays after the verb. 4. `d'eau` — `de` becomes `d'` before `eau`. 5. `qu'elle` — `que` becomes `qu'` before `elle`. 6. `l'école` — `la` becomes `l'` before `école`. the trap is leaving both vowels written out. forms such as **je ai** or **la école** are not slow french; they are wrong spelling. ## e. liaison mark where two words link, and give the **linking sound**. if there is no liaison, say so and explain why. 1. `les amis` 2. `vous avez` 3. `un grand arbre` 4. `mon ami` 5. `un livre et un stylo` 6. `nous allons` | # | phrase | liaison? | linking sound | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `les amis` | yes | final **s** becomes a **z** sound. | | 2 | `vous avez` | yes | final **s** becomes a **z** sound. | | 3 | `un grand arbre` | yes | final **d** becomes a **t** sound. | | 4 | `mon ami` | yes | final **n** links into the vowel. | | 5 | `un livre et un stylo` | no | liaison is never made after `et`; `stylo` also begins with a consonant. | | 6 | `nous allons` | yes | final **s** becomes a **z** sound. | do not wake up every silent consonant. liaison happens in specific environments, and `et` is the first hard no to memorize. ## f. consonant sounds give the sound of the letter or letters in bold type. 1. `ça` — the **ç** 2. `le garçon` — the **ç** 3. `la chaise` — the **ch** 4. `qui` — the **qu** 5. `la montagne` — the **gn** 6. `la maison` — the **s** 7. `le poisson` — the **ss** 8. `je` — the **j** 1. `ça` — the **ç** sounds like **s**; the cedilla forces the soft sound before **a**. 2. `le garçon` — the **ç** again sounds like **s**, this time before **o**. 3. `la chaise` — **ch** sounds like **sh**. 4. `qui` — **qu** sounds like **k** only; the **u** is not a separate sound. 5. `la montagne` — **gn** sounds like **ny**, as in canyon. 6. `la maison` — single **s** between vowels sounds like **z**. 7. `le poisson` — doubled **ss** sounds like **s**. 8. `je` — **j** sounds like the **s** in measure. the important minimal pair is `le poisson` versus `le poison`: doubled **ss** keeps the **s** sound, but one **s** between vowels becomes **z**. ## g. `tu` or `vous`? choose the correct form of address. 1. your closest friend. 2. a waiter you have just met. 3. two children together. 4. your grandmother. 5. a new manager at your workplace. 6. your younger brother. 1. `tu` — one close friend takes the familiar form. 2. `vous` — a waiter you have just met takes polite `vous`. 3. `vous` — any group takes `vous`, even a group of children. 4. `tu` — a family member normally takes `tu`. 5. `vous` — a new manager is a person in authority whom you do not know well. 6. `tu` — a younger brother is family and one person. item 3 is the common trap. `vous` is both formal singular and ordinary plural, so two people always require `vous`. ## h. what would you say? give one appropriate french sentence for each situation. 1. you walk into a bakery at ten in the morning. 2. you want to order a coffee politely. 3. someone has just thanked you. 4. you did not understand what a person said. 5. you want the person to speak more slowly. 6. you are leaving a shop at midday. answers vary, but each response must be polite and appropriate to the situation. 1. `bonjour.` — in a bakery, greet first. starting directly with the order sounds abrupt. 2. `un café, s'il vous plaît.` — the request needs `s'il vous plaît`; `je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.` is also correct. 3. `de rien.` — a standard reply to thanks. `je vous en prie.` is also correct but more formal. 4. `je ne comprends pas.` — this directly says you did not understand. `pardon ?` is also usable. 5. `plus lentement, s'il vous plaît.` — a clear survival phrase for asking someone to slow down. 6. `au revoir, bonne journée !` — a normal daytime farewell in a shop. mark yourself wrong if the french is understandable but socially wrong, such as ordering without `bonjour` or asking a stranger with `tu`. ## i. numbers write each number in french. 1. 3 2. 8 3. 12 4. 15 5. 17 6. 20 1. `trois` — memorize the small numbers as complete sound chunks. 2. `huit` — the final consonant is pronounced when the number stands alone. 3. `douze` — do not confuse it with `deux`; they are different words. 4. `quinze` — the **qu** makes a **k** sound. 5. `dix-sept` — `sept` keeps the **t** sound but not the **p**. 6. `vingt` — the final **t** is silent when the word stands alone. the spelling trap is that numbers do not all pronounce final letters the same way. learn each number aloud, not just visually. ## j. find and fix the error each sentence contains one mistake a beginner commonly makes. rewrite it correctly and name the rule. 1. `je habite à paris.` 2. `je suis vingt ans.` 3. you arrive at a friend's home at 8 p.m. and say: `bonne nuit !` 4. in a café you say: `je veux un café.` 5. you ask a stranger on the street: `tu parles anglais ?` 1. `j'habite à paris.` — `je` must become `j'` before the silent **h** of `habite`. 2. `j'ai vingt ans.` — french uses `avoir` for age, literally to have years. chapter 2 explains the rule fully. 3. `bonsoir !` — `bonne nuit` is for going to bed, not arriving at someone's home in the evening. 4. `je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.` — `je veux` sounds blunt in a café; soften the request and add `s'il vous plaît`. 5. `parlez-vous anglais ?` or `vous parlez anglais ?` — use `vous` with a stranger. the `tu` version is too familiar here. for each correction, name the rule you fixed. a corrected sentence without the rule is only half learned. ## k. production 1. write a five-line self-introduction: name, age, nationality, city, and language level. memorize it and say it aloud without reading. 2. write the exact four lines you would say in a bakery: greeting, request, thanks, farewell. 3. choose five phrases from `survival-phrases.md` that you personally will need this week. write them out and use each one aloud today. these are productive tasks, so answers vary. use these models and criteria to check yourself. **self-introduction model:** 1. `bonjour.` 2. `je m'appelle ana.` 3. `j'ai dix-neuf ans.` 4. `je suis canadienne et j'habite à montréal.` 5. `je parle un peu français.` a correct self-introduction includes name, age with `j'ai`, nationality with agreement, city with `j'habite à`, and language level. **bakery script model:** 1. `bonjour.` 2. `un croissant, s'il vous plaît.` 3. `merci beaucoup.` 4. `au revoir, bonne journée !` it is wrong if it does not open with `bonjour` or if the request omits `s'il vous plaît`. for your five survival phrases, answers vary. choose phrases you can actually use this week, copy accents and apostrophes exactly, and say each one aloud until it is a usable chunk rather than a word-by-word translation. ## l. dictation and oral recall 1. **dictation.** use text-to-speech on the website to hear six sentences from the \"sentence usage practice\" sections of `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in two passes: first the sounds you missed, then the silent letters and accents you left out. 2. **read aloud.** read the \"connected reading: mon premier jour\" passage aloud twice. record the second attempt and check it for even syllable rhythm. 3. **oral recall.** explain aloud, in simple english, and then give one french example from memory: - which final consonants are usually pronounced; - what makes a vowel nasal; - the difference between elision and liaison; - when to use `vous` instead of `tu`. these tasks do not have one hidden script, but they do have clear standards. **dictation:** score each sentence in three passes: words, accents, and silent letters you wrote but did not pronounce. the silent-letter pass matters because french spelling often records information your ear cannot hear. **read aloud:** judge rhythm first. even syllable rhythm is more important at this stage than a perfect french `r`. **oral recall model answers:** - **final consonants:** most are silent, but **c**, **r**, **f**, and **l** are often pronounced. the infinitive ending **-er** is a common exception. example: `avec`. - **nasal vowels:** a vowel followed by **n** or **m** is nasal when no vowel follows and the consonant is not doubled. example: `bon`. - **elision versus liaison:** elision removes a vowel and writes an apostrophe, as in `j'ai`; liaison wakes up a silent final consonant before a vowel sound, as in `les amis`. - **`vous`:** use it with strangers, older people, people in authority, and any group. example: `comment allez-vous ?` if you can state the rule but cannot give a french example, redo the recall tomorrow. production is the goal. "},{"id":"chapter-00-getting-started/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 0 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-0-answer-key\">Chapter 0 Answer Key</h1>\n<p>Correct by <strong>rule</strong>, not only by answer. For every mistake, write down the rule you broke. That sentence is worth more than the correction itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-silent-letters\">Section A: Silent letters</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Pronounced?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Most final consonants are silent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> is in CaReFuL.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonjour</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code> is in CaReFuL.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> is silent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f</code> is in CaReFuL.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> infinitive ending is a standard exception to CaReFuL.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avril</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l</code> is in CaReFuL.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d</code> is silent; the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">an</code> is nasal.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-b-u-versus-ou\">Section B: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code></h2>\n<p>The \"oo\" sound is in the second word each time: 1. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> 2. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">roue</code> 3. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> 4. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sourd</code></p>\n<p>The first word in each pair uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code>, the rounded-lip sound that does not exist in English. If you cannot yet hear a difference, return to Unit 3 and practice the pair slowly ten times before continuing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-c-nasal-vowels\">Section C: Nasal vowels</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code></td>\n<td>Nasal</td>\n<td>Vowel + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> with nothing after it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code></td>\n<td>Not nasal</td>\n<td>The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> is doubled, so it is pronounced as a consonant.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américain</code></td>\n<td>Nasal</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ain</code> at the end of the word.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américaine</code></td>\n<td>Not nasal</td>\n<td>A silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> follows, so the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> is pronounced.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code></td>\n<td>Not nasal</td>\n<td>A vowel follows the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien</code></td>\n<td>Nasal (twice)</td>\n<td>Both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">om</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ien</code> are nasal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></td>\n<td>Nasal</td>\n<td>Vowel + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> with nothing after it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></td>\n<td>Not nasal</td>\n<td>A vowel sound follows, so the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> is pronounced.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Pairs 1/2, 3/4, and 7/8 are the pronunciation side of gender agreement. Being able to hear them will make Chapter 1 noticeably easier.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-d-elision\">Section D: Elision</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hôtel</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code> is silent, so the word counts as beginning with a vowel)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'est pas</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'eau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'école</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Elision is required, not optional. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ai</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le hôtel</code> are always wrong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-e-liaison\">Section E: Liaison</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>Liaison</th>\n<th>Linking sound</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> becomes \"z\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> becomes \"z\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un grand arbre</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d</code> becomes \"t\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon ami</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> links to the vowel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un livre et un stylo</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Liaison is <strong>never</strong> made after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> becomes \"z\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>In item 5, note also that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un stylo</code> has no liaison because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">stylo</code> begins with a consonant.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-f-consonant-sounds\">Section F: Consonant sounds</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code> — \"s\" (the cedilla forces the soft sound before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le garçon</code> — \"s\" (same rule, before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code>)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la chaise</code> — \"sh\"</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> — \"k\" only; the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> is not pronounced</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la montagne</code> — \"ny,\" as in <em>canyon</em></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code> — \"z,\" because the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> sits between two vowels</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poisson</code> — \"s,\" because the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> is doubled</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> — \"zh,\" as in <em>measure</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Items 6 and 7 are a genuine minimal pair: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poisson</code> is the fish, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le poison</code> is poison.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-g-tu-or-vous\">Section G: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>?</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> — a close friend</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — a stranger in a service role</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — <strong>any</strong> group, even of children</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> — a family member</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — someone in authority you do not know well</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> — a family member</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Item 3 is the one most learners miss. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> is both the formal singular <strong>and</strong> the ordinary plural. Two people always take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-h-what-would-you-say\">Section H: What would you say?</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. Any of these is correct:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> — required before anything else. Saying only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un croissant, s'il vous plaît</code> sounds rude.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un café, s'il vous plaît.</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De rien.</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vous en prie.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne comprends pas.</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pardon ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Plus lentement, s'il vous plaît.</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pouvez-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au revoir, bonne journée !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>An answer is correct if it is understandable <strong>and</strong> appropriately polite for the situation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-i-numbers\">Section I: Numbers</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">douze</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quinze</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-sept</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check the pronunciation traps: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sept</code> has a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">p</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt</code> has a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t</code> when it stands alone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-j-find-and-fix-the-error\">Section J: Find and fix the error</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Correction</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite à Paris.</code></td>\n<td>Elision is required. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code> is silent, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code></td>\n<td>French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> (to have) for age, never <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>. Taught fully in Chapter 2.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonsoir !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne nuit</code> is used only when someone is going to bed, not on arrival.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux</code> sounds blunt. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je voudrais</code> for requests, and add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous anglais ?</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous parlez anglais ?</code></td>\n<td>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> with a stranger. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> here sounds rude.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"sections-k-l-production-dictation-and-recall\">Sections K-L: Production, dictation, and recall</h2>\n<p>These have no fixed answers. Score them against the following standards.</p>\n<h3 id=\"self-introduction\">Self-introduction</h3>\n<p>A correct introduction includes all five elements and uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'appelle</code> for the name;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai ___ ans</code> for the age, with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>;</li>\n<li>a nationality adjective with the correct ending for your gender (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code>);</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite à</code> + the city;</li>\n<li>a language statement such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle un peu français.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"bakery-script\">Bakery script</h3>\n<p>It counts as correct only if it <strong>opens with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code></strong> and <strong>includes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code></strong> in the request. These two habits matter more than perfect grammar.</p>\n<h3 id=\"dictation\">Dictation</h3>\n<p>Score each sentence out of three points:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>one point for the words;</li>\n<li>one point for the accents;</li>\n<li>one point for the silent letters you correctly wrote but did not pronounce.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Most beginners lose points on point 3 first. That is expected, and it is exactly what this chapter is training.</p>\n<h3 id=\"read-aloud\">Read aloud</h3>\n<p>Judge only one thing on the first pass: <strong>even syllable rhythm</strong>. A learner with imperfect individual sounds but even rhythm is far easier to understand than the reverse.</p>\n<h3 id=\"oral-recall\">Oral recall</h3>\n<p>Model answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Final consonants:</strong> usually silent, except <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">r</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l</code> (CaReFuL), with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> infinitives as a common exception.</li>\n<li><strong>Nasal vowels:</strong> a vowel followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code>, with no vowel after it and no doubled consonant; the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code> itself is not pronounced.</li>\n<li><strong>Elision vs liaison:</strong> elision <em>removes</em> a vowel and adds an apostrophe (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai</code>); liaison <em>adds</em> a sound by waking up a silent final consonant (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code>).</li>\n<li><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>:</strong> with strangers, older people, anyone in authority, and always with a group.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Sections A-J contain 63 scorable items. Count one point each.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Score</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>85-100%</td>\n<td>Move to Chapter 1 and keep reviewing this chapter's sounds daily.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>70-84%</td>\n<td>Redo only the sections you missed, then retry the next day.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Below 70%</td>\n<td>Repeat the chapter in two shorter sessions, one on sounds (Units 1-6) and one on phrases (Units 7-8).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Remember the standard used throughout this course: a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a <strong>different</strong> correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 0 answer key correct by **rule**, not only by answer. for every mistake, write down the rule you broke. that sentence is worth more than the correction itself. ## section a: silent letters | # | word | pronounced? | why | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `petit` | no | most final consonants are silent. | | 2 | `avec` | yes | `c` is in careful. | | 3 | `bonjour` | yes | `r` is in careful. | | 4 | `vous` | no | final `s` is silent. | | 5 | `neuf` | yes | `f` is in careful. | | 6 | `parler` | no | the `-er` infinitive ending is a standard exception to careful. | | 7 | `avril` | yes | `l` is in careful. | | 8 | `grand` | no | final `d` is silent; the `an` is nasal. | ## section b: `u` versus `ou` the \"oo\" sound is in the second word each time: 1. `tout` 2. `roue` 3. `vous` 4. `sourd` the first word in each pair uses `u`, the rounded-lip sound that does not exist in english. if you cannot yet hear a difference, return to unit 3 and practice the pair slowly ten times before continuing. ## section c: nasal vowels | # | word | answer | why | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `bon` | nasal | vowel + `n` with nothing after it. | | 2 | `bonne` | not nasal | the `n` is doubled, so it is pronounced as a consonant. | | 3 | `américain` | nasal | `ain` at the end of the word. | | 4 | `américaine` | not nasal | a silent `-e` follows, so the `n` is pronounced. | | 5 | `téléphone` | not nasal | a vowel follows the `n`. | | 6 | `combien` | nasal (twice) | both `om` and `ien` are nasal. | | 7 | `un` | nasal | vowel + `n` with nothing after it. | | 8 | `une` | not nasal | a vowel sound follows, so the `n` is pronounced. | pairs 1/2, 3/4, and 7/8 are the pronunciation side of gender agreement. being able to hear them will make chapter 1 noticeably easier. ## section d: elision 1. `j'ai` 2. `l'hôtel` (`h` is silent, so the word counts as beginning with a vowel) 3. `n'est pas` 4. `d'eau` 5. `qu'elle` 6. `l'école` elision is required, not optional. `je ai` and `le hôtel` are always wrong. ## section e: liaison | # | phrase | liaison | linking sound | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `les amis` | yes | `s` becomes \"z\" | | 2 | `vous avez` | yes | `s` becomes \"z\" | | 3 | `un grand arbre` | yes | `d` becomes \"t\" | | 4 | `mon ami` | yes | `n` links to the vowel | | 5 | `un livre et un stylo` | no | liaison is **never** made after `et`. | | 6 | `nous allons` | yes | `s` becomes \"z\" | in item 5, note also that `un stylo` has no liaison because `stylo` begins with a consonant. ## section f: consonant sounds 1. `ça` — \"s\" (the cedilla forces the soft sound before `a`) 2. `le garçon` — \"s\" (same rule, before `o`) 3. `la chaise` — \"sh\" 4. `qui` — \"k\" only; the `u` is not pronounced 5. `la montagne` — \"ny,\" as in *canyon* 6. `la maison` — \"z,\" because the `s` sits between two vowels 7. `le poisson` — \"s,\" because the `s` is doubled 8. `je` — \"zh,\" as in *measure* items 6 and 7 are a genuine minimal pair: `le poisson` is the fish, `le poison` is poison. ## section g: `tu` or `vous`? 1. `tu` — a close friend 2. `vous` — a stranger in a service role 3. `vous` — **any** group, even of children 4. `tu` — a family member 5. `vous` — someone in authority you do not know well 6. `tu` — a family member item 3 is the one most learners miss. `vous` is both the formal singular **and** the ordinary plural. two people always take `vous`. ## section h: what would you say? answers vary. any of these is correct: 1. `bonjour` — required before anything else. saying only `un croissant, s'il vous plaît` sounds rude. 2. `un café, s'il vous plaît.` or `je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.` 3. `de rien.` or `je vous en prie.` 4. `je ne comprends pas.` or `pardon ?` 5. `plus lentement, s'il vous plaît.` or `pouvez-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ?` 6. `au revoir, bonne journée !` an answer is correct if it is understandable **and** appropriately polite for the situation. ## section i: numbers 1. `trois` 2. `huit` 3. `douze` 4. `quinze` 5. `dix-sept` 6. `vingt` check the pronunciation traps: `sept` has a silent `p`, and `vingt` has a silent `t` when it stands alone. ## section j: find and fix the error | # | correction | rule | |---|---|---| | 1 | `j'habite à paris.` | elision is required. `h` is silent, so `je` becomes `j'`. | | 2 | `j'ai vingt ans.` | french uses `avoir` (to have) for age, never `être`. taught fully in chapter 2. | | 3 | `bonsoir !` | `bonne nuit` is used only when someone is going to bed, not on arrival. | | 4 | `je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.` | `je veux` sounds blunt. use `je voudrais` for requests, and add `s'il vous plaît`. | | 5 | `parlez-vous anglais ?` or `vous parlez anglais ?` | use `vous` with a stranger. `tu` here sounds rude. | ## sections k-l: production, dictation, and recall these have no fixed answers. score them against the following standards. ### self-introduction a correct introduction includes all five elements and uses: - `je m'appelle` for the name; - `j'ai ___ ans` for the age, with `avoir`, not `être`; - a nationality adjective with the correct ending for your gender (`canadien` / `canadienne`); - `j'habite à` + the city; - a language statement such as `je parle un peu français.` ### bakery script it counts as correct only if it **opens with `bonjour`** and **includes `s'il vous plaît`** in the request. these two habits matter more than perfect grammar. ### dictation score each sentence out of three points: 1. one point for the words; 2. one point for the accents; 3. one point for the silent letters you correctly wrote but did not pronounce. most beginners lose points on point 3 first. that is expected, and it is exactly what this chapter is training. ### read aloud judge only one thing on the first pass: **even syllable rhythm**. a learner with imperfect individual sounds but even rhythm is far easier to understand than the reverse. ### oral recall model answers: - **final consonants:** usually silent, except `c`, `r`, `f`, `l` (careful), with `-er` infinitives as a common exception. - **nasal vowels:** a vowel followed by `n` or `m`, with no vowel after it and no doubled consonant; the `n` itself is not pronounced. - **elision vs liaison:** elision *removes* a vowel and adds an apostrophe (`j'ai`); liaison *adds* a sound by waking up a silent final consonant (`les amis`). - **`vous`:** with strangers, older people, anyone in authority, and always with a group. ## self-scoring sections a-j contain 63 scorable items. count one point each. | score | what to do | |---|---| | 85-100% | move to chapter 1 and keep reviewing this chapter's sounds daily. | | 70-84% | redo only the sections you missed, then retry the next day. | | below 70% | repeat the chapter in two shorter sessions, one on sounds (units 1-6) and one on phrases (units 7-8). | remember the standard used throughout this course: a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a **different** correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-00-getting-started/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 0 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-0-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 0 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<p>Pronunciation improves through <strong>short daily contact</strong>, not long single sessions. Ten focused minutes a day beats one two-hour session every week.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Unit 1 (alphabet and accents)</strong> and <strong>Unit 2 (silent letters)</strong>. Spell your own name aloud in French. Say the pairs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code> ten times each.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes: name the five accents and the CaReFuL letters. Then study <strong>Unit 3 (vowel sounds)</strong>. Spend five full minutes on the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> minimal pairs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Unit 4 (nasal vowels)</strong>. Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> aloud five times each. Complete Sections A-C of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Unit 5 (consonants)</strong> and <strong>Unit 6 (elision, liaison, rhythm)</strong>. Complete Sections D-F. Read the \"mon premier jour\" passage aloud once, slowly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Unit 7 (greetings and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>)</strong>. Complete Sections G-H and J. Memorize the two conversations in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">survival-phrases.md</code>, section 12.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Unit 8 (numbers and introductions)</strong>. Complete Section I. Write and memorize your five-line self-introduction, then record it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Closed-book review:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>redo Sections A-J with no notes and score yourself;</li>\n<li>record the full final production assignment from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>;</li>\n<li>say your self-introduction and the bakery script from memory;</li>\n<li>review only the items you missed.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>read five unfamiliar French sentences aloud from a later chapter;</li>\n<li>say three <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">u</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ou</code> minimal pairs;</li>\n<li>say three masculine/feminine pairs with a nasal contrast;</li>\n<li>give your self-introduction from memory;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"daily-background-habit\">Daily background habit</h2>\n<p>While you work through Chapters 1-16, keep one two-minute habit:</p>\n<blockquote>Read any three French example sentences from the current chapter <strong>aloud</strong>, then tap to hear them and compare.</blockquote>\n<p>This is the single highest-value routine in the whole course. Pronunciation decays without use, and every later chapter gives you fresh material to practice on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"readiness-test-for-chapter-1\">Readiness test for Chapter 1</h2>\n<p>You are ready to move on when you can do all five of these:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Standard</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cold reading</td>\n<td>Read a French sentence you have never seen aloud without freezing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimal pair</td>\n<td>Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> so a listener could tell them apart.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gender by ear</td>\n<td>Hear the difference between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elision</td>\n<td>Correctly join <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">école</code> without thinking.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social French</td>\n<td>Greet, introduce yourself, and order something from memory.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>If you fail one, repeat only the matching unit. Do not repeat the whole chapter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-carry-into-chapter-1\">What to carry into Chapter 1</h2>\n<p>Keep these three things active from now on:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The habit of reading aloud.</strong> Never read a French example silently.</li>\n<li><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">glossary.md</code>.</strong> Look up every grammar term the moment you meet it.</li>\n<li><strong>The politeness reflex.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour</code> first, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'il vous plaît</code> with every request, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> when in doubt.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 0 seven-day review plan pronunciation improves through **short daily contact**, not long single sessions. ten focused minutes a day beats one two-hour session every week. ## day 1 study **unit 1 (alphabet and accents)** and **unit 2 (silent letters)**. spell your own name aloud in french. say the pairs `petit`/`petite` and `grand`/`grande` ten times each. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes: name the five accents and the careful letters. then study **unit 3 (vowel sounds)**. spend five full minutes on the `u` versus `ou` minimal pairs. ## day 3 study **unit 4 (nasal vowels)**. say `bon`/`bonne`, `un`/`une`, and `canadien`/`canadienne` aloud five times each. complete sections a-c of `exercises.md`. ## day 4 study **unit 5 (consonants)** and **unit 6 (elision, liaison, rhythm)**. complete sections d-f. read the \"mon premier jour\" passage aloud once, slowly. ## day 5 study **unit 7 (greetings and `tu`/`vous`)**. complete sections g-h and j. memorize the two conversations in `survival-phrases.md`, section 12. ## day 6 study **unit 8 (numbers and introductions)**. complete section i. write and memorize your five-line self-introduction, then record it. ## day 7 closed-book review: 1. redo sections a-j with no notes and score yourself; 2. record the full final production assignment from `lessons.md`; 3. say your self-introduction and the bakery script from memory; 4. review only the items you missed. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. read five unfamiliar french sentences aloud from a later chapter; 2. say three `u`/`ou` minimal pairs; 3. say three masculine/feminine pairs with a nasal contrast; 4. give your self-introduction from memory; 5. review only errors. ## daily background habit while you work through chapters 1-16, keep one two-minute habit: > read any three french example sentences from the current chapter **aloud**, then tap to hear them and compare. this is the single highest-value routine in the whole course. pronunciation decays without use, and every later chapter gives you fresh material to practice on. ## readiness test for chapter 1 you are ready to move on when you can do all five of these: | check | standard | |---|---| | cold reading | read a french sentence you have never seen aloud without freezing. | | minimal pair | say `tu` and `tout` so a listener could tell them apart. | | gender by ear | hear the difference between `bon` and `bonne`. | | elision | correctly join `je` + `ai` and `le` + `école` without thinking. | | social french | greet, introduce yourself, and order something from memory. | if you fail one, repeat only the matching unit. do not repeat the whole chapter. ## what to carry into chapter 1 keep these three things active from now on: 1. **the habit of reading aloud.** never read a french example silently. 2. **`glossary.md`.** look up every grammar term the moment you meet it. 3. **the politeness reflex.** `bonjour` first, `s'il vous plaît` with every request, `vous` when in doubt. "},{"id":"chapter-00-getting-started/glossary.md","title":"Grammar Glossary: Every Term This Course Uses","type":"Reference","html":"<h1 id=\"grammar-glossary-every-term-this-course-uses\">Grammar Glossary: Every Term This Course Uses</h1>\n<p>Grammar words are often the real obstacle for a beginner. A lesson may be perfectly clear except for one unexplained term such as <em>infinitive</em> or <em>partitive</em>.</p>\n<p>This page defines every grammar term used anywhere in Chapters 0-16, in plain English, with a French example and the chapter where you will study it properly. Keep it open beside the lessons.</p>\n<p>You do <strong>not</strong> need to read this page from start to finish. Look up a term when you meet it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-read-the-tables\">How to read the tables</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Chapter</strong> column tells you where the term is actually taught. If a term appears early but is taught later, you can safely treat it as a fixed phrase until you reach that chapter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-words-and-word-types\">1. Words and word types</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain-English meaning</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Noun</strong></td>\n<td>A word for a person, place, thing, animal, or idea.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le jardin</code> — the garden</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Article</strong></td>\n<td>The small word before a noun: \"the,\" \"a,\" \"some.\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Definite article</strong></td>\n<td>\"The.\" Points to something specific or a whole category.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le café</code> — the coffee</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indefinite article</strong></td>\n<td>\"A,\" \"an,\" or \"some.\" Points to an unspecified one.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un café</code> — a coffee</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Partitive article</strong></td>\n<td>\"Some\" of something you cannot count.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du café</code> — some coffee</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Adjective</strong></td>\n<td>A word describing a noun.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> — small</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Adverb</strong></td>\n<td>A word describing a verb, adjective, or other adverb. Often answers \"how?\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lentement</code> — slowly</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Verb</strong></td>\n<td>A word for an action or a state.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> — to speak</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Preposition</strong></td>\n<td>A small linking word of place, time, or relation.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code></td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Conjunction</strong></td>\n<td>A word joining two parts of a sentence.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code> — and; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mais</code> — but</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pronoun</strong></td>\n<td>A word replacing a noun so you don't repeat it.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code></td>\n<td>2, 9</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"2-gender-number-and-agreement\">2. Gender, number, and agreement</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain-English meaning</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gender</strong></td>\n<td>Every French noun is grammatically masculine or feminine. This is not about biology; a table is feminine.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> (m.), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> (f.)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Masculine / Feminine</strong></td>\n<td>The two genders. Learn the gender <strong>with</strong> the noun, never alone.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un problème</code> (m.)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Number</strong></td>\n<td>Whether a word is singular (one) or plural (more than one).</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Singular / Plural</strong></td>\n<td>One / more than one.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une porte</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des portes</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Agreement</strong></td>\n<td>Changing an article, adjective, or participle so it matches its noun's gender and number.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Invariable</strong></td>\n<td>A word that never changes form.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> — there is/are</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"3-sentence-parts\">3. Sentence parts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain-English meaning</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Subject</strong></td>\n<td>Who or what performs the verb.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie parle.</code> — Marie speaks.</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Object</strong></td>\n<td>Who or what receives the action.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois **le chien**.</code></td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Direct object</strong></td>\n<td>The thing acted on directly, with no preposition. Answers \"what?\" or \"whom?\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lis **le livre**.</code> — I read the book.</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indirect object</strong></td>\n<td>The person the action goes <em>to</em>, usually marked by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle **à Marie**.</code> — I speak to Marie.</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Clause</strong></td>\n<td>A group of words containing its own verb.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand j'arrive</code> — when I arrive</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Main clause</strong></td>\n<td>The clause that can stand alone as a sentence.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je mange</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je mange quand j'arrive.</code></td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Subordinate clause</strong></td>\n<td>A clause that depends on the main clause.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand j'arrive</code></td>\n<td>8, 16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Antecedent</strong></td>\n<td>The noun that a relative pronoun refers back to.</td>\n<td>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre que je lis</code>, the antecedent is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code>.</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"4-pronouns\">4. Pronouns</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain-English meaning</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Subject pronoun</strong></td>\n<td>Replaces the subject noun.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je, tu, il, elle, on, nous, vous, ils, elles</code></td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Direct object pronoun</strong></td>\n<td>Replaces a direct object; goes <strong>before</strong> the verb.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je **le** vois.</code> — I see him/it.</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indirect object pronoun</strong></td>\n<td>Replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> + a person.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je **lui** parle.</code> — I speak to him/her.</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stressed (disjunctive) pronoun</strong></td>\n<td>Stands alone or follows a preposition.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec **moi**</code> — with me</td>\n<td>11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reflexive pronoun</strong></td>\n<td>Shows the action returns to the subject.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je **me** lave.</code> — I wash myself.</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Relative pronoun</strong></td>\n<td>Joins two clauses: \"who,\" \"which,\" \"that.\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code></td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Demonstrative</strong></td>\n<td>\"This,\" \"that,\" \"these,\" \"those.\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce livre</code> — this book</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Possessive</strong></td>\n<td>Shows ownership: \"my,\" \"your,\" \"his.\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon livre</code> — my book</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indefinite pronoun/adjective</strong></td>\n<td>Vague quantity words: \"someone,\" \"each,\" \"several.\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelqu'un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs</code></td>\n<td>15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></strong></td>\n<td>Replaces a place or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> + a thing.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'**y** vais.</code> — I'm going there.</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></strong></td>\n<td>Replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + a thing, or a quantity.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'**en** veux.</code> — I want some.</td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"5-verbs-the-basics\">5. Verbs: the basics</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain-English meaning</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Infinitive</strong></td>\n<td>The unchanged \"dictionary\" form of a verb: \"to speak.\" French infinitives end in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code>.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Conjugate</strong></td>\n<td>To change a verb's ending to match its subject.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stem (radical)</strong></td>\n<td>What is left after removing the infinitive ending. It carries the meaning.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> → stem <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parl-</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ending</strong></td>\n<td>The part added to the stem to show who and when.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parl</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Regular verb</strong></td>\n<td>A verb following a predictable pattern for its group.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code></td>\n<td>3, 4, 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Irregular verb</strong></td>\n<td>A verb with forms you must memorize individually.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code></td>\n<td>2, 6, 7, 8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Auxiliary (helping verb)</strong></td>\n<td>A verb used with a participle to build a compound tense. French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'**ai** parlé</code> — I spoke</td>\n<td>13</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Participle</strong></td>\n<td>A verb form used with an auxiliary or as a describing word.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code> (past), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code> (present)</td>\n<td>12, 13</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Past participle</strong></td>\n<td>The form used after an auxiliary to build a past tense.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fini</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendu</code></td>\n<td>13</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Present participle</strong></td>\n<td>The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code> form, close to English \"-ing.\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en parlant</code> — while speaking</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pronominal (reflexive) verb</strong></td>\n<td>A verb used with a reflexive pronoun.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lever</code> — to get up</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"6-verbs-tense-and-mood\">6. Verbs: tense and mood</h2>\n<p><strong>Tense</strong> tells you <em>when</em>. <strong>Mood</strong> tells you <em>how the speaker views</em> the action: as a fact, a command, a wish, or a hypothesis.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain-English meaning</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tense</strong></td>\n<td>The time of the action: past, present, or future.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle</code> (present)</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mood</strong></td>\n<td>The speaker's attitude: statement, command, doubt, hypothesis.</td>\n<td>indicative, imperative, subjunctive</td>\n<td>11, 16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Present tense</strong></td>\n<td>Happening now, or generally true.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle</code> — I speak / I am speaking</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Near future (futur proche)</strong></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> + infinitive. Like \"going to.\"</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais parler</code> — I'm going to speak</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Passé composé</strong></td>\n<td>The main past tense for completed events.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai parlé</code> — I spoke</td>\n<td>13</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Imparfait</strong></td>\n<td>A past tense for ongoing states, habits, and background.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code> — I used to speak</td>\n<td>14</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pluperfect (plus-que-parfait)</strong></td>\n<td>A past <em>before</em> another past.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'avais parlé</code> — I had spoken</td>\n<td>15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Simple future (futur simple)</strong></td>\n<td>A one-word future tense.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlerai</code> — I will speak</td>\n<td>15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Conditional</strong></td>\n<td>\"Would.\" For hypotheses and polite requests.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlerais</code> — I would speak</td>\n<td>15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Imperative</strong></td>\n<td>A command or instruction.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle !</code> — Speak!</td>\n<td>11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Subjunctive</strong></td>\n<td>A mood for wish, doubt, emotion, and necessity, after certain triggers.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code></td>\n<td>16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indicative</strong></td>\n<td>The ordinary \"fact-stating\" mood: all normal tenses.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Do not be intimidated by this table. In everyday French, the present, the passé composé, the imparfait, and the near future cover the overwhelming majority of what you will say.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-sentence-operations\">7. Sentence operations</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain-English meaning</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Negation</strong></td>\n<td>Making a sentence mean \"not.\" French wraps the verb in two words.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je **ne** parle **pas**.</code></td>\n<td>2, 11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interrogative</strong></td>\n<td>Relating to questions.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où ?</code> — Where?</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Question word</strong></td>\n<td>Who, what, where, when, why, how.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui, que, où, quand, pourquoi, comment</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inversion</strong></td>\n<td>Flipping the verb and subject to form a question.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous ?</code> — Do you speak?</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code></strong></td>\n<td>A fixed phrase placed in front of a statement to turn it into a question.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu parles ?</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Comparative</strong></td>\n<td>Comparing two things: more, less, as.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus grand que</code> — bigger than</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Superlative</strong></td>\n<td>The extreme of a group: the most, the least.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le plus grand</code> — the biggest</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Register</strong></td>\n<td>How formal the language is.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> (informal) vs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> (formal)</td>\n<td>0, 2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"8-sounds-and-spelling\">8. Sounds and spelling</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain-English meaning</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vowel</strong></td>\n<td>The letters <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a, e, i, o, u, y</code> and their sounds.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Consonant</strong></td>\n<td>Any letter that is not a vowel.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">p</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Syllable</strong></td>\n<td>One beat of a word.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon-jour</code> = 2 syllables</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nasal vowel</strong></td>\n<td>A vowel sent through the nose, written vowel + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m</code>.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vin</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Elision</strong></td>\n<td>Dropping a final vowel before a vowel sound, marked by an apostrophe.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai</code></td>\n<td>0, 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Liaison</strong></td>\n<td>Pronouncing a normally silent final consonant because the next word starts with a vowel.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code> sounds \"lay-z-ami\"</td>\n<td>0, 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Silent letter</strong></td>\n<td>A written letter you do not pronounce.</td>\n<td>the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Accent mark</strong></td>\n<td>A symbol changing a letter's sound or distinguishing two words.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">é</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">è</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ê</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ç</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ë</code></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cognate</strong></td>\n<td>A word that looks like its English equivalent.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>False friend</strong></td>\n<td>A word that <em>looks</em> like an English word but means something else.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actuellement</code> means \"currently,\" not \"actually\"</td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"9-study-terms-used-in-this-course\">9. Study terms used in this course</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>What it means here</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Retrieval check</strong></td>\n<td>A short quiz you answer <strong>without looking</strong>. Struggling to recall is what builds memory.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Production task</strong></td>\n<td>Creating your own sentence, not copying one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transformation</strong></td>\n<td>Rewriting a sentence with one change: subject, tense, number, or polarity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Polarity</strong></td>\n<td>Whether a sentence is affirmative or negative.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dictation</strong></td>\n<td>Writing down French you hear, without seeing it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spaced review</strong></td>\n<td>Revisiting material after 1, 3, 7, and 30 days rather than all at once.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Closed-book</strong></td>\n<td>Working with no notes visible. The only score that measures real mastery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fixed chunk</strong></td>\n<td>A phrase memorized whole, before you understand its grammar.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Minimal pair</strong></td>\n<td>Two words differing by one sound, used to train your ear. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-ten-terms-that-matter-most-at-the-start\">The ten terms that matter most at the start</h2>\n<p>If this page feels overwhelming, learn only these before Chapter 1:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Noun</strong> — a person, place, or thing.</li>\n<li><strong>Gender</strong> — every noun is masculine or feminine.</li>\n<li><strong>Article</strong> — the small word before a noun.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjective</strong> — a describing word.</li>\n<li><strong>Agreement</strong> — making words match in gender and number.</li>\n<li><strong>Verb</strong> — an action or state word.</li>\n<li><strong>Infinitive</strong> — the \"to do\" dictionary form.</li>\n<li><strong>Conjugate</strong> — change the verb ending to fit the subject.</li>\n<li><strong>Subject</strong> — who does the action.</li>\n<li><strong>Elision</strong> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Everything else can wait until the chapter that teaches it.</p>","search":"# grammar glossary: every term this course uses grammar words are often the real obstacle for a beginner. a lesson may be perfectly clear except for one unexplained term such as *infinitive* or *partitive*. this page defines every grammar term used anywhere in chapters 0-16, in plain english, with a french example and the chapter where you will study it properly. keep it open beside the lessons. you do **not** need to read this page from start to finish. look up a term when you meet it. ## how to read the tables the **chapter** column tells you where the term is actually taught. if a term appears early but is taught later, you can safely treat it as a fixed phrase until you reach that chapter. ## 1. words and word types | term | plain-english meaning | french example | chapter | |---|---|---|---:| | **noun** | a word for a person, place, thing, animal, or idea. | `le jardin` — the garden | 1 | | **article** | the small word before a noun: \"the,\" \"a,\" \"some.\" | `le`, `une`, `des` | 1 | | **definite article** | \"the.\" points to something specific or a whole category. | `le café` — the coffee | 1 | | **indefinite article** | \"a,\" \"an,\" or \"some.\" points to an unspecified one. | `un café` — a coffee | 1 | | **partitive article** | \"some\" of something you cannot count. | `du café` — some coffee | 9 | | **adjective** | a word describing a noun. | `petit` — small | 1 | | **adverb** | a word describing a verb, adjective, or other adverb. often answers \"how?\" | `lentement` — slowly | 10 | | **verb** | a word for an action or a state. | `parler` — to speak | 3 | | **preposition** | a small linking word of place, time, or relation. | `à`, `de`, `dans`, `sur` | 6 | | **conjunction** | a word joining two parts of a sentence. | `et` — and; `mais` — but | 3 | | **pronoun** | a word replacing a noun so you don't repeat it. | `il`, `le`, `lui` | 2, 9 | ## 2. gender, number, and agreement | term | plain-english meaning | french example | chapter | |---|---|---|---:| | **gender** | every french noun is grammatically masculine or feminine. this is not about biology; a table is feminine. | `le livre` (m.), `la table` (f.) | 1 | | **masculine / feminine** | the two genders. learn the gender **with** the noun, never alone. | `un problème` (m.) | 1 | | **number** | whether a word is singular (one) or plural (more than one). | `le livre` / `les livres` | 1 | | **singular / plural** | one / more than one. | `une porte` / `des portes` | 1 | | **agreement** | changing an article, adjective, or participle so it matches its noun's gender and number. | `une petite maison` | 1 | | **invariable** | a word that never changes form. | `il y a` — there is/are | 2 | ## 3. sentence parts | term | plain-english meaning | french example | chapter | |---|---|---|---:| | **subject** | who or what performs the verb. | `marie parle.` — marie speaks. | 2 | | **object** | who or what receives the action. | `je vois **le chien**.` | 9 | | **direct object** | the thing acted on directly, with no preposition. answers \"what?\" or \"whom?\" | `je lis **le livre**.` — i read the book. | 9 | | **indirect object** | the person the action goes *to*, usually marked by `à`. | `je parle **à marie**.` — i speak to marie. | 9 | | **clause** | a group of words containing its own verb. | `quand j'arrive` — when i arrive | 8 | | **main clause** | the clause that can stand alone as a sentence. | `je mange` in `je mange quand j'arrive.` | 8 | | **subordinate clause** | a clause that depends on the main clause. | `quand j'arrive` | 8, 16 | | **antecedent** | the noun that a relative pronoun refers back to. | in `le livre que je lis`, the antecedent is `le livre`. | 8 | ## 4. pronouns | term | plain-english meaning | french example | chapter | |---|---|---|---:| | **subject pronoun** | replaces the subject noun. | `je, tu, il, elle, on, nous, vous, ils, elles` | 2 | | **direct object pronoun** | replaces a direct object; goes **before** the verb. | `je **le** vois.` — i see him/it. | 9 | | **indirect object pronoun** | replaces `à` + a person. | `je **lui** parle.` — i speak to him/her. | 9 | | **stressed (disjunctive) pronoun** | stands alone or follows a preposition. | `avec **moi**` — with me | 11 | | **reflexive pronoun** | shows the action returns to the subject. | `je **me** lave.` — i wash myself. | 12 | | **relative pronoun** | joins two clauses: \"who,\" \"which,\" \"that.\" | `qui`, `que`, `où`, `dont` | 8 | | **demonstrative** | \"this,\" \"that,\" \"these,\" \"those.\" | `ce livre` — this book | 10 | | **possessive** | shows ownership: \"my,\" \"your,\" \"his.\" | `mon livre` — my book | 10 | | **indefinite pronoun/adjective** | vague quantity words: \"someone,\" \"each,\" \"several.\" | `quelqu'un`, `chaque`, `plusieurs` | 15 | | **the pronoun `y`** | replaces a place or `à` + a thing. | `j'**y** vais.` — i'm going there. | 9 | | **the pronoun `en`** | replaces `de` + a thing, or a quantity. | `j'**en** veux.` — i want some. | 9 | ## 5. verbs: the basics | term | plain-english meaning | french example | chapter | |---|---|---|---:| | **infinitive** | the unchanged \"dictionary\" form of a verb: \"to speak.\" french infinitives end in `-er`, `-ir`, or `-re`. | `parler`, `finir`, `vendre` | 3 | | **conjugate** | to change a verb's ending to match its subject. | `je parle`, `nous parlons` | 3 | | **stem (radical)** | what is left after removing the infinitive ending. it carries the meaning. | `parler` -> stem `parl-` | 3 | | **ending** | the part added to the stem to show who and when. | `parl` + `-ons` | 3 | | **regular verb** | a verb following a predictable pattern for its group. | `parler`, `finir`, `vendre` | 3, 4, 5 | | **irregular verb** | a verb with forms you must memorize individually. | `être`, `avoir`, `aller`, `faire` | 2, 6, 7, 8 | | **auxiliary (helping verb)** | a verb used with a participle to build a compound tense. french uses `avoir` or `être`. | `j'**ai** parlé` — i spoke | 13 | | **participle** | a verb form used with an auxiliary or as a describing word. | `parlé` (past), `parlant` (present) | 12, 13 | | **past participle** | the form used after an auxiliary to build a past tense. | `parlé`, `fini`, `vendu` | 13 | | **present participle** | the `-ant` form, close to english \"-ing.\" | `en parlant` — while speaking | 12 | | **pronominal (reflexive) verb** | a verb used with a reflexive pronoun. | `se lever` — to get up | 12 | ## 6. verbs: tense and mood **tense** tells you *when*. **mood** tells you *how the speaker views* the action: as a fact, a command, a wish, or a hypothesis. | term | plain-english meaning | french example | chapter | |---|---|---|---:| | **tense** | the time of the action: past, present, or future. | `je parle` (present) | 3 | | **mood** | the speaker's attitude: statement, command, doubt, hypothesis. | indicative, imperative, subjunctive | 11, 16 | | **present tense** | happening now, or generally true. | `je parle` — i speak / i am speaking | 3 | | **near future (futur proche)** | `aller` + infinitive. like \"going to.\" | `je vais parler` — i'm going to speak | 6 | | **passé composé** | the main past tense for completed events. | `j'ai parlé` — i spoke | 13 | | **imparfait** | a past tense for ongoing states, habits, and background. | `je parlais` — i used to speak | 14 | | **pluperfect (plus-que-parfait)** | a past *before* another past. | `j'avais parlé` — i had spoken | 15 | | **simple future (futur simple)** | a one-word future tense. | `je parlerai` — i will speak | 15 | | **conditional** | \"would.\" for hypotheses and polite requests. | `je parlerais` — i would speak | 15 | | **imperative** | a command or instruction. | `parle !` — speak! | 11 | | **subjunctive** | a mood for wish, doubt, emotion, and necessity, after certain triggers. | `que je parle` | 16 | | **indicative** | the ordinary \"fact-stating\" mood: all normal tenses. | `je parle` | 3 | do not be intimidated by this table. in everyday french, the present, the passé composé, the imparfait, and the near future cover the overwhelming majority of what you will say. ## 7. sentence operations | term | plain-english meaning | french example | chapter | |---|---|---|---:| | **negation** | making a sentence mean \"not.\" french wraps the verb in two words. | `je **ne** parle **pas**.` | 2, 11 | | **interrogative** | relating to questions. | `où ?` — where? | 3 | | **question word** | who, what, where, when, why, how. | `qui, que, où, quand, pourquoi, comment` | 3 | | **inversion** | flipping the verb and subject to form a question. | `parlez-vous ?` — do you speak? | 3 | | **`est-ce que`** | a fixed phrase placed in front of a statement to turn it into a question. | `est-ce que tu parles ?` | 3 | | **comparative** | comparing two things: more, less, as. | `plus grand que` — bigger than | 10 | | **superlative** | the extreme of a group: the most, the least. | `le plus grand` — the biggest | 10 | | **register** | how formal the language is. | `tu` (informal) vs `vous` (formal) | 0, 2 | ## 8. sounds and spelling | term | plain-english meaning | french example | chapter | |---|---|---|---:| | **vowel** | the letters `a, e, i, o, u, y` and their sounds. | `a` in `la` | 0 | | **consonant** | any letter that is not a vowel. | `p` in `petit` | 0 | | **syllable** | one beat of a word. | `bon-jour` = 2 syllables | 0 | | **nasal vowel** | a vowel sent through the nose, written vowel + `n`/`m`. | `bon`, `vin`, `grand` | 0 | | **elision** | dropping a final vowel before a vowel sound, marked by an apostrophe. | `je` + `ai` -> `j'ai` | 0, 1 | | **liaison** | pronouncing a normally silent final consonant because the next word starts with a vowel. | `les amis` sounds \"lay-z-ami\" | 0, 1 | | **silent letter** | a written letter you do not pronounce. | the `t` in `petit` | 0 | | **accent mark** | a symbol changing a letter's sound or distinguishing two words. | `é`, `è`, `ê`, `ç`, `ë` | 0 | | **cognate** | a word that looks like its english equivalent. | `la table` — the table | 0 | | **false friend** | a word that *looks* like an english word but means something else. | `actuellement` means \"currently,\" not \"actually\" | 7 | ## 9. study terms used in this course | term | what it means here | |---|---| | **retrieval check** | a short quiz you answer **without looking**. struggling to recall is what builds memory. | | **production task** | creating your own sentence, not copying one. | | **transformation** | rewriting a sentence with one change: subject, tense, number, or polarity. | | **polarity** | whether a sentence is affirmative or negative. | | **dictation** | writing down french you hear, without seeing it. | | **spaced review** | revisiting material after 1, 3, 7, and 30 days rather than all at once. | | **closed-book** | working with no notes visible. the only score that measures real mastery. | | **fixed chunk** | a phrase memorized whole, before you understand its grammar. | | **minimal pair** | two words differing by one sound, used to train your ear. `tu` / `tout` | ## the ten terms that matter most at the start if this page feels overwhelming, learn only these before chapter 1: 1. **noun** — a person, place, or thing. 2. **gender** — every noun is masculine or feminine. 3. **article** — the small word before a noun. 4. **adjective** — a describing word. 5. **agreement** — making words match in gender and number. 6. **verb** — an action or state word. 7. **infinitive** — the \"to do\" dictionary form. 8. **conjugate** — change the verb ending to fit the subject. 9. **subject** — who does the action. 10. **elision** — `je` + `ai` becomes `j'ai`. everything else can wait until the chapter that teaches it. "}]},{"number":1,"slug":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives","title":"Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives","documents":[{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/README.md","title":"Chapter 1 Learning Pack: Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-1-learning-pack-nouns-articles-and-adjectives\">Chapter 1 Learning Pack: Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion course for Chapter 1 of <em>Easy French Step-by-Step</em>. It teaches the chapter's concepts without reproducing the book.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Recognize masculine and feminine nouns.</li>\n<li>Choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</li>\n<li>Choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>.</li>\n<li>Form common noun plurals.</li>\n<li>Make descriptive adjectives agree in gender and number.</li>\n<li>Place common adjectives before or after the noun.</li>\n<li>Use colors and nationalities accurately.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"best-learning-order\">Best learning order</h2>\n<p>Use the materials in this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Study one unit at a time in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Read the side-by-side English meaning, then say every French example aloud.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching section in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">practice/writing-drills.md</code></span> by hand.</li>\n<li>Close the lesson and answer from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching exercises in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">practice/exercises.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Correct your work with <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">practice/answer-key.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Record errors in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review/error-log.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Follow the review schedule in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review/7-day-plan.md</code></span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The existing <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">audio-script.md</code></span>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tts-narration.txt</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chapter-01-audio-script.docx</code> provide listening and pronunciation practice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"does-writing-help-you-learn-french-faster\">Does writing help you learn French faster?</h2>\n<p>Yes, especially when writing is combined with <strong>retrieval and correction</strong>. Copying a paragraph repeatedly is less effective than trying to produce a phrase from memory and then checking it.</p>\n<p>The most useful cycle is:</p>\n<blockquote>See it → say it → cover it → write it from memory → check it → rewrite only the correction</blockquote>\n<p>Handwriting can slow you down enough to notice accents, gender, endings, and word order. However, writing alone will not train listening or pronunciation. A balanced beginner session should include:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Activity</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Learn one small rule</td>\n<td>5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Say examples aloud</td>\n<td>5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Write from memory</td>\n<td>10 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complete exercises</td>\n<td>10 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Review mistakes</td>\n<td>5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-target\">Mastery target</h2>\n<p>Move to Chapter 2 when you can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>score at least <strong>85%</strong> on the mixed review without looking at notes;</li>\n<li>explain why an article or adjective form is correct;</li>\n<li>write five original noun phrases with correct agreement;</li>\n<li>read those phrases aloud comfortably.</li>\n</ul>","search":"# chapter 1 learning pack: nouns, articles, and adjectives this is an original companion course for chapter 1 of *easy french step-by-step*. it teaches the chapter's concepts without reproducing the book. ## what you will learn 1. recognize masculine and feminine nouns. 2. choose `le`, `la`, `l'`, and `les`. 3. choose `un`, `une`, and `des`. 4. form common noun plurals. 5. make descriptive adjectives agree in gender and number. 6. place common adjectives before or after the noun. 7. use colors and nationalities accurately. ## best learning order use the materials in this order: 1. study one unit at a time in [`lessons.md`](lessons.md). 2. read the side-by-side english meaning, then say every french example aloud. 3. complete the matching section in [`practice/writing-drills.md`](practice/writing-drills.md) by hand. 4. close the lesson and answer from memory. 5. complete the matching exercises in [`practice/exercises.md`](practice/exercises.md). 6. correct your work with [`practice/answer-key.md`](practice/answer-key.md). 7. record errors in [`review/error-log.md`](review/error-log.md). 8. follow the review schedule in [`review/7-day-plan.md`](review/7-day-plan.md). the existing [`audio-script.md`](audio-script.md), `tts-narration.txt`, and `chapter-01-audio-script.docx` provide listening and pronunciation practice. ## does writing help you learn french faster? yes, especially when writing is combined with **retrieval and correction**. copying a paragraph repeatedly is less effective than trying to produce a phrase from memory and then checking it. the most useful cycle is: > see it -> say it -> cover it -> write it from memory -> check it -> rewrite only the correction handwriting can slow you down enough to notice accents, gender, endings, and word order. however, writing alone will not train listening or pronunciation. a balanced beginner session should include: | activity | time | |---|---:| | learn one small rule | 5 minutes | | say examples aloud | 5 minutes | | write from memory | 10 minutes | | complete exercises | 10 minutes | | review mistakes | 5 minutes | ## mastery target move to chapter 2 when you can: - score at least **85%** on the mixed review without looking at notes; - explain why an article or adjective form is correct; - write five original noun phrases with correct agreement; - read those phrases aloud comfortably. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 1 Lessons: Nouns, Articles, and Descriptive Adjectives","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-1-lessons-nouns-articles-and-descriptive-adjectives\">Chapter 1 Lessons: Nouns, Articles, and Descriptive Adjectives</h1>\n<p>This chapter builds the noun phrase, one of the foundations of French. By the end, you should be able to choose an article, recognize a noun's gender and number, make an adjective agree, and place common adjectives naturally.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>treat a French noun and its gender as one vocabulary unit;</li>\n<li>use definite articles <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>;</li>\n<li>use indefinite articles <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>;</li>\n<li>form common noun plurals and recognize number in speech;</li>\n<li>change descriptive adjectives for gender and number;</li>\n<li>place common adjectives before or after the noun;</li>\n<li>use colors and nationality adjectives in complete sentences.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can identify a noun and an adjective in English.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In “a blue house,” <strong>house</strong> is the noun and <strong>blue</strong> is the adjective.</li>\n<li>In “the Canadian students,” <strong>students</strong> is the noun and <strong>Canadian</strong> is the adjective.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>French adds two decisions that English often does not show: grammatical gender and visible agreement.</p>\n<p>This chapter asks you to read every French example aloud. If you are not yet sure how French letters sound, work through <span class=\"reference-link\">Chapter 0</span> first: it covers the alphabet, silent letters, nasal vowels, elision, and liaison. If a grammar term here is unfamiliar, look it up in the <span class=\"reference-link\">glossary</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test the prerequisite rather than just rereading it.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In <strong>a blue house</strong>, which word is the noun? — <strong>house</strong>. The noun is the thing being named.</li>\n<li>In <strong>the Canadian students</strong>, which word is the adjective? — <strong>Canadian</strong>. The adjective describes the noun.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code>, which French word is the noun? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code>. It is the thing being described.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code>, which word is the adjective? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code>. It describes the house and agrees with it.</li>\n<li>Why does this chapter care about gender and number? — French articles and adjectives change to match the noun, so you must know whether the noun is masculine/feminine and singular/plural.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed more than one, review the glossary and Chapter 0 before continuing. Chapter 1 assumes you can reliably find the noun that controls the surrounding words.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>For each unit, read the explanation, say every French example aloud, cover the English and test comprehension, then cover the French and write it from memory. Complete the substitution prompts before moving to the next unit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-gender-and-definite-articles\">Unit 1: Gender and Definite Articles</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Every French noun has grammatical gender. Learn the article with the noun:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le jardin</code> — the garden</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la fenêtre</code> — the window</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'école</code> — the school</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voisins</code> — the neighbors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treat <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le jardin</code> as one vocabulary unit rather than memorizing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jardin</code> alone.</p>\n<h3 id=\"definite-articles\">Definite articles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the + masculine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le café</code></td>\n<td>the café</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the + feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la chaise</code></td>\n<td>the chair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the + singular before a vowel sound</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'ami</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'histoire</code></td>\n<td>the friend, the story</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the + plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les cafés</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les chaises</code></td>\n<td>the cafés, the chairs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> hides the noun's gender, so record the gender separately:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'arbre</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m.</code>) — the tree</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'adresse</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f.</code>) — the address</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"useful-patterns-not-guarantees\">Useful patterns, not guarantees</h3>\n<p>Some endings frequently suggest gender:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Often feminine: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-tion</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-sion</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-té</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ette</code></li>\n<li>Often masculine: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-age</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-eau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-isme</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are exceptions. Use these patterns to make a first guess, then verify.</p>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Without looking above, write the four French forms of “the.” Then add the correct article:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>___ musée</li>\n<li>___ question</li>\n<li>___ hôtel</li>\n<li>___ étudiants</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>The four forms of <strong>the</strong> are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le musée</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">musée</code> is masculine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la question</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">question</code> is feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hôtel</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code> begins with a silent <strong>h</strong>, so the article elides. The hidden gender is masculine.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les étudiants</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiants</code> is plural, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the room task, answers vary. A correct note records the article with every noun; if you write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, also record the hidden gender because the apostrophe hides it.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h3>\n<p>Look around your room. Find four objects, look up each noun, and write it with its article. Say each phrase twice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud twice. Then cover it and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le musée est près de la gare.</code> — The museum is near the train station.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La question est importante.</code> — The question is important.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'hôtel est dans le centre-ville.</code> — The hotel is downtown.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants regardent le tableau.</code> — The students are looking at the board.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'adresse est sur le document.</code> — The address is on the document.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le musée est près de la gare.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">musée</code> is masculine, so it takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gare</code> is feminine, so it takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La question est importante.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">question</code> is feminine, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">importante</code> shows feminine agreement.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'hôtel est dans le centre-ville.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code> is masculine, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before the silent <strong>h</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants regardent le tableau.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> marks plural; the final <strong>s</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiants</code> is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'adresse est sur le document.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">adresse</code> is feminine, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When checking from memory, score the article and the noun together. A correct noun with the wrong article is not learned yet.</p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h4>\n<p>Rewrite sentence 1 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">restaurant</code>, sentence 2 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponse</code>, and sentence 4 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">professeurs</code>. Adjust the articles as needed.</p>\n<h4 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h4>\n<p>Write three sentences about places or objects near you. Use one masculine noun, one feminine noun, and one plural noun.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le musée est près de la gare.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">musée</code> is masculine, so it takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gare</code> is feminine, so it takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La question est importante.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">question</code> is feminine, and the adjective <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">importante</code> shows feminine agreement with the final <strong>-e</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'hôtel est dans le centre-ville.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code> is masculine, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before the silent <strong>h</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants regardent le tableau.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> marks the noun as plural; the final <strong>s</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiants</code> is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'adresse est sur le document.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">adresse</code> is feminine, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le restaurant est près de la gare.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">restaurant</code> is masculine, so keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La réponse est importante.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponse</code> is feminine, so keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">importante</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les professeurs regardent le tableau.</code> — plural people take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>; the verb ending -ent is silent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For your personal sentences, make sure you have one masculine article, one feminine article, and one plural article. Mark yourself wrong if you used <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> but did not record the hidden gender somewhere in your notes.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-indefinite-articles-and-plurals\">Unit 2: Indefinite Articles and Plurals</h2>\n<h3 id=\"indefinite-articles\">Indefinite articles</h3>\n<p>Use these forms for “a,” “an,” or plural “some”:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Noun</th>\n<th>Article</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un cahier</code></td>\n<td>a notebook</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une lampe</code></td>\n<td>a lamp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des cahiers</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des lampes</code></td>\n<td>some notebooks, some lamps</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Compare:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le cahier</code> — the notebook</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un cahier</code> — a notebook</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les cahiers</code> — the notebooks</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des cahiers</code> — some notebooks</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"forming-plurals\">Forming plurals</h3>\n<p>The common pattern is to add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une porte</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des portes</code> — a door → some doors</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un village</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des villages</code> — a village → some villages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common special patterns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-eau</code> take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-x</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un tableau</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des tableaux</code> — a painting/board → some paintings/boards</li>\n<li>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-al</code> change to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-aux</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un animal</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des animaux</code> — an animal → some animals</li>\n<li>A noun already ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-x</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-z</code> often does not change in writing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Learn exceptions as complete singular/plural pairs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The final plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> is normally silent. The article often carries the clearest number signal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres</code> — the books</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Change each phrase to plural:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une photo</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bureau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le journal</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voix</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des photos</code> — plural indefinite article <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>, with a regular written <strong>-s</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des bureaux</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bureau</code> ends in <strong>-eau</strong>, so the common plural is <strong>-x</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les journaux</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">journal</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">journaux</code> in the plural, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voix</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voix</code> already ends in <strong>x</strong>, so the noun spelling does not change; only the article shows plural.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own objects, answers vary. Mark yourself wrong if you changed only the noun and forgot that the article must change too.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h3>\n<p>Write three singular objects you own. Convert each to plural and read both versions aloud.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<p>Read, copy once, and then reproduce each sentence from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.</code> — I have a blue notebook in my bag.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.</code> — She buys a lamp for the bedroom.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons des photos de Paris.</code> — We have some photos of Paris.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.</code> — The desks are near the windows.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il observe des animaux au parc.</code> — He watches some animals at the park.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cahier</code> is masculine singular, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bleu</code> match it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lampe</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chambre</code> are feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons des photos de Paris.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> marks an indefinite plural; the final plural <strong>s</strong> is written but silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bureau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bureaux</code>, and the verb is plural: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il observe des animaux au parc.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animal</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animaux</code>; this is not a regular <strong>-s</strong> plural.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the change task, change the whole group: article, noun, adjective, and sometimes the verb. Mark yourself wrong if only one word became plural.</p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h4>\n<p>Change each singular object to plural. Then change each plural object to singular. Remember to change both the article and the noun.</p>\n<h4 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h4>\n<p>Write four sentences beginning with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai...</code>: two with singular objects and two with plural objects.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cahier</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sac</code> are masculine singular, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bleu</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code> all match masculine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.</code> — both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lampe</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chambre</code> are feminine singular, so they take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons des photos de Paris.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> marks an indefinite plural. The final plural <strong>s</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">photos</code> is written but normally silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bureau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bureaux</code> in the plural, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> is the contraction of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il observe des animaux au parc.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animal</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animaux</code>; do not form it as a regular <strong>-s</strong> plural.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai des cahiers bleus dans mon sac.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.</code> — article, noun, and adjective all change together.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète des lampes pour la chambre.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.</code> — only the object changes; the rest of the sentence can stay.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons une photo de Paris.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons des photos de Paris.</code> — singular uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>; plural uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le bureau est près de la fenêtre.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.</code> — the verb changes too: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> for singular, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code> for plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il observe un animal au parc.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il observe des animaux au parc.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animal</code> has the special plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animaux</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For your own <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai...</code> sentences, check that every singular noun has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, every plural noun has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>, and plural <strong>-s</strong> or <strong>-x</strong> is not pronounced as a separate sound.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-adjective-agreement\">Unit 3: Adjective Agreement</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A descriptive adjective normally agrees with the noun it describes.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Noun form</th>\n<th>Typical adjective form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td>base form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td>add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine plural</td>\n<td>add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine plural</td>\n<td>add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Example with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit appartement</code> — a small apartment</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code> — a small house</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petits appartements</code> — some small apartments</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites maisons</code> — some small houses</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-spelling-patterns\">Common spelling patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Masculine</th>\n<th>Feminine</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">active</code></td>\n<td>active</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code></td>\n<td>happy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cher</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chère</code></td>\n<td>expensive/dear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">naturel</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">naturelle</code></td>\n<td>natural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code></td>\n<td>good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code></td>\n<td>Canadian</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Some adjectives already end in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> and do not change in the feminine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un garçon calme</code> — a calm boy</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une fille calme</code> — a calm girl</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"mixed-groups\">Mixed groups</h3>\n<p>A plural adjective describing a group containing at least one masculine noun traditionally uses the masculine plural:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marie sont contents.</code> — Paul and Marie are happy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<p>Read the sentences aloud and identify the noun controlled by each adjective.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul est content de son résultat.</code> — Paul is happy with his result.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est contente de son résultat.</code> — Marie is happy with her result.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marc sont contents de leurs résultats.</code> — Paul and Marc are happy with their results.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie et Léa sont contentes de leurs résultats.</code> — Marie and Léa are happy with their results.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marie sont contents de leurs résultats.</code> — Paul and Marie are happy with their results.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiantes canadiennes sont très actives.</code> — The Canadian female students are very active.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le garçon sportif parle avec une fille sportive.</code> — The athletic boy speaks with an athletic girl.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul est content de son résultat.</code> — one masculine person, so base form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est contente de son résultat.</code> — one feminine person, so add the feminine ending.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marc sont contents de leurs résultats.</code> — masculine plural group.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie et Léa sont contentes de leurs résultats.</code> — feminine plural group.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marie sont contents de leurs résultats.</code> — a mixed group uses masculine plural agreement in standard French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiantes canadiennes sont très actives.</code> — both adjectives agree with feminine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiantes</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le garçon sportif parle avec une fille sportive.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code> matches <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">garçon</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code> matches <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fille</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The noun or people being described control the adjective. Do not choose the ending by sound alone, because several endings are silent.</p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"notice-the-pattern\">Notice the pattern</h4>\n<p>Underline <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contente</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contents</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contentes</code>. Draw an arrow from each adjective to the noun or people it describes.</p>\n<h4 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h4>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-5 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigué</code> and then with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code>. Make every gender and number change.</p>\n<h4 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h4>\n<p>Write four sentences about people you know:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>one masculine singular;</li>\n<li>one feminine singular;</li>\n<li>one feminine plural;</li>\n<li>one mixed group.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Write the feminine form:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italien</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then write the feminine plural form of each.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Masculine</th>\n<th>Feminine</th>\n<th>Feminine plural</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportives</code></td>\n<td><strong>-f</strong> changes to <strong>-ve</strong>, then plural <strong>-s</strong> is added.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuses</code></td>\n<td><strong>-x</strong> changes to <strong>-se</strong> in the feminine.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italiennes</code></td>\n<td>The <strong>n</strong> doubles in the feminine.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calmes</code></td>\n<td>It already ends in <strong>-e</strong>, so the feminine singular looks the same.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The rule is agreement, not pronunciation. Some forms sound alike, so the noun's gender and number tell you what to write.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul est content de son résultat.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code> describes Paul, one masculine person, so it stays in the base form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est contente de son résultat.</code> — Marie is feminine singular, so add <strong>-e</strong>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contente</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marc sont contents de leurs résultats.</code> — two masculine people require masculine plural agreement: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contents</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie et Léa sont contentes de leurs résultats.</code> — two feminine people require feminine plural agreement: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contentes</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marie sont contents de leurs résultats.</code> — a mixed group uses masculine plural agreement in standard French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiantes canadiennes sont très actives.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiantes</code> is feminine plural, so both adjectives are feminine plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le garçon sportif parle avec une fille sportive.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code> agrees with masculine <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">garçon</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code> agrees with feminine <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fille</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigué</code>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul est fatigué de son résultat.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est fatiguée de son résultat.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marc sont fatigués de leurs résultats.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie et Léa sont fatiguées de leurs résultats.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marie sont fatigués de leurs résultats.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul est heureux de son résultat.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est heureuse de son résultat.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marc sont heureux de leurs résultats.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie et Léa sont heureuses de leurs résultats.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marie sont heureux de leurs résultats.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>In your personal sentences, the subject controls the adjective. Mark yourself wrong if a feminine or plural subject still has the masculine singular adjective by accident.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-adjective-position-colors-and-nationalities\">Unit 4: Adjective Position, Colors, and Nationalities</h2>\n<h3 id=\"position\">Position</h3>\n<p>Most descriptive adjectives follow the noun:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une rue tranquille</code> — a quiet street</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un exercice difficile</code> — a difficult exercise</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chemise blanche</code> — a white shirt</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Several frequent adjectives commonly precede the noun, especially short descriptions involving beauty, age, goodness, and size:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un beau quartier</code> — a beautiful neighborhood</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une jeune étudiante</code> — a young female student</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bon repas</code> — a good meal</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande ville</code> — a large city</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Learn position as part of the phrase. Instead of memorizing only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile</code>, memorize <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un exercice difficile</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"forms-before-a-vowel-sound\">Forms before a vowel sound</h3>\n<p>Some masculine singular adjectives have a special form before a vowel sound:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bel</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code> — a beautiful apartment</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel ami</code> — a new friend</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieil hôtel</code> — an old hotel</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"colors\">Colors</h3>\n<p>Colors usually follow the noun and agree:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sac noir</code> — a black bag</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une veste noire</code> — a black jacket</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des sacs noirs</code> — some black bags</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des vestes noires</code> — some black jackets</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some color expressions derived from nouns may remain unchanged. For now, master the regular high-frequency colors first.</p>\n<h3 id=\"nationalities\">Nationalities</h3>\n<p>Nationality adjectives <strong>agree</strong> with the person or noun they describe. This means the adjective changes its ending to match:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>gender: masculine or feminine;</li>\n<li>number: singular or plural.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code> changes like this:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Person or group</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un étudiant canadien</code></td>\n<td>a Canadian male student</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante canadienne</code></td>\n<td>a Canadian female student</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine or mixed plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants canadiens</code></td>\n<td>Canadian male/mixed students</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadiennes</code></td>\n<td>Canadian female students</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Nationality adjectives are normally lowercase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un acteur français</code> — a French actor</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une actrice française</code> — a French actress</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants canadiens</code> — some Canadian male/mixed students</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadiennes</code> — some Canadian female students</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The name of a language is also lowercase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français.</code> — I speak French.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a nationality is used as a noun for a person rather than as an adjective, it is capitalized:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est canadienne.</code> — She is Canadian. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> is an adjective.)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est une Canadienne.</code> — She is a Canadian woman. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Canadienne</code> is a noun.)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Correct each phrase:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture noir</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un appartement beau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiant canadien</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des maisons blanc</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture noire</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voiture</code> is feminine singular, so the color becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> comes before the noun and becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bel</code> before a masculine noun starting with a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante canadienne</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiante</code> is feminine singular, so both the article and nationality adjective must be feminine.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des maisons blanches</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maisons</code> is feminine plural, so the color is feminine plural too.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct the whole noun phrase, not just the word that looks visibly wrong. Article, noun, and adjective work as a unit.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h3>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud. Mark adjectives before the noun with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">B</code> and adjectives after the noun with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">A</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons dans une grande ville canadienne.</code> — We live in a large Canadian city.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle porte une petite veste noire.</code> — She is wearing a small black jacket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un bel appartement moderne.</code> — It is a beautiful modern apartment.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un nouvel étudiant français arrive aujourd'hui.</code> — A new French student arrives today.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les vieilles maisons blanches sont près de la rivière.</code> — The old white houses are near the river.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français avec mes collègues canadiens.</code> — I speak French with my Canadian colleagues.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Adjective position in the six sentences:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons dans une grande ville canadienne.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code> is before the noun; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> is after.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle porte une petite veste noire.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> is before; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code> is after.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un bel appartement moderne.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bel</code> is before; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moderne</code> is after.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un nouvel étudiant français arrive aujourd'hui.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> is before; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code> is after.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les vieilles maisons blanches sont près de la rivière.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieilles</code> is before; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanches</code> is after.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français avec mes collègues canadiens.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiens</code> is after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">collègues</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code> here names the language.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The short common adjectives such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code> often come before the noun. Colors and nationalities usually come after.</p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Make sentences 1, 2, and 3 plural.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rouge</code>, then to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bleue</code>.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code> in sentence 4 with a nationality that describes you or someone you know.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h4>\n<p>Describe three objects and two people. Use at least one adjective before a noun, three adjectives after nouns, one color, and one nationality.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model memory check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons dans une grande ville canadienne.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code> comes before the noun; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> comes after it. Both agree with feminine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ville</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle porte une petite veste noire.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> comes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veste</code>, while the color <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code> comes after. Both are feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un bel appartement moderne.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bel</code> is the masculine singular form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> before a vowel sound. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moderne</code> follows the noun and already ends in <strong>-e</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un nouvel étudiant français arrive aujourd'hui.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> appears before a masculine noun beginning with a vowel sound. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code> is a nationality adjective and stays lowercase.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les vieilles maisons blanches sont près de la rivière.</code> — both adjectives are feminine plural because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maisons</code> is feminine plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français avec mes collègues canadiens.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code> is the language, so it is lowercase; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiens</code> agrees with plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">collègues</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons dans des grandes villes canadiennes.</code> — article, noun, and both adjectives become plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles portent des petites vestes noires.</code> — plural subject and object require <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">portent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vestes</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noires</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce sont de beaux appartements modernes.</code> — before a plural adjective placed before the noun, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> often becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle porte une petite veste rouge.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle porte une petite veste bleue.</code> — colors follow the noun and agree with feminine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veste</code>.</li>\n<li>Sample nationality change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un nouvel étudiant canadien arrive aujourd'hui.</code> — the nationality remains lowercase and agrees with masculine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiant</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your personal descriptions, check adjective position first, then agreement. A correct answer must include at least one before-noun adjective and several after-noun adjectives, not just a list of nouns.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Learning a noun without its article.</strong> Learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code>, not only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">table</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Assuming every noun ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> is feminine.</strong> Many are, but words such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le problème</code> are masculine.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> can hide either gender.</strong> Record <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'école (f.)</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hôtel (m.)</code> in vocabulary notes.</li>\n<li><strong>Changing only the noun in the plural.</strong> The article and adjective must normally change too: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites maisons</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Adding feminine endings mechanically.</strong> Some adjectives have spelling changes: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Putting every adjective after the noun.</strong> Most go after, but frequent adjectives such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jeune</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> often go before.</li>\n<li><strong>Capitalizing nationality adjectives.</strong> Write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante française</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une Française</code> when the nationality is a noun.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-describing-a-new-apartment\">Mini-dialogue: describing a new apartment</h2>\n<p>Read the dialogue first in French. Then use the English version to check meaning.</p>\n<p><strong>Léa :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu habites dans un nouvel appartement ?</code> — Do you live in a new apartment?</p>\n<p><strong>Marc :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui. C'est un petit appartement moderne dans un quartier tranquille.</code> — Yes. It is a small modern apartment in a quiet neighborhood.</p>\n<p><strong>Léa :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a combien de chambres ?</code> — How many bedrooms are there?</p>\n<p><strong>Marc :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une grande chambre et deux petites chambres.</code> — There is one large bedroom and two small bedrooms.</p>\n<p><strong>Léa :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et la cuisine ?</code> — And the kitchen?</p>\n<p><strong>Marc :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La cuisine est blanche, avec une table noire et des chaises rouges.</code> — The kitchen is white, with a black table and red chairs.</p>\n<p><strong>Léa :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tes voisins sont français ?</code> — Are your neighbors French?</p>\n<p><strong>Marc :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Une voisine est française et deux voisins sont canadiens.</code> — One female neighbor is French and two male neighbors are Canadian.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find four indefinite articles and explain why each is masculine, feminine, or plural.</li>\n<li>Find three adjectives placed before nouns.</li>\n<li>Find five adjectives placed after nouns.</li>\n<li>Explain the endings in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petites chambres</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaises rouges</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voisins canadiens</code>.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appartement</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code> and rewrite Marc's first two answers with the required changes.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Four indefinite articles: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel appartement</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit appartement</code> are masculine singular; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un quartier</code> is masculine singular; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande chambre</code> is feminine singular; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des chaises rouges</code> is plural. Any four count if you explain the gender or number.</li>\n<li>Three adjectives before nouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petites</code>.</li>\n<li>Five adjectives after nouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moderne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tranquille</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanche</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rouges</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">française</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiens</code>. Any five count.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petites chambres</code> is feminine plural because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chambres</code> is feminine plural. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaises rouges</code> is feminine plural. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voisins canadiens</code> is masculine plural.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui. C'est une petite maison moderne dans un quartier tranquille.</code> Then: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une grande chambre et deux petites chambres.</code> The first answer changes because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code> is feminine; the second answer can stay the same.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The main rule is agreement: when <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appartement</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code>, words attached to that noun must become feminine.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-mon-quartier\">Connected reading: mon quartier</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite dans une petite rue près du centre-ville. La rue a de vieilles maisons blanches et de nouveaux appartements modernes. Mon immeuble a un grand jardin partagé. Une famille canadienne habite au premier étage, et deux étudiantes françaises habitent au deuxième étage. Les voisins sont sympathiques. Le samedi, nous prenons un café dans le petit jardin.</code></p>\n<p>— I live on a small street near downtown. The street has old white houses and new modern apartments. My building has a large shared garden. A Canadian family lives on the first floor, and two French female students live on the second floor. The neighbors are friendly. On Saturdays, we have coffee in the small garden.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List every noun with its article.</li>\n<li>Match every adjective to the noun it describes.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite rue</code> to plural.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une famille canadienne</code> to a masculine singular person and then to a mixed plural group.</li>\n<li>Write three true sentences about your own neighborhood using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Nouns with articles include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite rue</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du centre-ville</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La rue</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de vieilles maisons blanches</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de nouveaux appartements modernes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon immeuble</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un grand jardin partagé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Une famille canadienne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au premier étage</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux étudiantes françaises</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au deuxième étage</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les voisins</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un café</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le petit jardin</code>.</li>\n<li>Adjective matches: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rue</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieilles</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanches</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maisons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveaux</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">modernes</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appartements</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partagé</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jardin</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">famille</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">françaises</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiantes</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sympathiques</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voisins</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jardin</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite rue</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites rues</code>.</li>\n<li>Masculine singular person: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un étudiant canadien</code>. Mixed plural group: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants canadiens</code>.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. A model sentence is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite dans une rue calme près d'un petit parc.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own sentences, copy the structure, not the exact content. Every adjective must point to a noun and agree with it.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"articles\">Articles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Masculine singular</th>\n<th>Feminine singular</th>\n<th>Before vowel sound</th>\n<th>Plural</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a/an/some</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"regular-adjective-agreement\">Regular adjective agreement</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Typical ending</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td>base form</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine/mixed plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petits</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petites</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-habits\">Core habits</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Memorize article + noun together.</li>\n<li>Check article, noun, and adjective as one agreement group.</li>\n<li>Learn adjective position as part of a useful phrase.</li>\n<li>Read complete phrases aloud because articles often reveal gender and number in speech.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-10 sentences describing a room, neighborhood, classroom, or group of people. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least eight nouns with articles;</li>\n<li>masculine and feminine nouns;</li>\n<li>singular and plural forms;</li>\n<li>six descriptive adjectives;</li>\n<li>two adjectives before nouns and four after nouns;</li>\n<li>two colors;</li>\n<li>one nationality adjective;</li>\n<li>one mixed group using a masculine plural adjective.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every article;</li>\n<li>underline every noun;</li>\n<li>draw an arrow from every adjective to its noun;</li>\n<li>check gender and number agreement;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary, so mark against the checklist. Model paragraph:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite dans un petit appartement près d'une grande école. Le salon a une table noire, deux chaises rouges et un vieux canapé confortable. La cuisine est blanche et très calme. Dans ma chambre, il y a un bureau moderne et des livres français. Mes voisins canadiens sont sympathiques. Marie et Paul sont contents de vivre dans le quartier. Le jardin partagé est joli au printemps. Les fenêtres sont grandes et lumineuses.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: nouns with articles include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit appartement</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande école</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le salon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une table</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux chaises</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieux canapé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La cuisine</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma chambre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bureau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des livres</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes voisins</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le quartier</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le jardin</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les fenêtres</code>; it includes masculine, feminine, singular, and plural nouns; adjectives include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rouges</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">confortable</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanche</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moderne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiens</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sympathiques</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contents</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partagé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">joli</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grandes</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lumineuses</code>; colors are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rouges</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanche</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiens</code> is the nationality adjective; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie et Paul sont contents</code> shows a mixed group with masculine plural agreement.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a noun has no article where one is required;</li>\n<li>a color or nationality adjective does not agree;</li>\n<li>all adjectives come after nouns, with none before;</li>\n<li>a mixed group has feminine plural agreement;</li>\n<li>you capitalize a nationality adjective such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiens</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 1 lessons: nouns, articles, and descriptive adjectives this chapter builds the noun phrase, one of the foundations of french. by the end, you should be able to choose an article, recognize a noun's gender and number, make an adjective agree, and place common adjectives naturally. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. treat a french noun and its gender as one vocabulary unit; 2. use definite articles `le`, `la`, `l'`, and `les`; 3. use indefinite articles `un`, `une`, and `des`; 4. form common noun plurals and recognize number in speech; 5. change descriptive adjectives for gender and number; 6. place common adjectives before or after the noun; 7. use colors and nationality adjectives in complete sentences. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can identify a noun and an adjective in english. - in “a blue house,” **house** is the noun and **blue** is the adjective. - in “the canadian students,” **students** is the noun and **canadian** is the adjective. french adds two decisions that english often does not show: grammatical gender and visible agreement. this chapter asks you to read every french example aloud. if you are not yet sure how french letters sound, work through [chapter 0](../chapter-00-getting-started/) first: it covers the alphabet, silent letters, nasal vowels, elision, and liaison. if a grammar term here is unfamiliar, look it up in the [glossary](../chapter-00-getting-started/glossary.md). test the prerequisite rather than just rereading it. 1. in **a blue house**, which word is the noun? — **house**. the noun is the thing being named. 2. in **the canadian students**, which word is the adjective? — **canadian**. the adjective describes the noun. 3. in `une petite maison`, which french word is the noun? — `maison`. it is the thing being described. 4. in `une petite maison`, which word is the adjective? — `petite`. it describes the house and agrees with it. 5. why does this chapter care about gender and number? — french articles and adjectives change to match the noun, so you must know whether the noun is masculine/feminine and singular/plural. if you missed more than one, review the glossary and chapter 0 before continuing. chapter 1 assumes you can reliably find the noun that controls the surrounding words. ## how to study this chapter for each unit, read the explanation, say every french example aloud, cover the english and test comprehension, then cover the french and write it from memory. complete the substitution prompts before moving to the next unit. ## unit 1: gender and definite articles ### core idea every french noun has grammatical gender. learn the article with the noun: - `le jardin` — the garden - `la fenêtre` — the window - `l'école` — the school - `les voisins` — the neighbors treat `le jardin` as one vocabulary unit rather than memorizing `jardin` alone. ### definite articles | meaning | form | french example | english | |---|---|---|---| | the + masculine singular | `le` | `le café` | the café | | the + feminine singular | `la` | `la chaise` | the chair | | the + singular before a vowel sound | `l'` | `l'ami`, `l'histoire` | the friend, the story | | the + plural | `les` | `les cafés`, `les chaises` | the cafés, the chairs | `l'` hides the noun's gender, so record the gender separately: - `l'arbre` (`m.`) — the tree - `l'adresse` (`f.`) — the address ### useful patterns, not guarantees some endings frequently suggest gender: - often feminine: `-tion`, `-sion`, `-té`, `-ette` - often masculine: `-age`, `-ment`, `-eau`, `-isme` there are exceptions. use these patterns to make a first guess, then verify. ### retrieval check without looking above, write the four french forms of “the.” then add the correct article: 1. ___ musée 2. ___ question 3. ___ hôtel 4. ___ étudiants the four forms of **the** are `le`, `la`, `l'`, and `les`. 1. `le musée` — `musée` is masculine singular. 2. `la question` — `question` is feminine singular. 3. `l'hôtel` — `hôtel` begins with a silent **h**, so the article elides. the hidden gender is masculine. 4. `les étudiants` — `étudiants` is plural, so use `les`. for the room task, answers vary. a correct note records the article with every noun; if you write `l'`, also record the hidden gender because the apostrophe hides it. ### production task look around your room. find four objects, look up each noun, and write it with its article. say each phrase twice. ### sentence usage practice read each sentence aloud twice. then cover it and write it from memory. 1. `le musée est près de la gare.` — the museum is near the train station. 2. `la question est importante.` — the question is important. 3. `l'hôtel est dans le centre-ville.` — the hotel is downtown. 4. `les étudiants regardent le tableau.` — the students are looking at the board. 5. `l'adresse est sur le document.` — the address is on the document. model memory check: 1. `le musée est près de la gare.` — `musée` is masculine, so it takes `le`; `gare` is feminine, so it takes `la`. 2. `la question est importante.` — `question` is feminine, and `importante` shows feminine agreement. 3. `l'hôtel est dans le centre-ville.` — `hôtel` is masculine, but `le` becomes `l'` before the silent **h**. 4. `les étudiants regardent le tableau.` — `les` marks plural; the final **s** of `étudiants` is silent. 5. `l'adresse est sur le document.` — `adresse` is feminine, but `la` becomes `l'` before a vowel sound. when checking from memory, score the article and the noun together. a correct noun with the wrong article is not learned yet. #### change the sentence rewrite sentence 1 with `restaurant`, sentence 2 with `réponse`, and sentence 4 with `professeurs`. adjust the articles as needed. #### make it personal write three sentences about places or objects near you. use one masculine noun, one feminine noun, and one plural noun. model memory check: 1. `le musée est près de la gare.` — `musée` is masculine, so it takes `le`; `gare` is feminine, so it takes `la`. 2. `la question est importante.` — `question` is feminine, and the adjective `importante` shows feminine agreement with the final **-e**. 3. `l'hôtel est dans le centre-ville.` — `hôtel` is masculine, but `le` becomes `l'` before the silent **h**. 4. `les étudiants regardent le tableau.` — `les` marks the noun as plural; the final **s** of `étudiants` is silent. 5. `l'adresse est sur le document.` — `adresse` is feminine, but `la` becomes `l'` before a vowel sound. change the sentence: - `le restaurant est près de la gare.` — `restaurant` is masculine, so keep `le`. - `la réponse est importante.` — `réponse` is feminine, so keep `la` and `importante`. - `les professeurs regardent le tableau.` — plural people take `les`; the verb ending -ent is silent. for your personal sentences, make sure you have one masculine article, one feminine article, and one plural article. mark yourself wrong if you used `l'` but did not record the hidden gender somewhere in your notes. ## unit 2: indefinite articles and plurals ### indefinite articles use these forms for “a,” “an,” or plural “some”: | noun | article | french example | english | |---|---|---|---| | masculine singular | `un` | `un cahier` | a notebook | | feminine singular | `une` | `une lampe` | a lamp | | plural | `des` | `des cahiers`, `des lampes` | some notebooks, some lamps | compare: - `le cahier` — the notebook - `un cahier` — a notebook - `les cahiers` — the notebooks - `des cahiers` — some notebooks ### forming plurals the common pattern is to add `-s`: - `une porte` -> `des portes` — a door -> some doors - `un village` -> `des villages` — a village -> some villages common special patterns: - many nouns ending in `-eau` take `-x`: `un tableau` -> `des tableaux` — a painting/board -> some paintings/boards - many nouns ending in `-al` change to `-aux`: `un animal` -> `des animaux` — an animal -> some animals - a noun already ending in `-s`, `-x`, or `-z` often does not change in writing. learn exceptions as complete singular/plural pairs. ### pronunciation note the final plural `-s` is normally silent. the article often carries the clearest number signal: - `le livre` — the book - `les livres` — the books ### retrieval check change each phrase to plural: 1. `une photo` 2. `un bureau` 3. `le journal` 4. `la voix` 1. `des photos` — plural indefinite article `des`, with a regular written **-s**. 2. `des bureaux` — `bureau` ends in **-eau**, so the common plural is **-x**. 3. `les journaux` — `journal` changes to `journaux` in the plural, and `le` becomes `les`. 4. `les voix` — `voix` already ends in **x**, so the noun spelling does not change; only the article shows plural. for your own objects, answers vary. mark yourself wrong if you changed only the noun and forgot that the article must change too. ### production task write three singular objects you own. convert each to plural and read both versions aloud. ### sentence usage practice read, copy once, and then reproduce each sentence from memory. 1. `j'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.` — i have a blue notebook in my bag. 2. `elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.` — she buys a lamp for the bedroom. 3. `nous avons des photos de paris.` — we have some photos of paris. 4. `les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.` — the desks are near the windows. 5. `il observe des animaux au parc.` — he watches some animals at the park. model memory check: 1. `j'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.` — `cahier` is masculine singular, so `un` and `bleu` match it. 2. `elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.` — `lampe` and `chambre` are feminine singular. 3. `nous avons des photos de paris.` — `des` marks an indefinite plural; the final plural **s** is written but silent. 4. `les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.` — `bureau` becomes `bureaux`, and the verb is plural: `sont`. 5. `il observe des animaux au parc.` — `animal` becomes `animaux`; this is not a regular **-s** plural. for the change task, change the whole group: article, noun, adjective, and sometimes the verb. mark yourself wrong if only one word became plural. #### change the sentence change each singular object to plural. then change each plural object to singular. remember to change both the article and the noun. #### make it personal write four sentences beginning with `j'ai...`: two with singular objects and two with plural objects. model memory check: 1. `j'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.` — `cahier` and `sac` are masculine singular, so `un`, `bleu`, and `mon` all match masculine singular. 2. `elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.` — both `lampe` and `chambre` are feminine singular, so they take `une` and `la`. 3. `nous avons des photos de paris.` — `des` marks an indefinite plural. the final plural **s** in `photos` is written but normally silent. 4. `les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.` — `bureau` becomes `bureaux` in the plural, and `des` is the contraction of `de` + `les`. 5. `il observe des animaux au parc.` — `animal` becomes `animaux`; do not form it as a regular **-s** plural. change the sentence: - `j'ai des cahiers bleus dans mon sac.` / `j'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.` — article, noun, and adjective all change together. - `elle achète des lampes pour la chambre.` / `elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.` — only the object changes; the rest of the sentence can stay. - `nous avons une photo de paris.` / `nous avons des photos de paris.` — singular uses `une`; plural uses `des`. - `le bureau est près de la fenêtre.` / `les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.` — the verb changes too: `est` for singular, `sont` for plural. - `il observe un animal au parc.` / `il observe des animaux au parc.` — `animal` has the special plural `animaux`. for your own `j'ai...` sentences, check that every singular noun has `un` or `une`, every plural noun has `des`, and plural **-s** or **-x** is not pronounced as a separate sound. ## unit 3: adjective agreement ### core idea a descriptive adjective normally agrees with the noun it describes. | noun form | typical adjective form | |---|---| | masculine singular | base form | | feminine singular | add `-e` | | masculine plural | add `-s` | | feminine plural | add `-es` | example with `petit`: - `un petit appartement` — a small apartment - `une petite maison` — a small house - `des petits appartements` — some small apartments - `des petites maisons` — some small houses ### common spelling patterns | masculine | feminine | english | |---|---|---| | `actif` | `active` | active | | `heureux` | `heureuse` | happy | | `cher` | `chère` | expensive/dear | | `naturel` | `naturelle` | natural | | `bon` | `bonne` | good | | `canadien` | `canadienne` | canadian | some adjectives already end in `-e` and do not change in the feminine: - `un garçon calme` — a calm boy - `une fille calme` — a calm girl ### mixed groups a plural adjective describing a group containing at least one masculine noun traditionally uses the masculine plural: - `paul et marie sont contents.` — paul and marie are happy. ### sentence usage practice read the sentences aloud and identify the noun controlled by each adjective. 1. `paul est content de son résultat.` — paul is happy with his result. 2. `marie est contente de son résultat.` — marie is happy with her result. 3. `paul et marc sont contents de leurs résultats.` — paul and marc are happy with their results. 4. `marie et léa sont contentes de leurs résultats.` — marie and léa are happy with their results. 5. `paul et marie sont contents de leurs résultats.` — paul and marie are happy with their results. 6. `les étudiantes canadiennes sont très actives.` — the canadian female students are very active. 7. `le garçon sportif parle avec une fille sportive.` — the athletic boy speaks with an athletic girl. model memory check: 1. `paul est content de son résultat.` — one masculine person, so base form `content`. 2. `marie est contente de son résultat.` — one feminine person, so add the feminine ending. 3. `paul et marc sont contents de leurs résultats.` — masculine plural group. 4. `marie et léa sont contentes de leurs résultats.` — feminine plural group. 5. `paul et marie sont contents de leurs résultats.` — a mixed group uses masculine plural agreement in standard french. 6. `les étudiantes canadiennes sont très actives.` — both adjectives agree with feminine plural `étudiantes`. 7. `le garçon sportif parle avec une fille sportive.` — `sportif` matches `garçon`; `sportive` matches `fille`. the noun or people being described control the adjective. do not choose the ending by sound alone, because several endings are silent. #### notice the pattern underline `content`, `contente`, `contents`, and `contentes`. draw an arrow from each adjective to the noun or people it describes. #### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-5 with `fatigué` and then with `heureux`. make every gender and number change. #### make it personal write four sentences about people you know: 1. one masculine singular; 2. one feminine singular; 3. one feminine plural; 4. one mixed group. ### retrieval check write the feminine form: 1. `sportif` 2. `heureux` 3. `italien` 4. `calme` then write the feminine plural form of each. | masculine | feminine | feminine plural | why | |---|---|---|---| | `sportif` | `sportive` | `sportives` | **-f** changes to **-ve**, then plural **-s** is added. | | `heureux` | `heureuse` | `heureuses` | **-x** changes to **-se** in the feminine. | | `italien` | `italienne` | `italiennes` | the **n** doubles in the feminine. | | `calme` | `calme` | `calmes` | it already ends in **-e**, so the feminine singular looks the same. | the rule is agreement, not pronunciation. some forms sound alike, so the noun's gender and number tell you what to write. model memory check: 1. `paul est content de son résultat.` — `content` describes paul, one masculine person, so it stays in the base form. 2. `marie est contente de son résultat.` — marie is feminine singular, so add **-e**: `contente`. 3. `paul et marc sont contents de leurs résultats.` — two masculine people require masculine plural agreement: `contents`. 4. `marie et léa sont contentes de leurs résultats.` — two feminine people require feminine plural agreement: `contentes`. 5. `paul et marie sont contents de leurs résultats.` — a mixed group uses masculine plural agreement in standard french. 6. `les étudiantes canadiennes sont très actives.` — `étudiantes` is feminine plural, so both adjectives are feminine plural. 7. `le garçon sportif parle avec une fille sportive.` — `sportif` agrees with masculine `garçon`; `sportive` agrees with feminine `fille`. change the sentence with `fatigué`: 1. `paul est fatigué de son résultat.` 2. `marie est fatiguée de son résultat.` 3. `paul et marc sont fatigués de leurs résultats.` 4. `marie et léa sont fatiguées de leurs résultats.` 5. `paul et marie sont fatigués de leurs résultats.` change the sentence with `heureux`: 1. `paul est heureux de son résultat.` 2. `marie est heureuse de son résultat.` 3. `paul et marc sont heureux de leurs résultats.` 4. `marie et léa sont heureuses de leurs résultats.` 5. `paul et marie sont heureux de leurs résultats.` in your personal sentences, the subject controls the adjective. mark yourself wrong if a feminine or plural subject still has the masculine singular adjective by accident. ## unit 4: adjective position, colors, and nationalities ### position most descriptive adjectives follow the noun: - `une rue tranquille` — a quiet street - `un exercice difficile` — a difficult exercise - `une chemise blanche` — a white shirt several frequent adjectives commonly precede the noun, especially short descriptions involving beauty, age, goodness, and size: - `un beau quartier` — a beautiful neighborhood - `une jeune étudiante` — a young female student - `un bon repas` — a good meal - `une grande ville` — a large city learn position as part of the phrase. instead of memorizing only `difficile`, memorize `un exercice difficile`. ### forms before a vowel sound some masculine singular adjectives have a special form before a vowel sound: - `beau` -> `bel`: `un bel appartement` — a beautiful apartment - `nouveau` -> `nouvel`: `un nouvel ami` — a new friend - `vieux` -> `vieil`: `un vieil hôtel` — an old hotel ### colors colors usually follow the noun and agree: - `un sac noir` — a black bag - `une veste noire` — a black jacket - `des sacs noirs` — some black bags - `des vestes noires` — some black jackets some color expressions derived from nouns may remain unchanged. for now, master the regular high-frequency colors first. ### nationalities nationality adjectives **agree** with the person or noun they describe. this means the adjective changes its ending to match: - gender: masculine or feminine; - number: singular or plural. for example, `canadien` changes like this: | person or group | french | english | |---|---|---| | masculine singular | `un étudiant canadien` | a canadian male student | | feminine singular | `une étudiante canadienne` | a canadian female student | | masculine or mixed plural | `des étudiants canadiens` | canadian male/mixed students | | feminine plural | `des étudiantes canadiennes` | canadian female students | nationality adjectives are normally lowercase: - `un acteur français` — a french actor - `une actrice française` — a french actress - `des étudiants canadiens` — some canadian male/mixed students - `des étudiantes canadiennes` — some canadian female students the name of a language is also lowercase: - `je parle français.` — i speak french. when a nationality is used as a noun for a person rather than as an adjective, it is capitalized: - `elle est canadienne.` — she is canadian. (`canadienne` is an adjective.) - `c'est une canadienne.` — she is a canadian woman. (`canadienne` is a noun.) ### retrieval check correct each phrase: 1. `une voiture noir` 2. `un appartement beau` 3. `une étudiant canadien` 4. `des maisons blanc` 1. `une voiture noire` — `voiture` is feminine singular, so the color becomes `noire`. 2. `un bel appartement` — `beau` comes before the noun and becomes `bel` before a masculine noun starting with a vowel sound. 3. `une étudiante canadienne` — `étudiante` is feminine singular, so both the article and nationality adjective must be feminine. 4. `des maisons blanches` — `maisons` is feminine plural, so the color is feminine plural too. correct the whole noun phrase, not just the word that looks visibly wrong. article, noun, and adjective work as a unit. ### sentence usage practice read each sentence aloud. mark adjectives before the noun with `b` and adjectives after the noun with `a`. 1. `nous habitons dans une grande ville canadienne.` — we live in a large canadian city. 2. `elle porte une petite veste noire.` — she is wearing a small black jacket. 3. `c'est un bel appartement moderne.` — it is a beautiful modern apartment. 4. `un nouvel étudiant français arrive aujourd'hui.` — a new french student arrives today. 5. `les vieilles maisons blanches sont près de la rivière.` — the old white houses are near the river. 6. `je parle français avec mes collègues canadiens.` — i speak french with my canadian colleagues. adjective position in the six sentences: 1. `nous habitons dans une grande ville canadienne.` — `grande` is before the noun; `canadienne` is after. 2. `elle porte une petite veste noire.` — `petite` is before; `noire` is after. 3. `c'est un bel appartement moderne.` — `bel` is before; `moderne` is after. 4. `un nouvel étudiant français arrive aujourd'hui.` — `nouvel` is before; `français` is after. 5. `les vieilles maisons blanches sont près de la rivière.` — `vieilles` is before; `blanches` is after. 6. `je parle français avec mes collègues canadiens.` — `canadiens` is after `collègues`; `français` here names the language. the short common adjectives such as `grand`, `petit`, `beau`, `nouveau`, and `vieux` often come before the noun. colors and nationalities usually come after. #### change the sentence 1. make sentences 1, 2, and 3 plural. 2. change `noire` to `rouge`, then to `bleue`. 3. replace `français` in sentence 4 with a nationality that describes you or someone you know. #### make it personal describe three objects and two people. use at least one adjective before a noun, three adjectives after nouns, one color, and one nationality. model memory check: 1. `nous habitons dans une grande ville canadienne.` — `grande` comes before the noun; `canadienne` comes after it. both agree with feminine singular `ville`. 2. `elle porte une petite veste noire.` — `petite` comes before `veste`, while the color `noire` comes after. both are feminine singular. 3. `c'est un bel appartement moderne.` — `bel` is the masculine singular form of `beau` before a vowel sound. `moderne` follows the noun and already ends in **-e**. 4. `un nouvel étudiant français arrive aujourd'hui.` — `nouvel` appears before a masculine noun beginning with a vowel sound. `français` is a nationality adjective and stays lowercase. 5. `les vieilles maisons blanches sont près de la rivière.` — both adjectives are feminine plural because `maisons` is feminine plural. 6. `je parle français avec mes collègues canadiens.` — `français` is the language, so it is lowercase; `canadiens` agrees with plural `collègues`. change the sentence: 1. `nous habitons dans des grandes villes canadiennes.` — article, noun, and both adjectives become plural. 2. `elles portent des petites vestes noires.` — plural subject and object require `portent`, `des`, `vestes`, and `noires`. 3. `ce sont de beaux appartements modernes.` — before a plural adjective placed before the noun, `des` often becomes `de`. 4. `elle porte une petite veste rouge.` / `elle porte une petite veste bleue.` — colors follow the noun and agree with feminine singular `veste`. 5. sample nationality change: `un nouvel étudiant canadien arrive aujourd'hui.` — the nationality remains lowercase and agrees with masculine singular `étudiant`. for your personal descriptions, check adjective position first, then agreement. a correct answer must include at least one before-noun adjective and several after-noun adjectives, not just a list of nouns. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. **learning a noun without its article.** learn `la table`, not only `table`. 2. **assuming every noun ending in `-e` is feminine.** many are, but words such as `le problème` are masculine. 3. **forgetting that `l'` can hide either gender.** record `l'école (f.)` and `l'hôtel (m.)` in vocabulary notes. 4. **changing only the noun in the plural.** the article and adjective must normally change too: `une petite maison` -> `des petites maisons`. 5. **adding feminine endings mechanically.** some adjectives have spelling changes: `heureux` -> `heureuse`, `canadien` -> `canadienne`. 6. **putting every adjective after the noun.** most go after, but frequent adjectives such as `petit`, `grand`, `beau`, `jeune`, and `nouveau` often go before. 7. **capitalizing nationality adjectives.** write `une étudiante française`, but `une française` when the nationality is a noun. ## mini-dialogue: describing a new apartment read the dialogue first in french. then use the english version to check meaning. **léa :** `tu habites dans un nouvel appartement ?` — do you live in a new apartment? **marc :** `oui. c'est un petit appartement moderne dans un quartier tranquille.` — yes. it is a small modern apartment in a quiet neighborhood. **léa :** `il y a combien de chambres ?` — how many bedrooms are there? **marc :** `il y a une grande chambre et deux petites chambres.` — there is one large bedroom and two small bedrooms. **léa :** `et la cuisine ?` — and the kitchen? **marc :** `la cuisine est blanche, avec une table noire et des chaises rouges.` — the kitchen is white, with a black table and red chairs. **léa :** `tes voisins sont français ?` — are your neighbors french? **marc :** `une voisine est française et deux voisins sont canadiens.` — one female neighbor is french and two male neighbors are canadian. ### guided analysis 1. find four indefinite articles and explain why each is masculine, feminine, or plural. 2. find three adjectives placed before nouns. 3. find five adjectives placed after nouns. 4. explain the endings in `petites chambres`, `chaises rouges`, and `voisins canadiens`. 5. replace `appartement` with `maison` and rewrite marc's first two answers with the required changes. 1. four indefinite articles: `un nouvel appartement` and `un petit appartement` are masculine singular; `un quartier` is masculine singular; `une grande chambre` is feminine singular; `des chaises rouges` is plural. any four count if you explain the gender or number. 2. three adjectives before nouns: `nouvel`, `petit`, `grande`, and `petites`. 3. five adjectives after nouns: `moderne`, `tranquille`, `blanche`, `noire`, `rouges`, `française`, and `canadiens`. any five count. 4. `petites chambres` is feminine plural because `chambres` is feminine plural. `chaises rouges` is feminine plural. `voisins canadiens` is masculine plural. 5. with `maison`: `oui. c'est une petite maison moderne dans un quartier tranquille.` then: `il y a une grande chambre et deux petites chambres.` the first answer changes because `maison` is feminine; the second answer can stay the same. the main rule is agreement: when `appartement` changes to `maison`, words attached to that noun must become feminine. ## connected reading: mon quartier `j'habite dans une petite rue près du centre-ville. la rue a de vieilles maisons blanches et de nouveaux appartements modernes. mon immeuble a un grand jardin partagé. une famille canadienne habite au premier étage, et deux étudiantes françaises habitent au deuxième étage. les voisins sont sympathiques. le samedi, nous prenons un café dans le petit jardin.` — i live on a small street near downtown. the street has old white houses and new modern apartments. my building has a large shared garden. a canadian family lives on the first floor, and two french female students live on the second floor. the neighbors are friendly. on saturdays, we have coffee in the small garden. ### reading practice 1. list every noun with its article. 2. match every adjective to the noun it describes. 3. change `une petite rue` to plural. 4. change `une famille canadienne` to a masculine singular person and then to a mixed plural group. 5. write three true sentences about your own neighborhood using the reading as a model. 1. nouns with articles include `une petite rue`, `du centre-ville`, `la rue`, `de vieilles maisons blanches`, `de nouveaux appartements modernes`, `mon immeuble`, `un grand jardin partagé`, `une famille canadienne`, `au premier étage`, `deux étudiantes françaises`, `au deuxième étage`, `les voisins`, `le samedi`, `un café`, and `le petit jardin`. 2. adjective matches: `petite` → `rue`; `vieilles` and `blanches` → `maisons`; `nouveaux` and `modernes` → `appartements`; `grand` and `partagé` → `jardin`; `canadienne` → `famille`; `françaises` → `étudiantes`; `sympathiques` → `voisins`; `petit` → `jardin`. 3. `une petite rue` becomes `des petites rues`. 4. masculine singular person: `un étudiant canadien`. mixed plural group: `des étudiants canadiens`. 5. answers vary. a model sentence is `j'habite dans une rue calme près d'un petit parc.` for your own sentences, copy the structure, not the exact content. every adjective must point to a noun and agree with it. ## chapter reference sheet ### articles | meaning | masculine singular | feminine singular | before vowel sound | plural | |---|---|---|---|---| | the | `le` | `la` | `l'` | `les` | | a/an/some | `un` | `une` | — | `des` | ### regular adjective agreement | form | typical ending | example | |---|---|---| | masculine singular | base form | `petit` | | feminine singular | `-e` | `petite` | | masculine/mixed plural | `-s` | `petits` | | feminine plural | `-es` | `petites` | ### core habits - memorize article + noun together. - check article, noun, and adjective as one agreement group. - learn adjective position as part of a useful phrase. - read complete phrases aloud because articles often reveal gender and number in speech. ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-10 sentences describing a room, neighborhood, classroom, or group of people. include: - at least eight nouns with articles; - masculine and feminine nouns; - singular and plural forms; - six descriptive adjectives; - two adjectives before nouns and four after nouns; - two colors; - one nationality adjective; - one mixed group using a masculine plural adjective. after writing: 1. circle every article; 2. underline every noun; 3. draw an arrow from every adjective to its noun; 4. check gender and number agreement; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary, so mark against the checklist. model paragraph: `j'habite dans un petit appartement près d'une grande école. le salon a une table noire, deux chaises rouges et un vieux canapé confortable. la cuisine est blanche et très calme. dans ma chambre, il y a un bureau moderne et des livres français. mes voisins canadiens sont sympathiques. marie et paul sont contents de vivre dans le quartier. le jardin partagé est joli au printemps. les fenêtres sont grandes et lumineuses.` how it satisfies the brief: nouns with articles include `un petit appartement`, `une grande école`, `le salon`, `une table`, `deux chaises`, `un vieux canapé`, `la cuisine`, `ma chambre`, `un bureau`, `des livres`, `mes voisins`, `le quartier`, `le jardin`, and `les fenêtres`; it includes masculine, feminine, singular, and plural nouns; adjectives include `petit`, `grande`, `noire`, `rouges`, `vieux`, `confortable`, `blanche`, `calme`, `moderne`, `français`, `canadiens`, `sympathiques`, `contents`, `partagé`, `joli`, `grandes`, and `lumineuses`; colors are `noire`, `rouges`, and `blanche`; `canadiens` is the nationality adjective; `marie et paul sont contents` shows a mixed group with masculine plural agreement. mark yourself wrong if: - a noun has no article where one is required; - a color or nationality adjective does not agree; - all adjectives come after nouns, with none before; - a mixed group has feminine plural agreement; - you capitalize a nationality adjective such as `canadiens`. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/practice/writing-drills.md","title":"Handwriting and Active-Recall Drills","type":"Writing","html":"<h1 id=\"handwriting-and-active-recall-drills\">Handwriting and Active-Recall Drills</h1>\n<p>Use paper if possible. Do not copy while looking. Study the model, cover it, write it from memory, then compare.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-1-article-noun-chunks\">Drill 1: Article + noun chunks</h2>\n<p>Write each chunk three times. On the third repetition, cover the model.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le quartier</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la cuisine</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'appartement</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m.</code>)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'université</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f.</code>)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les fenêtres</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un voisin</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voisine</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then write the English meaning beside each phrase without using a dictionary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-2-four-corner-agreement\">Drill 2: Four-corner agreement</h2>\n<p>For each adjective, build all four forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"model\">Model</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit jardin</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite chambre</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petits jardins</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites chambres</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"your-turn\">Your turn</h3>\n<p>Use these adjectives:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Choose your own masculine and feminine nouns.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-3-singular-to-plural-transformations\">Drill 3: Singular-to-plural transformations</h2>\n<p>Write the singular phrase, cover it, and produce the plural:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chaise rouge</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel étudiant</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le beau tableau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la petite rue</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un animal actif</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"drill-4-dictation\">Drill 4: Dictation</h2>\n<p>Have your computer or pronunciation extension read these aloud. Do not look while writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande maison blanche</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants français</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieux quartier tranquille</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nouvelles voitures rouges</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une amie canadienne</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check articles, accents, adjective position, gender, and plural endings separately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-5-memory-sentences\">Drill 5: Memory sentences</h2>\n<p>Study each sentence for fifteen seconds. Hide it and write it from memory:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La petite bibliothèque est dans une rue tranquille.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les nouveaux étudiants ont des cahiers bleus.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Une voisine française habite dans le vieil immeuble.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Circle every article. Underline every noun. Draw an arrow from each adjective to its noun.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-6-personal-production\">Drill 6: Personal production</h2>\n<p>Write six original sentences:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe one room.</li>\n<li>Describe one person.</li>\n<li>Describe two objects.</li>\n<li>Use a color.</li>\n<li>Use a nationality.</li>\n<li>Use one adjective before the noun and another after it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Read the sentences aloud and correct them before adding new vocabulary.</p>","search":"# handwriting and active-recall drills use paper if possible. do not copy while looking. study the model, cover it, write it from memory, then compare. ## drill 1: article + noun chunks write each chunk three times. on the third repetition, cover the model. 1. `le quartier` 2. `la cuisine` 3. `l'appartement` (`m.`) 4. `l'université` (`f.`) 5. `les fenêtres` 6. `un voisin` 7. `une voisine` 8. `des étudiants` then write the english meaning beside each phrase without using a dictionary. ## drill 2: four-corner agreement for each adjective, build all four forms. ### model `petit` 1. `un petit jardin` 2. `une petite chambre` 3. `des petits jardins` 4. `des petites chambres` ### your turn use these adjectives: 1. `grand` 2. `sportif` 3. `heureux` 4. `canadien` choose your own masculine and feminine nouns. ## drill 3: singular-to-plural transformations write the singular phrase, cover it, and produce the plural: 1. `une chaise rouge` 2. `un nouvel étudiant` 3. `le beau tableau` 4. `la petite rue` 5. `un animal actif` ## drill 4: dictation have your computer or pronunciation extension read these aloud. do not look while writing: 1. `une grande maison blanche` 2. `des étudiants français` 3. `un vieux quartier tranquille` 4. `les nouvelles voitures rouges` 5. `une amie canadienne` check articles, accents, adjective position, gender, and plural endings separately. ## drill 5: memory sentences study each sentence for fifteen seconds. hide it and write it from memory: 1. `la petite bibliothèque est dans une rue tranquille.` 2. `les nouveaux étudiants ont des cahiers bleus.` 3. `une voisine française habite dans le vieil immeuble.` circle every article. underline every noun. draw an arrow from each adjective to its noun. ## drill 6: personal production write six original sentences: 1. describe one room. 2. describe one person. 3. describe two objects. 4. use a color. 5. use a nationality. 6. use one adjective before the noun and another after it. read the sentences aloud and correct them before adding new vocabulary. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/practice/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 1 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-1-exercises\">Chapter 1 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Do these without notes. Mark uncertain answers with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code> rather than guessing silently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-choose-the-definite-article\">A. Choose the definite article</h2>\n<p>Fill in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>___ restaurant</li>\n<li>___ gare</li>\n<li>___ école</li>\n<li>___ hôtels</li>\n<li>___ problème</li>\n<li>___ solution</li>\n<li>___ arbres</li>\n<li>___ histoire</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">restaurant</code> is masculine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gare</code> is feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">école</code> begins with a vowel sound, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> contracts.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtels</code> is plural, even though the <strong>h</strong> is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">problème</code> is masculine despite ending in <strong>-e</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">solution</code> is feminine.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arbres</code> is plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">histoire</code> begins with a silent <strong>h</strong>, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> contracts.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The trap is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>: it hides gender. You still need to learn whether the noun is masculine or feminine.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-choose-the-indefinite-article\">B. Choose the indefinite article</h2>\n<p>Fill in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>___ exercice</li>\n<li>___ question</li>\n<li>___ amis</li>\n<li>___ téléphone</li>\n<li>___ adresse</li>\n<li>___ voitures</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercice</code> is masculine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">question</code> is feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amis</code> is plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code> is masculine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">adresse</code> is feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voitures</code> is plural.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Indefinite articles mean a/an/some. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> for plural, not a plural form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-make-the-phrase-plural\">C. Make the phrase plural</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite fenêtre</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un tableau moderne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nouvel appartement</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voix française</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un animal heureux</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites fenêtres</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>, and both noun and adjective become plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des tableaux modernes</code> — nouns ending in <strong>-eau</strong> often take <strong>-x</strong> in the plural; the adjective takes <strong>-s</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nouveaux appartements</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> changes back to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveaux</code> in the masculine plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voix françaises</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voix</code> already ends in <strong>x</strong>, so the noun spelling does not change; the adjective becomes plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des animaux heureux</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animal</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animaux</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> already ends in <strong>x</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check the whole noun phrase: article, noun, and adjective. Changing only one part is the most common error.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-make-the-adjective-agree\">D. Make the adjective agree</h2>\n<p>Use the adjective in parentheses.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>une fille ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code>)</li>\n<li>des maisons ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanc</code>)</li>\n<li>un homme ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code>)</li>\n<li>des étudiantes ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code>)</li>\n<li>une rue ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code>)</li>\n<li>des garçons ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif</code>)</li>\n<li>une ___ voisine (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code>)</li>\n<li>un ___ hôtel (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code>)</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fille</code> is feminine singular, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanches</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maisons</code> is feminine plural, so add feminine <strong>-e</strong> and plural <strong>-s</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> — masculine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">homme</code> uses the base form; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> already ends in <strong>x</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiennes</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiantes</code> is feminine plural, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiennes</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code> — this adjective already ends in <strong>-e</strong>, so masculine and feminine singular look the same.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actifs</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">garçons</code> is masculine plural, so add <strong>-s</strong> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvelle</code> — before feminine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voisine</code>, use the feminine form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code> — masculine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code> begins with a silent <strong>h</strong>, so use the special before-vowel form.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The trap is assuming agreement always just adds <strong>-e</strong>. Several adjectives change spelling more deeply.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-put-the-words-in-a-natural-order\">E. Put the words in a natural order</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rouge / une / voiture</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appartement / un / beau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficiles / des / exercices</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ville / grande / une</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">françaises / étudiantes / des</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture rouge</code> — most color adjectives follow the noun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> comes before the noun and becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bel</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des exercices difficiles</code> — most descriptive adjectives follow the noun and agree in plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande ville</code> — common size adjectives such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code> often come before the noun and agree.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes françaises</code> — nationality adjectives normally follow the noun and agree.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Position and agreement are separate checks. First put the adjective in the natural place, then make it match the noun.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-correct-the-mistake\">F. Correct the mistake</h2>\n<p>Each phrase contains one error.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le maison</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une garçon</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voiture</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une sac noire</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un belle jardin</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadien</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chambre blancs</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouveau ami</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code> is feminine, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> is wrong.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un garçon</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">garçon</code> is masculine, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> is wrong.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voitures</code> — plural noun and plural article are both required.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sac noir</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sac</code> is masculine, so both article and adjective must be masculine.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un beau jardin</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jardin</code> is masculine, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadiennes</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiantes</code> is feminine plural, so the nationality adjective must be feminine plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chambre blanche</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chambre</code> is feminine singular, so the color must be feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel ami</code> — masculine <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ami</code> begins with a vowel sound, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Every error here is an agreement or article error. Correct the whole phrase, not just the word that first looks wrong.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"g-translate-into-french\">G. Translate into French</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>a small house</li>\n<li>the blue books</li>\n<li>a French student (female)</li>\n<li>some old neighborhoods</li>\n<li>the new hotel</li>\n<li>some happy Canadian women</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Reasonable answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code> is feminine, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> agrees with it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres bleus</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">livres</code> is masculine plural, so the color is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bleus</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante française</code> — female student requires feminine article and feminine nationality adjective.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de vieux quartiers</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code> comes before the plural noun, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> commonly becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nouvel hôtel</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hôtel</code> begins with a silent <strong>h</strong>, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> before it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des Canadiennes heureuses</code> — as a noun for people, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Canadiennes</code> is capitalized; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuses</code> agrees feminine plural.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Item 4 is the advanced-looking trap: before a plural adjective placed before the noun, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> often becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"h-guided-writing\">H. Guided writing</h2>\n<p>Write a four-sentence description of an apartment. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least four nouns with articles;</li>\n<li>one singular and one plural noun;</li>\n<li>two colors;</li>\n<li>one adjective before a noun;</li>\n<li>two adjectives after nouns.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model four-sentence description:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite dans un petit appartement.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une cuisine blanche et un salon moderne.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les grandes fenêtres sont près d'une table noire.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La chambre est calme.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-marking criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At least four nouns have articles: for example <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un appartement</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une cuisine</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un salon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les fenêtres</code>.</li>\n<li>There is at least one singular noun and one plural noun.</li>\n<li>Two colors appear and agree, such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanche</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code>.</li>\n<li>At least one adjective comes before a noun, such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grandes</code>.</li>\n<li>At least two adjectives come after nouns, such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanche</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moderne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if an adjective has the right meaning but the wrong gender or number.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"i-mixed-mastery-check\">I. Mixed mastery check</h2>\n<p>Choose the correct option.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ université</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le / la / l'</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une femme ___</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien / canadienne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ___ maisons</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand / grande / grandes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ___ immeuble</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau / nouvel / nouvelle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des sacs ___</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noir / noire / noirs</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ animaux</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un / une / des</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une leçon ___</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile / difficiles</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Anne sont ___</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse / heureux / heureuses</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">université</code> begins with a vowel sound, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> contracts.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">femme</code> is feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grandes</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maisons</code> is feminine plural, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code> comes before the noun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> — masculine <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">immeuble</code> begins with a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noirs</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sacs</code> is masculine plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">animaux</code> is plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leçon</code> is feminine singular, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile</code> already ends in <strong>-e</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> — a mixed group uses masculine plural agreement; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> already ends in <strong>x</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed an item, name the category: article, plural, adjective agreement, or special before-vowel adjective form.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 1 exercises do these without notes. mark uncertain answers with `?` rather than guessing silently. ## a. choose the definite article fill in `le`, `la`, `l'`, or `les`. 1. ___ restaurant 2. ___ gare 3. ___ école 4. ___ hôtels 5. ___ problème 6. ___ solution 7. ___ arbres 8. ___ histoire 1. `le` — `restaurant` is masculine singular. 2. `la` — `gare` is feminine singular. 3. `l'` — `école` begins with a vowel sound, so `la` contracts. 4. `les` — `hôtels` is plural, even though the **h** is silent. 5. `le` — `problème` is masculine despite ending in **-e**. 6. `la` — `solution` is feminine. 7. `les` — `arbres` is plural. 8. `l'` — `histoire` begins with a silent **h**, so `la` contracts. the trap is `l'`: it hides gender. you still need to learn whether the noun is masculine or feminine. ## b. choose the indefinite article fill in `un`, `une`, or `des`. 1. ___ exercice 2. ___ question 3. ___ amis 4. ___ téléphone 5. ___ adresse 6. ___ voitures 1. `un` — `exercice` is masculine singular. 2. `une` — `question` is feminine singular. 3. `des` — `amis` is plural. 4. `un` — `téléphone` is masculine singular. 5. `une` — `adresse` is feminine singular. 6. `des` — `voitures` is plural. indefinite articles mean a/an/some. use `des` for plural, not a plural form of `un` or `une`. ## c. make the phrase plural 1. `une petite fenêtre` 2. `un tableau moderne` 3. `le nouvel appartement` 4. `la voix française` 5. `un animal heureux` 1. `des petites fenêtres` — `une` becomes `des`, and both noun and adjective become plural. 2. `des tableaux modernes` — nouns ending in **-eau** often take **-x** in the plural; the adjective takes **-s**. 3. `les nouveaux appartements` — `nouvel` changes back to `nouveaux` in the masculine plural. 4. `les voix françaises` — `voix` already ends in **x**, so the noun spelling does not change; the adjective becomes plural. 5. `des animaux heureux` — `animal` becomes `animaux`; `heureux` already ends in **x**. check the whole noun phrase: article, noun, and adjective. changing only one part is the most common error. ## d. make the adjective agree use the adjective in parentheses. 1. une fille ___ (`sportif`) 2. des maisons ___ (`blanc`) 3. un homme ___ (`heureux`) 4. des étudiantes ___ (`canadien`) 5. une rue ___ (`calme`) 6. des garçons ___ (`actif`) 7. une ___ voisine (`nouveau`) 8. un ___ hôtel (`vieux`) 1. `sportive` — `fille` is feminine singular, so `sportif` becomes `sportive`. 2. `blanches` — `maisons` is feminine plural, so add feminine **-e** and plural **-s**. 3. `heureux` — masculine singular `homme` uses the base form; `heureux` already ends in **x**. 4. `canadiennes` — `étudiantes` is feminine plural, so `canadien` becomes `canadiennes`. 5. `calme` — this adjective already ends in **-e**, so masculine and feminine singular look the same. 6. `actifs` — `garçons` is masculine plural, so add **-s** to `actif`. 7. `nouvelle` — before feminine singular `voisine`, use the feminine form of `nouveau`. 8. `vieil` — masculine singular `hôtel` begins with a silent **h**, so use the special before-vowel form. the trap is assuming agreement always just adds **-e**. several adjectives change spelling more deeply. ## e. put the words in a natural order 1. `rouge / une / voiture` 2. `appartement / un / beau` 3. `difficiles / des / exercices` 4. `ville / grande / une` 5. `françaises / étudiantes / des` 1. `une voiture rouge` — most color adjectives follow the noun. 2. `un bel appartement` — `beau` comes before the noun and becomes `bel` before a vowel sound. 3. `des exercices difficiles` — most descriptive adjectives follow the noun and agree in plural. 4. `une grande ville` — common size adjectives such as `grand` often come before the noun and agree. 5. `des étudiantes françaises` — nationality adjectives normally follow the noun and agree. position and agreement are separate checks. first put the adjective in the natural place, then make it match the noun. ## f. correct the mistake each phrase contains one error. 1. `le maison` 2. `une garçon` 3. `des voiture` 4. `une sac noire` 5. `un belle jardin` 6. `des étudiantes canadien` 7. `une chambre blancs` 8. `un nouveau ami` 1. `la maison` — `maison` is feminine, so `le` is wrong. 2. `un garçon` — `garçon` is masculine, so `une` is wrong. 3. `des voitures` — plural noun and plural article are both required. 4. `un sac noir` — `sac` is masculine, so both article and adjective must be masculine. 5. `un beau jardin` — `jardin` is masculine, so use `un` and `beau`. 6. `des étudiantes canadiennes` — `étudiantes` is feminine plural, so the nationality adjective must be feminine plural. 7. `une chambre blanche` — `chambre` is feminine singular, so the color must be feminine singular. 8. `un nouvel ami` — masculine `ami` begins with a vowel sound, so `nouveau` becomes `nouvel`. every error here is an agreement or article error. correct the whole phrase, not just the word that first looks wrong. ## g. translate into french 1. a small house 2. the blue books 3. a french student (female) 4. some old neighborhoods 5. the new hotel 6. some happy canadian women reasonable answers: 1. `une petite maison` — `maison` is feminine, and `petite` agrees with it. 2. `les livres bleus` — `livres` is masculine plural, so the color is `bleus`. 3. `une étudiante française` — female student requires feminine article and feminine nationality adjective. 4. `de vieux quartiers` — `vieux` comes before the plural noun, so `des` commonly becomes `de`. 5. `le nouvel hôtel` — `hôtel` begins with a silent **h**, so use `nouvel` before it. 6. `des canadiennes heureuses` — as a noun for people, `canadiennes` is capitalized; `heureuses` agrees feminine plural. item 4 is the advanced-looking trap: before a plural adjective placed before the noun, `des` often becomes `de`. ## h. guided writing write a four-sentence description of an apartment. include: - at least four nouns with articles; - one singular and one plural noun; - two colors; - one adjective before a noun; - two adjectives after nouns. answers vary. a model four-sentence description: 1. `j'habite dans un petit appartement.` 2. `il y a une cuisine blanche et un salon moderne.` 3. `les grandes fenêtres sont près d'une table noire.` 4. `la chambre est calme.` self-marking criteria: - at least four nouns have articles: for example `un appartement`, `une cuisine`, `un salon`, `les fenêtres`. - there is at least one singular noun and one plural noun. - two colors appear and agree, such as `blanche` and `noire`. - at least one adjective comes before a noun, such as `petit` or `grandes`. - at least two adjectives come after nouns, such as `blanche`, `moderne`, `noire`, or `calme`. mark yourself wrong if an adjective has the right meaning but the wrong gender or number. ## i. mixed mastery check choose the correct option. 1. `___ université` — `le / la / l'` 2. `une femme ___` — `canadien / canadienne` 3. `des ___ maisons` — `grand / grande / grandes` 4. `un ___ immeuble` — `nouveau / nouvel / nouvelle` 5. `des sacs ___` — `noir / noire / noirs` 6. `___ animaux` — `un / une / des` 7. `une leçon ___` — `difficile / difficiles` 8. `paul et anne sont ___` — `heureuse / heureux / heureuses` 1. `l'` — `université` begins with a vowel sound, so `la` contracts. 2. `canadienne` — `femme` is feminine singular. 3. `grandes` — `maisons` is feminine plural, and `grand` comes before the noun. 4. `nouvel` — masculine `immeuble` begins with a vowel sound. 5. `noirs` — `sacs` is masculine plural. 6. `des` — `animaux` is plural. 7. `difficile` — `leçon` is feminine singular, but `difficile` already ends in **-e**. 8. `heureux` — a mixed group uses masculine plural agreement; `heureux` already ends in **x**. if you missed an item, name the category: article, plural, adjective agreement, or special before-vowel adjective form. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/practice/answer-key.md","title":"Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"answer-key\">Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-retrieval-checks\">Lesson retrieval checks</h2>\n<h3 id=\"lesson-1\">Lesson 1</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le musée</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la question</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hôtel</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m.</code>)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les étudiants</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"lesson-2\">Lesson 2</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des photos</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des bureaux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les journaux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voix</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"lesson-3\">Lesson 3</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Masculine</th>\n<th>Feminine</th>\n<th>Feminine plural</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportives</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuses</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italiennes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calmes</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"lesson-4\">Lesson 4</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture noire</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante canadienne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des maisons blanches</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-a\">Exercise A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-b\">Exercise B</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-c\">Exercise C</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites fenêtres</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des tableaux modernes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nouveaux appartements</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voix françaises</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des animaux heureux</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-d\">Exercise D</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanches</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiennes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actifs</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvelle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-e\">Exercise E</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture rouge</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des exercices difficiles</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande ville</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes françaises</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-f\">Exercise F</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un garçon</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voitures</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sac noir</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un beau jardin</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadiennes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chambre blanche</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel ami</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-g\">Exercise G</h2>\n<p>Reasonable answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres bleus</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante française</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de vieux quartiers</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nouvel hôtel</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des Canadiennes heureuses</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>In item 4, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> commonly becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> before a plural adjective placed before the noun.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-i\">Exercise I</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grandes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noirs</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"scoring\">Scoring</h2>\n<p>For Sections A-G and I, award one point per item: <strong>46 points total</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>42-46: ready for the writing task and delayed review</li>\n<li>36-41: review only the missed rule, then retry missed items</li>\n<li>28-35: repeat the relevant lessons and handwriting drill</li>\n<li>Below 28: divide the chapter into smaller two-day blocks</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not count a corrected answer as mastered until you can answer it correctly the following day.</p>","search":"# answer key ## lesson retrieval checks ### lesson 1 1. `le musée` 2. `la question` 3. `l'hôtel` (`m.`) 4. `les étudiants` ### lesson 2 1. `des photos` 2. `des bureaux` 3. `les journaux` 4. `les voix` ### lesson 3 | masculine | feminine | feminine plural | |---|---|---| | `sportif` | `sportive` | `sportives` | | `heureux` | `heureuse` | `heureuses` | | `italien` | `italienne` | `italiennes` | | `calme` | `calme` | `calmes` | ### lesson 4 1. `une voiture noire` 2. `un bel appartement` 3. `une étudiante canadienne` 4. `des maisons blanches` ## exercise a 1. `le` 2. `la` 3. `l'` 4. `les` 5. `le` 6. `la` 7. `les` 8. `l'` ## exercise b 1. `un` 2. `une` 3. `des` 4. `un` 5. `une` 6. `des` ## exercise c 1. `des petites fenêtres` 2. `des tableaux modernes` 3. `les nouveaux appartements` 4. `les voix françaises` 5. `des animaux heureux` ## exercise d 1. `sportive` 2. `blanches` 3. `heureux` 4. `canadiennes` 5. `calme` 6. `actifs` 7. `nouvelle` 8. `vieil` ## exercise e 1. `une voiture rouge` 2. `un bel appartement` 3. `des exercices difficiles` 4. `une grande ville` 5. `des étudiantes françaises` ## exercise f 1. `la maison` 2. `un garçon` 3. `des voitures` 4. `un sac noir` 5. `un beau jardin` 6. `des étudiantes canadiennes` 7. `une chambre blanche` 8. `un nouvel ami` ## exercise g reasonable answers: 1. `une petite maison` 2. `les livres bleus` 3. `une étudiante française` 4. `de vieux quartiers` 5. `le nouvel hôtel` 6. `des canadiennes heureuses` in item 4, `des` commonly becomes `de` before a plural adjective placed before the noun. ## exercise i 1. `l'` 2. `canadienne` 3. `grandes` 4. `nouvel` 5. `noirs` 6. `des` 7. `difficile` 8. `heureux` ## scoring for sections a-g and i, award one point per item: **46 points total**. - 42-46: ready for the writing task and delayed review - 36-41: review only the missed rule, then retry missed items - 28-35: repeat the relevant lessons and handwriting drill - below 28: divide the chapter into smaller two-day blocks do not count a corrected answer as mastered until you can answer it correctly the following day. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/review/7-day-plan.md","title":"Seven-Day Study Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"seven-day-study-plan\">Seven-Day Study Plan</h1>\n<p>Aim for 30-40 focused minutes per day. Stop before concentration drops.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-1-gender-and-definite-articles\">Day 1: Gender and definite articles</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read Lesson 1.</li>\n<li>Say every example twice.</li>\n<li>Complete Writing Drill 1.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercise A.</li>\n<li>Record errors.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-2-indefinite-articles-and-plurals\">Day 2: Indefinite articles and plurals</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Recall yesterday's four definite articles before opening notes.</li>\n<li>Read Lesson 2.</li>\n<li>Complete Writing Drill 3.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercises B and C.</li>\n<li>Review only yesterday's errors.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-3-adjective-agreement\">Day 3: Adjective agreement</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the four forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> from memory.</li>\n<li>Read Lesson 3.</li>\n<li>Complete Writing Drill 2.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercise D.</li>\n<li>Read answers aloud after correction.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-4-position-colors-and-nationalities\">Day 4: Position, colors, and nationalities</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Recall three adjectives that usually precede nouns.</li>\n<li>Read Lesson 4.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercises E and F.</li>\n<li>Do Writing Drill 4 as dictation.</li>\n<li>Add errors to the log.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-5-production-day\">Day 5: Production day</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Complete Exercise G.</li>\n<li>Complete Writing Drills 5 and 6.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercise H.</li>\n<li>Read your paragraph aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Correct only after finishing the full paragraph.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-6-listening-and-pronunciation\">Day 6: Listening and pronunciation</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">audio-script.md</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tts-narration.txt</code>.</li>\n<li>Shadow ten French phrases: listen, pause, and imitate rhythm.</li>\n<li>Write five heard phrases as dictation.</li>\n<li>Compare your spelling and agreement.</li>\n<li>Repeat difficult phrases slowly, then at natural speed.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-7-closed-book-mastery-check\">Day 7: Closed-book mastery check</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Complete Exercise I.</li>\n<li>Redo every item previously entered in the error log.</li>\n<li>Write five original sentences.</li>\n<li>Explain two corrections aloud in English.</li>\n<li>Schedule reviews for Day 10, Day 14, and Day 30.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"delayed-reviews\">Delayed reviews</h2>\n<p>At each delayed review:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the article table from memory.</li>\n<li>Transform two singular phrases into plural.</li>\n<li>Produce all four forms of one adjective.</li>\n<li>Write two original descriptive sentences.</li>\n<li>Redo only errors and uncertain items.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# seven-day study plan aim for 30-40 focused minutes per day. stop before concentration drops. ## day 1: gender and definite articles 1. read lesson 1. 2. say every example twice. 3. complete writing drill 1. 4. complete exercise a. 5. record errors. ## day 2: indefinite articles and plurals 1. recall yesterday's four definite articles before opening notes. 2. read lesson 2. 3. complete writing drill 3. 4. complete exercises b and c. 5. review only yesterday's errors. ## day 3: adjective agreement 1. write the four forms of `petit` from memory. 2. read lesson 3. 3. complete writing drill 2. 4. complete exercise d. 5. read answers aloud after correction. ## day 4: position, colors, and nationalities 1. recall three adjectives that usually precede nouns. 2. read lesson 4. 3. complete exercises e and f. 4. do writing drill 4 as dictation. 5. add errors to the log. ## day 5: production day 1. complete exercise g. 2. complete writing drills 5 and 6. 3. complete exercise h. 4. read your paragraph aloud twice. 5. correct only after finishing the full paragraph. ## day 6: listening and pronunciation 1. use `audio-script.md` or `tts-narration.txt`. 2. shadow ten french phrases: listen, pause, and imitate rhythm. 3. write five heard phrases as dictation. 4. compare your spelling and agreement. 5. repeat difficult phrases slowly, then at natural speed. ## day 7: closed-book mastery check 1. complete exercise i. 2. redo every item previously entered in the error log. 3. write five original sentences. 4. explain two corrections aloud in english. 5. schedule reviews for day 10, day 14, and day 30. ## delayed reviews at each delayed review: 1. write the article table from memory. 2. transform two singular phrases into plural. 3. produce all four forms of one adjective. 4. write two original descriptive sentences. 5. redo only errors and uncertain items. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/audio-script.md","title":"Chapter 1 Audio Lesson: French Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives","type":"Audio","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-1-audio-lesson-french-nouns-articles-and-adjectives\">Chapter 1 Audio Lesson: French Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives</h1>\n<p><strong>Format:</strong> Text-to-speech teaching script <strong>Audience:</strong> Complete beginners <strong>Suggested duration:</strong> 25–35 minutes <strong>Use:</strong> Companion lesson for <em>Easy French Step-by-Step</em>, Chapter 1</p>\n<h2 id=\"production-notes\">Production notes</h2>\n<p>This is an original companion lesson. It teaches the same foundational topics as Chapter 1 but does not read or reproduce the textbook. The narration should be calm and deliberate, with a short pause after every French phrase and a longer pause wherever <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">[PAUSE]</code> appears.</p>\n<p>Recommended voice settings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Native or highly fluent French pronunciation for French examples</li>\n<li>Slightly slower-than-normal pace for new vocabulary</li>\n<li>English explanation followed by French example</li>\n<li>Repeat every French phrase twice</li>\n<li>Leave 3–5 seconds after prompts so the learner can answer</li>\n<li>Use a different voice or tone for “Your turn”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whenever a French example appears by itself, the English meaning is now written immediately after an em dash. Practice prompts intentionally remain untranslated until after the pause so the learner can test comprehension.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"opening\">Opening</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Welcome to your first French grammar and listening lesson.</p>\n<p>Today we will build the foundation for describing people, places, and things. We will learn six connected ideas:</p>\n<p>First, French nouns have grammatical gender.</p>\n<p>Second, nouns can be singular or plural.</p>\n<p>Third, French uses articles, such as “the,” “a,” and “some.”</p>\n<p>Fourth, articles must agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>Fifth, descriptive adjectives usually agree with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>And sixth, adjectives can appear before or after the noun, depending on the adjective and sometimes on the meaning.</p>\n<p>Do not try to memorize everything in one sitting. Your goal today is to understand the system and begin recognizing its patterns.</p>\n<p>When you hear “Your turn,” pause the audio and say the answer aloud.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Let’s begin.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-1-what-is-a-noun\">Part 1: What is a noun?</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>A noun is a word for a person, place, object, animal, or idea.</p>\n<p>In English, nouns do not normally have grammatical gender. In French, every noun is classified as either masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>This grammatical gender is a property of the French word. It does not always correspond to the physical sex of the thing.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le téléphone</code> — the telephone.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voiture</code> — the car.</p>\n<p>The book is masculine in French. The table is feminine. The telephone is masculine. The car is feminine.</p>\n<p>These labels are part of French vocabulary. Learn the noun together with its article whenever possible.</p>\n<p>Do not learn only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">livre</code>. Learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code>.</p>\n<p>Do not learn only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voiture</code>. Learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voiture</code>.</p>\n<p>Say them with me.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Again: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voiture</code> — the car.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Again: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voiture</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le téléphone</code> — the telephone.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Again: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le téléphone</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Again: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code>.</p>\n<p>This habit will help you remember gender naturally.</p>\n<p>Think of the article as part of the noun's identity, not as a separate decoration. In English, you can often learn “table” by itself. In French, the useful unit is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code>. When you later build a sentence, the article gives you the first signal about gender and number.</p>\n<p>This also makes listening easier. Even if the noun is unfamiliar, the sound of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code> gives you an immediate grammatical clue. The more often you hear the complete phrase, the more automatic that clue becomes.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-2-the-definite-article-the\">Part 2: The definite article: “the”</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>The definite article is the French equivalent of “the.”</p>\n<p>For a singular masculine noun, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book.</p>\n<p>For a singular feminine noun, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table.</p>\n<p>For a plural noun, whether masculine or feminine, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres</code> — the books.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les tables</code> — the tables.</p>\n<p>There is also a shorter form, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code>, used before a vowel sound or before most words beginning with a silent “h.”</p>\n<p>This shortening is called elision. French avoids putting two vowel sounds next to each other when the article comes before a vowel-starting word. Instead of saying <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la amie</code>, French removes the final vowel of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> and writes and pronounces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’amie</code>.</p>\n<p>The important point is that elision changes the sound and spelling of the article, but it does not remove the noun's gender. You still need to remember whether the noun is masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’ami</code> — the friend, masculine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’amie</code> — the friend, feminine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’hôtel</code> — the hotel.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’idée</code> — the idea.</p>\n<p>Notice that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code> does not tell you whether the noun is masculine or feminine. You must memorize the gender separately.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’ami</code> is masculine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’amie</code> is feminine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’hôtel</code> is masculine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’idée</code> is feminine.</p>\n<p>Say the four forms.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres</code> — the books.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’idée</code> — the idea.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<h3 id=\"quick-recognition-practice\">Quick recognition practice</h3>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>I will say a noun phrase. Identify the article and the number.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code> — the house.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is feminine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les maisons</code> — the houses.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is plural. The noun could refer to a group of feminine houses, but the plural article itself does not show masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’université</code> — the university.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is singular. The noun is feminine, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code> hides the gender in pronunciation.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le projet</code> — the project.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is masculine singular.</p>\n<p>Notice the process: first identify the article, then ask whether the noun is singular or plural, and finally recall the noun's gender. Do not guess gender from the English translation. French gender belongs to the French word.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-3-how-to-learn-noun-gender\">Part 3: How to learn noun gender</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>The safest method is simple: memorize the noun with its article.</p>\n<p>There are patterns that can help, but they are not perfect rules.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-tion</code> are feminine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la décision</code> — the decision.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la conversation</code> — the conversation.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code> are masculine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le document</code> — the document.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le changement</code> — the change.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-isme</code> are masculine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le réalisme</code> — realism.</p>\n<p>However, French has exceptions. Patterns are clues, not guarantees.</p>\n<p>The noun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’eau</code>, meaning “water,” is feminine even though it does not fit the pattern you might expect.</p>\n<p>When a noun refers to a person, the word may change for masculine and feminine forms:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un étudiant</code> — a male student or a student described with the masculine form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante</code> — a female student.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ami</code> — a male friend.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une amie</code> — a female friend.</p>\n<p>Some nouns have the same spelling for a man or a woman. The article tells you the gender:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le journaliste</code> — the male journalist, or the masculine form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la journaliste</code> — the female journalist.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le professeur</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la professeure</code> are both used in modern French, depending on region and style.</p>\n<p>The key learning strategy is still the same: store the article with the noun.</p>\n<p>When you make vocabulary cards, put the article on the front of the card. A card that says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">livre = book</code> trains incomplete knowledge. A better card says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre = the book</code>, and ideally includes a short sentence such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le livre est intéressant</code> — The book is interesting.</p>\n<p>Gender patterns are useful for making an educated guess, but they should not replace memorization. When a new word breaks a pattern, record the complete phrase and move on. Accuracy grows through repeated exposure, not through trying to invent a perfect rule for every noun.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-4-the-indefinite-article-a-an-and-some\">Part 4: The indefinite article: “a,” “an,” and “some”</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>French has three common indefinite articles.</p>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> with a singular masculine noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un livre</code> — a book.</p>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> with a singular feminine noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une table</code> — a table.</p>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> with plural nouns of either gender.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des livres</code> — some books, or books.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des tables</code> — some tables, or tables.</p>\n<p>In natural English, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> is often not translated as “some.” The meaning depends on context.</p>\n<p>Compare the three ideas:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ordinateur</code> introduces one non-specific computer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’ordinateur</code> refers to a specific computer, or to computers as a general category depending on context.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ordinateurs</code> refers to more than one non-specific computer.</p>\n<p>English often leaves the article out when translating plural French phrases. That does not mean the French article is optional. In French, the article is normally part of the complete noun phrase.</p>\n<p>Listen:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J’ai un ordinateur.</code> — I have a computer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J’ai une question.</code> — I have a question.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J’ai des idées.</code> — I have ideas.</p>\n<p>The article must agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>This is a decision-making exercise, not just a pronunciation exercise. Before speaking, ask yourself two questions: Is the noun masculine or feminine? Is it singular or plural? The answer to those two questions determines the article.</p>\n<p>Your turn. Say the correct article.</p>\n<p>A masculine project: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ projet</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un projet</code>.</p>\n<p>A feminine question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ question</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une question</code>.</p>\n<p>Plural ideas: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ idées</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des idées</code>.</p>\n<p>Plural computers: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ ordinateurs</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ordinateurs</code>.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-5-singular-and-plural-nouns\">Part 5: Singular and plural nouns</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Most French nouns form the plural by adding <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> in writing.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un projet</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des projets</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une idée</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des idées</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le client</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les clients</code>.</p>\n<p>Usually, the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> is not pronounced.</p>\n<p>This creates an important difference between written and spoken French. On the page, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">client</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clients</code> look different. In speech, the noun may sound almost identical. The listener often relies on the article—<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>—to recognize the number.</p>\n<p>The difference between singular and plural is often easier to hear in the article:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un client</code> — a client.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des clients</code> — clients or some clients.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le client</code> — the client.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les clients</code> — the clients.</p>\n<p>Some nouns already end in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-x</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-z</code> in the singular. Their spelling stays the same in the plural.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un choix</code> — a choice.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des choix</code> — choices.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un prix</code> — a price.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des prix</code> — prices.</p>\n<p>The article and context show the number.</p>\n<p>Some nouns have special plural spellings:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bureau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des bureaux</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un travail</code> can become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des travaux</code> when referring to tasks or works.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un animal</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des animaux</code>.</p>\n<p>Do not worry about memorizing every irregular plural today. Learn each new plural with its vocabulary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"liaison-with-plural-articles\">Liaison with plural articles</h3>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>When <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> comes before a word beginning with a vowel sound, the final sound of the article may link to the next word.</p>\n<p>The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> is pronounced like a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">z</code>.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code> sounds like “lay zah-mee.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des enfants</code> sounds like “day zahn-fahn.”</p>\n<p>This linking is called liaison.</p>\n<p>Repeat:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code> — the friends.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des enfants</code> — children or some children.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les étudiants</code> — the students.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> of the noun itself is generally silent. The linking sound comes from the article.</p>\n<p>Listen for the boundary between the words. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code>, do not pronounce the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amis</code> as a separate sound. The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">z</code> sound belongs to the article and connects the two words smoothly. This is one reason French can sound as though words run together.</p>\n<p>For now, prioritize recognition over production. First learn to hear that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code> is plural. Later, with repeated shadowing, the liaison will become natural in your own speech.</p>\n<p>Do not force liaison everywhere. First become comfortable recognizing it in common phrases.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-6-adjectives-describe-nouns\">Part 6: Adjectives describe nouns</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>An adjective gives information about a noun.</p>\n<p>It can describe size, color, personality, quality, age, or many other characteristics.</p>\n<p>In French, the adjective usually comes after the noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un projet intéressant</code> — an interesting project.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une idée originale</code> — an original idea.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un problème difficile</code> — a difficult problem.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une solution simple</code> — a simple solution.</p>\n<p>The adjective can also follow the verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, meaning “to be.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le projet est intéressant.</code> — The project is interesting.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La solution est simple.</code> — The solution is simple.</p>\n<p>The most important rule is agreement.</p>\n<p>The adjective agrees with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>In English, the adjective usually stays unchanged: “an interesting project” and “interesting projects.” French makes the relationship visible in writing. The ending of the adjective tells us whether it describes a masculine or feminine noun and whether that noun is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>When you build a French noun phrase, use this order of thought: choose the noun, choose the article, place the adjective in its normal position, and then adjust the adjective to match the noun. The adjective is not agreeing with the English translation; it is agreeing with the French noun.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-7-masculine-and-feminine-adjective-forms\">Part 7: Masculine and feminine adjective forms</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>For many adjectives, the feminine form is created by adding <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> to the masculine form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un client intelligent</code> — an intelligent male client or a masculine phrase.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une cliente intelligente</code> — an intelligent female client.</p>\n<p>The adjective changes from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">intelligent</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">intelligente</code>.</p>\n<p>Another example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un employé fatigué</code> — a tired employee, masculine form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une employée fatiguée</code> — a tired employee, feminine form.</p>\n<p>Repeat:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">intelligent, intelligente</code> — intelligent, masculine and feminine forms.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigué, fatiguée</code> — tired, masculine and feminine forms.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Some adjectives already end in a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> in the masculine form. They do not change spelling in the feminine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un collègue calme</code> — a calm colleague.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une collègue calme</code> — a calm colleague.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un enfant drôle</code> — a funny child.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une enfant drôle</code> — a funny child.</p>\n<p>The adjective is the same in writing. The noun or article gives more information.</p>\n<p>Do not assume that a word ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> is automatically feminine. The ending can be identical in both genders. For example, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code> stays <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code>, but the complete phrases <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un collègue calme</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une collègue calme</code> still show different gender through the article and noun.</p>\n<p>This is another reason to study complete phrases instead of isolated adjective lists. You are training yourself to notice the whole grammatical pattern.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives have special feminine forms:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">active</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieux</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieuse</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">belle</code>.</p>\n<p>Do not try to derive every form from English. Learn the pair together.</p>\n<p>Some changes are predictable, such as adding <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code>, while others involve spelling or pronunciation changes. Treat irregular forms as families: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif, active</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux, heureuse</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau, belle</code>. Say both forms aloud and place each one in a short phrase.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-8-adjective-plurals\">Part 8: Adjective plurals</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>The regular plural of an adjective usually adds <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> in writing.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un projet intéressant</code> — an interesting project.</p>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des projets intéressants</code> — interesting projects.</p>\n<p>For feminine plural:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une solution simple</code> — a simple solution.</p>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des solutions simples</code> — simple solutions.</p>\n<p>Notice that an adjective ending in a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> may look the same in singular masculine and feminine, but the plural usually adds <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code>.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-x</code> do not change their spelling in the plural.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un homme sérieux</code> — a serious man.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des hommes sérieux</code> — serious men.</p>\n<p>The written adjective remains <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieux</code>.</p>\n<p>A useful reading habit is to scan from left to right and ask what each ending is doing. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des hommes sérieux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> signals plural, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hommes</code> is the plural noun, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieux</code> is the adjective that agrees with that plural noun. Even when the adjective's spelling does not visibly change, the grammar is still active.</p>\n<p>If a group contains both masculine and feminine nouns, standard written French normally uses the masculine plural form.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un frère et une sœur intelligents</code> — an intelligent brother and sister.</p>\n<p>The adjective is masculine plural because the group includes a masculine noun.</p>\n<p>For an entirely feminine group:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une mère et une sœur intelligentes</code> — an intelligent mother and sister.</p>\n<p>The adjective is feminine plural.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-9-colors\">Part 9: Colors</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Color words are adjectives, so they usually agree with the noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un écran noir</code> — a black screen.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture noire</code> — a black car.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des écrans noirs</code> — black screens.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voitures noires</code> — black cars.</p>\n<p>Many colors follow the regular pattern:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bleu, bleue</code> — blue.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vert, verte</code> — green.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gris, grise</code> — gray.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanc, blanche</code> — white.</p>\n<p>Some color words do not change for gender or number when used as adjectives.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">orange</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">marron</code> are commonly invariable.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sac orange</code> — an orange bag.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chemise orange</code> — an orange shirt.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des sacs orange</code> — orange bags.</p>\n<p>When a color word is used as a noun, it is masculine:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J’aime le bleu.</code> — I like blue.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le rouge est vif.</code> — Red is vivid.</p>\n<p>For now, focus on using colors as adjectives after nouns.</p>\n<p>Color is an excellent way to practice agreement because the meaning is concrete. Look around you and describe objects you can see. Start with the noun and article, then add the color, and finally check whether the color needs a feminine or plural ending.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-10-adjectives-before-the-noun\">Part 10: Adjectives before the noun</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Most descriptive adjectives follow the noun, but a group of common adjectives usually comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Examples include words related to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>size;</li>\n<li>age;</li>\n<li>beauty;</li>\n<li>goodness;</li>\n<li>quantity or order;</li>\n<li>and certain familiar descriptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Listen to these phrases:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande ville</code> — a large city.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit problème</code> — a small problem.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bon résultat</code> — a good result.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une jeune équipe</code> — a young team.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieux bâtiment</code> — an old building.</p>\n<p>The adjective still agrees with the noun even when it comes before it.</p>\n<p>Adjective position is not a reason to skip agreement. Whether the adjective comes before or after the noun, the noun still controls its form. Compare <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit projet</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite tâche</code>: the adjective moves with the noun's gender.</p>\n<p>At the beginner level, learn the common position together with each adjective. Do not try to memorize a long abstract list. Repeated phrases will teach you which adjectives normally come before the noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit projet</code> — a small project.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite tâche</code> — a small task.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de petits projets</code> — some small projects.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de petites tâches</code> — some small tasks.</p>\n<p>Three especially important adjectives have extra masculine forms before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “beautiful” can be:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bel</code> before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">belle</code> with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un beau quartier</code> — a beautiful neighborhood.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code> — a beautiful apartment.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une belle maison</code> — a beautiful house.</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “new” can be:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvelle</code> with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouveau projet</code> — a new project.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel emploi</code> — a new job.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une nouvelle idée</code> — a new idea.</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “old” can be:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code> before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code> before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieille</code> with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieux livre</code> — an old book.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieil ordinateur</code> — an old computer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une vieille voiture</code> — an old car.</p>\n<p>Practice the pattern:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau, bel, belle</code> — beautiful, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau, nouvel, nouvelle</code> — new, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux, vieil, vieille</code> — old, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<h3 id=\"meaning-can-change-with-position\">Meaning can change with position</h3>\n<p>Sometimes an adjective changes meaning depending on whether it comes before or after the noun.</p>\n<p>This is a meaning issue, not only a word-order issue. French speakers use position to communicate whether a description is familiar, subjective, literal, or part of a fixed expression. When you meet a new adjective, notice both its position and the meaning of the complete phrase.</p>\n<p>For example, “ancien professeur” can mean a former professor, while “professeur ancien” would suggest an old or ancient professor, although the second phrase is less common and context matters.</p>\n<p>Similarly, “un pauvre homme” often means an unfortunate or pitiable man, while “un homme pauvre” means a man who is not wealthy.</p>\n<p>Do not worry about mastering every meaning contrast at the beginning. Just notice that adjective position can matter.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-11-nationalities-and-languages\">Part 11: Nationalities and languages</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Nationality words can function as adjectives or nouns.</p>\n<p>As adjectives, nationality words agree with the person or thing they describe:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ingénieur canadien</code> — a Canadian male engineer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une ingénieure canadienne</code> — a Canadian female engineer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ingénieurs canadiens</code> — Canadian male engineers or a mixed-gender group of engineers.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ingénieures canadiennes</code> — Canadian female engineers.</p>\n<p>Nationality nouns referring to people are normally capitalized in French:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un Canadien</code> — a Canadian man.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une Canadienne</code> — a Canadian woman.</p>\n<p>But nationality adjectives are not capitalized:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un collègue canadien</code> — a Canadian colleague.</p>\n<p>Language names are masculine nouns and are not capitalized:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le français</code> — French, the language.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’anglais</code> — English, the language.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’espagnol</code> — Spanish, the language.</p>\n<p>Say these phrases:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français.</code> — I speak French.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle apprend l’anglais.</code> — She is learning English.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous étudions l’espagnol.</code> — We study Spanish.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-12-guided-review\">Part 12: Guided review</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Let’s review the complete system.</p>\n<p>Step one: identify the noun.</p>\n<p>Step two: identify its gender.</p>\n<p>Step three: identify whether it is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>Step four: choose the article.</p>\n<p>Step five: if there is an adjective, make the adjective agree.</p>\n<p>This five-step routine is the main skill you are building. Do not try to produce the entire phrase at once. Work from the inside out: identify the noun, recall its gender, choose the article, place the adjective, and then check the adjective ending.</p>\n<p>With practice, these steps will happen almost simultaneously. At the beginning, it is completely normal to pause and reason through them.</p>\n<p>Let’s do examples together.</p>\n<p>Example one:</p>\n<p>“A small house.”</p>\n<p>House is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code>, a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Small is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>, which becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Because the noun is singular and indefinite, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code> — a small house.</p>\n<p>Notice the reasoning: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code> is feminine, so we need <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>. The adjective <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> to agree with the feminine noun. Because this adjective normally comes before the noun, the final phrase is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Example two:</p>\n<p>“The new project.”</p>\n<p>Project is masculine singular.</p>\n<p>New is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nouveau projet</code> — the new project.</p>\n<p>Here, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">projet</code> is masculine singular, so we use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>. The noun begins with a consonant sound, so the masculine form is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code>. The adjective comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Example three:</p>\n<p>“A new apartment.”</p>\n<p>Apartment is masculine and begins with a vowel sound.</p>\n<p>The masculine form of “new” becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code>.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel appartement</code> — a new apartment.</p>\n<p>The noun is still masculine singular, so the article is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>. However, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appartement</code> begins with a vowel sound. French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> before that sound to make the phrase flow more smoothly.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Example four:</p>\n<p>“Some interesting books.”</p>\n<p>Books are masculine plural.</p>\n<p>Interesting becomes plural masculine.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des livres intéressants</code> — interesting books.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">livres</code> is masculine plural, so the article is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>. The adjective follows the noun and receives the masculine plural ending: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">intéressants</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Example five:</p>\n<p>“The green cars.”</p>\n<p>Cars are feminine plural.</p>\n<p>Green becomes feminine plural.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voitures vertes</code> — the green cars.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voitures</code> is feminine plural, so we use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>. The color follows the noun and becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vertes</code>, with both the feminine and plural endings.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-13-your-turn\">Part 13: Your turn</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Pause after each prompt and produce the French phrase.</p>\n<p>One: “a difficult problem.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un problème difficile</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is masculine singular, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>. The adjective <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile</code> follows the noun and has the same spelling in masculine and feminine singular.</p>\n<p>Two: “a beautiful city.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une belle ville</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is feminine singular, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">belle</code>. This common adjective normally comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Three: “some new ideas.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de nouvelles idées</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is feminine plural, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvelles</code>. Because this adjective comes before a plural noun, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> commonly shortens to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</p>\n<p>Four: “the old hotels.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vieux hôtels</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is masculine plural, so the adjective is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code>. The special singular form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code> is not used here because the phrase is plural.</p>\n<p>Five: “a black computer.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ordinateur noir</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ordinateur</code> is masculine singular. The color adjective follows the noun and remains masculine singular: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noir</code>.</p>\n<p>Six: “some orange bags.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des sacs orange</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is plural, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">orange</code> is an invariable color adjective in this use, so it does not add a plural ending.</p>\n<p>Seven: “the serious engineers.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les ingénieurs sérieux</code>.</p>\n<p>Eight: “a Canadian woman.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une Canadienne canadienne</code> is grammatically possible but repetitive and unnatural. A more natural phrase is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une ingénieure canadienne</code>, meaning “a Canadian female engineer.”</p>\n<p>This example shows why vocabulary and context matter. Do not translate word by word when a more natural phrase is available.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-14-listening-discrimination\">Part 14: Listening discrimination</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Listen for the article. I will say two phrases. Decide whether the noun is singular or plural.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le projet</code> — the project.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les projets</code> — the projects.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The first is singular. The second is plural.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une idée</code> — an idea.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des idées</code> — ideas.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The first is singular. The second is plural.</p>\n<p>Now listen for adjective agreement.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un client satisfait</code> — a satisfied client.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une cliente satisfaite</code> — a satisfied female client.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The second phrase has both a feminine noun form and a feminine adjective form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un collègue sérieux</code> — a serious colleague.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des collègues sérieux</code> — serious colleagues.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The adjective looks the same in writing, but the article and noun show the plural.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-15-end-of-lesson-summary\">Part 15: End-of-lesson summary</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Here are the essential rules from today.</p>\n<p>Every French noun has a grammatical gender: masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>Learn nouns together with their articles.</p>\n<p>The definite articles are:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> for masculine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> for feminine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code> before a vowel sound or most silent “h” words.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> for all plural nouns.</p>\n<p>The indefinite articles are:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> for masculine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> for feminine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> for plural nouns.</p>\n<p>Most plural nouns add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> in writing, but the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> is usually silent.</p>\n<p>Adjectives usually follow the noun.</p>\n<p>Adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>Many feminine adjectives add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code>.</p>\n<p>Many plural adjectives add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>.</p>\n<p>Some common adjectives usually come before the noun, especially adjectives related to size, age, beauty, goodness, and order.</p>\n<p>The forms <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code> change before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>Finally, practice aloud. French grammar is not only a written system. You need to connect the article, noun, and adjective in sound.</p>\n<p>For your next review, do three things:</p>\n<p>First, choose ten nouns and memorize each one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code>.</p>\n<p>Second, create five singular phrases and turn them into plural phrases.</p>\n<p>Third, describe five objects around you using a noun and an adjective.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un écran noir</code> — a black screen.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une tasse blanche</code> — a white cup.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit bureau</code> — a small office or desk.</p>\n<p>Speak slowly, listen carefully, and repeat often.</p>\n<p>That is the end of this lesson. À bientôt!</p>","search":"# chapter 1 audio lesson: french nouns, articles, and adjectives **format:** text-to-speech teaching script **audience:** complete beginners **suggested duration:** 25–35 minutes **use:** companion lesson for *easy french step-by-step*, chapter 1 ## production notes this is an original companion lesson. it teaches the same foundational topics as chapter 1 but does not read or reproduce the textbook. the narration should be calm and deliberate, with a short pause after every french phrase and a longer pause wherever `[pause]` appears. recommended voice settings: - native or highly fluent french pronunciation for french examples - slightly slower-than-normal pace for new vocabulary - english explanation followed by french example - repeat every french phrase twice - leave 3–5 seconds after prompts so the learner can answer - use a different voice or tone for “your turn” whenever a french example appears by itself, the english meaning is now written immediately after an em dash. practice prompts intentionally remain untranslated until after the pause so the learner can test comprehension. --- ## opening **narrator:** welcome to your first french grammar and listening lesson. today we will build the foundation for describing people, places, and things. we will learn six connected ideas: first, french nouns have grammatical gender. second, nouns can be singular or plural. third, french uses articles, such as “the,” “a,” and “some.” fourth, articles must agree with the noun. fifth, descriptive adjectives usually agree with the noun in gender and number. and sixth, adjectives can appear before or after the noun, depending on the adjective and sometimes on the meaning. do not try to memorize everything in one sitting. your goal today is to understand the system and begin recognizing its patterns. when you hear “your turn,” pause the audio and say the answer aloud. [pause] let’s begin. --- ## part 1: what is a noun? **narrator:** a noun is a word for a person, place, object, animal, or idea. in english, nouns do not normally have grammatical gender. in french, every noun is classified as either masculine or feminine. this grammatical gender is a property of the french word. it does not always correspond to the physical sex of the thing. for example: `le livre` — the book. `la table` — the table. `le téléphone` — the telephone. `la voiture` — the car. the book is masculine in french. the table is feminine. the telephone is masculine. the car is feminine. these labels are part of french vocabulary. learn the noun together with its article whenever possible. do not learn only `livre`. learn `le livre`. do not learn only `voiture`. learn `la voiture`. say them with me. `le livre` — the book. [pause] again: `le livre`. `la voiture` — the car. [pause] again: `la voiture`. `le téléphone` — the telephone. [pause] again: `le téléphone`. `la table` — the table. [pause] again: `la table`. this habit will help you remember gender naturally. think of the article as part of the noun's identity, not as a separate decoration. in english, you can often learn “table” by itself. in french, the useful unit is `la table`. when you later build a sentence, the article gives you the first signal about gender and number. this also makes listening easier. even if the noun is unfamiliar, the sound of `le`, `la`, or `l’` gives you an immediate grammatical clue. the more often you hear the complete phrase, the more automatic that clue becomes. --- ## part 2: the definite article: “the” **narrator:** the definite article is the french equivalent of “the.” for a singular masculine noun, use `le`. `le livre` — the book. for a singular feminine noun, use `la`. `la table` — the table. for a plural noun, whether masculine or feminine, use `les`. `les livres` — the books. `les tables` — the tables. there is also a shorter form, `l’`, used before a vowel sound or before most words beginning with a silent “h.” this shortening is called elision. french avoids putting two vowel sounds next to each other when the article comes before a vowel-starting word. instead of saying `la amie`, french removes the final vowel of `la` and writes and pronounces `l’amie`. the important point is that elision changes the sound and spelling of the article, but it does not remove the noun's gender. you still need to remember whether the noun is masculine or feminine. for example: `l’ami` — the friend, masculine. `l’amie` — the friend, feminine. `l’hôtel` — the hotel. `l’idée` — the idea. notice that `l’` does not tell you whether the noun is masculine or feminine. you must memorize the gender separately. `l’ami` is masculine. `l’amie` is feminine. `l’hôtel` is masculine. `l’idée` is feminine. say the four forms. `le livre` — the book. [pause] `la table` — the table. [pause] `les livres` — the books. [pause] `l’idée` — the idea. [pause] ### quick recognition practice **narrator:** i will say a noun phrase. identify the article and the number. `la maison` — the house. [pause] the answer is feminine singular. `les maisons` — the houses. [pause] the answer is plural. the noun could refer to a group of feminine houses, but the plural article itself does not show masculine or feminine. `l’université` — the university. [pause] the answer is singular. the noun is feminine, but `l’` hides the gender in pronunciation. `le projet` — the project. [pause] the answer is masculine singular. notice the process: first identify the article, then ask whether the noun is singular or plural, and finally recall the noun's gender. do not guess gender from the english translation. french gender belongs to the french word. --- ## part 3: how to learn noun gender **narrator:** the safest method is simple: memorize the noun with its article. there are patterns that can help, but they are not perfect rules. many nouns ending in `-tion` are feminine. for example: `la décision` — the decision. `la conversation` — the conversation. many nouns ending in `-ment` are masculine. for example: `le document` — the document. `le changement` — the change. many nouns ending in `-isme` are masculine. for example: `le réalisme` — realism. however, french has exceptions. patterns are clues, not guarantees. the noun `l’eau`, meaning “water,” is feminine even though it does not fit the pattern you might expect. when a noun refers to a person, the word may change for masculine and feminine forms: `un étudiant` — a male student or a student described with the masculine form. `une étudiante` — a female student. `un ami` — a male friend. `une amie` — a female friend. some nouns have the same spelling for a man or a woman. the article tells you the gender: `le journaliste` — the male journalist, or the masculine form. `la journaliste` — the female journalist. `le professeur` and `la professeure` are both used in modern french, depending on region and style. the key learning strategy is still the same: store the article with the noun. when you make vocabulary cards, put the article on the front of the card. a card that says `livre = book` trains incomplete knowledge. a better card says `le livre = the book`, and ideally includes a short sentence such as `le livre est intéressant` — the book is interesting. gender patterns are useful for making an educated guess, but they should not replace memorization. when a new word breaks a pattern, record the complete phrase and move on. accuracy grows through repeated exposure, not through trying to invent a perfect rule for every noun. --- ## part 4: the indefinite article: “a,” “an,” and “some” **narrator:** french has three common indefinite articles. use `un` with a singular masculine noun. `un livre` — a book. use `une` with a singular feminine noun. `une table` — a table. use `des` with plural nouns of either gender. `des livres` — some books, or books. `des tables` — some tables, or tables. in natural english, `des` is often not translated as “some.” the meaning depends on context. compare the three ideas: `un ordinateur` introduces one non-specific computer. `l’ordinateur` refers to a specific computer, or to computers as a general category depending on context. `des ordinateurs` refers to more than one non-specific computer. english often leaves the article out when translating plural french phrases. that does not mean the french article is optional. in french, the article is normally part of the complete noun phrase. listen: `j’ai un ordinateur.` — i have a computer. `j’ai une question.` — i have a question. `j’ai des idées.` — i have ideas. the article must agree with the noun. this is a decision-making exercise, not just a pronunciation exercise. before speaking, ask yourself two questions: is the noun masculine or feminine? is it singular or plural? the answer to those two questions determines the article. your turn. say the correct article. a masculine project: `___ projet`. [pause] the answer is `un projet`. a feminine question: `___ question`. [pause] the answer is `une question`. plural ideas: `___ idées`. [pause] the answer is `des idées`. plural computers: `___ ordinateurs`. [pause] the answer is `des ordinateurs`. --- ## part 5: singular and plural nouns **narrator:** most french nouns form the plural by adding `-s` in writing. `un projet` becomes `des projets`. `une idée` becomes `des idées`. `le client` becomes `les clients`. usually, the final `s` is not pronounced. this creates an important difference between written and spoken french. on the page, `client` and `clients` look different. in speech, the noun may sound almost identical. the listener often relies on the article—`le` versus `les`, or `un` versus `des`—to recognize the number. the difference between singular and plural is often easier to hear in the article: `un client` — a client. `des clients` — clients or some clients. `le client` — the client. `les clients` — the clients. some nouns already end in `-s`, `-x`, or `-z` in the singular. their spelling stays the same in the plural. for example: `un choix` — a choice. `des choix` — choices. `un prix` — a price. `des prix` — prices. the article and context show the number. some nouns have special plural spellings: `un bureau` becomes `des bureaux`. `un travail` can become `des travaux` when referring to tasks or works. `un animal` becomes `des animaux`. do not worry about memorizing every irregular plural today. learn each new plural with its vocabulary. ### liaison with plural articles **narrator:** when `les` or `des` comes before a word beginning with a vowel sound, the final sound of the article may link to the next word. the `s` is pronounced like a `z`. for example: `les amis` sounds like “lay zah-mee.” `des enfants` sounds like “day zahn-fahn.” this linking is called liaison. repeat: `les amis` — the friends. [pause] `des enfants` — children or some children. [pause] `les étudiants` — the students. [pause] the final `s` of the noun itself is generally silent. the linking sound comes from the article. listen for the boundary between the words. in `les amis`, do not pronounce the final `s` of `amis` as a separate sound. the `z` sound belongs to the article and connects the two words smoothly. this is one reason french can sound as though words run together. for now, prioritize recognition over production. first learn to hear that `les amis` is plural. later, with repeated shadowing, the liaison will become natural in your own speech. do not force liaison everywhere. first become comfortable recognizing it in common phrases. --- ## part 6: adjectives describe nouns **narrator:** an adjective gives information about a noun. it can describe size, color, personality, quality, age, or many other characteristics. in french, the adjective usually comes after the noun. `un projet intéressant` — an interesting project. `une idée originale` — an original idea. `un problème difficile` — a difficult problem. `une solution simple` — a simple solution. the adjective can also follow the verb `être`, meaning “to be.” `le projet est intéressant.` — the project is interesting. `la solution est simple.` — the solution is simple. the most important rule is agreement. the adjective agrees with the noun in gender and number. in english, the adjective usually stays unchanged: “an interesting project” and “interesting projects.” french makes the relationship visible in writing. the ending of the adjective tells us whether it describes a masculine or feminine noun and whether that noun is singular or plural. when you build a french noun phrase, use this order of thought: choose the noun, choose the article, place the adjective in its normal position, and then adjust the adjective to match the noun. the adjective is not agreeing with the english translation; it is agreeing with the french noun. --- ## part 7: masculine and feminine adjective forms **narrator:** for many adjectives, the feminine form is created by adding `-e` to the masculine form. `un client intelligent` — an intelligent male client or a masculine phrase. `une cliente intelligente` — an intelligent female client. the adjective changes from `intelligent` to `intelligente`. another example: `un employé fatigué` — a tired employee, masculine form. `une employée fatiguée` — a tired employee, feminine form. repeat: `intelligent, intelligente` — intelligent, masculine and feminine forms. [pause] `fatigué, fatiguée` — tired, masculine and feminine forms. [pause] some adjectives already end in a silent `e` in the masculine form. they do not change spelling in the feminine. `un collègue calme` — a calm colleague. `une collègue calme` — a calm colleague. `un enfant drôle` — a funny child. `une enfant drôle` — a funny child. the adjective is the same in writing. the noun or article gives more information. do not assume that a word ending in `-e` is automatically feminine. the ending can be identical in both genders. for example, `calme` stays `calme`, but the complete phrases `un collègue calme` and `une collègue calme` still show different gender through the article and noun. this is another reason to study complete phrases instead of isolated adjective lists. you are training yourself to notice the whole grammatical pattern. some adjectives have special feminine forms: `actif` becomes `active`. `heureux` becomes `heureuse`. `sérieux` becomes `sérieuse`. `sportif` becomes `sportive`. `beau` becomes `belle`. do not try to derive every form from english. learn the pair together. some changes are predictable, such as adding `-e`, while others involve spelling or pronunciation changes. treat irregular forms as families: `actif, active`; `heureux, heureuse`; `beau, belle`. say both forms aloud and place each one in a short phrase. --- ## part 8: adjective plurals **narrator:** the regular plural of an adjective usually adds `-s` in writing. `un projet intéressant` — an interesting project. becomes: `des projets intéressants` — interesting projects. for feminine plural: `une solution simple` — a simple solution. becomes: `des solutions simples` — simple solutions. notice that an adjective ending in a silent `e` may look the same in singular masculine and feminine, but the plural usually adds `s`. some adjectives ending in `-s` or `-x` do not change their spelling in the plural. for example: `un homme sérieux` — a serious man. `des hommes sérieux` — serious men. the written adjective remains `sérieux`. a useful reading habit is to scan from left to right and ask what each ending is doing. in `des hommes sérieux`, `des` signals plural, `hommes` is the plural noun, and `sérieux` is the adjective that agrees with that plural noun. even when the adjective's spelling does not visibly change, the grammar is still active. if a group contains both masculine and feminine nouns, standard written french normally uses the masculine plural form. for example: `un frère et une sœur intelligents` — an intelligent brother and sister. the adjective is masculine plural because the group includes a masculine noun. for an entirely feminine group: `une mère et une sœur intelligentes` — an intelligent mother and sister. the adjective is feminine plural. --- ## part 9: colors **narrator:** color words are adjectives, so they usually agree with the noun. `un écran noir` — a black screen. `une voiture noire` — a black car. `des écrans noirs` — black screens. `des voitures noires` — black cars. many colors follow the regular pattern: `bleu, bleue` — blue. `vert, verte` — green. `gris, grise` — gray. `blanc, blanche` — white. some color words do not change for gender or number when used as adjectives. for example: `orange` and `marron` are commonly invariable. `un sac orange` — an orange bag. `une chemise orange` — an orange shirt. `des sacs orange` — orange bags. when a color word is used as a noun, it is masculine: `j’aime le bleu.` — i like blue. `le rouge est vif.` — red is vivid. for now, focus on using colors as adjectives after nouns. color is an excellent way to practice agreement because the meaning is concrete. look around you and describe objects you can see. start with the noun and article, then add the color, and finally check whether the color needs a feminine or plural ending. --- ## part 10: adjectives before the noun **narrator:** most descriptive adjectives follow the noun, but a group of common adjectives usually comes before the noun. examples include words related to: - size; - age; - beauty; - goodness; - quantity or order; - and certain familiar descriptions. listen to these phrases: `une grande ville` — a large city. `un petit problème` — a small problem. `un bon résultat` — a good result. `une jeune équipe` — a young team. `un vieux bâtiment` — an old building. the adjective still agrees with the noun even when it comes before it. adjective position is not a reason to skip agreement. whether the adjective comes before or after the noun, the noun still controls its form. compare `un petit projet` and `une petite tâche`: the adjective moves with the noun's gender. at the beginner level, learn the common position together with each adjective. do not try to memorize a long abstract list. repeated phrases will teach you which adjectives normally come before the noun. `un petit projet` — a small project. `une petite tâche` — a small task. `de petits projets` — some small projects. `de petites tâches` — some small tasks. three especially important adjectives have extra masculine forms before a vowel sound or silent “h.” the adjective meaning “beautiful” can be: `beau` before a consonant sound. `bel` before a vowel sound or silent “h.” `belle` with a feminine noun. examples: `un beau quartier` — a beautiful neighborhood. `un bel appartement` — a beautiful apartment. `une belle maison` — a beautiful house. the adjective meaning “new” can be: `nouveau` before a consonant sound. `nouvel` before a vowel sound or silent “h.” `nouvelle` with a feminine noun. examples: `un nouveau projet` — a new project. `un nouvel emploi` — a new job. `une nouvelle idée` — a new idea. the adjective meaning “old” can be: `vieux` before a consonant sound. `vieil` before a vowel sound or silent “h.” `vieille` with a feminine noun. examples: `un vieux livre` — an old book. `un vieil ordinateur` — an old computer. `une vieille voiture` — an old car. practice the pattern: `beau, bel, belle` — beautiful, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. [pause] `nouveau, nouvel, nouvelle` — new, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. [pause] `vieux, vieil, vieille` — old, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. [pause] ### meaning can change with position sometimes an adjective changes meaning depending on whether it comes before or after the noun. this is a meaning issue, not only a word-order issue. french speakers use position to communicate whether a description is familiar, subjective, literal, or part of a fixed expression. when you meet a new adjective, notice both its position and the meaning of the complete phrase. for example, “ancien professeur” can mean a former professor, while “professeur ancien” would suggest an old or ancient professor, although the second phrase is less common and context matters. similarly, “un pauvre homme” often means an unfortunate or pitiable man, while “un homme pauvre” means a man who is not wealthy. do not worry about mastering every meaning contrast at the beginning. just notice that adjective position can matter. --- ## part 11: nationalities and languages **narrator:** nationality words can function as adjectives or nouns. as adjectives, nationality words agree with the person or thing they describe: `un ingénieur canadien` — a canadian male engineer. `une ingénieure canadienne` — a canadian female engineer. `des ingénieurs canadiens` — canadian male engineers or a mixed-gender group of engineers. `des ingénieures canadiennes` — canadian female engineers. nationality nouns referring to people are normally capitalized in french: `un canadien` — a canadian man. `une canadienne` — a canadian woman. but nationality adjectives are not capitalized: `un collègue canadien` — a canadian colleague. language names are masculine nouns and are not capitalized: `le français` — french, the language. `l’anglais` — english, the language. `l’espagnol` — spanish, the language. say these phrases: `je parle français.` — i speak french. `elle apprend l’anglais.` — she is learning english. `nous étudions l’espagnol.` — we study spanish. --- ## part 12: guided review **narrator:** let’s review the complete system. step one: identify the noun. step two: identify its gender. step three: identify whether it is singular or plural. step four: choose the article. step five: if there is an adjective, make the adjective agree. this five-step routine is the main skill you are building. do not try to produce the entire phrase at once. work from the inside out: identify the noun, recall its gender, choose the article, place the adjective, and then check the adjective ending. with practice, these steps will happen almost simultaneously. at the beginning, it is completely normal to pause and reason through them. let’s do examples together. example one: “a small house.” house is `maison`, a feminine noun. small is `petit`, which becomes `petite` with a feminine noun. because the noun is singular and indefinite, use `une`. the phrase is: `une petite maison` — a small house. notice the reasoning: `maison` is feminine, so we need `une`. the adjective `petit` becomes `petite` to agree with the feminine noun. because this adjective normally comes before the noun, the final phrase is `une petite maison`. [pause] example two: “the new project.” project is masculine singular. new is `nouveau` before a consonant sound. the phrase is: `le nouveau projet` — the new project. here, `projet` is masculine singular, so we use `le`. the noun begins with a consonant sound, so the masculine form is `nouveau`, not `nouvel`. the adjective comes before the noun. [pause] example three: “a new apartment.” apartment is masculine and begins with a vowel sound. the masculine form of “new” becomes `nouvel`. the phrase is: `un nouvel appartement` — a new apartment. the noun is still masculine singular, so the article is `un`. however, `appartement` begins with a vowel sound. french uses `nouvel` before that sound to make the phrase flow more smoothly. [pause] example four: “some interesting books.” books are masculine plural. interesting becomes plural masculine. the phrase is: `des livres intéressants` — interesting books. `livres` is masculine plural, so the article is `des`. the adjective follows the noun and receives the masculine plural ending: `intéressants`. [pause] example five: “the green cars.” cars are feminine plural. green becomes feminine plural. the phrase is: `les voitures vertes` — the green cars. `voitures` is feminine plural, so we use `les`. the color follows the noun and becomes `vertes`, with both the feminine and plural endings. [pause] --- ## part 13: your turn **narrator:** pause after each prompt and produce the french phrase. one: “a difficult problem.” [pause] suggested answer: `un problème difficile`. the noun is masculine singular, so use `un`. the adjective `difficile` follows the noun and has the same spelling in masculine and feminine singular. two: “a beautiful city.” [pause] suggested answer: `une belle ville`. the noun is feminine singular, so `beau` changes to `belle`. this common adjective normally comes before the noun. three: “some new ideas.” [pause] suggested answer: `de nouvelles idées`. the noun is feminine plural, so `nouveau` becomes `nouvelles`. because this adjective comes before a plural noun, `des` commonly shortens to `de`. four: “the old hotels.” [pause] suggested answer: `les vieux hôtels`. the noun is masculine plural, so the adjective is `vieux`. the special singular form `vieil` is not used here because the phrase is plural. five: “a black computer.” [pause] suggested answer: `un ordinateur noir`. `ordinateur` is masculine singular. the color adjective follows the noun and remains masculine singular: `noir`. six: “some orange bags.” [pause] suggested answer: `des sacs orange`. the noun is plural, but `orange` is an invariable color adjective in this use, so it does not add a plural ending. seven: “the serious engineers.” [pause] suggested answer: `les ingénieurs sérieux`. eight: “a canadian woman.” [pause] suggested answer: `une canadienne canadienne` is grammatically possible but repetitive and unnatural. a more natural phrase is `une ingénieure canadienne`, meaning “a canadian female engineer.” this example shows why vocabulary and context matter. do not translate word by word when a more natural phrase is available. --- ## part 14: listening discrimination **narrator:** listen for the article. i will say two phrases. decide whether the noun is singular or plural. `le projet` — the project. `les projets` — the projects. [pause] the first is singular. the second is plural. `une idée` — an idea. `des idées` — ideas. [pause] the first is singular. the second is plural. now listen for adjective agreement. `un client satisfait` — a satisfied client. `une cliente satisfaite` — a satisfied female client. [pause] the second phrase has both a feminine noun form and a feminine adjective form. `un collègue sérieux` — a serious colleague. `des collègues sérieux` — serious colleagues. [pause] the adjective looks the same in writing, but the article and noun show the plural. --- ## part 15: end-of-lesson summary **narrator:** here are the essential rules from today. every french noun has a grammatical gender: masculine or feminine. learn nouns together with their articles. the definite articles are: `le` for masculine singular. `la` for feminine singular. `l’` before a vowel sound or most silent “h” words. `les` for all plural nouns. the indefinite articles are: `un` for masculine singular. `une` for feminine singular. `des` for plural nouns. most plural nouns add `-s` in writing, but the final `s` is usually silent. adjectives usually follow the noun. adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number. many feminine adjectives add `-e`. many plural adjectives add `-s`. some common adjectives usually come before the noun, especially adjectives related to size, age, beauty, goodness, and order. the forms `beau`, `nouveau`, and `vieux` change before a vowel sound or silent “h.” finally, practice aloud. french grammar is not only a written system. you need to connect the article, noun, and adjective in sound. for your next review, do three things: first, choose ten nouns and memorize each one with `le`, `la`, or `l’`. second, create five singular phrases and turn them into plural phrases. third, describe five objects around you using a noun and an adjective. for example: `un écran noir` — a black screen. `une tasse blanche` — a white cup. `un petit bureau` — a small office or desk. speak slowly, listen carefully, and repeat often. that is the end of this lesson. à bientôt! "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/tts-narration.txt","title":"Tts Narration","type":"Audio","html":"<p>Opening</p>\n<p>Welcome to your first French grammar and listening lesson.</p>\n<p>Today we will build the foundation for describing people, places, and things. We will learn six connected ideas:</p>\n<p>First, French nouns have grammatical gender.</p>\n<p>Second, nouns can be singular or plural.</p>\n<p>Third, French uses articles, such as “the,” “a,” and “some.”</p>\n<p>Fourth, articles must agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>Fifth, descriptive adjectives usually agree with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>And sixth, adjectives can appear before or after the noun, depending on the adjective and sometimes on the meaning.</p>\n<p>Do not try to memorize everything in one sitting. Your goal today is to understand the system and begin recognizing its patterns.</p>\n<p>When you hear “Your turn,” pause the audio and say the answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Let’s begin.</p>\n<p>Part 1: What is a noun?</p>\n<p>A noun is a word for a person, place, object, animal, or idea.</p>\n<p>In English, nouns do not normally have grammatical gender. In French, every noun is classified as either masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>This grammatical gender is a property of the French word. It does not always correspond to the physical sex of the thing.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>le livre — the book.</p>\n<p>la table — the table.</p>\n<p>le téléphone — the telephone.</p>\n<p>la voiture — the car.</p>\n<p>The book is masculine in French. The table is feminine. The telephone is masculine. The car is feminine.</p>\n<p>These labels are part of French vocabulary. Learn the noun together with its article whenever possible.</p>\n<p>Do not learn only livre. Learn le livre.</p>\n<p>Do not learn only voiture. Learn la voiture.</p>\n<p>Say them with me.</p>\n<p>le livre — the book.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Again: le livre.</p>\n<p>la voiture — the car.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Again: la voiture.</p>\n<p>le téléphone — the telephone.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Again: le téléphone.</p>\n<p>la table — the table.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Again: la table.</p>\n<p>This habit will help you remember gender naturally.</p>\n<p>Think of the article as part of the noun’s identity, not as a separate decoration. In English, you can often learn “table” by itself. In French, the useful unit is la table. When you later build a sentence, the article gives you the first signal about gender and number.</p>\n<p>This also makes listening easier. Even if the noun is unfamiliar, the sound of le, la, or l’ gives you an immediate grammatical clue. The more often you hear the complete phrase, the more automatic that clue becomes.</p>\n<p>Part 2: The definite article: “the”</p>\n<p>The definite article is the French equivalent of “the.”</p>\n<p>For a singular masculine noun, use le.</p>\n<p>le livre — the book.</p>\n<p>For a singular feminine noun, use la.</p>\n<p>la table — the table.</p>\n<p>For a plural noun, whether masculine or feminine, use les.</p>\n<p>les livres — the books.</p>\n<p>les tables — the tables.</p>\n<p>There is also a shorter form, l’, used before a vowel sound or before most words beginning with a silent “h.”</p>\n<p>This shortening is called elision. French avoids putting two vowel sounds next to each other when the article comes before a vowel-starting word. Instead of saying la amie, French removes the final vowel of la and writes and pronounces l’amie.</p>\n<p>The important point is that elision changes the sound and spelling of the article, but it does not remove the noun’s gender. You still need to remember whether the noun is masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>l’ami — the friend, masculine.</p>\n<p>l’amie — the friend, feminine.</p>\n<p>l’hôtel — the hotel.</p>\n<p>l’idée — the idea.</p>\n<p>Notice that l’ does not tell you whether the noun is masculine or feminine. You must memorize the gender separately.</p>\n<p>l’ami is masculine.</p>\n<p>l’amie is feminine.</p>\n<p>l’hôtel is masculine.</p>\n<p>l’idée is feminine.</p>\n<p>Say the four forms.</p>\n<p>le livre — the book.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>la table — the table.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>les livres — the books.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>l’idée — the idea.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Quick recognition practice</p>\n<p>I will say a noun phrase. Identify the article and the number.</p>\n<p>la maison — the house.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is feminine singular.</p>\n<p>les maisons — the houses.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is plural. The noun could refer to a group of feminine houses, but the plural article itself does not show masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>l’université — the university.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is singular. The noun is feminine, but l’ hides the gender in pronunciation.</p>\n<p>le projet — the project.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is masculine singular.</p>\n<p>Notice the process: first identify the article, then ask whether the noun is singular or plural, and finally recall the noun’s gender. Do not guess gender from the English translation. French gender belongs to the French word.</p>\n<p>Part 3: How to learn noun gender</p>\n<p>The safest method is simple: memorize the noun with its article.</p>\n<p>There are patterns that can help, but they are not perfect rules.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in -tion are feminine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>la décision — the decision.</p>\n<p>la conversation — the conversation.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in -ment are masculine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>le document — the document.</p>\n<p>le changement — the change.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in -isme are masculine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>le réalisme — realism.</p>\n<p>However, French has exceptions. Patterns are clues, not guarantees.</p>\n<p>The noun l’eau, meaning “water,” is feminine even though it does not fit the pattern you might expect.</p>\n<p>When a noun refers to a person, the word may change for masculine and feminine forms:</p>\n<p>un étudiant — a male student or a student described with the masculine form.</p>\n<p>une étudiante — a female student.</p>\n<p>un ami — a male friend.</p>\n<p>une amie — a female friend.</p>\n<p>Some nouns have the same spelling for a man or a woman. The article tells you the gender:</p>\n<p>le journaliste — the male journalist, or the masculine form.</p>\n<p>la journaliste — the female journalist.</p>\n<p>le professeur and la professeure are both used in modern French, depending on region and style.</p>\n<p>The key learning strategy is still the same: store the article with the noun.</p>\n<p>When you make vocabulary cards, put the article on the front of the card. A card that says livre = book trains incomplete knowledge. A better card says le livre = the book, and ideally includes a short sentence such as Le livre est intéressant — The book is interesting.</p>\n<p>Gender patterns are useful for making an educated guess, but they should not replace memorization. When a new word breaks a pattern, record the complete phrase and move on. Accuracy grows through repeated exposure, not through trying to invent a perfect rule for every noun.</p>\n<p>Part 4: The indefinite article: “a,” “an,” and “some”</p>\n<p>French has three common indefinite articles.</p>\n<p>Use un with a singular masculine noun.</p>\n<p>un livre — a book.</p>\n<p>Use une with a singular feminine noun.</p>\n<p>une table — a table.</p>\n<p>Use des with plural nouns of either gender.</p>\n<p>des livres — some books, or books.</p>\n<p>des tables — some tables, or tables.</p>\n<p>In natural English, des is often not translated as “some.” The meaning depends on context.</p>\n<p>Compare the three ideas:</p>\n<p>un ordinateur introduces one non-specific computer.</p>\n<p>l’ordinateur refers to a specific computer, or to computers as a general category depending on context.</p>\n<p>des ordinateurs refers to more than one non-specific computer.</p>\n<p>English often leaves the article out when translating plural French phrases. That does not mean the French article is optional. In French, the article is normally part of the complete noun phrase.</p>\n<p>Listen:</p>\n<p>J’ai un ordinateur. — I have a computer.</p>\n<p>J’ai une question. — I have a question.</p>\n<p>J’ai des idées. — I have ideas.</p>\n<p>The article must agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>This is a decision-making exercise, not just a pronunciation exercise. Before speaking, ask yourself two questions: Is the noun masculine or feminine? Is it singular or plural? The answer to those two questions determines the article.</p>\n<p>Your turn. Say the correct article aloud.</p>\n<p>A masculine project: ___ projet.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is un projet.</p>\n<p>A feminine question: ___ question.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is une question.</p>\n<p>Plural ideas: ___ idées.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is des idées.</p>\n<p>Plural computers: ___ ordinateurs.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is des ordinateurs.</p>\n<p>Part 5: Singular and plural nouns</p>\n<p>Most French nouns form the plural by adding -s in writing.</p>\n<p>un projet becomes des projets.</p>\n<p>une idée becomes des idées.</p>\n<p>le client becomes les clients.</p>\n<p>Usually, the final s is not pronounced.</p>\n<p>This creates an important difference between written and spoken French. On the page, client and clients look different. In speech, the noun may sound almost identical. The listener often relies on the article—le versus les, or un versus des—to recognize the number.</p>\n<p>The difference between singular and plural is often easier to hear in the article:</p>\n<p>un client — a client.</p>\n<p>des clients — clients or some clients.</p>\n<p>le client — the client.</p>\n<p>les clients — the clients.</p>\n<p>Some nouns already end in -s, -x, or -z in the singular. Their spelling stays the same in the plural.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>un choix — a choice.</p>\n<p>des choix — choices.</p>\n<p>un prix — a price.</p>\n<p>des prix — prices.</p>\n<p>The article and context show the number.</p>\n<p>Some nouns have special plural spellings:</p>\n<p>un bureau becomes des bureaux.</p>\n<p>un travail can become des travaux when referring to tasks or works.</p>\n<p>un animal becomes des animaux.</p>\n<p>Do not worry about memorizing every irregular plural today. Learn each new plural with its vocabulary.</p>\n<p>Liaison with plural articles</p>\n<p>When les or des comes before a word beginning with a vowel sound, the final sound of the article may link to the next word.</p>\n<p>The s is pronounced like a z.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>les amis sounds like “lay zah-mee.”</p>\n<p>des enfants sounds like “day zahn-fahn.”</p>\n<p>This linking is called liaison.</p>\n<p>Repeat:</p>\n<p>les amis — the friends.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>des enfants — children or some children.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>les étudiants — the students.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The final s of the noun itself is generally silent. The linking sound comes from the article.</p>\n<p>Listen for the boundary between the words. In les amis, do not pronounce the final s of amis as a separate sound. The z sound belongs to the article and connects the two words smoothly. This is one reason French can sound as though words run together.</p>\n<p>For now, prioritize recognition over production. First learn to hear that les amis is plural. Later, with repeated shadowing, the liaison will become natural in your own speech.</p>\n<p>Do not force liaison everywhere. First become comfortable recognizing it in common phrases.</p>\n<p>Part 6: Adjectives describe nouns</p>\n<p>An adjective gives information about a noun.</p>\n<p>It can describe size, color, personality, quality, age, or many other characteristics.</p>\n<p>In French, the adjective usually comes after the noun.</p>\n<p>un projet intéressant — an interesting project.</p>\n<p>une idée originale — an original idea.</p>\n<p>un problème difficile — a difficult problem.</p>\n<p>une solution simple — a simple solution.</p>\n<p>The adjective can also follow the verb être, meaning “to be.”</p>\n<p>Le projet est intéressant. — The project is interesting.</p>\n<p>La solution est simple. — The solution is simple.</p>\n<p>The most important rule is agreement.</p>\n<p>The adjective agrees with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>In English, the adjective usually stays unchanged: “an interesting project” and “interesting projects.” French makes the relationship visible in writing. The ending of the adjective tells us whether it describes a masculine or feminine noun and whether that noun is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>When you build a French noun phrase, use this order of thought: choose the noun, choose the article, place the adjective in its normal position, and then adjust the adjective to match the noun. The adjective is not agreeing with the English translation; it is agreeing with the French noun.</p>\n<p>Part 7: Masculine and feminine adjective forms</p>\n<p>For many adjectives, the feminine form is created by adding -e to the masculine form.</p>\n<p>un client intelligent — an intelligent male client or a masculine phrase.</p>\n<p>une cliente intelligente — an intelligent female client.</p>\n<p>The adjective changes from intelligent to intelligente.</p>\n<p>Another example:</p>\n<p>un employé fatigué — a tired employee, masculine form.</p>\n<p>une employée fatiguée — a tired employee, feminine form.</p>\n<p>Repeat:</p>\n<p>intelligent, intelligente — intelligent, masculine and feminine forms.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>fatigué, fatiguée — tired, masculine and feminine forms.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives already end in a silent e in the masculine form. They do not change spelling in the feminine.</p>\n<p>un collègue calme — a calm colleague.</p>\n<p>une collègue calme — a calm colleague.</p>\n<p>un enfant drôle — a funny child.</p>\n<p>une enfant drôle — a funny child.</p>\n<p>The adjective is the same in writing. The noun or article gives more information.</p>\n<p>Do not assume that a word ending in -e is automatically feminine. The ending can be identical in both genders. For example, calme stays calme, but the complete phrases un collègue calme and une collègue calme still show different gender through the article and noun.</p>\n<p>This is another reason to study complete phrases instead of isolated adjective lists. You are training yourself to notice the whole grammatical pattern.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives have special feminine forms:</p>\n<p>actif becomes active.</p>\n<p>heureux becomes heureuse.</p>\n<p>sérieux becomes sérieuse.</p>\n<p>sportif becomes sportive.</p>\n<p>beau becomes belle.</p>\n<p>Do not try to derive every form from English. Learn the pair together.</p>\n<p>Some changes are predictable, such as adding -e, while others involve spelling or pronunciation changes. Treat irregular forms as families: actif, active; heureux, heureuse; beau, belle. Say both forms aloud and place each one in a short phrase.</p>\n<p>Part 8: Adjective plurals</p>\n<p>The regular plural of an adjective usually adds -s in writing.</p>\n<p>un projet intéressant — an interesting project.</p>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<p>des projets intéressants — interesting projects.</p>\n<p>For feminine plural:</p>\n<p>une solution simple — a simple solution.</p>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<p>des solutions simples — simple solutions.</p>\n<p>Notice that an adjective ending in a silent e may look the same in singular masculine and feminine, but the plural usually adds s.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives ending in -s or -x do not change their spelling in the plural.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>un homme sérieux — a serious man.</p>\n<p>des hommes sérieux — serious men.</p>\n<p>The written adjective remains sérieux.</p>\n<p>A useful reading habit is to scan from left to right and ask what each ending is doing. In des hommes sérieux, des signals plural, hommes is the plural noun, and sérieux is the adjective that agrees with that plural noun. Even when the adjective’s spelling does not visibly change, the grammar is still active.</p>\n<p>If a group contains both masculine and feminine nouns, standard written French normally uses the masculine plural form.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>un frère et une sœur intelligents — an intelligent brother and sister.</p>\n<p>The adjective is masculine plural because the group includes a masculine noun.</p>\n<p>For an entirely feminine group:</p>\n<p>une mère et une sœur intelligentes — an intelligent mother and sister.</p>\n<p>The adjective is feminine plural.</p>\n<p>Part 9: Colors</p>\n<p>Color words are adjectives, so they usually agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>un écran noir — a black screen.</p>\n<p>une voiture noire — a black car.</p>\n<p>des écrans noirs — black screens.</p>\n<p>des voitures noires — black cars.</p>\n<p>Many colors follow the regular pattern:</p>\n<p>bleu, bleue — blue.</p>\n<p>vert, verte — green.</p>\n<p>gris, grise — gray.</p>\n<p>blanc, blanche — white.</p>\n<p>Some color words do not change for gender or number when used as adjectives.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>orange and marron are commonly invariable.</p>\n<p>un sac orange — an orange bag.</p>\n<p>une chemise orange — an orange shirt.</p>\n<p>des sacs orange — orange bags.</p>\n<p>When a color word is used as a noun, it is masculine:</p>\n<p>J’aime le bleu. — I like blue.</p>\n<p>Le rouge est vif. — Red is vivid.</p>\n<p>For now, focus on using colors as adjectives after nouns.</p>\n<p>Color is an excellent way to practice agreement because the meaning is concrete. Look around you and describe objects you can see. Start with the noun and article, then add the color, and finally check whether the color needs a feminine or plural ending.</p>\n<p>Part 10: Adjectives before the noun</p>\n<p>Most descriptive adjectives follow the noun, but a group of common adjectives usually comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Examples include words related to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>size;</li>\n<li>age;</li>\n<li>beauty;</li>\n<li>goodness;</li>\n<li>quantity or order;</li>\n<li>and certain familiar descriptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Listen to these phrases:</p>\n<p>une grande ville — a large city.</p>\n<p>un petit problème — a small problem.</p>\n<p>un bon résultat — a good result.</p>\n<p>une jeune équipe — a young team.</p>\n<p>un vieux bâtiment — an old building.</p>\n<p>The adjective still agrees with the noun even when it comes before it.</p>\n<p>Adjective position is not a reason to skip agreement. Whether the adjective comes before or after the noun, the noun still controls its form. Compare un petit projet and une petite tâche: the adjective moves with the noun’s gender.</p>\n<p>At the beginner level, learn the common position together with each adjective. Do not try to memorize a long abstract list. Repeated phrases will teach you which adjectives normally come before the noun.</p>\n<p>un petit projet — a small project.</p>\n<p>une petite tâche — a small task.</p>\n<p>de petits projets — some small projects.</p>\n<p>de petites tâches — some small tasks.</p>\n<p>Three especially important adjectives have extra masculine forms before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “beautiful” can be:</p>\n<p>beau before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>bel before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>belle with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>un beau quartier — a beautiful neighborhood.</p>\n<p>un bel appartement — a beautiful apartment.</p>\n<p>une belle maison — a beautiful house.</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “new” can be:</p>\n<p>nouveau before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>nouvel before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>nouvelle with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>un nouveau projet — a new project.</p>\n<p>un nouvel emploi — a new job.</p>\n<p>une nouvelle idée — a new idea.</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “old” can be:</p>\n<p>vieux before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>vieil before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>vieille with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>un vieux livre — an old book.</p>\n<p>un vieil ordinateur — an old computer.</p>\n<p>une vieille voiture — an old car.</p>\n<p>Practice the pattern:</p>\n<p>beau, bel, belle — beautiful, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>nouveau, nouvel, nouvelle — new, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>vieux, vieil, vieille — old, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Meaning can change with position</p>\n<p>Sometimes an adjective changes meaning depending on whether it comes before or after the noun.</p>\n<p>This is a meaning issue, not only a word-order issue. French speakers use position to communicate whether a description is familiar, subjective, literal, or part of a fixed expression. When you meet a new adjective, notice both its position and the meaning of the complete phrase.</p>\n<p>For example, “ancien professeur” can mean a former professor, while “professeur ancien” would suggest an old or ancient professor, although the second phrase is less common and context matters.</p>\n<p>Similarly, “un pauvre homme” often means an unfortunate or pitiable man, while “un homme pauvre” means a man who is not wealthy.</p>\n<p>Do not worry about mastering every meaning contrast at the beginning. Just notice that adjective position can matter.</p>\n<p>Part 11: Nationalities and languages</p>\n<p>Nationality words can function as adjectives or nouns.</p>\n<p>As adjectives, nationality words agree with the person or thing they describe:</p>\n<p>un ingénieur canadien — a Canadian male engineer.</p>\n<p>une ingénieure canadienne — a Canadian female engineer.</p>\n<p>des ingénieurs canadiens — Canadian male engineers or a mixed-gender group of engineers.</p>\n<p>des ingénieures canadiennes — Canadian female engineers.</p>\n<p>Nationality nouns referring to people are normally capitalized in French:</p>\n<p>un Canadien — a Canadian man.</p>\n<p>une Canadienne — a Canadian woman.</p>\n<p>But nationality adjectives are not capitalized:</p>\n<p>un collègue canadien — a Canadian colleague.</p>\n<p>Language names are masculine nouns and are not capitalized:</p>\n<p>le français — French, the language.</p>\n<p>l’anglais — English, the language.</p>\n<p>l’espagnol — Spanish, the language.</p>\n<p>Say these phrases:</p>\n<p>Je parle français. — I speak French.</p>\n<p>Elle apprend l’anglais. — She is learning English.</p>\n<p>Nous étudions l’espagnol. — We study Spanish.</p>\n<p>Part 12: Guided review</p>\n<p>Let’s review the complete system.</p>\n<p>Step one: identify the noun.</p>\n<p>Step two: identify its gender.</p>\n<p>Step three: identify whether it is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>Step four: choose the article.</p>\n<p>Step five: if there is an adjective, make the adjective agree.</p>\n<p>This five-step routine is the main skill you are building. Do not try to produce the entire phrase at once. Work from the inside out: identify the noun, recall its gender, choose the article, place the adjective, and then check the adjective ending.</p>\n<p>With practice, these steps will happen almost simultaneously. At the beginning, it is completely normal to pause and reason through them.</p>\n<p>Let’s do examples together.</p>\n<p>Example one:</p>\n<p>“A small house.”</p>\n<p>House is maison, a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Small is petit, which becomes petite with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Because the noun is singular and indefinite, use une.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>une petite maison — a small house.</p>\n<p>Notice the reasoning: maison is feminine, so we need une. The adjective petit becomes petite to agree with the feminine noun. Because this adjective normally comes before the noun, the final phrase is une petite maison.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Example two:</p>\n<p>“The new project.”</p>\n<p>Project is masculine singular.</p>\n<p>New is nouveau before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>le nouveau projet — the new project.</p>\n<p>Here, projet is masculine singular, so we use le. The noun begins with a consonant sound, so the masculine form is nouveau, not nouvel. The adjective comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Example three:</p>\n<p>“A new apartment.”</p>\n<p>Apartment is masculine and begins with a vowel sound.</p>\n<p>The masculine form of “new” becomes nouvel.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>un nouvel appartement — a new apartment.</p>\n<p>The noun is still masculine singular, so the article is un. However, appartement begins with a vowel sound. French uses nouvel before that sound to make the phrase flow more smoothly.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Example four:</p>\n<p>“Some interesting books.”</p>\n<p>Books are masculine plural.</p>\n<p>Interesting becomes plural masculine.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>des livres intéressants — interesting books.</p>\n<p>livres is masculine plural, so the article is des. The adjective follows the noun and receives the masculine plural ending: intéressants.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Example five:</p>\n<p>“The green cars.”</p>\n<p>Cars are feminine plural.</p>\n<p>Green becomes feminine plural.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>les voitures vertes — the green cars.</p>\n<p>voitures is feminine plural, so we use les. The color follows the noun and becomes vertes, with both the feminine and plural endings.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Part 13: Your turn</p>\n<p>Pause after each prompt and produce the French phrase aloud.</p>\n<p>One: “a difficult problem.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is un problème difficile.</p>\n<p>The noun is masculine singular, so use un. The adjective difficile follows the noun and has the same spelling in masculine and feminine singular.</p>\n<p>Two: “a beautiful city.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is une belle ville.</p>\n<p>The noun is feminine singular, so beau changes to belle. This common adjective normally comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Three: “some new ideas.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is de nouvelles idées.</p>\n<p>The noun is feminine plural, so nouveau becomes nouvelles. Because this adjective comes before a plural noun, des commonly shortens to de.</p>\n<p>Four: “the old hotels.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is les vieux hôtels.</p>\n<p>The noun is masculine plural, so the adjective is vieux. The special singular form vieil is not used here because the phrase is plural.</p>\n<p>Five: “a black computer.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is un ordinateur noir.</p>\n<p>ordinateur is masculine singular. The color adjective follows the noun and remains masculine singular: noir.</p>\n<p>Six: “some orange bags.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is des sacs orange.</p>\n<p>The noun is plural, but orange is an invariable color adjective in this use, so it does not add a plural ending.</p>\n<p>Seven: “the serious engineers.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is les ingénieurs sérieux.</p>\n<p>Eight: “a Canadian woman.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is une Canadienne canadienne is grammatically possible but repetitive and unnatural. A more natural phrase is une ingénieure canadienne, meaning “a Canadian female engineer.”</p>\n<p>This example shows why vocabulary and context matter. Do not translate word by word when a more natural phrase is available.</p>\n<p>Part 14: Listening discrimination</p>\n<p>Listen for the article. I will say two phrases. Decide whether the noun is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>le projet — the project.</p>\n<p>les projets — the projects.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The first is singular. The second is plural.</p>\n<p>une idée — an idea.</p>\n<p>des idées — ideas.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The first is singular. The second is plural.</p>\n<p>Now listen for adjective agreement.</p>\n<p>un client satisfait — a satisfied client.</p>\n<p>une cliente satisfaite — a satisfied female client.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The second phrase has both a feminine noun form and a feminine adjective form.</p>\n<p>un collègue sérieux — a serious colleague.</p>\n<p>des collègues sérieux — serious colleagues.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The adjective looks the same in writing, but the article and noun show the plural.</p>\n<p>Part 15: End-of-lesson summary</p>\n<p>Here are the essential rules from today.</p>\n<p>Every French noun has a grammatical gender: masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>Learn nouns together with their articles.</p>\n<p>The definite articles are:</p>\n<p>le for masculine singular.</p>\n<p>la for feminine singular.</p>\n<p>l’ before a vowel sound or most silent “h” words.</p>\n<p>les for all plural nouns.</p>\n<p>The indefinite articles are:</p>\n<p>un for masculine singular.</p>\n<p>une for feminine singular.</p>\n<p>des for plural nouns.</p>\n<p>Most plural nouns add -s in writing, but the final s is usually silent.</p>\n<p>Adjectives usually follow the noun.</p>\n<p>Adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>Many feminine adjectives add -e.</p>\n<p>Many plural adjectives add -s.</p>\n<p>Some common adjectives usually come before the noun, especially adjectives related to size, age, beauty, goodness, and order.</p>\n<p>The forms beau, nouveau, and vieux change before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>Finally, practice aloud. French grammar is not only a written system. You need to connect the article, noun, and adjective in sound.</p>\n<p>For your next review, do three things:</p>\n<p>First, choose ten nouns and memorize each one with le, la, or l’.</p>\n<p>Second, create five singular phrases and turn them into plural phrases.</p>\n<p>Third, describe five objects around you using a noun and an adjective.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>un écran noir — a black screen.</p>\n<p>une tasse blanche — a white cup.</p>\n<p>un petit bureau — a small office or desk.</p>\n<p>Speak slowly, listen carefully, and repeat often.</p>\n<p>That is the end of this lesson. À bientôt!</p>","search":"opening welcome to your first french grammar and listening lesson. today we will build the foundation for describing people, places, and things. we will learn six connected ideas: first, french nouns have grammatical gender. second, nouns can be singular or plural. third, french uses articles, such as “the,” “a,” and “some.” fourth, articles must agree with the noun. fifth, descriptive adjectives usually agree with the noun in gender and number. and sixth, adjectives can appear before or after the noun, depending on the adjective and sometimes on the meaning. do not try to memorize everything in one sitting. your goal today is to understand the system and begin recognizing its patterns. when you hear “your turn,” pause the audio and say the answer aloud. pause now and answer aloud. let’s begin. part 1: what is a noun? a noun is a word for a person, place, object, animal, or idea. in english, nouns do not normally have grammatical gender. in french, every noun is classified as either masculine or feminine. this grammatical gender is a property of the french word. it does not always correspond to the physical sex of the thing. for example: le livre — the book. la table — the table. le téléphone — the telephone. la voiture — the car. the book is masculine in french. the table is feminine. the telephone is masculine. the car is feminine. these labels are part of french vocabulary. learn the noun together with its article whenever possible. do not learn only livre. learn le livre. do not learn only voiture. learn la voiture. say them with me. le livre — the book. pause now and answer aloud. again: le livre. la voiture — the car. pause now and answer aloud. again: la voiture. le téléphone — the telephone. pause now and answer aloud. again: le téléphone. la table — the table. pause now and answer aloud. again: la table. this habit will help you remember gender naturally. think of the article as part of the noun’s identity, not as a separate decoration. in english, you can often learn “table” by itself. in french, the useful unit is la table. when you later build a sentence, the article gives you the first signal about gender and number. this also makes listening easier. even if the noun is unfamiliar, the sound of le, la, or l’ gives you an immediate grammatical clue. the more often you hear the complete phrase, the more automatic that clue becomes. part 2: the definite article: “the” the definite article is the french equivalent of “the.” for a singular masculine noun, use le. le livre — the book. for a singular feminine noun, use la. la table — the table. for a plural noun, whether masculine or feminine, use les. les livres — the books. les tables — the tables. there is also a shorter form, l’, used before a vowel sound or before most words beginning with a silent “h.” this shortening is called elision. french avoids putting two vowel sounds next to each other when the article comes before a vowel-starting word. instead of saying la amie, french removes the final vowel of la and writes and pronounces l’amie. the important point is that elision changes the sound and spelling of the article, but it does not remove the noun’s gender. you still need to remember whether the noun is masculine or feminine. for example: l’ami — the friend, masculine. l’amie — the friend, feminine. l’hôtel — the hotel. l’idée — the idea. notice that l’ does not tell you whether the noun is masculine or feminine. you must memorize the gender separately. l’ami is masculine. l’amie is feminine. l’hôtel is masculine. l’idée is feminine. say the four forms. le livre — the book. pause now and answer aloud. la table — the table. pause now and answer aloud. les livres — the books. pause now and answer aloud. l’idée — the idea. pause now and answer aloud. quick recognition practice i will say a noun phrase. identify the article and the number. la maison — the house. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is feminine singular. les maisons — the houses. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is plural. the noun could refer to a group of feminine houses, but the plural article itself does not show masculine or feminine. l’université — the university. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is singular. the noun is feminine, but l’ hides the gender in pronunciation. le projet — the project. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is masculine singular. notice the process: first identify the article, then ask whether the noun is singular or plural, and finally recall the noun’s gender. do not guess gender from the english translation. french gender belongs to the french word. part 3: how to learn noun gender the safest method is simple: memorize the noun with its article. there are patterns that can help, but they are not perfect rules. many nouns ending in -tion are feminine. for example: la décision — the decision. la conversation — the conversation. many nouns ending in -ment are masculine. for example: le document — the document. le changement — the change. many nouns ending in -isme are masculine. for example: le réalisme — realism. however, french has exceptions. patterns are clues, not guarantees. the noun l’eau, meaning “water,” is feminine even though it does not fit the pattern you might expect. when a noun refers to a person, the word may change for masculine and feminine forms: un étudiant — a male student or a student described with the masculine form. une étudiante — a female student. un ami — a male friend. une amie — a female friend. some nouns have the same spelling for a man or a woman. the article tells you the gender: le journaliste — the male journalist, or the masculine form. la journaliste — the female journalist. le professeur and la professeure are both used in modern french, depending on region and style. the key learning strategy is still the same: store the article with the noun. when you make vocabulary cards, put the article on the front of the card. a card that says livre = book trains incomplete knowledge. a better card says le livre = the book, and ideally includes a short sentence such as le livre est intéressant — the book is interesting. gender patterns are useful for making an educated guess, but they should not replace memorization. when a new word breaks a pattern, record the complete phrase and move on. accuracy grows through repeated exposure, not through trying to invent a perfect rule for every noun. part 4: the indefinite article: “a,” “an,” and “some” french has three common indefinite articles. use un with a singular masculine noun. un livre — a book. use une with a singular feminine noun. une table — a table. use des with plural nouns of either gender. des livres — some books, or books. des tables — some tables, or tables. in natural english, des is often not translated as “some.” the meaning depends on context. compare the three ideas: un ordinateur introduces one non-specific computer. l’ordinateur refers to a specific computer, or to computers as a general category depending on context. des ordinateurs refers to more than one non-specific computer. english often leaves the article out when translating plural french phrases. that does not mean the french article is optional. in french, the article is normally part of the complete noun phrase. listen: j’ai un ordinateur. — i have a computer. j’ai une question. — i have a question. j’ai des idées. — i have ideas. the article must agree with the noun. this is a decision-making exercise, not just a pronunciation exercise. before speaking, ask yourself two questions: is the noun masculine or feminine? is it singular or plural? the answer to those two questions determines the article. your turn. say the correct article aloud. a masculine project: ___ projet. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is un projet. a feminine question: ___ question. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is une question. plural ideas: ___ idées. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is des idées. plural computers: ___ ordinateurs. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is des ordinateurs. part 5: singular and plural nouns most french nouns form the plural by adding -s in writing. un projet becomes des projets. une idée becomes des idées. le client becomes les clients. usually, the final s is not pronounced. this creates an important difference between written and spoken french. on the page, client and clients look different. in speech, the noun may sound almost identical. the listener often relies on the article—le versus les, or un versus des—to recognize the number. the difference between singular and plural is often easier to hear in the article: un client — a client. des clients — clients or some clients. le client — the client. les clients — the clients. some nouns already end in -s, -x, or -z in the singular. their spelling stays the same in the plural. for example: un choix — a choice. des choix — choices. un prix — a price. des prix — prices. the article and context show the number. some nouns have special plural spellings: un bureau becomes des bureaux. un travail can become des travaux when referring to tasks or works. un animal becomes des animaux. do not worry about memorizing every irregular plural today. learn each new plural with its vocabulary. liaison with plural articles when les or des comes before a word beginning with a vowel sound, the final sound of the article may link to the next word. the s is pronounced like a z. for example: les amis sounds like “lay zah-mee.” des enfants sounds like “day zahn-fahn.” this linking is called liaison. repeat: les amis — the friends. pause now and answer aloud. des enfants — children or some children. pause now and answer aloud. les étudiants — the students. pause now and answer aloud. the final s of the noun itself is generally silent. the linking sound comes from the article. listen for the boundary between the words. in les amis, do not pronounce the final s of amis as a separate sound. the z sound belongs to the article and connects the two words smoothly. this is one reason french can sound as though words run together. for now, prioritize recognition over production. first learn to hear that les amis is plural. later, with repeated shadowing, the liaison will become natural in your own speech. do not force liaison everywhere. first become comfortable recognizing it in common phrases. part 6: adjectives describe nouns an adjective gives information about a noun. it can describe size, color, personality, quality, age, or many other characteristics. in french, the adjective usually comes after the noun. un projet intéressant — an interesting project. une idée originale — an original idea. un problème difficile — a difficult problem. une solution simple — a simple solution. the adjective can also follow the verb être, meaning “to be.” le projet est intéressant. — the project is interesting. la solution est simple. — the solution is simple. the most important rule is agreement. the adjective agrees with the noun in gender and number. in english, the adjective usually stays unchanged: “an interesting project” and “interesting projects.” french makes the relationship visible in writing. the ending of the adjective tells us whether it describes a masculine or feminine noun and whether that noun is singular or plural. when you build a french noun phrase, use this order of thought: choose the noun, choose the article, place the adjective in its normal position, and then adjust the adjective to match the noun. the adjective is not agreeing with the english translation; it is agreeing with the french noun. part 7: masculine and feminine adjective forms for many adjectives, the feminine form is created by adding -e to the masculine form. un client intelligent — an intelligent male client or a masculine phrase. une cliente intelligente — an intelligent female client. the adjective changes from intelligent to intelligente. another example: un employé fatigué — a tired employee, masculine form. une employée fatiguée — a tired employee, feminine form. repeat: intelligent, intelligente — intelligent, masculine and feminine forms. pause now and answer aloud. fatigué, fatiguée — tired, masculine and feminine forms. pause now and answer aloud. some adjectives already end in a silent e in the masculine form. they do not change spelling in the feminine. un collègue calme — a calm colleague. une collègue calme — a calm colleague. un enfant drôle — a funny child. une enfant drôle — a funny child. the adjective is the same in writing. the noun or article gives more information. do not assume that a word ending in -e is automatically feminine. the ending can be identical in both genders. for example, calme stays calme, but the complete phrases un collègue calme and une collègue calme still show different gender through the article and noun. this is another reason to study complete phrases instead of isolated adjective lists. you are training yourself to notice the whole grammatical pattern. some adjectives have special feminine forms: actif becomes active. heureux becomes heureuse. sérieux becomes sérieuse. sportif becomes sportive. beau becomes belle. do not try to derive every form from english. learn the pair together. some changes are predictable, such as adding -e, while others involve spelling or pronunciation changes. treat irregular forms as families: actif, active; heureux, heureuse; beau, belle. say both forms aloud and place each one in a short phrase. part 8: adjective plurals the regular plural of an adjective usually adds -s in writing. un projet intéressant — an interesting project. becomes: des projets intéressants — interesting projects. for feminine plural: une solution simple — a simple solution. becomes: des solutions simples — simple solutions. notice that an adjective ending in a silent e may look the same in singular masculine and feminine, but the plural usually adds s. some adjectives ending in -s or -x do not change their spelling in the plural. for example: un homme sérieux — a serious man. des hommes sérieux — serious men. the written adjective remains sérieux. a useful reading habit is to scan from left to right and ask what each ending is doing. in des hommes sérieux, des signals plural, hommes is the plural noun, and sérieux is the adjective that agrees with that plural noun. even when the adjective’s spelling does not visibly change, the grammar is still active. if a group contains both masculine and feminine nouns, standard written french normally uses the masculine plural form. for example: un frère et une sœur intelligents — an intelligent brother and sister. the adjective is masculine plural because the group includes a masculine noun. for an entirely feminine group: une mère et une sœur intelligentes — an intelligent mother and sister. the adjective is feminine plural. part 9: colors color words are adjectives, so they usually agree with the noun. un écran noir — a black screen. une voiture noire — a black car. des écrans noirs — black screens. des voitures noires — black cars. many colors follow the regular pattern: bleu, bleue — blue. vert, verte — green. gris, grise — gray. blanc, blanche — white. some color words do not change for gender or number when used as adjectives. for example: orange and marron are commonly invariable. un sac orange — an orange bag. une chemise orange — an orange shirt. des sacs orange — orange bags. when a color word is used as a noun, it is masculine: j’aime le bleu. — i like blue. le rouge est vif. — red is vivid. for now, focus on using colors as adjectives after nouns. color is an excellent way to practice agreement because the meaning is concrete. look around you and describe objects you can see. start with the noun and article, then add the color, and finally check whether the color needs a feminine or plural ending. part 10: adjectives before the noun most descriptive adjectives follow the noun, but a group of common adjectives usually comes before the noun. examples include words related to: - size; - age; - beauty; - goodness; - quantity or order; - and certain familiar descriptions. listen to these phrases: une grande ville — a large city. un petit problème — a small problem. un bon résultat — a good result. une jeune équipe — a young team. un vieux bâtiment — an old building. the adjective still agrees with the noun even when it comes before it. adjective position is not a reason to skip agreement. whether the adjective comes before or after the noun, the noun still controls its form. compare un petit projet and une petite tâche: the adjective moves with the noun’s gender. at the beginner level, learn the common position together with each adjective. do not try to memorize a long abstract list. repeated phrases will teach you which adjectives normally come before the noun. un petit projet — a small project. une petite tâche — a small task. de petits projets — some small projects. de petites tâches — some small tasks. three especially important adjectives have extra masculine forms before a vowel sound or silent “h.” the adjective meaning “beautiful” can be: beau before a consonant sound. bel before a vowel sound or silent “h.” belle with a feminine noun. examples: un beau quartier — a beautiful neighborhood. un bel appartement — a beautiful apartment. une belle maison — a beautiful house. the adjective meaning “new” can be: nouveau before a consonant sound. nouvel before a vowel sound or silent “h.” nouvelle with a feminine noun. examples: un nouveau projet — a new project. un nouvel emploi — a new job. une nouvelle idée — a new idea. the adjective meaning “old” can be: vieux before a consonant sound. vieil before a vowel sound or silent “h.” vieille with a feminine noun. examples: un vieux livre — an old book. un vieil ordinateur — an old computer. une vieille voiture — an old car. practice the pattern: beau, bel, belle — beautiful, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. pause now and answer aloud. nouveau, nouvel, nouvelle — new, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. pause now and answer aloud. vieux, vieil, vieille — old, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. pause now and answer aloud. meaning can change with position sometimes an adjective changes meaning depending on whether it comes before or after the noun. this is a meaning issue, not only a word-order issue. french speakers use position to communicate whether a description is familiar, subjective, literal, or part of a fixed expression. when you meet a new adjective, notice both its position and the meaning of the complete phrase. for example, “ancien professeur” can mean a former professor, while “professeur ancien” would suggest an old or ancient professor, although the second phrase is less common and context matters. similarly, “un pauvre homme” often means an unfortunate or pitiable man, while “un homme pauvre” means a man who is not wealthy. do not worry about mastering every meaning contrast at the beginning. just notice that adjective position can matter. part 11: nationalities and languages nationality words can function as adjectives or nouns. as adjectives, nationality words agree with the person or thing they describe: un ingénieur canadien — a canadian male engineer. une ingénieure canadienne — a canadian female engineer. des ingénieurs canadiens — canadian male engineers or a mixed-gender group of engineers. des ingénieures canadiennes — canadian female engineers. nationality nouns referring to people are normally capitalized in french: un canadien — a canadian man. une canadienne — a canadian woman. but nationality adjectives are not capitalized: un collègue canadien — a canadian colleague. language names are masculine nouns and are not capitalized: le français — french, the language. l’anglais — english, the language. l’espagnol — spanish, the language. say these phrases: je parle français. — i speak french. elle apprend l’anglais. — she is learning english. nous étudions l’espagnol. — we study spanish. part 12: guided review let’s review the complete system. step one: identify the noun. step two: identify its gender. step three: identify whether it is singular or plural. step four: choose the article. step five: if there is an adjective, make the adjective agree. this five-step routine is the main skill you are building. do not try to produce the entire phrase at once. work from the inside out: identify the noun, recall its gender, choose the article, place the adjective, and then check the adjective ending. with practice, these steps will happen almost simultaneously. at the beginning, it is completely normal to pause and reason through them. let’s do examples together. example one: “a small house.” house is maison, a feminine noun. small is petit, which becomes petite with a feminine noun. because the noun is singular and indefinite, use une. the phrase is: une petite maison — a small house. notice the reasoning: maison is feminine, so we need une. the adjective petit becomes petite to agree with the feminine noun. because this adjective normally comes before the noun, the final phrase is une petite maison. pause now and answer aloud. example two: “the new project.” project is masculine singular. new is nouveau before a consonant sound. the phrase is: le nouveau projet — the new project. here, projet is masculine singular, so we use le. the noun begins with a consonant sound, so the masculine form is nouveau, not nouvel. the adjective comes before the noun. pause now and answer aloud. example three: “a new apartment.” apartment is masculine and begins with a vowel sound. the masculine form of “new” becomes nouvel. the phrase is: un nouvel appartement — a new apartment. the noun is still masculine singular, so the article is un. however, appartement begins with a vowel sound. french uses nouvel before that sound to make the phrase flow more smoothly. pause now and answer aloud. example four: “some interesting books.” books are masculine plural. interesting becomes plural masculine. the phrase is: des livres intéressants — interesting books. livres is masculine plural, so the article is des. the adjective follows the noun and receives the masculine plural ending: intéressants. pause now and answer aloud. example five: “the green cars.” cars are feminine plural. green becomes feminine plural. the phrase is: les voitures vertes — the green cars. voitures is feminine plural, so we use les. the color follows the noun and becomes vertes, with both the feminine and plural endings. pause now and answer aloud. part 13: your turn pause after each prompt and produce the french phrase aloud. one: “a difficult problem.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is un problème difficile. the noun is masculine singular, so use un. the adjective difficile follows the noun and has the same spelling in masculine and feminine singular. two: “a beautiful city.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is une belle ville. the noun is feminine singular, so beau changes to belle. this common adjective normally comes before the noun. three: “some new ideas.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is de nouvelles idées. the noun is feminine plural, so nouveau becomes nouvelles. because this adjective comes before a plural noun, des commonly shortens to de. four: “the old hotels.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is les vieux hôtels. the noun is masculine plural, so the adjective is vieux. the special singular form vieil is not used here because the phrase is plural. five: “a black computer.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is un ordinateur noir. ordinateur is masculine singular. the color adjective follows the noun and remains masculine singular: noir. six: “some orange bags.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is des sacs orange. the noun is plural, but orange is an invariable color adjective in this use, so it does not add a plural ending. seven: “the serious engineers.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is les ingénieurs sérieux. eight: “a canadian woman.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is une canadienne canadienne is grammatically possible but repetitive and unnatural. a more natural phrase is une ingénieure canadienne, meaning “a canadian female engineer.” this example shows why vocabulary and context matter. do not translate word by word when a more natural phrase is available. part 14: listening discrimination listen for the article. i will say two phrases. decide whether the noun is singular or plural. le projet — the project. les projets — the projects. pause now and answer aloud. the first is singular. the second is plural. une idée — an idea. des idées — ideas. pause now and answer aloud. the first is singular. the second is plural. now listen for adjective agreement. un client satisfait — a satisfied client. une cliente satisfaite — a satisfied female client. pause now and answer aloud. the second phrase has both a feminine noun form and a feminine adjective form. un collègue sérieux — a serious colleague. des collègues sérieux — serious colleagues. pause now and answer aloud. the adjective looks the same in writing, but the article and noun show the plural. part 15: end-of-lesson summary here are the essential rules from today. every french noun has a grammatical gender: masculine or feminine. learn nouns together with their articles. the definite articles are: le for masculine singular. la for feminine singular. l’ before a vowel sound or most silent “h” words. les for all plural nouns. the indefinite articles are: un for masculine singular. une for feminine singular. des for plural nouns. most plural nouns add -s in writing, but the final s is usually silent. adjectives usually follow the noun. adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number. many feminine adjectives add -e. many plural adjectives add -s. some common adjectives usually come before the noun, especially adjectives related to size, age, beauty, goodness, and order. the forms beau, nouveau, and vieux change before a vowel sound or silent “h.” finally, practice aloud. french grammar is not only a written system. you need to connect the article, noun, and adjective in sound. for your next review, do three things: first, choose ten nouns and memorize each one with le, la, or l’. second, create five singular phrases and turn them into plural phrases. third, describe five objects around you using a noun and an adjective. for example: un écran noir — a black screen. une tasse blanche — a white cup. un petit bureau — a small office or desk. speak slowly, listen carefully, and repeat often. that is the end of this lesson. à bientôt! "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/review/error-log.md","title":"Error Log","type":"Resource","html":"<h1 id=\"error-log\">Error Log</h1>\n<p>Record errors by rule rather than merely copying the correct answer.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>My answer</th>\n<th>Correct answer</th>\n<th>Rule I missed</th>\n<th>New example from memory</th>\n<th>Retest date</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"error-categories\">Error categories</h2>\n<p>Use one of these labels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>noun gender</li>\n<li>definite article</li>\n<li>indefinite article</li>\n<li>noun plural</li>\n<li>adjective gender</li>\n<li>adjective plural</li>\n<li>adjective position</li>\n<li>special adjective form</li>\n<li>spelling/accent</li>\n<li>pronunciation/listening</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An error is closed only after you answer a new example correctly from memory on a later day.</p>","search":"# error log record errors by rule rather than merely copying the correct answer. | date | my answer | correct answer | rule i missed | new example from memory | retest date | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | ## error categories use one of these labels: - noun gender - definite article - indefinite article - noun plural - adjective gender - adjective plural - adjective position - special adjective form - spelling/accent - pronunciation/listening an error is closed only after you answer a new example correctly from memory on a later day. "}]},{"number":2,"slug":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation","title":"Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation","documents":[{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/README.md","title":"Chapter 2: Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-tre-avoir-subject-pronouns-and-negation\">Chapter 2: Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate être and avoir in the present.</li>\n<li>Choose the correct subject pronoun.</li>\n<li>Form basic negative sentences with ne...pas.</li>\n<li>Use il y a and common expressions with être and avoir.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject pronouns</li>\n<li>Être</li>\n<li>Avoir and il y a</li>\n<li>Negation</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 2: être, avoir, subject pronouns, and negation this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate être and avoir in the present. - choose the correct subject pronoun. - form basic negative sentences with ne...pas. - use il y a and common expressions with être and avoir. ## lesson sequence 1. subject pronouns 2. être 3. avoir and il y a 4. negation ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 2 Lessons: Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-lessons-tre-avoir-subject-pronouns-and-negation\">Chapter 2 Lessons: Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation</h1>\n<p>This chapter gives you the two most important verbs in French and the tools to make any sentence negative. By the end, you should be able to choose the right subject pronoun for any situation, conjugate être and avoir from memory, use each verb's fixed expressions, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>, and negate any of these structures correctly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>choose the correct subject pronoun, including the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> distinction and the uses of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code>;</li>\n<li>conjugate and use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> for identity, description, nationality, profession, and location;</li>\n<li>recognize and use common fixed expressions built on <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>;</li>\n<li>conjugate and use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, including idioms where English uses \"to be\";</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> to say what exists somewhere;</li>\n<li>form negative sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> and apply the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule after a negated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can do the following from Chapter 1:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>give the four forms of \"the\" (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>) and the four forms of \"a/some\" (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>);</li>\n<li>make an adjective agree in gender and number, for example <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contente</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contents</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contentes</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If either skill feels shaky, review Chapter 1 briefly, because this chapter's example sentences use both articles and adjective agreement constantly.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test yourself before starting.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Give the four forms of <strong>the</strong>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before a vowel sound, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> for plural.</li>\n<li>Give the three forms of <strong>a/some</strong>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> for plural indefinite nouns.</li>\n<li>Make <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code> feminine singular. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contente</code>. Add the feminine ending because it describes one feminine noun or person.</li>\n<li>Make <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code> feminine plural. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contentes</code>. Feminine plus plural both appear in the spelling.</li>\n<li>Choose the correct phrase: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit appartement</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petit appartement</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit appartement</code>, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appartement</code> is masculine singular.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review Chapter 1 first. This chapter uses articles and adjective agreement while adding two irregular verbs, so shaky noun phrases will make the new work feel harder than it is.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>For each unit, read the explanation, say every French example aloud, cover the English and test comprehension, then cover the French and write it from memory. Complete the substitution prompts before moving to the next unit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-subject-pronouns\">Unit 1: Subject Pronouns</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Every French sentence with a conjugated verb needs a subject: a noun or a subject pronoun. The subject pronoun tells you which verb ending to use.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Person</th>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1st singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound)</td>\n<td>I</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2nd singular, familiar</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td>you (one person, informal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd singular, masculine</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code></td>\n<td>he/it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd singular, feminine</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n<td>she/it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd singular, indefinite</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code></td>\n<td>one/we/people (informal \"we\")</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1st plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td>we</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2nd plural or formal singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td>you (plural, or formal singular)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd plural, masculine/mixed</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code></td>\n<td>they</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd plural, feminine</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code></td>\n<td>they (all feminine)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"tu-versus-vous\">Tu versus vous</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> with one person you know well, a family member, a child, or a peer in an informal context.</li>\n<li>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> with one person you do not know well, someone older, a person in authority, or any group of people, informal or formal.</li>\n<li>When in doubt with a single adult you have just met, choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>. Native speakers rarely mind a too-formal <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, but an unwanted <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> can sound rude.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-many-uses-of-on\">The many uses of on</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On</code> is grammatically 3rd person singular (it always takes the same verb form as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>), but its meaning is flexible:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On parle français ici.</code> — People speak French here. / French is spoken here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On y va ?</code> — Shall we go?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous, on habite à Lyon.</code> — We live in Lyon. (very common spoken French: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In everyday spoken French, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code> very often simply replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>. Written and formal French still prefer <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code> sound identical before a consonant (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlent</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles parlent</code> both start with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il-</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">el-</code> but the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> is silent). Before a vowel sound, the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> links and is pronounced <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">z</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils arrivent</code> sounds like \"il-z-arrivent.\" Listen for this liaison; it is often the only spoken clue that the subject is plural.</p>\n<h3 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h3>\n<p>Choose the correct pronoun for each situation:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You are talking to your grandmother.</li>\n<li>You are talking about your two female cousins together.</li>\n<li>You are talking about yourself and your brother (a mixed pair).</li>\n<li>You are asking your new coworker (whom you have just met) a question.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Grandmother: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> — one family member normally takes the familiar form.</li>\n<li>Two female cousins together: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code> — the group is plural and entirely feminine.</li>\n<li>Yourself and your brother: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> or spoken <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code> — the group includes you, and it is mixed.</li>\n<li>New coworker: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> — one adult you have just met takes polite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the skeleton task, model answers are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> + verb, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code> + verb, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> + verb or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code> + verb, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> + verb. The trap is that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> can be one formal person or any group.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Build a French subject + verb skeleton for each situation above, without conjugating yet: for example, \"you asking your grandmother\" → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> + verb. Say each pronoun aloud three times before moving on.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model skeletons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Grandmother: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> + verb — one family member normally takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n<li>Two female cousins: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code> + verb — the group is plural and entirely feminine.</li>\n<li>You and your brother: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> + verb or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code> + verb — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> is the standard written form; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code> is common in speech and still takes a singular verb form.</li>\n<li>New coworker: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> + verb — one adult you have just met takes polite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The trap is that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> can mean either one formal person or any group. The verb form will be the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form in both cases.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-tre-to-be\">Unit 2: Être (to be)</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Être</code> is the single most common French verb. Learn its six forms as one block, out loud, before you need them individually.</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-of-tre\">Present tense of être</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">es</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"main-uses-of-tre\">Main uses of être</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Être</code> links a subject to a description, identity, nationality, profession, or location.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Identity</strong>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est mon frère.</code> — This is my brother.</li>\n<li><strong>Description</strong>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est fatiguée.</code> — She is tired.</li>\n<li><strong>Nationality</strong>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes canadiens.</code> — We are Canadian.</li>\n<li><strong>Profession</strong> (no article before an unmodified profession noun): <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est ingénieur.</code> — He is an engineer.</li>\n<li>Compare: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un bon ingénieur.</code> — He is a good engineer. (An adjective before the profession requires <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'est un/une...</code>.)</li>\n<li><strong>Location</strong>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous êtes à Paris.</code> — You are in Paris.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake\">Common mistake</h3>\n<p>English speakers often add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un/une</code> before a profession after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>: incorrect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Il est un ingénieur.</code> The article disappears unless the profession noun is modified by an adjective, in which case you switch to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'est un/une...</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"fixed-expressions-with-tre\">Fixed expressions with être</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Expression</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être d'accord</code></td>\n<td>to agree</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être en retard</code></td>\n<td>to be late</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être en avance</code></td>\n<td>to be early</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être à l'heure</code></td>\n<td>to be on time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être en train de + infinitive</code></td>\n<td>to be in the middle of doing something</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être content(e)/heureux(-se) de</code></td>\n<td>to be happy about</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis d'accord avec toi.</code> — I agree with you.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes en train de travailler.</code> — We are in the middle of working.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est en retard pour la réunion.</code> — She is late for the meeting.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Complete each frame with a different subject and a true or invented detail:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ suis ___.</code> (a nationality or profession)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ est en train de ___.</code> (an -er verb infinitive)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ sommes d'accord avec ___.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then read all three aloud and rewrite them from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary, but each frame must use the required form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and a complete ending.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis canadien.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code>. Change the adjective to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> if it describes a female speaker.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est en train de travailler.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en train de</code> must be followed by an infinitive such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travailler</code>, not a conjugated form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes d'accord avec vous.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code> introduces the person you agree with.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if the subject and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> form do not match, or if you wrote an article before an unmodified profession, as in the beginner error <strong>Il est un ingénieur</strong>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-avoir-to-have-and-il-y-a\">Unit 3: Avoir (to have) and il y a</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avoir</code> translates literally as \"to have,\" but French uses it in many situations where English uses \"to be.\" Learning avoir's idioms as fixed chunks avoids many literal-translation mistakes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-of-avoir\">Present tense of avoir</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ont</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"expressions-with-avoir\">Expressions with avoir</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>Literal meaning</th>\n<th>Natural English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir ___ ans</code></td>\n<td>to have ___ years</td>\n<td>to be ___ years old</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir faim</code></td>\n<td>to have hunger</td>\n<td>to be hungry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir soif</code></td>\n<td>to have thirst</td>\n<td>to be thirsty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir chaud</code></td>\n<td>to have heat</td>\n<td>to be hot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir froid</code></td>\n<td>to have cold</td>\n<td>to be cold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir raison</code></td>\n<td>to have reason</td>\n<td>to be right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir tort</code></td>\n<td>to have wrong</td>\n<td>to be wrong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir peur de</code></td>\n<td>to have fear of</td>\n<td>to be afraid of</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir besoin de</code></td>\n<td>to have need of</td>\n<td>to need</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir envie de</code></td>\n<td>to have desire of</td>\n<td>to feel like/want</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir de la chance</code></td>\n<td>to have luck</td>\n<td>to be lucky</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt-cinq ans.</code> — I am twenty-five years old.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as froid ? Prends une veste.</code> — Are you cold? Take a jacket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a besoin d'un stylo.</code> — She needs a pen.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons envie de sortir ce soir.</code> — We feel like going out tonight.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake\">Common mistake</h3>\n<p>English speakers often translate age with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>: incorrect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Je suis vingt-cinq ans.</code> French always uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> for age: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt-cinq ans.</code></p>\n<h3 id=\"il-y-a-there-is-there-are\">Il y a: there is/there are</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code> is invariable — it never changes for number. Use it to state that something exists.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une pharmacie au coin de la rue.</code> — There is a pharmacy on the corner.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a des enfants dans le jardin.</code> — There are children in the garden.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a un problème.</code> — There is a problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Write four sentences using four different <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> expressions from the table, then one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> describing your home or neighborhood. Read them aloud, cover them, and rewrite from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A good set might be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code> — French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> for age, literally to have years. Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> for age.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons faim.</code> — hunger uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a besoin d'un stylo.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir besoin de</code> is the fixed expression for needing something.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils ont froid.</code> — feeling cold uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>; the subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ont</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une boulangerie dans ma rue.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> means there is/there are and does not change for plural nouns.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own sentences, check the verb form first: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu as</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils ont</code>. Then check that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> stayed fixed.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-negation-with-ne-pas\">Unit 4: Negation with ne...pas</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>To make a sentence negative, place <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> before the conjugated verb and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> immediately after it. Before a vowel sound, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> shortens to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pattern\">Pattern</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Affirmative</th>\n<th>Negative</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis prêt.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas prêt.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a un chat.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'a pas de chat.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a du café.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de café.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne sommes pas en retard.</code> — We are not late.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils n'ont pas faim maintenant.</code> — They are not hungry right now.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de problème.</code> — There is no problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-de-rule-after-a-negated-avoir\">The de rule after a negated avoir</h3>\n<p>When a direct object introduced by an indefinite article (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>) or a partitive article (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'</code>, covered in Chapter 9) follows a negated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>, that article normally becomes simply <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code> before a vowel sound). This rule does <strong>not</strong> apply after a negated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, and it does not change a <em>definite</em> article (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai une voiture.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas de voiture.</code> — I have a car. → I do not have a car.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a des chaises.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de chaises.</code> — There are chairs. → There are no chairs.</li>\n<li>but: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est une voiture.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce n'est pas une voiture.</code> — It is a car. → It is not a car. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, so the article stays)</li>\n<li>and: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime le café.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'aime pas le café.</code> — I like coffee. → I do not like coffee. (definite article stays)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Forgetting the second negation word.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> alone is not enough in standard written French: write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne sais pas</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Je sais pas</code> (that form exists only in very casual speech).</li>\n<li><strong>Applying the de rule after être.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce n'est pas un problème</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Ce n'est pas de problème</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Applying the de rule to a definite article.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'aime pas les légumes</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Elle n'aime pas de légumes</code>, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les légumes</code> here is a definite article expressing a general category, not an indefinite quantity.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before a vowel sound.</strong> Write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Il ne y a pas</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Turn each sentence negative, applying the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule where it belongs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai un ordinateur.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes fatigués.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une solution.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu aimes le sport.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas d'ordinateur.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ordinateur</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'ordinateur</code> after negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>; use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne sommes pas fatigués.</code> — with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, there is no <strong>de</strong> rule. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> simply wrap the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de solution.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il n'y a pas</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une solution</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de solution</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'aimes pas le sport.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimes</code> begins with a vowel sound, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code>. The definite article <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> does not change after negation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The main trap is overusing the <strong>de</strong> rule. It applies after negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> with indefinite or partitive objects, not after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and not with definite articles such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine subject pronouns, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>, and negation together, the way you will actually need them in conversation. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis étudiant, mais ma sœur est ingénieure.</code> — I am a student, but my sister is an engineer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons deux chambres et un petit jardin.</code> — We have two bedrooms and a small garden.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une pharmacie près de la maison.</code> — There is a pharmacy near the house.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes voisins ne sont pas bruyants.</code> — My neighbors are not noisy.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On n'a pas de café, alors on a soif.</code> — We don't have coffee, so we're thirsty.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a vingt ans et elle est en train d'étudier le français.</code> — She is twenty years old and she is in the middle of studying French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous n'êtes pas en retard ; il est encore tôt.</code> — You are not late; it is still early.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>The sentences are visible, so check the version you wrote from memory against these traps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Verb forms.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code> are not interchangeable; the subject controls the form.</li>\n<li><strong>Negation frames.</strong> Sentence 4 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne sont pas</code>; sentence 5 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'a pas de</code>; sentence 7 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'êtes pas</code>. Both halves must be present.</li>\n<li><strong>The </strong>de<strong> rule.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On n'a pas de café</code> changes the object after negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>. Do not write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas un café</code> here.</li>\n<li><strong>Accents and apostrophes.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sœur</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiant</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'étudier</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'êtes</code> all need exact spelling.</li>\n<li><strong>Silent endings.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voisins</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bruyants</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigués</code> may hide letters you still must write.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Score each sentence for meaning, verb form, negation, and spelling. Rewrite only the sentence whose score is not full.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence above, circle the conjugated verb (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>) and underline the word or phrase that tells you the subject.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Conjugated verb or verbs</th>\n<th>Subject cue</th>\n<th>Verb family</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma sœur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code></td>\n<td>fixed expression <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> idea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes voisins</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The subject cue is the word that forces the verb form. In sentence 5, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code> takes a singular verb form even when it means <strong>we</strong>.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail (the profession, the number of rooms, the neighbor's trait).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to the verb form, the article, and the negation.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>One worked set; your details may differ.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes étudiants, mais notre sœur est ingénieure.</code> Detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis professeur, mais ma sœur est médecin.</code></li>\n<li>Subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils ont deux chambres et un petit jardin.</code> Detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons trois chambres et une petite terrasse.</code></li>\n<li>Subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une pharmacie près de notre maison.</code> Detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une boulangerie près de la maison.</code></li>\n<li>Subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma voisine n'est pas bruyante.</code> Detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes voisins ne sont pas patients.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The rule is that a changed subject can force several changes at once: verb form, possessive word, adjective agreement, and sometimes the article.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>, and one negative sentence. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis étudiant.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a un parc près de ma maison.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas de voiture.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-marking criteria: include at least one correct form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, one correct form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, one fixed <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>, and one negative sentence with both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code>. If the negative uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> with an indefinite object, the article should become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code>, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas de voiture</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-meeting-a-new-roommate\">Mini-dialogue: meeting a new roommate</h2>\n<p><strong>Sophie :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour ! Tu es la nouvelle coloc' ?</code> — Hi! Are you the new roommate?</p>\n<p><strong>Amina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, c'est moi. Je suis Amina, je suis étudiante en médecine.</code> — Yes, that's me. I'm Amina, I'm a medical student.</p>\n<p><strong>Sophie :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Enchantée. Moi, c'est Sophie. Tu as faim ? Il y a du pain et des fruits dans la cuisine.</code> — Nice to meet you. I'm Sophie. Are you hungry? There is bread and fruit in the kitchen.</p>\n<p><strong>Amina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Non merci, je n'ai pas très faim. Mais j'ai soif !</code> — No thank you, I'm not very hungry. But I'm thirsty!</p>\n<p><strong>Sophie :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a de l'eau et du jus dans le frigo. Tu n'es pas fatiguée après le voyage ?</code> — There is water and juice in the fridge. Aren't you tired after the trip?</p>\n<p><strong>Amina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un peu. Mais je suis contente d'être ici. Il n'y a pas de bruit dans la rue, c'est agréable.</code> — A little. But I'm happy to be here. There's no noise on the street, that's nice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find every form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> in the dialogue and identify which subject pronoun or noun controls it.</li>\n<li>Find every form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> and match it to its meaning (possession, hunger, thirst, age, or an idiom).</li>\n<li>Find two negative sentences and explain whether the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule applied and why.</li>\n<li>This dialogue never uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être en train de</code>. Rewrite one of Amina's lines so that it does.</li>\n<li>Rewrite Sophie's second line using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, making every necessary change.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">es</code> is controlled by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'est moi</code> is fixed with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> is controlled by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je</code>; another <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> is controlled by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'est Sophie</code> is fixed with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">es</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'es pas fatiguée</code> is controlled by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je suis contente</code> is controlled by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code> is an infinitive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'est agréable</code> is fixed with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code>.</li>\n<li>Forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as faim ?</code> means hunger; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code> states existence; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je n'ai pas très faim</code> means hunger; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai soif</code> means thirst; another <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code> states existence; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas</code> states non-existence.</li>\n<li>Negative sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je n'ai pas très faim</code> uses negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, but there is no object article to change to <strong>de</strong>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'es pas fatiguée</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, so no <strong>de</strong> rule. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de bruit</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>, so the object becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de bruit</code>.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. One natural rewrite: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un peu. Je suis en train de défaire mes valises, mais je suis contente d'être ici.</code> — the structure is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> conjugated, then <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en train de</code>, then an <strong>infinitive</strong>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">défaire</code> stays in the infinitive; writing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je suis en train de je défais</code> is the classic error. Note that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'être ici</code> in the original line is a different structure: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content de</code> simply takes an infinitive, and describes a state rather than an action in progress.</li>\n<li>Sophie's second line with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Enchantée. Moi, c'est Sophie. Vous avez faim ? Il y a du pain et des fruits dans la cuisine.</code> The verb changes from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avez</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-ma-nouvelle-vie-montr-al\">Connected reading: ma nouvelle vie à Montréal</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'appelle Karim et je suis à Montréal depuis deux mois. Je suis étudiant et j'ai vingt-trois ans. Mon appartement n'est pas très grand, mais il y a une belle vue sur le fleuve. Le matin, j'ai souvent faim, alors je mange un fruit et je bois un café. Mes colocataires sont sympathiques : Léa est québécoise et elle est en train d'apprendre l'espagnol ; Tom est américain et il a toujours des histoires intéressantes. On n'est jamais seuls ici, et je n'ai pas peur de parler français, même si je fais des erreurs.</code></p>\n<p>— My name is Karim and I have been in Montreal for two months. I am a student and I am twenty-three years old. My apartment is not very big, but there is a beautiful view of the river. In the morning, I am often hungry, so I eat a fruit and drink a coffee. My roommates are nice: Léa is from Quebec and she is in the process of learning Spanish; Tom is American and he always has interesting stories. We are never alone here, and I am not afraid to speak French, even if I make mistakes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List every form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> in the text and give its subject.</li>\n<li>Find the one negative sentence built with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...jamais</code> (a cousin of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> you will study fully in Chapter 11) and the one built with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>.</li>\n<li>Identify the two nationality adjectives and explain their agreement.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the first three sentences replacing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Karim</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nadia</code> and making every needed change (pronouns, adjective agreement, verb forms).</li>\n<li>Write three true sentences about your own living situation using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m'appelle</code> is a naming expression, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>; another <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'est</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon appartement</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code> is fixed; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes colocataires</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Léa</code>; another <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tom</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'est</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> has subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On n'est jamais seuls ici</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...jamais</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je n'ai pas peur de parler français</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>.</li>\n<li>Nationality adjectives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">québécoise</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Léa</code>, one feminine person; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">américain</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tom</code>, one masculine person.</li>\n<li>With Nadia: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'appelle Nadia et je suis à Montréal depuis deux mois. Je suis étudiante et j'ai vingt-trois ans. Mon appartement n'est pas très grand, mais il y a une belle vue sur le fleuve.</code> The main change is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiant</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiante</code>.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. A model sentence is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai un petit appartement, mais il y a une grande fenêtre.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check the subject before the verb every time. The passage mixes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>, named people, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code>, so guessing by nearby nouns is risky.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"tre-and-avoir-side-by-side\">Être and avoir, side by side</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>être</th>\n<th>avoir</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">es</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ont</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"negation-quick-reference\">Negation quick reference</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Any conjugated verb</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code>) + verb + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indefinite/partitive object after negated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td>article becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Definite article object</td>\n<td>stays unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Object after negated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td>stays unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-habits\">Core habits</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> as two complete six-form blocks, not as isolated forms.</li>\n<li>Memorize <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> idioms (age, hunger, thirst, needs, fear) as full phrases, not word-by-word translations.</li>\n<li>Say the negative frame <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> as a single unit surrounding the verb.</li>\n<li>Check every negated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> sentence for the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule before moving on.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-10 sentences introducing yourself and your living situation, as in the connected reading. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least three different subject pronouns;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> used at least three times, including once with a profession or nationality and once in an expression such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être en train de</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être d'accord</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> used at least three times, including one idiom (age, hunger, thirst, need, or fear);</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>;</li>\n<li>two negative sentences, at least one of which correctly applies the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb and label it <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>;</li>\n<li>underline every negation frame;</li>\n<li>check each negated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> sentence for the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Model paragraph:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'appelle Ana et je suis à Toronto. Je suis étudiante et j'ai vingt ans. Mon appartement est petit, mais il y a une grande fenêtre dans ma chambre. Nous avons une cuisine calme et deux chambres. Mes colocataires sont sympathiques. Le matin, j'ai souvent faim et j'ai besoin d'un café. En ce moment, je suis en train d'étudier le français. On n'a pas de jardin, mais nous ne sommes pas tristes.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: subject pronouns include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> appears in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je suis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon appartement est</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes colocataires sont</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je suis en train de</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> appears in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai vingt ans</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai souvent faim</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai besoin de</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On n'a pas</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> appears once; the negatives are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On n'a pas de jardin</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous ne sommes pas tristes</code>, with the <strong>de</strong> rule correctly applied after negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>you use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> for age, hunger, thirst, or need;</li>\n<li>a negative sentence lacks either <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code>;</li>\n<li>a negated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> sentence keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> is needed;</li>\n<li>an adjective such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiante</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sympathiques</code> does not agree;</li>\n<li>you use only one subject pronoun across the whole paragraph.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 2 lessons: être, avoir, subject pronouns, and negation this chapter gives you the two most important verbs in french and the tools to make any sentence negative. by the end, you should be able to choose the right subject pronoun for any situation, conjugate être and avoir from memory, use each verb's fixed expressions, use `il y a`, and negate any of these structures correctly. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. choose the correct subject pronoun, including the `tu`/`vous` distinction and the uses of `on`; 2. conjugate and use `être` for identity, description, nationality, profession, and location; 3. recognize and use common fixed expressions built on `être`; 4. conjugate and use `avoir`, including idioms where english uses \"to be\"; 5. use `il y a` to say what exists somewhere; 6. form negative sentences with `ne...pas` and apply the `de` rule after a negated `avoir`. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can do the following from chapter 1: - give the four forms of \"the\" (`le`, `la`, `l'`, `les`) and the four forms of \"a/some\" (`un`, `une`, `des`); - make an adjective agree in gender and number, for example `content` -> `contente` -> `contents` -> `contentes`. if either skill feels shaky, review chapter 1 briefly, because this chapter's example sentences use both articles and adjective agreement constantly. test yourself before starting. 1. give the four forms of **the**. — `le`, `la`, `l'`, `les`. use `l'` before a vowel sound, and `les` for plural. 2. give the three forms of **a/some**. — `un`, `une`, `des`. use `des` for plural indefinite nouns. 3. make `content` feminine singular. — `contente`. add the feminine ending because it describes one feminine noun or person. 4. make `content` feminine plural. — `contentes`. feminine plus plural both appear in the spelling. 5. choose the correct phrase: `un petit appartement` or `une petit appartement`. — `un petit appartement`, because `appartement` is masculine singular. if you missed two or more, review chapter 1 first. this chapter uses articles and adjective agreement while adding two irregular verbs, so shaky noun phrases will make the new work feel harder than it is. ## how to study this chapter for each unit, read the explanation, say every french example aloud, cover the english and test comprehension, then cover the french and write it from memory. complete the substitution prompts before moving to the next unit. ## unit 1: subject pronouns ### core idea every french sentence with a conjugated verb needs a subject: a noun or a subject pronoun. the subject pronoun tells you which verb ending to use. | person | pronoun | english | |---|---|---| | 1st singular | `je` (`j'` before a vowel sound) | i | | 2nd singular, familiar | `tu` | you (one person, informal) | | 3rd singular, masculine | `il` | he/it | | 3rd singular, feminine | `elle` | she/it | | 3rd singular, indefinite | `on` | one/we/people (informal \"we\") | | 1st plural | `nous` | we | | 2nd plural or formal singular | `vous` | you (plural, or formal singular) | | 3rd plural, masculine/mixed | `ils` | they | | 3rd plural, feminine | `elles` | they (all feminine) | ### tu versus vous - use `tu` with one person you know well, a family member, a child, or a peer in an informal context. - use `vous` with one person you do not know well, someone older, a person in authority, or any group of people, informal or formal. - when in doubt with a single adult you have just met, choose `vous`. native speakers rarely mind a too-formal `vous`, but an unwanted `tu` can sound rude. ### the many uses of on `on` is grammatically 3rd person singular (it always takes the same verb form as `il`/`elle`), but its meaning is flexible: - `on parle français ici.` — people speak french here. / french is spoken here. - `on y va ?` — shall we go? - `nous, on habite à lyon.` — we live in lyon. (very common spoken french: `on` replaces `nous`) in everyday spoken french, `on` very often simply replaces `nous`. written and formal french still prefer `nous`. ### pronunciation note `ils` and `elles` sound identical before a consonant (`ils parlent` and `elles parlent` both start with `il-`/`el-` but the final `-s` is silent). before a vowel sound, the `-s` links and is pronounced `z`: `ils arrivent` sounds like \"il-z-arrivent.\" listen for this liaison; it is often the only spoken clue that the subject is plural. ### retrieval check choose the correct pronoun for each situation: 1. you are talking to your grandmother. 2. you are talking about your two female cousins together. 3. you are talking about yourself and your brother (a mixed pair). 4. you are asking your new coworker (whom you have just met) a question. 1. grandmother: `tu` — one family member normally takes the familiar form. 2. two female cousins together: `elles` — the group is plural and entirely feminine. 3. yourself and your brother: `nous` or spoken `on` — the group includes you, and it is mixed. 4. new coworker: `vous` — one adult you have just met takes polite `vous`. for the skeleton task, model answers are `tu` + verb, `elles` + verb, `nous` + verb or `on` + verb, and `vous` + verb. the trap is that `vous` can be one formal person or any group. ### sentence-building practice build a french subject + verb skeleton for each situation above, without conjugating yet: for example, \"you asking your grandmother\" -> `tu` + verb. say each pronoun aloud three times before moving on. model skeletons: 1. grandmother: `tu` + verb — one family member normally takes `tu`. 2. two female cousins: `elles` + verb — the group is plural and entirely feminine. 3. you and your brother: `nous` + verb or `on` + verb — `nous` is the standard written form; `on` is common in speech and still takes a singular verb form. 4. new coworker: `vous` + verb — one adult you have just met takes polite `vous`. the trap is that `vous` can mean either one formal person or any group. the verb form will be the `vous` form in both cases. ## unit 2: être (to be) ### core idea `être` is the single most common french verb. learn its six forms as one block, out loud, before you need them individually. ### present tense of être | pronoun | form | pronoun | form | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `suis` | `nous` | `sommes` | | `tu` | `es` | `vous` | `êtes` | | `il/elle/on` | `est` | `ils/elles` | `sont` | ### main uses of être `être` links a subject to a description, identity, nationality, profession, or location. - **identity**: `c'est mon frère.` — this is my brother. - **description**: `elle est fatiguée.` — she is tired. - **nationality**: `nous sommes canadiens.` — we are canadian. - **profession** (no article before an unmodified profession noun): `il est ingénieur.` — he is an engineer. - compare: `c'est un bon ingénieur.` — he is a good engineer. (an adjective before the profession requires `c'est un/une...`.) - **location**: `vous êtes à paris.` — you are in paris. ### common mistake english speakers often add `un/une` before a profession after `être`: incorrect `*il est un ingénieur.` the article disappears unless the profession noun is modified by an adjective, in which case you switch to `c'est un/une...`. ### fixed expressions with être | expression | meaning | |---|---| | `être d'accord` | to agree | | `être en retard` | to be late | | `être en avance` | to be early | | `être à l'heure` | to be on time | | `être en train de + infinitive` | to be in the middle of doing something | | `être content(e)/heureux(-se) de` | to be happy about | - `je suis d'accord avec toi.` — i agree with you. - `nous sommes en train de travailler.` — we are in the middle of working. - `elle est en retard pour la réunion.` — she is late for the meeting. ### sentence-building practice complete each frame with a different subject and a true or invented detail: 1. `___ suis ___.` (a nationality or profession) 2. `___ est en train de ___.` (an -er verb infinitive) 3. `___ sommes d'accord avec ___.` then read all three aloud and rewrite them from memory. answers vary, but each frame must use the required form of `être` and a complete ending. 1. `je suis canadien.` — `je` takes `suis`. change the adjective to `canadienne` if it describes a female speaker. 2. `elle est en train de travailler.` — `elle` takes `est`, and `en train de` must be followed by an infinitive such as `travailler`, not a conjugated form. 3. `nous sommes d'accord avec vous.` — `nous` takes `sommes`; `avec` introduces the person you agree with. mark yourself wrong if the subject and `être` form do not match, or if you wrote an article before an unmodified profession, as in the beginner error **il est un ingénieur**. ## unit 3: avoir (to have) and il y a ### core idea `avoir` translates literally as \"to have,\" but french uses it in many situations where english uses \"to be.\" learning avoir's idioms as fixed chunks avoids many literal-translation mistakes. ### present tense of avoir | pronoun | form | pronoun | form | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `ai` | `nous` | `avons` | | `tu` | `as` | `vous` | `avez` | | `il/elle/on` | `a` | `ils/elles` | `ont` | ### expressions with avoir | french | literal meaning | natural english | |---|---|---| | `avoir ___ ans` | to have ___ years | to be ___ years old | | `avoir faim` | to have hunger | to be hungry | | `avoir soif` | to have thirst | to be thirsty | | `avoir chaud` | to have heat | to be hot | | `avoir froid` | to have cold | to be cold | | `avoir raison` | to have reason | to be right | | `avoir tort` | to have wrong | to be wrong | | `avoir peur de` | to have fear of | to be afraid of | | `avoir besoin de` | to have need of | to need | | `avoir envie de` | to have desire of | to feel like/want | | `avoir de la chance` | to have luck | to be lucky | - `j'ai vingt-cinq ans.` — i am twenty-five years old. - `tu as froid ? prends une veste.` — are you cold? take a jacket. - `elle a besoin d'un stylo.` — she needs a pen. - `nous avons envie de sortir ce soir.` — we feel like going out tonight. ### common mistake english speakers often translate age with `être`: incorrect `*je suis vingt-cinq ans.` french always uses `avoir` for age: `j'ai vingt-cinq ans.` ### il y a: there is/there are `il y a` is invariable — it never changes for number. use it to state that something exists. - `il y a une pharmacie au coin de la rue.` — there is a pharmacy on the corner. - `il y a des enfants dans le jardin.` — there are children in the garden. - `il y a un problème.` — there is a problem. ### sentence-building practice write four sentences using four different `avoir` expressions from the table, then one sentence with `il y a` describing your home or neighborhood. read them aloud, cover them, and rewrite from memory. answers vary. a good set might be: 1. `j'ai vingt ans.` — french uses `avoir` for age, literally to have years. do not use `être` for age. 2. `nous avons faim.` — hunger uses `avoir`, not `être`. 3. `elle a besoin d'un stylo.` — `avoir besoin de` is the fixed expression for needing something. 4. `ils ont froid.` — feeling cold uses `avoir`; the subject `ils` takes `ont`. 5. `il y a une boulangerie dans ma rue.` — `il y a` means there is/there are and does not change for plural nouns. for your own sentences, check the verb form first: `j'ai`, `tu as`, `il a`, `nous avons`, `vous avez`, `ils ont`. then check that `il y a` stayed fixed. ## unit 4: negation with ne...pas ### core idea to make a sentence negative, place `ne` before the conjugated verb and `pas` immediately after it. before a vowel sound, `ne` shortens to `n'`. ### pattern | affirmative | negative | |---|---| | `je suis prêt.` | `je ne suis pas prêt.` | | `elle a un chat.` | `elle n'a pas de chat.` | | `il y a du café.` | `il n'y a pas de café.` | - `nous ne sommes pas en retard.` — we are not late. - `ils n'ont pas faim maintenant.` — they are not hungry right now. - `il n'y a pas de problème.` — there is no problem. ### the de rule after a negated avoir when a direct object introduced by an indefinite article (`un`, `une`, `des`) or a partitive article (`du`, `de la`, `de l'`, covered in chapter 9) follows a negated `avoir` or `il y a`, that article normally becomes simply `de` (`d'` before a vowel sound). this rule does **not** apply after a negated `être`, and it does not change a *definite* article (`le`, `la`, `les`). - `j'ai une voiture.` -> `je n'ai pas de voiture.` — i have a car. -> i do not have a car. - `il y a des chaises.` -> `il n'y a pas de chaises.` — there are chairs. -> there are no chairs. - but: `c'est une voiture.` -> `ce n'est pas une voiture.` — it is a car. -> it is not a car. (`être`, so the article stays) - and: `j'aime le café.` -> `je n'aime pas le café.` — i like coffee. -> i do not like coffee. (definite article stays) ### common mistakes to avoid 1. **forgetting the second negation word.** `ne` alone is not enough in standard written french: write `je ne sais pas`, not `*je sais pas` (that form exists only in very casual speech). 2. **applying the de rule after être.** `ce n'est pas un problème`, not `*ce n'est pas de problème`. 3. **applying the de rule to a definite article.** `elle n'aime pas les légumes`, not `*elle n'aime pas de légumes`, because `les légumes` here is a definite article expressing a general category, not an indefinite quantity. 4. **forgetting `n'` before a vowel sound.** write `il n'y a pas`, not `*il ne y a pas`. ### sentence-building practice turn each sentence negative, applying the `de` rule where it belongs: 1. `j'ai un ordinateur.` 2. `nous sommes fatigués.` 3. `il y a une solution.` 4. `tu aimes le sport.` 1. `je n'ai pas d'ordinateur.` — `un ordinateur` becomes `d'ordinateur` after negative `avoir`; use `d'` before a vowel sound. 2. `nous ne sommes pas fatigués.` — with `être`, there is no **de** rule. `ne` and `pas` simply wrap the conjugated verb. 3. `il n'y a pas de solution.` — `il y a` becomes `il n'y a pas`, and `une solution` changes to `de solution`. 4. `tu n'aimes pas le sport.` — `aimes` begins with a vowel sound, so `ne` becomes `n'`. the definite article `le` does not change after negation. the main trap is overusing the **de** rule. it applies after negative `avoir` and `il y a` with indefinite or partitive objects, not after `être` and not with definite articles such as `le`. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine subject pronouns, `être`, `avoir`, `il y a`, and negation together, the way you will actually need them in conversation. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je suis étudiant, mais ma sœur est ingénieure.` — i am a student, but my sister is an engineer. 2. `nous avons deux chambres et un petit jardin.` — we have two bedrooms and a small garden. 3. `il y a une pharmacie près de la maison.` — there is a pharmacy near the house. 4. `mes voisins ne sont pas bruyants.` — my neighbors are not noisy. 5. `on n'a pas de café, alors on a soif.` — we don't have coffee, so we're thirsty. 6. `elle a vingt ans et elle est en train d'étudier le français.` — she is twenty years old and she is in the middle of studying french. 7. `vous n'êtes pas en retard ; il est encore tôt.` — you are not late; it is still early. the sentences are visible, so check the version you wrote from memory against these traps. - **verb forms.** `suis`, `est`, `sommes`, `sont`, `a`, `avons`, and `êtes` are not interchangeable; the subject controls the form. - **negation frames.** sentence 4 has `ne sont pas`; sentence 5 has `n'a pas de`; sentence 7 has `n'êtes pas`. both halves must be present. - **the **de** rule.** `on n'a pas de café` changes the object after negative `avoir`. do not write `pas un café` here. - **accents and apostrophes.** `sœur`, `étudiant`, `d'étudier`, and `n'êtes` all need exact spelling. - **silent endings.** `voisins`, `bruyants`, and `fatigués` may hide letters you still must write. score each sentence for meaning, verb form, negation, and spelling. rewrite only the sentence whose score is not full. ### notice and mark for each sentence above, circle the conjugated verb (`être` or `avoir`) and underline the word or phrase that tells you the subject. | # | conjugated verb or verbs | subject cue | verb family | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `suis`, `est` | `je`, `ma sœur` | `être`, `être` | | 2 | `avons` | `nous` | `avoir` | | 3 | `il y a` | fixed expression `il y a` | `avoir` idea | | 4 | `sont` | `mes voisins` | `être` | | 5 | `a`, `a` | `on`, `on` | `avoir`, `avoir` | | 6 | `a`, `est` | `elle`, `elle` | `avoir`, `être` | | 7 | `êtes`, `est` | `vous`, `il` | `être`, `être` | the subject cue is the word that forces the verb form. in sentence 5, `on` takes a singular verb form even when it means **we**. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail (the profession, the number of rooms, the neighbor's trait). make every required change to the verb form, the article, and the negation. one worked set; your details may differ. 1. subject change: `nous sommes étudiants, mais notre sœur est ingénieure.` detail change: `je suis professeur, mais ma sœur est médecin.` 2. subject change: `ils ont deux chambres et un petit jardin.` detail change: `nous avons trois chambres et une petite terrasse.` 3. subject change: `il y a une pharmacie près de notre maison.` detail change: `il y a une boulangerie près de la maison.` 4. subject change: `ma voisine n'est pas bruyante.` detail change: `mes voisins ne sont pas patients.` the rule is that a changed subject can force several changes at once: verb form, possessive word, adjective agreement, and sometimes the article. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using `être`, `avoir`, `il y a`, and one negative sentence. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. answers vary. a model set: 1. `je suis étudiant.` 2. `j'ai vingt ans.` 3. `il y a un parc près de ma maison.` 4. `je n'ai pas de voiture.` self-marking criteria: include at least one correct form of `être`, one correct form of `avoir`, one fixed `il y a`, and one negative sentence with both `ne` and `pas`. if the negative uses `avoir` with an indefinite object, the article should become `de` or `d'`, as in `je n'ai pas de voiture`. ## mini-dialogue: meeting a new roommate **sophie :** `bonjour ! tu es la nouvelle coloc' ?` — hi! are you the new roommate? **amina :** `oui, c'est moi. je suis amina, je suis étudiante en médecine.` — yes, that's me. i'm amina, i'm a medical student. **sophie :** `enchantée. moi, c'est sophie. tu as faim ? il y a du pain et des fruits dans la cuisine.` — nice to meet you. i'm sophie. are you hungry? there is bread and fruit in the kitchen. **amina :** `non merci, je n'ai pas très faim. mais j'ai soif !` — no thank you, i'm not very hungry. but i'm thirsty! **sophie :** `il y a de l'eau et du jus dans le frigo. tu n'es pas fatiguée après le voyage ?` — there is water and juice in the fridge. aren't you tired after the trip? **amina :** `un peu. mais je suis contente d'être ici. il n'y a pas de bruit dans la rue, c'est agréable.` — a little. but i'm happy to be here. there's no noise on the street, that's nice. ### guided analysis 1. find every form of `être` in the dialogue and identify which subject pronoun or noun controls it. 2. find every form of `avoir` and match it to its meaning (possession, hunger, thirst, age, or an idiom). 3. find two negative sentences and explain whether the `de` rule applied and why. 4. this dialogue never uses `être en train de`. rewrite one of amina's lines so that it does. 5. rewrite sophie's second line using `vous` instead of `tu`, making every necessary change. 1. forms of `être`: `es` is controlled by `tu`; `est` in `c'est moi` is fixed with `ce`; `suis` is controlled by `je`; another `suis` is controlled by `je`; `est` in `c'est sophie` is fixed with `ce`; `es` in `tu n'es pas fatiguée` is controlled by `tu`; `suis` in `je suis contente` is controlled by `je`; `être` after `d'` is an infinitive; `est` in `c'est agréable` is fixed with `ce`. 2. forms of `avoir`: `as` in `tu as faim ?` means hunger; `il y a` states existence; `ai` in `je n'ai pas très faim` means hunger; `ai` in `j'ai soif` means thirst; another `il y a` states existence; `il n'y a pas` states non-existence. 3. negative sentences: `je n'ai pas très faim` uses negative `avoir`, but there is no object article to change to **de**. `tu n'es pas fatiguée` uses `être`, so no **de** rule. `il n'y a pas de bruit` uses `il y a`, so the object becomes `de bruit`. 4. answers vary. one natural rewrite: `un peu. je suis en train de défaire mes valises, mais je suis contente d'être ici.` — the structure is `être` conjugated, then `en train de`, then an **infinitive**. `défaire` stays in the infinitive; writing `je suis en train de je défais` is the classic error. note that `d'être ici` in the original line is a different structure: `content de` simply takes an infinitive, and describes a state rather than an action in progress. 5. sophie's second line with `vous`: `enchantée. moi, c'est sophie. vous avez faim ? il y a du pain et des fruits dans la cuisine.` the verb changes from `as` to `avez`. ## connected reading: ma nouvelle vie à montréal `je m'appelle karim et je suis à montréal depuis deux mois. je suis étudiant et j'ai vingt-trois ans. mon appartement n'est pas très grand, mais il y a une belle vue sur le fleuve. le matin, j'ai souvent faim, alors je mange un fruit et je bois un café. mes colocataires sont sympathiques : léa est québécoise et elle est en train d'apprendre l'espagnol ; tom est américain et il a toujours des histoires intéressantes. on n'est jamais seuls ici, et je n'ai pas peur de parler français, même si je fais des erreurs.` — my name is karim and i have been in montreal for two months. i am a student and i am twenty-three years old. my apartment is not very big, but there is a beautiful view of the river. in the morning, i am often hungry, so i eat a fruit and drink a coffee. my roommates are nice: léa is from quebec and she is in the process of learning spanish; tom is american and he always has interesting stories. we are never alone here, and i am not afraid to speak french, even if i make mistakes. ### reading practice 1. list every form of `être` and `avoir` in the text and give its subject. 2. find the one negative sentence built with `ne...jamais` (a cousin of `ne...pas` you will study fully in chapter 11) and the one built with `ne...pas`. 3. identify the two nationality adjectives and explain their agreement. 4. rewrite the first three sentences replacing `karim` with `nadia` and making every needed change (pronouns, adjective agreement, verb forms). 5. write three true sentences about your own living situation using the reading as a model. 1. forms of `être` and `avoir`: `m'appelle` is a naming expression, not `être` or `avoir`; `suis` has subject `je`; another `suis` has subject `je`; `ai` has subject `j'`; `n'est` has subject `mon appartement`; `il y a` is fixed; `ai` has subject `j'`; `sont` has subject `mes colocataires`; `est` has subject `léa`; another `est` has subject `elle`; `est` has subject `tom`; `a` has subject `il`; `n'est` has subject `on`; `ai` has subject `je`. 2. `on n'est jamais seuls ici` uses `ne...jamais`. `je n'ai pas peur de parler français` uses `ne...pas`. 3. nationality adjectives: `québécoise` agrees with `léa`, one feminine person; `américain` agrees with `tom`, one masculine person. 4. with nadia: `je m'appelle nadia et je suis à montréal depuis deux mois. je suis étudiante et j'ai vingt-trois ans. mon appartement n'est pas très grand, mais il y a une belle vue sur le fleuve.` the main change is `étudiant` to `étudiante`. 5. answers vary. a model sentence is `j'ai un petit appartement, mais il y a une grande fenêtre.` check the subject before the verb every time. the passage mixes `je`, `il y a`, named people, and `on`, so guessing by nearby nouns is risky. ## chapter reference sheet ### être and avoir, side by side | pronoun | être | avoir | |---|---|---| | `je` | `suis` | `ai` | | `tu` | `es` | `as` | | `il/elle/on` | `est` | `a` | | `nous` | `sommes` | `avons` | | `vous` | `êtes` | `avez` | | `ils/elles` | `sont` | `ont` | ### negation quick reference | structure | rule | |---|---| | any conjugated verb | `ne` (`n'`) + verb + `pas` | | indefinite/partitive object after negated `avoir` | article becomes `de`/`d'` | | definite article object | stays unchanged | | object after negated `être` | stays unchanged | ### core habits - learn `être` and `avoir` as two complete six-form blocks, not as isolated forms. - memorize `avoir` idioms (age, hunger, thirst, needs, fear) as full phrases, not word-by-word translations. - say the negative frame `ne...pas` as a single unit surrounding the verb. - check every negated `avoir`/`il y a` sentence for the `de` rule before moving on. ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-10 sentences introducing yourself and your living situation, as in the connected reading. include: - at least three different subject pronouns; - `être` used at least three times, including once with a profession or nationality and once in an expression such as `être en train de` or `être d'accord`; - `avoir` used at least three times, including one idiom (age, hunger, thirst, need, or fear); - one sentence with `il y a`; - two negative sentences, at least one of which correctly applies the `de` rule. after writing: 1. circle every conjugated verb and label it `être` or `avoir`; 2. underline every negation frame; 3. check each negated `avoir`/`il y a` sentence for the `de` rule; 4. read the paragraph aloud twice; 5. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary. model paragraph: `je m'appelle ana et je suis à toronto. je suis étudiante et j'ai vingt ans. mon appartement est petit, mais il y a une grande fenêtre dans ma chambre. nous avons une cuisine calme et deux chambres. mes colocataires sont sympathiques. le matin, j'ai souvent faim et j'ai besoin d'un café. en ce moment, je suis en train d'étudier le français. on n'a pas de jardin, mais nous ne sommes pas tristes.` how it satisfies the brief: subject pronouns include `je`, `nous`, and `on`; `être` appears in `je suis`, `mon appartement est`, `mes colocataires sont`, and `je suis en train de`; `avoir` appears in `j'ai vingt ans`, `nous avons`, `j'ai souvent faim`, `j'ai besoin de`, and `on n'a pas`; `il y a` appears once; the negatives are `on n'a pas de jardin` and `nous ne sommes pas tristes`, with the **de** rule correctly applied after negative `avoir`. mark yourself wrong if: - you use `être` for age, hunger, thirst, or need; - a negative sentence lacks either `ne` or `pas`; - a negated `avoir` sentence keeps `un`, `une`, or `des` where `de` is needed; - an adjective such as `étudiante`, `petit`, or `sympathiques` does not agree; - you use only one subject pronoun across the whole paragraph. "},{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 2 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-exercises\">Chapter 2 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Je suis content.</li>\n<li>Nous avons une maison.</li>\n<li>Il y a des fenêtres.</li>\n<li>Marie et Léa sont françaises.</li>\n<li>Paul a faim.</li>\n<li>Tu es en retard.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas content.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> wrap the conjugated verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code>; no <strong>de</strong> rule is involved with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'avons pas de maison.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une maison</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de maison</code> after negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de fenêtres.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il n'y a pas</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des fenêtres</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de fenêtres</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles sont françaises.</code> — Marie and Léa are two female people, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>; the adjective is feminine plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a faim.</code> — Paul is one male person, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code>; hunger uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous êtes en retard.</code> — changing from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> requires the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These match the answer key exactly. The main traps are the <strong>de</strong> rule after negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>, and agreement after replacing names with a pronoun.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>subject pronouns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis ici.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>être</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes calmes.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>avoir and il y a</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>negation</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas fatigué.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary by design. A correct answer must clearly show the named structure, use the correct verb form, and keep agreement and word order.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est ici.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> replaces one feminine person and controls the verb form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Être</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous êtes très patients.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code>; the adjective is plural because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> can address more than one person.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avoir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a deux fenêtres dans la chambre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code> stays fixed even with a plural idea.</li>\n<li>Negation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne sommes pas fatigués.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> wrap the conjugated verb.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you reused the model sentence's content, matched the verb to the wrong subject, or changed an article to <strong>de</strong> after a negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai une petite maison.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons une petite maison.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> changes from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code> because the subject changed.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas de petite maison.</code> — after negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu as une petite maison ?</code> — this uses the safe <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code> question pattern from Chapter 3.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai un petit appartement.</code> — changing the noun to masculine also changes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check that every version still has one conjugated verb and that any adjective still agrees with its noun.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis étudiant, mais ma sœur est ingénieure.</code> — trap: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sœur</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ingénieure</code> need exact accents and agreement.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons deux chambres et un petit jardin.</code> — trap: final <strong>s</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code> is silent, but the verb form must still be plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une pharmacie près de la maison.</code> — trap: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code> is fixed, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près</code> needs the accent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On n'a pas de café, alors on a soif.</code> — trap: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> elides to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code>, and negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> changes the object to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de café</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a vingt ans et elle est en train d'étudier le français.</code> — trap: age uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> elides before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudier</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous n'êtes pas en retard ; il est encore tôt.</code> — trap: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tôt</code> both need accents, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'êtes</code> needs the apostrophe.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct in separate passes. A silent ending you failed to write is a spelling problem; a wrong verb form is a grammar problem.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write six sentences introducing yourself and your home. Use être twice, avoir twice, il y a once, and one negative.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model six-sentence version:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis étudiant.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis canadien.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai soif.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une cuisine dans mon appartement.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas en retard.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-marking criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Être</code> appears twice and agrees with the subject.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avoir</code> appears twice; if you mention age, hunger, thirst, heat, or cold, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code> appears once and stays fixed.</li>\n<li>The negative sentence has both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code>; use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li>Any adjective agrees with the person or noun it describes.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Subject pronouns.</strong> Choose the pronoun by person, number, gender, and formality. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous êtes ici.</code></li>\n<li><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Être</code>.</strong> Present forms are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">es</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code>; use it for identity, description, nationality, profession, and location. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est française.</code></li>\n<li><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avoir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il y a</code>.</strong> Present forms are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ont</code>; use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> for age and many body-state expressions. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a</code> means there is or there are. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai faim.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Negation.</strong> Put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> before the conjugated verb and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> after it; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before a vowel sound. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de café.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you can explain the rule but cannot produce your own French example, review that unit before continuing.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 2 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. je suis content. 2. nous avons une maison. 3. il y a des fenêtres. 4. marie et léa sont françaises. 5. paul a faim. 6. tu es en retard. 1. `je ne suis pas content.` — `ne` and `pas` wrap the conjugated verb `suis`; no **de** rule is involved with `être`. 2. `nous n'avons pas de maison.` — `ne` becomes `n'` before `avons`, and `une maison` becomes `de maison` after negative `avoir`. 3. `il n'y a pas de fenêtres.` — `il y a` becomes `il n'y a pas`, and `des fenêtres` becomes `de fenêtres`. 4. `elles sont françaises.` — marie and léa are two female people, so use `elles`; the adjective is feminine plural. 5. `il a faim.` — paul is one male person, so use `il`; hunger uses `avoir`, not `être`. 6. `vous êtes en retard.` — changing from `tu` to `vous` requires the `être` form `êtes`. these match the answer key exactly. the main traps are the **de** rule after negative `avoir` or `il y a`, and agreement after replacing names with a pronoun. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **subject pronouns**. use `je suis ici.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **être**. use `nous sommes calmes.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **avoir and il y a**. use `j'ai vingt ans.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **negation**. use `je ne suis pas fatigué.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary by design. a correct answer must clearly show the named structure, use the correct verb form, and keep agreement and word order. sample answers: 1. subject pronouns: `elle est ici.` — `elle` replaces one feminine person and controls the verb form `est`. 2. `être`: `vous êtes très patients.` — `vous` takes `êtes`; the adjective is plural because `vous` can address more than one person. 3. `avoir` and `il y a`: `il y a deux fenêtres dans la chambre.` — `il y a` stays fixed even with a plural idea. 4. negation: `nous ne sommes pas fatigués.` — `ne` and `pas` wrap the conjugated verb. mark yourself wrong if you reused the model sentence's content, matched the verb to the wrong subject, or changed an article to **de** after a negative `être`. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `j'ai une petite maison.` 1. change the subject: `nous avons une petite maison.` — `avoir` changes from `ai` to `avons` because the subject changed. 2. make it negative: `je n'ai pas de petite maison.` — after negative `avoir`, `une` becomes `de`; `ne` becomes `n'` before `ai`. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que tu as une petite maison ?` — this uses the safe `est-ce que` question pattern from chapter 3. 4. change one meaningful detail: `j'ai un petit appartement.` — changing the noun to masculine also changes `une petite` to `un petit`. check that every version still has one conjugated verb and that any adjective still agrees with its noun. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `je suis étudiant, mais ma sœur est ingénieure.` — trap: `sœur` and `ingénieure` need exact accents and agreement. 2. `nous avons deux chambres et un petit jardin.` — trap: final **s** in `avons` is silent, but the verb form must still be plural. 3. `il y a une pharmacie près de la maison.` — trap: `il y a` is fixed, and `près` needs the accent. 4. `on n'a pas de café, alors on a soif.` — trap: `ne` elides to `n'`, and negative `avoir` changes the object to `de café`. 5. `elle a vingt ans et elle est en train d'étudier le français.` — trap: age uses `avoir`, and `de` elides before `étudier`. 6. `vous n'êtes pas en retard ; il est encore tôt.` — trap: `êtes` and `tôt` both need accents, and `n'êtes` needs the apostrophe. correct in separate passes. a silent ending you failed to write is a spelling problem; a wrong verb form is a grammar problem. ## e. personal writing write six sentences introducing yourself and your home. use être twice, avoir twice, il y a once, and one negative. answers vary. a model six-sentence version: 1. `je suis étudiant.` 2. `je suis canadien.` 3. `j'ai vingt ans.` 4. `j'ai soif.` 5. `il y a une cuisine dans mon appartement.` 6. `je ne suis pas en retard.` self-marking criteria: - `être` appears twice and agrees with the subject. - `avoir` appears twice; if you mention age, hunger, thirst, heat, or cold, use `avoir`, not `être`. - `il y a` appears once and stays fixed. - the negative sentence has both `ne` and `pas`; use `n'` before a vowel sound. - any adjective agrees with the person or noun it describes. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **subject pronouns.** choose the pronoun by person, number, gender, and formality. example: `vous êtes ici.` 2. **`être`.** present forms are `suis`, `es`, `est`, `sommes`, `êtes`, `sont`; use it for identity, description, nationality, profession, and location. example: `elle est française.` 3. **`avoir` and `il y a`.** present forms are `ai`, `as`, `a`, `avons`, `avez`, `ont`; use `avoir` for age and many body-state expressions. `il y a` means there is or there are. example: `j'ai faim.` 4. **negation.** put `ne` before the conjugated verb and `pas` after it; `ne` becomes `n'` before a vowel sound. example: `il n'y a pas de café.` if you can explain the rule but cannot produce your own french example, review that unit before continuing. "},{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 2 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-answer-key\">Chapter 2 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas content.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'avons pas de maison.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de fenêtres.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles sont françaises.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a faim.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous êtes en retard.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subject pronouns:</strong> French verbs change with je, tu, il/elle/on, nous, vous, and ils/elles. Use vous for a plural group or polite singular address.</li>\n<li><strong>Être:</strong> Present forms: suis, es, est, sommes, êtes, sont. Use être for identity, description, nationality, location in many basic statements, and selected expressions.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoir and il y a:</strong> Present forms: ai, as, a, avons, avez, ont. French uses avoir in many expressions where English uses “to be.” Il y a means there is or there are.</li>\n<li><strong>Negation:</strong> Place ne before the conjugated verb and pas after it. Before a vowel sound, ne becomes n'. With indefinite or partitive objects after a negative avoir, the article often becomes de.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 2 answer key ## section a 1. `je ne suis pas content.` 2. `nous n'avons pas de maison.` 3. `il n'y a pas de fenêtres.` 4. `elles sont françaises.` 5. `il a faim.` 6. `vous êtes en retard.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **subject pronouns:** french verbs change with je, tu, il/elle/on, nous, vous, and ils/elles. use vous for a plural group or polite singular address. - **être:** present forms: suis, es, est, sommes, êtes, sont. use être for identity, description, nationality, location in many basic statements, and selected expressions. - **avoir and il y a:** present forms: ai, as, a, avons, avez, ont. french uses avoir in many expressions where english uses “to be.” il y a means there is or there are. - **negation:** place ne before the conjugated verb and pas after it. before a vowel sound, ne becomes n'. with indefinite or partitive objects after a negative avoir, the article often becomes de. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 2 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 2 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Subject pronouns</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Être</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Avoir and il y a</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Negation</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 2 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **subject pronouns**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **être** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **avoir and il y a**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **negation**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":3,"slug":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions","title":"Days, Months, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions","documents":[{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/README.md","title":"Chapter 3: Days, Months, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-days-months-regular-er-verbs-and-questions\">Chapter 3: Days, Months, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use days, months, seasons, and parts of the day.</li>\n<li>Conjugate regular -er verbs in the present.</li>\n<li>Ask yes/no and information questions.</li>\n<li>Write a simple weekly routine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Calendar language</li>\n<li>Regular -er verbs</li>\n<li>Yes/no questions</li>\n<li>Information questions</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 3: days, months, regular -er verbs, and questions this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - use days, months, seasons, and parts of the day. - conjugate regular -er verbs in the present. - ask yes/no and information questions. - write a simple weekly routine. ## lesson sequence 1. calendar language 2. regular -er verbs 3. yes/no questions 4. information questions ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 3 Lessons: Calendar Language, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-lessons-calendar-language-regular-er-verbs-and-questions\">Chapter 3 Lessons: Calendar Language, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions</h1>\n<p>This chapter moves French from isolated words to daily life. You will learn how to place actions in the week or the year, conjugate the very common regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs, and ask the kinds of questions that keep a conversation moving.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>say the days of the week, months, seasons, and parts of the day;</li>\n<li>use core time expressions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le lundi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en septembre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en hiver</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir</code>;</li>\n<li>conjugate regular present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs in all six persons;</li>\n<li>recognize the main uses of the French present tense;</li>\n<li>ask yes/no questions with intonation, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>, and inversion;</li>\n<li>ask information questions with common question words;</li>\n<li>combine chapter grammar in short speech, dialogue, and paragraph writing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before you begin, make sure you can still:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>choose the correct subject pronoun from Chapter 2;</li>\n<li>conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> in the present;</li>\n<li>recognize article + noun groups from Chapter 1;</li>\n<li>read short negative sentences such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas prêt</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de café</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If those skills feel shaky, review them briefly first. This chapter assumes you can already see the subject of a sentence and match a verb to it.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test yourself properly rather than trusting the feeling of recognition. Answer these five out loud, then check.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Which subject pronoun replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie et moi</code> ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>. Any group that includes <em>me</em> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Give <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous êtes</code>. Note the circumflex.</li>\n<li>Give <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils ont</code>. Say it carefully: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils ont</code> links as \"ilz-on\", while <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils sont</code> has an <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> sound. Confusing the two is the single most common Chapter 2 error.</li>\n<li>What is the article in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ université</code> ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">université</code> starts with a vowel sound. It is feminine, so it would be <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> without the elision.</li>\n<li>Make <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a un café</code> negative. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de café</code>. After a negative, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> all collapse to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, spend ten minutes in Chapter 2 before continuing. This chapter adds a whole verb family on top of these, and the errors compound.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Say every French example aloud. Then cover the French, reproduce it from memory, and do the substitution task before moving on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-days-months-seasons-and-parts-of-the-day\">Unit 1: Days, months, seasons, and parts of the day</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French uses time words constantly, and most of them stay lowercase in normal writing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"core-vocabulary-tables\">Core vocabulary tables</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Days</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>Months</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lundi</code></td>\n<td>Monday</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">janvier</code></td>\n<td>January</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mardi</code></td>\n<td>Tuesday</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">février</code></td>\n<td>February</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mercredi</code></td>\n<td>Wednesday</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mars</code></td>\n<td>March</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jeudi</code></td>\n<td>Thursday</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avril</code></td>\n<td>April</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendredi</code></td>\n<td>Friday</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mai</code></td>\n<td>May</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">samedi</code></td>\n<td>Saturday</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">juin</code></td>\n<td>June</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dimanche</code></td>\n<td>Sunday</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">juillet</code></td>\n<td>July</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">août</code></td>\n<td>August</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">septembre</code></td>\n<td>September</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">octobre</code></td>\n<td>October</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">novembre</code></td>\n<td>November</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">décembre</code></td>\n<td>December</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le printemps</code></td>\n<td>spring</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le matin</code></td>\n<td>morning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'été</code></td>\n<td>summer</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'après-midi</code></td>\n<td>afternoon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'automne</code></td>\n<td>autumn, fall</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le soir</code></td>\n<td>evening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hiver</code></td>\n<td>winter</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nuit</code></td>\n<td>night</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes mardi.</code> — It is Tuesday.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, c'est mercredi.</code> — Tomorrow is Wednesday.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est en mai.</code> — My birthday is in May.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'adore l'automne.</code> — I love fall.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, mes voisins téléphonent à leur famille.</code> — In the evening, my neighbors call their family.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette nuit, le vent est fort.</code> — Tonight / Last night, the wind is strong.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Many final consonants are silent in these words: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mars</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avril</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">septembre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soir</code>. Also notice that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">août</code> is short and crisp.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not capitalize days and months in ordinary French writing: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lundi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">septembre</code>, not <em>Monday</em>, <em>September</em> style capitals.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Complete aloud:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes ___ .</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon cours est en ___ .</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime ___ parce que ___ .</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, je ___ .</code></li>\n<p>Then write one original sentence with a day, one with a month, one with a season, and one with a part of the day.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model answers — yours will differ, and that is fine. Check the <strong>structure</strong>, not the vocabulary.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes lundi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes</code> + a bare day name. No article and no capital letter.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon cours est en septembre.</code> — Months always take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, never <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime l'été parce que je voyage.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code> takes the definite article when you like something in general, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'été</code>, not bare <em>été</em>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parce que</code> must be followed by a full clause with its own verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, je travaille.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le soir</code> means <em>in the evenings</em> as a habit. Drop <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> and say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir</code> if you mean tonight only.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Your four original sentences, one of each type:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Day: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, je regarde un film.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> + singular day = every Saturday.</li>\n<li>Month: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En août, ma famille voyage.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> + month.</li>\n<li>Season: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps, le parc est joli.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">printemps</code> is the exception: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au printemps</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en été</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en automne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en hiver</code>.</li>\n<li>Part of the day: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le matin, j'étudie le français.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound, never <em>je étudie</em>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-using-calendar-expressions-naturally\">Unit 2: Using calendar expressions naturally</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French needs a few small structure words to turn calendar vocabulary into usable sentences.</p>\n<h3 id=\"high-frequency-patterns\">High-frequency patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes + day/month</code></td>\n<td>what day or month it is</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes lundi.</code></td>\n<td>It's Monday.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le + day</code></td>\n<td>repeated action on that day</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, je travaille.</code></td>\n<td>On Saturdays, I work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + month</code></td>\n<td>in a month</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voyageons en juillet.</code></td>\n<td>We travel in July.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + season</code></td>\n<td>in a season</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleut souvent en hiver.</code></td>\n<td>It often rains in winter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au printemps</code></td>\n<td>in spring</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps, le parc change vite.</code></td>\n<td>In spring, the park changes quickly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce matin / cet après-midi / ce soir</code></td>\n<td>this morning / afternoon / evening</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce matin, j'étudie.</code></td>\n<td>This morning I'm studying.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">demain matin / demain soir</code></td>\n<td>tomorrow morning / evening</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain soir, on dîne dehors.</code></td>\n<td>Tomorrow evening we're eating out.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nuit</code></td>\n<td>at night</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La nuit, la ville est calme.</code></td>\n<td>At night the city is calm.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, je travaille à la maison.</code> — On Mondays, I work at home.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En septembre, nous étudions le français avec un nouveau professeur.</code> — In September, we study French with a new teacher.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps, les cafés ouvrent leurs terrasses.</code> — In spring, cafés open their patios.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce soir, je téléphone à ma sœur.</code> — This evening, I am calling my sister.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain matin, vous arrivez tôt ?</code> — Are you arriving early tomorrow morning?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Notice <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet après-midi</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cet</code> appears before a vowel sound.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>French usually uses singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le lundi</code> for a habit, not a direct copy of English plural \"on Mondays.\" Also say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en juillet</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à juillet</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Turn each prompt into a full sentence: every Friday / we / study at the library; in December / my family / visit Paris; this evening / I / watch a movie; at night / the children / sleep badly. Then change each sentence to a different time expression.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le vendredi, nous étudions à la bibliothèque.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> + a singular day means <em>every Friday</em>. French does not pluralise it the way English says \"on Fridays.\"</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En décembre, ma famille visite Paris.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> + month. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">famille</code> is singular, so the verb is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visite</code>, even though a family contains several people.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce soir, je regarde un film.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir</code> is one specific evening. Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le soir</code> here would change the meaning to <em>in the evenings</em>, as a habit.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La nuit, les enfants dorment mal.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nuit</code> means <em>at night</em> in general. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dormir</code> is irregular and comes in Chapter 4; for now just recognise <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dorment</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Changed to a different time expression:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, nous étudions à la bibliothèque.</code> — a different recurring day.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps, ma famille visite Paris.</code> — season instead of month, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">printemps</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain soir, je regarde un film.</code> — a future evening instead of tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le matin, les enfants dorment mal.</code> — a different part of the day.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-regular-er-verbs-in-the-present-tense\">Unit 3: Regular -er verbs in the present tense</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Most beginner verbs are regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs. Remove <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> from the infinitive and add the present-tense endings.</p>\n<h3 id=\"model-endings-with-parler\">Model endings with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle</code></td>\n<td>I speak / I am speaking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu parles</code></td>\n<td>you speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parle</code></td>\n<td>he speaks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code></td>\n<td>we speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous parlez</code></td>\n<td>you speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlent</code></td>\n<td>they speak</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"other-common-regular-er-verbs\">Other common regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code></td>\n<td>to like, to love</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travailler</code></td>\n<td>to work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habiter</code></td>\n<td>to live</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regarder</code></td>\n<td>to watch, look at</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudier</code></td>\n<td>to study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code></td>\n<td>to arrive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chercher</code></td>\n<td>to look for</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">danser</code></td>\n<td>to dance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français avec mes collègues.</code> — I speak French with my coworkers.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles demain ?</code> — Are you working tomorrow?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle habite près de la gare.</code> — She lives near the train station.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous regardons un film français le vendredi soir.</code> — We watch a French film on Friday evenings.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous aimez la musique classique ?</code> — Do you like classical music?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils arrivent en septembre.</code> — They are arriving in September.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In most regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu parles</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parle</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlent</code> sound almost the same. The written endings differ more than the spoken ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not pronounce the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlent</code>. Also remember <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aime</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'étudie</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travailler</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code> for all six persons. Then write three short original sentences with three different subjects.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>All three are regular, so the stem never changes: drop <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code>, then add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travailler</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je travaille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu parles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu travailles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu aimes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle travaille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on aime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous travaillons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous aimons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous parlez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous travaillez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous aimez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles travaillent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils aiment</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two things to check in your own work:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aime</code>, not <em>je aime</em>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> contracts to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li>Four of the six forms — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parles</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlent</code> — sound identical. Only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> are audibly different. Spelling carries the distinction that your ear cannot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Three original sentences with different subjects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français avec ma voisine.</code> — I speak French with my neighbour.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous travaillons à la maison le lundi.</code> — We work at home on Mondays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles aiment la musique classique.</code> — They like classical music.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-what-the-present-tense-means\">Unit 4: What the present tense means</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>One French present-tense form can cover several meanings that English sometimes separates.</p>\n<h3 id=\"main-uses-of-the-present-tense\">Main uses of the present tense</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>English meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>habitual action</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mardi, nous étudions au café.</code></td>\n<td>On Tuesdays, we study at the café.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>action happening now</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je regarde le tableau.</code></td>\n<td>I am looking at the board.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>general truth</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants parlent avec le professeur.</code></td>\n<td>Students speak with the teacher.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>near future / scheduled event</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le train arrive demain matin.</code></td>\n<td>The train arrives tomorrow morning.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille à la maison le lundi.</code> — I work at home on Mondays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous étudions le français en septembre.</code> — We study French in September.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On regarde la carte maintenant.</code> — We are looking at the map now.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le magasin ferme tôt le dimanche.</code> — The store closes early on Sundays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur arrive jeudi soir.</code> — My sister is arriving Thursday evening.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-or-listening-note\">Pronunciation or listening note</h3>\n<p>Time clues often tell you which meaning is intended. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur arrive jeudi soir</code>, the present tense points to a future scheduled event because of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jeudi soir</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not force an English-style separate \"am doing\" form into every sentence. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille</code> may mean either <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">I work</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">I am working</code>, depending on context.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Label each sentence as habit, happening now, general truth, or near future: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous parlons avec la directrice.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le jeudi, vous dansez ?</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon train arrive demain.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants regardent la neige.</code> Then rewrite two sentences with a different time clue.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous parlons avec la directrice.</code> — <strong>happening now.</strong> With no time clue, the default reading is the action in progress. Add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souvent</code> and it becomes a habit.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le jeudi, vous dansez ?</code> — <strong>habit.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> + a singular day is the clearest habit marker in French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon train arrive demain.</code> — <strong>near future.</strong> French uses the plain present for scheduled events; no separate future tense is needed here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants regardent la neige.</code> — <strong>happening now.</strong> They are watching it at this moment.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The lesson here is that the French present tense is one form doing four jobs. The time expression, not the verb, tells you which job it is doing.</p>\n<p>Rewritten with a different time clue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En hiver, nous parlons avec la directrice.</code> — now a seasonal habit rather than something happening now.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon train arrive le lundi matin.</code> — now a recurring schedule rather than a one-off future event.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-yes-no-questions-in-three-common-forms\">Unit 5: Yes/no questions in three common forms</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French gives you three common ways to ask a yes/no question, and you should be able to recognize all three.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-patterns\">The three patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>rising intonation</td>\n<td>statement + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles demain ?</code></td>\n<td>Are you working tomorrow?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code> + statement order</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous aimez Paris ?</code></td>\n<td>Do you like Paris?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inversion</td>\n<td>verb-pronoun order</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous anglais ?</code></td>\n<td>Do you speak English?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous regardez souvent des films français ?</code> — Do you often watch French movies?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous regardez souvent des films français ?</code> — Do you often watch French movies?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regardez-vous souvent des films français ?</code> — Do you often watch French movies?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce qu'on travaille ce soir ?</code> — Are we working this evening?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Aime-t-elle la musique ?</code> — Does she like music?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Treat <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code> as one fixed sound group. In inversion, keep the hyphen: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous ?</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Aime-t-elle ?</code> The extra <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-t-</code> makes pronunciation smoother.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not mix two systems: avoid <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Est-ce que parlez-vous anglais ?</code> French also does not use an extra word like English <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">do/does</code> in questions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Turn each statement into three yes/no questions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu habites à Lyon.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous aimez ce quartier.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils arrivent demain matin.</code> Then decide which version feels most natural in casual speech.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Each statement gives you the same three options, in rising order of formality.</p>\n<p><strong>1. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu habites à Lyon.</code></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intonation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu habites à Lyon ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu habites à Lyon ?</code></li>\n<li>Inversion: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Habites-tu à Lyon ?</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous aimez ce quartier.</code></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intonation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous aimez ce quartier ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous aimez ce quartier ?</code></li>\n<li>Inversion: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Aimez-vous ce quartier ?</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils arrivent demain matin.</code></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intonation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils arrivent demain matin ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce qu'ils arrivent demain matin ?</code></li>\n<li>Inversion: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Arrivent-ils demain matin ?</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two spelling traps: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce qu'</code> before a vowel sound, and inversion always keeps its hyphen.</p>\n<p><strong>Which is most natural in casual speech?</strong> The intonation version. Among friends, French speakers mostly keep statement word order and simply raise the pitch at the end. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code> is the safe middle register and works everywhere. Inversion sounds careful or formal in speech, though it is normal in writing — which is why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous anglais ?</code> sounds like a phrasebook rather than a friend.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-6-information-questions-with-question-words\">Unit 6: Information questions with question words</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Information questions ask for a specific detail: place, time, reason, quantity, identity, or category.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-question-words\">Common question words</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code></td>\n<td>where</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où habitez-vous ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code></td>\n<td>when</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourquoi</code></td>\n<td>why</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi étudient-ils ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comment</code></td>\n<td>how</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment travaillez-vous ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien de</code></td>\n<td>how many / how much</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Combien de films regardez-vous ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code></td>\n<td>who</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui parle ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'est-ce que</code></td>\n<td>what</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qu'est-ce que tu regardes ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quel / quelle / quels / quelles</code></td>\n<td>which / what</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quelle saison préférez-vous ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"useful-structures-and-examples\">Useful structures and examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où habitez-vous ?</code> — Where do you live?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?</code> — When is she arriving?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tôt ?</code> — Why is she arriving so early?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment travaillez-vous le soir ?</code> — How do you work in the evening?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Combien de cours regardez-vous en ligne ?</code> — How many classes do you watch online?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quel jour sommes-nous ?</code> — What day is it?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quelle musique aimes-tu ?</code> — What kind of music do you like?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'est-ce que</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand est-ce qu'</code> as chunks. They are much easier to hear and produce that way.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not confuse <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'est-ce que</code>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> for a person doing the action, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'est-ce que</code> for a thing that is the object of the action. Also remember agreement in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quel</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quel jour</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelle saison</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quels films</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelles langues</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Write a full question for each answer clue: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À Montréal.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le vendredi soir.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parce qu'elle travaille demain.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Deux films.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le printemps.</code> Use a different question word each time.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Work backwards: the form of the answer tells you which question word is required.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À Montréal.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où habitez-vous ?</code> — a place, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le vendredi soir.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce que vous regardez des films ?</code> — a time, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parce qu'elle travaille demain.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tôt ?</code> — the answer opens with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parce que</code>, which only ever answers <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourquoi</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Deux films.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Combien de films regardez-vous ?</code> — a quantity, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien de</code>. Keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> with no article: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien de films</code>, never <em>combien des films</em>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le printemps.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quelle saison préférez-vous ?</code> — choosing from a known set, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quel</code>. It agrees with the noun, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">saison</code> is feminine, hence <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelle</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The pairing to memorise is question word to answer type: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> → place, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> → time, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourquoi</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parce que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">combien de</code> → number, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quel</code> → a choice from a set.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, je travaille à la maison.</code> — On Mondays, I work at home.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous étudions le français en septembre et en octobre.</code> — We study French in September and October.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous regardez souvent des films français le soir ?</code> — Do you often watch French movies in the evening?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tôt le dimanche matin ?</code> — Why is she arriving so early on Sunday morning?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps, mes amis visitent souvent le marché près de la rivière.</code> — In spring, my friends often visit the market near the river.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous avec vos voisins quand vous rentrez du travail ?</code> — Do you speak with your neighbors when you come home from work?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>The sentences are in front of you, so the answer here is what to check once you have written each one from memory and uncovered it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Silent endings you cannot hear.</strong> Sentence 3 <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regardez</code> and sentence 5 <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visitent</code> sound like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regarde</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visite</code>. If you wrote the singular, that is a spelling problem, not a listening failure — the sound really is identical.</li>\n<li><strong>The little words that vanish in speech.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des films</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près de</code> in sentence 5 are unstressed and easy to drop.</li>\n<li><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce qu'</code> before a vowel</strong> in sentence 4, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que elle</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Accents.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si tôt</code> — a missing accent is a wrong word, not a typo.</li>\n<li><strong>The space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code></strong> in sentences 3, 4 and 6.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Score yourself per sentence: one point for meaning, one for every ending correct. Rewrite from memory only the sentences you did not score full marks on.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Circle the conjugated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb.</li>\n<li>Underline the time expression.</li>\n<li>Box the question marker if the sentence is a question.</li>\n<li>Decide whether the sentence shows a habit, a current action, or a scheduled event.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb</th>\n<th>Time expression</th>\n<th>Question marker</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travaille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>habit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudions</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en septembre et en octobre</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>habit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regardez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le soir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code></td>\n<td>habit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le dimanche matin</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce qu'</code></td>\n<td>habit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visitent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>habit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand vous rentrez du travail</code></td>\n<td>inversion <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous</code></td>\n<td>habit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every sentence here is a habit, and that is the point: each one carries <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> plus a day, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> plus a month or season, or a repeated-time word such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souvent</code>. Strip <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le</code> from sentence 1 and it becomes a scheduled event — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lundi, je travaille à la maison</code> means this coming Monday only.</p>\n<p>Sentence 4 is worth a second look: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce qu'</code> before the vowel of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: first change the subject, then change one meaningful detail such as the day, month, season, place, or activity. Make every necessary change to the verb ending and word order.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>One worked version of each. Yours will differ; what matters is that the verb ending follows the new subject.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, nous travaillons à la maison.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, je travaille au bureau.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle étudie le français en septembre et en octobre.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous étudions le français en hiver.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu regardes souvent des films français le soir ?</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous regardez souvent des films français le week-end ?</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce qu'ils arrivent si tôt le dimanche matin ?</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tard le lundi soir ?</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The traps, in order: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> forces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> drops the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code> back to a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> adds a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>; and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> takes the silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code>, which sounds identical to the singular even though it looks different. Only the subject tells you what to write.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four true sentences about your own life: one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le + day</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + month</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + season</code>, one yes/no question, and one information question.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mardi, je téléphone à ma mère.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En août, je voyage avec ma famille.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu travailles le week-end ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce que tu études le français ?</code> — better as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce que tu étudies le français ?</code>; the accent-free <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">études</code> is a noun, not a verb.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check your four against this: the day and month are lowercase, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> introduces the month, every verb ends in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code> to match <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, and both questions have a space before the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-planning-a-study-week\">Mini-dialogue: planning a study week</h2>\n<p><strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Salut, Malik. Tu travailles demain matin ?</code> — Hi, Malik. Are you working tomorrow morning?</p>\n<p><strong>Malik :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Non, je travaille l'après-midi, mais le matin, j'étudie à la bibliothèque.</code> — No, I work in the afternoon, but in the morning I study at the library.</p>\n<p><strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu révises aussi le jeudi soir ?</code> — Do you also review on Thursday evening?</p>\n<p><strong>Malik :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, et le vendredi, je regarde une vidéo en français avec ma sœur.</code> — Yes, and on Fridays I watch a video in French with my sister.</p>\n<p><strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce que vous étudiez ensemble ?</code> — Why do you study together?</p>\n<p><strong>Malik :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parce que nous parlons français pendant une heure, et ça nous aide beaucoup.</code> — Because we speak French for an hour, and that helps us a lot.</p>\n<p><strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quelle saison préférez-vous pour étudier au parc ?</code> — Which season do you prefer for studying in the park?</p>\n<p><strong>Malik :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous préférons le printemps, parce qu'il fait doux et le parc est calme.</code> — We prefer spring because the weather is mild and the park is calm.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find three different time expressions.</li>\n<li>List the conjugated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs and identify their subjects.</li>\n<li>Find one yes/no question with intonation and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>.</li>\n<li>Find two information questions and name their question words.</li>\n<li>Rewrite Malik's first answer with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> where possible.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Three time expressions:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">demain matin</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'après-midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le matin</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le jeudi soir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vendredi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pendant une heure</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le printemps</code>. Any three count. Notice <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le jeudi soir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vendredi</code> describe habits, while <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">demain matin</code> is a one-off.</li>\n<li><strong>Conjugated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs and subjects:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travailles</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travaille</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudie</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">révises</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regarde</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiez</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlons</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférez</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférons</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>). Both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aide</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ça</code>) and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le parc</code>) appear too, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, not an <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb.</li>\n<li><strong>Intonation:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles demain matin ?</code> — statement word order, with only the rise in the voice marking the question. <strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code>:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu révises aussi le jeudi soir ?</code></li>\n<li><strong>Information questions:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce que vous étudiez ensemble ?</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourquoi</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quelle saison préférez-vous pour étudier au parc ?</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelle</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quelle</code> is feminine to agree with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">saison</code>, and this one uses inversion rather than <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Non, nous travaillons l'après-midi, mais le matin, nous étudions à la bibliothèque.</code> — both verbs shift to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code>. The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> links onto the vowel in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous étudions</code>, so it sounds like \"nou-zétudions\".</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-ma-routine-du-mercredi\">Connected reading: ma routine du mercredi</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mercredi, je commence la journée assez tôt. Le matin, j'arrive au café du quartier vers huit heures et j'étudie pendant une heure avant le travail. À midi, je déjeune avec une collègue qui parle aussi français, alors nous pratiquons ensemble. L'après-midi, je travaille à la maison et je téléphone souvent à mes clients. Le soir, je regarde une courte vidéo, puis je note cinq nouvelles expressions dans mon cahier. En hiver, cette routine demande plus d'énergie, mais au printemps, je travaille près de la fenêtre et tout semble plus facile.</code></p>\n<p>— On Wednesdays, I start the day fairly early. In the morning, I arrive at the neighborhood café around eight o'clock and study for an hour before work. At noon, I have lunch with a coworker who also speaks French, so we practice together. In the afternoon, I work at home and often call my clients. In the evening, I watch a short video, then I write down five new expressions in my notebook. In winter, this routine takes more energy, but in spring, I work near the window and everything seems easier.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List every time expression in the paragraph.</li>\n<li>Find four regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs and give their infinitives.</li>\n<li>Identify one sentence showing a habit and one showing a scheduled action.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the paragraph in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form where reasonable.</li>\n<li>Write three true sentences about your own study routine using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Time expressions:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mercredi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le matin</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vers huit heures</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pendant une heure</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant le travail</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'après-midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En hiver</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au printemps</code>. Note <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En hiver</code> but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au printemps</code> — spring is the odd one out.</li>\n<li><strong>Four regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrive</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudie</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudier</code>), <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjeune</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjeuner</code>). Also acceptable: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pratiquons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travaille</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regarde</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">note</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">demande</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">semble</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Habit:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mercredi, je commence la journée assez tôt.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> plus a day means every Wednesday. <strong>Scheduled action:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'arrive au café du quartier vers huit heures</code> — a clock time, not a repeated pattern.</li>\n<li><strong>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mercredi, nous commençons la journée assez tôt. Le matin, nous arrivons au café du quartier vers huit heures et nous étudions pendant une heure avant le travail. À midi, nous déjeunons avec une collègue…</code> — watch <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commençons</code>, where the cedilla keeps the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> soft, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous étudions</code>, where the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> links onto the vowel.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. Check three things: every verb ends in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, your time expressions sit at the front of the sentence where French likes them, and days and months are lowercase.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Capitalizing time words as in English instead of writing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lundi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">septembre</code>.</li>\n<li>Using the wrong preposition: say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en juillet</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en hiver</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au printemps</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that repeated days usually take singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le dimanche</code>.</li>\n<li>Pronouncing every written ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs, especially the silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code>.</li>\n<li>Adding English <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">do/does</code> to French questions.</li>\n<li>Mixing question systems instead of choosing one.</li>\n<li>Forgetting agreement in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quel</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"calendar-expressions-at-a-glance\">Calendar expressions at a glance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>French pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>it is Monday</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes lundi.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes lundi.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>on Mondays</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le lundi</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, je cours.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in July</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en juillet</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous partons en juillet.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in winter</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en hiver</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En hiver, il neige.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in spring</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au printemps</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps, tout change.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>this morning</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce matin</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce matin, je travaille.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>this afternoon</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet après-midi</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cet après-midi, nous visitons un musée.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>this evening</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce soir, ils dînent dehors.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>at night</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nuit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La nuit, la rue est calme.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"regular-er-endings\">Regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>Example with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudier</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'étudie</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu étudies</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle étudie</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous étudions</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous étudiez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils étudient</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"question-patterns\">Question patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>yes/no, casual</td>\n<td>statement + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu regardes la série ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>yes/no, neutral</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code> + statement order</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu regardes la série ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>yes/no, careful</td>\n<td>inversion</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regardes-tu la série ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>information</td>\n<td>question word + chosen structure</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce qu'il arrive tôt ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about your weekly routine, your favorite season, and the questions you might ask a classmate about their schedule. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least four different time expressions;</li>\n<li>at least five regular present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le + day</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + month</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + season</code>;</li>\n<li>one yes/no question;</li>\n<li>two information questions using different question words.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb;</li>\n<li>underline every time expression;</li>\n<li>label each question as intonation, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>, or inversion;</li>\n<li>check that each verb matches its subject;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary, so mark against the checklist rather than against a single correct text. Here is a paragraph that meets every requirement:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, je commence à huit heures. En hiver, j'étudie le français à la maison, parce que le café est trop bruyant. Le matin, je travaille et l'après-midi, je révise mes notes. En juillet, je voyage avec ma famille, mais je continue à écouter des podcasts. Au printemps, je préfère étudier au parc. Tu travailles le samedi ? Quand est-ce que tu étudies le français ? Pourquoi est-ce que tu aimes cette saison ?</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: the time expressions are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à huit heures</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En hiver</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le matin</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'après-midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En juillet</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps</code>; the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travaille</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">révise</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyage</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">continue</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimes</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi</code> is the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le + day</code> sentence; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En juillet</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Au printemps</code> cover the month and season; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles le samedi ?</code> is the yes/no question; and the last two are information questions using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourquoi</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if any of these appear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a capitalised day or month, such as <strong>Lundi</strong> or <strong>Juillet</strong>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code> before a month or before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hiver</code> — only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">printemps</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code>;</li>\n<li>an <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb that does not match its subject, especially a missing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que</code> combined with inversion in the same question, which doubles the question marker;</li>\n<li>a missing space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>, which French requires.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 3 lessons: calendar language, regular -er verbs, and questions this chapter moves french from isolated words to daily life. you will learn how to place actions in the week or the year, conjugate the very common regular `-er` verbs, and ask the kinds of questions that keep a conversation moving. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. say the days of the week, months, seasons, and parts of the day; 2. use core time expressions such as `le lundi`, `en septembre`, `en hiver`, and `ce soir`; 3. conjugate regular present-tense `-er` verbs in all six persons; 4. recognize the main uses of the french present tense; 5. ask yes/no questions with intonation, `est-ce que`, and inversion; 6. ask information questions with common question words; 7. combine chapter grammar in short speech, dialogue, and paragraph writing. ## prerequisite quick check before you begin, make sure you can still: - choose the correct subject pronoun from chapter 2; - conjugate `être` and `avoir` in the present; - recognize article + noun groups from chapter 1; - read short negative sentences such as `je ne suis pas prêt` and `il n'y a pas de café`. if those skills feel shaky, review them briefly first. this chapter assumes you can already see the subject of a sentence and match a verb to it. test yourself properly rather than trusting the feeling of recognition. answer these five out loud, then check. 1. which subject pronoun replaces `marie et moi` ? — `nous`. any group that includes *me* becomes `nous`. 2. give `être` for `vous`. — `vous êtes`. note the circumflex. 3. give `avoir` for `ils`. — `ils ont`. say it carefully: `ils ont` links as \"ilz-on\", while `ils sont` has an `s` sound. confusing the two is the single most common chapter 2 error. 4. what is the article in `___ université` ? — `l'`, because `université` starts with a vowel sound. it is feminine, so it would be `la` without the elision. 5. make `il y a un café` negative. — `il n'y a pas de café`. after a negative, `un`, `une`, and `des` all collapse to `de`. if you missed two or more, spend ten minutes in chapter 2 before continuing. this chapter adds a whole verb family on top of these, and the errors compound. ## how to study this chapter say every french example aloud. then cover the french, reproduce it from memory, and do the substitution task before moving on. ## unit 1: days, months, seasons, and parts of the day ### core idea french uses time words constantly, and most of them stay lowercase in normal writing. ### core vocabulary tables | days | english | months | english | |---|---|---|---| | `lundi` | monday | `janvier` | january | | `mardi` | tuesday | `février` | february | | `mercredi` | wednesday | `mars` | march | | `jeudi` | thursday | `avril` | april | | `vendredi` | friday | `mai` | may | | `samedi` | saturday | `juin` | june | | `dimanche` | sunday | `juillet` | july | | | | `août` | august | | | | `septembre` | september | | | | `octobre` | october | | | | `novembre` | november | | | | `décembre` | december | | french | english | french | english | |---|---|---|---| | `le printemps` | spring | `le matin` | morning | | `l'été` | summer | `l'après-midi` | afternoon | | `l'automne` | autumn, fall | `le soir` | evening | | `l'hiver` | winter | `la nuit` | night | ### examples - `nous sommes mardi.` — it is tuesday. - `demain, c'est mercredi.` — tomorrow is wednesday. - `mon anniversaire est en mai.` — my birthday is in may. - `j'adore l'automne.` — i love fall. - `le soir, mes voisins téléphonent à leur famille.` — in the evening, my neighbors call their family. - `cette nuit, le vent est fort.` — tonight / last night, the wind is strong. ### pronunciation note many final consonants are silent in these words: `mars`, `avril`, `septembre`, `soir`. also notice that `août` is short and crisp. ### common mistake for english speakers do not capitalize days and months in ordinary french writing: `lundi`, `septembre`, not *monday*, *september* style capitals. ### sentence-building practice complete aloud: 1. `nous sommes ___ .` 2. `mon cours est en ___ .` 3. `j'aime ___ parce que ___ .` 4. `le soir, je ___ .` then write one original sentence with a day, one with a month, one with a season, and one with a part of the day. model answers — yours will differ, and that is fine. check the **structure**, not the vocabulary. 1. `nous sommes lundi.` — `nous sommes` + a bare day name. no article and no capital letter. 2. `mon cours est en septembre.` — months always take `en`, never `à`. 3. `j'aime l'été parce que je voyage.` — `aimer` takes the definite article when you like something in general, so `l'été`, not bare *été*. `parce que` must be followed by a full clause with its own verb. 4. `le soir, je travaille.` — `le soir` means *in the evenings* as a habit. drop `le` and say `ce soir` if you mean tonight only. your four original sentences, one of each type: - day: `le samedi, je regarde un film.` — `le` + singular day = every saturday. - month: `en août, ma famille voyage.` — `en` + month. - season: `au printemps, le parc est joli.` — `printemps` is the exception: `au printemps`, but `en été`, `en automne`, `en hiver`. - part of the day: `le matin, j'étudie le français.` — `j'` before a vowel sound, never *je étudie*. ## unit 2: using calendar expressions naturally ### core idea french needs a few small structure words to turn calendar vocabulary into usable sentences. ### high-frequency patterns | pattern | use | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `nous sommes + day/month` | what day or month it is | `nous sommes lundi.` | it's monday. | | `le + day` | repeated action on that day | `le samedi, je travaille.` | on saturdays, i work. | | `en + month` | in a month | `nous voyageons en juillet.` | we travel in july. | | `en + season` | in a season | `il pleut souvent en hiver.` | it often rains in winter. | | `au printemps` | in spring | `au printemps, le parc change vite.` | in spring, the park changes quickly. | | `ce matin / cet après-midi / ce soir` | this morning / afternoon / evening | `ce matin, j'étudie.` | this morning i'm studying. | | `demain matin / demain soir` | tomorrow morning / evening | `demain soir, on dîne dehors.` | tomorrow evening we're eating out. | | `la nuit` | at night | `la nuit, la ville est calme.` | at night the city is calm. | ### examples - `le lundi, je travaille à la maison.` — on mondays, i work at home. - `en septembre, nous étudions le français avec un nouveau professeur.` — in september, we study french with a new teacher. - `au printemps, les cafés ouvrent leurs terrasses.` — in spring, cafés open their patios. - `ce soir, je téléphone à ma sœur.` — this evening, i am calling my sister. - `demain matin, vous arrivez tôt ?` — are you arriving early tomorrow morning? ### pronunciation note notice `ce` versus `cet`: `ce soir`, but `cet après-midi`. `cet` appears before a vowel sound. ### common mistake for english speakers french usually uses singular `le lundi` for a habit, not a direct copy of english plural \"on mondays.\" also say `en juillet`, not `à juillet`. ### sentence-building practice turn each prompt into a full sentence: every friday / we / study at the library; in december / my family / visit paris; this evening / i / watch a movie; at night / the children / sleep badly. then change each sentence to a different time expression. 1. `le vendredi, nous étudions à la bibliothèque.` — `le` + a singular day means *every friday*. french does not pluralise it the way english says \"on fridays.\" 2. `en décembre, ma famille visite paris.` — `en` + month. `famille` is singular, so the verb is `visite`, even though a family contains several people. 3. `ce soir, je regarde un film.` — `ce soir` is one specific evening. using `le soir` here would change the meaning to *in the evenings*, as a habit. 4. `la nuit, les enfants dorment mal.` — `la nuit` means *at night* in general. `dormir` is irregular and comes in chapter 4; for now just recognise `dorment`. changed to a different time expression: - `le samedi, nous étudions à la bibliothèque.` — a different recurring day. - `au printemps, ma famille visite paris.` — season instead of month, and `printemps` takes `au`. - `demain soir, je regarde un film.` — a future evening instead of tonight. - `le matin, les enfants dorment mal.` — a different part of the day. ## unit 3: regular -er verbs in the present tense ### core idea most beginner verbs are regular `-er` verbs. remove `-er` from the infinitive and add the present-tense endings. ### model endings with `parler` | subject | ending | form | english | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `-e` | `je parle` | i speak / i am speaking | | `tu` | `-es` | `tu parles` | you speak | | `il / elle / on` | `-e` | `il parle` | he speaks | | `nous` | `-ons` | `nous parlons` | we speak | | `vous` | `-ez` | `vous parlez` | you speak | | `ils / elles` | `-ent` | `ils parlent` | they speak | ### other common regular `-er` verbs | infinitive | meaning | |---|---| | `aimer` | to like, to love | | `travailler` | to work | | `habiter` | to live | | `regarder` | to watch, look at | | `étudier` | to study | | `arriver` | to arrive | | `chercher` | to look for | | `danser` | to dance | ### examples - `je parle français avec mes collègues.` — i speak french with my coworkers. - `tu travailles demain ?` — are you working tomorrow? - `elle habite près de la gare.` — she lives near the train station. - `nous regardons un film français le vendredi soir.` — we watch a french film on friday evenings. - `vous aimez la musique classique ?` — do you like classical music? - `ils arrivent en septembre.` — they are arriving in september. ### pronunciation note in most regular `-er` verbs, `je parle`, `tu parles`, `il parle`, and `ils parlent` sound almost the same. the written endings differ more than the spoken ones. ### common mistake for english speakers do not pronounce the final `-ent` in `ils parlent`. also remember `j'` before a vowel sound: `j'aime`, `j'étudie`. ### sentence-building practice conjugate `parler`, `travailler`, and `aimer` for all six persons. then write three short original sentences with three different subjects. all three are regular, so the stem never changes: drop `-er`, then add `-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent`. | subject | `parler` | `travailler` | `aimer` | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `je parle` | `je travaille` | `j'aime` | | `tu` | `tu parles` | `tu travailles` | `tu aimes` | | `il / elle / on` | `il parle` | `elle travaille` | `on aime` | | `nous` | `nous parlons` | `nous travaillons` | `nous aimons` | | `vous` | `vous parlez` | `vous travaillez` | `vous aimez` | | `ils / elles` | `ils parlent` | `elles travaillent` | `ils aiment` | two things to check in your own work: - `j'aime`, not *je aime*. `je` contracts to `j'` before a vowel sound. - four of the six forms — `parle`, `parles`, `parle`, `parlent` — sound identical. only `nous` and `vous` are audibly different. spelling carries the distinction that your ear cannot. three original sentences with different subjects: - `je parle français avec ma voisine.` — i speak french with my neighbour. - `nous travaillons à la maison le lundi.` — we work at home on mondays. - `elles aiment la musique classique.` — they like classical music. ## unit 4: what the present tense means ### core idea one french present-tense form can cover several meanings that english sometimes separates. ### main uses of the present tense | use | french example | english meaning | |---|---|---| | habitual action | `le mardi, nous étudions au café.` | on tuesdays, we study at the café. | | action happening now | `je regarde le tableau.` | i am looking at the board. | | general truth | `les étudiants parlent avec le professeur.` | students speak with the teacher. | | near future / scheduled event | `le train arrive demain matin.` | the train arrives tomorrow morning. | ### examples - `je travaille à la maison le lundi.` — i work at home on mondays. - `nous étudions le français en septembre.` — we study french in september. - `on regarde la carte maintenant.` — we are looking at the map now. - `le magasin ferme tôt le dimanche.` — the store closes early on sundays. - `ma sœur arrive jeudi soir.` — my sister is arriving thursday evening. ### pronunciation or listening note time clues often tell you which meaning is intended. in `ma sœur arrive jeudi soir`, the present tense points to a future scheduled event because of `jeudi soir`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not force an english-style separate \"am doing\" form into every sentence. `je travaille` may mean either `i work` or `i am working`, depending on context. ### sentence-building practice label each sentence as habit, happening now, general truth, or near future: `nous parlons avec la directrice.` / `le jeudi, vous dansez ?` / `mon train arrive demain.` / `les enfants regardent la neige.` then rewrite two sentences with a different time clue. 1. `nous parlons avec la directrice.` — **happening now.** with no time clue, the default reading is the action in progress. add `souvent` and it becomes a habit. 2. `le jeudi, vous dansez ?` — **habit.** `le` + a singular day is the clearest habit marker in french. 3. `mon train arrive demain.` — **near future.** french uses the plain present for scheduled events; no separate future tense is needed here. 4. `les enfants regardent la neige.` — **happening now.** they are watching it at this moment. the lesson here is that the french present tense is one form doing four jobs. the time expression, not the verb, tells you which job it is doing. rewritten with a different time clue: - `en hiver, nous parlons avec la directrice.` — now a seasonal habit rather than something happening now. - `mon train arrive le lundi matin.` — now a recurring schedule rather than a one-off future event. ## unit 5: yes/no questions in three common forms ### core idea french gives you three common ways to ask a yes/no question, and you should be able to recognize all three. ### the three patterns | pattern | structure | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | rising intonation | statement + `?` | `tu travailles demain ?` | are you working tomorrow? | | `est-ce que` | `est-ce que` + statement order | `est-ce que vous aimez paris ?` | do you like paris? | | inversion | verb-pronoun order | `parlez-vous anglais ?` | do you speak english? | ### examples - `vous regardez souvent des films français ?` — do you often watch french movies? - `est-ce que vous regardez souvent des films français ?` — do you often watch french movies? - `regardez-vous souvent des films français ?` — do you often watch french movies? - `est-ce qu'on travaille ce soir ?` — are we working this evening? - `aime-t-elle la musique ?` — does she like music? ### pronunciation note treat `est-ce que` as one fixed sound group. in inversion, keep the hyphen: `parlez-vous ?`, `aime-t-elle ?` the extra `-t-` makes pronunciation smoother. ### common mistake for english speakers do not mix two systems: avoid `*est-ce que parlez-vous anglais ?` french also does not use an extra word like english `do/does` in questions. ### sentence-building practice turn each statement into three yes/no questions: `tu habites à lyon.` / `vous aimez ce quartier.` / `ils arrivent demain matin.` then decide which version feels most natural in casual speech. each statement gives you the same three options, in rising order of formality. **1. `tu habites à lyon.`** - intonation: `tu habites à lyon ?` - `est-ce que`: `est-ce que tu habites à lyon ?` - inversion: `habites-tu à lyon ?` **2. `vous aimez ce quartier.`** - intonation: `vous aimez ce quartier ?` - `est-ce que`: `est-ce que vous aimez ce quartier ?` - inversion: `aimez-vous ce quartier ?` **3. `ils arrivent demain matin.`** - intonation: `ils arrivent demain matin ?` - `est-ce que`: `est-ce qu'ils arrivent demain matin ?` - inversion: `arrivent-ils demain matin ?` two spelling traps: `est-ce que` becomes `est-ce qu'` before a vowel sound, and inversion always keeps its hyphen. **which is most natural in casual speech?** the intonation version. among friends, french speakers mostly keep statement word order and simply raise the pitch at the end. `est-ce que` is the safe middle register and works everywhere. inversion sounds careful or formal in speech, though it is normal in writing — which is why `parlez-vous anglais ?` sounds like a phrasebook rather than a friend. ## unit 6: information questions with question words ### core idea information questions ask for a specific detail: place, time, reason, quantity, identity, or category. ### common question words | french | meaning | example | |---|---|---| | `où` | where | `où habitez-vous ?` | | `quand` | when | `quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?` | | `pourquoi` | why | `pourquoi étudient-ils ?` | | `comment` | how | `comment travaillez-vous ?` | | `combien de` | how many / how much | `combien de films regardez-vous ?` | | `qui` | who | `qui parle ?` | | `qu'est-ce que` | what | `qu'est-ce que tu regardes ?` | | `quel / quelle / quels / quelles` | which / what | `quelle saison préférez-vous ?` | ### useful structures and examples - `où habitez-vous ?` — where do you live? - `quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?` — when is she arriving? - `pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tôt ?` — why is she arriving so early? - `comment travaillez-vous le soir ?` — how do you work in the evening? - `combien de cours regardez-vous en ligne ?` — how many classes do you watch online? - `quel jour sommes-nous ?` — what day is it? - `quelle musique aimes-tu ?` — what kind of music do you like? ### pronunciation note learn `qu'est-ce que` and `quand est-ce qu'` as chunks. they are much easier to hear and produce that way. ### common mistake for english speakers do not confuse `qui` and `qu'est-ce que`. use `qui` for a person doing the action, and `qu'est-ce que` for a thing that is the object of the action. also remember agreement in `quel`: `quel jour`, `quelle saison`, `quels films`, `quelles langues`. ### sentence-building practice write a full question for each answer clue: `à montréal.` / `le vendredi soir.` / `parce qu'elle travaille demain.` / `deux films.` / `le printemps.` use a different question word each time. work backwards: the form of the answer tells you which question word is required. 1. `à montréal.` → `où habitez-vous ?` — a place, so `où`. 2. `le vendredi soir.` → `quand est-ce que vous regardez des films ?` — a time, so `quand`. 3. `parce qu'elle travaille demain.` → `pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tôt ?` — the answer opens with `parce que`, which only ever answers `pourquoi`. 4. `deux films.` → `combien de films regardez-vous ?` — a quantity, so `combien de`. keep `de` with no article: `combien de films`, never *combien des films*. 5. `le printemps.` → `quelle saison préférez-vous ?` — choosing from a known set, so `quel`. it agrees with the noun, and `saison` is feminine, hence `quelle`. the pairing to memorise is question word to answer type: `où` → place, `quand` → time, `pourquoi` → `parce que`, `combien de` → number, `quel` → a choice from a set. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `le lundi, je travaille à la maison.` — on mondays, i work at home. 2. `nous étudions le français en septembre et en octobre.` — we study french in september and october. 3. `est-ce que vous regardez souvent des films français le soir ?` — do you often watch french movies in the evening? 4. `pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tôt le dimanche matin ?` — why is she arriving so early on sunday morning? 5. `au printemps, mes amis visitent souvent le marché près de la rivière.` — in spring, my friends often visit the market near the river. 6. `parlez-vous avec vos voisins quand vous rentrez du travail ?` — do you speak with your neighbors when you come home from work? the sentences are in front of you, so the answer here is what to check once you have written each one from memory and uncovered it. - **silent endings you cannot hear.** sentence 3 `regardez` and sentence 5 `visitent` sound like `regarde` and `visite`. if you wrote the singular, that is a spelling problem, not a listening failure — the sound really is identical. - **the little words that vanish in speech.** `le` in `le lundi`, `des` in `des films`, and `près de` in sentence 5 are unstressed and easy to drop. - **`est-ce qu'` before a vowel** in sentence 4, not `est-ce que elle`. - **accents.** `français`, `préférez`, `si tôt` — a missing accent is a wrong word, not a typo. - **the space before `?`** in sentences 3, 4 and 6. score yourself per sentence: one point for meaning, one for every ending correct. rewrite from memory only the sentences you did not score full marks on. ### notice and mark 1. circle the conjugated `-er` verb. 2. underline the time expression. 3. box the question marker if the sentence is a question. 4. decide whether the sentence shows a habit, a current action, or a scheduled event. | # | `-er` verb | time expression | question marker | type | |---|---|---|---|---| | 1 | `travaille` | `le lundi` | — | habit | | 2 | `étudions` | `en septembre et en octobre` | — | habit | | 3 | `regardez` | `le soir` | `est-ce que` | habit | | 4 | `arrive` | `le dimanche matin` | `pourquoi est-ce qu'` | habit | | 5 | `visitent` | `au printemps` | — | habit | | 6 | `parlez` | `quand vous rentrez du travail` | inversion `parlez-vous` | habit | every sentence here is a habit, and that is the point: each one carries `le` plus a day, `en` plus a month or season, or a repeated-time word such as `souvent`. strip `le` from sentence 1 and it becomes a scheduled event — `lundi, je travaille à la maison` means this coming monday only. sentence 4 is worth a second look: `est-ce que` becomes `est-ce qu'` before the vowel of `elle`. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: first change the subject, then change one meaningful detail such as the day, month, season, place, or activity. make every necessary change to the verb ending and word order. one worked version of each. yours will differ; what matters is that the verb ending follows the new subject. 1. subject: `le lundi, nous travaillons à la maison.` detail: `le samedi, je travaille au bureau.` 2. subject: `elle étudie le français en septembre et en octobre.` detail: `nous étudions le français en hiver.` 3. subject: `est-ce que tu regardes souvent des films français le soir ?` detail: `est-ce que vous regardez souvent des films français le week-end ?` 4. subject: `pourquoi est-ce qu'ils arrivent si tôt le dimanche matin ?` detail: `pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tard le lundi soir ?` the traps, in order: `nous` forces `-ons`; `elle` drops the `-ons` back to a silent `-e`; `tu` adds a silent `-s`; and `ils` takes the silent `-ent`, which sounds identical to the singular even though it looks different. only the subject tells you what to write. ### make it personal write four true sentences about your own life: one with `le + day`, one with `en + month` or `en + season`, one yes/no question, and one information question. answers vary. a model set: 1. `le mardi, je téléphone à ma mère.` 2. `en août, je voyage avec ma famille.` 3. `est-ce que tu travailles le week-end ?` 4. `quand est-ce que tu études le français ?` — better as `quand est-ce que tu étudies le français ?`; the accent-free `études` is a noun, not a verb. check your four against this: the day and month are lowercase, `en` introduces the month, every verb ends in `-e` or `-es` to match `je` or `tu`, and both questions have a space before the `?`. ## mini-dialogue: planning a study week **nora :** `salut, malik. tu travailles demain matin ?` — hi, malik. are you working tomorrow morning? **malik :** `non, je travaille l'après-midi, mais le matin, j'étudie à la bibliothèque.` — no, i work in the afternoon, but in the morning i study at the library. **nora :** `est-ce que tu révises aussi le jeudi soir ?` — do you also review on thursday evening? **malik :** `oui, et le vendredi, je regarde une vidéo en français avec ma sœur.` — yes, and on fridays i watch a video in french with my sister. **nora :** `pourquoi est-ce que vous étudiez ensemble ?` — why do you study together? **malik :** `parce que nous parlons français pendant une heure, et ça nous aide beaucoup.` — because we speak french for an hour, and that helps us a lot. **nora :** `quelle saison préférez-vous pour étudier au parc ?` — which season do you prefer for studying in the park? **malik :** `nous préférons le printemps, parce qu'il fait doux et le parc est calme.` — we prefer spring because the weather is mild and the park is calm. ### guided analysis 1. find three different time expressions. 2. list the conjugated `-er` verbs and identify their subjects. 3. find one yes/no question with intonation and one with `est-ce que`. 4. find two information questions and name their question words. 5. rewrite malik's first answer with `nous` instead of `je` where possible. 1. **three time expressions:** `demain matin`, `l'après-midi`, `le matin`, `le jeudi soir`, `le vendredi`, `pendant une heure`, `le printemps`. any three count. notice `le jeudi soir` and `le vendredi` describe habits, while `demain matin` is a one-off. 2. **conjugated `-er` verbs and subjects:** `travailles` (`tu`), `travaille` (`je`), `étudie` (`je`), `révises` (`tu`), `regarde` (`je`), `étudiez` (`vous`), `parlons` (`nous`), `préférez` (`vous`), `préférons` (`nous`). both `aide` (`ça`) and `est` (`le parc`) appear too, but `est` is `être`, not an `-er` verb. 3. **intonation:** `tu travailles demain matin ?` — statement word order, with only the rise in the voice marking the question. **`est-ce que`:** `est-ce que tu révises aussi le jeudi soir ?` 4. **information questions:** `pourquoi est-ce que vous étudiez ensemble ?` uses `pourquoi`, and `quelle saison préférez-vous pour étudier au parc ?` uses `quelle`. `quelle` is feminine to agree with `saison`, and this one uses inversion rather than `est-ce que`. 5. `non, nous travaillons l'après-midi, mais le matin, nous étudions à la bibliothèque.` — both verbs shift to `-ons`. the `s` of `nous` links onto the vowel in `nous étudions`, so it sounds like \"nou-zétudions\". ## connected reading: ma routine du mercredi `le mercredi, je commence la journée assez tôt. le matin, j'arrive au café du quartier vers huit heures et j'étudie pendant une heure avant le travail. à midi, je déjeune avec une collègue qui parle aussi français, alors nous pratiquons ensemble. l'après-midi, je travaille à la maison et je téléphone souvent à mes clients. le soir, je regarde une courte vidéo, puis je note cinq nouvelles expressions dans mon cahier. en hiver, cette routine demande plus d'énergie, mais au printemps, je travaille près de la fenêtre et tout semble plus facile.` — on wednesdays, i start the day fairly early. in the morning, i arrive at the neighborhood café around eight o'clock and study for an hour before work. at noon, i have lunch with a coworker who also speaks french, so we practice together. in the afternoon, i work at home and often call my clients. in the evening, i watch a short video, then i write down five new expressions in my notebook. in winter, this routine takes more energy, but in spring, i work near the window and everything seems easier. ### reading practice 1. list every time expression in the paragraph. 2. find four regular `-er` verbs and give their infinitives. 3. identify one sentence showing a habit and one showing a scheduled action. 4. rewrite the paragraph in the `nous` form where reasonable. 5. write three true sentences about your own study routine using the reading as a model. 1. **time expressions:** `le mercredi`, `le matin`, `vers huit heures`, `pendant une heure`, `avant le travail`, `à midi`, `l'après-midi`, `le soir`, `en hiver`, `au printemps`. note `en hiver` but `au printemps` — spring is the odd one out. 2. **four regular `-er` verbs:** `commence` (`commencer`), `arrive` (`arriver`), `étudie` (`étudier`), `déjeune` (`déjeuner`). also acceptable: `parle`, `pratiquons`, `travaille`, `téléphone`, `regarde`, `note`, `demande`, `semble`. 3. **habit:** `le mercredi, je commence la journée assez tôt.` — `le` plus a day means every wednesday. **scheduled action:** `j'arrive au café du quartier vers huit heures` — a clock time, not a repeated pattern. 4. **in `nous`:** `le mercredi, nous commençons la journée assez tôt. le matin, nous arrivons au café du quartier vers huit heures et nous étudions pendant une heure avant le travail. à midi, nous déjeunons avec une collègue…` — watch `commençons`, where the cedilla keeps the `c` soft, and `nous étudions`, where the `s` of `nous` links onto the vowel. 5. answers vary. check three things: every verb ends in `-e` after `je`, your time expressions sit at the front of the sentence where french likes them, and days and months are lowercase. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. capitalizing time words as in english instead of writing `lundi` and `septembre`. 2. using the wrong preposition: say `en juillet`, `en hiver`, but `au printemps`. 3. forgetting that repeated days usually take singular `le`: `le dimanche`. 4. pronouncing every written ending in `-er` verbs, especially the silent `-ent`. 5. adding english `do/does` to french questions. 6. mixing question systems instead of choosing one. 7. forgetting agreement in `quel`. ## chapter reference sheet ### calendar expressions at a glance | meaning | french pattern | example | |---|---|---| | it is monday | `nous sommes lundi.` | `nous sommes lundi.` | | on mondays | `le lundi` | `le lundi, je cours.` | | in july | `en juillet` | `nous partons en juillet.` | | in winter | `en hiver` | `en hiver, il neige.` | | in spring | `au printemps` | `au printemps, tout change.` | | this morning | `ce matin` | `ce matin, je travaille.` | | this afternoon | `cet après-midi` | `cet après-midi, nous visitons un musée.` | | this evening | `ce soir` | `ce soir, ils dînent dehors.` | | at night | `la nuit` | `la nuit, la rue est calme.` | ### regular `-er` endings | pronoun | ending | example with `étudier` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `-e` | `j'étudie` | | `tu` | `-es` | `tu étudies` | | `il / elle / on` | `-e` | `elle étudie` | | `nous` | `-ons` | `nous étudions` | | `vous` | `-ez` | `vous étudiez` | | `ils / elles` | `-ent` | `ils étudient` | ### question patterns | type | pattern | example | |---|---|---| | yes/no, casual | statement + `?` | `tu regardes la série ?` | | yes/no, neutral | `est-ce que` + statement order | `est-ce que tu regardes la série ?` | | yes/no, careful | inversion | `regardes-tu la série ?` | | information | question word + chosen structure | `pourquoi est-ce qu'il arrive tôt ?` | ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about your weekly routine, your favorite season, and the questions you might ask a classmate about their schedule. include: - at least four different time expressions; - at least five regular present-tense `-er` verbs; - one sentence with `le + day`; - one sentence with `en + month` or `en + season`; - one yes/no question; - two information questions using different question words. after writing: 1. circle every conjugated `-er` verb; 2. underline every time expression; 3. label each question as intonation, `est-ce que`, or inversion; 4. check that each verb matches its subject; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary, so mark against the checklist rather than against a single correct text. here is a paragraph that meets every requirement: `le lundi, je commence à huit heures. en hiver, j'étudie le français à la maison, parce que le café est trop bruyant. le matin, je travaille et l'après-midi, je révise mes notes. en juillet, je voyage avec ma famille, mais je continue à écouter des podcasts. au printemps, je préfère étudier au parc. tu travailles le samedi ? quand est-ce que tu étudies le français ? pourquoi est-ce que tu aimes cette saison ?` how it satisfies the brief: the time expressions are `le lundi`, `à huit heures`, `en hiver`, `le matin`, `l'après-midi`, `en juillet`, `au printemps`; the `-er` verbs are `commence`, `étudie`, `travaille`, `révise`, `voyage`, `continue`, `préfère`, `aimes`; `le lundi` is the `le + day` sentence; `en juillet` and `au printemps` cover the month and season; `tu travailles le samedi ?` is the yes/no question; and the last two are information questions using `quand` and `pourquoi`. mark yourself wrong if any of these appear: - a capitalised day or month, such as **lundi** or **juillet**; - `au` before a month or before `hiver` — only `printemps` takes `au`; - an `-er` verb that does not match its subject, especially a missing `-s` after `tu`; - `est-ce que` combined with inversion in the same question, which doubles the question marker; - a missing space before `?`, which french requires. "},{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 3 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-exercises\">Chapter 3 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>nous / parler</li>\n<li>ils / étudier</li>\n<li>Vous habitez à Lyon.</li>\n<li>Elle arrive mardi.</li>\n<li>Je travaille le matin.</li>\n<li>tu / commencer</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Items 1, 2 and 6 ask you to conjugate. Items 3, 4 and 5 ask you to turn a statement into a question.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code> — regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code>: drop <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code>, add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils étudient</code> — the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code> ending is completely silent, so this sounds identical to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il étudie</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous habitez à Lyon ?</code> — a yes/no question, because the statement contains no detail to ask about. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous habitez à Lyon ?</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Habitez-vous à Lyon ?</code> are equally correct.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mardi</code> is a time, so the question word is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code>. Note <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'</code> before the vowel of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand travaillez-vous ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le matin</code> is again a time. This one uses inversion, so keep the hyphen.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu commences</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> is regular here; the cedilla only appears in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commençons</code>, where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> would otherwise harden before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>calendar language</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes lundi.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>regular -er verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>yes/no questions</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles demain ?</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>information questions</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où habitez-vous ?</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary by design — the point is to produce your own sentence, not to recover a hidden one. A correct answer must clearly show the named structure, conjugate its verb correctly, and keep French word order.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Calendar language: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En octobre, nous visitons ma grand-mère.</code> — uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> + month and keeps the month lowercase.</li>\n<li>Regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous travaillez avec des collègues sympathiques.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ez</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n<li>Yes/no question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu danses souvent ?</code> — no English-style <em>do</em> appears anywhere.</li>\n<li>Information question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce que le magasin ferme ?</code> — question word first, then a complete question structure.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you reused the model sentence's content, if the verb ending does not match the subject, or if you inserted a word translating English <em>do</em> or <em>am</em>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français le lundi.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous parlons français le lundi.</code> — the ending must move with the subject, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code>.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne parle pas français le lundi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> wrap the conjugated verb. Before a vowel sound <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code>, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'aime pas</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu parles français le lundi ?</code></li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle espagnol le lundi.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check that each version still has exactly one conjugated verb agreeing with its subject, and that changing the subject did not leave the old ending behind — that is the most common slip in this drill.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes le douze octobre.</code> — the date takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, and months stay lowercase in French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le vendredi, je travaille à la maison.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> before a day means <em>every</em> Friday. Without it, you mean this coming Friday.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu étudies le français ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudies</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudie</code> sound identical, so only the subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> tells you to write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> elides to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils parlent avec le professeur.</code> — the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code> ending is silent, so this sounds exactly like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parle</code>. The plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils</code> is your only clue.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En hiver, nous commençons à huit heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> with a season, and the cedilla in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commençons</code> keeps the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> soft before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark each pass separately. A silent ending you failed to write is a listening problem; an ending you heard but spelled wrong is a spelling problem, and they need different practice.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a seven-line weekly routine. Include four -er verbs and two questions you could ask a classmate.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model to compare against:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, je travaille à la maison.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mardi, j'étudie le français avec une amie.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mercredi, nous regardons un film.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le jeudi, je téléphone à ma sœur.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le vendredi soir, nous dînons au restaurant.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu travailles le samedi ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce que tu étudies ?</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Count before you check: four different <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs (here <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travailler</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudier</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regarder</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphoner</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dîner</code>) and two questions. Verify that every <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> + day means a genuine habit, and that each verb ending matches its subject.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Calendar language.</strong> Days and months are lowercase. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> with months and most seasons, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">printemps</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> + a singular day for a habit. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, je travaille.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs.</strong> Drop <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> and add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent</code>. Four of the six forms sound identical. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous étudions le français.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Yes/no questions.</strong> Three ways: rising intonation, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>, or inversion. French never adds a word for English <em>do</em>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous aimez Paris ?</code></li>\n<li><strong>Information questions.</strong> Put a question word first, then a question structure. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quel</code> agrees with its noun. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où habitez-vous ?</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you could state the rule but not produce the example, the rule is not learned yet — recognition and production are different skills. Redo this section tomorrow before moving on.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 3 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. nous / parler 2. ils / étudier 3. vous habitez à lyon. 4. elle arrive mardi. 5. je travaille le matin. 6. tu / commencer items 1, 2 and 6 ask you to conjugate. items 3, 4 and 5 ask you to turn a statement into a question. 1. `nous parlons` — regular `-er`: drop `-er`, add `-ons`. 2. `ils étudient` — the `-ent` ending is completely silent, so this sounds identical to `il étudie`. 3. `est-ce que vous habitez à lyon ?` — a yes/no question, because the statement contains no detail to ask about. `vous habitez à lyon ?` and `habitez-vous à lyon ?` are equally correct. 4. `quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?` — `mardi` is a time, so the question word is `quand`. note `qu'` before the vowel of `elle`. 5. `quand travaillez-vous ?` — `le matin` is again a time. this one uses inversion, so keep the hyphen. 6. `tu commences` — `commencer` is regular here; the cedilla only appears in `nous commençons`, where `c` would otherwise harden before `o`. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **calendar language**. use `nous sommes lundi.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **regular -er verbs**. use `je parle français.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **yes/no questions**. use `tu travailles demain ?` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **information questions**. use `où habitez-vous ?` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary by design — the point is to produce your own sentence, not to recover a hidden one. a correct answer must clearly show the named structure, conjugate its verb correctly, and keep french word order. sample answers: 1. calendar language: `en octobre, nous visitons ma grand-mère.` — uses `en` + month and keeps the month lowercase. 2. regular `-er` verbs: `vous travaillez avec des collègues sympathiques.` — `-ez` for `vous`. 3. yes/no question: `est-ce que tu danses souvent ?` — no english-style *do* appears anywhere. 4. information question: `quand est-ce que le magasin ferme ?` — question word first, then a complete question structure. mark yourself wrong if you reused the model sentence's content, if the verb ending does not match the subject, or if you inserted a word translating english *do* or *am*. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `je parle français le lundi.` 1. change the subject: `nous parlons français le lundi.` — the ending must move with the subject, `-e` to `-ons`. 2. make it negative: `je ne parle pas français le lundi.` — `ne` and `pas` wrap the conjugated verb. before a vowel sound `ne` becomes `n'`, as in `je n'aime pas`. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que tu parles français le lundi ?` 4. change one meaningful detail: `je parle espagnol le lundi.` check that each version still has exactly one conjugated verb agreeing with its subject, and that changing the subject did not leave the old ending behind — that is the most common slip in this drill. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `nous sommes le douze octobre.` — the date takes `le`, and months stay lowercase in french. 2. `le vendredi, je travaille à la maison.` — `le` before a day means *every* friday. without it, you mean this coming friday. 3. `est-ce que tu étudies le français ?` — `étudies` and `étudie` sound identical, so only the subject `tu` tells you to write `-es`. 4. `quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?` — `que` elides to `qu'` before `elle`. 5. `ils parlent avec le professeur.` — the `-ent` ending is silent, so this sounds exactly like `il parle`. the plural `ils` is your only clue. 6. `en hiver, nous commençons à huit heures.` — `en` with a season, and the cedilla in `commençons` keeps the `c` soft before `o`. mark each pass separately. a silent ending you failed to write is a listening problem; an ending you heard but spelled wrong is a spelling problem, and they need different practice. ## e. personal writing write a seven-line weekly routine. include four -er verbs and two questions you could ask a classmate. answers vary. a model to compare against: 1. `le lundi, je travaille à la maison.` 2. `le mardi, j'étudie le français avec une amie.` 3. `le mercredi, nous regardons un film.` 4. `le jeudi, je téléphone à ma sœur.` 5. `le vendredi soir, nous dînons au restaurant.` 6. `est-ce que tu travailles le samedi ?` 7. `quand est-ce que tu étudies ?` count before you check: four different `-er` verbs (here `travailler`, `étudier`, `regarder`, `téléphoner`, `dîner`) and two questions. verify that every `le` + day means a genuine habit, and that each verb ending matches its subject. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **calendar language.** days and months are lowercase. use `en` with months and most seasons, `au` with `printemps`, and `le` + a singular day for a habit. example: `le samedi, je travaille.` 2. **regular `-er` verbs.** drop `-er` and add `-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent`. four of the six forms sound identical. example: `nous étudions le français.` 3. **yes/no questions.** three ways: rising intonation, `est-ce que`, or inversion. french never adds a word for english *do*. example: `est-ce que vous aimez paris ?` 4. **information questions.** put a question word first, then a question structure. `quel` agrees with its noun. example: `où habitez-vous ?` if you could state the rule but not produce the example, the rule is not learned yet — recognition and production are different skills. redo this section tomorrow before moving on. "},{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 3 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-answer-key\">Chapter 3 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils étudient</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous habitez à Lyon ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand travaillez-vous ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu commences</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Calendar language:</strong> Days and months are normally lowercase. Use en with months and seasons in common date expressions, and le with a recurring day.</li>\n<li><strong>Regular -er verbs:</strong> Remove -er and add -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent. The written endings differ more than their pronunciation.</li>\n<li><strong>Yes/no questions:</strong> Use rising intonation, est-ce que, or inversion. Est-ce que is reliable in everyday speech.</li>\n<li><strong>Information questions:</strong> Combine question words such as où, quand, comment, pourquoi, combien, and qui with a question structure.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 3 answer key ## section a 1. `nous parlons` 2. `ils étudient` 3. `est-ce que vous habitez à lyon ?` 4. `quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?` 5. `quand travaillez-vous ?` 6. `tu commences` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **calendar language:** days and months are normally lowercase. use en with months and seasons in common date expressions, and le with a recurring day. - **regular -er verbs:** remove -er and add -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent. the written endings differ more than their pronunciation. - **yes/no questions:** use rising intonation, est-ce que, or inversion. est-ce que is reliable in everyday speech. - **information questions:** combine question words such as où, quand, comment, pourquoi, combien, and qui with a question structure. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 3 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 3 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Calendar language</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Regular -er verbs</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Yes/no questions</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Information questions</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 3 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **calendar language**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **regular -er verbs** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **yes/no questions**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **information questions**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":4,"slug":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs","title":"Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs","documents":[{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/README.md","title":"Chapter 4: Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-numbers-dates-time-and-regular-ir-verbs\">Chapter 4: Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Say cardinal and ordinal numbers.</li>\n<li>Write dates and tell time.</li>\n<li>Conjugate regular -ir verbs.</li>\n<li>Use shopping and meal vocabulary in short exchanges.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Numbers and dates</li>\n<li>Time</li>\n<li>Regular -ir verbs</li>\n<li>Functional practice</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 4: numbers, dates, time, and regular -ir verbs this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - say cardinal and ordinal numbers. - write dates and tell time. - conjugate regular -ir verbs. - use shopping and meal vocabulary in short exchanges. ## lesson sequence 1. numbers and dates 2. time 3. regular -ir verbs 4. functional practice ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 4 Lessons: Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-lessons-numbers-dates-time-and-regular-ir-verbs\">Chapter 4 Lessons: Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs</h1>\n<p>This chapter gives you the language you need for real-world details: prices, schedules, birthdays, addresses, appointments, and everyday choices. You will also learn the regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verb pattern, which opens the door to many useful verbs of finishing, choosing, succeeding, and reacting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>say and recognize French numbers from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">0</code> through <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">100+</code>;</li>\n<li>form useful larger numbers for years, prices, room numbers, and quantities;</li>\n<li>use common ordinal numbers such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deuxième</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">troisième</code>;</li>\n<li>say dates naturally, including the special pattern for the first day of the month;</li>\n<li>tell time with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est...</code>, including <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">minuit</code>, quarter hours, and 24-hour time;</li>\n<li>conjugate regular present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verbs in all six persons;</li>\n<li>combine numbers, time, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verbs in practical speech and writing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can still:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>use chapter 3 time expressions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le lundi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en septembre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir</code>;</li>\n<li>recognize the three basic question patterns from Chapter 3;</li>\n<li>conjugate regular present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> from Chapter 2 for identity, age, and simple descriptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That background matters because this chapter adds new vocabulary and a new verb family, but it still depends on earlier sentence structure.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test the old skills before you add the new ones. Answer these five without notes, then check.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How do you say that something happens every Monday? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, je travaille.</code> The pattern <strong>le plus a day</strong> means a repeated habit.</li>\n<li>Ask a yes/no question from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles demain.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles demain ?</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu travailles demain ?</code> French does not add an English-style helper verb.</li>\n<li>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudier</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous étudions</code>. Regular -er verbs use <strong>-ons</strong> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li>Say that you are twenty years old. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code> French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> for age, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</li>\n<li>Give <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles sont</code>. This form is irregular, so you must know it by memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review Chapters 2 and 3 first. Numbers and regular -ir verbs will feel much harder if the subject, question pattern, or basic verb form is still unstable.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Say every number aloud instead of only reading it silently. For time and verb tables, cover one column, reproduce it from memory, then use the practice blocks to force quick recall.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-cardinal-numbers-from-0-to-69\">Unit 1: Cardinal numbers from 0 to 69</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French numbers follow clear patterns, but you must know the first group very well before the larger patterns feel automatic.</p>\n<h3 id=\"numbers-0-20\">Numbers 0-20</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>French</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">0</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">zéro</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">11</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">onze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">1</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">12</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">douze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">2</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">13</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">treize</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">3</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">14</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatorze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">4</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">15</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quinze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">5</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinq</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">16</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">seize</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">6</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">17</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-sept</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">7</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sept</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">18</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-huit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">8</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">19</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">9</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">20</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">10</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"tens-and-building-patterns-through-69\">Tens and building patterns through 69</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>French</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">21</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt et un</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">40</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quarante</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">22</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt-deux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">50</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinquante</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">30</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trente</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">60</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">31</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trente et un</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">61</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante et un</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">35</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trente-cinq</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">68</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante-huit</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-patterns-to-notice\">Core patterns to notice</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">17-19</code> combine <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-</code> with another number: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-sept</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-huit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">21</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">31</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">41</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">51</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">61</code> use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et un</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt et un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trente et un</code>.</li>\n<li>Most other numbers in these ranges use a hyphen: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt-deux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trente-neuf</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinquante-sept</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai deux billets.</code> — I have two tickets.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous cherchons vingt-deux chaises.</code> — We are looking for twenty-two chairs.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le café coûte cinq euros.</code> — The coffee costs five euros.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils habitent au trente-et-un, rue Victor-Hugo.</code> — They live at 31 Victor Hugo Street.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle travaille avec soixante clients.</code> — She works with sixty clients.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The final consonants in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinq</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix</code> change depending on what follows. In fast speech, listen carefully to the number as a whole chunk rather than trying to sound out each letter separately.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not forget the hyphen in written compound numbers like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt-deux</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quarante-neuf</code>. Also watch <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt et un</code>: the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code> appears with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, but not with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code>, and the others.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Say these aloud in French, then write them:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>14 books</li>\n<li>21 students</li>\n<li>36 euros</li>\n<li>58 minutes</li>\n<p>Then invent two more number phrases from your own life.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatorze livres</code> — fourteen is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatorze</code>; the noun is plural because there is more than one.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt et un étudiants</code> — numbers ending in one use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et un</code> through this range. The noun is plural in normal counting.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trente-six euros</code> — most compound numbers use a hyphen, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinquante-huit minutes</code> — use a hyphen between the tens and ones.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Two personal examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux cafés</code> — the noun is plural with more than one item.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt minutes</code> — the final <strong>t</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt</code> is normally silent when it stands before a consonant.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-numbers-from-70-upward-and-bigger-numbers\">Unit 2: Numbers from 70 upward, and bigger numbers</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French keeps building numbers rather than starting brand-new words at <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">70</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">90</code>, so pattern recognition matters.</p>\n<h3 id=\"key-numbers-from-70-to-100\">Key numbers from 70 to 100+</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>French</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">70</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante-dix</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">90</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-dix</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">71</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante et onze</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">91</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-onze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">72</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante-douze</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">95</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-quinze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">80</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingts</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">100</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">81</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-un</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">101</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cent un</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">88</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-huit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">200</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux cents</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">99</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-dix-neuf</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">1 000</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mille</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"useful-large-number-reminders\">Useful large-number reminders</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cent</code> + number</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cent trois</code></td>\n<td>one hundred three</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cents</code> when nothing follows</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux cents</code></td>\n<td>two hundred</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> when another number follows</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux cent cinq</code></td>\n<td>two hundred five</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mille</code> never takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois mille</code></td>\n<td>three thousand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>modern years often begin with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux mille...</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux mille vingt-six</code></td>\n<td>2026</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le voyage coûte quatre-vingt-dix euros.</code> — The trip costs ninety euros.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous invitons soixante-quinze personnes.</code> — We are inviting seventy-five people.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le bureau est au cent un, avenue Centrale.</code> — The office is at 101 Central Avenue.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon code est mille deux.</code> — My code is 1002.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le cours compte cent étudiants.</code> — The course has one hundred students.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quatre-vingts</code> has an <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> only when it stands alone as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">80</code>. The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> disappears in numbers like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-un</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-dix</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>English speakers often want a neat single word for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">70</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">90</code>. Standard French instead uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante-dix</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-dix</code>, so train yourself to hear the pattern instead of fighting it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Write these in French: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">71</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">84</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">99</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">105</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">230</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">2,026</code>. Then say them aloud without looking.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante et onze</code> — seventy-one is built as sixty and eleven, and this form keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-quatre</code> — after eighty, the final <strong>s</strong> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingts</code> disappears because another number follows.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-dix-neuf</code> — ninety-nine is literally four-twenty-ten-nine. Keep the hyphens.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cent cinq</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cent</code> takes no <strong>s</strong> because another number follows.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux cent trente</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cent</code> again has no <strong>s</strong> because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trente</code> follows.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux mille vingt-six</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mille</code> never takes <strong>s</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The common trap is writing the plural <strong>s</strong> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingts</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux cents</code> even when another number follows. It disappears before a following number.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-ordinal-numbers-dates-and-years\">Unit 3: Ordinal numbers, dates, and years</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Cardinal numbers count (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code>), while ordinal numbers place things in order (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deuxième</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">troisième</code>). Dates use both systems, but mostly cardinal numbers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-ordinal-numbers\">Common ordinal numbers</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier / première</code></td>\n<td>first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deuxième</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">second / seconde</code></td>\n<td>second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">troisième</code></td>\n<td>third</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatrième</code></td>\n<td>fourth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinquième</code></td>\n<td>fifth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sixième</code></td>\n<td>sixth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">septième</code></td>\n<td>seventh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huitième</code></td>\n<td>eighth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuvième</code></td>\n<td>ninth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dixième</code></td>\n<td>tenth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"dates-in-french\">Dates in French</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le + cardinal + month</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le quinze septembre</code></td>\n<td>September fifteenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>special form for the first</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier novembre</code></td>\n<td>November first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>full date with year</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le 14 juillet 2027</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le quatorze juillet deux mille vingt-sept</code></td>\n<td>July 14, 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + year</code> for \"in\" a year</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en 2027</code></td>\n<td>in 2027</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.</code> — My birthday is November first.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La réunion est le vingt-trois avril.</code> — The meeting is April twenty-third.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous partons en deux mille vingt-sept.</code> — We are leaving in 2027.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le troisième chapitre est plus long.</code> — The third chapter is longer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle habite au deuxième étage.</code> — She lives on the second floor.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>For ordinary dates, only the first day of the month uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code>. Every other date uses a cardinal number: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le deux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le douze</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vingt-huit</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not automatically use ordinal numbers for every date. French says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le cinq mai</code>, not the exact equivalent of \"the fifth of May\" in basic modern usage. The big exception is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Answer in full French phrases:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is your birthday?</li>\n<li>What month is a major holiday for you?</li>\n<li>What floor do you live or work on?</li>\n<li>What year is it now?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary for personal details. Model full phrases:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Birthday: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le premier mai.</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code> only for the first day of the month.</li>\n<li>Major holiday month: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Une fête importante est en décembre.</code> — months take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> and stay lowercase.</li>\n<li>Floor: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite au deuxième étage.</code> — ordinal numbers place things in order, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deuxième</code> for second.</li>\n<li>Current year: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes en deux mille vingt-six.</code> — years use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mille</code> does not take <strong>s</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own birthday, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier</code> only if the date is the first. For every other day, use the cardinal number, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le deux avril</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vingt-trois juin</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-telling-time\">Unit 4: Telling time</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est...</code> to tell the time. French can use either a 12-hour style with context or a 24-hour style, especially in schedules and transportation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"basic-time-patterns\">Basic time patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est + number + heure(s)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est trois heures.</code></td>\n<td>It is three o'clock.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est + hour + minute</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est neuf heures quinze.</code></td>\n<td>It is 9:15.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi.</code></td>\n<td>It is noon.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est minuit.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est minuit.</code></td>\n<td>It is midnight.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>quarter past</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi et quart.</code></td>\n<td>It is 12:15.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>half past</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est deux heures et demie.</code></td>\n<td>It is 2:30.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>quarter to</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est cinq heures moins le quart.</code></td>\n<td>It is 4:45.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"12-hour-and-24-hour-examples\">12-hour and 24-hour examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est huit heures du matin.</code> — It is 8:00 a.m.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est deux heures de l'après-midi.</code> — It is 2:00 p.m.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est dix-neuf heures trente.</code> — It is 7:30 p.m.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le cours commence à neuf heures quinze.</code> — The class starts at 9:15.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous finissons à midi.</code> — We finish at noon.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le train arrive à vingt et une heures.</code> — The train arrives at 9:00 p.m.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Use singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heure</code> only for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une heure</code>; use plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heures</code> for everything else. In everyday speech, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">minuit</code> replace longer forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code> with a subject other than the fixed expression <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code>. Clock time uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est...</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est...</code> for this core pattern. Also remember that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et demie</code> agrees with the understood feminine noun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heure</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux heures et demie</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Say and write these times in French:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>7:00</li>\n<li>9:15</li>\n<li>12:00</li>\n<li>12:15</li>\n<li>4:45</li>\n<li>19:30</li>\n<p>Then write one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> to say when an event starts.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est sept heures.</code> — clock time uses the fixed expression <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est neuf heures quinze.</code> — put the minutes directly after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heures</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi.</code> — noon has the special word <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code>, not a longer twelve-hour phrase.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi et quart.</code> — quarter past uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et quart</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est cinq heures moins le quart.</code> — this means a quarter to five, so it is 4:45.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est dix-neuf heures trente.</code> — 24-hour time is common for schedules.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Event sentence: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le cours commence à neuf heures quinze.</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> to say at what time something starts. Do not replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est</code> for basic clock time.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-regular-ir-verbs-in-the-present-tense\">Unit 5: Regular -ir verbs in the present tense</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A major group of regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verbs follows the pattern of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>. These verbs add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iss-</code> in the plural forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"model-conjugation-finir\">Model conjugation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code></td>\n<td>I finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code></td>\n<td>you finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finit</code></td>\n<td>he/she/one finishes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissons</code></td>\n<td>we finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissez</code></td>\n<td>you finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissent</code></td>\n<td>they finish</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"more-common-regular-ir-verbs\">More common regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir</code></td>\n<td>to choose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussir</code></td>\n<td>to succeed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réfléchir</code></td>\n<td>to think, reflect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grandir</code></td>\n<td>to grow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">obéir</code></td>\n<td>to obey</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rougir</code></td>\n<td>to blush, turn red</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je finis tôt le jeudi.</code> — I finish early on Thursdays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons deux desserts pour huit euros.</code> — We choose two desserts for eight euros.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle réussit souvent ses examens.</code> — She often succeeds on her exams.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous réfléchissez trop longtemps.</code> — You think for too long.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils grandissent vite.</code> — They are growing quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On obéit aux règles du musée.</code> — People obey the museum rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The plural forms are easy to hear because of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-issons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-issez</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-issent</code>. Say them aloud as a family: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissent</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Not every verb ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> is regular like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>. Verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dormir</code> follow a different pattern, so do not assume every <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verb works the same way.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussir</code> for all six persons. Then write three original sentences using three different verbs from the table.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>All three verbs are regular in this pattern: remove <strong>-ir</strong>, then add <strong>-is</strong>, <strong>-is</strong>, <strong>-it</strong>, <strong>-issons</strong>, <strong>-issez</strong>, <strong>-issent</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je finis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je choisis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je réussis</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu finis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu choisis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu réussis</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il finit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle choisit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on réussit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous choisissons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous réussissons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous finissez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous choisissez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous réussissez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils finissent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles choisissent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils réussissent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Original sentence models:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je finis le travail à dix-huit heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons deux desserts.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> takes the audible <strong>-issons</strong> form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle réussit son examen en mai.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussit</code>, ending in silent <strong>-t</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The main trap is forgetting the <strong>-iss-</strong> in the plural forms.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-6-combining-numbers-time-and-ir-verbs\">Unit 6: Combining numbers, time, and -ir verbs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>This chapter becomes truly useful when you combine its pieces: a time, a quantity, and a regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verb inside one sentence.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-practical-contexts\">Examples in practical contexts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le cours commence à neuf heures quinze, et nous finissons à midi.</code> — The class starts at 9:15, and we finish at noon.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le premier novembre, alors je choisis souvent un bon restaurant.</code> — My birthday is November first, so I often choose a good restaurant.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les magasins finissent leur journée à dix-neuf heures.</code> — The stores finish their day at 7 p.m.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous réfléchissez pendant cinq minutes avant de répondre.</code> — You think for five minutes before answering.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous réussissons mieux quand nous étudions le matin.</code> — We do better when we study in the morning.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-or-listening-note\">Pronunciation or listening note</h3>\n<p>In a long sentence, the quantity and time expressions often carry the most audible stress. Practice reading them as smooth groups: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à neuf heures quinze</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour huit euros</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier novembre</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not let the number expression distract you from the verb ending. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous choisissons deux desserts</code>, the subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> still controls the verb, no matter how long the object phrase becomes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Build one sentence from each set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous / choisir / trois cafés / à dix heures</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je / finir / le travail / à dix-huit heures</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle / réussir / l'examen / en mai</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous / réfléchir / cinq minutes / avant la réponse</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons trois cafés à dix heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> requires <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisissons</code>; the object phrase does not affect the verb ending.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je finis le travail à dix-huit heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> introduces the clock time.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle réussit l'examen en mai.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussit</code>; use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> before a month.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous réfléchissez cinq minutes avant la réponse.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réfléchissez</code>. The time quantity <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinq minutes</code> does not change the verb.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check subject before details. Long number and time phrases can distract you, but the subject still controls the conjugated verb.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le cours commence à neuf heures quinze.</code> — The class starts at 9:15.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.</code> — My birthday is November first.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons deux desserts pour huit euros.</code> — We choose two desserts for eight euros.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les magasins finissent leur journée à dix-neuf heures.</code> — The stores finish their day at 7 p.m.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En deux mille vingt-six, je réussis enfin mon objectif.</code> — In 2026, I finally achieve my goal.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quelle heure finissez-vous le vendredi ?</code> — What time do you finish on Fridays?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>The sentences are visible, so use this block as a correction checklist after you write them from memory.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Number spelling and hyphens.</strong> Sentence 5 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux mille vingt-six</code>, and sentence 4 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf heures</code>. Do not drop the hyphen inside <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Special date form.</strong> Sentence 2 uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier novembre</code> because the day is the first. For any other day, you would use a cardinal number.</li>\n<li><strong>Clock-time wording.</strong> Sentence 1 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf heures quinze</code>, and sentence 4 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf heures</code>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heures</code> for clock time, not an English-style colon in the written sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Regular -ir endings.</strong> Sentence 3 <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisissons</code>, sentence 4 <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissent</code>, sentence 5 <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussis</code>, and sentence 6 <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissez</code> must match their subjects.</li>\n<li><strong>Question formatting.</strong> Sentence 6 uses inversion in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissez-vous</code> and has a space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Score the content and the spelling separately. If the meaning is right but an ending or hyphen is wrong, rewrite that one sentence from memory again.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Circle the number or date expression in each sentence.</li>\n<li>Underline the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li>Box the time phrase if there is one.</li>\n<li>Say whether each sentence is about a date, a clock time, a quantity, or a combination.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Number or date expression</th>\n<th>Conjugated verb</th>\n<th>Time phrase</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf heures quinze</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à neuf heures quinze</code></td>\n<td>clock time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier novembre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux desserts</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit euros</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisissons</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>quantity and price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf heures</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à dix-neuf heures</code></td>\n<td>clock time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux mille vingt-six</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En deux mille vingt-six</code></td>\n<td>year/date expression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vendredi</code></td>\n<td>repeated day/time expression</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The rule behind the table is simple: number phrases can describe dates, clock times, prices, or quantities. Do not let the number phrase distract you from the conjugated verb.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: first change the subject, then change one detail such as the time, price, date, number, or object. Make every necessary change to the verb ending and article.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>One worked set is below. Your details may differ, but the verb ending must follow the new subject.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les cours commencent à neuf heures quinze.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le cours commence à dix heures trente.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nos anniversaires sont le premier novembre.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le quinze juin.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je choisis deux desserts pour huit euros.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons trois cafés pour neuf euros.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le magasin finit sa journée à dix-neuf heures.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les magasins finissent leur journée à vingt heures.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Watch the regular -ir forms especially: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je choisis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous choisissons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le magasin finit</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les magasins finissent</code>. The price, time, or object never controls the verb; the subject does.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four true sentences about your own life using these chapter structures: one date, one clock time, one price or quantity, and one regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verb sentence.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Date: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le vingt avril.</code> — Use a cardinal number because it is not the first of the month.</li>\n<li>Clock time: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je commence le travail à neuf heures.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> to say when an event starts.</li>\n<li>Price or quantity: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète deux cafés pour six euros.</code> — The number phrase describes the object and price.</li>\n<li>Regular -ir verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je choisis un livre le samedi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisis</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if your date uses an ordinal after the first, if your time sentence uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est</code> for basic clock time, or if your regular -ir verb is missing the <strong>-iss-</strong> sound in a plural form such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous choisissons</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-choosing-dessert-after-class\">Mini-dialogue: choosing dessert after class</h2>\n<p><strong>Aïcha :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est quelle heure ?</code> — What time is it?</p>\n<p><strong>Lucas :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi vingt, et le cours finit enfin.</code> — It is 12:20, and the class is finally ending.</p>\n<p><strong>Aïcha :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu choisis quoi pour le déjeuner ?</code> — What are you choosing for lunch?</p>\n<p><strong>Lucas :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je choisis une soupe et deux tartes pour onze euros.</code> — I am choosing a soup and two tarts for eleven euros.</p>\n<p><strong>Aïcha :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu réussis toujours à manger vite avant le travail ?</code> — Do you always manage to eat quickly before work?</p>\n<p><strong>Lucas :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pas toujours, mais aujourd'hui je finis tôt.</code> — Not always, but today I finish early.</p>\n<p><strong>Aïcha :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ton anniversaire, c'est bien le premier novembre ?</code> — Your birthday really is November first, right?</p>\n<p><strong>Lucas :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, et cette année, je choisis un grand dîner avec huit amis.</code> — Yes, and this year I am choosing a big dinner with eight friends.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find two regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verbs and identify their subjects.</li>\n<li>List every number expression.</li>\n<li>Find one clock time and one date.</li>\n<li>Rewrite Lucas's second line with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier novembre</code> to a different date of your choice.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Two regular -ir verbs in the dialogue include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finit</code> with subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le cours</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisis</code> with subjects <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussis</code> with subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code> with subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je choisis</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu choisis</code> look the same because both singular forms end in <strong>-is</strong>.</li>\n<li>Number expressions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi vingt</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux tartes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">onze euros</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier novembre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit amis</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette année</code> is a time expression, but it is not a number expression.</li>\n<li>Clock time: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi vingt</code>. Date: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier novembre</code>. The date uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code> because it is the first day of the month.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons une soupe et deux tartes pour onze euros.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> requires the regular -ir ending <strong>-issons</strong>.</li>\n<li>A different date: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le quinze septembre</code>. Use a cardinal number for ordinary dates; only the first uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-un-samedi-bien-organis\">Connected reading: un samedi bien organisé</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, je commence lentement, mais à dix heures je finis déjà mes courses du marché. J'achète souvent trois légumes, deux fromages et un dessert pour environ vingt euros. À midi, je déjeune avec mon frère, puis nous choisissons un film pour l'après-midi. Si le temps est mauvais, nous réfléchissons à la maison; si le soleil revient, nous sortons. Mon père téléphone parfois vers dix-sept heures parce qu'il veut savoir si je réussis ma recette. Le soir, tout le monde arrive à la même heure, et le dîner commence vers vingt heures.</code></p>\n<p>— On Saturdays, I start slowly, but by ten o'clock I have already finished my market shopping. I often buy three vegetables, two cheeses, and a dessert for about twenty euros. At noon, I have lunch with my brother, then we choose a movie for the afternoon. If the weather is bad, we think things over at home; if the sun comes back, we go out. My father sometimes calls around five o'clock because he wants to know whether I succeed with my recipe. In the evening, everyone arrives at the same time, and dinner starts around eight o'clock.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List every number or time expression in the paragraph.</li>\n<li>Find four present-tense verbs and sort them into <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> or regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the second sentence with different quantities and a different price.</li>\n<li>Find the sentence that contains a yes/no idea indirectly and explain it.</li>\n<li>Write three new schedule sentences about your own Saturday.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Number and time expressions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à dix heures</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois légumes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux fromages</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un dessert</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt euros</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'après-midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vers dix-sept heures</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à la même heure</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vers vingt heures</code>.</li>\n<li>Present-tense verbs sorted by type: regular -er verbs include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjeune</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrive</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code>; regular -ir verbs include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisissons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réfléchissons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussis</code>. The verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortons</code> is an -ir verb, but it is not regular like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewritten second sentence: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète souvent quatre pommes, trois fromages et deux desserts pour environ trente euros.</code> — The quantities and price changed, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'achète</code> still agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li>The indirect yes/no idea is in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il veut savoir si je réussis ma recette</code>. The word <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> means <strong>whether</strong> here; it embeds the question “Do I succeed with my recipe?” without using a question mark.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. Models: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, je finis mes courses à onze heures.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À midi, je choisis un sandwich.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, je réussis souvent une recette simple.</code> Check that each schedule sentence has a time expression and that every regular -ir verb matches its subject.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forgetting the hyphens in compound numbers such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quarante-deux</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-dix-neuf</code>.</li>\n<li>Writing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingts-un</code> with an <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code>; the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> disappears when another number follows.</li>\n<li>Using ordinal numbers for every date instead of only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est...</code> for clock time.</li>\n<li>Mixing up <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">minuit</code>.</li>\n<li>Assuming every <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verb is regular like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iss-</code> pattern in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code> forms.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"essential-number-patterns\">Essential number patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Range or form</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">0-16</code></td>\n<td>memorize individually</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">zéro</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">seize</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">17-19</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-</code> + number</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-huit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">21/31/41/51/61</code></td>\n<td>tens + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et un</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt et un</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">22-69</code></td>\n<td>tens + hyphen + number</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinquante-deux</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">70-79</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante-</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">10-19</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soixante-quatorze</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">80</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingts</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">80</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">81-99</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-</code> + number</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-sept</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">100+</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mille</code> patterns</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux cent trois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mille</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"dates-and-time-at-a-glance\">Dates and time at a glance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>November first</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier novembre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>April 23</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vingt-trois avril</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La réunion est le vingt-trois avril.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in 2028</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en deux mille vingt-huit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous partons en deux mille vingt-huit.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>it is 3:00</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est trois heures.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est trois heures.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>it is noon</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>it is midnight</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est minuit.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est minuit.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>it is 7:30 p.m.</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est dix-neuf heures trente.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le train part à dix-neuf heures trente.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"regular-ir-endings\">Regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>Example with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-is</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je choisis</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-is</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu choisis</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-it</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle choisit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-issons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous choisissons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-issez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous choisissez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-issent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils choisissent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a real or imagined day that includes appointments, prices, and choices. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least five number expressions;</li>\n<li>one full date and one year;</li>\n<li>three clock times, including either <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">minuit</code>;</li>\n<li>at least four regular present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verbs;</li>\n<li>one question about time or quantity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every number, date, and time phrase;</li>\n<li>underline each regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verb;</li>\n<li>check whether each number is spelled and hyphenated correctly;</li>\n<li>make sure your date uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier</code> only when needed;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any sentence you corrected from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary, so compare your paragraph to the requirements. Here is one model that satisfies the brief:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le quinze avril deux mille vingt-six, je commence une journée très organisée. À huit heures, je choisis deux cafés pour six euros. À neuf heures trente, je finis un dossier important. À midi, nous choisissons trois sandwichs pour vingt-quatre euros. À quatorze heures, ma sœur réussit son examen. Le soir, je réfléchis pendant quinze minutes avant de choisir un dessert. Nous finissons la journée à dix-neuf heures. Combien de cafés est-ce que tu choisis le matin ?</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: number expressions include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quinze</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux mille vingt-six</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit heures</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux cafés</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">six euros</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf heures trente</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois sandwichs</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt-quatre euros</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatorze heures</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quinze minutes</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf heures</code>; the full date is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le quinze avril deux mille vingt-six</code>; the clock times are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">huit heures</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">neuf heures trente</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatorze heures</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf heures</code>; regular -ir verbs include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisissons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réfléchis</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissons</code>; the final sentence asks about quantity.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if any of these appear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>an ordinal date such as <strong>deuxième avril</strong> for an ordinary date;</li>\n<li>missing hyphens in numbers such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt-quatre</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est</code> for basic clock time;</li>\n<li>a plural regular -ir form without <strong>-iss-</strong>, such as <strong>nous choisons</strong>;</li>\n<li>a question without the required space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 4 lessons: numbers, dates, time, and regular -ir verbs this chapter gives you the language you need for real-world details: prices, schedules, birthdays, addresses, appointments, and everyday choices. you will also learn the regular `-ir` verb pattern, which opens the door to many useful verbs of finishing, choosing, succeeding, and reacting. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. say and recognize french numbers from `0` through `100+`; 2. form useful larger numbers for years, prices, room numbers, and quantities; 3. use common ordinal numbers such as `premier`, `deuxième`, and `troisième`; 4. say dates naturally, including the special pattern for the first day of the month; 5. tell time with `il est...`, including `midi`, `minuit`, quarter hours, and 24-hour time; 6. conjugate regular present-tense `-ir` verbs in all six persons; 7. combine numbers, time, and `-ir` verbs in practical speech and writing. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can still: - use chapter 3 time expressions such as `le lundi`, `en septembre`, and `ce soir`; - recognize the three basic question patterns from chapter 3; - conjugate regular present-tense `-er` verbs; - use `être` and `avoir` from chapter 2 for identity, age, and simple descriptions. that background matters because this chapter adds new vocabulary and a new verb family, but it still depends on earlier sentence structure. test the old skills before you add the new ones. answer these five without notes, then check. 1. how do you say that something happens every monday? — `le lundi, je travaille.` the pattern **le plus a day** means a repeated habit. 2. ask a yes/no question from `tu travailles demain.` — `tu travailles demain ?` or `est-ce que tu travailles demain ?` french does not add an english-style helper verb. 3. conjugate `étudier` with `nous`. — `nous étudions`. regular -er verbs use **-ons** with `nous`, and `je` becomes `j'` before a vowel sound. 4. say that you are twenty years old. — `j'ai vingt ans.` french uses `avoir` for age, not `être`. 5. give `être` for `elles`. — `elles sont`. this form is irregular, so you must know it by memory. if you missed two or more, review chapters 2 and 3 first. numbers and regular -ir verbs will feel much harder if the subject, question pattern, or basic verb form is still unstable. ## how to study this chapter say every number aloud instead of only reading it silently. for time and verb tables, cover one column, reproduce it from memory, then use the practice blocks to force quick recall. ## unit 1: cardinal numbers from 0 to 69 ### core idea french numbers follow clear patterns, but you must know the first group very well before the larger patterns feel automatic. ### numbers 0-20 | number | french | number | french | |---|---|---|---| | `0` | `zéro` | `11` | `onze` | | `1` | `un` | `12` | `douze` | | `2` | `deux` | `13` | `treize` | | `3` | `trois` | `14` | `quatorze` | | `4` | `quatre` | `15` | `quinze` | | `5` | `cinq` | `16` | `seize` | | `6` | `six` | `17` | `dix-sept` | | `7` | `sept` | `18` | `dix-huit` | | `8` | `huit` | `19` | `dix-neuf` | | `9` | `neuf` | `20` | `vingt` | | `10` | `dix` | | | ### tens and building patterns through 69 | number | french | number | french | |---|---|---|---| | `21` | `vingt et un` | `40` | `quarante` | | `22` | `vingt-deux` | `50` | `cinquante` | | `30` | `trente` | `60` | `soixante` | | `31` | `trente et un` | `61` | `soixante et un` | | `35` | `trente-cinq` | `68` | `soixante-huit` | ### core patterns to notice - `17-19` combine `dix-` with another number: `dix-sept`, `dix-huit`, `dix-neuf`. - `21`, `31`, `41`, `51`, and `61` use `et un`: `vingt et un`, `trente et un`. - most other numbers in these ranges use a hyphen: `vingt-deux`, `trente-neuf`, `cinquante-sept`. ### examples - `j'ai deux billets.` — i have two tickets. - `nous cherchons vingt-deux chaises.` — we are looking for twenty-two chairs. - `le café coûte cinq euros.` — the coffee costs five euros. - `ils habitent au trente-et-un, rue victor-hugo.` — they live at 31 victor hugo street. - `elle travaille avec soixante clients.` — she works with sixty clients. ### pronunciation note the final consonants in `cinq`, `six`, and `dix` change depending on what follows. in fast speech, listen carefully to the number as a whole chunk rather than trying to sound out each letter separately. ### common mistake for english speakers do not forget the hyphen in written compound numbers like `vingt-deux` and `quarante-neuf`. also watch `vingt et un`: the `et` appears with `un`, but not with `deux`, `trois`, and the others. ### sentence-building practice say these aloud in french, then write them: 1. 14 books 2. 21 students 3. 36 euros 4. 58 minutes then invent two more number phrases from your own life. 1. `quatorze livres` — fourteen is `quatorze`; the noun is plural because there is more than one. 2. `vingt et un étudiants` — numbers ending in one use `et un` through this range. the noun is plural in normal counting. 3. `trente-six euros` — most compound numbers use a hyphen, not `et`. 4. `cinquante-huit minutes` — use a hyphen between the tens and ones. two personal examples: - `deux cafés` — the noun is plural with more than one item. - `vingt minutes` — the final **t** of `vingt` is normally silent when it stands before a consonant. ## unit 2: numbers from 70 upward, and bigger numbers ### core idea french keeps building numbers rather than starting brand-new words at `70` and `90`, so pattern recognition matters. ### key numbers from 70 to 100+ | number | french | number | french | |---|---|---|---| | `70` | `soixante-dix` | `90` | `quatre-vingt-dix` | | `71` | `soixante et onze` | `91` | `quatre-vingt-onze` | | `72` | `soixante-douze` | `95` | `quatre-vingt-quinze` | | `80` | `quatre-vingts` | `100` | `cent` | | `81` | `quatre-vingt-un` | `101` | `cent un` | | `88` | `quatre-vingt-huit` | `200` | `deux cents` | | `99` | `quatre-vingt-dix-neuf` | `1 000` | `mille` | ### useful large-number reminders | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---| | `cent` + number | `cent trois` | one hundred three | | plural `cents` when nothing follows | `deux cents` | two hundred | | no `s` when another number follows | `deux cent cinq` | two hundred five | | `mille` never takes `s` | `trois mille` | three thousand | | modern years often begin with `deux mille...` | `deux mille vingt-six` | 2026 | ### examples - `le voyage coûte quatre-vingt-dix euros.` — the trip costs ninety euros. - `nous invitons soixante-quinze personnes.` — we are inviting seventy-five people. - `le bureau est au cent un, avenue centrale.` — the office is at 101 central avenue. - `mon code est mille deux.` — my code is 1002. - `le cours compte cent étudiants.` — the course has one hundred students. ### pronunciation note `quatre-vingts` has an `s` only when it stands alone as `80`. the `s` disappears in numbers like `quatre-vingt-un` and `quatre-vingt-dix`. ### common mistake for english speakers english speakers often want a neat single word for `70` and `90`. standard french instead uses `soixante-dix` and `quatre-vingt-dix`, so train yourself to hear the pattern instead of fighting it. ### sentence-building practice write these in french: `71`, `84`, `99`, `105`, `230`, `2,026`. then say them aloud without looking. 1. `soixante et onze` — seventy-one is built as sixty and eleven, and this form keeps `et`. 2. `quatre-vingt-quatre` — after eighty, the final **s** of `quatre-vingts` disappears because another number follows. 3. `quatre-vingt-dix-neuf` — ninety-nine is literally four-twenty-ten-nine. keep the hyphens. 4. `cent cinq` — `cent` takes no **s** because another number follows. 5. `deux cent trente` — `cent` again has no **s** because `trente` follows. 6. `deux mille vingt-six` — `mille` never takes **s**. the common trap is writing the plural **s** from `quatre-vingts` or `deux cents` even when another number follows. it disappears before a following number. ## unit 3: ordinal numbers, dates, and years ### core idea cardinal numbers count (`un`, `deux`, `trois`), while ordinal numbers place things in order (`premier`, `deuxième`, `troisième`). dates use both systems, but mostly cardinal numbers. ### common ordinal numbers | french | english | |---|---| | `premier / première` | first | | `deuxième` or `second / seconde` | second | | `troisième` | third | | `quatrième` | fourth | | `cinquième` | fifth | | `sixième` | sixth | | `septième` | seventh | | `huitième` | eighth | | `neuvième` | ninth | | `dixième` | tenth | ### dates in french | structure | example | english | |---|---|---| | `le + cardinal + month` | `le quinze septembre` | september fifteenth | | special form for the first | `le premier novembre` | november first | | full date with year | `le 14 juillet 2027` / `le quatorze juillet deux mille vingt-sept` | july 14, 2027 | | `en + year` for \"in\" a year | `en 2027` | in 2027 | ### examples - `mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.` — my birthday is november first. - `la réunion est le vingt-trois avril.` — the meeting is april twenty-third. - `nous partons en deux mille vingt-sept.` — we are leaving in 2027. - `le troisième chapitre est plus long.` — the third chapter is longer. - `elle habite au deuxième étage.` — she lives on the second floor. ### pronunciation note for ordinary dates, only the first day of the month uses `premier`. every other date uses a cardinal number: `le deux`, `le douze`, `le vingt-huit`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not automatically use ordinal numbers for every date. french says `le cinq mai`, not the exact equivalent of \"the fifth of may\" in basic modern usage. the big exception is `le premier`. ### sentence-building practice answer in full french phrases: 1. what is your birthday? 2. what month is a major holiday for you? 3. what floor do you live or work on? 4. what year is it now? answers vary for personal details. model full phrases: 1. birthday: `mon anniversaire est le premier mai.` — use `premier` only for the first day of the month. 2. major holiday month: `une fête importante est en décembre.` — months take `en` and stay lowercase. 3. floor: `j'habite au deuxième étage.` — ordinal numbers place things in order, so use `deuxième` for second. 4. current year: `nous sommes en deux mille vingt-six.` — years use `en`; `mille` does not take **s**. for your own birthday, use `le premier` only if the date is the first. for every other day, use the cardinal number, as in `le deux avril` or `le vingt-trois juin`. ## unit 4: telling time ### core idea use `il est...` to tell the time. french can use either a 12-hour style with context or a 24-hour style, especially in schedules and transportation. ### basic time patterns | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---| | `il est + number + heure(s)` | `il est trois heures.` | it is three o'clock. | | `il est + hour + minute` | `il est neuf heures quinze.` | it is 9:15. | | `il est midi.` | `il est midi.` | it is noon. | | `il est minuit.` | `il est minuit.` | it is midnight. | | quarter past | `il est midi et quart.` | it is 12:15. | | half past | `il est deux heures et demie.` | it is 2:30. | | quarter to | `il est cinq heures moins le quart.` | it is 4:45. | ### 12-hour and 24-hour examples - `il est huit heures du matin.` — it is 8:00 a.m. - `il est deux heures de l'après-midi.` — it is 2:00 p.m. - `il est dix-neuf heures trente.` — it is 7:30 p.m. - `le cours commence à neuf heures quinze.` — the class starts at 9:15. - `nous finissons à midi.` — we finish at noon. - `le train arrive à vingt et une heures.` — the train arrives at 9:00 p.m. ### pronunciation note use singular `heure` only for `une heure`; use plural `heures` for everything else. in everyday speech, `midi` and `minuit` replace longer forms. ### common mistake for english speakers do not say `est` with a subject other than the fixed expression `il`. clock time uses `il est...`, not `c'est...` for this core pattern. also remember that `et demie` agrees with the understood feminine noun `heure`: `deux heures et demie`. ### sentence-building practice say and write these times in french: 1. 7:00 2. 9:15 3. 12:00 4. 12:15 5. 4:45 6. 19:30 then write one sentence with `à` to say when an event starts. 1. `il est sept heures.` — clock time uses the fixed expression `il est`. 2. `il est neuf heures quinze.` — put the minutes directly after `heures`. 3. `il est midi.` — noon has the special word `midi`, not a longer twelve-hour phrase. 4. `il est midi et quart.` — quarter past uses `et quart`. 5. `il est cinq heures moins le quart.` — this means a quarter to five, so it is 4:45. 6. `il est dix-neuf heures trente.` — 24-hour time is common for schedules. event sentence: `le cours commence à neuf heures quinze.` — use `à` to say at what time something starts. do not replace `il est` with `c'est` for basic clock time. ## unit 5: regular -ir verbs in the present tense ### core idea a major group of regular `-ir` verbs follows the pattern of `finir`. these verbs add `-iss-` in the plural forms. ### model conjugation: `finir` | subject | form | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `finis` | i finish | | `tu` | `finis` | you finish | | `il / elle / on` | `finit` | he/she/one finishes | | `nous` | `finissons` | we finish | | `vous` | `finissez` | you finish | | `ils / elles` | `finissent` | they finish | ### more common regular `-ir` verbs | infinitive | meaning | |---|---| | `choisir` | to choose | | `réussir` | to succeed | | `réfléchir` | to think, reflect | | `grandir` | to grow | | `obéir` | to obey | | `rougir` | to blush, turn red | ### examples - `je finis tôt le jeudi.` — i finish early on thursdays. - `nous choisissons deux desserts pour huit euros.` — we choose two desserts for eight euros. - `elle réussit souvent ses examens.` — she often succeeds on her exams. - `vous réfléchissez trop longtemps.` — you think for too long. - `ils grandissent vite.` — they are growing quickly. - `on obéit aux règles du musée.` — people obey the museum rules. ### pronunciation note the plural forms are easy to hear because of `-issons`, `-issez`, and `-issent`. say them aloud as a family: `finissons`, `finissez`, `finissent`. ### common mistake for english speakers not every verb ending in `-ir` is regular like `finir`. verbs such as `partir` and `dormir` follow a different pattern, so do not assume every `-ir` verb works the same way. ### sentence-building practice conjugate `finir`, `choisir`, and `réussir` for all six persons. then write three original sentences using three different verbs from the table. all three verbs are regular in this pattern: remove **-ir**, then add **-is**, **-is**, **-it**, **-issons**, **-issez**, **-issent**. | subject | `finir` | `choisir` | `réussir` | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `je finis` | `je choisis` | `je réussis` | | `tu` | `tu finis` | `tu choisis` | `tu réussis` | | `il / elle / on` | `il finit` | `elle choisit` | `on réussit` | | `nous` | `nous finissons` | `nous choisissons` | `nous réussissons` | | `vous` | `vous finissez` | `vous choisissez` | `vous réussissez` | | `ils / elles` | `ils finissent` | `elles choisissent` | `ils réussissent` | original sentence models: - `je finis le travail à dix-huit heures.` — `je` takes `finis`. - `nous choisissons deux desserts.` — `nous` takes the audible **-issons** form. - `elle réussit son examen en mai.` — `elle` takes `réussit`, ending in silent **-t**. the main trap is forgetting the **-iss-** in the plural forms. ## unit 6: combining numbers, time, and -ir verbs ### core idea this chapter becomes truly useful when you combine its pieces: a time, a quantity, and a regular `-ir` verb inside one sentence. ### examples in practical contexts - `le cours commence à neuf heures quinze, et nous finissons à midi.` — the class starts at 9:15, and we finish at noon. - `mon anniversaire est le premier novembre, alors je choisis souvent un bon restaurant.` — my birthday is november first, so i often choose a good restaurant. - `les magasins finissent leur journée à dix-neuf heures.` — the stores finish their day at 7 p.m. - `vous réfléchissez pendant cinq minutes avant de répondre.` — you think for five minutes before answering. - `nous réussissons mieux quand nous étudions le matin.` — we do better when we study in the morning. ### pronunciation or listening note in a long sentence, the quantity and time expressions often carry the most audible stress. practice reading them as smooth groups: `à neuf heures quinze`, `pour huit euros`, `le premier novembre`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not let the number expression distract you from the verb ending. in `nous choisissons deux desserts`, the subject `nous` still controls the verb, no matter how long the object phrase becomes. ### sentence-building practice build one sentence from each set: 1. `nous / choisir / trois cafés / à dix heures` 2. `je / finir / le travail / à dix-huit heures` 3. `elle / réussir / l'examen / en mai` 4. `vous / réfléchir / cinq minutes / avant la réponse` 1. `nous choisissons trois cafés à dix heures.` — `nous` requires `choisissons`; the object phrase does not affect the verb ending. 2. `je finis le travail à dix-huit heures.` — `je` takes `finis`, and `à` introduces the clock time. 3. `elle réussit l'examen en mai.` — `elle` takes `réussit`; use `en` before a month. 4. `vous réfléchissez cinq minutes avant la réponse.` — `vous` takes `réfléchissez`. the time quantity `cinq minutes` does not change the verb. check subject before details. long number and time phrases can distract you, but the subject still controls the conjugated verb. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `le cours commence à neuf heures quinze.` — the class starts at 9:15. 2. `mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.` — my birthday is november first. 3. `nous choisissons deux desserts pour huit euros.` — we choose two desserts for eight euros. 4. `les magasins finissent leur journée à dix-neuf heures.` — the stores finish their day at 7 p.m. 5. `en deux mille vingt-six, je réussis enfin mon objectif.` — in 2026, i finally achieve my goal. 6. `à quelle heure finissez-vous le vendredi ?` — what time do you finish on fridays? the sentences are visible, so use this block as a correction checklist after you write them from memory. - **number spelling and hyphens.** sentence 5 has `deux mille vingt-six`, and sentence 4 has `dix-neuf heures`. do not drop the hyphen inside `dix-neuf`. - **special date form.** sentence 2 uses `le premier novembre` because the day is the first. for any other day, you would use a cardinal number. - **clock-time wording.** sentence 1 has `neuf heures quinze`, and sentence 4 has `dix-neuf heures`. use `heures` for clock time, not an english-style colon in the written sentence. - **regular -ir endings.** sentence 3 `choisissons`, sentence 4 `finissent`, sentence 5 `réussis`, and sentence 6 `finissez` must match their subjects. - **question formatting.** sentence 6 uses inversion in `finissez-vous` and has a space before `?`. score the content and the spelling separately. if the meaning is right but an ending or hyphen is wrong, rewrite that one sentence from memory again. ### notice and mark 1. circle the number or date expression in each sentence. 2. underline the conjugated verb. 3. box the time phrase if there is one. 4. say whether each sentence is about a date, a clock time, a quantity, or a combination. | # | number or date expression | conjugated verb | time phrase | type | |---|---|---|---|---| | 1 | `neuf heures quinze` | `commence` | `à neuf heures quinze` | clock time | | 2 | `le premier novembre` | `est` | — | date | | 3 | `deux desserts`, `huit euros` | `choisissons` | — | quantity and price | | 4 | `dix-neuf heures` | `finissent` | `à dix-neuf heures` | clock time | | 5 | `deux mille vingt-six` | `réussis` | `en deux mille vingt-six` | year/date expression | | 6 | — | `finissez` | `le vendredi` | repeated day/time expression | the rule behind the table is simple: number phrases can describe dates, clock times, prices, or quantities. do not let the number phrase distract you from the conjugated verb. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: first change the subject, then change one detail such as the time, price, date, number, or object. make every necessary change to the verb ending and article. one worked set is below. your details may differ, but the verb ending must follow the new subject. 1. subject: `les cours commencent à neuf heures quinze.` detail: `le cours commence à dix heures trente.` 2. subject: `nos anniversaires sont le premier novembre.` detail: `mon anniversaire est le quinze juin.` 3. subject: `je choisis deux desserts pour huit euros.` detail: `nous choisissons trois cafés pour neuf euros.` 4. subject: `le magasin finit sa journée à dix-neuf heures.` detail: `les magasins finissent leur journée à vingt heures.` watch the regular -ir forms especially: `je choisis`, `nous choisissons`, `le magasin finit`, and `les magasins finissent`. the price, time, or object never controls the verb; the subject does. ### make it personal write four true sentences about your own life using these chapter structures: one date, one clock time, one price or quantity, and one regular `-ir` verb sentence. answers vary. a model set: 1. date: `mon anniversaire est le vingt avril.` — use a cardinal number because it is not the first of the month. 2. clock time: `je commence le travail à neuf heures.` — use `à` to say when an event starts. 3. price or quantity: `j'achète deux cafés pour six euros.` — the number phrase describes the object and price. 4. regular -ir verb: `je choisis un livre le samedi.` — `je` takes `choisis`. mark yourself wrong if your date uses an ordinal after the first, if your time sentence uses `c'est` for basic clock time, or if your regular -ir verb is missing the **-iss-** sound in a plural form such as `nous choisissons`. ## mini-dialogue: choosing dessert after class **aïcha :** `il est quelle heure ?` — what time is it? **lucas :** `il est midi vingt, et le cours finit enfin.` — it is 12:20, and the class is finally ending. **aïcha :** `tu choisis quoi pour le déjeuner ?` — what are you choosing for lunch? **lucas :** `je choisis une soupe et deux tartes pour onze euros.` — i am choosing a soup and two tarts for eleven euros. **aïcha :** `tu réussis toujours à manger vite avant le travail ?` — do you always manage to eat quickly before work? **lucas :** `pas toujours, mais aujourd'hui je finis tôt.` — not always, but today i finish early. **aïcha :** `ton anniversaire, c'est bien le premier novembre ?` — your birthday really is november first, right? **lucas :** `oui, et cette année, je choisis un grand dîner avec huit amis.` — yes, and this year i am choosing a big dinner with eight friends. ### guided analysis 1. find two regular `-ir` verbs and identify their subjects. 2. list every number expression. 3. find one clock time and one date. 4. rewrite lucas's second line with `nous` instead of `je`. 5. change `le premier novembre` to a different date of your choice. 1. two regular -ir verbs in the dialogue include `finit` with subject `le cours`, `choisis` with subjects `tu` and `je`, `réussis` with subject `tu`, and `finis` with subject `je`. `je choisis` and `tu choisis` look the same because both singular forms end in **-is**. 2. number expressions: `midi vingt`, `deux tartes`, `onze euros`, `le premier novembre`, and `huit amis`. `cette année` is a time expression, but it is not a number expression. 3. clock time: `il est midi vingt`. date: `le premier novembre`. the date uses `premier` because it is the first day of the month. 4. `nous choisissons une soupe et deux tartes pour onze euros.` — `nous` requires the regular -ir ending **-issons**. 5. a different date: `le quinze septembre`. use a cardinal number for ordinary dates; only the first uses `premier`. ## connected reading: un samedi bien organisé `le samedi, je commence lentement, mais à dix heures je finis déjà mes courses du marché. j'achète souvent trois légumes, deux fromages et un dessert pour environ vingt euros. à midi, je déjeune avec mon frère, puis nous choisissons un film pour l'après-midi. si le temps est mauvais, nous réfléchissons à la maison; si le soleil revient, nous sortons. mon père téléphone parfois vers dix-sept heures parce qu'il veut savoir si je réussis ma recette. le soir, tout le monde arrive à la même heure, et le dîner commence vers vingt heures.` — on saturdays, i start slowly, but by ten o'clock i have already finished my market shopping. i often buy three vegetables, two cheeses, and a dessert for about twenty euros. at noon, i have lunch with my brother, then we choose a movie for the afternoon. if the weather is bad, we think things over at home; if the sun comes back, we go out. my father sometimes calls around five o'clock because he wants to know whether i succeed with my recipe. in the evening, everyone arrives at the same time, and dinner starts around eight o'clock. ### reading practice 1. list every number or time expression in the paragraph. 2. find four present-tense verbs and sort them into `-er` or regular `-ir`. 3. rewrite the second sentence with different quantities and a different price. 4. find the sentence that contains a yes/no idea indirectly and explain it. 5. write three new schedule sentences about your own saturday. 1. number and time expressions: `le samedi`, `à dix heures`, `trois légumes`, `deux fromages`, `un dessert`, `vingt euros`, `à midi`, `l'après-midi`, `vers dix-sept heures`, `le soir`, `à la même heure`, and `vers vingt heures`. 2. present-tense verbs sorted by type: regular -er verbs include `commence`, `achète`, `déjeune`, `téléphone`, `arrive`, and `commence`; regular -ir verbs include `finis`, `choisissons`, `réfléchissons`, and `réussis`. the verb `sortons` is an -ir verb, but it is not regular like `finir`. 3. rewritten second sentence: `j'achète souvent quatre pommes, trois fromages et deux desserts pour environ trente euros.` — the quantities and price changed, but `j'achète` still agrees with `je`. 4. the indirect yes/no idea is in `il veut savoir si je réussis ma recette`. the word `si` means **whether** here; it embeds the question “do i succeed with my recipe?” without using a question mark. 5. answers vary. models: `le samedi, je finis mes courses à onze heures.` `à midi, je choisis un sandwich.` `le soir, je réussis souvent une recette simple.` check that each schedule sentence has a time expression and that every regular -ir verb matches its subject. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. forgetting the hyphens in compound numbers such as `quarante-deux` and `quatre-vingt-dix-neuf`. 2. writing `quatre-vingts-un` with an `s`; the `s` disappears when another number follows. 3. using ordinal numbers for every date instead of only `le premier`. 4. forgetting `il est...` for clock time. 5. mixing up `midi` and `minuit`. 6. assuming every `-ir` verb is regular like `finir`. 7. forgetting the plural `-iss-` pattern in `nous`, `vous`, and `ils/elles` forms. ## chapter reference sheet ### essential number patterns | range or form | pattern | example | |---|---|---| | `0-16` | memorize individually | `zéro`, `seize` | | `17-19` | `dix-` + number | `dix-huit` | | `21/31/41/51/61` | tens + `et un` | `vingt et un` | | `22-69` | tens + hyphen + number | `cinquante-deux` | | `70-79` | `soixante-` + `10-19` | `soixante-quatorze` | | `80` | `quatre-vingts` | `80` | | `81-99` | `quatre-vingt-` + number | `quatre-vingt-sept` | | `100+` | `cent`, `mille` patterns | `deux cent trois`, `mille` | ### dates and time at a glance | meaning | french | example | |---|---|---| | november first | `le premier novembre` | `mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.` | | april 23 | `le vingt-trois avril` | `la réunion est le vingt-trois avril.` | | in 2028 | `en deux mille vingt-huit` | `nous partons en deux mille vingt-huit.` | | it is 3:00 | `il est trois heures.` | `il est trois heures.` | | it is noon | `il est midi.` | `il est midi.` | | it is midnight | `il est minuit.` | `il est minuit.` | | it is 7:30 p.m. | `il est dix-neuf heures trente.` | `le train part à dix-neuf heures trente.` | ### regular `-ir` endings | pronoun | ending | example with `choisir` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `-is` | `je choisis` | | `tu` | `-is` | `tu choisis` | | `il / elle / on` | `-it` | `elle choisit` | | `nous` | `-issons` | `nous choisissons` | | `vous` | `-issez` | `vous choisissez` | | `ils / elles` | `-issent` | `ils choisissent` | ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a real or imagined day that includes appointments, prices, and choices. include: - at least five number expressions; - one full date and one year; - three clock times, including either `midi` or `minuit`; - at least four regular present-tense `-ir` verbs; - one question about time or quantity. after writing: 1. circle every number, date, and time phrase; 2. underline each regular `-ir` verb; 3. check whether each number is spelled and hyphenated correctly; 4. make sure your date uses `le premier` only when needed; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any sentence you corrected from memory. answers vary, so compare your paragraph to the requirements. here is one model that satisfies the brief: `le quinze avril deux mille vingt-six, je commence une journée très organisée. à huit heures, je choisis deux cafés pour six euros. à neuf heures trente, je finis un dossier important. à midi, nous choisissons trois sandwichs pour vingt-quatre euros. à quatorze heures, ma sœur réussit son examen. le soir, je réfléchis pendant quinze minutes avant de choisir un dessert. nous finissons la journée à dix-neuf heures. combien de cafés est-ce que tu choisis le matin ?` how it satisfies the brief: number expressions include `quinze`, `deux mille vingt-six`, `huit heures`, `deux cafés`, `six euros`, `neuf heures trente`, `trois sandwichs`, `vingt-quatre euros`, `quatorze heures`, `quinze minutes`, and `dix-neuf heures`; the full date is `le quinze avril deux mille vingt-six`; the clock times are `huit heures`, `neuf heures trente`, `midi`, `quatorze heures`, and `dix-neuf heures`; regular -ir verbs include `choisis`, `finis`, `choisissons`, `réussit`, `réfléchis`, and `finissons`; the final sentence asks about quantity. mark yourself wrong if any of these appear: - an ordinal date such as **deuxième avril** for an ordinary date; - missing hyphens in numbers such as `vingt-quatre`; - `c'est` instead of `il est` for basic clock time; - a plural regular -ir form without **-iss-**, such as **nous choisons**; - a question without the required space before `?`. "},{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 4 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-exercises\">Chapter 4 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>42</li>\n<li>1 May</li>\n<li>14:30</li>\n<li>nous / finir</li>\n<li>elles / choisir</li>\n<li>vous / réussir</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quarante-deux</code> — compound numbers usually use hyphens.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier mai</code> — dates use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code> only for the first day of the month.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est quatorze heures trente.</code> — clock time uses fixed <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est</code>; 14:30 is common 24-hour schedule style.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code> — regular -ir verbs use the <strong>-issons</strong> form with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles choisissent</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code> takes the <strong>-issent</strong> ending; the ending is written and the <strong>iss</strong> sound is heard.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous réussissez</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> takes the <strong>-issez</strong> ending.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These match the answer key exactly. The common traps are hyphens in numbers, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code> only on the first, and the plural <strong>-iss-</strong> forms of regular -ir verbs.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>numbers and dates</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier mai</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>time</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est huit heures.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>regular -ir verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je finis tôt.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>functional practice</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le magasin ouvre à neuf heures.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary by design. A correct answer must clearly show the named structure and preserve number spelling, time wording, verb endings, and word order.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Numbers and dates: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le deux juin</code> — for dates after the first, French uses a cardinal number, not an ordinal.</li>\n<li>Time: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est dix heures trente.</code> — basic clock time uses the fixed expression <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est</code>.</li>\n<li>Regular -ir verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons un dessert.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> requires the <strong>-issons</strong> form.</li>\n<li>Functional practice: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le train arrive à dix-neuf heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> introduces the scheduled time.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you reused the model content, wrote a digit when the task expects French words, used <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est</code> for clock time, or forgot the <strong>-iss-</strong> in a plural regular -ir form.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons deux desserts à midi.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je choisis deux desserts à midi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisissons</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisis</code> because the subject is now <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne choisissons pas deux desserts à midi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> wrap the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que nous choisissons deux desserts à midi ?</code> — this keeps statement word order after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons trois cafés à dix heures.</code> — the number, object, and time changed, but the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> verb form stayed the same.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check that changing a number or time did not distract you from the verb ending. The subject still controls the verb.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le café coûte cinq euros.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cinq</code> may sound different depending on what follows, but the written number stays the same.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le voyage coûte quatre-vingt-dix euros.</code> — ninety is built as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre-vingt-dix</code>; keep the hyphens and do not add an <strong>s</strong> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt</code> when another number follows.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.</code> — dates use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">premier</code> only for the first day of the month, and months stay lowercase.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est dix-neuf heures trente.</code> — 24-hour clock time uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heures</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-neuf</code> keeps its hyphen.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons deux desserts pour huit euros.</code> — the regular -ir plural form has the audible <strong>-issons</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En deux mille vingt-six, je réussis enfin mon objectif.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mille</code> never takes <strong>s</strong>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussis</code> has the accent on <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">é</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct in passes. A missed number hyphen, a wrong verb ending, and a missing accent are different errors, so do not count them as the same problem.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a one-day schedule with five times, three prices, and four regular -ir verb forms.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model one-day schedule:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À huit heures, je choisis un café pour trois euros.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À neuf heures quinze, je finis un exercice.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À midi, nous choisissons deux sandwichs pour douze euros.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À dix-sept heures, vous réussissez la réservation.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À vingt heures, ils finissent le dîner pour vingt euros.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-marking criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include five time expressions.</li>\n<li>Include three prices.</li>\n<li>Include four regular -ir verb forms, with correct endings for their subjects.</li>\n<li>Spell number words correctly and use hyphens in compound numbers such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dix-sept</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vingt-deux</code>.</li>\n<li>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before times when saying when something happens.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Numbers and dates.</strong> Use the French number patterns, including hyphens in compound numbers. Dates use cardinal numbers except <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier</code>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier mai</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Time.</strong> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est...</code> for clock time, with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">minuit</code> as special forms. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est quatorze heures trente.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Regular -ir verbs.</strong> For verbs like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>, remove <strong>-ir</strong> and add <strong>-is</strong>, <strong>-is</strong>, <strong>-it</strong>, <strong>-issons</strong>, <strong>-issez</strong>, <strong>-issent</strong>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous finissons tôt.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Functional practice.</strong> Combine quantities, prices, times, and verbs in real situations. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je choisis deux cafés pour huit euros.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If your explanation is correct but your French example has a wrong ending or missing hyphen, count the item as not mastered yet.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 4 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. 42 2. 1 may 3. 14:30 4. nous / finir 5. elles / choisir 6. vous / réussir 1. `quarante-deux` — compound numbers usually use hyphens. 2. `le premier mai` — dates use `premier` only for the first day of the month. 3. `il est quatorze heures trente.` — clock time uses fixed `il est`; 14:30 is common 24-hour schedule style. 4. `nous finissons` — regular -ir verbs use the **-issons** form with `nous`. 5. `elles choisissent` — `elles` takes the **-issent** ending; the ending is written and the **iss** sound is heard. 6. `vous réussissez` — `vous` takes the **-issez** ending. these match the answer key exactly. the common traps are hyphens in numbers, `premier` only on the first, and the plural **-iss-** forms of regular -ir verbs. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **numbers and dates**. use `le premier mai` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **time**. use `il est huit heures.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **regular -ir verbs**. use `je finis tôt.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **functional practice**. use `le magasin ouvre à neuf heures.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary by design. a correct answer must clearly show the named structure and preserve number spelling, time wording, verb endings, and word order. sample answers: 1. numbers and dates: `le deux juin` — for dates after the first, french uses a cardinal number, not an ordinal. 2. time: `il est dix heures trente.` — basic clock time uses the fixed expression `il est`. 3. regular -ir verbs: `nous choisissons un dessert.` — `nous` requires the **-issons** form. 4. functional practice: `le train arrive à dix-neuf heures.` — `à` introduces the scheduled time. mark yourself wrong if you reused the model content, wrote a digit when the task expects french words, used `c'est` for clock time, or forgot the **-iss-** in a plural regular -ir form. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `nous choisissons deux desserts à midi.` 1. change the subject: `je choisis deux desserts à midi.` — `choisissons` changes to `choisis` because the subject is now `je`. 2. make it negative: `nous ne choisissons pas deux desserts à midi.` — `ne` and `pas` wrap the conjugated verb. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que nous choisissons deux desserts à midi ?` — this keeps statement word order after `est-ce que`. 4. change one meaningful detail: `nous choisissons trois cafés à dix heures.` — the number, object, and time changed, but the `nous` verb form stayed the same. check that changing a number or time did not distract you from the verb ending. the subject still controls the verb. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `le café coûte cinq euros.` — `cinq` may sound different depending on what follows, but the written number stays the same. 2. `le voyage coûte quatre-vingt-dix euros.` — ninety is built as `quatre-vingt-dix`; keep the hyphens and do not add an **s** to `vingt` when another number follows. 3. `mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.` — dates use `premier` only for the first day of the month, and months stay lowercase. 4. `il est dix-neuf heures trente.` — 24-hour clock time uses `heures`; `dix-neuf` keeps its hyphen. 5. `nous choisissons deux desserts pour huit euros.` — the regular -ir plural form has the audible **-issons**. 6. `en deux mille vingt-six, je réussis enfin mon objectif.` — `mille` never takes **s**, and `réussis` has the accent on `é`. correct in passes. a missed number hyphen, a wrong verb ending, and a missing accent are different errors, so do not count them as the same problem. ## e. personal writing write a one-day schedule with five times, three prices, and four regular -ir verb forms. answers vary. a model one-day schedule: 1. `à huit heures, je choisis un café pour trois euros.` 2. `à neuf heures quinze, je finis un exercice.` 3. `à midi, nous choisissons deux sandwichs pour douze euros.` 4. `à dix-sept heures, vous réussissez la réservation.` 5. `à vingt heures, ils finissent le dîner pour vingt euros.` self-marking criteria: - include five time expressions. - include three prices. - include four regular -ir verb forms, with correct endings for their subjects. - spell number words correctly and use hyphens in compound numbers such as `dix-sept` or `vingt-deux`. - use `à` before times when saying when something happens. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **numbers and dates.** use the french number patterns, including hyphens in compound numbers. dates use cardinal numbers except `le premier`. example: `le premier mai`. 2. **time.** use `il est...` for clock time, with `midi` and `minuit` as special forms. example: `il est quatorze heures trente.` 3. **regular -ir verbs.** for verbs like `finir`, remove **-ir** and add **-is**, **-is**, **-it**, **-issons**, **-issez**, **-issent**. example: `nous finissons tôt.` 4. **functional practice.** combine quantities, prices, times, and verbs in real situations. example: `je choisis deux cafés pour huit euros.` if your explanation is correct but your french example has a wrong ending or missing hyphen, count the item as not mastered yet. "},{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 4 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-answer-key\">Chapter 4 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quarante-deux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier mai</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est quatorze heures trente.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles choisissent</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous réussissez</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Numbers and dates:</strong> Practice numbers in meaningful chunks: prices, addresses, years, and phone numbers. Dates use cardinal numbers except premier for the first.</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> Use Il est... for clock time. Add du matin, de l'après-midi, or du soir when useful. Midi and minuit are special forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Regular -ir verbs:</strong> For verbs like finir, remove -ir and add -is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent.</li>\n<li><strong>Functional practice:</strong> Combine quantities, time, and -ir verbs in realistic situations such as stores, meals, and appointments.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 4 answer key ## section a 1. `quarante-deux` 2. `le premier mai` 3. `il est quatorze heures trente.` 4. `nous finissons` 5. `elles choisissent` 6. `vous réussissez` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **numbers and dates:** practice numbers in meaningful chunks: prices, addresses, years, and phone numbers. dates use cardinal numbers except premier for the first. - **time:** use il est... for clock time. add du matin, de l'après-midi, or du soir when useful. midi and minuit are special forms. - **regular -ir verbs:** for verbs like finir, remove -ir and add -is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent. - **functional practice:** combine quantities, time, and -ir verbs in realistic situations such as stores, meals, and appointments. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 4 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 4 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Numbers and dates</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Time</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Regular -ir verbs</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Functional practice</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 4 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **numbers and dates**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **time** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **regular -ir verbs**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **functional practice**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":5,"slug":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes","title":"Regular -re Verbs and -er Spelling Changes","documents":[{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/README.md","title":"Chapter 5: Regular -re Verbs and -er Spelling Changes","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-regular-re-verbs-and-er-spelling-changes\">Chapter 5: Regular -re Verbs and -er Spelling Changes</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate regular -re verbs.</li>\n<li>Use depuis for an action continuing into the present.</li>\n<li>Handle common -er spelling-change patterns.</li>\n<li>Describe clothing and shopping choices.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Regular -re verbs</li>\n<li>Depuis</li>\n<li>Spelling changes</li>\n<li>Shopping language</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 5: regular -re verbs and -er spelling changes this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate regular -re verbs. - use depuis for an action continuing into the present. - handle common -er spelling-change patterns. - describe clothing and shopping choices. ## lesson sequence 1. regular -re verbs 2. depuis 3. spelling changes 4. shopping language ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 5 Lessons: Regular -re Verbs, Depuis, and Spelling-Change Verbs","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-lessons-regular-re-verbs-depuis-and-spelling-change-verbs\">Chapter 5 Lessons: Regular -re Verbs, Depuis, and Spelling-Change Verbs</h1>\n<p>This chapter adds two important ideas at the same time: a new regular verb family, the regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs, and a group of familiar-looking <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs whose spelling changes in some forms. You will also learn how French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> with the present tense to talk about actions and situations that started in the past and continue now.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>conjugate regular present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs in all six persons;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> with the present tense to express an ongoing action or state;</li>\n<li>conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-cer</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ger</code> verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manger</code>;</li>\n<li>conjugate accent-changing verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code>;</li>\n<li>conjugate double-consonant verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jeter</code>;</li>\n<li>conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>-changing verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code>;</li>\n<li>combine these patterns in realistic everyday sentences.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can still:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>conjugate regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs from Chapter 3;</li>\n<li>use chapter 4 numbers and time expressions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à dix-neuf heures</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis trois ans</code>-style time phrases in English meaning;</li>\n<li>form yes/no and information questions;</li>\n<li>recognize that the subject controls the verb ending, even in longer sentences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You will need that foundation because the new spellings in this chapter only make sense if the regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> pattern is already solid.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test yourself before starting. Answer these five without notes.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code>. Regular -er verbs take <strong>-ons</strong> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Ask a yes/no question from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous travaillez ce soir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous travaillez ce soir ?</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous travaillez ce soir ?</code> French does not add a separate word for English <strong>do</strong>.</li>\n<li>Write a clock-time phrase for 7 p.m. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à dix-neuf heures</code>. Chapter 4 schedule style often uses the 24-hour clock.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants regardent la carte</code>, what controls <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regardent</code>? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants</code>. The subject controls the verb ending, not the object <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la carte</code>.</li>\n<li>What does a phrase like “for three years and still now” require in French once you learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code>? — a present-tense verb, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis trois ans</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review the earlier verb and time chapters first. This chapter adds spelling changes; it does not replace the basic subject-verb system.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>For every table, say the six forms aloud from memory before copying them. When a spelling change appears, compare it to the regular pattern and ask yourself exactly <strong>where</strong> the change happens and where it does not.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-regular-re-verbs-in-the-present-tense\">Unit 1: Regular -re verbs in the present tense</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs form the present tense by removing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> and adding a new set of endings.</p>\n<h3 id=\"model-conjugation-attendre\">Model conjugation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code></td>\n<td>I wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code></td>\n<td>you wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attend</code></td>\n<td>he/she/one waits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendons</code></td>\n<td>we wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendez</code></td>\n<td>you wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendent</code></td>\n<td>they wait</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-ending-pattern\">The ending pattern</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>Example with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vends</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu vends</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td>no ending</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vend</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous vendons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous vendez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils vendent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"more-common-regular-re-verbs\">More common regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code></td>\n<td>to wait (for)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code></td>\n<td>to sell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre</code></td>\n<td>to answer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">perdre</code></td>\n<td>to lose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">entendre</code></td>\n<td>to hear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">descendre</code></td>\n<td>to go down, descend</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends mon ami devant le magasin.</code> — I am waiting for my friend in front of the store.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous vendons des livres le samedi.</code> — We sell books on Saturdays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle répond vite à ma question.</code> — She answers my question quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous perdez souvent vos clés ?</code> — Do you often lose your keys?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils entendent la musique dans la rue.</code> — They hear the music in the street.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In many regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs, the singular forms sound very close to one another, just as with regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs. The final written consonants are not always clearly heard, while <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> forms are easier to distinguish.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not add an extra written ending in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code> form. It is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il attend</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*il attends</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">perdre</code> for all six persons. Then write three original sentences using three different subjects.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>All three verbs are regular -re verbs: remove the final -re, then add -s, -s, no ending, -ons, -ez, -ent.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">perdre</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'attends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je réponds</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je perds</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu attends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu réponds</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu perds</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il attend</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle répond</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on perd</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous attendons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous répondons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous perdons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous attendez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous répondez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous perdez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils attendent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles répondent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils perdent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The trap is the singular spelling: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il attend</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle répond</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on perd</code> have no added ending after the stem.</p>\n<p>Sample original sentences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends le bus devant la gare.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> takes -s, and the final written s is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous répondons au professeur après le cours.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> takes -ons, so this form is easy to hear.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles perdent souvent leurs clés.</code> — -ent is written for plural agreement but is silent.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-depuis-with-the-present-tense\">Unit 2: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> with the present tense</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> + a point in time or a duration with the <strong>present tense</strong> when something started in the past and is still true now.</p>\n<h3 id=\"main-structures\">Main structures</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Natural English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>present + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> + duration</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis deux ans.</code></td>\n<td>I have lived here for two years.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>present + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> + starting point</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle attend depuis midi.</code></td>\n<td>She has been waiting since noon.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand</code> + present?</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand travaillez-vous ici ?</code></td>\n<td>How long have you worked here?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.</code> — We have lived here for three years.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'étudie le français depuis septembre.</code> — I have been studying French since September.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il attend le bus depuis vingt minutes.</code> — He has been waiting for the bus for twenty minutes.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous travaillez ensemble depuis quand ?</code> — Since when have you been working together?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle préfère ce café depuis son arrivée en ville.</code> — She has preferred this café since her arrival in the city.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Treat <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> as one common time marker. In fast speech, you will hear it very often right after the verb phrase: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'habite ici depuis...</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on attend depuis...</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>English usually uses the present perfect here: \"I have lived,\" \"she has been waiting.\" French usually keeps the ordinary present tense: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle attend</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Complete the sentences with your own ideas:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'étudie le français depuis ___ .</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis ___ .</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il attend depuis ___ .</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand est-ce que vous ___ ?</code></li>\n<p>Then ask and answer one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand... ?</code> question aloud.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model completions — yours can use different time phrases.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'étudie le français depuis septembre.</code> — The studying began in September and still continues, so French keeps the present tense.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.</code> — English says <em>have lived</em>, but French says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habitons</code> because the situation is still true now.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il attend depuis midi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> can introduce a starting point such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">midi</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand est-ce que vous travaillez ici ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis quand</code> asks when the continuing action began; the verb stays present.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Question and answer example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand est-ce que tu apprends le français ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'apprends le français depuis deux mois.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you used a past tense for an action that is still continuing, or if your answer after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> does not give either a duration or a starting point.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-cer-and-ger-verbs-to-keep-the-soft-sound\">Unit 3: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-cer</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ger</code> verbs to keep the soft sound</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Some regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs change spelling in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form so that the pronunciation stays natural.</p>\n<h3 id=\"commencer-add-a-cedilla-before-o\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code>: add a cedilla before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commences</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commençons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"manger-add-e-before-ons\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manger</code>: add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ons</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mange</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manges</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mange</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-spelling-changes\">Why the spelling changes</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb type</th>\n<th>Change</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-cer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ç</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code></td>\n<td>keep the soft <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> sound</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ger</code></td>\n<td>add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code></td>\n<td>keep the soft <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j</code> sound</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons le cours à huit heures.</code> — We start class at eight o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je commence toujours par les exercices faciles.</code> — I always start with the easy exercises.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.</code> — We eat at the restaurant before starting the shopping.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle mange tôt le soir.</code> — She eats early in the evening.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils commencent en septembre.</code> — They start in September.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>These are spelling changes, not new endings. The verbs are still regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs; the extra mark simply protects the original sound.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Learners often forget that the change usually appears only where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">o</code> would otherwise harden the sound. The most important form to memorize first is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commençons</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous mangeons</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manger</code> for all six persons. Then rewrite these with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je commence le travail.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu manges ici.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence tôt.</code></p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>These are still regular -er verbs, but the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> spelling changes to protect the sound.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manger</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je commence</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je mange</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu commences</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu manges</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il commence</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle mange</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commençons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous mangeons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous commencez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous mangez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils commencent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles mangent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rewrites with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons le travail.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> needs ç before -ons so the sound stays soft.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons ici.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manger</code> adds e before -ons so the g stays soft.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons tôt.</code> — The subject changed, so the verb must change too; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commence</code> is not possible.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-accent-changing-verbs-such-as-acheter-and-pr-f-rer\">Unit 4: Accent-changing verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Some verbs change the vowel in the stem so that the stressed syllable sounds natural. The change usually appears in all forms <strong>except</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"model-1-acheter-e\">Model 1: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">è</code>)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achètes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achetons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achetez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achètent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"model-2-pr-f-rer\">Model 2: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">é</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">è</code>)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfères</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfèrent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète du pain le matin.</code> — I buy bread in the morning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.</code> — She buys a shirt and tries on a jacket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous achetons nos billets en ligne.</code> — We buy our tickets online.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère la chemise bleue.</code> — I prefer the blue shirt.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous préférons le train.</code> — We prefer the train.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils préfèrent étudier le soir.</code> — They prefer to study in the evening.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Say the pairs aloud: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'achète / nous achetons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je préfère / nous préférons</code>. The sound changes are easier to feel than to memorize abstractly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not spread the accented form across all six persons. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous achetons</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous préférez</code> keep the simpler stem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code> for all six persons. Then replace the subject in these sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète un livre.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle préfère le café.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils achètent des fruits.</code></p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>The changed stem appears in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code>, but not in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'achète</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je préfère</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu achètes</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu préfères</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle achète</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il préfère</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous achetons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous préférons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous achetez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous préférez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils achètent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles préfèrent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Model subject replacements:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous achetons un livre.</code> — With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, the stem returns to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achet-</code>; do not write the accent here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous préférez le café.</code> — With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, keep the é in the ending pattern: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférez</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète des fruits.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound, and the stem changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achèt-</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-double-consonant-verbs-and-y-changing-verbs\">Unit 5: Double-consonant verbs and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>-changing verbs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A few high-frequency <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs keep their pronunciation by doubling a consonant or changing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i</code> in certain forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"model-1-appeler-double-consonant\">Model 1: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code> (double consonant)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelle</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelles</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelle</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appellent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Jeter</code> follows the same idea: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je jette</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous jetons</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"model-2-essayer-y-i-in-the-common-spelling-shown-here\">Model 2: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i</code> in the common spelling shown here)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaies</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaient</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"another-common-y-verb-envoyer\">Another common <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoie</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoies</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoie</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoient</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'appelle ma sœur ce soir.</code> — I am calling my sister this evening.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous appelons un taxi.</code> — We call a taxi.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle jette le ticket.</code> — She throws away the ticket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'essaie une veste noire.</code> — I am trying on a black jacket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous essayons plusieurs idées.</code> — We are trying several ideas.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'envoie un message après le cours.</code> — I send a message after class.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous envoyons les documents demain.</code> — We send the documents tomorrow.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The doubled consonant or changed vowel helps keep the stressed sound consistent. Compare <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'appelle</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous appelons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'essaie</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous essayons</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not assume the spelling change appears everywhere. As with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code>, the simplest stem usually returns in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code> for all six persons. Then write one sentence with each verb using a different subject.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>These verbs also change mainly outside the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> forms.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'appelle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'essaie</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'envoie</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu appelles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu essaies</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu envoies</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle appelle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il essaie</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on envoie</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous appelons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous essayons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous envoyons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous appelez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous essayez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous envoyez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils appellent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles essaient</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils envoient</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Sample sentences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'appelle ma mère ce soir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code> doubles the l in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous essayons une autre solution.</code> — With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, keep y in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils envoient un message au professeur.</code> — The y changes to i in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils envoient</code>, and -ent is silent.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-6-putting-the-chapter-patterns-together\">Unit 6: Putting the chapter patterns together</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>This chapter becomes useful when you can move quickly from one pattern to another without losing track of the subject.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-practical-situations\">Examples in practical situations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends mon ami devant le magasin depuis midi.</code> — I have been waiting for my friend in front of the store since noon.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.</code> — We have lived here for three years.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.</code> — She buys a shirt and tries on a jacket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.</code> — We eat at the restaurant before starting the shopping.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère ce quartier, mais j'appelle souvent mes parents parce qu'ils habitent loin.</code> — I prefer this neighborhood, but I often call my parents because they live far away.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous répondez vite, puis vous envoyez le message.</code> — You answer quickly, then you send the message.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-or-listening-note\">Pronunciation or listening note</h3>\n<p>When several verbs appear in the same sentence, pause mentally at each subject change. That habit keeps endings accurate even when the sentence becomes long.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not let the more unusual spelling changes distract you from the basic sentence frame. First identify the subject, then choose the verb family, then apply the spelling change only if needed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Build one sentence from each set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je / attendre / le bus / depuis vingt minutes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous / commencer / la réunion / à neuf heures</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle / préférer / cette robe / et / essayer / l'autre</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous / répondre / au professeur / puis / envoyer / le devoir</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends le bus depuis vingt minutes.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code> is a regular -re verb, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> takes the present because the waiting is still happening.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons la réunion à neuf heures.</code> — The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> needs ç before -ons.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle préfère cette robe et essaie l'autre.</code> — Both verbs agree with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code> changes é to è, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code> changes y to i in this common spelling.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous répondez au professeur, puis vous envoyez le devoir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre</code> takes the -ez ending with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code> keeps y in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If your sentence has two verbs, check both. A correct first verb does not protect the second one from agreement or spelling-change errors.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends mon ami devant le magasin.</code> — I am waiting for my friend in front of the store.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.</code> — We have lived here for three years.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.</code> — She buys a shirt and tries on a jacket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.</code> — We eat at the restaurant before starting the shopping.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère ce livre, mais j'appelle ma sœur pour demander son avis.</code> — I prefer this book, but I call my sister to ask for her opinion.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous répondez depuis votre bureau ou vous envoyez un message plus tard ?</code> — Are you answering from your office, or are you sending a message later?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>After writing the six sentences from memory, check for these traps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Silent singular endings.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends</code> has a written <strong>s</strong>, but you do not hear it clearly.</li>\n<li><strong>Present tense with ongoing time.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans</code> uses the present because the living situation still continues.</li>\n<li><strong>Spelling-change stems.</strong> Sentence 3 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code>; sentence 5 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelle</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Nous protection spellings.</strong> Sentence 4 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code>, with an extra <strong>e</strong> to keep the soft sound before <strong>-ons</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Question spacing and accents.</strong> Sentence 6 needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyez</code>, and a space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you got the sound right but missed an accent or stem change, rewrite that exact form three times and then use it in a new sentence.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Underline the subject of each sentence.</li>\n<li>Circle the conjugated verb or verbs.</li>\n<li>Box any spelling change.</li>\n<li>Mark the sentence that uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> and explain why the present tense appears there.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Subject or subjects</th>\n<th>Conjugated verb or verbs</th>\n<th>Spelling change</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code>?</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code></td>\n<td>none; regular -re</td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habitons</code></td>\n<td>none; regular -er</td>\n<td>yes, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis trois ans</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code></td>\n<td>accent change; y changes to i</td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code></td>\n<td>extra <strong>e</strong> before <strong>-ons</strong></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelle</code></td>\n<td>accent change; doubled <strong>l</strong></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyez</code> keeps <strong>y</strong> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td>yes, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis votre bureau</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The clearest continuing-time sentence is sentence 2: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans</code>. French uses present tense because the situation began earlier and is still true now. Sentence 6 also contains <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code>, but there it means “from your office,” not “for/since a length of time.”</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: first change the subject, then change one important detail such as the place, object, or time expression. Make every required spelling and agreement change.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>One worked set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous attendons notre ami devant le magasin.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends mon frère devant la gare.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle habite ici depuis trois ans.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis six mois.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous achetons une chemise et essayons une veste.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète un manteau et essaie une écharpe.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je mange au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons au café avant de commencer le travail.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The important lesson is that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> often removes the visible stem change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achetons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code>. If you change the subject but leave the old spelling, the sentence is wrong even if the meaning is clear.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four true sentences about your own life: one with a regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verb, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code>, one with a spelling-change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb, and one sentence with two chapter verbs together.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model check for the cumulative practice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends mon ami devant le magasin.</code> — Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>; verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code>. This is a regular -re form with -s, even though the final s is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.</code> — Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>; verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habitons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis trois ans</code> explains why French uses the present for a continuing situation.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.</code> — Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>; verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code>. Both show spelling changes in the stem.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.</code> — Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>; verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code>. The extra e keeps the g soft before -ons.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère ce livre, mais j'appelle ma sœur pour demander son avis.</code> — Subjects: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code>; verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelle</code>. Watch both the accent change and the doubled l.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous répondez depuis votre bureau ou vous envoyez un message plus tard ?</code> — Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>; verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondez</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyez</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code> keeps y in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the rewrite task, answers vary. If you change sentence 3 to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous achetons une chemise et essayons une veste.</code>, both spelling changes disappear because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> uses the simpler stem. If you change only a detail, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une écharpe et essaie un manteau.</code>, the verb forms stay the same.</p>\n<p>For your personal sentences, mark yourself wrong if the -re verb ending does not match the subject, if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> is followed by a past-tense idea for something still true now, or if a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form keeps a spelling change it should drop.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-shopping-and-waiting\">Mini-dialogue: shopping and waiting</h2>\n<p><strong>Mina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu attends depuis longtemps ?</code> — Have you been waiting a long time?</p>\n<p><strong>Paul :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, j'attends depuis midi, mais je commence juste à me détendre.</code> — Yes, I have been waiting since noon, but I am just starting to relax.</p>\n<p><strong>Mina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi ?</code> — Why?</p>\n<p><strong>Paul :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parce que ma sœur essaie trois vestes et elle préfère toujours une autre couleur.</code> — Because my sister is trying on three jackets, and she always prefers another color.</p>\n<p><strong>Mina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et toi, tu achètes quelque chose ?</code> — And you, are you buying anything?</p>\n<p><strong>Paul :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, j'achète un cadeau, puis j'appelle mon frère pour demander son avis.</code> — Yes, I am buying a gift, then I am calling my brother to ask for his opinion.</p>\n<p><strong>Mina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous mangez ici après les achats ?</code> — Are you eating here after the shopping?</p>\n<p><strong>Paul :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, nous mangeons au café d'en face avant d'envoyer les photos à la famille.</code> — Yes, we are eating at the café across the street before sending the photos to the family.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> and explain the time meaning.</li>\n<li>List three spelling-change verbs and name their pattern.</li>\n<li>Find one regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verb and identify its subject.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle préfère toujours une autre couleur</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Change the dialogue so that the waiting starts <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis dix minutes</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis midi</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>The dialogue uses continuing-time <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu attends depuis longtemps ?</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'attends depuis midi</code>. The waiting started earlier and is still happening, so French keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code> in the present tense.</li>\n<li>Three spelling-change verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code> belongs to the -cer family, though this form does not need ç; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code> changes y to i; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code> changes é to è; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achètes</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code> change e to è; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelle</code> doubles <strong>l</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code> adds <strong>e</strong> before <strong>-ons</strong>.</li>\n<li>A regular -re verb is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code>. Its subject is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu attends depuis longtemps ?</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'attends depuis midi</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous préférons toujours une autre couleur.</code> — With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code> keeps the simpler stem and takes <strong>-ons</strong>.</li>\n<li>Revised waiting lines: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu attends depuis dix minutes ?</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, j'attends depuis dix minutes, mais je commence juste à me détendre.</code> The time phrase changes, but the present tense stays because the waiting continues.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-une-soir-e-bien-remplie\">Connected reading: une soirée bien remplie</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le jeudi, je descends du bus vers dix-huit heures et j'entre dans un petit centre commercial près de chez moi. J'attends parfois quelques minutes devant la boulangerie parce que beaucoup de gens achètent du pain à cette heure-là. Ensuite, je regarde les vitrines, j'essaie rarement des vêtements, mais je préfère comparer les prix. Si je trouve un cadeau intéressant, j'appelle ma sœur et je lui envoie une photo. Nous habitons dans la même ville depuis peu, alors nous répondons vite l'un à l'autre et nous mangeons souvent ensemble après les courses.</code></p>\n<p>— On Thursdays, I get off the bus around six o'clock and enter a small shopping center near my home. I sometimes wait a few minutes in front of the bakery because many people buy bread at that time. Then I look at the shop windows; I rarely try on clothes, but I prefer to compare prices. If I find an interesting gift, I call my sister and send her a photo. We have lived in the same city only a short time, so we answer each other quickly and often eat together after shopping.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find all the regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs in the paragraph.</li>\n<li>Find all the spelling-change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs and sort them by pattern.</li>\n<li>Explain why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habitons</code> appears with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis peu</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the last sentence in the singular, changing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> wherever it still makes sense.</li>\n<li>Write three new sentences about your own after-work routine using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Regular -re verbs in the paragraph: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">descends</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">descendre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondons</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre</code>. Each follows the regular -re pattern.</li>\n<li>Spelling-change -er verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achètent</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> changes e to è; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code> changes y to i; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code> changes é to è; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelle</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code> doubles <strong>l</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoie</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code> changes y to i; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manger</code> adds <strong>e</strong> before <strong>-ons</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons dans la même ville depuis peu</code> uses the present tense because the living situation started recently and still continues now.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite dans la même ville depuis peu, alors je réponds vite et je mange souvent avec elle après les courses.</code> Three things change. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondons</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponds</code>, where the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d</code> is written but silent. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code> loses its extra <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> and becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mange</code>, because the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> was only ever there to keep the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">g</code> soft before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code>. And <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'un à l'autre</code> has to go: it means <em>to each other</em>, which needs two people, so the singular takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vite</code> alone or names the other person.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. Models: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Après le travail, j'attends le bus devant le bureau.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète parfois du pain depuis peu.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère rentrer tôt, puis j'envoie un message à ma famille.</code> Check that each verb ending matches its subject.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Writing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il attends</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il attend</code>.</li>\n<li>Using an English-style past tense after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> for an action that is still continuing.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the cedilla in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commençons</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the extra <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous mangeons</code>.</li>\n<li>Extending the changed stem to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> in verbs like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code> change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> in the forms shown here: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'essaie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'envoie</code>.</li>\n<li>Focusing on spelling so much that you lose the basic subject-verb agreement.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"regular-re-endings\">Regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>Example with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je réponds</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu réponds</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td>no ending</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle répond</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous répondons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous répondez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils répondent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"depuis-reminder\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> reminder</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>French pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>for two years and still true now</td>\n<td>present + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis deux ans</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis deux ans.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>since noon and still happening</td>\n<td>present + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis midi</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle attend depuis midi.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>how long?</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand... ?</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand travaillez-vous ici ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"spelling-change-snapshot\">Spelling-change snapshot</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Key form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-cer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commençons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ger</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manger</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous mangeons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">è</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'achète / nous achetons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">é</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">è</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je préfère / nous préférons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>double consonant</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'appelle / nous appelons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">i</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'essaie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'envoie</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a shopping trip, daily routine, or after-work routine. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least three regular present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> showing an action or situation that still continues;</li>\n<li>one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-cer</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ger</code> verb;</li>\n<li>one accent-changing verb (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code> type);</li>\n<li>one double-consonant or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>-changing verb;</li>\n<li>one question you could realistically ask another person.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>label each verb family (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code>, regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code>, or spelling-change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code>);</li>\n<li>underline the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> phrase;</li>\n<li>check whether any spelling change should disappear in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any sentence you corrected from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is one model paragraph that meets every requirement:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le jeudi, j'attends ma sœur devant le magasin. Elle descend du bus vers dix-huit heures et répond vite à mon message. Nous habitons dans le même quartier depuis deux ans. Nous commençons les courses au petit centre commercial. J'achète une veste noire, mais je préfère la bleue. Ma sœur essaie un manteau et appelle notre mère pour demander son avis. Ensuite, nous mangeons au café d'en face. Est-ce que tu attends souvent quelqu'un après le travail ?</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: regular -re verbs are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">descend</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répond</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis deux ans</code> shows a situation that still continues; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commençons</code> is a -cer verb; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code> are accent-changing verbs; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code> changes y to i and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appelle</code> doubles <strong>l</strong>; the final sentence is a realistic yes/no question.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if any of these appear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il attends</code> or another regular -re form with the wrong singular ending;</li>\n<li>a past tense with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> for something still true now;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commencons</code> without ç or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous mangons</code> without the extra <strong>e</strong>;</li>\n<li>a changed stem carried into <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, such as <strong>nous achètons</strong>;</li>\n<li>a question without a space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 5 lessons: regular -re verbs, depuis, and spelling-change verbs this chapter adds two important ideas at the same time: a new regular verb family, the regular `-re` verbs, and a group of familiar-looking `-er` verbs whose spelling changes in some forms. you will also learn how french uses `depuis` with the present tense to talk about actions and situations that started in the past and continue now. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. conjugate regular present-tense `-re` verbs in all six persons; 2. use `depuis` with the present tense to express an ongoing action or state; 3. conjugate `-cer` and `-ger` verbs such as `commencer` and `manger`; 4. conjugate accent-changing verbs such as `acheter` and `préférer`; 5. conjugate double-consonant verbs such as `appeler` and `jeter`; 6. conjugate `y`-changing verbs such as `envoyer` and `essayer`; 7. combine these patterns in realistic everyday sentences. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can still: - conjugate regular `-er` verbs from chapter 3; - use chapter 4 numbers and time expressions such as `midi`, `à dix-neuf heures`, and `depuis trois ans`-style time phrases in english meaning; - form yes/no and information questions; - recognize that the subject controls the verb ending, even in longer sentences. you will need that foundation because the new spellings in this chapter only make sense if the regular `-er` pattern is already solid. test yourself before starting. answer these five without notes. 1. conjugate `parler` with `nous`. — `nous parlons`. regular -er verbs take **-ons** with `nous`. 2. ask a yes/no question from `vous travaillez ce soir.` — `vous travaillez ce soir ?` or `est-ce que vous travaillez ce soir ?` french does not add a separate word for english **do**. 3. write a clock-time phrase for 7 p.m. — `à dix-neuf heures`. chapter 4 schedule style often uses the 24-hour clock. 4. in `les étudiants regardent la carte`, what controls `regardent`? — `les étudiants`. the subject controls the verb ending, not the object `la carte`. 5. what does a phrase like “for three years and still now” require in french once you learn `depuis`? — a present-tense verb, as in `j'habite ici depuis trois ans`. if you missed two or more, review the earlier verb and time chapters first. this chapter adds spelling changes; it does not replace the basic subject-verb system. ## how to study this chapter for every table, say the six forms aloud from memory before copying them. when a spelling change appears, compare it to the regular pattern and ask yourself exactly **where** the change happens and where it does not. ## unit 1: regular -re verbs in the present tense ### core idea regular `-re` verbs form the present tense by removing `-re` and adding a new set of endings. ### model conjugation: `attendre` | subject | form | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `attends` | i wait | | `tu` | `attends` | you wait | | `il / elle / on` | `attend` | he/she/one waits | | `nous` | `attendons` | we wait | | `vous` | `attendez` | you wait | | `ils / elles` | `attendent` | they wait | ### the ending pattern | subject | ending | example with `vendre` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `-s` | `je vends` | | `tu` | `-s` | `tu vends` | | `il / elle / on` | no ending | `il vend` | | `nous` | `-ons` | `nous vendons` | | `vous` | `-ez` | `vous vendez` | | `ils / elles` | `-ent` | `ils vendent` | ### more common regular `-re` verbs | infinitive | meaning | |---|---| | `attendre` | to wait (for) | | `vendre` | to sell | | `répondre` | to answer | | `perdre` | to lose | | `entendre` | to hear | | `descendre` | to go down, descend | ### examples - `j'attends mon ami devant le magasin.` — i am waiting for my friend in front of the store. - `nous vendons des livres le samedi.` — we sell books on saturdays. - `elle répond vite à ma question.` — she answers my question quickly. - `vous perdez souvent vos clés ?` — do you often lose your keys? - `ils entendent la musique dans la rue.` — they hear the music in the street. ### pronunciation note in many regular `-re` verbs, the singular forms sound very close to one another, just as with regular `-er` verbs. the final written consonants are not always clearly heard, while `nous` and `vous` forms are easier to distinguish. ### common mistake for english speakers do not add an extra written ending in the `il/elle/on` form. it is `il attend`, not `*il attends`. ### sentence-building practice conjugate `attendre`, `répondre`, and `perdre` for all six persons. then write three original sentences using three different subjects. all three verbs are regular -re verbs: remove the final -re, then add -s, -s, no ending, -ons, -ez, -ent. | subject | `attendre` | `répondre` | `perdre` | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `j'attends` | `je réponds` | `je perds` | | `tu` | `tu attends` | `tu réponds` | `tu perds` | | `il / elle / on` | `il attend` | `elle répond` | `on perd` | | `nous` | `nous attendons` | `nous répondons` | `nous perdons` | | `vous` | `vous attendez` | `vous répondez` | `vous perdez` | | `ils / elles` | `ils attendent` | `elles répondent` | `ils perdent` | the trap is the singular spelling: `il attend`, `elle répond`, and `on perd` have no added ending after the stem. sample original sentences: - `j'attends le bus devant la gare.` — `je` takes -s, and the final written s is silent. - `nous répondons au professeur après le cours.` — `nous` takes -ons, so this form is easy to hear. - `elles perdent souvent leurs clés.` — -ent is written for plural agreement but is silent. ## unit 2: `depuis` with the present tense ### core idea use `depuis` + a point in time or a duration with the **present tense** when something started in the past and is still true now. ### main structures | structure | example | natural english | |---|---|---| | present + `depuis` + duration | `j'habite ici depuis deux ans.` | i have lived here for two years. | | present + `depuis` + starting point | `elle attend depuis midi.` | she has been waiting since noon. | | `depuis quand` + present? | `depuis quand travaillez-vous ici ?` | how long have you worked here? | ### examples - `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.` — we have lived here for three years. - `j'étudie le français depuis septembre.` — i have been studying french since september. - `il attend le bus depuis vingt minutes.` — he has been waiting for the bus for twenty minutes. - `vous travaillez ensemble depuis quand ?` — since when have you been working together? - `elle préfère ce café depuis son arrivée en ville.` — she has preferred this café since her arrival in the city. ### pronunciation note treat `depuis` as one common time marker. in fast speech, you will hear it very often right after the verb phrase: `j'habite ici depuis...`, `on attend depuis...`. ### common mistake for english speakers english usually uses the present perfect here: \"i have lived,\" \"she has been waiting.\" french usually keeps the ordinary present tense: `j'habite`, `elle attend`. ### sentence-building practice complete the sentences with your own ideas: 1. `j'étudie le français depuis ___ .` 2. `nous habitons ici depuis ___ .` 3. `il attend depuis ___ .` 4. `depuis quand est-ce que vous ___ ?` then ask and answer one `depuis quand... ?` question aloud. model completions — yours can use different time phrases. 1. `j'étudie le français depuis septembre.` — the studying began in september and still continues, so french keeps the present tense. 2. `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.` — english says *have lived*, but french says `habitons` because the situation is still true now. 3. `il attend depuis midi.` — `depuis` can introduce a starting point such as `midi`. 4. `depuis quand est-ce que vous travaillez ici ?` — `depuis quand` asks when the continuing action began; the verb stays present. question and answer example: - `depuis quand est-ce que tu apprends le français ?` - `j'apprends le français depuis deux mois.` mark yourself wrong if you used a past tense for an action that is still continuing, or if your answer after `depuis` does not give either a duration or a starting point. ## unit 3: `-cer` and `-ger` verbs to keep the soft sound ### core idea some regular `-er` verbs change spelling in the `nous` form so that the pronunciation stays natural. ### `commencer`: add a cedilla before `o` | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `commence` | | `tu` | `commences` | | `il / elle / on` | `commence` | | `nous` | `commençons` | | `vous` | `commencez` | | `ils / elles` | `commencent` | ### `manger`: add `e` before `ons` | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `mange` | | `tu` | `manges` | | `il / elle / on` | `mange` | | `nous` | `mangeons` | | `vous` | `mangez` | | `ils / elles` | `mangent` | ### why the spelling changes | verb type | change | purpose | |---|---|---| | `-cer` | `c` -> `ç` before `o` | keep the soft `s` sound | | `-ger` | add `e` before `o` | keep the soft `j` sound | ### examples - `nous commençons le cours à huit heures.` — we start class at eight o'clock. - `je commence toujours par les exercices faciles.` — i always start with the easy exercises. - `nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.` — we eat at the restaurant before starting the shopping. - `elle mange tôt le soir.` — she eats early in the evening. - `ils commencent en septembre.` — they start in september. ### pronunciation note these are spelling changes, not new endings. the verbs are still regular `-er` verbs; the extra mark simply protects the original sound. ### common mistake for english speakers learners often forget that the change usually appears only where `o` would otherwise harden the sound. the most important form to memorize first is `nous commençons` and `nous mangeons`. ### sentence-building practice conjugate `commencer` and `manger` for all six persons. then rewrite these with `nous`: `je commence le travail.` / `tu manges ici.` / `elle commence tôt.` these are still regular -er verbs, but the `nous` spelling changes to protect the sound. | subject | `commencer` | `manger` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `je commence` | `je mange` | | `tu` | `tu commences` | `tu manges` | | `il / elle / on` | `il commence` | `elle mange` | | `nous` | `nous commençons` | `nous mangeons` | | `vous` | `vous commencez` | `vous mangez` | | `ils / elles` | `ils commencent` | `elles mangent` | rewrites with `nous`: 1. `nous commençons le travail.` — `commencer` needs ç before -ons so the sound stays soft. 2. `nous mangeons ici.` — `manger` adds e before -ons so the g stays soft. 3. `nous commençons tôt.` — the subject changed, so the verb must change too; `nous commence` is not possible. ## unit 4: accent-changing verbs such as `acheter` and `préférer` ### core idea some verbs change the vowel in the stem so that the stressed syllable sounds natural. the change usually appears in all forms **except** `nous` and `vous`. ### model 1: `acheter` (`e` -> `è`) | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `achète` | | `tu` | `achètes` | | `il / elle / on` | `achète` | | `nous` | `achetons` | | `vous` | `achetez` | | `ils / elles` | `achètent` | ### model 2: `préférer` (`é` -> `è`) | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `préfère` | | `tu` | `préfères` | | `il / elle / on` | `préfère` | | `nous` | `préférons` | | `vous` | `préférez` | | `ils / elles` | `préfèrent` | ### examples - `j'achète du pain le matin.` — i buy bread in the morning. - `elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.` — she buys a shirt and tries on a jacket. - `nous achetons nos billets en ligne.` — we buy our tickets online. - `je préfère la chemise bleue.` — i prefer the blue shirt. - `nous préférons le train.` — we prefer the train. - `ils préfèrent étudier le soir.` — they prefer to study in the evening. ### pronunciation note say the pairs aloud: `j'achète / nous achetons`, `je préfère / nous préférons`. the sound changes are easier to feel than to memorize abstractly. ### common mistake for english speakers do not spread the accented form across all six persons. `nous achetons` and `vous préférez` keep the simpler stem. ### sentence-building practice conjugate `acheter` and `préférer` for all six persons. then replace the subject in these sentences: `j'achète un livre.` / `elle préfère le café.` / `ils achètent des fruits.` the changed stem appears in `je`, `tu`, `il / elle / on`, and `ils / elles`, but not in `nous` or `vous`. | subject | `acheter` | `préférer` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `j'achète` | `je préfère` | | `tu` | `tu achètes` | `tu préfères` | | `il / elle / on` | `elle achète` | `il préfère` | | `nous` | `nous achetons` | `nous préférons` | | `vous` | `vous achetez` | `vous préférez` | | `ils / elles` | `ils achètent` | `elles préfèrent` | model subject replacements: 1. `nous achetons un livre.` — with `nous`, the stem returns to `achet-`; do not write the accent here. 2. `vous préférez le café.` — with `vous`, keep the é in the ending pattern: `préférez`. 3. `j'achète des fruits.` — `je` becomes `j'` before a vowel sound, and the stem changes to `achèt-`. ## unit 5: double-consonant verbs and `y`-changing verbs ### core idea a few high-frequency `-er` verbs keep their pronunciation by doubling a consonant or changing `y` to `i` in certain forms. ### model 1: `appeler` (double consonant) | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `appelle` | | `tu` | `appelles` | | `il / elle / on` | `appelle` | | `nous` | `appelons` | | `vous` | `appelez` | | `ils / elles` | `appellent` | `jeter` follows the same idea: `je jette`, `nous jetons`. ### model 2: `essayer` (`y` -> `i` in the common spelling shown here) | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `essaie` | | `tu` | `essaies` | | `il / elle / on` | `essaie` | | `nous` | `essayons` | | `vous` | `essayez` | | `ils / elles` | `essaient` | ### another common `y` verb: `envoyer` | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `envoie` | | `tu` | `envoies` | | `il / elle / on` | `envoie` | | `nous` | `envoyons` | | `vous` | `envoyez` | | `ils / elles` | `envoient` | ### examples - `j'appelle ma sœur ce soir.` — i am calling my sister this evening. - `nous appelons un taxi.` — we call a taxi. - `elle jette le ticket.` — she throws away the ticket. - `j'essaie une veste noire.` — i am trying on a black jacket. - `nous essayons plusieurs idées.` — we are trying several ideas. - `j'envoie un message après le cours.` — i send a message after class. - `nous envoyons les documents demain.` — we send the documents tomorrow. ### pronunciation note the doubled consonant or changed vowel helps keep the stressed sound consistent. compare `j'appelle` with `nous appelons`, and `j'essaie` with `nous essayons`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not assume the spelling change appears everywhere. as with `acheter` and `préférer`, the simplest stem usually returns in `nous` and `vous`. ### sentence-building practice conjugate `appeler`, `essayer`, and `envoyer` for all six persons. then write one sentence with each verb using a different subject. these verbs also change mainly outside the `nous` and `vous` forms. | subject | `appeler` | `essayer` | `envoyer` | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `j'appelle` | `j'essaie` | `j'envoie` | | `tu` | `tu appelles` | `tu essaies` | `tu envoies` | | `il / elle / on` | `elle appelle` | `il essaie` | `on envoie` | | `nous` | `nous appelons` | `nous essayons` | `nous envoyons` | | `vous` | `vous appelez` | `vous essayez` | `vous envoyez` | | `ils / elles` | `ils appellent` | `elles essaient` | `ils envoient` | sample sentences: - `j'appelle ma mère ce soir.` — `appeler` doubles the l in the `je` form. - `nous essayons une autre solution.` — with `nous`, keep y in `essayons`. - `ils envoient un message au professeur.` — the y changes to i in `ils envoient`, and -ent is silent. ## unit 6: putting the chapter patterns together ### core idea this chapter becomes useful when you can move quickly from one pattern to another without losing track of the subject. ### examples in practical situations - `j'attends mon ami devant le magasin depuis midi.` — i have been waiting for my friend in front of the store since noon. - `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.` — we have lived here for three years. - `elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.` — she buys a shirt and tries on a jacket. - `nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.` — we eat at the restaurant before starting the shopping. - `je préfère ce quartier, mais j'appelle souvent mes parents parce qu'ils habitent loin.` — i prefer this neighborhood, but i often call my parents because they live far away. - `vous répondez vite, puis vous envoyez le message.` — you answer quickly, then you send the message. ### pronunciation or listening note when several verbs appear in the same sentence, pause mentally at each subject change. that habit keeps endings accurate even when the sentence becomes long. ### common mistake for english speakers do not let the more unusual spelling changes distract you from the basic sentence frame. first identify the subject, then choose the verb family, then apply the spelling change only if needed. ### sentence-building practice build one sentence from each set: 1. `je / attendre / le bus / depuis vingt minutes` 2. `nous / commencer / la réunion / à neuf heures` 3. `elle / préférer / cette robe / et / essayer / l'autre` 4. `vous / répondre / au professeur / puis / envoyer / le devoir` 1. `j'attends le bus depuis vingt minutes.` — `attendre` is a regular -re verb, and `depuis` takes the present because the waiting is still happening. 2. `nous commençons la réunion à neuf heures.` — the `nous` form of `commencer` needs ç before -ons. 3. `elle préfère cette robe et essaie l'autre.` — both verbs agree with `elle`; `préférer` changes é to è, and `essayer` changes y to i in this common spelling. 4. `vous répondez au professeur, puis vous envoyez le devoir.` — `répondre` takes the -ez ending with `vous`; `envoyer` keeps y in the `vous` form. if your sentence has two verbs, check both. a correct first verb does not protect the second one from agreement or spelling-change errors. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `j'attends mon ami devant le magasin.` — i am waiting for my friend in front of the store. 2. `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.` — we have lived here for three years. 3. `elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.` — she buys a shirt and tries on a jacket. 4. `nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.` — we eat at the restaurant before starting the shopping. 5. `je préfère ce livre, mais j'appelle ma sœur pour demander son avis.` — i prefer this book, but i call my sister to ask for her opinion. 6. `vous répondez depuis votre bureau ou vous envoyez un message plus tard ?` — are you answering from your office, or are you sending a message later? after writing the six sentences from memory, check for these traps. - **silent singular endings.** `j'attends` has a written **s**, but you do not hear it clearly. - **present tense with ongoing time.** `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans` uses the present because the living situation still continues. - **spelling-change stems.** sentence 3 has `achète` and `essaie`; sentence 5 has `préfère` and `appelle`. - **nous protection spellings.** sentence 4 has `mangeons`, with an extra **e** to keep the soft sound before **-ons**. - **question spacing and accents.** sentence 6 needs `répondez`, `envoyez`, and a space before `?`. if you got the sound right but missed an accent or stem change, rewrite that exact form three times and then use it in a new sentence. ### notice and mark 1. underline the subject of each sentence. 2. circle the conjugated verb or verbs. 3. box any spelling change. 4. mark the sentence that uses `depuis` and explain why the present tense appears there. | # | subject or subjects | conjugated verb or verbs | spelling change | `depuis`? | |---|---|---|---|---| | 1 | `je` | `attends` | none; regular -re | no | | 2 | `nous` | `habitons` | none; regular -er | yes, `depuis trois ans` | | 3 | `elle` | `achète`, `essaie` | accent change; y changes to i | no | | 4 | `nous` | `mangeons` | extra **e** before **-ons** | no | | 5 | `je`, `j'` | `préfère`, `appelle` | accent change; doubled **l** | no | | 6 | `vous`, `vous` | `répondez`, `envoyez` | `envoyez` keeps **y** with `vous` | yes, `depuis votre bureau` | the clearest continuing-time sentence is sentence 2: `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans`. french uses present tense because the situation began earlier and is still true now. sentence 6 also contains `depuis`, but there it means “from your office,” not “for/since a length of time.” ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: first change the subject, then change one important detail such as the place, object, or time expression. make every required spelling and agreement change. one worked set: 1. subject: `nous attendons notre ami devant le magasin.` detail: `j'attends mon frère devant la gare.` 2. subject: `elle habite ici depuis trois ans.` detail: `nous habitons ici depuis six mois.` 3. subject: `nous achetons une chemise et essayons une veste.` detail: `elle achète un manteau et essaie une écharpe.` 4. subject: `je mange au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.` detail: `nous mangeons au café avant de commencer le travail.` the important lesson is that `nous` often removes the visible stem change: `achetons`, `essayons`, and `mangeons`. if you change the subject but leave the old spelling, the sentence is wrong even if the meaning is clear. ### make it personal write four true sentences about your own life: one with a regular `-re` verb, one with `depuis`, one with a spelling-change `-er` verb, and one sentence with two chapter verbs together. model check for the cumulative practice: 1. `j'attends mon ami devant le magasin.` — subject: `je`; verb: `attends`. this is a regular -re form with -s, even though the final s is silent. 2. `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.` — subject: `nous`; verb: `habitons`; `depuis trois ans` explains why french uses the present for a continuing situation. 3. `elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.` — subject: `elle`; verbs: `achète` and `essaie`. both show spelling changes in the stem. 4. `nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.` — subject: `nous`; verb: `mangeons`. the extra e keeps the g soft before -ons. 5. `je préfère ce livre, mais j'appelle ma sœur pour demander son avis.` — subjects: `je` and `j'`; verbs: `préfère` and `appelle`. watch both the accent change and the doubled l. 6. `vous répondez depuis votre bureau ou vous envoyez un message plus tard ?` — subject: `vous`; verbs: `répondez` and `envoyez`. `envoyer` keeps y in the `vous` form. for the rewrite task, answers vary. if you change sentence 3 to `nous achetons une chemise et essayons une veste.`, both spelling changes disappear because `nous` uses the simpler stem. if you change only a detail, as in `elle achète une écharpe et essaie un manteau.`, the verb forms stay the same. for your personal sentences, mark yourself wrong if the -re verb ending does not match the subject, if `depuis` is followed by a past-tense idea for something still true now, or if a `nous` or `vous` form keeps a spelling change it should drop. ## mini-dialogue: shopping and waiting **mina :** `tu attends depuis longtemps ?` — have you been waiting a long time? **paul :** `oui, j'attends depuis midi, mais je commence juste à me détendre.` — yes, i have been waiting since noon, but i am just starting to relax. **mina :** `pourquoi ?` — why? **paul :** `parce que ma sœur essaie trois vestes et elle préfère toujours une autre couleur.` — because my sister is trying on three jackets, and she always prefers another color. **mina :** `et toi, tu achètes quelque chose ?` — and you, are you buying anything? **paul :** `oui, j'achète un cadeau, puis j'appelle mon frère pour demander son avis.` — yes, i am buying a gift, then i am calling my brother to ask for his opinion. **mina :** `vous mangez ici après les achats ?` — are you eating here after the shopping? **paul :** `oui, nous mangeons au café d'en face avant d'envoyer les photos à la famille.` — yes, we are eating at the café across the street before sending the photos to the family. ### guided analysis 1. find the sentence with `depuis` and explain the time meaning. 2. list three spelling-change verbs and name their pattern. 3. find one regular `-re` verb and identify its subject. 4. rewrite `elle préfère toujours une autre couleur` with `nous`. 5. change the dialogue so that the waiting starts `depuis dix minutes` instead of `depuis midi`. 1. the dialogue uses continuing-time `depuis` in `tu attends depuis longtemps ?` and `j'attends depuis midi`. the waiting started earlier and is still happening, so french keeps `attends` in the present tense. 2. three spelling-change verbs: `commence` belongs to the -cer family, though this form does not need ç; `essaie` changes y to i; `préfère` changes é to è; `achètes` and `achète` change e to è; `appelle` doubles **l**; `mangeons` adds **e** before **-ons**. 3. a regular -re verb is `attends`. its subject is `tu` in `tu attends depuis longtemps ?` and `je` in `j'attends depuis midi`. 4. `nous préférons toujours une autre couleur.` — with `nous`, `préférer` keeps the simpler stem and takes **-ons**. 5. revised waiting lines: `tu attends depuis dix minutes ?` `oui, j'attends depuis dix minutes, mais je commence juste à me détendre.` the time phrase changes, but the present tense stays because the waiting continues. ## connected reading: une soirée bien remplie `le jeudi, je descends du bus vers dix-huit heures et j'entre dans un petit centre commercial près de chez moi. j'attends parfois quelques minutes devant la boulangerie parce que beaucoup de gens achètent du pain à cette heure-là. ensuite, je regarde les vitrines, j'essaie rarement des vêtements, mais je préfère comparer les prix. si je trouve un cadeau intéressant, j'appelle ma sœur et je lui envoie une photo. nous habitons dans la même ville depuis peu, alors nous répondons vite l'un à l'autre et nous mangeons souvent ensemble après les courses.` — on thursdays, i get off the bus around six o'clock and enter a small shopping center near my home. i sometimes wait a few minutes in front of the bakery because many people buy bread at that time. then i look at the shop windows; i rarely try on clothes, but i prefer to compare prices. if i find an interesting gift, i call my sister and send her a photo. we have lived in the same city only a short time, so we answer each other quickly and often eat together after shopping. ### reading practice 1. find all the regular `-re` verbs in the paragraph. 2. find all the spelling-change `-er` verbs and sort them by pattern. 3. explain why `habitons` appears with `depuis peu`. 4. rewrite the last sentence in the singular, changing `nous` to `je` wherever it still makes sense. 5. write three new sentences about your own after-work routine using the reading as a model. 1. regular -re verbs in the paragraph: `descends` from `descendre`, `attends` from `attendre`, and `répondons` from `répondre`. each follows the regular -re pattern. 2. spelling-change -er verbs: `achètent` from `acheter` changes e to è; `essaie` from `essayer` changes y to i; `préfère` from `préférer` changes é to è; `appelle` from `appeler` doubles **l**; `envoie` from `envoyer` changes y to i; `mangeons` from `manger` adds **e** before **-ons**. 3. `nous habitons dans la même ville depuis peu` uses the present tense because the living situation started recently and still continues now. 4. `j'habite dans la même ville depuis peu, alors je réponds vite et je mange souvent avec elle après les courses.` three things change. `répondons` becomes `réponds`, where the `d` is written but silent. `mangeons` loses its extra `e` and becomes `mange`, because the `e` was only ever there to keep the `g` soft before `-ons`. and `l'un à l'autre` has to go: it means *to each other*, which needs two people, so the singular takes `vite` alone or names the other person. 5. answers vary. models: `après le travail, j'attends le bus devant le bureau.` `j'achète parfois du pain depuis peu.` `je préfère rentrer tôt, puis j'envoie un message à ma famille.` check that each verb ending matches its subject. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. writing `il attends` instead of `il attend`. 2. using an english-style past tense after `depuis` for an action that is still continuing. 3. forgetting the cedilla in `nous commençons`. 4. forgetting the extra `e` in `nous mangeons`. 5. extending the changed stem to `nous` and `vous` in verbs like `acheter`, `préférer`, and `appeler`. 6. forgetting that `essayer` and `envoyer` change `y` in the forms shown here: `j'essaie`, `j'envoie`. 7. focusing on spelling so much that you lose the basic subject-verb agreement. ## chapter reference sheet ### regular `-re` endings | pronoun | ending | example with `répondre` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `-s` | `je réponds` | | `tu` | `-s` | `tu réponds` | | `il / elle / on` | no ending | `elle répond` | | `nous` | `-ons` | `nous répondons` | | `vous` | `-ez` | `vous répondez` | | `ils / elles` | `-ent` | `ils répondent` | ### `depuis` reminder | meaning | french pattern | example | |---|---|---| | for two years and still true now | present + `depuis deux ans` | `j'habite ici depuis deux ans.` | | since noon and still happening | present + `depuis midi` | `elle attend depuis midi.` | | how long? | `depuis quand... ?` | `depuis quand travaillez-vous ici ?` | ### spelling-change snapshot | pattern | model | key form | |---|---|---| | `-cer` | `commencer` | `nous commençons` | | `-ger` | `manger` | `nous mangeons` | | `e` -> `è` | `acheter` | `j'achète / nous achetons` | | `é` -> `è` | `préférer` | `je préfère / nous préférons` | | double consonant | `appeler` | `j'appelle / nous appelons` | | `y` -> `i` | `essayer`, `envoyer` | `j'essaie`, `j'envoie` | ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a shopping trip, daily routine, or after-work routine. include: - at least three regular present-tense `-re` verbs; - one sentence with `depuis` showing an action or situation that still continues; - one `-cer` or `-ger` verb; - one accent-changing verb (`acheter` or `préférer` type); - one double-consonant or `y`-changing verb; - one question you could realistically ask another person. after writing: 1. circle every conjugated verb; 2. label each verb family (`-re`, regular `-er`, or spelling-change `-er`); 3. underline the `depuis` phrase; 4. check whether any spelling change should disappear in the `nous` or `vous` form; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any sentence you corrected from memory. answers vary. here is one model paragraph that meets every requirement: `le jeudi, j'attends ma sœur devant le magasin. elle descend du bus vers dix-huit heures et répond vite à mon message. nous habitons dans le même quartier depuis deux ans. nous commençons les courses au petit centre commercial. j'achète une veste noire, mais je préfère la bleue. ma sœur essaie un manteau et appelle notre mère pour demander son avis. ensuite, nous mangeons au café d'en face. est-ce que tu attends souvent quelqu'un après le travail ?` how it satisfies the brief: regular -re verbs are `attends`, `descend`, and `répond`; `depuis deux ans` shows a situation that still continues; `commençons` is a -cer verb; `achète` and `préfère` are accent-changing verbs; `essaie` changes y to i and `appelle` doubles **l**; the final sentence is a realistic yes/no question. mark yourself wrong if any of these appear: - `il attends` or another regular -re form with the wrong singular ending; - a past tense with `depuis` for something still true now; - `nous commencons` without ç or `nous mangons` without the extra **e**; - a changed stem carried into `nous` or `vous`, such as **nous achètons**; - a question without a space before `?`. "},{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 5 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-exercises\">Chapter 5 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>nous / attendre</li>\n<li>il / répondre</li>\n<li>nous / commencer</li>\n<li>nous / manger</li>\n<li>elle / acheter</li>\n<li>J'habite ici. I started three years ago.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous attendons</code> — Regular -re verb: remove -re from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code>, then add -ons for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il répond</code> — The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code> form has no extra ending after the stem; do not write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponds</code> here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commençons</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> needs ç in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form to keep the soft sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous mangeons</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">manger</code> adds e before -ons so the g stays soft.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle achète</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> changes e to è in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis trois ans.</code> — French uses present tense with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> because the living situation began three years ago and still continues.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>regular -re verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends le bus.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>depuis</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis deux ans.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>spelling changes</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>shopping language</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère la chemise bleue.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must demonstrate the named structure, use the right verb form, and not simply copy the model sentence.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Regular -re verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous répondons au message ce soir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre</code> is regular, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> takes -ons.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle travaille ici depuis janvier.</code> — The work began in January and still continues, so the verb is present tense.</li>\n<li>Spelling changes: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons au café.</code> — The extra e in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code> keeps the g soft.</li>\n<li>Shopping language: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète une veste noire.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if the verb ending does not match the subject, if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> describes a finished past action, or if your shopping sentence has no actual shopping word or object.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une chemise.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous achetons une chemise.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> drops the accent change and takes -ons.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'achète pas une chemise.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before the vowel sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achète</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce qu'elle achète une chemise ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une veste.</code> — The verb stays the same because the subject did not change.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Your own starting sentence may be different. Check that each rewrite changes only the requested feature and that the subject change did not leave the old verb form behind.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends mon ami devant le magasin.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before a vowel sound, and the final <strong>s</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code> is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> uses the present tense because the living situation still continues.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons le cours à huit heures.</code> — the cedilla in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commençons</code> keeps the soft sound before <strong>-ons</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mangeons</code> needs an extra <strong>e</strong> before <strong>-ons</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.</code> — both verbs have spelling changes: e to è, and y to i.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous répondez depuis votre bureau ou vous envoyez un message plus tard ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondez</code> is regular -re with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyez</code> keeps <strong>y</strong> in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct accents and stem changes in a separate pass. These are visual spelling problems even when the sentence sounds right.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a short store dialogue using one -re verb, three spelling-change verbs, a color, and depuis.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model dialogue:</p>\n<p><strong>Client :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour, j'attends ma sœur depuis dix minutes.</code> <strong>Vendeuse :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pas de problème. Vous cherchez quelque chose ?</code> <strong>Client :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, j'achète une veste noire et j'essaie cette chemise.</code> <strong>Vendeuse :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Votre sœur préfère quelle couleur ?</code> <strong>Client :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle préfère le bleu, mais nous commençons par la veste noire.</code></p>\n<p>Self-check:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one -re verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'attends</code>;</li>\n<li>three spelling-change verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'achète</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'essaie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code>, plus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commençons</code>;</li>\n<li>a color: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bleu</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> with present tense: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'attends ... depuis dix minutes</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark it wrong if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">depuis</code> is paired with a completed past action, if a color fails to agree where it should, or if your spelling-change verbs keep the wrong stem for their subject.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Regular -re verbs.</strong> Remove -re and add -s, -s, no ending, -ons, -ez, -ent. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle répond vite.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Depuis.</strong> Use present tense for something that started before now and still continues. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'étudie le français depuis septembre.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Spelling-change -er verbs.</strong> The verb is still an -er verb, but the stem changes to protect the sound or stress. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons à huit heures.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Shopping language.</strong> Use the same verb patterns with real objects, colors, and preferences. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète une chemise bleue.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you can explain a rule but cannot produce a fresh French example, review that unit again. The goal is active production, not recognition only.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 5 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. nous / attendre 2. il / répondre 3. nous / commencer 4. nous / manger 5. elle / acheter 6. j'habite ici. i started three years ago. 1. `nous attendons` — regular -re verb: remove -re from `attendre`, then add -ons for `nous`. 2. `il répond` — the `il / elle / on` form has no extra ending after the stem; do not write `réponds` here. 3. `nous commençons` — `commencer` needs ç in the `nous` form to keep the soft sound. 4. `nous mangeons` — `manger` adds e before -ons so the g stays soft. 5. `elle achète` — `acheter` changes e to è in the `elle` form. 6. `j'habite ici depuis trois ans.` — french uses present tense with `depuis` because the living situation began three years ago and still continues. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **regular -re verbs**. use `j'attends le bus.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **depuis**. use `j'habite ici depuis deux ans.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **spelling changes**. use `nous commençons.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **shopping language**. use `je préfère la chemise bleue.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct sentence must demonstrate the named structure, use the right verb form, and not simply copy the model sentence. sample answers: 1. regular -re verbs: `nous répondons au message ce soir.` — `répondre` is regular, and `nous` takes -ons. 2. `depuis`: `elle travaille ici depuis janvier.` — the work began in january and still continues, so the verb is present tense. 3. spelling changes: `nous mangeons au café.` — the extra e in `mangeons` keeps the g soft. 4. shopping language: `j'achète une veste noire.` — `acheter` changes to `achète` with `je`, and `je` becomes `j'` before a vowel sound. mark yourself wrong if the verb ending does not match the subject, if `depuis` describes a finished past action, or if your shopping sentence has no actual shopping word or object. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `elle achète une chemise.` 1. change the subject: `nous achetons une chemise.` — `nous` drops the accent change and takes -ons. 2. make it negative: `elle n'achète pas une chemise.` — `ne` becomes `n'` before the vowel sound in `achète`. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce qu'elle achète une chemise ?` — `que` becomes `qu'` before `elle`. 4. change one meaningful detail: `elle achète une veste.` — the verb stays the same because the subject did not change. your own starting sentence may be different. check that each rewrite changes only the requested feature and that the subject change did not leave the old verb form behind. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `j'attends mon ami devant le magasin.` — `je` becomes `j'` before a vowel sound, and the final **s** in `attends` is silent. 2. `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.` — `depuis` uses the present tense because the living situation still continues. 3. `nous commençons le cours à huit heures.` — the cedilla in `commençons` keeps the soft sound before **-ons**. 4. `nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.` — `mangeons` needs an extra **e** before **-ons**. 5. `elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.` — both verbs have spelling changes: e to è, and y to i. 6. `vous répondez depuis votre bureau ou vous envoyez un message plus tard ?` — `répondez` is regular -re with `vous`, and `envoyez` keeps **y** in the `vous` form. correct accents and stem changes in a separate pass. these are visual spelling problems even when the sentence sounds right. ## e. personal writing write a short store dialogue using one -re verb, three spelling-change verbs, a color, and depuis. answers vary. a model dialogue: **client :** `bonjour, j'attends ma sœur depuis dix minutes.` **vendeuse :** `pas de problème. vous cherchez quelque chose ?` **client :** `oui, j'achète une veste noire et j'essaie cette chemise.` **vendeuse :** `votre sœur préfère quelle couleur ?` **client :** `elle préfère le bleu, mais nous commençons par la veste noire.` self-check: - one -re verb: `j'attends`; - three spelling-change verbs: `j'achète`, `j'essaie`, `préfère`, plus `commençons`; - a color: `noire`, `bleu`; - `depuis` with present tense: `j'attends ... depuis dix minutes`. mark it wrong if `depuis` is paired with a completed past action, if a color fails to agree where it should, or if your spelling-change verbs keep the wrong stem for their subject. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **regular -re verbs.** remove -re and add -s, -s, no ending, -ons, -ez, -ent. example: `elle répond vite.` 2. **depuis.** use present tense for something that started before now and still continues. example: `j'étudie le français depuis septembre.` 3. **spelling-change -er verbs.** the verb is still an -er verb, but the stem changes to protect the sound or stress. example: `nous commençons à huit heures.` 4. **shopping language.** use the same verb patterns with real objects, colors, and preferences. example: `j'achète une chemise bleue.` if you can explain a rule but cannot produce a fresh french example, review that unit again. the goal is active production, not recognition only. "},{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 5 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-answer-key\">Chapter 5 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous attendons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il répond</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commençons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous mangeons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle achète</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis trois ans.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Regular -re verbs:</strong> Remove -re and add -s, -s, nothing, -ons, -ez, -ent. The final consonant is often heard in plural forms but not singular forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Depuis:</strong> Use depuis with the present tense for something that began in the past and is still continuing.</li>\n<li><strong>Spelling changes:</strong> Some -er verbs change spelling to preserve sound or stress: commençons, mangeons, j'achète, je préfère, j'appelle, j'essaie.</li>\n<li><strong>Shopping language:</strong> Apply verb forms to clothing, toiletries, preferences, and time expressions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 5 answer key ## section a 1. `nous attendons` 2. `il répond` 3. `nous commençons` 4. `nous mangeons` 5. `elle achète` 6. `j'habite ici depuis trois ans.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **regular -re verbs:** remove -re and add -s, -s, nothing, -ons, -ez, -ent. the final consonant is often heard in plural forms but not singular forms. - **depuis:** use depuis with the present tense for something that began in the past and is still continuing. - **spelling changes:** some -er verbs change spelling to preserve sound or stress: commençons, mangeons, j'achète, je préfère, j'appelle, j'essaie. - **shopping language:** apply verb forms to clothing, toiletries, preferences, and time expressions. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 5 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 5 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Regular -re verbs</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Depuis</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Spelling changes</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Shopping language</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 5 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **regular -re verbs**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **depuis** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **spelling changes**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **shopping language**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":6,"slug":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire","title":"Aller, the Near Future, Prepositions, and Faire","documents":[{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/README.md","title":"Chapter 6: Aller, the Near Future, Prepositions, and Faire","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-aller-the-near-future-prepositions-and-faire\">Chapter 6: Aller, the Near Future, Prepositions, and Faire</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate aller and faire.</li>\n<li>Form the near future.</li>\n<li>Use à, de, location, time, and geographical prepositions.</li>\n<li>Describe weather and travel plans.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Aller and the near future</li>\n<li>À and de</li>\n<li>Geographical names</li>\n<li>Faire</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 6: aller, the near future, prepositions, and faire this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate aller and faire. - form the near future. - use à, de, location, time, and geographical prepositions. - describe weather and travel plans. ## lesson sequence 1. aller and the near future 2. à and de 3. geographical names 4. faire ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 6 Lessons: Aller, Prepositions, Geography, and Faire","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-lessons-aller-prepositions-geography-and-faire\">Chapter 6 Lessons: Aller, Prepositions, Geography, and Faire</h1>\n<p>This chapter expands what you can do with motion, place, and everyday action. You will learn how French talks about going somewhere, where things are located, where people come from, what is about to happen, and what someone is doing when the verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> appears in weather, activities, and common expressions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> confidently in the present tense;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code> to talk about the near future;</li>\n<li>choose between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> and form the contractions <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>;</li>\n<li>use common prepositions of location and sequence, including <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">entre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devant</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de</code>;</li>\n<li>ask and answer questions built after prepositions, including <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de quoi</code>, and forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code>;</li>\n<li>choose the correct preposition with cities, countries, and regions;</li>\n<li>conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> and use it for weather, activities, and fixed expressions.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> in the present tense;</li>\n<li>make negative sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>;</li>\n<li>recognize singular and plural noun phrases;</li>\n<li>understand that a French infinitive is the dictionary form of the verb, such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code>.</li>\n<p>You will use all of those skills in this chapter. For example, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il ne va pas venir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles font attention</code> all depend on earlier chapters.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Check these five basics before you begin.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Give <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons</code>. You will need this kind of irregular form beside <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>.</li>\n<li>Make <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais partir</code> negative. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vais pas partir</code>. The negative wraps the conjugated verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code>, not the infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code>.</li>\n<li>Is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres</code> singular or plural? — plural. Plural noun phrases trigger contractions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> when they follow <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li>Find the infinitive in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles vont faire attention</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>. The infinitive is the unchanged dictionary form after the conjugated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vont</code>.</li>\n<li>Give <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est</code>. You still need <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, but weather expressions in this chapter usually use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review the earlier verb and negation chapters first. This chapter has many small words, and they depend on recognizing the verb and noun phrase accurately.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Say the conjugation tables aloud before you try to memorize them silently. With prepositions, always learn a short phrase rather than a single word: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez mes parents</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de sortir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire du vélo</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-the-verb-aller-in-the-present-tense\">Unit 1: The verb aller in the present tense</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Aller</code> means <strong>to go</strong> and is one of the most useful irregular verbs in French. Learn all six forms as a complete block.</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-of-aller\">Present tense of aller</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>English idea</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code></td>\n<td>I go / I am going</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vas</code></td>\n<td>you go / you are going</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code></td>\n<td>he/she/one goes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code></td>\n<td>we go</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez</code></td>\n<td>you go</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vont</code></td>\n<td>they go</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"main-uses-of-aller\">Main uses of aller</h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> to talk about movement to a destination or about how things are going in a general sense.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais à la bibliothèque après le déjeuner.</code> — I go to the library after lunch.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu vas souvent chez ta sœur le dimanche ?</code> — Do you often go to your sister's place on Sundays?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons au marché très tôt.</code> — We go to the market very early.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vont en ville à pied.</code> — They go into town on foot.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Comment va ton grand-père ?</code> — How is your grandfather doing?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça va bien aujourd'hui.</code> — It is going well today.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The forms <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vas</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code> all sound different from their spelling suggests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code> sounds roughly like <strong>vè</strong>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vas</code> sounds like <strong>va</strong>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code> sounds like <strong>va</strong>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vont</code> ends with a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-nt</code>.</li>\n<p>The written endings are irregular, so you cannot predict them from a regular pattern. Memorize them by sound and by sight.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not try to build <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> from the endings of a regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb. Incorrect forms such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je alle</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*nous allonss</code> happen when learners guess instead of recalling the full irregular set.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Complete each idea aloud, then write it.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais ...</code> with a place in your town.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons ...</code> with a plural destination.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle va ...</code> with a person's home using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vont ...</code> with a city.</li>\n<p>Then turn one sentence negative with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model answers — your places can differ.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais à la bibliothèque.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code>, and a specific destination usually needs a preposition.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons aux magasins.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> contracts to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle va chez sa tante.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez</code> for someone's home or business, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vont à Lyon.</code> — Cities usually take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> for to or in.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Negative example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vais pas à la bibliothèque.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> wrap the conjugated verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code>; the place phrase stays after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you built <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> like a regular -er verb or forgot the contraction in a phrase such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux magasins</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-the-near-future-with-aller-infinitive\">Unit 2: The near future with aller + infinitive</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code> to say that something is <strong>going to happen soon</strong>. This is called the <strong>near future</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"structure\">Structure</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Conjugated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></th>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudier</code></td>\n<td>I am going to study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vas</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code></td>\n<td>you are going to leave</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préparer</code></td>\n<td>she is going to prepare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visiter</code></td>\n<td>we are going to visit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code></td>\n<td>you are going to wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vont</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dormir</code></td>\n<td>they are going to sleep</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"how-the-tense-works\">How the tense works</h3>\n<p>The infinitive does <strong>not</strong> change. Only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> changes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais téléphoner ce soir.</code> — I am going to call tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu vas finir ce chapitre demain.</code> — You are going to finish this chapter tomorrow.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On va dîner chez des amis.</code> — We are going to have dinner at friends' house.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons visiter un village près de Québec demain.</code> — Tomorrow, we are going to visit a village near Quebec City.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous allez partir pour l'aéroport à six heures.</code> — You are going to leave for the airport at six o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vont faire leurs devoirs après le repas.</code> — They are going to do their homework after the meal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"negative-form\">Negative form</h3>\n<p>Put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> around the conjugated form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, not around the infinitive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vais pas sortir ce soir.</code> — I am not going to go out tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'allons pas prendre le train demain.</code> — We are not going to take the train tomorrow.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not insert a preposition between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> and the infinitive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais étudier.</code> — I am going to study.</li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Je vais à étudier.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Rewrite each prompt in French.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>We are going to buy bread.</li>\n<li>She is not going to stay at home.</li>\n<li>Are you going to call your parents?</li>\n<li>They are going to do the dishes after dinner.</li>\n<p>Then change sentence 1 to the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form and sentence 3 to the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons acheter du pain.</code> — Near future = conjugated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> + infinitive. Only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code> changes; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">acheter</code> stays infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne va pas rester à la maison.</code> — The negative wraps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rester</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu vas appeler tes parents ?</code> — In a yes/no question with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>, keep normal subject-verb order.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vont faire la vaisselle après le dîner.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> stays infinitive after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vont</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Changed forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous allez acheter du pain.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> requires <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que nous allons appeler nos parents ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> requires <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tes parents</code> changes naturally to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nos parents</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-de-and-their-contractions\">Unit 3: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, and their contractions</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The prepositions <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> appear everywhere in French. They often express destination, location, belonging, source, or topic. Before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, they contract.</p>\n<h3 id=\"contractions-with-definite-articles\">Contractions with definite articles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Full form</th>\n<th>Contracted form</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + le</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au cinéma</code></td>\n<td>to the movie theater / at the movie theater</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux enfants</code></td>\n<td>to the children</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + le</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du professeur</code></td>\n<td>of the teacher / from the teacher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voisins</code></td>\n<td>of the neighbors / from the neighbors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + la</code></td>\n<td>no contraction</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à la gare</code></td>\n<td>to the station</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + l'</code></td>\n<td>no contraction</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à l'hôtel</code></td>\n<td>to the hotel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + la</code></td>\n<td>no contraction</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la bibliothèque</code></td>\n<td>from the library</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + l'</code></td>\n<td>no contraction</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'aéroport</code></td>\n<td>from the airport</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-uses-of\">Common uses of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>destination: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais au bureau.</code> — I go to the office.</li>\n<li>location: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est à la maison.</code> — She is at home.</li>\n<li>indirect object with a person in some structures: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle à mon frère.</code> — I speak to my brother.</li>\n<li>time in certain expressions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au printemps</code> — in spring</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-uses-of-de\">Common uses of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>origin: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il revient du Canada.</code> — He is coming back from Canada.</li>\n<li>possession or relation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre de Marie</code> — Marie's book</li>\n<li>topic: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous parlons du film.</code> — We are talking about the movie.</li>\n<li>content or quantity in many expressions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un verre d'eau</code> — a glass of water</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants vont au musée avec leur professeur.</code> — The students go to the museum with their teacher.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sors de la classe à midi.</code> — I leave the classroom at noon.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est la voiture du voisin.</code> — It is the neighbor's car.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle des examens avec ses amis.</code> — She talks about the exams with her friends.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu vas à la banque ou au supermarché ?</code> — Are you going to the bank or to the supermarket?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous revenons des montagnes dimanche.</code> — We are coming back from the mountains on Sunday.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, the contraction creates one smooth sound group. Read <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au musée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux États-Unis</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du Canada</code> as complete chunks rather than word by word.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Never write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*à le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*à les</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*de le</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*de les</code> before a regular definite article. French requires the contracted forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Complete the pairs.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais ...</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le parc</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous parlons ...</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voisins</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle revient ...</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la pharmacie</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est le sac ...</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le professeur</code>.</li>\n<p>Then explain why each phrase uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais au parc.</code> — Motion toward a place uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le parc</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au parc</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous parlons des voisins.</code> — Talking about a topic uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voisins</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voisins</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle revient de la pharmacie.</code> — Returning from a place uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>; there is no contraction with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est le sac du professeur.</code> — Possession or relation uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le professeur</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du professeur</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The main trap is writing two separate words where French requires one contracted form: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-prepositions-of-place-and-sequence\">Unit 4: Prepositions of place and sequence</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French uses different prepositions to describe where something is located and when one action happens in relation to another. Translate the <strong>relationship</strong>, not the English word alone.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-prepositions-of-place\">Common prepositions of place</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Preposition</th>\n<th>Core meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans</code></td>\n<td>in, inside</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans le sac</code></td>\n<td>in the bag</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code></td>\n<td>on, on top of</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur la table</code></td>\n<td>on the table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sous</code></td>\n<td>under</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sous la chaise</code></td>\n<td>under the chair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devant</code></td>\n<td>in front of</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devant l'école</code></td>\n<td>in front of the school</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">derrière</code></td>\n<td>behind</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">derrière la porte</code></td>\n<td>behind the door</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">entre</code></td>\n<td>between</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">entre les deux maisons</code></td>\n<td>between the two houses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez</code></td>\n<td>at the home/business of</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez le médecin</code></td>\n<td>at the doctor's office</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près de</code></td>\n<td>near</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près de la rivière</code></td>\n<td>near the river</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">loin de</code></td>\n<td>far from</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">loin du centre</code></td>\n<td>far from downtown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en face de</code></td>\n<td>opposite, across from</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en face du café</code></td>\n<td>across from the café</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-prepositions-of-time-and-sequence\">Common prepositions of time and sequence</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Expression</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de + infinitive</code></td>\n<td>before doing something</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de partir</code></td>\n<td>before leaving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">après</code></td>\n<td>after</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">après le cours</code></td>\n<td>after class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pendant</code></td>\n<td>during, for</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pendant le voyage</code></td>\n<td>during the trip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jusqu'à</code></td>\n<td>until, up to</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jusqu'à demain</code></td>\n<td>until tomorrow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vers</code></td>\n<td>around, toward (time/direction)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vers huit heures</code></td>\n<td>around eight o'clock</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les clés sont sur la table, derrière le livre.</code> — The keys are on the table, behind the book.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le chat dort sous la chaise.</code> — The cat is sleeping under the chair.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous attendons devant le musée.</code> — We are waiting in front of the museum.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle habite chez ses grands-parents pendant l'été.</code> — She stays at her grandparents' house during the summer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais téléphoner avant de sortir.</code> — I am going to call before going out.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vont travailler jusqu'à neuf heures.</code> — They are going to work until nine o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On arrive vers midi.</code> — We arrive around noon.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> for every English \"in\" or \"at.\" Compare:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Paris</code> — in Paris</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans la voiture</code> — in the car</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez Paul</code> — at Paul's place</li>\n<p>The correct preposition depends on the relationship, not on one English word.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Answer each question in a complete sentence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Où sont tes chaussures ? Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devant</code>.</li>\n<li>Où vas-tu après le cours ? Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez</code>.</li>\n<li>Que vas-tu faire avant de dormir ? Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de + infinitive</code>.</li>\n<li>Pendant quelle saison fais-tu le plus de promenades ? Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pendant</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Sample answers — yours can be true for you.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes chaussures sont sous la table.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sous</code> gives the location under something.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Après le cours, je vais chez mon ami.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez</code> for a person's home.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avant de dormir, je vais lire un chapitre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de</code> must be followed by an infinitive, here <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dormir</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je fais le plus de promenades pendant l'été.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pendant</code> marks the season during which the activity happens.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you translate every English in or at with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>. The correct preposition depends on the relationship: under, at someone's home, before doing, or during.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-geography-and-questions-after-prepositions\">Unit 5: Geography and questions after prepositions</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>When you talk about cities and countries, French chooses the preposition according to the type of place. When you ask a question after a preposition, French often keeps the preposition directly before the question word.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cities-countries-and-regions\">Cities, countries, and regions</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type of place</th>\n<th>To / in</th>\n<th>From</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>city</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Montréal</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de Montréal</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine country</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en France</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine country</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Japon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du Japon</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>country starting with vowel sound</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en Iran</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'Iran</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plural country</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux Pays-Bas</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des Pays-Bas</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>many regions/islands</td>\n<td>varies; learn phrase</td>\n<td>varies</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en Provence</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Québec</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient de France, mais elle habite au Canada.</code> — She comes from France, but she lives in Canada.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons en Italie au mois de mai.</code> — We are going to Italy in May.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils reviennent du Mexique samedi.</code> — They are coming back from Mexico on Saturday.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous travaillez aux États-Unis maintenant ?</code> — Are you working in the United States now?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes amis viennent des Pays-Bas.</code> — My friends come from the Netherlands.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis à Lyon, mais ma sœur est en Belgique.</code> — I am in Lyon, but my sister is in Belgium.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"questions-after-prepositions\">Questions after prepositions</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Preposition + question word</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à qui</code></td>\n<td>to whom</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À qui écris-tu ?</code> — To whom are you writing?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de qui</code></td>\n<td>from/about whom</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De qui parlez-vous ?</code> — About whom are you speaking?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à quoi</code></td>\n<td>to what</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quoi penses-tu ?</code> — What are you thinking about?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de quoi</code></td>\n<td>of/about what</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De quoi avez-vous besoin ?</code> — What do you need?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec qui</code></td>\n<td>with whom</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec qui allez-vous dîner ?</code> — With whom are you going to have dinner?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour quoi</code></td>\n<td>for what</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pour quoi est-ce que tu paies ?</code> — What are you paying for?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"forms-of-lequel-after-a-preposition\">Forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code> after a preposition</h3>\n<p>Use forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code> after a preposition when you mean <strong>which one</strong> or <strong>which ones</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code></td>\n<td>which one (masculine singular)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lequel de ces trains vas-tu prendre ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">laquelle</code></td>\n<td>which one (feminine singular)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Laquelle de ces rues mène au centre ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquels</code></td>\n<td>which ones (masculine/mixed plural)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lesquels de ces livres parles-tu ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquelles</code></td>\n<td>which ones (feminine plural)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lesquelles de ces photos viens-tu de choisir ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>After <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, forms contract just as articles do.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Base form</th>\n<th>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code></th>\n<th>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auquel</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">duquel</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">laquelle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à laquelle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de laquelle</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquels</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auxquels</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">desquels</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquelles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auxquelles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">desquelles</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À laquelle de ces dates pensez-vous ?</code> — Which of these dates are you thinking about?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le pont sous lequel nous passons est très ancien.</code> — The bridge under which we are passing is very old.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici les dossiers auxquels le directeur répond ce soir.</code> — Here are the files to which the director is replying tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne sais pas de laquelle de ces routes tu parles.</code> — I do not know which of these roads you are talking about.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*à France</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*de le Canada</code>. Geography phrases are fixed chunks: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en France</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du Canada</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux Pays-Bas</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de Montréal</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Translate and say aloud.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>We are going to Belgium in July.</li>\n<li>He comes from Japan.</li>\n<li>What are you thinking about?</li>\n<li>Which chair are you sitting on?</li>\n<p>For sentence 4, make two versions: one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur quelle chaise</code> and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur laquelle</code>.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons en Belgique en juillet.</code> — Belgium is treated as feminine here, so to or in Belgium is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en Belgique</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il vient du Japon.</code> — Japan is masculine, so from Japan is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du Japon</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quoi penses-tu ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">penser à</code> keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before the question word.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Sur quelle chaise est-ce que tu es assis ?</code> — This asks with the full noun phrase <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelle chaise</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Sur laquelle est-ce que tu es assis ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">laquelle</code> replaces the feminine singular noun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaise</code> after the preposition <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The trap is losing the preposition in the question. French keeps it: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à quoi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur quelle chaise</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur laquelle</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-6-the-verb-faire-weather-and-common-expressions\">Unit 6: The verb faire, weather, and common expressions</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Faire</code> means <strong>to do</strong> or <strong>to make</strong>, but in French it also appears in weather expressions and many daily activities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-of-faire\">Present tense of faire</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>English idea</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fais</code></td>\n<td>I do / I make</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fais</code></td>\n<td>you do / you make</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code></td>\n<td>he/she/one does / makes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisons</code></td>\n<td>we do / we make</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faites</code></td>\n<td>you do / you make</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">font</code></td>\n<td>they do / they make</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"weather-expressions-with-faire\">Weather expressions with faire</h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il fait...</code> for many statements about weather.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait beau.</code></td>\n<td>The weather is nice.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait mauvais.</code></td>\n<td>The weather is bad.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait chaud.</code></td>\n<td>It is hot.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid.</code></td>\n<td>It is cold.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait frais.</code></td>\n<td>It is cool.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait du vent.</code></td>\n<td>It is windy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait quel temps ?</code></td>\n<td>What is the weather like?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-expressions-with-faire\">Common expressions with faire</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Expression</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire attention</code></td>\n<td>to pay attention / be careful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire la cuisine</code></td>\n<td>to cook</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire les courses</code></td>\n<td>to do the shopping / grocery shopping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire ses devoirs</code></td>\n<td>to do one's homework</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire du vélo</code></td>\n<td>to ride a bicycle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire une promenade</code></td>\n<td>to take a walk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire du sport</code></td>\n<td>to play sports / exercise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire la vaisselle</code></td>\n<td>to do the dishes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.</code> — It is cold, so I am going to put on my coat.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous faisons les courses le samedi matin.</code> — We do the shopping on Saturday morning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu fais du vélo près du lac ?</code> — Do you ride your bike near the lake?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Faites attention devant cette porte.</code> — Be careful in front of that door.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles font leurs devoirs avant de sortir.</code> — They do their homework before going out.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On fait une promenade après le dîner.</code> — We take a walk after dinner.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisons</code> is pronounced like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">z</code>: <strong>feh-zon</strong>. This makes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous faisons</code> easy to recognize by ear.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not translate weather with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> in these expressions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait chaud.</code> — It is hot.</li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Il est chaud.</code> in a weather context</li>\n<p>Also learn activity phrases as complete chunks: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire du vélo</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*faire vélo</code>.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Answer with a full sentence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Quel temps fait-il aujourd'hui ?</li>\n<li>Que fais-tu avant le dîner ?</li>\n<li>Est-ce que vous faites du sport le week-end ?</li>\n<li>Que vont faire tes amis demain ? Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire les courses</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire une promenade</code>, or another <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> expression.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait beau aujourd'hui.</code> — Weather expressions commonly use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il fait</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avant le dîner, je fais la vaisselle.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire la vaisselle</code> is a fixed activity phrase.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, nous faisons du sport le week-end.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisons</code>; the activity phrase is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire du sport</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes amis vont faire les courses demain.</code> — In the near future, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vont</code> is conjugated and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> stays infinitive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you wrote a weather sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, or if you dropped the article in fixed expressions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire du vélo</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire les courses</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine motion, prepositions, geography, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> the way they appear in real conversation. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous allons visiter un village près de Québec.</code> — Tomorrow, we are going to visit a village near Quebec City.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient de France, mais elle habite au Canada.</code> — She comes from France, but she lives in Canada.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.</code> — It is cold, so I am going to put on my coat.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quelle heure allez-vous partir pour l'aéroport ?</code> — What time are you going to leave for the airport?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants jouent dans le jardin derrière la maison.</code> — The children are playing in the garden behind the house.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.</code> — We are going to do the shopping before going back home.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Use this checklist after writing the sentences from memory.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aller plus infinitive.</strong> Sentence 1 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons visiter</code>, sentence 3 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais mettre</code>, sentence 4 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez partir</code>, and sentence 6 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons faire</code>. Only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> changes; the infinitive stays unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Preposition chunks.</strong> Copy small phrases exactly: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près de Québec</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour l'aéroport</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans le jardin</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">derrière la maison</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de rentrer</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez nous</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Contractions.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code> is not two words, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez nous</code> does not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Weather.</strong> Sentence 3 uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est froid</code> for weather.</li>\n<li><strong>Question formatting.</strong> Sentence 4 uses inversion in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez-vous</code> and needs a space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the preposition is wrong, the sentence may still be understandable, but the chapter skill is not mastered. Rewrite the whole phrase, not just the isolated preposition.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence above:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle the conjugated verb or verbs;</li>\n<li>underline every prepositional phrase;</li>\n<li>label each use of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> as either <strong>movement</strong> or <strong>near future</strong>;</li>\n<li>find the one weather expression and the one geography phrase.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Conjugated verb or verbs</th>\n<th>Prepositional phrases</th>\n<th>Use of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près de Québec</code></td>\n<td>near future in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons visiter</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vient</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habite</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code></td>\n<td>none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>near future in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais mettre</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quelle heure</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour l'aéroport</code></td>\n<td>near future in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez-vous partir</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jouent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans le jardin</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">derrière la maison</code></td>\n<td>none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de rentrer</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez nous</code></td>\n<td>near future in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons faire</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The weather expression is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid</code>. The geography phrase is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code> in sentence 2. Sentence 1 also contains <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près de Québec</code>, a place phrase with a city.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the destination, time, country, or weather.</li>\n<p>Then rewrite sentence 6 in the negative.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked rewrites for sentences 1-4:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, je vais visiter un village près de Québec.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous allons visiter un musée près de Montréal.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils viennent de France, mais ils habitent au Canada.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient d'Italie, mais elle habite aux États-Unis.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait chaud, alors nous allons mettre des lunettes de soleil.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait beau, alors je vais faire une promenade.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quelle heure vas-tu partir pour l'aéroport ?</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quelle heure allez-vous partir pour la gare ?</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Negative sentence 6: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'allons pas faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.</code> — the negative wraps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rentrer</code> stay infinitives.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write five new sentences about your own routine or travel plans. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with a country or city;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model check for the cumulative practice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous allons visiter un village près de Québec.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons visiter</code> is near future: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code> is conjugated, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visiter</code> is infinitive. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près de Québec</code> is a place phrase.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient de France, mais elle habite au Canada.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code> is origin; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code> is location in a masculine country.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid</code> is the weather expression; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais mettre</code> is near future.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quelle heure allez-vous partir pour l'aéroport ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez partir</code> is near future, and the question keeps the prepositional phrase <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour l'aéroport</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants jouent dans le jardin derrière la maison.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans le jardin</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">derrière la maison</code> are place phrases.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons faire</code> is near future; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de rentrer</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + infinitive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez nous</code> means our place or home.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Negative rewrite of sentence 6: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'allons pas faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.</code> — The negative wraps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>.</p>\n<p>Personal answers vary. Check that every use of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code> keeps the second verb unchanged, every country phrase follows the geography chart, and every <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> expression is learned as a complete chunk.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-planning-a-weekend-trip\">Mini-dialogue: planning a weekend trip</h2>\n<p><strong>Camille :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Alors, où est-ce qu'on va ce week-end ?</code> — So, where are we going this weekend? <strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On va à Québec samedi matin, puis on va passer la nuit chez ma cousine.</code> — We are going to Quebec City on Saturday morning, then we are going to spend the night at my cousin's place. <strong>Camille :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu viens toujours de Montréal en voiture ?</code> — Are you still coming from Montreal by car? <strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, mais je vais d'abord m'arrêter au marché.</code> — Yes, but I am going to stop at the market first. <strong>Camille :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quel temps va-t-il faire là-bas ?</code> — What is the weather going to be like there? <strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On dit qu'il va faire beau, mais il va faire frais le soir.</code> — They say the weather is going to be nice, but it is going to be cool in the evening. <strong>Camille :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parfait. Avant de partir, je vais acheter du café et du pain.</code> — Perfect. Before leaving, I am going to buy coffee and bread. <strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Très bien. À quelle station de bus vas-tu m'attendre ?</code> — Very good. At which bus station are you going to wait for me?</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find every form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> and decide whether it means simple movement or the near future.</li>\n<li>Explain why the dialogue uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Québec</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez ma cousine</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de Montréal</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code>.</li>\n<li>Find the weather expressions and explain why they use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite Nora's second line with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code>, making every necessary change.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à quelle station</code> in the last line with a version using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">laquelle</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on va</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où est-ce qu'on va</code> means movement; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On va à Québec</code> means movement; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on va passer</code> is near future; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais m'arrêter</code> is near future; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va-t-il faire</code> is near future with weather; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il va faire beau</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il va faire frais</code> are near future; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais acheter</code> is near future; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vas-tu m'attendre</code> is near future.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Québec</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> because Québec is a city. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez ma cousine</code> means at my cousin's home. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de Montréal</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> for origin from a city. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code> is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> plus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le marché</code>, so it contracts to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code>.</li>\n<li>Weather expressions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quel temps va-t-il faire là-bas ?</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il va faire beau</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il va faire frais</code>. French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> for these weather chunks, and here <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> puts them in the near future.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons à Québec samedi matin, puis nous allons passer la nuit chez ma cousine.</code> — changing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> changes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>; the infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">passer</code> stays unchanged.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À laquelle des stations de bus vas-tu m'attendre ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">station</code> is feminine singular, so the form is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">laquelle</code>; the preposition <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> stays in front.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-une-semaine-de-voyage\">Connected reading: une semaine de voyage</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La semaine prochaine, nous allons passer quatre jours au Québec. Nous partons de Toronto lundi matin et nous arrivons à Montréal vers midi. Pendant les deux premiers jours, nous allons rester chez des amis qui habitent près du centre. Ensuite, nous allons faire une excursion dans un petit village au bord du fleuve. S'il fait beau, nous allons faire une longue promenade après le déjeuner ; s'il fait froid, nous allons visiter un musée. Avant de rentrer, je veux acheter des cadeaux pour ma famille.</code> — Next week, we are going to spend four days in Quebec. We are leaving Toronto on Monday morning and arriving in Montreal around noon. During the first two days, we are going to stay with friends who live near downtown. Then we are going to take a trip to a small village on the river. If the weather is nice, we are going to take a long walk after lunch; if it is cold, we are going to visit a museum. Before going back, I want to buy gifts for my family.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List every destination phrase and every origin phrase in the reading.</li>\n<li>Find two uses of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code> and explain the infinitive that follows each one.</li>\n<li>Underline the place expressions built with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au bord de</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez</code>.</li>\n<li>Change the reading from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> for the first three sentences.</li>\n<li>Write three true travel sentences of your own using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Destination and location phrases include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Québec</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Montréal</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez des amis</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans un petit village au bord du fleuve</code>. The clear origin phrase is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de Toronto</code>.</li>\n<li>Uses of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons passer</code> has infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">passer</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons rester</code> has infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rester</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons faire</code> has infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons visiter</code> has infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visiter</code>. In each case, only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code> is conjugated.</li>\n<li>Place expressions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez des amis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près du centre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au bord du fleuve</code>. Notice the contraction in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du fleuve</code>, from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> plus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le fleuve</code>.</li>\n<li>First three sentences in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> form: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La semaine prochaine, je vais passer quatre jours au Québec. Je pars de Toronto lundi matin et j'arrive à Montréal vers midi. Pendant les deux premiers jours, je vais rester chez des amis qui habitent près du centre.</code></li>\n<li>Answers vary. Models: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais passer deux jours à Montréal.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars de Vancouver lundi matin.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avant de rentrer, je vais acheter un cadeau au marché.</code> Check every city, country, and sequence phrase against the chapter chart.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Mixing up motion and destination prepositions.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller à Paris</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de Paris</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting contractions.</strong> Write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au bureau</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du Canada</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*à le bureau</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*de le Canada</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> with a finite verb.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais partir</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je vais je pars</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Using the wrong preposition with countries.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en France</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Japon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux Pays-Bas</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> for weather.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il fait froid</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*il est froid</code> for the weather.</li>\n<li><strong>Overusing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans</code> for every kind of location.</strong> Compare <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans la voiture</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à la gare</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez mes amis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur la table</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ignoring the preposition in a question.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À qui ?</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De quoi ?</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec qui ?</code>, not only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui ?</code> when the verb requires a preposition.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"aller-and-faire-side-by-side\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, side by side</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vas</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faites</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vont</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">font</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"contractions-with-and-de\">Contractions with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + le</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + le</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + l'</code></td>\n<td>no contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + l'</code></td>\n<td>no contraction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"geography-quick-reference\">Geography quick reference</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Place type</th>\n<th>To / in</th>\n<th>From</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>city</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine country</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine country</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plural country</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vowel-opening country</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"preposition-question-patterns\">Preposition-question patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à qui</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À qui téléphones-tu ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de quoi</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De quoi parle-t-il ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec qui</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec qui sors-tu ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auquel / à laquelle / auxquels / auxquelles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Auquel penses-tu ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">duquel / de laquelle / desquels / desquelles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Duquel parlez-vous ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-habits\">Core habits</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Learn prepositions inside useful phrases, not as isolated translation equivalents.</li>\n<li>Check whether a place is a city, a feminine country, a masculine country, or a plural country.</li>\n<li>In the near future, change only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>; keep the infinitive unchanged.</li>\n<li>Treat weather expressions with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> as fixed chunks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a short trip, weekend plan, or busy day. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> used once for movement and twice in the near future;</li>\n<li>at least four different prepositions of place or sequence;</li>\n<li>one city and two countries or regions with correct prepositions;</li>\n<li>one question built after a preposition;</li>\n<li>two expressions with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, including one weather expression;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de + infinitive</code>.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>box every preposition;</li>\n<li>check each geography phrase against the reference chart;</li>\n<li>underline the infinitive after each use of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code>;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any sentence you corrected from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is a model paragraph that satisfies every requirement:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Samedi matin, je vais à Montréal avec deux amis. Nous allons visiter un musée près du centre. Ensuite, nous allons faire une promenade dans le vieux quartier. Ma sœur vient de France, mais elle habite au Canada depuis un an. Dimanche, nous allons parler avec des amis aux États-Unis par vidéo. Il fait froid, alors je vais mettre un manteau. Avant de partir, je vais acheter du café au marché. Chez ma cousine, nous faisons la cuisine le soir. Avec qui est-ce que tu vas voyager cet été ?</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais à Montréal</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> for movement; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons visiter</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons faire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais mettre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais acheter</code> use near future; prepositions include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près du centre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dans le vieux quartier</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux États-Unis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez ma cousine</code>; geography includes Montréal, France, Canada, and the United States; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec qui</code> is a question after a preposition; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous faisons la cuisine</code> use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avant de partir</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de</code> plus infinitive.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if any of these appear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à le</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de le</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais je visite</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais visiter</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à France</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de le Canada</code>;</li>\n<li>a weather sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>;</li>\n<li>a preposition question that drops the preposition, such as asking only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui ?</code> when you mean <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec qui ?</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 6 lessons: aller, prepositions, geography, and faire this chapter expands what you can do with motion, place, and everyday action. you will learn how french talks about going somewhere, where things are located, where people come from, what is about to happen, and what someone is doing when the verb `faire` appears in weather, activities, and common expressions. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. conjugate `aller` confidently in the present tense; 2. use `aller + infinitive` to talk about the near future; 3. choose between `à` and `de` and form the contractions `au`, `aux`, `du`, and `des`; 4. use common prepositions of location and sequence, including `chez`, `entre`, `devant`, and `avant de`; 5. ask and answer questions built after prepositions, including `à qui`, `de quoi`, and forms of `lequel`; 6. choose the correct preposition with cities, countries, and regions; 7. conjugate `faire` and use it for weather, activities, and fixed expressions. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - conjugate `être` and `avoir` in the present tense; - make negative sentences with `ne...pas`; - recognize singular and plural noun phrases; - understand that a french infinitive is the dictionary form of the verb, such as `parler`, `finir`, or `prendre`. you will use all of those skills in this chapter. for example, `nous allons partir`, `il ne va pas venir`, and `elles font attention` all depend on earlier chapters. check these five basics before you begin. 1. give `avoir` with `nous`. — `nous avons`. you will need this kind of irregular form beside `aller` and `faire`. 2. make `je vais partir` negative. — `je ne vais pas partir`. the negative wraps the conjugated verb `vais`, not the infinitive `partir`. 3. is `les livres` singular or plural? — plural. plural noun phrases trigger contractions such as `aux` and `des` when they follow `à` or `de`. 4. find the infinitive in `elles vont faire attention`. — `faire`. the infinitive is the unchanged dictionary form after the conjugated `vont`. 5. give `être` with `il`. — `il est`. you still need `être`, but weather expressions in this chapter usually use `faire`, as in `il fait froid`. if you missed two or more, review the earlier verb and negation chapters first. this chapter has many small words, and they depend on recognizing the verb and noun phrase accurately. ## how to study this chapter say the conjugation tables aloud before you try to memorize them silently. with prepositions, always learn a short phrase rather than a single word: `au canada`, `chez mes parents`, `avant de sortir`, `faire du vélo`. ## unit 1: the verb aller in the present tense ### core idea `aller` means **to go** and is one of the most useful irregular verbs in french. learn all six forms as a complete block. ### present tense of aller | pronoun | form | english idea | |---|---|---| | `je` | `vais` | i go / i am going | | `tu` | `vas` | you go / you are going | | `il/elle/on` | `va` | he/she/one goes | | `nous` | `allons` | we go | | `vous` | `allez` | you go | | `ils/elles` | `vont` | they go | ### main uses of aller use `aller` to talk about movement to a destination or about how things are going in a general sense. - `je vais à la bibliothèque après le déjeuner.` — i go to the library after lunch. - `tu vas souvent chez ta sœur le dimanche ?` — do you often go to your sister's place on sundays? - `nous allons au marché très tôt.` — we go to the market very early. - `ils vont en ville à pied.` — they go into town on foot. - `comment va ton grand-père ?` — how is your grandfather doing? - `ça va bien aujourd'hui.` — it is going well today. ### pronunciation note the forms `vais`, `vas`, and `va` all sound different from their spelling suggests: - `vais` sounds roughly like **vè**; - `vas` sounds like **va**; - `va` sounds like **va**; - `vont` ends with a silent `-nt`. the written endings are irregular, so you cannot predict them from a regular pattern. memorize them by sound and by sight. ### common mistake for english speakers do not try to build `aller` from the endings of a regular `-er` verb. incorrect forms such as `*je alle` or `*nous allonss` happen when learners guess instead of recalling the full irregular set. ### sentence-building practice complete each idea aloud, then write it. 1. `je vais ...` with a place in your town. 2. `nous allons ...` with a plural destination. 3. `elle va ...` with a person's home using `chez`. 4. `ils vont ...` with a city. then turn one sentence negative with `ne...pas`. model answers — your places can differ. 1. `je vais à la bibliothèque.` — `je` takes `vais`, and a specific destination usually needs a preposition. 2. `nous allons aux magasins.` — `nous` takes `allons`; `à` + `les` contracts to `aux`. 3. `elle va chez sa tante.` — use `chez` for someone's home or business, not `à`. 4. `ils vont à lyon.` — cities usually take `à` for to or in. negative example: - `je ne vais pas à la bibliothèque.` — `ne` and `pas` wrap the conjugated verb `vais`; the place phrase stays after it. mark yourself wrong if you built `aller` like a regular -er verb or forgot the contraction in a phrase such as `aux magasins`. ## unit 2: the near future with aller + infinitive ### core idea french uses `aller + infinitive` to say that something is **going to happen soon**. this is called the **near future**. ### structure | subject | conjugated `aller` | infinitive | meaning | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `vais` | `étudier` | i am going to study | | `tu` | `vas` | `partir` | you are going to leave | | `elle` | `va` | `préparer` | she is going to prepare | | `nous` | `allons` | `visiter` | we are going to visit | | `vous` | `allez` | `attendre` | you are going to wait | | `ils` | `vont` | `dormir` | they are going to sleep | ### how the tense works the infinitive does **not** change. only `aller` changes. - `je vais téléphoner ce soir.` — i am going to call tonight. - `tu vas finir ce chapitre demain.` — you are going to finish this chapter tomorrow. - `on va dîner chez des amis.` — we are going to have dinner at friends' house. - `nous allons visiter un village près de québec demain.` — tomorrow, we are going to visit a village near quebec city. - `vous allez partir pour l'aéroport à six heures.` — you are going to leave for the airport at six o'clock. - `ils vont faire leurs devoirs après le repas.` — they are going to do their homework after the meal. ### negative form put `ne...pas` around the conjugated form of `aller`, not around the infinitive. - `je ne vais pas sortir ce soir.` — i am not going to go out tonight. - `nous n'allons pas prendre le train demain.` — we are not going to take the train tomorrow. ### common mistake for english speakers do not insert a preposition between `aller` and the infinitive. - correct: `je vais étudier.` — i am going to study. - incorrect: `*je vais à étudier.` ### sentence-building practice rewrite each prompt in french. 1. we are going to buy bread. 2. she is not going to stay at home. 3. are you going to call your parents? 4. they are going to do the dishes after dinner. then change sentence 1 to the `vous` form and sentence 3 to the `nous` form. 1. `nous allons acheter du pain.` — near future = conjugated `aller` + infinitive. only `allons` changes; `acheter` stays infinitive. 2. `elle ne va pas rester à la maison.` — the negative wraps `va`, not `rester`. 3. `est-ce que tu vas appeler tes parents ?` — in a yes/no question with `est-ce que`, keep normal subject-verb order. 4. `ils vont faire la vaisselle après le dîner.` — `faire` stays infinitive after `vont`. changed forms: - `vous allez acheter du pain.` — `vous` requires `allez`. - `est-ce que nous allons appeler nos parents ?` — `nous` requires `allons`, and `tes parents` changes naturally to `nos parents`. ## unit 3: `à`, `de`, and their contractions ### core idea the prepositions `à` and `de` appear everywhere in french. they often express destination, location, belonging, source, or topic. before `le` and `les`, they contract. ### contractions with definite articles | full form | contracted form | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `à + le` | `au` | `au cinéma` | to the movie theater / at the movie theater | | `à + les` | `aux` | `aux enfants` | to the children | | `de + le` | `du` | `du professeur` | of the teacher / from the teacher | | `de + les` | `des` | `des voisins` | of the neighbors / from the neighbors | | `à + la` | no contraction | `à la gare` | to the station | | `à + l'` | no contraction | `à l'hôtel` | to the hotel | | `de + la` | no contraction | `de la bibliothèque` | from the library | | `de + l'` | no contraction | `de l'aéroport` | from the airport | ### common uses of `à` - destination: `je vais au bureau.` — i go to the office. - location: `elle est à la maison.` — she is at home. - indirect object with a person in some structures: `je parle à mon frère.` — i speak to my brother. - time in certain expressions: `au printemps` — in spring ### common uses of `de` - origin: `il revient du canada.` — he is coming back from canada. - possession or relation: `le livre de marie` — marie's book - topic: `nous parlons du film.` — we are talking about the movie. - content or quantity in many expressions: `un verre d'eau` — a glass of water ### examples in context - `les étudiants vont au musée avec leur professeur.` — the students go to the museum with their teacher. - `je sors de la classe à midi.` — i leave the classroom at noon. - `c'est la voiture du voisin.` — it is the neighbor's car. - `elle parle des examens avec ses amis.` — she talks about the exams with her friends. - `tu vas à la banque ou au supermarché ?` — are you going to the bank or to the supermarket? - `nous revenons des montagnes dimanche.` — we are coming back from the mountains on sunday. ### pronunciation note in `au`, `aux`, and `du`, the contraction creates one smooth sound group. read `au musée`, `aux états-unis`, and `du canada` as complete chunks rather than word by word. ### common mistake for english speakers never write `*à le`, `*à les`, `*de le`, or `*de les` before a regular definite article. french requires the contracted forms. ### sentence-building practice complete the pairs. 1. `je vais ...` with `le parc`. 2. `nous parlons ...` with `les voisins`. 3. `elle revient ...` with `la pharmacie`. 4. `c'est le sac ...` with `le professeur`. then explain why each phrase uses `à` or `de`. 1. `je vais au parc.` — motion toward a place uses `à`; `à` + `le parc` becomes `au parc`. 2. `nous parlons des voisins.` — talking about a topic uses `de`; `de` + `les voisins` becomes `des voisins`. 3. `elle revient de la pharmacie.` — returning from a place uses `de`; there is no contraction with `la`. 4. `c'est le sac du professeur.` — possession or relation uses `de`; `de` + `le professeur` becomes `du professeur`. the main trap is writing two separate words where french requires one contracted form: `au`, `aux`, `du`, or `des`. ## unit 4: prepositions of place and sequence ### core idea french uses different prepositions to describe where something is located and when one action happens in relation to another. translate the **relationship**, not the english word alone. ### common prepositions of place | preposition | core meaning | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `dans` | in, inside | `dans le sac` | in the bag | | `sur` | on, on top of | `sur la table` | on the table | | `sous` | under | `sous la chaise` | under the chair | | `devant` | in front of | `devant l'école` | in front of the school | | `derrière` | behind | `derrière la porte` | behind the door | | `entre` | between | `entre les deux maisons` | between the two houses | | `chez` | at the home/business of | `chez le médecin` | at the doctor's office | | `près de` | near | `près de la rivière` | near the river | | `loin de` | far from | `loin du centre` | far from downtown | | `en face de` | opposite, across from | `en face du café` | across from the café | ### common prepositions of time and sequence | expression | use | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `avant de + infinitive` | before doing something | `avant de partir` | before leaving | | `après` | after | `après le cours` | after class | | `pendant` | during, for | `pendant le voyage` | during the trip | | `jusqu'à` | until, up to | `jusqu'à demain` | until tomorrow | | `vers` | around, toward (time/direction) | `vers huit heures` | around eight o'clock | ### examples in context - `les clés sont sur la table, derrière le livre.` — the keys are on the table, behind the book. - `le chat dort sous la chaise.` — the cat is sleeping under the chair. - `nous attendons devant le musée.` — we are waiting in front of the museum. - `elle habite chez ses grands-parents pendant l'été.` — she stays at her grandparents' house during the summer. - `je vais téléphoner avant de sortir.` — i am going to call before going out. - `ils vont travailler jusqu'à neuf heures.` — they are going to work until nine o'clock. - `on arrive vers midi.` — we arrive around noon. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use `à` for every english \"in\" or \"at.\" compare: - `à paris` — in paris - `dans la voiture` — in the car - `chez paul` — at paul's place the correct preposition depends on the relationship, not on one english word. ### sentence-building practice answer each question in a complete sentence. 1. où sont tes chaussures ? use `sous`, `dans`, or `devant`. 2. où vas-tu après le cours ? use `chez`. 3. que vas-tu faire avant de dormir ? use `avant de + infinitive`. 4. pendant quelle saison fais-tu le plus de promenades ? use `pendant`. sample answers — yours can be true for you. 1. `mes chaussures sont sous la table.` — `sous` gives the location under something. 2. `après le cours, je vais chez mon ami.` — use `chez` for a person's home. 3. `avant de dormir, je vais lire un chapitre.` — `avant de` must be followed by an infinitive, here `dormir`. 4. `je fais le plus de promenades pendant l'été.` — `pendant` marks the season during which the activity happens. mark yourself wrong if you translate every english in or at with `à`. the correct preposition depends on the relationship: under, at someone's home, before doing, or during. ## unit 5: geography and questions after prepositions ### core idea when you talk about cities and countries, french chooses the preposition according to the type of place. when you ask a question after a preposition, french often keeps the preposition directly before the question word. ### cities, countries, and regions | type of place | to / in | from | example | |---|---|---|---| | city | `à` | `de` | `à montréal`, `de montréal` | | feminine country | `en` | `de` / `d'` | `en france`, `de france` | | masculine country | `au` | `du` | `au japon`, `du japon` | | country starting with vowel sound | `en` | `d'` | `en iran`, `d'iran` | | plural country | `aux` | `des` | `aux pays-bas`, `des pays-bas` | | many regions/islands | varies; learn phrase | varies | `en provence`, `au québec` | ### examples in context - `elle vient de france, mais elle habite au canada.` — she comes from france, but she lives in canada. - `nous allons en italie au mois de mai.` — we are going to italy in may. - `ils reviennent du mexique samedi.` — they are coming back from mexico on saturday. - `vous travaillez aux états-unis maintenant ?` — are you working in the united states now? - `mes amis viennent des pays-bas.` — my friends come from the netherlands. - `je suis à lyon, mais ma sœur est en belgique.` — i am in lyon, but my sister is in belgium. ### questions after prepositions | preposition + question word | use | example | |---|---|---| | `à qui` | to whom | `à qui écris-tu ?` — to whom are you writing? | | `de qui` | from/about whom | `de qui parlez-vous ?` — about whom are you speaking? | | `à quoi` | to what | `à quoi penses-tu ?` — what are you thinking about? | | `de quoi` | of/about what | `de quoi avez-vous besoin ?` — what do you need? | | `avec qui` | with whom | `avec qui allez-vous dîner ?` — with whom are you going to have dinner? | | `pour quoi` | for what | `pour quoi est-ce que tu paies ?` — what are you paying for? | ### forms of `lequel` after a preposition use forms of `lequel` after a preposition when you mean **which one** or **which ones**. | form | meaning | example | |---|---|---| | `lequel` | which one (masculine singular) | `lequel de ces trains vas-tu prendre ?` | | `laquelle` | which one (feminine singular) | `laquelle de ces rues mène au centre ?` | | `lesquels` | which ones (masculine/mixed plural) | `lesquels de ces livres parles-tu ?` | | `lesquelles` | which ones (feminine plural) | `lesquelles de ces photos viens-tu de choisir ?` | after `à` and `de`, forms contract just as articles do. | base form | with `à` | with `de` | |---|---|---| | `lequel` | `auquel` | `duquel` | | `laquelle` | `à laquelle` | `de laquelle` | | `lesquels` | `auxquels` | `desquels` | | `lesquelles` | `auxquelles` | `desquelles` | - `à laquelle de ces dates pensez-vous ?` — which of these dates are you thinking about? - `le pont sous lequel nous passons est très ancien.` — the bridge under which we are passing is very old. - `voici les dossiers auxquels le directeur répond ce soir.` — here are the files to which the director is replying tonight. - `je ne sais pas de laquelle de ces routes tu parles.` — i do not know which of these roads you are talking about. ### common mistake for english speakers do not say `*à france` or `*de le canada`. geography phrases are fixed chunks: `en france`, `du canada`, `aux pays-bas`, `de montréal`. ### sentence-building practice translate and say aloud. 1. we are going to belgium in july. 2. he comes from japan. 3. what are you thinking about? 4. which chair are you sitting on? for sentence 4, make two versions: one with `sur quelle chaise` and one with `sur laquelle`. 1. `nous allons en belgique en juillet.` — belgium is treated as feminine here, so to or in belgium is `en belgique`. 2. `il vient du japon.` — japan is masculine, so from japan is `du japon`. 3. `à quoi penses-tu ?` — `penser à` keeps `à` before the question word. 4. `sur quelle chaise est-ce que tu es assis ?` — this asks with the full noun phrase `quelle chaise`. 5. `sur laquelle est-ce que tu es assis ?` — `laquelle` replaces the feminine singular noun `chaise` after the preposition `sur`. the trap is losing the preposition in the question. french keeps it: `à quoi`, `sur quelle chaise`, `sur laquelle`. ## unit 6: the verb faire, weather, and common expressions ### core idea `faire` means **to do** or **to make**, but in french it also appears in weather expressions and many daily activities. ### present tense of faire | pronoun | form | english idea | |---|---|---| | `je` | `fais` | i do / i make | | `tu` | `fais` | you do / you make | | `il/elle/on` | `fait` | he/she/one does / makes | | `nous` | `faisons` | we do / we make | | `vous` | `faites` | you do / you make | | `ils/elles` | `font` | they do / they make | ### weather expressions with faire use `il fait...` for many statements about weather. | french | english | |---|---| | `il fait beau.` | the weather is nice. | | `il fait mauvais.` | the weather is bad. | | `il fait chaud.` | it is hot. | | `il fait froid.` | it is cold. | | `il fait frais.` | it is cool. | | `il fait du vent.` | it is windy. | | `il fait quel temps ?` | what is the weather like? | ### common expressions with faire | expression | meaning | |---|---| | `faire attention` | to pay attention / be careful | | `faire la cuisine` | to cook | | `faire les courses` | to do the shopping / grocery shopping | | `faire ses devoirs` | to do one's homework | | `faire du vélo` | to ride a bicycle | | `faire une promenade` | to take a walk | | `faire du sport` | to play sports / exercise | | `faire la vaisselle` | to do the dishes | ### examples in context - `il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.` — it is cold, so i am going to put on my coat. - `nous faisons les courses le samedi matin.` — we do the shopping on saturday morning. - `tu fais du vélo près du lac ?` — do you ride your bike near the lake? - `faites attention devant cette porte.` — be careful in front of that door. - `elles font leurs devoirs avant de sortir.` — they do their homework before going out. - `on fait une promenade après le dîner.` — we take a walk after dinner. ### pronunciation note the `s` in `faisons` is pronounced like `z`: **feh-zon**. this makes `nous faisons` easy to recognize by ear. ### common mistake for english speakers do not translate weather with `être` in these expressions. - correct: `il fait chaud.` — it is hot. - incorrect: `*il est chaud.` in a weather context also learn activity phrases as complete chunks: `faire du vélo`, not `*faire vélo`. ### sentence-building practice answer with a full sentence. 1. quel temps fait-il aujourd'hui ? 2. que fais-tu avant le dîner ? 3. est-ce que vous faites du sport le week-end ? 4. que vont faire tes amis demain ? use `faire les courses`, `faire une promenade`, or another `faire` expression. sample answers: 1. `il fait beau aujourd'hui.` — weather expressions commonly use `il fait`, not `il est`. 2. `avant le dîner, je fais la vaisselle.` — `faire la vaisselle` is a fixed activity phrase. 3. `oui, nous faisons du sport le week-end.` — `nous` takes `faisons`; the activity phrase is `faire du sport`. 4. `mes amis vont faire les courses demain.` — in the near future, `vont` is conjugated and `faire` stays infinitive. mark yourself wrong if you wrote a weather sentence with `être`, or if you dropped the article in fixed expressions such as `faire du vélo` or `faire les courses`. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine motion, prepositions, geography, and `faire` the way they appear in real conversation. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `demain, nous allons visiter un village près de québec.` — tomorrow, we are going to visit a village near quebec city. 2. `elle vient de france, mais elle habite au canada.` — she comes from france, but she lives in canada. 3. `il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.` — it is cold, so i am going to put on my coat. 4. `à quelle heure allez-vous partir pour l'aéroport ?` — what time are you going to leave for the airport? 5. `les enfants jouent dans le jardin derrière la maison.` — the children are playing in the garden behind the house. 6. `nous allons faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.` — we are going to do the shopping before going back home. use this checklist after writing the sentences from memory. - **aller plus infinitive.** sentence 1 has `allons visiter`, sentence 3 has `vais mettre`, sentence 4 has `allez partir`, and sentence 6 has `allons faire`. only `aller` changes; the infinitive stays unchanged. - **preposition chunks.** copy small phrases exactly: `près de québec`, `de france`, `au canada`, `pour l'aéroport`, `dans le jardin`, `derrière la maison`, `avant de rentrer`, `chez nous`. - **contractions.** `au canada` is not two words, and `chez nous` does not use `à`. - **weather.** sentence 3 uses `il fait froid`, not `il est froid` for weather. - **question formatting.** sentence 4 uses inversion in `allez-vous` and needs a space before `?`. if the preposition is wrong, the sentence may still be understandable, but the chapter skill is not mastered. rewrite the whole phrase, not just the isolated preposition. ### notice and mark for each sentence above: 1. circle the conjugated verb or verbs; 2. underline every prepositional phrase; 3. label each use of `aller` as either **movement** or **near future**; 4. find the one weather expression and the one geography phrase. | # | conjugated verb or verbs | prepositional phrases | use of `aller` | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `allons` | `près de québec` | near future in `allons visiter` | | 2 | `vient`, `habite` | `de france`, `au canada` | none | | 3 | `fait`, `vais` | — | near future in `vais mettre` | | 4 | `allez` | `à quelle heure`, `pour l'aéroport` | near future in `allez-vous partir` | | 5 | `jouent` | `dans le jardin`, `derrière la maison` | none | | 6 | `allons` | `avant de rentrer`, `chez nous` | near future in `allons faire` | the weather expression is `il fait froid`. the geography phrase is `de france` and `au canada` in sentence 2. sentence 1 also contains `près de québec`, a place phrase with a city. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the destination, time, country, or weather. then rewrite sentence 6 in the negative. worked rewrites for sentences 1-4: 1. subject: `demain, je vais visiter un village près de québec.` detail: `demain, nous allons visiter un musée près de montréal.` 2. subject: `ils viennent de france, mais ils habitent au canada.` detail: `elle vient d'italie, mais elle habite aux états-unis.` 3. subject: `il fait chaud, alors nous allons mettre des lunettes de soleil.` detail: `il fait beau, alors je vais faire une promenade.` 4. subject: `à quelle heure vas-tu partir pour l'aéroport ?` detail: `à quelle heure allez-vous partir pour la gare ?` negative sentence 6: `nous n'allons pas faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.` — the negative wraps `allons`; `faire` and `rentrer` stay infinitives. ### make it personal write five new sentences about your own routine or travel plans. include: - one sentence with `aller + infinitive`; - one sentence with a country or city; - one sentence with `chez`; - one sentence with `avant de`; - one sentence with `faire`. model check for the cumulative practice: 1. `demain, nous allons visiter un village près de québec.` — `allons visiter` is near future: `allons` is conjugated, `visiter` is infinitive. `près de québec` is a place phrase. 2. `elle vient de france, mais elle habite au canada.` — `de france` is origin; `au canada` is location in a masculine country. 3. `il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.` — `il fait froid` is the weather expression; `vais mettre` is near future. 4. `à quelle heure allez-vous partir pour l'aéroport ?` — `allez partir` is near future, and the question keeps the prepositional phrase `pour l'aéroport`. 5. `les enfants jouent dans le jardin derrière la maison.` — `dans le jardin` and `derrière la maison` are place phrases. 6. `nous allons faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.` — `allons faire` is near future; `avant de rentrer` uses `de` + infinitive; `chez nous` means our place or home. negative rewrite of sentence 6: `nous n'allons pas faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.` — the negative wraps `allons`, not `faire`. personal answers vary. check that every use of `aller + infinitive` keeps the second verb unchanged, every country phrase follows the geography chart, and every `faire` expression is learned as a complete chunk. ## mini-dialogue: planning a weekend trip **camille :** `alors, où est-ce qu'on va ce week-end ?` — so, where are we going this weekend? **nora :** `on va à québec samedi matin, puis on va passer la nuit chez ma cousine.` — we are going to quebec city on saturday morning, then we are going to spend the night at my cousin's place. **camille :** `tu viens toujours de montréal en voiture ?` — are you still coming from montreal by car? **nora :** `oui, mais je vais d'abord m'arrêter au marché.` — yes, but i am going to stop at the market first. **camille :** `quel temps va-t-il faire là-bas ?` — what is the weather going to be like there? **nora :** `on dit qu'il va faire beau, mais il va faire frais le soir.` — they say the weather is going to be nice, but it is going to be cool in the evening. **camille :** `parfait. avant de partir, je vais acheter du café et du pain.` — perfect. before leaving, i am going to buy coffee and bread. **nora :** `très bien. à quelle station de bus vas-tu m'attendre ?` — very good. at which bus station are you going to wait for me? ### guided analysis 1. find every form of `aller` and decide whether it means simple movement or the near future. 2. explain why the dialogue uses `à québec`, `chez ma cousine`, `de montréal`, and `au marché`. 3. find the weather expressions and explain why they use `faire`. 4. rewrite nora's second line with `nous` instead of `on`, making every necessary change. 5. replace `à quelle station` in the last line with a version using `laquelle`. 1. forms of `aller`: `on va` in `où est-ce qu'on va` means movement; `on va à québec` means movement; `on va passer` is near future; `je vais m'arrêter` is near future; `va-t-il faire` is near future with weather; `il va faire beau` and `il va faire frais` are near future; `je vais acheter` is near future; `vas-tu m'attendre` is near future. 2. `à québec` uses `à` because québec is a city. `chez ma cousine` means at my cousin's home. `de montréal` uses `de` for origin from a city. `au marché` is `à` plus `le marché`, so it contracts to `au`. 3. weather expressions: `quel temps va-t-il faire là-bas ?`, `il va faire beau`, and `il va faire frais`. french uses `faire` for these weather chunks, and here `aller` puts them in the near future. 4. `nous allons à québec samedi matin, puis nous allons passer la nuit chez ma cousine.` — changing `on` to `nous` changes `va` to `allons`; the infinitive `passer` stays unchanged. 5. `à laquelle des stations de bus vas-tu m'attendre ?` — `station` is feminine singular, so the form is `laquelle`; the preposition `à` stays in front. ## connected reading: une semaine de voyage `la semaine prochaine, nous allons passer quatre jours au québec. nous partons de toronto lundi matin et nous arrivons à montréal vers midi. pendant les deux premiers jours, nous allons rester chez des amis qui habitent près du centre. ensuite, nous allons faire une excursion dans un petit village au bord du fleuve. s'il fait beau, nous allons faire une longue promenade après le déjeuner ; s'il fait froid, nous allons visiter un musée. avant de rentrer, je veux acheter des cadeaux pour ma famille.` — next week, we are going to spend four days in quebec. we are leaving toronto on monday morning and arriving in montreal around noon. during the first two days, we are going to stay with friends who live near downtown. then we are going to take a trip to a small village on the river. if the weather is nice, we are going to take a long walk after lunch; if it is cold, we are going to visit a museum. before going back, i want to buy gifts for my family. ### reading practice 1. list every destination phrase and every origin phrase in the reading. 2. find two uses of `aller + infinitive` and explain the infinitive that follows each one. 3. underline the place expressions built with `près de`, `au bord de`, and `chez`. 4. change the reading from `nous` to `je` for the first three sentences. 5. write three true travel sentences of your own using the reading as a model. 1. destination and location phrases include `au québec`, `à montréal`, `chez des amis`, and `dans un petit village au bord du fleuve`. the clear origin phrase is `de toronto`. 2. uses of `aller + infinitive`: `nous allons passer` has infinitive `passer`; `nous allons rester` has infinitive `rester`; `nous allons faire` has infinitive `faire`; `nous allons visiter` has infinitive `visiter`. in each case, only `allons` is conjugated. 3. place expressions: `chez des amis`, `près du centre`, and `au bord du fleuve`. notice the contraction in `du fleuve`, from `de` plus `le fleuve`. 4. first three sentences in `je` form: `la semaine prochaine, je vais passer quatre jours au québec. je pars de toronto lundi matin et j'arrive à montréal vers midi. pendant les deux premiers jours, je vais rester chez des amis qui habitent près du centre.` 5. answers vary. models: `je vais passer deux jours à montréal.` `je pars de vancouver lundi matin.` `avant de rentrer, je vais acheter un cadeau au marché.` check every city, country, and sequence phrase against the chapter chart. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. **mixing up motion and destination prepositions.** say `aller à paris`, but `venir de paris`. 2. **forgetting contractions.** write `au bureau` and `du canada`, not `*à le bureau` or `*de le canada`. 3. **using `aller` with a finite verb.** say `je vais partir`, not `*je vais je pars`. 4. **using the wrong preposition with countries.** say `en france`, `au japon`, `aux pays-bas`. 5. **using `être` for weather.** say `il fait froid`, not `*il est froid` for the weather. 6. **overusing `dans` for every kind of location.** compare `dans la voiture`, `à la gare`, `chez mes amis`, `sur la table`. 7. **ignoring the preposition in a question.** say `à qui ?`, `de quoi ?`, `avec qui ?`, not only `qui ?` when the verb requires a preposition. ## chapter reference sheet ### `aller` and `faire`, side by side | pronoun | `aller` | `faire` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `vais` | `fais` | | `tu` | `vas` | `fais` | | `il/elle/on` | `va` | `fait` | | `nous` | `allons` | `faisons` | | `vous` | `allez` | `faites` | | `ils/elles` | `vont` | `font` | ### contractions with `à` and `de` | meaning | form | |---|---| | `à + le` | `au` | | `à + les` | `aux` | | `de + le` | `du` | | `de + les` | `des` | | `à + la`, `à + l'` | no contraction | | `de + la`, `de + l'` | no contraction | ### geography quick reference | place type | to / in | from | |---|---|---| | city | `à` | `de` | | feminine country | `en` | `de` / `d'` | | masculine country | `au` | `du` | | plural country | `aux` | `des` | | vowel-opening country | `en` | `d'` | ### preposition-question patterns | pattern | example | |---|---| | `à qui` | `à qui téléphones-tu ?` | | `de quoi` | `de quoi parle-t-il ?` | | `avec qui` | `avec qui sors-tu ?` | | `auquel / à laquelle / auxquels / auxquelles` | `auquel penses-tu ?` | | `duquel / de laquelle / desquels / desquelles` | `duquel parlez-vous ?` | ### core habits - learn prepositions inside useful phrases, not as isolated translation equivalents. - check whether a place is a city, a feminine country, a masculine country, or a plural country. - in the near future, change only `aller`; keep the infinitive unchanged. - treat weather expressions with `faire` as fixed chunks. ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a short trip, weekend plan, or busy day. include: - `aller` used once for movement and twice in the near future; - at least four different prepositions of place or sequence; - one city and two countries or regions with correct prepositions; - one question built after a preposition; - two expressions with `faire`, including one weather expression; - one sentence with `avant de + infinitive`. after writing: 1. circle every conjugated verb; 2. box every preposition; 3. check each geography phrase against the reference chart; 4. underline the infinitive after each use of `aller + infinitive`; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any sentence you corrected from memory. answers vary. here is a model paragraph that satisfies every requirement: `samedi matin, je vais à montréal avec deux amis. nous allons visiter un musée près du centre. ensuite, nous allons faire une promenade dans le vieux quartier. ma sœur vient de france, mais elle habite au canada depuis un an. dimanche, nous allons parler avec des amis aux états-unis par vidéo. il fait froid, alors je vais mettre un manteau. avant de partir, je vais acheter du café au marché. chez ma cousine, nous faisons la cuisine le soir. avec qui est-ce que tu vas voyager cet été ?` how it satisfies the brief: `je vais à montréal` uses `aller` for movement; `allons visiter`, `allons faire`, `vais mettre`, and `vais acheter` use near future; prepositions include `près du centre`, `dans le vieux quartier`, `de france`, `au canada`, `aux états-unis`, `avant de partir`, `au marché`, and `chez ma cousine`; geography includes montréal, france, canada, and the united states; `avec qui` is a question after a preposition; `il fait froid` and `nous faisons la cuisine` use `faire`; `avant de partir` uses `avant de` plus infinitive. mark yourself wrong if any of these appear: - `à le` or `de le` instead of `au` or `du`; - `je vais je visite` instead of `je vais visiter`; - `à france` or `de le canada`; - a weather sentence with `être` instead of `faire`; - a preposition question that drops the preposition, such as asking only `qui ?` when you mean `avec qui ?`. "},{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 6 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-exercises\">Chapter 6 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I am going to travel.</li>\n<li>to Canada</li>\n<li>from France</li>\n<li>to the United States</li>\n<li>nous / faire</li>\n<li>The weather is nice.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais voyager.</code> — Near future uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code> is conjugated, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyager</code> stays infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code> — Canada is masculine in this pattern, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le Canada</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code> — France is feminine, and origin from a feminine country usually uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> with no article.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux États-Unis</code> — The United States is plural, so to or in takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous faisons</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> is irregular; the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisons</code>, with s pronounced like z.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait beau.</code> — Weather uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il fait</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est</code>, in this common expression.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>aller and the near future</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais partir.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>à and de</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>geographical names</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en France</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>faire</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must show the target structure and use the right preposition or verb form.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Aller and near future: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons visiter le musée demain.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code> is conjugated and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visiter</code> stays infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais au bureau après le déjeuner.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le bureau</code> contracts to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au bureau</code>.</li>\n<li>Geographical names: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle habite au Canada.</code> — Canada takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au</code> in this pattern.</li>\n<li>Faire: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait beau aujourd'hui.</code> — Weather uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> with impersonal <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if your near-future sentence conjugates both verbs, if you write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à le</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de le</code>, or if your weather sentence uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons visiter Québec demain.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais visiter Québec demain.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">visiter</code> stays infinitive.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'allons pas visiter Québec demain.</code> — The negative wraps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que nous allons visiter Québec demain ?</code> — Normal word order follows <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons visiter Montréal demain.</code> — The destination changed, but the grammar frame stayed the same.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check that a subject change affects the conjugated verb only. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code>, the infinitive should not receive a subject ending.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais à la bibliothèque après le déjeuner.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais</code> is the irregular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à la</code> does not contract.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'allons pas prendre le train demain.</code> — the negative wraps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code> stays infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais au bureau.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> plus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le bureau</code> contracts to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au bureau</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient de France, mais elle habite au Canada.</code> — origin from a feminine country is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code>; location in Canada is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.</code> — weather uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, and the near future keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code> as an infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire les courses</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avant de rentrer</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez nous</code> are fixed chunks worth memorizing whole.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct prepositions as complete phrases. If you wrote the right verb but the wrong contraction, the sentence still needs another pass.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Plan a four-day trip. State destinations, origins, weather, and at least four near-future activities.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model plan:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lundi, je pars de Montréal et je vais aller à Québec.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il va faire frais, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mardi, je vais visiter un musée près du fleuve.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mercredi, je vais faire une promenade chez des amis à Trois-Rivières.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Jeudi, je vais revenir du Canada vers les États-Unis.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">S'il fait beau, je vais acheter du pain au marché.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: include at least one origin phrase, at least one destination, a weather expression with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, and four near-future forms such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais visiter</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va faire</code>. Mark yourself wrong if you conjugate the infinitive after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, or if the country and city prepositions do not match the chart.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Aller and the near future.</strong> Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, then add an unchanged infinitive. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais partir demain.</code></li>\n<li><strong>À and de.</strong> These prepositions contract with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons au marché.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Geographical names.</strong> Cities, countries, and plural place names use different prepositions. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient de France et habite au Canada.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Faire.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Faire</code> is irregular and appears in weather and activity expressions. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait beau, et nous faisons une promenade.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you can say the English rule but not give a new French example, redo the shortest drill for that unit before moving on.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 6 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i am going to travel. 2. to canada 3. from france 4. to the united states 5. nous / faire 6. the weather is nice. 1. `je vais voyager.` — near future uses `aller + infinitive`: `vais` is conjugated, `voyager` stays infinitive. 2. `au canada` — canada is masculine in this pattern, so `à` + `le canada` becomes `au canada`. 3. `de france` — france is feminine, and origin from a feminine country usually uses `de` with no article. 4. `aux états-unis` — the united states is plural, so to or in takes `aux`. 5. `nous faisons` — `faire` is irregular; the `nous` form is `faisons`, with s pronounced like z. 6. `il fait beau.` — weather uses `il fait`, not `il est`, in this common expression. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **aller and the near future**. use `je vais partir.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **à and de**. use `au marché` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **geographical names**. use `en france` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **faire**. use `il fait froid.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct sentence must show the target structure and use the right preposition or verb form. sample answers: 1. aller and near future: `nous allons visiter le musée demain.` — `allons` is conjugated and `visiter` stays infinitive. 2. `à` and `de`: `je vais au bureau après le déjeuner.` — `à` + `le bureau` contracts to `au bureau`. 3. geographical names: `elle habite au canada.` — canada takes `au` in this pattern. 4. faire: `il fait beau aujourd'hui.` — weather uses `faire` with impersonal `il`. mark yourself wrong if your near-future sentence conjugates both verbs, if you write `à le` or `de le`, or if your weather sentence uses `être` instead of `faire`. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `nous allons visiter québec demain.` 1. change the subject: `je vais visiter québec demain.` — `allons` changes to `vais`; `visiter` stays infinitive. 2. make it negative: `nous n'allons pas visiter québec demain.` — the negative wraps `allons`. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que nous allons visiter québec demain ?` — normal word order follows `est-ce que`. 4. change one meaningful detail: `nous allons visiter montréal demain.` — the destination changed, but the grammar frame stayed the same. check that a subject change affects the conjugated verb only. in `aller + infinitive`, the infinitive should not receive a subject ending. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `je vais à la bibliothèque après le déjeuner.` — `vais` is the irregular `je` form of `aller`, and `à la` does not contract. 2. `nous n'allons pas prendre le train demain.` — the negative wraps `allons`; `prendre` stays infinitive. 3. `je vais au bureau.` — `à` plus `le bureau` contracts to `au bureau`. 4. `elle vient de france, mais elle habite au canada.` — origin from a feminine country is `de france`; location in canada is `au canada`. 5. `il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.` — weather uses `faire`, and the near future keeps `mettre` as an infinitive. 6. `nous allons faire les courses avant de rentrer chez nous.` — `faire les courses`, `avant de rentrer`, and `chez nous` are fixed chunks worth memorizing whole. correct prepositions as complete phrases. if you wrote the right verb but the wrong contraction, the sentence still needs another pass. ## e. personal writing plan a four-day trip. state destinations, origins, weather, and at least four near-future activities. answers vary. a model plan: 1. `lundi, je pars de montréal et je vais aller à québec.` 2. `il va faire frais, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.` 3. `mardi, je vais visiter un musée près du fleuve.` 4. `mercredi, je vais faire une promenade chez des amis à trois-rivières.` 5. `jeudi, je vais revenir du canada vers les états-unis.` 6. `s'il fait beau, je vais acheter du pain au marché.` self-check: include at least one origin phrase, at least one destination, a weather expression with `faire`, and four near-future forms such as `vais visiter` or `va faire`. mark yourself wrong if you conjugate the infinitive after `aller`, or if the country and city prepositions do not match the chart. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **aller and the near future.** conjugate `aller`, then add an unchanged infinitive. example: `je vais partir demain.` 2. **à and de.** these prepositions contract with `le` and `les`. example: `nous allons au marché.` 3. **geographical names.** cities, countries, and plural place names use different prepositions. example: `elle vient de france et habite au canada.` 4. **faire.** `faire` is irregular and appears in weather and activity expressions. example: `il fait beau, et nous faisons une promenade.` if you can say the english rule but not give a new french example, redo the shortest drill for that unit before moving on. "},{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 6 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-answer-key\">Chapter 6 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais voyager.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux États-Unis</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous faisons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait beau.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aller and the near future:</strong> Aller is irregular: vais, vas, va, allons, allez, vont. Follow it with an infinitive to say what is going to happen.</li>\n<li><strong>À and de:</strong> À often marks destination or location; de often marks origin, possession, or source. Both contract with le and les.</li>\n<li><strong>Geographical names:</strong> Use en with many feminine countries and masculine countries beginning with a vowel, au with many masculine countries, and aux with plural country names.</li>\n<li><strong>Faire:</strong> Faire forms: fais, fais, fait, faisons, faites, font. It appears in weather, activities, and many fixed expressions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 6 answer key ## section a 1. `je vais voyager.` 2. `au canada` 3. `de france` 4. `aux états-unis` 5. `nous faisons` 6. `il fait beau.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **aller and the near future:** aller is irregular: vais, vas, va, allons, allez, vont. follow it with an infinitive to say what is going to happen. - **à and de:** à often marks destination or location; de often marks origin, possession, or source. both contract with le and les. - **geographical names:** use en with many feminine countries and masculine countries beginning with a vowel, au with many masculine countries, and aux with plural country names. - **faire:** faire forms: fais, fais, fait, faisons, faites, font. it appears in weather, activities, and many fixed expressions. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 6 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 6 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Aller and the near future</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>À and de</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Geographical names</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Faire</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 6 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **aller and the near future**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **à and de** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **geographical names**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **faire**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":7,"slug":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives","title":"Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Constructions","documents":[{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/README.md","title":"Chapter 7: Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Constructions","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-irregular-verbs-i-and-verb-infinitive-constructions\">Chapter 7: Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Constructions</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use high-frequency irregular verbs.</li>\n<li>Distinguish partir, sortir, and quitter.</li>\n<li>Combine conjugated verbs with infinitives.</li>\n<li>Choose no preposition, à, or de before an infinitive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Movement and arrival</li>\n<li>Communication and action</li>\n<li>Ability, desire, obligation</li>\n<li>Verb + verb patterns</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 7: irregular verbs i and verb + infinitive constructions this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - use high-frequency irregular verbs. - distinguish partir, sortir, and quitter. - combine conjugated verbs with infinitives. - choose no preposition, à, or de before an infinitive. ## lesson sequence 1. movement and arrival 2. communication and action 3. ability, desire, obligation 4. verb + verb patterns ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 7 Lessons: Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Patterns","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-lessons-irregular-verbs-i-and-verb-infinitive-patterns\">Chapter 7 Lessons: Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Patterns</h1>\n<p>This chapter gives you a new group of high-frequency irregular verbs and shows you how French links one verb to another. By the end, you should be able to talk about leaving, coming, holding, saying, reading, writing, putting, receiving, being able to, wanting to, having to, and having just done something.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>distinguish <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> in natural contexts;</li>\n<li>conjugate and use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenir</code>, including <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code> for the recent past;</li>\n<li>conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code> in the present tense;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> with the correct meaning in context;</li>\n<li>recognize when a second verb follows directly, follows <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, or follows <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>;</li>\n<li>build longer sentences with two verbs without translating word for word from English.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code> for the near future from Chapter 6;</li>\n<li>form negative sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>;</li>\n<li>use common time expressions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">demain</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">après le travail</code>;</li>\n<li>recognize an infinitive such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code>.</li>\n<p>This chapter adds new irregular forms, but the sentence frame will often feel familiar: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je veux partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je viens de partir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je dois partir</code> all depend on your earlier control of infinitives.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test yourself before adding this chapter's irregular verbs. Answer these five without looking back, then check.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Turn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars demain</code> into near future. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais partir demain.</code> The second verb stays in the infinitive after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>.</li>\n<li>Make <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lis le message</code> negative. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne lis pas le message.</code> <strong>Ne</strong> and <strong>pas</strong> wrap the conjugated verb, not the object.</li>\n<li>Say <strong>tonight</strong> and <strong>after work</strong> in French. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">après le travail</code>. These time expressions can sit naturally at the beginning or end of the sentence.</li>\n<li>Which word is the infinitive in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons lire</code> ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>. The conjugated verb is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulons</code>; the second verb stays unchanged.</li>\n<li>Choose the correct frame for <strong>aller</strong>, <strong>vouloir</strong>, and <strong>venir de</strong>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je veux partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je viens de partir</code>. The recent past needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> before the infinitive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review Chapters 6 and the negative pattern first. This chapter depends on seeing which verb is conjugated and which verb remains an infinitive.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Memorize the verb blocks aloud and in contrast. For example, say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pars</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je sors</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je quitte</code> together so the meaning differences stay attached to the forms.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-partir-sortir-and-quitter\">Unit 1: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>These three verbs can all relate to leaving, but they do <strong>not</strong> mean the same thing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> = to leave, to depart</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code> = to go out, to take out</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> = to leave something or someone behind; it takes a direct object</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-forms\">Present tense forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pars</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sors</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitte</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pars</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sors</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">part</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sort</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitte</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"how-the-meanings-differ\">How the meanings differ</h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> when the focus is the departure itself.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars demain matin.</code> — I am leaving tomorrow morning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le train part à sept heures.</code> — The train leaves at seven o'clock.</li>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code> when someone goes out or takes something out.</p>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle sort ce soir avec ses collègues.</code> — She is going out tonight with her coworkers.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sortons les chaises sur le balcon.</code> — We are taking the chairs out onto the balcony.</li>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> with a direct object.</p>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils quittent le bureau à six heures.</code> — They leave the office at six o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je quitte Paris vendredi.</code> — I leave Paris on Friday.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The plural forms <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortent</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittent</code> end in written <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code>, but that ending is silent. By ear, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils partent</code> sounds like the same stem as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il part</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je quitte à six heures</code> when you mean \"I leave at six.\" <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quitter</code> needs an object: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je quitte le bureau à six heures</code>. If there is no object, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Choose the correct verb and write a complete sentence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You are leaving tomorrow for Lyon.</li>\n<li>We are going out after dinner.</li>\n<li>She is leaving the classroom now.</li>\n<li>They are taking the dog outside.</li>\n<p>Then explain why one sentence needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code>.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu pars demain pour Lyon.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> because the focus is departure; no direct object is needed.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sortons après le dîner.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code> for going out.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle quitte la salle de classe maintenant.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> because there is a direct object: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la salle de classe</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils sortent le chien.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code> can also mean to take something or someone out.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sentence 3 needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> because you are leaving a specific place behind. The common trap is using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> without an object; if there is no object, choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> instead.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-venir-tenir-and-venir-de-infinitive\">Unit 2: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Venir</code> means <strong>to come</strong> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenir</code> means <strong>to hold / keep</strong>. Their forms are closely related, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code> creates the <strong>recent past</strong>: \"to have just done something.\"</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-forms\">Present tense forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tiens</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tiens</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vient</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tient</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viennent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tiennent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"main-uses-of-venir\">Main uses of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu viens au café avec nous ?</code> — Are you coming to the café with us?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient de Marseille.</code> — She comes from Marseille.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes cousins viennent ce week-end.</code> — My cousins are coming this weekend.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"main-uses-of-tenir\">Main uses of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenir</code></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je tiens la porte pour vous.</code> — I am holding the door for you.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous tenons beaucoup à ce projet.</code> — We care a lot about this project.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle tient toujours un carnet dans son sac.</code> — She always keeps a notebook in her bag.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-recent-past-venir-de-infinitive\">The recent past: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code></h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code> for something that has <strong>just happened</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></th>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code></td>\n<td>I have just finished</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rentrer</code></td>\n<td>we have just come back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viennent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code></td>\n<td>they have just arrived</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens de lire ton message.</code> — I have just read your message.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous venons de dîner.</code> — We have just had dinner.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il vient de partir.</code> — He has just left.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not confuse <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code> with origin.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient du Canada.</code> — She comes from Canada.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient de téléphoner.</code> — She has just called.</li>\n<p>The grammar after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> tells you which meaning is intended.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Translate and say aloud.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>We are coming from the station.</li>\n<li>I have just written the email.</li>\n<li>Are you holding my keys?</li>\n<li>They have just gone out.</li>\n<p>Then change sentence 2 to the negative.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous venons de la gare.</code> — This is origin: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de</code> + place.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens d'écrire le mail.</code> — This is recent past: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de</code> + infinitive. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code> before the vowel sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu tiens mes clés ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenir</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tiens</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils viennent de sortir.</code> — Recent past again: they have just gone out.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Negative form of sentence 2:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne viens pas d'écrire le mail.</code> — The negative wraps the conjugated verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viens</code>, not the infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The trap is distinguishing origin from recent past. A place after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> gives origin; an infinitive after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> gives something that just happened.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-dire-lire-crire-mettre-and-recevoir\">Unit 3: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>These verbs appear constantly in daily life. They do not follow one single pattern, so learn them in clear families and useful phrases.</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-forms\">Present tense forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écris</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écris</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">met</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçoit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">disons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrivons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dites</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrivez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">disent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrivent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçoivent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous dites toujours la vérité.</code> — You always tell the truth.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lis davantage le soir.</code> — I read more in the evening.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous écrivons à nos amis chaque semaine.</code> — We write to our friends every week.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils mettent leurs manteaux près de la porte.</code> — They put their coats near the door.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle reçoit souvent des lettres de sa grand-mère.</code> — She often receives letters from her grandmother.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Que disent les enfants ?</code> — What are the children saying?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu lis quel journal ?</code> — Which newspaper do you read?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je mets la table avant le dîner.</code> — I set the table before dinner.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The written endings in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous dites</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous lisez</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous écrivez</code> help distinguish the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form in writing, but in fast speech you must listen for the whole sound group. Also notice <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous écrivons</code>: the stem changes, and the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">v</code> is clearly pronounced.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not forget that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire à quelqu'un</code> needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before a person: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'écris à mon frère</code>. But <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code> takes a direct object: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je lis le message</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Complete or transform each sentence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ___ un message à ma mère.</code> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ___ le journal pendant le petit-déjeuner.</code> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ___ ses chaussures près du lit.</code> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn sentence 2 into a question with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn sentence 1 into the recent past with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'écris un message à ma mère.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before the person receiving the writing.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous lisons le journal pendant le petit-déjeuner.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code> has the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle met ses chaussures près du lit.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code> has the singular form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">met</code>; the final t is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lisez-vous le journal pendant le petit-déjeuner ?</code> — With inversion, switch verb and pronoun and keep the hyphen.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens d'écrire un message à ma mère.</code> — Recent past uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de</code> plus the infinitive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you wrote <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'écris ma mère</code>. In this meaning, the person needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-pouvoir-vouloir-and-devoir\">Unit 4: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>These verbs are often followed directly by an infinitive. They express ability, desire, necessity, probability, and debt depending on context.</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-forms\">Present tense forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peut</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veut</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peuvent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veulent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doivent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"direct-link-to-the-infinitive\">Direct link to the infinitive</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir + infinitive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux venir.</code></td>\n<td>I can come.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir + infinitive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons apprendre.</code></td>\n<td>We want to learn.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir + infinitive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu dois travailler.</code></td>\n<td>You must work.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-meanings-of-devoir\">The meanings of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Devoir</code> has three important uses.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>obligation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dois finir ce travail ce soir.</code> — I have to finish this work tonight.</li>\n<li>probability: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il doit être chez lui maintenant.</code> — He is probably at home now.</li>\n<li>debt/owing: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dois dix euros à Paul.</code> — I owe Paul ten euros.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux lire davantage, mais je dois finir mon travail.</code> — I want to read more, but I must finish my work.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous pouvons sortir après le cours.</code> — We can go out after class.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle veut recevoir plus de nouvelles de sa famille.</code> — She wants to receive more news from her family.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous devez mettre vos téléphones sur la table.</code> — You must put your phones on the table.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils ne peuvent pas venir ce soir.</code> — They cannot come tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On veut partir tôt demain.</code> — We want to leave early tomorrow.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> after these three verbs before a second verb.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je peux venir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous voulons apprendre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle doit partir</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je peux de venir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*nous voulons à apprendre</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Translate into French.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>I can read this book.</li>\n<li>We want to leave now.</li>\n<li>He probably has to be at the office. Make the meaning probability, not obligation.</li>\n<li>They owe their friend twenty euros.</li>\n<p>Then rewrite sentence 1 in the negative and sentence 2 as a question.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux lire ce livre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code> is followed directly by an infinitive; no <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons partir maintenant.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code> also links directly to the infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il doit être au bureau.</code> — This can mean he is probably at the office; context makes it probability, not obligation.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils doivent vingt euros à leur ami.</code> — With a sum of money, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> means to owe.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Rewrites:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne peux pas lire ce livre.</code> — The negative wraps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peux</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que nous voulons partir maintenant ?</code> — A yes/no question can use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code> with normal word order.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not add a preposition before the infinitive after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-verb-infinitive-patterns-no-preposition-or-de\">Unit 5: Verb + infinitive patterns: no preposition, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French verbs do not all connect to a following infinitive in the same way. Some take <strong>no preposition</strong>, some take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, and others take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>. Learn the whole frame.</p>\n<h3 id=\"group-1-no-preposition-before-the-infinitive\">Group 1: no preposition before the infinitive</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime lire le matin.</code></td>\n<td>I like to read in the morning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous préférons rester ici.</code></td>\n<td>We prefer to stay here.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle va téléphoner.</code></td>\n<td>She is going to call.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu peux sortir.</code></td>\n<td>You can go out.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils veulent apprendre.</code></td>\n<td>They want to learn.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dois partir.</code></td>\n<td>I must leave.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il vient de finir.</code></td>\n<td>He has just finished.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"group-2-verbs-followed-by\">Group 2: verbs followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code></th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à apprendre le français.</code></td>\n<td>She is beginning to learn French.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprendre à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous apprenons à cuisiner.</code></td>\n<td>We are learning to cook.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aider à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu m'aides à porter les sacs ?</code></td>\n<td>Are you helping me carry the bags?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussir à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils réussissent à finir à temps.</code></td>\n<td>They manage to finish on time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se mettre à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me mets à rire.</code></td>\n<td>I start laughing.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"group-3-verbs-followed-by-de\">Group 3: verbs followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'essaie de parler chaque jour.</code></td>\n<td>I try to speak every day.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrêter de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle arrête de travailler à six heures.</code></td>\n<td>She stops working at six o'clock.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oublier de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous oublions de fermer la fenêtre.</code></td>\n<td>We forget to close the window.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils choisissent de rester.</code></td>\n<td>They choose to stay.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">refuser de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il refuse de répondre.</code></td>\n<td>He refuses to answer.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-study-the-pattern\">How to study the pattern</h3>\n<p>Never memorize only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code> or only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code>. Memorize <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code> as full frames.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>English often uses \"to\" before the second verb, but French does not always match that structure. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux partir</code> has no preposition, while <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je commence à partir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'essaie de partir</code> do.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Sort each frame into the correct group, then use it in a complete sentence: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code>.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Frame</th>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Sample sentence</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td>no preposition</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux partir tôt.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before infinitive</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à écrire.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> before infinitive</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous essayons de comprendre.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code></td>\n<td>no preposition</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils aiment lire le soir.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussir à</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before infinitive</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu réussis à finir le travail.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></td>\n<td>no preposition</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous devez répondre aujourd'hui.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Learn the frame, not just the verb. English uses <em>to</em> in many of these, but French divides them into no preposition, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine the chapter's new verbs and infinitive patterns the way they appear in real speech. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux lire davantage, mais je dois finir mon travail.</code> — I want to read more, but I must finish my work.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous pouvons sortir après le cours et écrire le message plus tard.</code> — We can go out after class and write the message later.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à apprendre le français et essaie de parler chaque jour.</code> — She is beginning to learn French and tries to speak every day.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils quittent le bureau et partent à six heures.</code> — They leave the office and depart at six o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens de recevoir ta lettre et je vais y répondre ce soir.</code> — I have just received your letter and I am going to answer it tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous dites que vous voulez venir, mais vous ne pouvez pas sortir avant neuf heures.</code> — You say that you want to come, but you cannot go out before nine o'clock.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>After you write the six sentences from memory, uncover them and check the details your ear may not give you.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Silent endings.</strong> In sentence 4, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittent</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partent</code> both end in silent <strong>-ent</strong>. If you wrote singular forms, the sound did not warn you.</li>\n<li><strong>Infinitive links.</strong> Sentence 1 has no preposition before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>; sentence 3 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à apprendre</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de parler</code>; sentence 5 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de recevoir</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viens</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular stems.</strong> Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dites</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulez</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvez</code>. These cannot be guessed from regular -er patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>The object after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code>.</strong> Sentence 4 says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittent le bureau</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> needs the place or person left behind.</li>\n<li><strong>Elision and accents.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'essaie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> need the apostrophe or accent you see in the model.</li>\n<li><strong>Question spacing.</strong> If you turn any of these into a question later, keep the French space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Give yourself full credit only when the conjugated verb, the infinitive, and the connector before the infinitive all match the original.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence above:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>box each infinitive;</li>\n<li>label the link before the infinitive as <strong>none</strong>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>;</li>\n<li>identify the sentence that uses the recent past.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, time, or activity.</li>\n<p>Then turn sentence 5 into the near future and sentence 6 into a yes/no question.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write five new sentences about your own plans or habits. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> meaning obligation;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> meaning probability or debt;</li>\n<li>one sentence with a verb followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model check for the cumulative practice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux lire davantage, mais je dois finir mon travail.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux lire</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dois finir</code> use no preposition before the infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous pouvons sortir après le cours et écrire le message plus tard.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvons sortir</code> uses no preposition; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> is another infinitive linked by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">et</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à apprendre le français et essaie de parler chaque jour.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils quittent le bureau et partent à six heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> has a direct object, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le bureau</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> does not need one.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens de recevoir ta lettre et je vais y répondre ce soir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viens de recevoir</code> is recent past; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais répondre</code> is near future.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous dites que vous voulez venir, mais vous ne pouvez pas sortir avant neuf heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulez venir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvez sortir</code> take no preposition.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Near-future rewrite of sentence 5: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais recevoir ta lettre et je vais y répondre ce soir.</code> — Both near-future forms use conjugated <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> plus an infinitive.</p>\n<p>Question version of sentence 6: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous dites que vous voulez venir, mais que vous ne pouvez pas sortir avant neuf heures ?</code> — The grammar is possible, though long; in real speech you might split it into two questions.</p>\n<p>For your personal sentences, mark yourself wrong if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> has no object, if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de</code> is missing before a recent-past infinitive, or if a verb that needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> is linked directly by mistake.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-after-work-plans\">Mini-dialogue: after work plans</h2>\n<p><strong>Julie :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu pars directement après le bureau ce soir ?</code> — Are you leaving directly after the office tonight? <strong>Samir :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Non, je viens de recevoir un message de ma sœur.</code> — No, I have just received a message from my sister. <strong>Julie :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qu'est-ce qu'elle dit ?</code> — What is she saying? <strong>Samir :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle veut sortir, mais elle doit d'abord écrire à son professeur.</code> — She wants to go out, but she first has to write to her professor. <strong>Julie :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et toi, tu peux venir au restaurant avec nous ?</code> — And you, can you come to the restaurant with us? <strong>Samir :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux venir plus tard, mais je dois quitter le bureau à l'heure et passer chez moi.</code> — I can come later, but I have to leave the office on time and stop by my place. <strong>Julie :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">D'accord. Nous allons te garder une place près de la fenêtre.</code> — Okay. We are going to save you a seat by the window. <strong>Samir :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parfait. J'essaie de finir ce rapport avant huit heures.</code> — Perfect. I am trying to finish this report before eight o'clock.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find one example each of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer de</code>.</li>\n<li>Explain why the dialogue uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter le bureau</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir le bureau</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite Samir's last answer with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle doit d'abord écrire à son professeur</code> into the recent past.</li>\n<li>Create another natural sentence using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenir</code>, for example one meaning to hold something or to care about something.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Examples from the dialogue: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu pars directement après le bureau ce soir ?</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pars</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je viens de recevoir un message de ma sœur</code> uses recent past; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qu'est-ce qu'elle dit ?</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle veut sortir</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veut</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle doit d'abord écrire</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doit</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'essaie de finir ce rapport</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie de</code>.</li>\n<li>The dialogue says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter le bureau</code> because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> takes a direct object: the office is the place Samir leaves behind. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir le bureau</code> is wrong because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> does not take the place left as a direct object.</li>\n<li>Samir's last answer with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous essayons de finir ce rapport avant huit heures.</code> The conjugated verb changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayons</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de finir</code> keeps the same infinitive frame.</li>\n<li>Recent past: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient d'écrire à son professeur.</code> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de</code> plus the infinitive, and shorten <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code> before the vowel sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>.</li>\n<li>A natural sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tenir</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je tiens la porte pour vous.</code> This means I am holding the door; for caring about something, say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous tenons beaucoup à ce projet.</code></li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-un-nouveau-rythme\">Connected reading: un nouveau rythme</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis le début du semestre, ma routine change beaucoup. Je pars plus tôt le matin parce que je veux lire dans le bus avant les cours. Après la classe, je sors souvent avec deux amis, mais je quitte la bibliothèque seulement quand je finis vraiment mon travail. Nous venons de commencer un projet important, alors nous devons écrire beaucoup et nous recevons des messages du professeur presque tous les jours. Le soir, j'essaie de me détendre, mais je ne peux pas toujours arrêter de penser au travail. Heureusement, ma sœur vient parfois dîner avec moi, et nous tenons encore à notre petit repas du jeudi.</code> — Since the beginning of the semester, my routine has been changing a lot. I leave earlier in the morning because I want to read on the bus before class. After class, I often go out with two friends, but I leave the library only when I truly finish my work. We have just started an important project, so we have to write a lot and we receive messages from the professor almost every day. In the evening, I try to relax, but I cannot always stop thinking about work. Fortunately, my sister sometimes comes to have dinner with me, and we still care about our little Thursday meal.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find three different movement verbs and explain the nuance of each one.</li>\n<li>Underline every infinitive and identify the frame before it.</li>\n<li>Find the sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code> and explain the time meaning.</li>\n<li>Change the first two sentences from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Write three true sentences about your own schedule using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Movement verbs:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars plus tôt</code> focuses on departure; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je sors souvent avec deux amis</code> means I go out socially; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je quitte la bibliothèque</code> needs the direct object, the library left behind. The reading also has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma sœur vient parfois dîner</code>, meaning she comes.</li>\n<li><strong>Infinitives and frames:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code> has no preposition; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venons de</code> is recent past with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devons</code> has no preposition; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me détendre</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie de</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrêter</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peux</code> has no preposition; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">penser</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrêter de</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dîner</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vient</code> is a purpose infinitive in this sentence.</li>\n<li>The recent past sentence is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous venons de commencer un projet important</code>. It means the project has just started, not that we come from a place.</li>\n<li>First two sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis le début du semestre, notre routine change beaucoup. Nous partons plus tôt le matin parce que nous voulons lire dans le bus avant les cours.</code> The main changes are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous partons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je veux</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous voulons</code>.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. A model set: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars tôt le lundi parce que je veux lire dans le train.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens de commencer un nouveau livre.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, j'essaie de ne pas penser au travail.</code> Mark yours wrong if an infinitive connector is missing or if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> has no object.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> without an object.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pars maintenant</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je quitte la maison maintenant</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Confusing origin with recent past.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de Paris</code> is not the same as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de partir</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Adding an article before an infinitive.</strong> Write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je veux partir</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je veux le partir</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Using the wrong connector before the infinitive.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer à</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer de</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> often uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> with a person.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'écris à ma tante</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Missing the special <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> forms.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous venons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous dites</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous écrivons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous pouvons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous voulez</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"movement-and-arrival-verbs\">Movement and arrival verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pars</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sors</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitte</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viens</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pars</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sors</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittes</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viens</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">part</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sort</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitte</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vient</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quittent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viennent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"communication-and-action-verbs\">Communication and action verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écris</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écris</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">met</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçoit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">disons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrivons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dites</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrivez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">disent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrivent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçoivent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peut</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veut</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peuvent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veulent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doivent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"verb-infinitive-quick-chart\">Verb + infinitive quick chart</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Common verbs</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>no preposition</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + infinitive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprendre à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aider à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussir à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se mettre à</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + infinitive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrêter de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oublier de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">refuser de</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-habits\">Core habits</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Learn each verb with a useful phrase and a contrasting verb where possible.</li>\n<li>Check whether the second verb needs no preposition, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code> for something that happened a moment ago.</li>\n<li>Keep the infinitive unchanged; only the first verb is conjugated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a busy day, a work routine, or plans for the week. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir de + infinitive</code>;</li>\n<li>two different verbs from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code>;</li>\n<li>one verb followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + infinitive</code> and one followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + infinitive</code>.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>underline every infinitive;</li>\n<li>label each infinitive link as <strong>none</strong>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>;</li>\n<li>check whether every use of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> has a direct object;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary, so compare your paragraph to the checklist. Here is one model that satisfies every requirement:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, je pars tôt parce que je veux lire dans le bus. Après le travail, je sors avec mes collègues, mais je quitte le bureau seulement à six heures. Je viens de recevoir un message de ma sœur. Elle dit qu'elle veut dîner avec moi ce soir. Je peux venir au restaurant, mais je dois finir un rapport avant huit heures. Je mets mon téléphone sur la table et j'écris une réponse courte. Ensuite, je commence à préparer mon sac et j'essaie de rester calme.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pars</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je sors</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je quitte le bureau</code> cover the three leaving verbs; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens de recevoir</code> is recent past; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écris</code> come from the communication/action group; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veut</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dois</code> appear with infinitives or complements; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence à préparer</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>; and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie de rester</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> without a direct object;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> before an infinitive;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer de</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer à</code>;</li>\n<li>a conjugated second verb where an infinitive is required;</li>\n<li>missing accents in forms such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écris</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponse</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 7 lessons: irregular verbs i and verb + infinitive patterns this chapter gives you a new group of high-frequency irregular verbs and shows you how french links one verb to another. by the end, you should be able to talk about leaving, coming, holding, saying, reading, writing, putting, receiving, being able to, wanting to, having to, and having just done something. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. distinguish `partir`, `sortir`, and `quitter` in natural contexts; 2. conjugate and use `venir` and `tenir`, including `venir de + infinitive` for the recent past; 3. conjugate `dire`, `lire`, `écrire`, `mettre`, and `recevoir` in the present tense; 4. use `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, and `devoir` with the correct meaning in context; 5. recognize when a second verb follows directly, follows `à`, or follows `de`; 6. build longer sentences with two verbs without translating word for word from english. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - use `aller + infinitive` for the near future from chapter 6; - form negative sentences with `ne...pas`; - use common time expressions such as `demain`, `ce soir`, and `après le travail`; - recognize an infinitive such as `partir`, `lire`, or `mettre`. this chapter adds new irregular forms, but the sentence frame will often feel familiar: `je vais partir`, `je veux partir`, `je viens de partir`, and `je dois partir` all depend on your earlier control of infinitives. test yourself before adding this chapter's irregular verbs. answer these five without looking back, then check. 1. turn `je pars demain` into near future. — `je vais partir demain.` the second verb stays in the infinitive after `aller`. 2. make `je lis le message` negative. — `je ne lis pas le message.` **ne** and **pas** wrap the conjugated verb, not the object. 3. say **tonight** and **after work** in french. — `ce soir` and `après le travail`. these time expressions can sit naturally at the beginning or end of the sentence. 4. which word is the infinitive in `nous voulons lire` ? — `lire`. the conjugated verb is `voulons`; the second verb stays unchanged. 5. choose the correct frame for **aller**, **vouloir**, and **venir de**. — `je vais partir`, `je veux partir`, `je viens de partir`. the recent past needs `de` before the infinitive. if you missed two or more, review chapters 6 and the negative pattern first. this chapter depends on seeing which verb is conjugated and which verb remains an infinitive. ## how to study this chapter memorize the verb blocks aloud and in contrast. for example, say `je pars`, `je sors`, `je quitte` together so the meaning differences stay attached to the forms. ## unit 1: `partir`, `sortir`, and `quitter` ### core idea these three verbs can all relate to leaving, but they do **not** mean the same thing. - `partir` = to leave, to depart - `sortir` = to go out, to take out - `quitter` = to leave something or someone behind; it takes a direct object ### present tense forms | pronoun | `partir` | `sortir` | `quitter` | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `pars` | `sors` | `quitte` | | `tu` | `pars` | `sors` | `quittes` | | `il/elle/on` | `part` | `sort` | `quitte` | | `nous` | `partons` | `sortons` | `quittons` | | `vous` | `partez` | `sortez` | `quittez` | | `ils/elles` | `partent` | `sortent` | `quittent` | ### how the meanings differ use `partir` when the focus is the departure itself. - `je pars demain matin.` — i am leaving tomorrow morning. - `le train part à sept heures.` — the train leaves at seven o'clock. use `sortir` when someone goes out or takes something out. - `elle sort ce soir avec ses collègues.` — she is going out tonight with her coworkers. - `nous sortons les chaises sur le balcon.` — we are taking the chairs out onto the balcony. use `quitter` with a direct object. - `ils quittent le bureau à six heures.` — they leave the office at six o'clock. - `je quitte paris vendredi.` — i leave paris on friday. ### pronunciation note the plural forms `partent`, `sortent`, and `quittent` end in written `-ent`, but that ending is silent. by ear, `ils partent` sounds like the same stem as `il part`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not say `*je quitte à six heures` when you mean \"i leave at six.\" `quitter` needs an object: `je quitte le bureau à six heures`. if there is no object, use `partir`. ### sentence-building practice choose the correct verb and write a complete sentence. 1. you are leaving tomorrow for lyon. 2. we are going out after dinner. 3. she is leaving the classroom now. 4. they are taking the dog outside. then explain why one sentence needs `quitter`. 1. `tu pars demain pour lyon.` — use `partir` because the focus is departure; no direct object is needed. 2. `nous sortons après le dîner.` — use `sortir` for going out. 3. `elle quitte la salle de classe maintenant.` — use `quitter` because there is a direct object: `la salle de classe`. 4. `ils sortent le chien.` — `sortir` can also mean to take something or someone out. sentence 3 needs `quitter` because you are leaving a specific place behind. the common trap is using `quitter` without an object; if there is no object, choose `partir` instead. ## unit 2: `venir`, `tenir`, and `venir de + infinitive` ### core idea `venir` means **to come** and `tenir` means **to hold / keep**. their forms are closely related, and `venir de + infinitive` creates the **recent past**: \"to have just done something.\" ### present tense forms | pronoun | `venir` | `tenir` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `viens` | `tiens` | | `tu` | `viens` | `tiens` | | `il/elle/on` | `vient` | `tient` | | `nous` | `venons` | `tenons` | | `vous` | `venez` | `tenez` | | `ils/elles` | `viennent` | `tiennent` | ### main uses of `venir` - `tu viens au café avec nous ?` — are you coming to the café with us? - `elle vient de marseille.` — she comes from marseille. - `mes cousins viennent ce week-end.` — my cousins are coming this weekend. ### main uses of `tenir` - `je tiens la porte pour vous.` — i am holding the door for you. - `nous tenons beaucoup à ce projet.` — we care a lot about this project. - `elle tient toujours un carnet dans son sac.` — she always keeps a notebook in her bag. ### the recent past: `venir de + infinitive` use `venir de + infinitive` for something that has **just happened**. | subject | `venir` | `de` | infinitive | meaning | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `viens` | `de` | `finir` | i have just finished | | `nous` | `venons` | `de` | `rentrer` | we have just come back | | `ils` | `viennent` | `d'` | `arriver` | they have just arrived | - `je viens de lire ton message.` — i have just read your message. - `nous venons de dîner.` — we have just had dinner. - `il vient de partir.` — he has just left. ### common mistake for english speakers do not confuse `venir de + infinitive` with origin. - `elle vient du canada.` — she comes from canada. - `elle vient de téléphoner.` — she has just called. the grammar after `de` tells you which meaning is intended. ### sentence-building practice translate and say aloud. 1. we are coming from the station. 2. i have just written the email. 3. are you holding my keys? 4. they have just gone out. then change sentence 2 to the negative. 1. `nous venons de la gare.` — this is origin: `venir de` + place. 2. `je viens d'écrire le mail.` — this is recent past: `venir de` + infinitive. `de` becomes `d'` before the vowel sound in `écrire`. 3. `tu tiens mes clés ?` — `tenir` uses `tiens` with `tu`. 4. `ils viennent de sortir.` — recent past again: they have just gone out. negative form of sentence 2: - `je ne viens pas d'écrire le mail.` — the negative wraps the conjugated verb `viens`, not the infinitive `écrire`. the trap is distinguishing origin from recent past. a place after `de` gives origin; an infinitive after `de` gives something that just happened. ## unit 3: `dire`, `lire`, `écrire`, `mettre`, and `recevoir` ### core idea these verbs appear constantly in daily life. they do not follow one single pattern, so learn them in clear families and useful phrases. ### present tense forms | pronoun | `dire` | `lire` | `écrire` | `mettre` | `recevoir` | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `dis` | `lis` | `écris` | `mets` | `reçois` | | `tu` | `dis` | `lis` | `écris` | `mets` | `reçois` | | `il/elle/on` | `dit` | `lit` | `écrit` | `met` | `reçoit` | | `nous` | `disons` | `lisons` | `écrivons` | `mettons` | `recevons` | | `vous` | `dites` | `lisez` | `écrivez` | `mettez` | `recevez` | | `ils/elles` | `disent` | `lisent` | `écrivent` | `mettent` | `reçoivent` | ### examples in context - `vous dites toujours la vérité.` — you always tell the truth. - `je lis davantage le soir.` — i read more in the evening. - `nous écrivons à nos amis chaque semaine.` — we write to our friends every week. - `ils mettent leurs manteaux près de la porte.` — they put their coats near the door. - `elle reçoit souvent des lettres de sa grand-mère.` — she often receives letters from her grandmother. - `que disent les enfants ?` — what are the children saying? - `tu lis quel journal ?` — which newspaper do you read? - `je mets la table avant le dîner.` — i set the table before dinner. ### pronunciation note the written endings in `vous dites`, `vous lisez`, and `vous écrivez` help distinguish the `vous` form in writing, but in fast speech you must listen for the whole sound group. also notice `nous écrivons`: the stem changes, and the `v` is clearly pronounced. ### common mistake for english speakers do not forget that `écrire à quelqu'un` needs `à` before a person: `j'écris à mon frère`. but `lire` takes a direct object: `je lis le message`. ### sentence-building practice complete or transform each sentence. 1. `je ___ un message à ma mère.` use `écrire`. 2. `nous ___ le journal pendant le petit-déjeuner.` use `lire`. 3. `elle ___ ses chaussures près du lit.` use `mettre`. 4. turn sentence 2 into a question with `vous`. 5. turn sentence 1 into the recent past with `venir de`. 1. `j'écris un message à ma mère.` — `écrire` takes `à` before the person receiving the writing. 2. `nous lisons le journal pendant le petit-déjeuner.` — `lire` has the `nous` form `lisons`. 3. `elle met ses chaussures près du lit.` — `mettre` has the singular form `met`; the final t is silent. 4. `lisez-vous le journal pendant le petit-déjeuner ?` — with inversion, switch verb and pronoun and keep the hyphen. 5. `je viens d'écrire un message à ma mère.` — recent past uses `venir de` plus the infinitive; `de` becomes `d'` before `écrire`. mark yourself wrong if you wrote `j'écris ma mère`. in this meaning, the person needs `à`. ## unit 4: `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, and `devoir` ### core idea these verbs are often followed directly by an infinitive. they express ability, desire, necessity, probability, and debt depending on context. ### present tense forms | pronoun | `pouvoir` | `vouloir` | `devoir` | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `peux` | `veux` | `dois` | | `tu` | `peux` | `veux` | `dois` | | `il/elle/on` | `peut` | `veut` | `doit` | | `nous` | `pouvons` | `voulons` | `devons` | | `vous` | `pouvez` | `voulez` | `devez` | | `ils/elles` | `peuvent` | `veulent` | `doivent` | ### direct link to the infinitive | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---| | `pouvoir + infinitive` | `je peux venir.` | i can come. | | `vouloir + infinitive` | `nous voulons apprendre.` | we want to learn. | | `devoir + infinitive` | `tu dois travailler.` | you must work. | ### the meanings of `devoir` `devoir` has three important uses. 1. obligation: `je dois finir ce travail ce soir.` — i have to finish this work tonight. 2. probability: `il doit être chez lui maintenant.` — he is probably at home now. 3. debt/owing: `je dois dix euros à paul.` — i owe paul ten euros. ### examples in context - `je veux lire davantage, mais je dois finir mon travail.` — i want to read more, but i must finish my work. - `nous pouvons sortir après le cours.` — we can go out after class. - `elle veut recevoir plus de nouvelles de sa famille.` — she wants to receive more news from her family. - `vous devez mettre vos téléphones sur la table.` — you must put your phones on the table. - `ils ne peuvent pas venir ce soir.` — they cannot come tonight. - `on veut partir tôt demain.` — we want to leave early tomorrow. ### common mistake for english speakers do not add `de` or `à` after these three verbs before a second verb. - correct: `je peux venir`, `nous voulons apprendre`, `elle doit partir` - incorrect: `*je peux de venir`, `*nous voulons à apprendre` ### sentence-building practice translate into french. 1. i can read this book. 2. we want to leave now. 3. he probably has to be at the office. make the meaning probability, not obligation. 4. they owe their friend twenty euros. then rewrite sentence 1 in the negative and sentence 2 as a question. 1. `je peux lire ce livre.` — `pouvoir` is followed directly by an infinitive; no `à` or `de`. 2. `nous voulons partir maintenant.` — `vouloir` also links directly to the infinitive. 3. `il doit être au bureau.` — this can mean he is probably at the office; context makes it probability, not obligation. 4. `ils doivent vingt euros à leur ami.` — with a sum of money, `devoir` means to owe. rewrites: - `je ne peux pas lire ce livre.` — the negative wraps `peux`. - `est-ce que nous voulons partir maintenant ?` — a yes/no question can use `est-ce que` with normal word order. do not add a preposition before the infinitive after `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, or `devoir`. ## unit 5: verb + infinitive patterns: no preposition, `à`, or `de` ### core idea french verbs do not all connect to a following infinitive in the same way. some take **no preposition**, some take `à`, and others take `de`. learn the whole frame. ### group 1: no preposition before the infinitive | verb | example | english | |---|---|---| | `aimer` | `j'aime lire le matin.` | i like to read in the morning. | | `préférer` | `nous préférons rester ici.` | we prefer to stay here. | | `aller` | `elle va téléphoner.` | she is going to call. | | `pouvoir` | `tu peux sortir.` | you can go out. | | `vouloir` | `ils veulent apprendre.` | they want to learn. | | `devoir` | `je dois partir.` | i must leave. | | `venir de` | `il vient de finir.` | he has just finished. | ### group 2: verbs followed by `à` | verb + `à` | example | english | |---|---|---| | `commencer à` | `elle commence à apprendre le français.` | she is beginning to learn french. | | `apprendre à` | `nous apprenons à cuisiner.` | we are learning to cook. | | `aider à` | `tu m'aides à porter les sacs ?` | are you helping me carry the bags? | | `réussir à` | `ils réussissent à finir à temps.` | they manage to finish on time. | | `se mettre à` | `je me mets à rire.` | i start laughing. | ### group 3: verbs followed by `de` | verb + `de` | example | english | |---|---|---| | `essayer de` | `j'essaie de parler chaque jour.` | i try to speak every day. | | `arrêter de` | `elle arrête de travailler à six heures.` | she stops working at six o'clock. | | `oublier de` | `nous oublions de fermer la fenêtre.` | we forget to close the window. | | `choisir de` | `ils choisissent de rester.` | they choose to stay. | | `refuser de` | `il refuse de répondre.` | he refuses to answer. | ### how to study the pattern never memorize only `essayer` or only `commencer`. memorize `essayer de`, `commencer à`, `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, `devoir`, and `aller + infinitive` as full frames. ### common mistake for english speakers english often uses \"to\" before the second verb, but french does not always match that structure. `je veux partir` has no preposition, while `je commence à partir` and `j'essaie de partir` do. ### sentence-building practice sort each frame into the correct group, then use it in a complete sentence: `vouloir`, `commencer`, `essayer`, `aimer`, `réussir`, `devoir`. | frame | group | sample sentence | |---|---|---| | `vouloir` | no preposition | `je veux partir tôt.` | | `commencer à` | `à` before infinitive | `elle commence à écrire.` | | `essayer de` | `de` before infinitive | `nous essayons de comprendre.` | | `aimer` | no preposition | `ils aiment lire le soir.` | | `réussir à` | `à` before infinitive | `tu réussis à finir le travail.` | | `devoir` | no preposition | `vous devez répondre aujourd'hui.` | learn the frame, not just the verb. english uses *to* in many of these, but french divides them into no preposition, `à`, and `de`. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine the chapter's new verbs and infinitive patterns the way they appear in real speech. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je veux lire davantage, mais je dois finir mon travail.` — i want to read more, but i must finish my work. 2. `nous pouvons sortir après le cours et écrire le message plus tard.` — we can go out after class and write the message later. 3. `elle commence à apprendre le français et essaie de parler chaque jour.` — she is beginning to learn french and tries to speak every day. 4. `ils quittent le bureau et partent à six heures.` — they leave the office and depart at six o'clock. 5. `je viens de recevoir ta lettre et je vais y répondre ce soir.` — i have just received your letter and i am going to answer it tonight. 6. `vous dites que vous voulez venir, mais vous ne pouvez pas sortir avant neuf heures.` — you say that you want to come, but you cannot go out before nine o'clock. after you write the six sentences from memory, uncover them and check the details your ear may not give you. - **silent endings.** in sentence 4, `quittent` and `partent` both end in silent **-ent**. if you wrote singular forms, the sound did not warn you. - **infinitive links.** sentence 1 has no preposition before `lire` or `finir`; sentence 3 has `à apprendre` and `de parler`; sentence 5 has `de recevoir` after `viens`. - **irregular stems.** check `veux`, `dois`, `pouvons`, `dites`, `voulez`, and `pouvez`. these cannot be guessed from regular -er patterns. - **the object after `quitter`.** sentence 4 says `quittent le bureau`; `quitter` needs the place or person left behind. - **elision and accents.** `j'essaie`, `à`, and `écrire` need the apostrophe or accent you see in the model. - **question spacing.** if you turn any of these into a question later, keep the french space before `?`. give yourself full credit only when the conjugated verb, the infinitive, and the connector before the infinitive all match the original. ### notice and mark for each sentence above: 1. circle every conjugated verb; 2. box each infinitive; 3. label the link before the infinitive as **none**, `à`, or `de`; 4. identify the sentence that uses the recent past. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, time, or activity. then turn sentence 5 into the near future and sentence 6 into a yes/no question. ### make it personal write five new sentences about your own plans or habits. include: - one sentence with `venir de + infinitive`; - one sentence with `pouvoir`; - one sentence with `devoir` meaning obligation; - one sentence with `devoir` meaning probability or debt; - one sentence with a verb followed by `à` or `de`. model check for the cumulative practice: 1. `je veux lire davantage, mais je dois finir mon travail.` — `veux lire` and `dois finir` use no preposition before the infinitive. 2. `nous pouvons sortir après le cours et écrire le message plus tard.` — `pouvons sortir` uses no preposition; `écrire` is another infinitive linked by `et`. 3. `elle commence à apprendre le français et essaie de parler chaque jour.` — `commencer` takes `à`; `essayer` takes `de`. 4. `ils quittent le bureau et partent à six heures.` — `quitter` has a direct object, `le bureau`; `partir` does not need one. 5. `je viens de recevoir ta lettre et je vais y répondre ce soir.` — `viens de recevoir` is recent past; `vais répondre` is near future. 6. `vous dites que vous voulez venir, mais vous ne pouvez pas sortir avant neuf heures.` — `voulez venir` and `pouvez sortir` take no preposition. near-future rewrite of sentence 5: `je vais recevoir ta lettre et je vais y répondre ce soir.` — both near-future forms use conjugated `aller` plus an infinitive. question version of sentence 6: `est-ce que vous dites que vous voulez venir, mais que vous ne pouvez pas sortir avant neuf heures ?` — the grammar is possible, though long; in real speech you might split it into two questions. for your personal sentences, mark yourself wrong if `quitter` has no object, if `venir de` is missing before a recent-past infinitive, or if a verb that needs `à` or `de` is linked directly by mistake. ## mini-dialogue: after work plans **julie :** `tu pars directement après le bureau ce soir ?` — are you leaving directly after the office tonight? **samir :** `non, je viens de recevoir un message de ma sœur.` — no, i have just received a message from my sister. **julie :** `qu'est-ce qu'elle dit ?` — what is she saying? **samir :** `elle veut sortir, mais elle doit d'abord écrire à son professeur.` — she wants to go out, but she first has to write to her professor. **julie :** `et toi, tu peux venir au restaurant avec nous ?` — and you, can you come to the restaurant with us? **samir :** `je peux venir plus tard, mais je dois quitter le bureau à l'heure et passer chez moi.` — i can come later, but i have to leave the office on time and stop by my place. **julie :** `d'accord. nous allons te garder une place près de la fenêtre.` — okay. we are going to save you a seat by the window. **samir :** `parfait. j'essaie de finir ce rapport avant huit heures.` — perfect. i am trying to finish this report before eight o'clock. ### guided analysis 1. find one example each of `partir`, `venir de + infinitive`, `dire`, `vouloir`, `devoir`, and `essayer de`. 2. explain why the dialogue uses `quitter le bureau` instead of `partir le bureau`. 3. rewrite samir's last answer with `nous` instead of `je`. 4. change `elle doit d'abord écrire à son professeur` into the recent past. 5. create another natural sentence using `tenir`, for example one meaning to hold something or to care about something. 1. examples from the dialogue: `tu pars directement après le bureau ce soir ?` uses `pars`; `je viens de recevoir un message de ma sœur` uses recent past; `qu'est-ce qu'elle dit ?` uses `dit`; `elle veut sortir` uses `veut`; `elle doit d'abord écrire` uses `doit`; `j'essaie de finir ce rapport` uses `essaie de`. 2. the dialogue says `quitter le bureau` because `quitter` takes a direct object: the office is the place samir leaves behind. `partir le bureau` is wrong because `partir` does not take the place left as a direct object. 3. samir's last answer with `nous`: `nous essayons de finir ce rapport avant huit heures.` the conjugated verb changes to `essayons`, but `de finir` keeps the same infinitive frame. 4. recent past: `elle vient d'écrire à son professeur.` use `venir de` plus the infinitive, and shorten `de` to `d'` before the vowel sound in `écrire`. 5. a natural sentence with `tenir`: `je tiens la porte pour vous.` this means i am holding the door; for caring about something, say `nous tenons beaucoup à ce projet.` ## connected reading: un nouveau rythme `depuis le début du semestre, ma routine change beaucoup. je pars plus tôt le matin parce que je veux lire dans le bus avant les cours. après la classe, je sors souvent avec deux amis, mais je quitte la bibliothèque seulement quand je finis vraiment mon travail. nous venons de commencer un projet important, alors nous devons écrire beaucoup et nous recevons des messages du professeur presque tous les jours. le soir, j'essaie de me détendre, mais je ne peux pas toujours arrêter de penser au travail. heureusement, ma sœur vient parfois dîner avec moi, et nous tenons encore à notre petit repas du jeudi.` — since the beginning of the semester, my routine has been changing a lot. i leave earlier in the morning because i want to read on the bus before class. after class, i often go out with two friends, but i leave the library only when i truly finish my work. we have just started an important project, so we have to write a lot and we receive messages from the professor almost every day. in the evening, i try to relax, but i cannot always stop thinking about work. fortunately, my sister sometimes comes to have dinner with me, and we still care about our little thursday meal. ### reading practice 1. find three different movement verbs and explain the nuance of each one. 2. underline every infinitive and identify the frame before it. 3. find the sentence with `venir de + infinitive` and explain the time meaning. 4. change the first two sentences from `je` to `nous`. 5. write three true sentences about your own schedule using the reading as a model. 1. **movement verbs:** `je pars plus tôt` focuses on departure; `je sors souvent avec deux amis` means i go out socially; `je quitte la bibliothèque` needs the direct object, the library left behind. the reading also has `ma sœur vient parfois dîner`, meaning she comes. 2. **infinitives and frames:** `lire` after `veux` has no preposition; `commencer` after `venons de` is recent past with `de`; `écrire` after `devons` has no preposition; `me détendre` after `essaie de` uses `de`; `arrêter` after `peux` has no preposition; `penser` after `arrêter de` uses `de`; `dîner` after `vient` is a purpose infinitive in this sentence. 3. the recent past sentence is `nous venons de commencer un projet important`. it means the project has just started, not that we come from a place. 4. first two sentences with `nous`: `depuis le début du semestre, notre routine change beaucoup. nous partons plus tôt le matin parce que nous voulons lire dans le bus avant les cours.` the main changes are `ma` to `notre`, `je pars` to `nous partons`, and `je veux` to `nous voulons`. 5. answers vary. a model set: `je pars tôt le lundi parce que je veux lire dans le train.` `je viens de commencer un nouveau livre.` `le soir, j'essaie de ne pas penser au travail.` mark yours wrong if an infinitive connector is missing or if `quitter` has no object. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. **using `quitter` without an object.** say `je pars maintenant`, but `je quitte la maison maintenant`. 2. **confusing origin with recent past.** `venir de paris` is not the same as `venir de partir`. 3. **adding an article before an infinitive.** write `je veux partir`, not `*je veux le partir`. 4. **using the wrong connector before the infinitive.** `commencer à`, but `essayer de`. 5. **forgetting that `écrire` often uses `à` with a person.** `j'écris à ma tante`. 6. **missing the special `nous` and `vous` forms.** `nous venons`, `vous dites`, `nous écrivons`, `nous pouvons`, `vous voulez`. ## chapter reference sheet ### movement and arrival verbs | pronoun | `partir` | `sortir` | `quitter` | `venir` | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `pars` | `sors` | `quitte` | `viens` | | `tu` | `pars` | `sors` | `quittes` | `viens` | | `il/elle/on` | `part` | `sort` | `quitte` | `vient` | | `nous` | `partons` | `sortons` | `quittons` | `venons` | | `vous` | `partez` | `sortez` | `quittez` | `venez` | | `ils/elles` | `partent` | `sortent` | `quittent` | `viennent` | ### communication and action verbs | pronoun | `dire` | `lire` | `écrire` | `mettre` | `recevoir` | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `dis` | `lis` | `écris` | `mets` | `reçois` | | `tu` | `dis` | `lis` | `écris` | `mets` | `reçois` | | `il/elle/on` | `dit` | `lit` | `écrit` | `met` | `reçoit` | | `nous` | `disons` | `lisons` | `écrivons` | `mettons` | `recevons` | | `vous` | `dites` | `lisez` | `écrivez` | `mettez` | `recevez` | | `ils/elles` | `disent` | `lisent` | `écrivent` | `mettent` | `reçoivent` | ### modal verbs | pronoun | `pouvoir` | `vouloir` | `devoir` | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `peux` | `veux` | `dois` | | `tu` | `peux` | `veux` | `dois` | | `il/elle/on` | `peut` | `veut` | `doit` | | `nous` | `pouvons` | `voulons` | `devons` | | `vous` | `pouvez` | `voulez` | `devez` | | `ils/elles` | `peuvent` | `veulent` | `doivent` | ### verb + infinitive quick chart | pattern | common verbs | |---|---| | no preposition | `aller`, `aimer`, `préférer`, `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, `devoir` | | `à + infinitive` | `commencer à`, `apprendre à`, `aider à`, `réussir à`, `se mettre à` | | `de + infinitive` | `venir de`, `essayer de`, `arrêter de`, `oublier de`, `choisir de`, `refuser de` | ### core habits - learn each verb with a useful phrase and a contrasting verb where possible. - check whether the second verb needs no preposition, `à`, or `de`. - use `venir de + infinitive` for something that happened a moment ago. - keep the infinitive unchanged; only the first verb is conjugated. ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a busy day, a work routine, or plans for the week. include: - one sentence with `partir`, one with `sortir`, and one with `quitter`; - one sentence with `venir de + infinitive`; - two different verbs from `dire`, `lire`, `écrire`, `mettre`, or `recevoir`; - one sentence with `pouvoir`, one with `vouloir`, and one with `devoir`; - one verb followed by `à + infinitive` and one followed by `de + infinitive`. after writing: 1. circle every conjugated verb; 2. underline every infinitive; 3. label each infinitive link as **none**, `à`, or `de`; 4. check whether every use of `quitter` has a direct object; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary, so compare your paragraph to the checklist. here is one model that satisfies every requirement: `le lundi, je pars tôt parce que je veux lire dans le bus. après le travail, je sors avec mes collègues, mais je quitte le bureau seulement à six heures. je viens de recevoir un message de ma sœur. elle dit qu'elle veut dîner avec moi ce soir. je peux venir au restaurant, mais je dois finir un rapport avant huit heures. je mets mon téléphone sur la table et j'écris une réponse courte. ensuite, je commence à préparer mon sac et j'essaie de rester calme.` how it satisfies the brief: `je pars`, `je sors`, and `je quitte le bureau` cover the three leaving verbs; `je viens de recevoir` is recent past; `dit`, `mets`, and `écris` come from the communication/action group; `peux`, `veut`, and `dois` appear with infinitives or complements; `commence à préparer` uses `à`; and `essaie de rester` uses `de`. mark yourself wrong if you have: - `quitter` without a direct object; - `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, or `devoir` followed by `à` or `de` before an infinitive; - `commencer de` or `essayer à`; - a conjugated second verb where an infinitive is required; - missing accents in forms such as `à`, `écris`, or `réponse`. "},{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 7 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-exercises\">Chapter 7 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I can read.</li>\n<li>We want to leave.</li>\n<li>She begins to write.</li>\n<li>They try to understand.</li>\n<li>I leave the house.</li>\n<li>We go out tonight.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux lire.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code> is followed directly by the infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>; no preposition appears.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons partir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code> also takes the infinitive directly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à écrire.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> requires <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before a following infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils essaient de comprendre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code> requires <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> before a following infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je quitte la maison.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> needs a direct object, here <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sortons ce soir.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code> for going out.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>movement and arrival</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars demain.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>communication and action</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous dites la vérité.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>ability, desire, obligation</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux venir.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>verb + verb patterns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle aime lire.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct answer must demonstrate the named structure and use the correct verb frame.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Movement and arrival: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars pour Lyon demain.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> works without a direct object.</li>\n<li>Communication and action: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous écrivons à nos amis.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before the people receiving the message.</li>\n<li>Ability, desire, obligation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils doivent finir le travail.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> is followed directly by the infinitive.</li>\n<li>Verb + verb patterns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à lire le chapitre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before the infinitive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> appears without an object, if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> to a person lacks <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, or if you add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux lire ce livre.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons lire ce livre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code> stays infinitive.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne veux pas lire ce livre.</code> — The negative wraps the conjugated verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu veux lire ce livre ?</code> — The second verb stays infinitive.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux écrire ce message.</code> — The activity changed, but the no-preposition frame after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code> stayed the same.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check that only the conjugated verb changes for subject agreement. The infinitive should not be conjugated.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars demain matin.</code> — The final <strong>s</strong> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pars</code> is silent; the subject tells you it is first person.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous venons de dîner.</code> — This is recent past. Listen for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> before the infinitive and the accent in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dîner</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous écrivons à nos amis chaque semaine.</code> — The trap is the <strong>v</strong> sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrivons</code> and the required <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before the people.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils ne peuvent pas venir ce soir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peuvent</code> has a special plural stem, and the negative wraps the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à apprendre le français.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before the infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'essaie de finir ce rapport avant huit heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> elides to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> before the infinitive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct in separate passes. A missing silent ending is a verb-form problem; a missing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> is a frame problem.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write eight decisions for the coming month using pouvoir, vouloir, devoir, commencer à, and essayer de.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux lire un livre en français.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux étudier vingt minutes chaque matin.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dois finir ce chapitre avant vendredi.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais commencer à écrire un petit journal.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais essayer de parler français au café.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons sortir moins tard le soir.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dois répondre à mes messages plus vite.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux mettre mon téléphone loin de mon bureau.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: include all five required patterns. Mark yourself wrong if you wrote <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencer de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essayer à</code>, or added a preposition after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> before the infinitive.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Movement and arrival.</strong> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> for departure, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code> for going out, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quitter</code> with a direct object. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je quitte le bureau à six heures.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Communication and action.</strong> Learn irregular forms such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code> in whole phrases. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous écrivons à nos amis.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Ability, desire, obligation.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> are followed directly by an infinitive. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux venir.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Verb + verb patterns.</strong> Some verbs take no preposition, some take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, and some take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à écrire et essaie de comprendre.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If your example does not match the rule you named, redo the rule aloud with a different sentence.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 7 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i can read. 2. we want to leave. 3. she begins to write. 4. they try to understand. 5. i leave the house. 6. we go out tonight. 1. `je peux lire.` — `pouvoir` is followed directly by the infinitive `lire`; no preposition appears. 2. `nous voulons partir.` — `vouloir` also takes the infinitive directly. 3. `elle commence à écrire.` — `commencer` requires `à` before a following infinitive. 4. `ils essaient de comprendre.` — `essayer` requires `de` before a following infinitive. 5. `je quitte la maison.` — `quitter` needs a direct object, here `la maison`. 6. `nous sortons ce soir.` — use `sortir` for going out. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **movement and arrival**. use `je pars demain.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **communication and action**. use `vous dites la vérité.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **ability, desire, obligation**. use `je peux venir.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **verb + verb patterns**. use `elle aime lire.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct answer must demonstrate the named structure and use the correct verb frame. sample answers: 1. movement and arrival: `je pars pour lyon demain.` — `partir` works without a direct object. 2. communication and action: `nous écrivons à nos amis.` — `écrire` needs `à` before the people receiving the message. 3. ability, desire, obligation: `ils doivent finir le travail.` — `devoir` is followed directly by the infinitive. 4. verb + verb patterns: `elle commence à lire le chapitre.` — `commencer` takes `à` before the infinitive. mark yourself wrong if `quitter` appears without an object, if `écrire` to a person lacks `à`, or if you add `à` or `de` after `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, or `devoir`. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `je veux lire ce livre.` 1. change the subject: `nous voulons lire ce livre.` — `veux` changes to `voulons`; `lire` stays infinitive. 2. make it negative: `je ne veux pas lire ce livre.` — the negative wraps the conjugated verb `veux`. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que tu veux lire ce livre ?` — the second verb stays infinitive. 4. change one meaningful detail: `je veux écrire ce message.` — the activity changed, but the no-preposition frame after `vouloir` stayed the same. check that only the conjugated verb changes for subject agreement. the infinitive should not be conjugated. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `je pars demain matin.` — the final **s** in `pars` is silent; the subject tells you it is first person. 2. `nous venons de dîner.` — this is recent past. listen for `de` before the infinitive and the accent in `dîner`. 3. `nous écrivons à nos amis chaque semaine.` — the trap is the **v** sound in `écrivons` and the required `à` before the people. 4. `ils ne peuvent pas venir ce soir.` — `peuvent` has a special plural stem, and the negative wraps the conjugated verb. 5. `elle commence à apprendre le français.` — `commencer` takes `à` before the infinitive. 6. `j'essaie de finir ce rapport avant huit heures.` — `je` elides to `j'`, and `essayer` takes `de` before the infinitive. correct in separate passes. a missing silent ending is a verb-form problem; a missing `à` or `de` is a frame problem. ## e. personal writing write eight decisions for the coming month using pouvoir, vouloir, devoir, commencer à, and essayer de. answers vary. a model set: 1. `je veux lire un livre en français.` 2. `je peux étudier vingt minutes chaque matin.` 3. `je dois finir ce chapitre avant vendredi.` 4. `je vais commencer à écrire un petit journal.` 5. `je vais essayer de parler français au café.` 6. `nous voulons sortir moins tard le soir.` 7. `je dois répondre à mes messages plus vite.` 8. `je peux mettre mon téléphone loin de mon bureau.` self-check: include all five required patterns. mark yourself wrong if you wrote `commencer de`, `essayer à`, or added a preposition after `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, or `devoir` before the infinitive. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **movement and arrival.** use `partir` for departure, `sortir` for going out, and `quitter` with a direct object. example: `je quitte le bureau à six heures.` 2. **communication and action.** learn irregular forms such as `dire`, `lire`, `écrire`, `mettre`, and `recevoir` in whole phrases. example: `nous écrivons à nos amis.` 3. **ability, desire, obligation.** `pouvoir`, `vouloir`, and `devoir` are followed directly by an infinitive. example: `je peux venir.` 4. **verb + verb patterns.** some verbs take no preposition, some take `à`, and some take `de`. example: `elle commence à écrire et essaie de comprendre.` if your example does not match the rule you named, redo the rule aloud with a different sentence. "},{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 7 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-answer-key\">Chapter 7 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux lire.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons partir.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à écrire.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils essaient de comprendre.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je quitte la maison.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sortons ce soir.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Movement and arrival:</strong> Partir means to leave/depart, sortir means to go out or take out, quitter takes a direct object, and venir means to come.</li>\n<li><strong>Communication and action:</strong> Practice dire, lire, écrire, mettre, recevoir, and related forms as complete phrases.</li>\n<li><strong>Ability, desire, obligation:</strong> Pouvoir, vouloir, and devoir are followed directly by an infinitive.</li>\n<li><strong>Verb + verb patterns:</strong> Some verbs take no preposition, some take à, and others take de before an infinitive. Memorize the whole frame.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 7 answer key ## section a 1. `je peux lire.` 2. `nous voulons partir.` 3. `elle commence à écrire.` 4. `ils essaient de comprendre.` 5. `je quitte la maison.` 6. `nous sortons ce soir.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **movement and arrival:** partir means to leave/depart, sortir means to go out or take out, quitter takes a direct object, and venir means to come. - **communication and action:** practice dire, lire, écrire, mettre, recevoir, and related forms as complete phrases. - **ability, desire, obligation:** pouvoir, vouloir, and devoir are followed directly by an infinitive. - **verb + verb patterns:** some verbs take no preposition, some take à, and others take de before an infinitive. memorize the whole frame. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 7 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 7 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Movement and arrival</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Communication and action</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Ability, desire, obligation</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Verb + verb patterns</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 7 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **movement and arrival**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **communication and action** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **ability, desire, obligation**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **verb + verb patterns**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":8,"slug":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns","title":"Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns","documents":[{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/README.md","title":"Chapter 8: Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-irregular-verbs-ii-and-relative-pronouns\">Chapter 8: Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Distinguish savoir and connaître.</li>\n<li>Use additional irregular verb families.</li>\n<li>Join ideas with qui, que, où, and dont.</li>\n<li>Use lequel after selected prepositions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Savoir versus connaître</li>\n<li>Irregular verb families</li>\n<li>Qui, que, and où</li>\n<li>Dont and lequel</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 8: irregular verbs ii and relative pronouns this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - distinguish savoir and connaître. - use additional irregular verb families. - join ideas with qui, que, où, and dont. - use lequel after selected prepositions. ## lesson sequence 1. savoir versus connaître 2. irregular verb families 3. qui, que, and où 4. dont and lequel ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 8 Lessons: Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-lessons-irregular-verbs-ii-and-relative-pronouns\">Chapter 8 Lessons: Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This chapter helps you speak more precisely about knowledge, perception, belief, movement, and connection between ideas. You will learn several irregular verb families and then use relative pronouns to join two short sentences into one smoother, more natural French sentence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>distinguish <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> accurately;</li>\n<li>conjugate and use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rire</code>, the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir</code> group, the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduire</code> group, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivre</code>, and the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craindre</code> group;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> to connect clauses;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> and forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code> after prepositions;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code> when there is no named antecedent;</li>\n<li>read and create longer sentences without losing track of the subject and object.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>recognize a subject and a direct object in a simple sentence;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> with confidence from Chapter 6;</li>\n<li>build two-verb frames such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je veux partir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'essaie de comprendre</code> from Chapter 7;</li>\n<li>hear the difference between a conjugated form and an infinitive.</li>\n<p>You will need those earlier skills immediately. For example, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre que je lis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'étudiante à laquelle j'écris</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont nous avons besoin</code> all depend on knowing what the verb controls.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test the foundation before you add relative pronouns. Answer these five without notes.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lis le livre</code>, what is the direct object ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code>. It receives the action of reading directly.</li>\n<li>Choose the right preposition: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle ___ mon ami</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai besoin ___ ce livre</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle à mon ami</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai besoin de ce livre</code>. These prepositions matter later for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> and forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code>.</li>\n<li>Identify the infinitive in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'essaie de comprendre</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprendre</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">essaie</code> is conjugated; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de comprendre</code> is the verb frame.</li>\n<li>Which is conjugated and which is infinitive: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle veut partir</code> ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veut</code> is conjugated, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code> is infinitive. Only the first verb changes for the subject.</li>\n<li>Split <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre que je lis</code> into two ideas. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est le livre. Je lis le livre.</code> The repeated noun is the object in the second idea, so later it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review direct objects, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, and Chapter 7 verb frames first. Relative pronouns are much easier when you can see the missing role.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>When you study the relative pronouns, split each sentence into two shorter ideas and identify the role of the missing word. Ask yourself: is it the subject, the object, a place/time word, or part of a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> phrase?</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-savoir-versus-conna-tre\">Unit 1: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Both verbs translate as \"to know,\" but they divide the job differently.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> = to know a fact, to know information, to know how to do something</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> = to know a person, a place, or something through familiarity</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-forms\">Present tense forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sais</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sais</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sait</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaît</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"how-the-meanings-differ\">How the meanings differ</h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> with information or with an infinitive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais la réponse.</code> — I know the answer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle sait conduire.</code> — She knows how to drive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous savons que le musée ferme à six heures.</code> — We know that the museum closes at six.</li>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> with people, places, and familiarity.</p>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.</code> — I know the woman who runs this company.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous connaissons Paris assez bien.</code> — We know Paris fairly well.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu connais ce film ?</code> — Do you know this film? / Are you familiar with this film?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Connaissons</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissent</code> look related, but they sound very different. Say the whole forms aloud: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous connaissons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils connaissent</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> before an infinitive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle sait nager.</code> — She knows how to swim.</li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Elle connaît nager.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> and write the full sentence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>I know your brother.</li>\n<li>We know how to solve the problem.</li>\n<li>Do you know this neighborhood?</li>\n<li>They know that the restaurant is closed.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais ton frère.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> for a person you are familiar with.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous savons résoudre le problème.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> before an infinitive for knowing how to do something.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu connais ce quartier ?</code> — A neighborhood is a place you know through familiarity, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils savent que le restaurant est fermé.</code> — A fact introduced by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The trap is translating English <em>know</em> automatically. Ask whether the object is a person/place/familiar thing, or a fact/skill.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-voir-croire-courir-and-rire\">Unit 2: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rire</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>These verbs often describe perception, belief, and quick everyday actions. They are irregular in different ways, so learn them as a family of high-frequency forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-forms\">Present tense forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rire</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cours</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ris</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cours</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ris</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">court</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croyons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rions</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croyez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">riez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voient</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croient</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rient</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois le problème maintenant.</code> — I see the problem now.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu crois tout ce qu'il dit ?</code> — Do you believe everything he says?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous courons dans le parc le matin.</code> — We run in the park in the morning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles rient toujours aux mêmes blagues.</code> — They always laugh at the same jokes.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On voit les montagnes de notre fenêtre.</code> — We can see the mountains from our window.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne crois pas cette histoire.</code> — I do not believe that story.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyons</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croyez</code> changes the sound of the stem. Listen especially for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous voyons</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous voyez</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not overtranslate English \"to think\" as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croire</code> in every case. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Croire</code> is about belief: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je crois que...</code> means \"I believe / I think that...\" in the sense of opinion, not mental effort or planning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Translate into French.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>We see the station from here.</li>\n<li>She believes that you are right.</li>\n<li>They run every Saturday.</li>\n<li>Why are you laughing?</li>\n<p>Then change sentence 2 to the negative.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voyons la gare d'ici.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code> has the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle croit que tu as raison.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croire que</code> introduces a belief or opinion.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils courent tous les samedis.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courir</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courent</code> with plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>; the -ent ending is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce que tu ris ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rire</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu ris</code>, and the question word <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourquoi</code> asks for the reason.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Negative form of sentence 2:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne croit pas que tu aies raison.</code> — The negative wraps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croit</code>. The form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aies</code> is a later subjunctive form used after negated belief; for now, copy the whole sentence as a chunk.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-other-irregular-verb-families\">Unit 3: Other irregular verb families</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>This unit groups several important patterns that often surprise learners: the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir</code> family, the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduire</code> family, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivre</code>, and the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craindre</code> family.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-offrir-family\">The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir</code> family</h3>\n<p>These verbs look like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> verbs but behave in many ways like common <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs in the present tense.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souffrir</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souffre</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offres</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvres</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souffres</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souffre</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souffrons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souffrez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souffrent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle offre un livre à sa mère.</code> — She gives her mother a book.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ouvrons les fenêtres.</code> — We open the windows.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il souffre du dos.</code> — He suffers from back pain.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-conduire-family-and-related-verbs\">The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduire</code> family and related verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craindre</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craint</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduisons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craignons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduisez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craignez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduisent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craignent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu conduis souvent la nuit ?</code> — Do you often drive at night?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous suivons ce cours ensemble.</code> — We are following this course together.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes grands-parents vivent à la campagne.</code> — My grandparents live in the countryside.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je crains le froid en hiver.</code> — I fear the cold in winter.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici la route que nous suivons chaque été.</code> — Here is the road that we follow every summer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vivent dans un quartier que je connais bien.</code> — They live in a neighborhood that I know well.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not confuse <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> when you see it without context. The subject and the rest of the sentence tell you which verb is meant.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Complete the sentence with the right verb and form.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ___ un guide très précis.</code> — follow</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ___ la porte pour laisser entrer le chat.</code> — open</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ___ toujours les règles de sécurité.</code> — follow / obey in the sense of follow</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils ___ une vie simple près de la mer.</code> — live</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu ___ vraiment conduire jusqu'à Marseille ?</code> — know how / be driving; choose the correct chapter verb if needed.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous suivons un guide très précis.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code> means to follow, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ouvre la porte pour laisser entrer le chat.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrir</code> belongs to the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir</code> family and uses regular-looking -er endings in the singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis toujours les règles de sécurité.</code> — Here <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> comes from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code>, meaning I follow or obey; context prevents confusion with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vivent une vie simple près de la mer.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivre</code> has the plural form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivent</code>; the -ent ending is silent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu sais vraiment conduire jusqu'à Marseille ?</code> — Knowing how to do something uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> + infinitive, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The main trap is sentence 3: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je suis</code> can mean I am or I follow. Read the object that follows before deciding.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-qui-que-and-o\">Unit 4: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Relative pronouns connect two ideas. They replace a repeated noun and begin a relative clause.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> = subject of the relative clause</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'</code> = direct object of the relative clause</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> = place or time</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-choose-the-form\">How to choose the form</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Relative pronoun</th>\n<th>What it replaces</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code></td>\n<td>the subject</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La femme qui parle...</code></td>\n<td>the woman who is speaking...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code></td>\n<td>the direct object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le livre que je lis...</code></td>\n<td>the book that I am reading...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code></td>\n<td>place or time</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La ville où j'habite...</code></td>\n<td>the city where I live...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"building-the-sentence-step-by-step\">Building the sentence step by step</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais la femme. La femme dirige cette entreprise.</code></li>\n<li>Replace the repeated noun as the <strong>subject</strong> of the second clause.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.</code> — I know the woman who runs this company.</li>\n<p>More examples:</p>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.</code> — Here is the book that my teacher recommends.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montréal est la ville où ils veulent vivre.</code> — Montreal is the city where they want to live.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le jour où nous partons arrive vite.</code> — The day when we are leaving is coming quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois les enfants qui jouent dans la cour.</code> — I see the children who are playing in the yard.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Que</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'</code> before a vowel sound: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le film qu'elle aime</code>. The function stays the same even when the spelling shortens.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> just because English uses \"who.\" Ask what role the missing word plays inside the relative clause.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la femme qui parle</code> — she is the subject of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la femme que je vois</code> — she is the object of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vois</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Combine each pair into one sentence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je regarde un film. Le film commence à huit heures.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous visitons une ville. Mes parents connaissent cette ville.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le café. Nous allons souvent dans ce café.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est le jour. Elle arrive ce jour-là.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je regarde un film qui commence à huit heures.</code> — The film is the subject of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code>, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous visitons une ville que mes parents connaissent.</code> — The city is the direct object of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissent</code>, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le café où nous allons souvent.</code> — The café is a place, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est le jour où elle arrive.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> can also refer to time, not only physical places.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do not choose by the English word <em>who</em> or <em>that</em> alone. Choose by the missing role inside the second clause: subject, object, or place/time.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-dont-forms-of-lequel-and-indefinite-relatives\">Unit 5: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>, forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code>, and indefinite relatives</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> when the second clause needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>. Use forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code> after a preposition when you need \"which one\" or when a more explicit prepositional relative is useful. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code> when there is no named noun before the clause.</p>\n<h3 id=\"dont\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>verb + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un métier dont elle parle souvent.</code></td>\n<td>It is a profession that she often talks about.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir besoin de</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le document dont j'ai besoin.</code></td>\n<td>Here is the document I need.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>possession link</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'étudiant dont la sœur habite ici vient du Chili.</code></td>\n<td>The student whose sister lives here comes from Chile.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"forms-of-lequel\">Forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code></td>\n<td>which one / which masculine singular one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">laquelle</code></td>\n<td>which feminine singular one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquels</code></td>\n<td>which masculine or mixed plural ones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquelles</code></td>\n<td>which feminine plural ones</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>After <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, these forms contract.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Base form</th>\n<th>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code></th>\n<th>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auquel</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">duquel</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">laquelle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à laquelle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de laquelle</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquels</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auxquels</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">desquels</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquelles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auxquelles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">desquelles</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le projet auquel je pense change souvent.</code> — The project I am thinking about changes often.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La chaise sur laquelle il pose son sac est cassée.</code> — The chair on which he puts his bag is broken.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les collègues avec lesquels je travaille sont patients.</code> — The coworkers with whom I work are patient.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne sais pas de laquelle de ces routes tu parles.</code> — I do not know which of these roads you are talking about.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"ce-qui-ce-que-and-ce-dont\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code></h3>\n<p>Use these forms when there is <strong>no named antecedent</strong> before the relative clause.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Role in the clause</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code></td>\n<td>subject</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je comprends ce qui se passe.</code></td>\n<td>I understand what is happening.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qu'</code></td>\n<td>direct object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dis-moi ce que tu veux.</code></td>\n<td>Tell me what you want.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> phrase</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voilà ce dont nous avons besoin.</code></td>\n<td>That is what we need.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"turning-a-question-into-a-statement\">Turning a question into a statement</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qu'est-ce qui se passe ?</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais ce qui se passe.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qu'est-ce que tu cherches ?</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois ce que tu cherches.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">De quoi parlent-ils ?</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je comprends ce dont ils parlent.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code> when the missing word is the subject. Compare:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais ce qui arrive.</code> — I know what is happening.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais ce que tu lis.</code> — I know what you are reading.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Choose the correct form: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est le problème ___ nous discutons.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne comprends pas ___ te fait rire.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici la table sur ___ nous allons manger.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dis-moi ___ tu crois.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voilà ___ j'ai besoin pour le voyage.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est le problème dont nous discutons.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">discuter de</code> needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> replaces that de-phrase.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne comprends pas ce qui te fait rire.</code> — The missing idea is the subject of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait rire</code>, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici la table sur laquelle nous allons manger.</code> — After the preposition <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sur</code>, use the feminine singular form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">laquelle</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">table</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dis-moi ce que tu crois.</code> — The missing idea is the direct object of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crois</code>, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voilà ce dont j'ai besoin pour le voyage.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir besoin de</code> requires <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, so the unnamed thing is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The trap is forgetting the preposition hidden inside <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code>. If the expression needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, do not replace it with plain <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine the chapter's verb families and relative pronouns in longer, more natural French. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.</code> — I know the woman who runs this company.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.</code> — Here is the book that my teacher recommends.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montréal est la ville où ils veulent vivre.</code> — Montreal is the city where they want to live.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un métier dont elle parle souvent.</code> — It is a profession that she often talks about.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je comprends ce que tu veux, mais je ne crois pas que ce soit facile.</code> — I understand what you want, but I do not believe that it is easy.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les amis avec lesquels nous courons vivent dans le quartier que tu connais.</code> — The friends with whom we run live in the neighborhood that you know.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>After writing the six sentences from memory, check the parts that are easy to lose in a long sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relative pronoun role.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> is the subject in sentence 1; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> is the object in sentences 2 and 6; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> marks place in sentence 3; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> replaces a <strong>de</strong> phrase in sentence 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Indefinite relatives.</strong> Sentence 5 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que tu veux</code>, with no named antecedent before it.</li>\n<li><strong>Forms after prepositions.</strong> Sentence 6 uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec lesquels</code> because it follows <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code> and refers to plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amis</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Elision.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'</code> before a vowel sound need apostrophes.</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular verbs.</strong> Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivent</code>; none follows a simple regular -er pattern.</li>\n<li><strong>Silent endings.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivent</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code> contain letters you may not hear clearly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Score the sentence as wrong if the relative pronoun no longer matches its job inside the clause, even if the English translation still sounds close.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence above:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>underline the antecedent before each relative pronoun when there is one;</li>\n<li>label the role of each pronoun as <strong>subject</strong>, <strong>object</strong>, <strong>place/time</strong>, or <strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> phrase</strong>;</li>\n<li>identify the one sentence with an indefinite relative pronoun.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the person, place, profession, or object.</li>\n<p>Then replace the relative pronoun in sentence 6 with a different correct structure if possible.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write five new sentences about your own life. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> or a form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model check for the cumulative practice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> is the subject of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dirige</code>; the woman is doing the directing.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> is the direct object of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recommande</code>; the professor recommends the book.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montréal est la ville où ils veulent vivre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> refers to a place.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un métier dont elle parle souvent.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler de</code> needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> is required.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je comprends ce que tu veux, mais je ne crois pas que ce soit facile.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code> is the direct object of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code>. The form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soit</code> belongs to a later grammar point; here, just recognize and copy the whole sentence.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les amis avec lesquels nous courons vivent dans le quartier que tu connais.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec lesquels</code> follows a preposition and agrees with plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amis</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> is the object of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For sentence 6, another correct structure is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les amis avec qui nous courons vivent dans le quartier que tu connais.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec qui</code> is often used for people and avoids the agreement of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lesquels</code>.</p>\n<p>Personal answers vary. Mark yourself wrong if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> is used for a person, if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> is used before an infinitive, or if a relative pronoun does not match its role inside the clause.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-choosing-a-research-topic\">Mini-dialogue: choosing a research topic</h2>\n<p><strong>Lina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu connais le professeur qui anime le séminaire du jeudi ?</code> — Do you know the professor who leads the Thursday seminar? <strong>Marc :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, je le connais un peu, mais je ne sais pas tout ce qu'il attend.</code> — Yes, I know him a little, but I do not know everything he expects. <strong>Lina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois. Moi, je cherche un sujet dont je peux parler avec plaisir.</code> — I see. I am looking for a topic that I can talk about with pleasure. <strong>Marc :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu peux choisir le film dont tu me parles souvent.</code> — You can choose the film that you often tell me about. <strong>Lina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Peut-être. Tu crois que c'est un sujet auquel il va s'intéresser ?</code> — Maybe. Do you think it is a topic that he is going to be interested in? <strong>Marc :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, parce qu'il aime tout ce qui touche à la mémoire et à la ville.</code> — Yes, because he likes everything that has to do with memory and the city. <strong>Lina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dans ce cas, je vais suivre ton conseil et écrire ce que je pense ce soir.</code> — In that case, I am going to follow your advice and write what I think tonight. <strong>Marc :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parfait. Dis-moi demain ce dont tu as encore besoin.</code> — Perfect. Tell me tomorrow what you still need.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find one example each of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auquel</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code>.</li>\n<li>Explain why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le connais</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne sais pas</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite Lina's first line with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu peux choisir le film dont tu me parles souvent</code> into two short sentences.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout ce qui touche à la mémoire</code> with a version using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code> if possible; if not, explain why not.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>The dialogue gives all six: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le professeur qui anime le séminaire</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sujet dont je peux parler</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le film dont tu me parles souvent</code> have <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sujet auquel il va s'intéresser</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">auquel</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout ce qui touche à la mémoire</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que je pense</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont tu as encore besoin</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code>.</li>\n<li>Marc says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le connais</code> because he knows a person, the professor, through familiarity. He says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne sais pas tout ce qu'il attend</code> because that is information or a fact he does not know.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous connaissez le professeur qui anime le séminaire du jeudi ?</code> The verb changes from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissez</code>; the relative clause stays the same because the professor is still the subject of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">anime</code>.</li>\n<li>Two short sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu peux choisir le film. Tu me parles souvent de ce film.</code> The second sentence contains <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de ce film</code>, which is why the joined version uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>.</li>\n<li>You cannot replace it naturally with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code> here, because the missing idea is the subject of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">touche à la mémoire</code>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code> for an unnamed subject; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code> would be for an unnamed direct object.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-un-quartier-qu-on-apprend-aimer\">Connected reading: un quartier qu'on apprend à aimer</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite maintenant dans un quartier que je connais encore mal. Au début, je ne sais pas toujours où faire mes courses ni quels bus suivre. Peu à peu, je connais de mieux en mieux les rues qui montent vers le parc et les cafés où les étudiants rient pendant des heures. J'aime la petite librairie dont la propriétaire recommande toujours un roman surprenant. Le samedi, je vois des voisins avec lesquels je parle parfois, et je crois que c'est cela qui me fait rester ici avec plaisir. Ce que j'aime le plus, pourtant, c'est la place centrale : c'est là que la ville semble vraiment vivre.</code> — I now live in a neighborhood that I still do not know very well. At first, I do not always know where to do my shopping or which buses to follow. Little by little, I know better and better the streets that climb toward the park and the cafés where students laugh for hours. I like the small bookstore whose owner always recommends a surprising novel. On Saturdays, I see neighbors with whom I sometimes speak, and I believe that that is what makes me stay here happily. What I like most, however, is the central square: that is where the city really seems to live.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List every relative pronoun in the reading and give its role.</li>\n<li>Find one example of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> and two of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code>; explain the difference.</li>\n<li>Underline the verbs from the irregular families studied in this chapter.</li>\n<li>Change the last two sentences from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Write three true sentences about a place you know using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Relative pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je connais encore mal</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> as object; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui montent vers le parc</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> as subject; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où les étudiants rient</code> marks place; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont la propriétaire recommande...</code> shows possession; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec lesquels je parle</code> follows the preposition <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui me fait rester</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> as subject; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce que j'aime</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code> as object. The phrase <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où faire mes courses</code> is an indirect question of place, not a relative pronoun, so do not count it for this item.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne sais pas toujours où faire mes courses</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> for information. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un quartier que je connais encore mal</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je connais de mieux en mieux les rues</code> use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> for familiarity with a place.</li>\n<li>Irregular-family verbs include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rient</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crois</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivre</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Semble</code> is not from this chapter's irregular families.</li>\n<li>Last two sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, nous voyons des voisins avec lesquels nous parlons parfois, et nous croyons que c'est cela qui nous fait rester ici avec plaisir. Ce que nous aimons le plus, pourtant, c'est la place centrale : c'est là que la ville semble vraiment vivre.</code> The main changes are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vois</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je crois</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous croyons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aime</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous aimons</code>.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. A model set: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais un café où les étudiants travaillent.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime la rue qui monte vers le parc.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce que j'aime le plus, c'est la bibliothèque.</code> Mark yours wrong if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> is used for a place or if a relative pronoun has the wrong role.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> for people and places.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je connais ce professeur</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je sais ce professeur</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Choosing the relative pronoun from English meaning alone.</strong> Check the role inside the clause first.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> inside <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>.</strong> If the verb or expression needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> is often the right choice.</li>\n<li><strong>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code> as a subject.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui m'intéresse</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*ce que m'intéresse</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ignoring agreement in forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code>.</strong> Match masculine/feminine and singular/plural carefully.</li>\n<li><strong>Confusing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code> from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</strong> Read the whole sentence before deciding.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"savoir-and-conna-tre\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sais</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sais</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sait</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaît</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"key-irregular-families\">Key irregular families</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduire</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craindre</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cours</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ris</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cours</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ris</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offres</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">crains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">court</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craint</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croyons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rions</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduisons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craignons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croyez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">riez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduisez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craignez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voient</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croient</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rient</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">conduisent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vivent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">craignent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"relative-pronoun-quick-chart\">Relative pronoun quick chart</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code></td>\n<td>subject of the relative clause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'</code></td>\n<td>direct object of the relative clause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code></td>\n<td>place or time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code></td>\n<td>replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + noun or shows possession link</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code> forms</td>\n<td>after prepositions, often meaning which one / which ones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code></td>\n<td>unnamed subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code></td>\n<td>unnamed direct object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code></td>\n<td>unnamed <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> phrase</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-habits\">Core habits</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Ask what the verb needs before you choose the relative pronoun.</li>\n<li>Keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> for facts and know-how, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> for familiarity.</li>\n<li>Learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> with common expressions like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler de</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir besoin de</code>.</li>\n<li>Split long sentences into two smaller ones when checking your grammar.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences describing a person, place, course, or project that matters to you. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code>;</li>\n<li>at least three different irregular verbs from this chapter;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> or a form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce dont</code>.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>underline every relative pronoun;</li>\n<li>draw an arrow from each relative pronoun to its antecedent when there is one;</li>\n<li>label each pronoun's role as subject, object, place/time, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> phrase;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is one model paragraph that meets every requirement:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais une professeure qui travaille dans mon quartier. Je sais qu'elle ouvre son bureau très tôt. Elle voit beaucoup d'étudiants et elle croit que chacun peut progresser. Nous suivons un cours où elle explique la grammaire avec patience. C'est un cours que je recommande à mes amis. Elle parle souvent d'un projet dont nous avons besoin pour pratiquer. Ce que j'aime le plus, c'est son calme. Les étudiants avec lesquels je travaille disent aussi qu'elle nous aide beaucoup.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais</code> gives information, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais</code> gives familiarity with a person. The irregular verbs include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suivons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">disent</code>. The relative pronouns are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec lesquels</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> for a person or place;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> before an infinitive;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code> where the missing word is an object;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> where the missing word is the subject;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> when there is no hidden <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>;</li>\n<li>a form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code> that does not agree with its antecedent.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 8 lessons: irregular verbs ii and relative pronouns this chapter helps you speak more precisely about knowledge, perception, belief, movement, and connection between ideas. you will learn several irregular verb families and then use relative pronouns to join two short sentences into one smoother, more natural french sentence. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. distinguish `savoir` and `connaître` accurately; 2. conjugate and use `voir`, `croire`, `courir`, `rire`, the `offrir` group, the `conduire` group, `suivre`, `vivre`, and the `craindre` group; 3. use `qui`, `que`, and `où` to connect clauses; 4. use `dont` and forms of `lequel` after prepositions; 5. use `ce qui`, `ce que`, and `ce dont` when there is no named antecedent; 6. read and create longer sentences without losing track of the subject and object. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - recognize a subject and a direct object in a simple sentence; - use `à` and `de` with confidence from chapter 6; - build two-verb frames such as `je veux partir` and `j'essaie de comprendre` from chapter 7; - hear the difference between a conjugated form and an infinitive. you will need those earlier skills immediately. for example, `le livre que je lis`, `l'étudiante à laquelle j'écris`, and `ce dont nous avons besoin` all depend on knowing what the verb controls. test the foundation before you add relative pronouns. answer these five without notes. 1. in `je lis le livre`, what is the direct object ? — `le livre`. it receives the action of reading directly. 2. choose the right preposition: `je parle ___ mon ami` and `j'ai besoin ___ ce livre`. — `je parle à mon ami` and `j'ai besoin de ce livre`. these prepositions matter later for `dont` and forms of `lequel`. 3. identify the infinitive in `j'essaie de comprendre`. — `comprendre`. `essaie` is conjugated; `de comprendre` is the verb frame. 4. which is conjugated and which is infinitive: `elle veut partir` ? — `veut` is conjugated, and `partir` is infinitive. only the first verb changes for the subject. 5. split `le livre que je lis` into two ideas. — `c'est le livre. je lis le livre.` the repeated noun is the object in the second idea, so later it becomes `que`. if you missed two or more, review direct objects, `à`/`de`, and chapter 7 verb frames first. relative pronouns are much easier when you can see the missing role. ## how to study this chapter when you study the relative pronouns, split each sentence into two shorter ideas and identify the role of the missing word. ask yourself: is it the subject, the object, a place/time word, or part of a `de` phrase? ## unit 1: `savoir` versus `connaître` ### core idea both verbs translate as \"to know,\" but they divide the job differently. - `savoir` = to know a fact, to know information, to know how to do something - `connaître` = to know a person, a place, or something through familiarity ### present tense forms | pronoun | `savoir` | `connaître` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `sais` | `connais` | | `tu` | `sais` | `connais` | | `il/elle/on` | `sait` | `connaît` | | `nous` | `savons` | `connaissons` | | `vous` | `savez` | `connaissez` | | `ils/elles` | `savent` | `connaissent` | ### how the meanings differ use `savoir` with information or with an infinitive. - `je sais la réponse.` — i know the answer. - `elle sait conduire.` — she knows how to drive. - `nous savons que le musée ferme à six heures.` — we know that the museum closes at six. use `connaître` with people, places, and familiarity. - `je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.` — i know the woman who runs this company. - `nous connaissons paris assez bien.` — we know paris fairly well. - `tu connais ce film ?` — do you know this film? / are you familiar with this film? ### pronunciation note `connaissons` and `connaissent` look related, but they sound very different. say the whole forms aloud: `nous connaissons`, `ils connaissent`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use `connaître` before an infinitive. - correct: `elle sait nager.` — she knows how to swim. - incorrect: `*elle connaît nager.` ### sentence-building practice choose `savoir` or `connaître` and write the full sentence. 1. i know your brother. 2. we know how to solve the problem. 3. do you know this neighborhood? 4. they know that the restaurant is closed. 1. `je connais ton frère.` — use `connaître` for a person you are familiar with. 2. `nous savons résoudre le problème.` — use `savoir` before an infinitive for knowing how to do something. 3. `tu connais ce quartier ?` — a neighborhood is a place you know through familiarity, so use `connaître`. 4. `ils savent que le restaurant est fermé.` — a fact introduced by `que` takes `savoir`. the trap is translating english *know* automatically. ask whether the object is a person/place/familiar thing, or a fact/skill. ## unit 2: `voir`, `croire`, `courir`, and `rire` ### core idea these verbs often describe perception, belief, and quick everyday actions. they are irregular in different ways, so learn them as a family of high-frequency forms. ### present tense forms | pronoun | `voir` | `croire` | `courir` | `rire` | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `vois` | `crois` | `cours` | `ris` | | `tu` | `vois` | `crois` | `cours` | `ris` | | `il/elle/on` | `voit` | `croit` | `court` | `rit` | | `nous` | `voyons` | `croyons` | `courons` | `rions` | | `vous` | `voyez` | `croyez` | `courez` | `riez` | | `ils/elles` | `voient` | `croient` | `courent` | `rient` | ### examples in context - `je vois le problème maintenant.` — i see the problem now. - `tu crois tout ce qu'il dit ?` — do you believe everything he says? - `nous courons dans le parc le matin.` — we run in the park in the morning. - `elles rient toujours aux mêmes blagues.` — they always laugh at the same jokes. - `on voit les montagnes de notre fenêtre.` — we can see the mountains from our window. - `je ne crois pas cette histoire.` — i do not believe that story. ### pronunciation note the `y` in `voyons` and `croyez` changes the sound of the stem. listen especially for `nous voyons` versus `vous voyez`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not overtranslate english \"to think\" as `croire` in every case. `croire` is about belief: `je crois que...` means \"i believe / i think that...\" in the sense of opinion, not mental effort or planning. ### sentence-building practice translate into french. 1. we see the station from here. 2. she believes that you are right. 3. they run every saturday. 4. why are you laughing? then change sentence 2 to the negative. 1. `nous voyons la gare d'ici.` — `voir` has the `nous` form `voyons`. 2. `elle croit que tu as raison.` — `croire que` introduces a belief or opinion. 3. `ils courent tous les samedis.` — `courir` takes `courent` with plural `ils`; the -ent ending is silent. 4. `pourquoi est-ce que tu ris ?` — `rire` has `tu ris`, and the question word `pourquoi` asks for the reason. negative form of sentence 2: - `elle ne croit pas que tu aies raison.` — the negative wraps `croit`. the form `aies` is a later subjunctive form used after negated belief; for now, copy the whole sentence as a chunk. ## unit 3: other irregular verb families ### core idea this unit groups several important patterns that often surprise learners: the `offrir` family, the `conduire` family, `suivre` and `vivre`, and the `craindre` family. ### the `offrir` family these verbs look like `-ir` verbs but behave in many ways like common `-er` verbs in the present tense. | pronoun | `offrir` | `ouvrir` | `souffrir` | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `offre` | `ouvre` | `souffre` | | `tu` | `offres` | `ouvres` | `souffres` | | `il/elle/on` | `offre` | `ouvre` | `souffre` | | `nous` | `offrons` | `ouvrons` | `souffrons` | | `vous` | `offrez` | `ouvrez` | `souffrez` | | `ils/elles` | `offrent` | `ouvrent` | `souffrent` | - `elle offre un livre à sa mère.` — she gives her mother a book. - `nous ouvrons les fenêtres.` — we open the windows. - `il souffre du dos.` — he suffers from back pain. ### the `conduire` family and related verbs | pronoun | `conduire` | `suivre` | `vivre` | `craindre` | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `conduis` | `suis` | `vis` | `crains` | | `tu` | `conduis` | `suis` | `vis` | `crains` | | `il/elle/on` | `conduit` | `suit` | `vit` | `craint` | | `nous` | `conduisons` | `suivons` | `vivons` | `craignons` | | `vous` | `conduisez` | `suivez` | `vivez` | `craignez` | | `ils/elles` | `conduisent` | `suivent` | `vivent` | `craignent` | ### examples in context - `tu conduis souvent la nuit ?` — do you often drive at night? - `nous suivons ce cours ensemble.` — we are following this course together. - `mes grands-parents vivent à la campagne.` — my grandparents live in the countryside. - `je crains le froid en hiver.` — i fear the cold in winter. - `voici la route que nous suivons chaque été.` — here is the road that we follow every summer. - `ils vivent dans un quartier que je connais bien.` — they live in a neighborhood that i know well. ### common mistake for english speakers do not confuse `suis` from `suivre` with `suis` from `être` when you see it without context. the subject and the rest of the sentence tell you which verb is meant. ### sentence-building practice complete the sentence with the right verb and form. 1. `nous ___ un guide très précis.` — follow 2. `elle ___ la porte pour laisser entrer le chat.` — open 3. `je ___ toujours les règles de sécurité.` — follow / obey in the sense of follow 4. `ils ___ une vie simple près de la mer.` — live 5. `tu ___ vraiment conduire jusqu'à marseille ?` — know how / be driving; choose the correct chapter verb if needed. 1. `nous suivons un guide très précis.` — `suivre` means to follow, and `nous` takes `suivons`. 2. `elle ouvre la porte pour laisser entrer le chat.` — `ouvrir` belongs to the `offrir` family and uses regular-looking -er endings in the singular. 3. `je suis toujours les règles de sécurité.` — here `suis` comes from `suivre`, meaning i follow or obey; context prevents confusion with `être`. 4. `ils vivent une vie simple près de la mer.` — `vivre` has the plural form `vivent`; the -ent ending is silent. 5. `tu sais vraiment conduire jusqu'à marseille ?` — knowing how to do something uses `savoir` + infinitive, not `connaître`. the main trap is sentence 3: `je suis` can mean i am or i follow. read the object that follows before deciding. ## unit 4: `qui`, `que`, and `où` ### core idea relative pronouns connect two ideas. they replace a repeated noun and begin a relative clause. - `qui` = subject of the relative clause - `que` / `qu'` = direct object of the relative clause - `où` = place or time ### how to choose the form | relative pronoun | what it replaces | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `qui` | the subject | `la femme qui parle...` | the woman who is speaking... | | `que` | the direct object | `le livre que je lis...` | the book that i am reading... | | `où` | place or time | `la ville où j'habite...` | the city where i live... | ### building the sentence step by step 1. `je connais la femme. la femme dirige cette entreprise.` 2. replace the repeated noun as the **subject** of the second clause. 3. `je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.` — i know the woman who runs this company. more examples: - `voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.` — here is the book that my teacher recommends. - `montréal est la ville où ils veulent vivre.` — montreal is the city where they want to live. - `le jour où nous partons arrive vite.` — the day when we are leaving is coming quickly. - `je vois les enfants qui jouent dans la cour.` — i see the children who are playing in the yard. ### pronunciation note `que` becomes `qu'` before a vowel sound: `le film qu'elle aime`. the function stays the same even when the spelling shortens. ### common mistake for english speakers do not choose `qui` just because english uses \"who.\" ask what role the missing word plays inside the relative clause. - `la femme qui parle` — she is the subject of `parle` - `la femme que je vois` — she is the object of `vois` ### sentence-building practice combine each pair into one sentence. 1. `je regarde un film. le film commence à huit heures.` 2. `nous visitons une ville. mes parents connaissent cette ville.` 3. `voici le café. nous allons souvent dans ce café.` 4. `c'est le jour. elle arrive ce jour-là.` 1. `je regarde un film qui commence à huit heures.` — the film is the subject of `commence`, so use `qui`. 2. `nous visitons une ville que mes parents connaissent.` — the city is the direct object of `connaissent`, so use `que`. 3. `voici le café où nous allons souvent.` — the café is a place, so use `où`. 4. `c'est le jour où elle arrive.` — `où` can also refer to time, not only physical places. do not choose by the english word *who* or *that* alone. choose by the missing role inside the second clause: subject, object, or place/time. ## unit 5: `dont`, forms of `lequel`, and indefinite relatives ### core idea use `dont` when the second clause needs `de`. use forms of `lequel` after a preposition when you need \"which one\" or when a more explicit prepositional relative is useful. use `ce qui`, `ce que`, and `ce dont` when there is no named noun before the clause. ### `dont` | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---| | verb + `de` | `c'est un métier dont elle parle souvent.` | it is a profession that she often talks about. | | `avoir besoin de` | `voici le document dont j'ai besoin.` | here is the document i need. | | possession link | `l'étudiant dont la sœur habite ici vient du chili.` | the student whose sister lives here comes from chile. | ### forms of `lequel` | form | meaning | |---|---| | `lequel` | which one / which masculine singular one | | `laquelle` | which feminine singular one | | `lesquels` | which masculine or mixed plural ones | | `lesquelles` | which feminine plural ones | after `à` and `de`, these forms contract. | base form | with `à` | with `de` | |---|---|---| | `lequel` | `auquel` | `duquel` | | `laquelle` | `à laquelle` | `de laquelle` | | `lesquels` | `auxquels` | `desquels` | | `lesquelles` | `auxquelles` | `desquelles` | examples: - `le projet auquel je pense change souvent.` — the project i am thinking about changes often. - `la chaise sur laquelle il pose son sac est cassée.` — the chair on which he puts his bag is broken. - `les collègues avec lesquels je travaille sont patients.` — the coworkers with whom i work are patient. - `je ne sais pas de laquelle de ces routes tu parles.` — i do not know which of these roads you are talking about. ### `ce qui`, `ce que`, and `ce dont` use these forms when there is **no named antecedent** before the relative clause. | form | role in the clause | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `ce qui` | subject | `je comprends ce qui se passe.` | i understand what is happening. | | `ce que` / `ce qu'` | direct object | `dis-moi ce que tu veux.` | tell me what you want. | | `ce dont` | `de` phrase | `voilà ce dont nous avons besoin.` | that is what we need. | ### turning a question into a statement - `qu'est-ce qui se passe ?` -> `je sais ce qui se passe.` - `qu'est-ce que tu cherches ?` -> `je vois ce que tu cherches.` - `de quoi parlent-ils ?` -> `je comprends ce dont ils parlent.` ### common mistake for english speakers do not use `ce que` when the missing word is the subject. compare: - `je sais ce qui arrive.` — i know what is happening. - `je sais ce que tu lis.` — i know what you are reading. ### sentence-building practice choose the correct form: `dont`, `lequel`, `ce qui`, `ce que`, or `ce dont`. 1. `c'est le problème ___ nous discutons.` 2. `je ne comprends pas ___ te fait rire.` 3. `voici la table sur ___ nous allons manger.` 4. `dis-moi ___ tu crois.` 5. `voilà ___ j'ai besoin pour le voyage.` 1. `c'est le problème dont nous discutons.` — `discuter de` needs `de`, and `dont` replaces that de-phrase. 2. `je ne comprends pas ce qui te fait rire.` — the missing idea is the subject of `fait rire`, so use `ce qui`. 3. `voici la table sur laquelle nous allons manger.` — after the preposition `sur`, use the feminine singular form `laquelle` for `table`. 4. `dis-moi ce que tu crois.` — the missing idea is the direct object of `crois`, so use `ce que`. 5. `voilà ce dont j'ai besoin pour le voyage.` — `avoir besoin de` requires `de`, so the unnamed thing is `ce dont`. the trap is forgetting the preposition hidden inside `dont` and `ce dont`. if the expression needs `de`, do not replace it with plain `que`. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine the chapter's verb families and relative pronouns in longer, more natural french. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.` — i know the woman who runs this company. 2. `voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.` — here is the book that my teacher recommends. 3. `montréal est la ville où ils veulent vivre.` — montreal is the city where they want to live. 4. `c'est un métier dont elle parle souvent.` — it is a profession that she often talks about. 5. `je comprends ce que tu veux, mais je ne crois pas que ce soit facile.` — i understand what you want, but i do not believe that it is easy. 6. `les amis avec lesquels nous courons vivent dans le quartier que tu connais.` — the friends with whom we run live in the neighborhood that you know. after writing the six sentences from memory, check the parts that are easy to lose in a long sentence. - **relative pronoun role.** `qui` is the subject in sentence 1; `que` is the object in sentences 2 and 6; `où` marks place in sentence 3; `dont` replaces a **de** phrase in sentence 4. - **indefinite relatives.** sentence 5 has `ce que tu veux`, with no named antecedent before it. - **forms after prepositions.** sentence 6 uses `avec lesquels` because it follows `avec` and refers to plural `amis`. - **elision.** `c'est` and `qu'` before a vowel sound need apostrophes. - **irregular verbs.** check `connais`, `comprends`, `crois`, `courons`, and `vivent`; none follows a simple regular -er pattern. - **silent endings.** `vivent` and `connais` contain letters you may not hear clearly. score the sentence as wrong if the relative pronoun no longer matches its job inside the clause, even if the english translation still sounds close. ### notice and mark for each sentence above: 1. circle every conjugated verb; 2. underline the antecedent before each relative pronoun when there is one; 3. label the role of each pronoun as **subject**, **object**, **place/time**, or **`de` phrase**; 4. identify the one sentence with an indefinite relative pronoun. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the person, place, profession, or object. then replace the relative pronoun in sentence 6 with a different correct structure if possible. ### make it personal write five new sentences about your own life. include: - one sentence with `savoir`; - one sentence with `connaître`; - one sentence with `qui`, `que`, or `où`; - one sentence with `dont` or a form of `lequel`; - one sentence with `ce qui`, `ce que`, or `ce dont`. model check for the cumulative practice: 1. `je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.` — `qui` is the subject of `dirige`; the woman is doing the directing. 2. `voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.` — `que` is the direct object of `recommande`; the professor recommends the book. 3. `montréal est la ville où ils veulent vivre.` — `où` refers to a place. 4. `c'est un métier dont elle parle souvent.` — `parler de` needs `de`, so `dont` is required. 5. `je comprends ce que tu veux, mais je ne crois pas que ce soit facile.` — `ce que` is the direct object of `veux`. the form `soit` belongs to a later grammar point; here, just recognize and copy the whole sentence. 6. `les amis avec lesquels nous courons vivent dans le quartier que tu connais.` — `avec lesquels` follows a preposition and agrees with plural `amis`; `que` is the object of `connais`. for sentence 6, another correct structure is `les amis avec qui nous courons vivent dans le quartier que tu connais.` — `avec qui` is often used for people and avoids the agreement of `lesquels`. personal answers vary. mark yourself wrong if `savoir` is used for a person, if `connaître` is used before an infinitive, or if a relative pronoun does not match its role inside the clause. ## mini-dialogue: choosing a research topic **lina :** `tu connais le professeur qui anime le séminaire du jeudi ?` — do you know the professor who leads the thursday seminar? **marc :** `oui, je le connais un peu, mais je ne sais pas tout ce qu'il attend.` — yes, i know him a little, but i do not know everything he expects. **lina :** `je vois. moi, je cherche un sujet dont je peux parler avec plaisir.` — i see. i am looking for a topic that i can talk about with pleasure. **marc :** `tu peux choisir le film dont tu me parles souvent.` — you can choose the film that you often tell me about. **lina :** `peut-être. tu crois que c'est un sujet auquel il va s'intéresser ?` — maybe. do you think it is a topic that he is going to be interested in? **marc :** `oui, parce qu'il aime tout ce qui touche à la mémoire et à la ville.` — yes, because he likes everything that has to do with memory and the city. **lina :** `dans ce cas, je vais suivre ton conseil et écrire ce que je pense ce soir.` — in that case, i am going to follow your advice and write what i think tonight. **marc :** `parfait. dis-moi demain ce dont tu as encore besoin.` — perfect. tell me tomorrow what you still need. ### guided analysis 1. find one example each of `qui`, `dont`, `auquel`, `ce qui`, `ce que`, and `ce dont`. 2. explain why `je le connais` uses `connaître` but `je ne sais pas` uses `savoir`. 3. rewrite lina's first line with `vous` instead of `tu`. 4. change `tu peux choisir le film dont tu me parles souvent` into two short sentences. 5. replace `tout ce qui touche à la mémoire` with a version using `ce que` if possible; if not, explain why not. 1. the dialogue gives all six: `le professeur qui anime le séminaire` has `qui`; `un sujet dont je peux parler` and `le film dont tu me parles souvent` have `dont`; `un sujet auquel il va s'intéresser` has `auquel`; `tout ce qui touche à la mémoire` has `ce qui`; `ce que je pense` has `ce que`; `ce dont tu as encore besoin` has `ce dont`. 2. marc says `je le connais` because he knows a person, the professor, through familiarity. he says `je ne sais pas tout ce qu'il attend` because that is information or a fact he does not know. 3. with `vous`: `vous connaissez le professeur qui anime le séminaire du jeudi ?` the verb changes from `connais` to `connaissez`; the relative clause stays the same because the professor is still the subject of `anime`. 4. two short sentences: `tu peux choisir le film. tu me parles souvent de ce film.` the second sentence contains `de ce film`, which is why the joined version uses `dont`. 5. you cannot replace it naturally with `ce que` here, because the missing idea is the subject of `touche à la mémoire`. use `ce qui` for an unnamed subject; `ce que` would be for an unnamed direct object. ## connected reading: un quartier qu'on apprend à aimer `j'habite maintenant dans un quartier que je connais encore mal. au début, je ne sais pas toujours où faire mes courses ni quels bus suivre. peu à peu, je connais de mieux en mieux les rues qui montent vers le parc et les cafés où les étudiants rient pendant des heures. j'aime la petite librairie dont la propriétaire recommande toujours un roman surprenant. le samedi, je vois des voisins avec lesquels je parle parfois, et je crois que c'est cela qui me fait rester ici avec plaisir. ce que j'aime le plus, pourtant, c'est la place centrale : c'est là que la ville semble vraiment vivre.` — i now live in a neighborhood that i still do not know very well. at first, i do not always know where to do my shopping or which buses to follow. little by little, i know better and better the streets that climb toward the park and the cafés where students laugh for hours. i like the small bookstore whose owner always recommends a surprising novel. on saturdays, i see neighbors with whom i sometimes speak, and i believe that that is what makes me stay here happily. what i like most, however, is the central square: that is where the city really seems to live. ### reading practice 1. list every relative pronoun in the reading and give its role. 2. find one example of `savoir` and two of `connaître`; explain the difference. 3. underline the verbs from the irregular families studied in this chapter. 4. change the last two sentences from `je` to `nous`. 5. write three true sentences about a place you know using the reading as a model. 1. relative pronouns: `que je connais encore mal` uses `que` as object; `qui montent vers le parc` uses `qui` as subject; `où les étudiants rient` marks place; `dont la propriétaire recommande...` shows possession; `avec lesquels je parle` follows the preposition `avec`; `qui me fait rester` uses `qui` as subject; `ce que j'aime` uses `ce que` as object. the phrase `où faire mes courses` is an indirect question of place, not a relative pronoun, so do not count it for this item. 2. `je ne sais pas toujours où faire mes courses` uses `savoir` for information. `un quartier que je connais encore mal` and `je connais de mieux en mieux les rues` use `connaître` for familiarity with a place. 3. irregular-family verbs include `sais`, `suivre`, `connais`, `rient`, `vois`, `crois`, and `vivre`. `semble` is not from this chapter's irregular families. 4. last two sentences with `nous`: `le samedi, nous voyons des voisins avec lesquels nous parlons parfois, et nous croyons que c'est cela qui nous fait rester ici avec plaisir. ce que nous aimons le plus, pourtant, c'est la place centrale : c'est là que la ville semble vraiment vivre.` the main changes are `vois` to `voyons`, `je parle` to `nous parlons`, `je crois` to `nous croyons`, and `j'aime` to `nous aimons`. 5. answers vary. a model set: `je connais un café où les étudiants travaillent.` `j'aime la rue qui monte vers le parc.` `ce que j'aime le plus, c'est la bibliothèque.` mark yours wrong if `savoir` is used for a place or if a relative pronoun has the wrong role. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. **using `savoir` for people and places.** say `je connais ce professeur`, not `*je sais ce professeur`. 2. **choosing the relative pronoun from english meaning alone.** check the role inside the clause first. 3. **forgetting `de` inside `dont`.** if the verb or expression needs `de`, `dont` is often the right choice. 4. **using `ce que` as a subject.** say `ce qui m'intéresse`, not `*ce que m'intéresse`. 5. **ignoring agreement in forms of `lequel`.** match masculine/feminine and singular/plural carefully. 6. **confusing `suis` from `suivre` with `suis` from `être`.** read the whole sentence before deciding. ## chapter reference sheet ### `savoir` and `connaître` | pronoun | `savoir` | `connaître` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `sais` | `connais` | | `tu` | `sais` | `connais` | | `il/elle/on` | `sait` | `connaît` | | `nous` | `savons` | `connaissons` | | `vous` | `savez` | `connaissez` | | `ils/elles` | `savent` | `connaissent` | ### key irregular families | pronoun | `voir` | `croire` | `courir` | `rire` | `offrir` | `conduire` | `suivre` | `vivre` | `craindre` | |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `vois` | `crois` | `cours` | `ris` | `offre` | `conduis` | `suis` | `vis` | `crains` | | `tu` | `vois` | `crois` | `cours` | `ris` | `offres` | `conduis` | `suis` | `vis` | `crains` | | `il/elle/on` | `voit` | `croit` | `court` | `rit` | `offre` | `conduit` | `suit` | `vit` | `craint` | | `nous` | `voyons` | `croyons` | `courons` | `rions` | `offrons` | `conduisons` | `suivons` | `vivons` | `craignons` | | `vous` | `voyez` | `croyez` | `courez` | `riez` | `offrez` | `conduisez` | `suivez` | `vivez` | `craignez` | | `ils/elles` | `voient` | `croient` | `courent` | `rient` | `offrent` | `conduisent` | `suivent` | `vivent` | `craignent` | ### relative pronoun quick chart | form | use | |---|---| | `qui` | subject of the relative clause | | `que` / `qu'` | direct object of the relative clause | | `où` | place or time | | `dont` | replaces `de` + noun or shows possession link | | `lequel` forms | after prepositions, often meaning which one / which ones | | `ce qui` | unnamed subject | | `ce que` | unnamed direct object | | `ce dont` | unnamed `de` phrase | ### core habits - ask what the verb needs before you choose the relative pronoun. - keep `savoir` for facts and know-how, `connaître` for familiarity. - learn `dont` with common expressions like `parler de` and `avoir besoin de`. - split long sentences into two smaller ones when checking your grammar. ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences describing a person, place, course, or project that matters to you. include: - one sentence with `savoir` and one with `connaître`; - at least three different irregular verbs from this chapter; - one sentence with `qui`, one with `que` or `où`, and one with `dont` or a form of `lequel`; - one sentence with `ce qui`, `ce que`, or `ce dont`. after writing: 1. circle every conjugated verb; 2. underline every relative pronoun; 3. draw an arrow from each relative pronoun to its antecedent when there is one; 4. label each pronoun's role as subject, object, place/time, or `de` phrase; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary. here is one model paragraph that meets every requirement: `je connais une professeure qui travaille dans mon quartier. je sais qu'elle ouvre son bureau très tôt. elle voit beaucoup d'étudiants et elle croit que chacun peut progresser. nous suivons un cours où elle explique la grammaire avec patience. c'est un cours que je recommande à mes amis. elle parle souvent d'un projet dont nous avons besoin pour pratiquer. ce que j'aime le plus, c'est son calme. les étudiants avec lesquels je travaille disent aussi qu'elle nous aide beaucoup.` how it satisfies the brief: `je sais` gives information, and `je connais` gives familiarity with a person. the irregular verbs include `ouvre`, `voit`, `croit`, `suivons`, and `disent`. the relative pronouns are `qui`, `où`, `que`, `dont`, `ce que`, and `avec lesquels`. mark yourself wrong if you have: - `savoir` for a person or place; - `connaître` before an infinitive; - `qui` where the missing word is an object; - `que` where the missing word is the subject; - `dont` when there is no hidden `de`; - a form of `lequel` that does not agree with its antecedent. "},{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 8 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-exercises\">Chapter 8 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I know Marie.</li>\n<li>I know the answer.</li>\n<li>The man speaks. He is my teacher.</li>\n<li>I read the book. The book is useful.</li>\n<li>This is the city. I live there.</li>\n<li>Here is the friend. I speak about him.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais Marie.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> for a person.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais la réponse.</code> — Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> for a fact or piece of information.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'homme qui parle est mon professeur.</code> — The man is the subject of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code>, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le livre que je lis est utile.</code> — The book is the direct object of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lis</code>, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est la ville où j'habite.</code> — A place takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> in a relative clause.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici l'ami dont je parle.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler de</code> requires <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de lui</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>savoir versus connaître</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais la réponse.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>irregular verb families</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois le problème.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>qui, que, and où</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La femme qui parle...</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>dont and lequel</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'ami dont je parle...</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct answer must show the target contrast or pronoun role, not merely copy the model.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Savoir versus connaître: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais ce quartier, mais je ne sais pas son histoire.</code> — Familiarity with a place uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code>; information uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code>.</li>\n<li>Irregular verb families: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voyons la montagne depuis la fenêtre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code> has the special <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le café où nous étudions est calme.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code> marks the place where the studying happens.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dont</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le projet dont je parle est important.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler de</code> needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> is the right connector.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if the relative pronoun has no clear antecedent when it needs one, if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> does not replace a de-phrase, or if a form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code> does not agree with the noun it replaces.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais la femme qui dirige ce musée.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous connaissons la femme qui dirige ce musée.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaissons</code>; the relative clause still describes the woman.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne connais pas la femme qui dirige ce musée.</code> — The negative wraps the conjugated verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu connais la femme qui dirige ce musée ?</code> — The main clause becomes a question; the relative clause stays attached to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la femme</code>.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais l'homme qui dirige ce musée.</code> — Changing the antecedent from woman to man does not change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, because it is chosen by role, not gender.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check that your relative pronoun still makes sense after every rewrite. A changed noun may require agreement elsewhere, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> themselves do not change for gender or number.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais la réponse.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sais</code> has a silent final <strong>s</strong>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponse</code> needs the accent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On voit les montagnes de notre fenêtre.</code> — The singular verb form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voit</code> ends with a silent <strong>t</strong>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">montagnes</code> keeps its final <strong>s</strong> in writing.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous courons dans le parc le matin.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courons</code> is the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courir</code>; do not spell it like a regular -er verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ouvrons les fenêtres.</code> — The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir</code> family has regular-looking endings, but the infinitive is not an -er verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> is not elided here because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code> begins with a consonant sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voilà ce dont nous avons besoin.</code> — The trap is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir besoin de</code> hides the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> inside the relative pronoun.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct first for meaning, then for the irregular verb form, then for the relative pronoun. A sentence can sound familiar and still have the wrong connector.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Describe a profession or person in six sentences. Include savoir, connaître, and four different relative pronouns.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model description:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais une médecin qui travaille dans mon quartier.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle sait parler avec les patients qui ont peur.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le cabinet où elle travaille est près de la gare.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est une personne que tout le monde respecte.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle explique les traitements dont les patients ont besoin.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voilà ce que j'admire chez elle.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: this model includes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sait</code>, and the relative pronouns <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">où</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce que</code>. Mark yourself wrong if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> is used for knowing a person, if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> is used before an infinitive, or if one relative pronoun does not replace a clear role in its clause.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Savoir versus connaître.</strong> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> for facts and know-how; use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code> for people, places, and familiarity. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais Marie, mais je ne sais pas son adresse.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Irregular verb families.</strong> Learn common forms in phrase groups, not by guessing regular endings. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voyons le problème.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Qui, que, and où.</strong> Choose by role: subject, direct object, or place/time. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le livre que je lis.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Dont and lequel.</strong> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dont</code> for a de-phrase and forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lequel</code> after many other prepositions. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est le sujet dont nous parlons.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If your example uses the right French words but you cannot explain why, split it into two shorter sentences and identify the missing role.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 8 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i know marie. 2. i know the answer. 3. the man speaks. he is my teacher. 4. i read the book. the book is useful. 5. this is the city. i live there. 6. here is the friend. i speak about him. 1. `je connais marie.` — use `connaître` for a person. 2. `je sais la réponse.` — use `savoir` for a fact or piece of information. 3. `l'homme qui parle est mon professeur.` — the man is the subject of `parle`, so use `qui`. 4. `le livre que je lis est utile.` — the book is the direct object of `lis`, so use `que`. 5. `c'est la ville où j'habite.` — a place takes `où` in a relative clause. 6. `voici l'ami dont je parle.` — `parler de` requires `de`, and `dont` replaces `de lui`. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **savoir versus connaître**. use `je sais la réponse.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **irregular verb families**. use `je vois le problème.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **qui, que, and où**. use `la femme qui parle...` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **dont and lequel**. use `l'ami dont je parle...` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct answer must show the target contrast or pronoun role, not merely copy the model. sample answers: 1. savoir versus connaître: `je connais ce quartier, mais je ne sais pas son histoire.` — familiarity with a place uses `connaître`; information uses `savoir`. 2. irregular verb families: `nous voyons la montagne depuis la fenêtre.` — `voir` has the special `nous` form `voyons`. 3. `qui`, `que`, and `où`: `le café où nous étudions est calme.` — `où` marks the place where the studying happens. 4. `dont` and `lequel`: `le projet dont je parle est important.` — `parler de` needs `de`, so `dont` is the right connector. mark yourself wrong if the relative pronoun has no clear antecedent when it needs one, if `dont` does not replace a de-phrase, or if a form of `lequel` does not agree with the noun it replaces. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `je connais la femme qui dirige ce musée.` 1. change the subject: `nous connaissons la femme qui dirige ce musée.` — `connais` changes to `connaissons`; the relative clause still describes the woman. 2. make it negative: `je ne connais pas la femme qui dirige ce musée.` — the negative wraps the conjugated verb `connais`. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que tu connais la femme qui dirige ce musée ?` — the main clause becomes a question; the relative clause stays attached to `la femme`. 4. change one meaningful detail: `je connais l'homme qui dirige ce musée.` — changing the antecedent from woman to man does not change `qui`, because it is chosen by role, not gender. check that your relative pronoun still makes sense after every rewrite. a changed noun may require agreement elsewhere, but `qui`, `que`, `où`, and `dont` themselves do not change for gender or number. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `je sais la réponse.` — `sais` has a silent final **s**, and `réponse` needs the accent. 2. `on voit les montagnes de notre fenêtre.` — the singular verb form `voit` ends with a silent **t**, and `montagnes` keeps its final **s** in writing. 3. `nous courons dans le parc le matin.` — `courons` is the `nous` form of `courir`; do not spell it like a regular -er verb. 4. `nous ouvrons les fenêtres.` — the `offrir` family has regular-looking endings, but the infinitive is not an -er verb. 5. `voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.` — `que` is not elided here because `mon` begins with a consonant sound. 6. `voilà ce dont nous avons besoin.` — the trap is `dont`, because `avoir besoin de` hides the `de` inside the relative pronoun. correct first for meaning, then for the irregular verb form, then for the relative pronoun. a sentence can sound familiar and still have the wrong connector. ## e. personal writing describe a profession or person in six sentences. include savoir, connaître, and four different relative pronouns. answers vary. a model description: 1. `je connais une médecin qui travaille dans mon quartier.` 2. `elle sait parler avec les patients qui ont peur.` 3. `le cabinet où elle travaille est près de la gare.` 4. `c'est une personne que tout le monde respecte.` 5. `elle explique les traitements dont les patients ont besoin.` 6. `voilà ce que j'admire chez elle.` self-check: this model includes `connais`, `sait`, and the relative pronouns `qui`, `où`, `que`, `dont`, and `ce que`. mark yourself wrong if `savoir` is used for knowing a person, if `connaître` is used before an infinitive, or if one relative pronoun does not replace a clear role in its clause. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **savoir versus connaître.** use `savoir` for facts and know-how; use `connaître` for people, places, and familiarity. example: `je connais marie, mais je ne sais pas son adresse.` 2. **irregular verb families.** learn common forms in phrase groups, not by guessing regular endings. example: `nous voyons le problème.` 3. **qui, que, and où.** choose by role: subject, direct object, or place/time. example: `voici le livre que je lis.` 4. **dont and lequel.** use `dont` for a de-phrase and forms of `lequel` after many other prepositions. example: `c'est le sujet dont nous parlons.` if your example uses the right french words but you cannot explain why, split it into two shorter sentences and identify the missing role. "},{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 8 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-answer-key\">Chapter 8 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais Marie.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais la réponse.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'homme qui parle est mon professeur.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le livre que je lis est utile.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est la ville où j'habite.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici l'ami dont je parle.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Savoir versus connaître:</strong> Savoir is used for facts, information, and knowing how; connaître is used for people, places, and familiarity.</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular verb families:</strong> Learn voir/croire, courir/rire, offrir, conduire, suivre/vivre, and craindre through frequent first-person and plural forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Qui, que, and où:</strong> Qui is the subject of the relative clause; que is its direct object; où refers to a place or time.</li>\n<li><strong>Dont and lequel:</strong> Dont replaces de + noun. Forms of lequel commonly follow prepositions and agree with their antecedent.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 8 answer key ## section a 1. `je connais marie.` 2. `je sais la réponse.` 3. `l'homme qui parle est mon professeur.` 4. `le livre que je lis est utile.` 5. `c'est la ville où j'habite.` 6. `voici l'ami dont je parle.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **savoir versus connaître:** savoir is used for facts, information, and knowing how; connaître is used for people, places, and familiarity. - **irregular verb families:** learn voir/croire, courir/rire, offrir, conduire, suivre/vivre, and craindre through frequent first-person and plural forms. - **qui, que, and où:** qui is the subject of the relative clause; que is its direct object; où refers to a place or time. - **dont and lequel:** dont replaces de + noun. forms of lequel commonly follow prepositions and agree with their antecedent. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 8 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 8 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Savoir versus connaître</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Irregular verb families</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Qui, que, and où</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Dont and lequel</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 8 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **savoir versus connaître**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **irregular verb families** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **qui, que, and où**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **dont and lequel**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":9,"slug":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns","title":"Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns","documents":[{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/README.md","title":"Chapter 9: Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-prendre-boire-partitive-articles-and-object-pronouns\">Chapter 9: Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate prendre-family verbs and boire.</li>\n<li>Use du, de la, de l', and des.</li>\n<li>Replace direct and indirect objects.</li>\n<li>Use y and en.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Prendre and boire</li>\n<li>Partitive articles</li>\n<li>Direct and indirect objects</li>\n<li>Y and en</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 9: prendre, boire, partitive articles, and object pronouns this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate prendre-family verbs and boire. - use du, de la, de l', and des. - replace direct and indirect objects. - use y and en. ## lesson sequence 1. prendre and boire 2. partitive articles 3. direct and indirect objects 4. y and en ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 9 Lessons: Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-lessons-prendre-boire-partitive-articles-and-object-pronouns\">Chapter 9 Lessons: Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This chapter helps you speak about food, drink, choice, and replacement. You will learn how French expresses an unspecified quantity, how objects move in front of the verb as pronouns, and how short words like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> keep sentences natural and less repetitive.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprendre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprendre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code> in the present tense;</li>\n<li>use the partitive articles <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>;</li>\n<li>contrast partitive, definite, and indefinite articles;</li>\n<li>replace direct objects with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>;</li>\n<li>replace indirect objects with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> for place and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + thing</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + thing</code> and quantities;</li>\n<li>place a single pronoun before the conjugated verb with confidence.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>recognize direct objects and indirect objects in English and in simple French sentences;</li>\n<li>form negative sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> from Chapter 6;</li>\n<li>follow verb + infinitive patterns from Chapter 7.</li>\n<p>This chapter builds directly on those ideas. If you can identify the object in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je prends le livre</code>, you can turn it into <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le prends</code>. If you know that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler à</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>, you can understand why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je lui parle</code> is indirect rather than direct.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Check that the earlier grammar is active, not just familiar. Answer these five before continuing.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le livre</code>, what is the direct object ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code>. It receives the action directly, with no preposition.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle à Marie</code>, what is the indirect object ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Marie</code>. The preposition <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> makes it indirect.</li>\n<li>Make <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois du café</code> negative. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne bois pas de café.</code> After a simple negative, the partitive becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li>Choose the preposition: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais ___ marché</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle ___ ce problème</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais au marché</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle de ce problème</code>. These become important for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</li>\n<li>Identify the infinitive in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux prendre le train</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code>. The object belongs to the infinitive idea, but the first verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veux</code> is conjugated.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review negatives, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, and Chapter 7 verb frames before starting. Object pronouns require you to identify the object before you replace it.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Always start from the full sentence. Identify the noun you want to replace, ask whether it is direct or indirect, and only then choose the pronoun. For food and drink, memorize complete chunks: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du pain</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la soupe</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en prendre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y aller</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-prendre-its-family-and-boire\">Unit 1: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code>, its family, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Prendre</code> means <strong>to take</strong>, but it also appears in many daily expressions: taking the bus, taking a seat, taking breakfast, taking notes. Its close relatives <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprendre</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprendre</code> follow the same pattern. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Boire</code> adds an important food-and-drink verb.</p>\n<h3 id=\"present-tense-forms\">Present tense forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprendre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprendre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prend</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprend</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprend</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprenons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprenons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">buvons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprenez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprenez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">buvez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prennent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprennent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprennent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boivent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le métro chaque matin.</code> — I take the subway every morning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous prenons un café après le déjeuner.</code> — We have a coffee after lunch.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle apprend le français avec sa voisine.</code> — She is learning French with her neighbor.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu comprends cette règle ?</code> — Do you understand this rule?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils boivent de l'eau pendant le cours.</code> — They drink water during class.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends des notes quand le professeur parle.</code> — I take notes when the professor speaks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The plural stem in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prennent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprennent</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprennent</code> sounds different from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenons</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenez</code>. Also notice <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous buvons</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous buvez</code>, which sound very different from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je bois</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not expect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code> to translate literally every time. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre le métro</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre une décision</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre un café</code> are normal French chunks.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Translate into French.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>We understand the question.</li>\n<li>I am learning a new expression.</li>\n<li>Do you drink tea in the morning?</li>\n<li>They take the train at eight o'clock.</li>\n<p>Then change sentence 4 to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> and sentence 2 to the negative.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous comprenons la question.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprendre</code> is irregular: with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, it is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprenons</code>. The object stays after the verb here because you have not been asked to replace it with a pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'apprends une nouvelle expression.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprendre</code> follows the same family pattern as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code> before the vowel sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprends</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu bois du thé le matin ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bois</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du thé</code> means an unspecified amount of tea; if you said <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le thé</code>, you would mean tea in general.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils prennent le train à huit heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prennent</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> for clock time: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à huit heures</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le train à huit heures.</code> — the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> form is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prend</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'apprends pas de nouvelle expression.</code> — in a simple negative, an indefinite article often becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>. Keep <strong>ne</strong> before the verb chunk and <strong>pas</strong> after the conjugated verb.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-partitive-articles\">Unit 2: Partitive articles</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Use a partitive article when you mean <strong>some</strong>, <strong>an unspecified quantity</strong>, or <strong>part of a larger whole</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"forms-of-the-partitive\">Forms of the partitive</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Before</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular noun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du pain</code></td>\n<td>some bread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular noun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la soupe</code></td>\n<td>some soup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vowel or mute h singular noun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'eau</code></td>\n<td>some water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plural noun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des légumes</code></td>\n<td>some vegetables</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"main-uses\">Main uses</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je mange du pain.</code> — I eat some bread.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle prépare de la soupe.</code> — She is making some soup.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous buvons de l'eau.</code> — We are drinking some water.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils achètent des fraises.</code> — They are buying some strawberries.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"partitive-versus-definite-versus-indefinite\">Partitive versus definite versus indefinite</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Article type</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>partitive</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du fromage.</code></td>\n<td>I am taking some cheese.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>definite</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le fromage.</code></td>\n<td>I am taking the cheese / the particular cheese.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indefinite singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends un sandwich.</code></td>\n<td>I am taking a sandwich.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indefinite plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends des biscuits.</code></td>\n<td>I am taking some cookies / cookies.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"after-negation-and-quantity-expressions\">After negation and quantity expressions</h3>\n<p>In many negative sentences or after quantity expressions, the article becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne bois pas de lait.</code> — I do not drink milk.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'achetons pas de tomates.</code> — We are not buying tomatoes.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il prend beaucoup de café.</code> — He drinks a lot of coffee.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle veut un kilo de pommes.</code> — She wants a kilo of apples.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le/la/les</code> automatically when English has no article. French often needs the partitive: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je bois de l'eau</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je bois eau</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Choose the correct article.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois ___ thé.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons ___ salade ce soir.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'achète pas ___ sucre.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils prennent deux tasses ___ café.</code></li>\n<p>Then explain why sentence 3 and sentence 4 do not use the ordinary partitive form.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois du thé.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">thé</code> is masculine singular, so the partitive is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons de la salade ce soir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">salade</code> is feminine singular, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code> for an unspecified amount.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'achète pas de sucre.</code> — after a simple negative, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du sucre</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de sucre</code>. The trap is keeping the ordinary partitive after <strong>pas</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils prennent deux tasses de café.</code> — after a quantity expression such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux tasses</code>, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sentence 3 does not use the ordinary partitive because <strong>ne ... pas</strong> changes an unspecified amount to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>. Sentence 4 does not use the ordinary partitive because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux tasses</code> already gives the quantity, so French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de café</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-direct-object-pronouns\">Unit 3: Direct object pronouns</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A direct object receives the action <strong>without</strong> a preposition. When you replace it with a pronoun, the pronoun usually goes <strong>before</strong> the conjugated verb.</p>\n<h3 id=\"direct-object-pronouns\">Direct object pronouns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Person or thing replaced</th>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>me</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il me voit.</code></td>\n<td>He sees me.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>you singular informal</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je te comprends.</code></td>\n<td>I understand you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>him / it masculine</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le prends.</code></td>\n<td>I take it/him.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>her / it feminine</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je la vois.</code></td>\n<td>I see her/it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>us</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle nous appelle.</code></td>\n<td>She calls us.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>you plural/formal</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vous invite.</code></td>\n<td>I invite you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>them</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On les apprend.</code></td>\n<td>We learn them.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"position-before-the-verb\">Position before the verb</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Full sentence</th>\n<th>Pronoun version</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le métro.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le prends.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle invite ses voisins.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle les invite.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous comprenons la règle.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous la comprenons.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu manges les pommes ?</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu les manges ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"with-negation\">With negation</h3>\n<p>The pronoun still stays before the verb, inside the negative frame.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne le prends pas.</code> — I am not taking it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne nous comprend pas.</code> — She does not understand us.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"with-an-infinitive-after-the-verb\">With an infinitive after the verb</h3>\n<p>With a structure like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir prendre</code>, the object pronoun usually goes before the infinitive it belongs to in later chapters, but for now focus on the core present-tense pattern with one conjugated verb. In this chapter, master sentences such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le prends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous la buvons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils les comprennent</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not leave the object pronoun after the verb as in English. French says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le prends</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je prends le</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Replace the underlined noun with a direct object pronoun.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends _le pain_.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous buvons _l'eau_.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle comprend _les instructions_.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu invites _Marie_ ?</code></li>\n<p>Then turn sentence 1 and sentence 3 negative.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le prends.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le pain</code> is a direct object, so it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>. The pronoun goes before the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous la buvons.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'eau</code> is feminine, so the pronoun is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>; it does not elide here because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">buvons</code> begins with a consonant sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle les comprend.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les instructions</code> is plural, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>. Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>; there is no <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before the object.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu l'invites ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie</code> is a direct object of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">inviter</code>, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, shortened to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before the vowel sound.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Negative forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne le prends pas.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> comes before the object pronoun, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> follows the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne les comprend pas.</code> — direct object pronouns stay before the verb even in the negative.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-indirect-object-pronouns\">Unit 4: Indirect object pronouns</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>An indirect object usually follows <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before a person: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire à</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphoner à</code>. When you replace that person, you use an indirect object pronoun.</p>\n<h3 id=\"indirect-object-pronouns\">Indirect object pronouns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Person replaced</th>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>to me</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il me parle.</code></td>\n<td>He is speaking to me.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>to you singular informal</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je t'écris.</code></td>\n<td>I am writing to you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>to him / to her</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous lui offrons un café.</code></td>\n<td>We offer him/her a coffee.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>to us</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle nous répond.</code></td>\n<td>She answers us.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>to you plural/formal</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vous téléphone.</code></td>\n<td>I am calling you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>to them</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On leur parle.</code></td>\n<td>We are speaking to them.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"direct-versus-indirect\">Direct versus indirect</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb pattern</th>\n<th>Full sentence</th>\n<th>Pronoun version</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>direct object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois Paul.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le vois.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indirect object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle à Paul.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lui parle.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>direct object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous prenons les billets.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous les prenons.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indirect object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous écrivons aux voisins.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous leur écrivons.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle à ses voisins et elle leur offre du café.</code> — She speaks to her neighbors and offers them coffee.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je téléphone à ma sœur tous les soirs.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lui téléphone tous les soirs.</code> — I call my sister every evening.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous écrivons à nos amis.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous leur écrivons.</code> — We write to our friends.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu me réponds demain ?</code> — Are you answering me tomorrow?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> after verbs that require <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before a person. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lui parle</code> is correct; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je le parle</code> is not.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Replace the underlined phrase with an indirect object pronoun.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je donne le livre _à Paul_.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous écrivons _à nos parents_.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle téléphone _à moi_ chaque matin.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous répondez _à la cliente_ ?</code></li>\n<p>Then explain why sentence 1 still keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code>.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lui donne le livre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Paul</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> because it is <strong>à + person</strong>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> stays because only the person phrase was replaced.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous leur écrivons.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à nos parents</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>. The pronoun goes before the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle me téléphone chaque matin.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à moi</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>. Before the verb, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, not the stressed form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous lui répondez ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à la cliente</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, even though the person is feminine; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> serves both <strong>to him</strong> and <strong>to her</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sentence 1 still keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> replaces only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Paul</code>. The direct object and the indirect object are different pieces of the sentence.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-y-en-and-one-pronoun-order\">Unit 5: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, and one-pronoun order</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Y</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> are short pronouns that replace whole prepositional phrases. They also follow the same basic rule as the other object pronouns: when only one pronoun is used, it comes before the conjugated verb.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-pronoun-y\">The pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> for a place or for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + thing</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>What <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> replaces</th>\n<th>Full sentence</th>\n<th>Pronoun version</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>place</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons au marché.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous y allons.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>place</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle reste à la maison.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle y reste.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> + thing</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense à mon travail.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'y pense.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> + thing</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu réponds à la question ?</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu y réponds ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Y</code> does <strong>not</strong> usually replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + person</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-pronoun-en\">The pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + thing</code>, for an unspecified quantity, or after quantity expressions.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>What <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces</th>\n<th>Full sentence</th>\n<th>Pronoun version</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> + thing</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle de ce film.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle en parle.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>unspecified quantity</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du pain.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'en prends.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plural quantity</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il veut trois pommes.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il en veut trois.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>source/amount idea</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous achetons beaucoup de légumes.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous en achetons beaucoup.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>When a number or quantity word is expressed, it stays after the verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'en prends deux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous en voulons beaucoup</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"one-pronoun-order\">One-pronoun order</h3>\n<p>When only one pronoun is used in the present tense, put it before the conjugated verb.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun type</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>direct object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le prends.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indirect object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lui parle.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'y vais.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'en prends.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>With negation, the pattern remains the same.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'y vais pas.</code> — I am not going there.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'en buvons pas.</code> — We are not drinking any.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je vais y</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je prends en</code>. The pronoun moves before the verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'y vais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'en prends</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<p>Replace the highlighted phrase with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons _au marché_ le samedi.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois _du jus_ le matin.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle pense _à son avenir_ souvent.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils veulent _deux billets_.</code></li>\n<p>Then turn sentence 1 and sentence 2 negative.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous y allons le samedi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code> is a place, so it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>. The pronoun goes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'en bois le matin.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du jus</code> is a <strong>de + thing</strong> idea, so it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>. Do not say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le bois</code> unless you mean a specific juice already identified.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle y pense souvent.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à son avenir</code> is <strong>à + thing/idea</strong>, so it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils en veulent deux.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux billets</code> is a quantity phrase, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> and keep the number <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux</code> after the verb.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Negative forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'y allons pas le samedi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> shortens to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> follows the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'en bois pas le matin.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> stays before the verb; <strong>ne ... pas</strong> surrounds the pronoun plus verb chunk.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine food vocabulary, articles, and pronouns the way they appear in everyday French. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du pain et je le mets sur la table.</code> — I take some bread and put it on the table.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle à ses voisins et elle leur offre du café.</code> — She speaks to her neighbors and offers them coffee.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons au marché le samedi et nous y achetons des légumes.</code> — We go to the market on Saturdays and buy vegetables there.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il veut trois pommes, alors il en prend trois.</code> — He wants three apples, so he takes three of them.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu comprends cette recette ou je l'explique encore ?</code> — Do you understand this recipe, or should I explain it again?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne buvons pas de lait, mais nous en achetons pour les enfants.</code> — We do not drink milk, but we buy some for the children.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>After copying the six sentences from memory, check the small words before you check style.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Partitives and negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</strong> Sentence 1 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du pain</code>; sentence 2 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du café</code>; sentence 6 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas de lait</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas du lait</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pronoun placement.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> all come before the conjugated verb they belong to.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct versus indirect.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> replace direct objects; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à ses voisins</code>, an indirect object.</li>\n<li><strong><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> replaces the place <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces a quantity or a <strong>de</strong> idea, and sentence 4 keeps the number after the verb.</li>\n<li><strong>Elision.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'explique</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><strong>Silent endings.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achetons</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">buvons</code> are clear <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> forms; do not replace them with singular forms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark a sentence wrong if the pronoun is correct but sits after the verb. Position is part of the answer in this chapter.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence above:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>underline each noun phrase replaced by a pronoun;</li>\n<li>label each pronoun as direct, indirect, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>;</li>\n<li>identify the sentence that shows the negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the food, place, or quantity.</li>\n<p>Then rewrite sentence 6 without any pronouns.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write five new sentences about what you eat, drink, buy, understand, or tell people. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one partitive article;</li>\n<li>one direct object pronoun;</li>\n<li>one indirect object pronoun;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> and a number.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Notice and mark:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du pain et je le mets sur la table.</code> — verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> is a direct object pronoun referring back to the bread.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle à ses voisins et elle leur offre du café.</code> — verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offre</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> is an indirect object pronoun meaning <strong>to them</strong>, referring to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à ses voisins</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons au marché le samedi et nous y achetons des légumes.</code> — verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achetons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> replaces the place <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il veut trois pommes, alors il en prend trois.</code> — verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veut</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prend</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces the noun idea in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois pommes</code>, and the number <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code> stays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu comprends cette recette ou je l'explique encore ?</code> — verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">explique</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> is a direct object pronoun replacing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette recette</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne buvons pas de lait, mais nous en achetons pour les enfants.</code> — verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">buvons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achetons</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces the milk idea. This is the sentence with the negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas de lait</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence — sample answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sentence 1, subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous prenons du pain et nous le mettons sur la table.</code> — both verbs change with the new subject.</li>\n<li>Sentence 1, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du fromage et je le mets sur la table.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> still works because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fromage</code> is masculine singular.</li>\n<li>Sentence 2, subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle à mes voisins et je leur offre du café.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> stays plural because the neighbors are still plural.</li>\n<li>Sentence 2, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle à sa voisine et elle lui offre du thé.</code> — singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à sa voisine</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>.</li>\n<li>Sentence 3, subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vont au marché le samedi et ils y achètent des légumes.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> still replaces the place.</li>\n<li>Sentence 3, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons à la boulangerie le dimanche et nous y achetons du pain.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à la boulangerie</code> is also a place, so it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>.</li>\n<li>Sentence 4, subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons trois pommes, alors nous en prenons trois.</code> — keep the number after the verb.</li>\n<li>Sentence 4, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il veut deux poires, alors il en prend deux.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces the noun, not the number.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sentence 6 without pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne buvons pas de lait, mais nous achetons du lait pour les enfants.</code> — removing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> means you must say the noun phrase again.</p>\n<p>Make it personal — sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois du café le matin.</code> — partitive article for an unspecified amount.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète le pain et je le mets dans mon sac.</code> — direct object pronoun before the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je téléphone à ma mère et je lui raconte ma journée.</code> — indirect object pronoun for <strong>to my mother</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais au marché et j'y achète des tomates.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> replaces the place.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux trois croissants et j'en prends trois.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces the noun in a quantity phrase; the number remains.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-at-the-market\">Mini-dialogue: at the market</h2>\n<p><strong>Vendeur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour, vous prenez quelque chose à boire ?</code> — Hello, are you taking something to drink? <strong>Claire :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, je prends du jus d'orange, et mon frère prend de l'eau.</code> — Yes, I am taking some orange juice, and my brother is taking some water. <strong>Vendeur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Très bien. Vous voulez aussi des pommes ?</code> — Very good. Do you also want apples? <strong>Claire :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, j'en prends quatre, s'il vous plaît.</code> — Yes, I am taking four of them, please. <strong>Vendeur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et ce fromage, vous le connaissez ?</code> — And this cheese, do you know it? <strong>Claire :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Non, mais ma mère le prend souvent et elle en parle beaucoup.</code> — No, but my mother buys it often and talks about it a lot. <strong>Vendeur :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dans ce cas, j'en donne un petit morceau à goûter.</code> — In that case, I can give you a small piece to taste. <strong>Claire :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Merci ! Je vais y réfléchir, mais je le prends peut-être aussi.</code> — Thank you! I am going to think about it, but I may take it too.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find every partitive article and explain why it is partitive rather than definite or indefinite.</li>\n<li>Identify each pronoun in the dialogue and label it direct, indirect, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</li>\n<li>Explain why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'en prends quatre</code> keeps the number after the verb.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma mère le prend souvent et elle en parle beaucoup</code> without pronouns.</li>\n<li>Change Claire's first answer from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Partitives in the dialogue: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du jus d'orange</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'eau</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des pommes</code> mean an unspecified amount or some items, not a specific identified juice, water, or apples. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du café</code> does not appear in this dialogue; the drink is juice.</li>\n<li>Pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'en prends quatre</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> for the apples; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous le connaissez</code> uses direct <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce fromage</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma mère le prend</code> uses direct <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> for the cheese; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle en parle</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> for talking about it; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'en donne un petit morceau</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> for the cheese; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais y réfléchir</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> for thinking about that choice or matter; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le prends</code> uses direct <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> for the cheese.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'en prends quatre</code> keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quatre</code> because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces the noun idea, not the number. French keeps the quantity after the verb.</li>\n<li>Without pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma mère prend ce fromage souvent et elle parle beaucoup de ce fromage.</code> This is repetitive, which is why French normally uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, nous prenons du jus d'orange, et notre frère prend de l'eau.</code> The verb becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon frère</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre frère</code> if Claire is speaking for a group.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-un-d-ner-simple-chez-moi\">Connected reading: un dîner simple chez moi</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, je prépare souvent un dîner simple. Je prends des légumes dans le frigo et je les coupe pendant que l'eau chauffe. Ensuite, je fais du riz ou je prends du pain si je manque de temps. Je bois rarement du soda, alors j'en achète peu ; je préfère le thé, et j'en bois presque tous les jours. Quand des amis viennent chez moi, je leur montre la recette et je l'explique étape par étape. À la fin du repas, s'il reste du dessert, nous en mangeons encore un peu.</code> — In the evening, I often prepare a simple dinner. I take vegetables from the fridge and cut them while the water heats up. Next, I make rice or take some bread if I am short on time. I rarely drink soda, so I buy little of it; I prefer tea, and I drink some almost every day. When friends come to my place, I show them the recipe and explain it step by step. At the end of the meal, if there is dessert left, we eat a little more of it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List all the partitive or quantity expressions in the reading.</li>\n<li>Find every pronoun before the verb and label its type.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the first three sentences without pronouns if possible.</li>\n<li>Change the reading from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code> where it makes sense.</li>\n<li>Write three true sentences about your own eating or shopping habits using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Partitive or quantity expressions include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des légumes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du riz</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du pain</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du soda</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le thé</code> as a general preference rather than partitive, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du dessert</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un peu</code> with dessert. The phrase <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des amis</code> is indefinite plural people, not a food partitive, but it uses the same visible form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>.</li>\n<li>Pronouns before the verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je les coupe</code> is direct and replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des légumes</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'en achète peu</code> replaces soda; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'en bois presque tous les jours</code> replaces tea; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je leur montre</code> is indirect for the friends; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je l'explique</code> is direct for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la recette</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous en mangeons encore un peu</code> replaces dessert.</li>\n<li>Without pronouns where possible: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, je prépare souvent un dîner simple. Je prends des légumes dans le frigo et je coupe les légumes pendant que l'eau chauffe. Ensuite, je fais du riz ou je prends du pain si je manque de temps.</code> Only the second sentence had a pronoun to expand.</li>\n<li>Changed to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, elle prépare souvent un dîner simple. Elle prend des légumes dans le frigo et elle les coupe pendant que l'eau chauffe. Ensuite, elle fait du riz ou elle prend du pain si elle manque de temps. Elle boit rarement du soda, alors elle en achète peu ; elle préfère le thé, et elle en boit presque tous les jours. Quand des amies viennent chez elle, elle leur montre la recette et elle l'explique étape par étape. À la fin du repas, s'il reste du dessert, elles en mangent encore un peu.</code></li>\n<li>Answers vary. A model set: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois du thé le matin.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète des légumes et je les coupe le soir.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais au marché et j'y prends du pain.</code> Mark yours wrong if a food noun has no article or if a pronoun stays after the verb.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Dropping the article before food or drink.</strong> Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je bois de l'eau</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je bois eau</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> where the meaning is partitive.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du pain</code> means some bread, not a specific bread.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaving the pronoun after the verb.</strong> French says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le prends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je lui parle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'y vais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'en veux</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Confusing direct and indirect objects.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois Paul</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je le vois</code>, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parle à Paul</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je lui parle</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> after negation or quantity expressions.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne bois pas de café</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup de lait</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> for people.</strong> Usually <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> replaces place or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + thing</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + person</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting to keep the number after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'en prends trois</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*j'en trois prends</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"prendre-family-and-boire\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code> family and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprendre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprendre</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bois</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prend</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprend</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprend</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprenons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprenons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">buvons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprenez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprenez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">buvez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prennent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprennent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprennent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boivent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"article-contrast\">Article contrast</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>part of a mass / some</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du riz</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la soupe</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'eau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des fruits</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>definite, specific</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le riz sur la table</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indefinite countable item</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un café</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une pomme</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des biscuits</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>after negation/quantity in many cases</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">d'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas de lait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beaucoup d'eau</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"one-pronoun-chart\">One-pronoun chart</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Pronouns</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>direct object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le/la/l'</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>indirect object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>place / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à + thing</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + thing</code> / quantity</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-habits\">Core habits</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Identify the full noun phrase before replacing it with a pronoun.</li>\n<li>Ask whether the verb takes its object directly or through <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code>.</li>\n<li>Keep one pronoun before the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, keep the number or quantity word after the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a meal, a market trip, or your weekly food routine. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apprendre</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprendre</code>;</li>\n<li>at least three partitive expressions;</li>\n<li>one direct object pronoun, one indirect object pronoun, one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, and one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> plus a number or quantity expression;</li>\n<li>one negative sentence that correctly changes the article to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> when needed.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>underline every article;</li>\n<li>box each pronoun before the verb;</li>\n<li>label each boxed pronoun as direct, indirect, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>;</li>\n<li>check every food and drink noun for the right article choice;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice and rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is a model paragraph that meets every requirement:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, je vais au marché et j'y achète des légumes. Je prends du pain et je le mets dans mon sac. Je bois de l'eau pendant que je marche. À la maison, je comprends la recette, mais je l'explique aussi à ma sœur. Je lui montre les tomates et je lui donne un couteau. Nous préparons de la soupe et du riz. Je ne mets pas de lait dans la soupe. Elle veut trois pommes, alors j'en coupe trois.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bois</code> appear; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprends</code> appears; the partitives are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des légumes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du pain</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'eau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la soupe</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du riz</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> are direct object pronouns; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> is indirect; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> replaces the market; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> appears with the number <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code>; and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas de lait</code> shows the negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> rule.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a food or drink noun with no article;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> after a simple negative where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> is needed;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> replacing a direct object;</li>\n<li>a direct object pronoun after the verb;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> without the number or quantity that should stay after the verb.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 9 lessons: prendre, boire, partitive articles, and object pronouns this chapter helps you speak about food, drink, choice, and replacement. you will learn how french expresses an unspecified quantity, how objects move in front of the verb as pronouns, and how short words like `y` and `en` keep sentences natural and less repetitive. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. conjugate `prendre`, `apprendre`, `comprendre`, and `boire` in the present tense; 2. use the partitive articles `du`, `de la`, `de l'`, and `des`; 3. contrast partitive, definite, and indefinite articles; 4. replace direct objects with `me`, `te`, `le`, `la`, `nous`, `vous`, and `les`; 5. replace indirect objects with `me`, `te`, `lui`, `nous`, `vous`, and `leur`; 6. use `y` for place and `à + thing`, and `en` for `de + thing` and quantities; 7. place a single pronoun before the conjugated verb with confidence. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - recognize direct objects and indirect objects in english and in simple french sentences; - form negative sentences with `ne...pas`; - use `à` and `de` from chapter 6; - follow verb + infinitive patterns from chapter 7. this chapter builds directly on those ideas. if you can identify the object in `je prends le livre`, you can turn it into `je le prends`. if you know that `parler à` uses `à`, you can understand why `je lui parle` is indirect rather than direct. check that the earlier grammar is active, not just familiar. answer these five before continuing. 1. in `je prends le livre`, what is the direct object ? — `le livre`. it receives the action directly, with no preposition. 2. in `je parle à marie`, what is the indirect object ? — `à marie`. the preposition `à` makes it indirect. 3. make `je bois du café` negative. — `je ne bois pas de café.` after a simple negative, the partitive becomes `de`. 4. choose the preposition: `je vais ___ marché` and `je parle ___ ce problème`. — `je vais au marché` and `je parle de ce problème`. these become important for `y` and `en`. 5. identify the infinitive in `je veux prendre le train`. — `prendre`. the object belongs to the infinitive idea, but the first verb `veux` is conjugated. if you missed two or more, review negatives, `à`/`de`, and chapter 7 verb frames before starting. object pronouns require you to identify the object before you replace it. ## how to study this chapter always start from the full sentence. identify the noun you want to replace, ask whether it is direct or indirect, and only then choose the pronoun. for food and drink, memorize complete chunks: `du pain`, `de la soupe`, `en prendre`, `y aller`. ## unit 1: `prendre`, its family, and `boire` ### core idea `prendre` means **to take**, but it also appears in many daily expressions: taking the bus, taking a seat, taking breakfast, taking notes. its close relatives `apprendre` and `comprendre` follow the same pattern. `boire` adds an important food-and-drink verb. ### present tense forms | pronoun | `prendre` | `apprendre` | `comprendre` | `boire` | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `prends` | `apprends` | `comprends` | `bois` | | `tu` | `prends` | `apprends` | `comprends` | `bois` | | `il/elle/on` | `prend` | `apprend` | `comprend` | `boit` | | `nous` | `prenons` | `apprenons` | `comprenons` | `buvons` | | `vous` | `prenez` | `apprenez` | `comprenez` | `buvez` | | `ils/elles` | `prennent` | `apprennent` | `comprennent` | `boivent` | ### examples in context - `je prends le métro chaque matin.` — i take the subway every morning. - `nous prenons un café après le déjeuner.` — we have a coffee after lunch. - `elle apprend le français avec sa voisine.` — she is learning french with her neighbor. - `tu comprends cette règle ?` — do you understand this rule? - `ils boivent de l'eau pendant le cours.` — they drink water during class. - `je prends des notes quand le professeur parle.` — i take notes when the professor speaks. ### pronunciation note the plural stem in `prennent`, `apprennent`, and `comprennent` sounds different from `prenons` and `prenez`. also notice `nous buvons` and `vous buvez`, which sound very different from `je bois`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not expect `prendre` to translate literally every time. `prendre le métro`, `prendre une décision`, and `prendre un café` are normal french chunks. ### sentence-building practice translate into french. 1. we understand the question. 2. i am learning a new expression. 3. do you drink tea in the morning? 4. they take the train at eight o'clock. then change sentence 4 to `je` and sentence 2 to the negative. 1. `nous comprenons la question.` — `comprendre` is irregular: with `nous`, it is `comprenons`. the object stays after the verb here because you have not been asked to replace it with a pronoun. 2. `j'apprends une nouvelle expression.` — `apprendre` follows the same family pattern as `prendre`. `je` becomes `j'` before the vowel sound in `apprends`. 3. `tu bois du thé le matin ?` — `boire` becomes `bois` with `tu`. `du thé` means an unspecified amount of tea; if you said `le thé`, you would mean tea in general. 4. `ils prennent le train à huit heures.` — `prendre` becomes `prennent` with `ils`. use `à` for clock time: `à huit heures`. changes: - `je prends le train à huit heures.` — the `je` form is `prends`, not `prend`. - `je n'apprends pas de nouvelle expression.` — in a simple negative, an indefinite article often becomes `de`. keep **ne** before the verb chunk and **pas** after the conjugated verb. ## unit 2: partitive articles ### core idea use a partitive article when you mean **some**, **an unspecified quantity**, or **part of a larger whole**. ### forms of the partitive | before | form | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | masculine singular noun | `du` | `du pain` | some bread | | feminine singular noun | `de la` | `de la soupe` | some soup | | vowel or mute h singular noun | `de l'` | `de l'eau` | some water | | plural noun | `des` | `des légumes` | some vegetables | ### main uses - `je mange du pain.` — i eat some bread. - `elle prépare de la soupe.` — she is making some soup. - `nous buvons de l'eau.` — we are drinking some water. - `ils achètent des fraises.` — they are buying some strawberries. ### partitive versus definite versus indefinite | article type | example | meaning | |---|---|---| | partitive | `je prends du fromage.` | i am taking some cheese. | | definite | `je prends le fromage.` | i am taking the cheese / the particular cheese. | | indefinite singular | `je prends un sandwich.` | i am taking a sandwich. | | indefinite plural | `je prends des biscuits.` | i am taking some cookies / cookies. | ### after negation and quantity expressions in many negative sentences or after quantity expressions, the article becomes `de` or `d'`. - `je ne bois pas de lait.` — i do not drink milk. - `nous n'achetons pas de tomates.` — we are not buying tomatoes. - `il prend beaucoup de café.` — he drinks a lot of coffee. - `elle veut un kilo de pommes.` — she wants a kilo of apples. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use `le/la/les` automatically when english has no article. french often needs the partitive: `je bois de l'eau`, not `*je bois eau`. ### sentence-building practice choose the correct article. 1. `je bois ___ thé.` 2. `nous mangeons ___ salade ce soir.` 3. `elle n'achète pas ___ sucre.` 4. `ils prennent deux tasses ___ café.` then explain why sentence 3 and sentence 4 do not use the ordinary partitive form. 1. `je bois du thé.` — `thé` is masculine singular, so the partitive is `du`. 2. `nous mangeons de la salade ce soir.` — `salade` is feminine singular, so use `de la` for an unspecified amount. 3. `elle n'achète pas de sucre.` — after a simple negative, `du sucre` changes to `de sucre`. the trap is keeping the ordinary partitive after **pas**. 4. `ils prennent deux tasses de café.` — after a quantity expression such as `deux tasses`, use `de`, not `du`. sentence 3 does not use the ordinary partitive because **ne ... pas** changes an unspecified amount to `de`. sentence 4 does not use the ordinary partitive because `deux tasses` already gives the quantity, so french uses `de café`. ## unit 3: direct object pronouns ### core idea a direct object receives the action **without** a preposition. when you replace it with a pronoun, the pronoun usually goes **before** the conjugated verb. ### direct object pronouns | person or thing replaced | pronoun | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | me | `me` / `m'` | `il me voit.` | he sees me. | | you singular informal | `te` / `t'` | `je te comprends.` | i understand you. | | him / it masculine | `le` / `l'` | `je le prends.` | i take it/him. | | her / it feminine | `la` / `l'` | `je la vois.` | i see her/it. | | us | `nous` | `elle nous appelle.` | she calls us. | | you plural/formal | `vous` | `je vous invite.` | i invite you. | | them | `les` | `on les apprend.` | we learn them. | ### position before the verb | full sentence | pronoun version | |---|---| | `je prends le métro.` | `je le prends.` | | `elle invite ses voisins.` | `elle les invite.` | | `nous comprenons la règle.` | `nous la comprenons.` | | `tu manges les pommes ?` | `tu les manges ?` | ### with negation the pronoun still stays before the verb, inside the negative frame. - `je ne le prends pas.` — i am not taking it. - `elle ne nous comprend pas.` — she does not understand us. ### with an infinitive after the verb with a structure like `vouloir prendre`, the object pronoun usually goes before the infinitive it belongs to in later chapters, but for now focus on the core present-tense pattern with one conjugated verb. in this chapter, master sentences such as `je le prends`, `nous la buvons`, and `ils les comprennent`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not leave the object pronoun after the verb as in english. french says `je le prends`, not `*je prends le`. ### sentence-building practice replace the underlined noun with a direct object pronoun. 1. `je prends _le pain_.` 2. `nous buvons _l'eau_.` 3. `elle comprend _les instructions_.` 4. `tu invites _marie_ ?` then turn sentence 1 and sentence 3 negative. 1. `je le prends.` — `le pain` is a direct object, so it becomes `le`. the pronoun goes before the conjugated verb. 2. `nous la buvons.` — `l'eau` is feminine, so the pronoun is `la`; it does not elide here because `buvons` begins with a consonant sound. 3. `elle les comprend.` — `les instructions` is plural, so use `les`. do not use `leur`; there is no `à` before the object. 4. `tu l'invites ?` — `marie` is a direct object of `inviter`, so use `la`, shortened to `l'` before the vowel sound. negative forms: - `je ne le prends pas.` — `ne` comes before the object pronoun, and `pas` follows the conjugated verb. - `elle ne les comprend pas.` — direct object pronouns stay before the verb even in the negative. ## unit 4: indirect object pronouns ### core idea an indirect object usually follows `à` before a person: `parler à`, `offrir à`, `écrire à`, `téléphoner à`. when you replace that person, you use an indirect object pronoun. ### indirect object pronouns | person replaced | pronoun | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | to me | `me` / `m'` | `il me parle.` | he is speaking to me. | | to you singular informal | `te` / `t'` | `je t'écris.` | i am writing to you. | | to him / to her | `lui` | `nous lui offrons un café.` | we offer him/her a coffee. | | to us | `nous` | `elle nous répond.` | she answers us. | | to you plural/formal | `vous` | `je vous téléphone.` | i am calling you. | | to them | `leur` | `on leur parle.` | we are speaking to them. | ### direct versus indirect | verb pattern | full sentence | pronoun version | |---|---|---| | direct object | `je vois paul.` | `je le vois.` | | indirect object | `je parle à paul.` | `je lui parle.` | | direct object | `nous prenons les billets.` | `nous les prenons.` | | indirect object | `nous écrivons aux voisins.` | `nous leur écrivons.` | ### examples in context - `elle parle à ses voisins et elle leur offre du café.` — she speaks to her neighbors and offers them coffee. - `je téléphone à ma sœur tous les soirs.` -> `je lui téléphone tous les soirs.` — i call my sister every evening. - `nous écrivons à nos amis.` -> `nous leur écrivons.` — we write to our friends. - `tu me réponds demain ?` — are you answering me tomorrow? ### common mistake for english speakers do not use `le` or `la` after verbs that require `à` before a person. `je lui parle` is correct; `*je le parle` is not. ### sentence-building practice replace the underlined phrase with an indirect object pronoun. 1. `je donne le livre _à paul_.` 2. `nous écrivons _à nos parents_.` 3. `elle téléphone _à moi_ chaque matin.` 4. `vous répondez _à la cliente_ ?` then explain why sentence 1 still keeps `le livre`. 1. `je lui donne le livre.` — `à paul` becomes `lui` because it is **à + person**. `le livre` stays because only the person phrase was replaced. 2. `nous leur écrivons.` — `à nos parents` becomes `leur`. the pronoun goes before the conjugated verb. 3. `elle me téléphone chaque matin.` — `à moi` becomes `me`. before the verb, use `me`, not the stressed form `moi`. 4. `vous lui répondez ?` — `à la cliente` becomes `lui`, even though the person is feminine; `lui` serves both **to him** and **to her**. sentence 1 still keeps `le livre` because `lui` replaces only `à paul`. the direct object and the indirect object are different pieces of the sentence. ## unit 5: `y`, `en`, and one-pronoun order ### core idea `y` and `en` are short pronouns that replace whole prepositional phrases. they also follow the same basic rule as the other object pronouns: when only one pronoun is used, it comes before the conjugated verb. ### the pronoun `y` use `y` for a place or for `à + thing`. | what `y` replaces | full sentence | pronoun version | |---|---|---| | place | `nous allons au marché.` | `nous y allons.` | | place | `elle reste à la maison.` | `elle y reste.` | | `à` + thing | `je pense à mon travail.` | `j'y pense.` | | `à` + thing | `tu réponds à la question ?` | `tu y réponds ?` | `y` does **not** usually replace `à + person`. ### the pronoun `en` use `en` for `de + thing`, for an unspecified quantity, or after quantity expressions. | what `en` replaces | full sentence | pronoun version | |---|---|---| | `de` + thing | `elle parle de ce film.` | `elle en parle.` | | unspecified quantity | `je prends du pain.` | `j'en prends.` | | plural quantity | `il veut trois pommes.` | `il en veut trois.` | | source/amount idea | `nous achetons beaucoup de légumes.` | `nous en achetons beaucoup.` | when a number or quantity word is expressed, it stays after the verb: `j'en prends deux`, `nous en voulons beaucoup`. ### one-pronoun order when only one pronoun is used in the present tense, put it before the conjugated verb. | pronoun type | example | |---|---| | direct object | `je le prends.` | | indirect object | `je lui parle.` | | `y` | `j'y vais.` | | `en` | `j'en prends.` | with negation, the pattern remains the same. - `je n'y vais pas.` — i am not going there. - `nous n'en buvons pas.` — we are not drinking any. ### common mistake for english speakers do not say `*je vais y` or `*je prends en`. the pronoun moves before the verb: `j'y vais`, `j'en prends`. ### sentence-building practice replace the highlighted phrase with `y` or `en`. 1. `nous allons _au marché_ le samedi.` 2. `je bois _du jus_ le matin.` 3. `elle pense _à son avenir_ souvent.` 4. `ils veulent _deux billets_.` then turn sentence 1 and sentence 2 negative. 1. `nous y allons le samedi.` — `au marché` is a place, so it becomes `y`. the pronoun goes before `allons`. 2. `j'en bois le matin.` — `du jus` is a **de + thing** idea, so it becomes `en`. do not say `je le bois` unless you mean a specific juice already identified. 3. `elle y pense souvent.` — `à son avenir` is **à + thing/idea**, so it becomes `y`. 4. `ils en veulent deux.` — `deux billets` is a quantity phrase, so use `en` and keep the number `deux` after the verb. negative forms: - `nous n'y allons pas le samedi.` — `ne` shortens to `n'` before `y`, and `pas` follows the conjugated verb. - `je n'en bois pas le matin.` — `en` stays before the verb; **ne ... pas** surrounds the pronoun plus verb chunk. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine food vocabulary, articles, and pronouns the way they appear in everyday french. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je prends du pain et je le mets sur la table.` — i take some bread and put it on the table. 2. `elle parle à ses voisins et elle leur offre du café.` — she speaks to her neighbors and offers them coffee. 3. `nous allons au marché le samedi et nous y achetons des légumes.` — we go to the market on saturdays and buy vegetables there. 4. `il veut trois pommes, alors il en prend trois.` — he wants three apples, so he takes three of them. 5. `tu comprends cette recette ou je l'explique encore ?` — do you understand this recipe, or should i explain it again? 6. `nous ne buvons pas de lait, mais nous en achetons pour les enfants.` — we do not drink milk, but we buy some for the children. after copying the six sentences from memory, check the small words before you check style. - **partitives and negative `de`.** sentence 1 has `du pain`; sentence 2 has `du café`; sentence 6 has `pas de lait`, not `pas du lait`. - **pronoun placement.** `le`, `leur`, `y`, `en`, and `l'` all come before the conjugated verb they belong to. - **direct versus indirect.** `le` and `l'` replace direct objects; `leur` replaces `à ses voisins`, an indirect object. - **`y` and `en`.** `y` replaces the place `au marché`; `en` replaces a quantity or a **de** idea, and sentence 4 keeps the number after the verb. - **elision.** `l'explique` has `l'` before a vowel sound. - **silent endings.** `achetons` and `buvons` are clear `nous` forms; do not replace them with singular forms. mark a sentence wrong if the pronoun is correct but sits after the verb. position is part of the answer in this chapter. ### notice and mark for each sentence above: 1. circle every conjugated verb; 2. underline each noun phrase replaced by a pronoun; 3. label each pronoun as direct, indirect, `y`, or `en`; 4. identify the sentence that shows the negative `de` rule. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the food, place, or quantity. then rewrite sentence 6 without any pronouns. ### make it personal write five new sentences about what you eat, drink, buy, understand, or tell people. include: - one partitive article; - one direct object pronoun; - one indirect object pronoun; - one sentence with `y`; - one sentence with `en` and a number. notice and mark: 1. `je prends du pain et je le mets sur la table.` — verbs: `prends`, `mets`; `le` is a direct object pronoun referring back to the bread. 2. `elle parle à ses voisins et elle leur offre du café.` — verbs: `parle`, `offre`; `leur` is an indirect object pronoun meaning **to them**, referring to `à ses voisins`. 3. `nous allons au marché le samedi et nous y achetons des légumes.` — verbs: `allons`, `achetons`; `y` replaces the place `au marché`. 4. `il veut trois pommes, alors il en prend trois.` — verbs: `veut`, `prend`; `en` replaces the noun idea in `trois pommes`, and the number `trois` stays. 5. `tu comprends cette recette ou je l'explique encore ?` — verbs: `comprends`, `explique`; `l'` is a direct object pronoun replacing `cette recette`. 6. `nous ne buvons pas de lait, mais nous en achetons pour les enfants.` — verbs: `buvons`, `achetons`; `en` replaces the milk idea. this is the sentence with the negative `de` rule: `pas de lait`. change the sentence — sample answers: - sentence 1, subject change: `nous prenons du pain et nous le mettons sur la table.` — both verbs change with the new subject. - sentence 1, detail change: `je prends du fromage et je le mets sur la table.` — `le` still works because `fromage` is masculine singular. - sentence 2, subject change: `je parle à mes voisins et je leur offre du café.` — `leur` stays plural because the neighbors are still plural. - sentence 2, detail change: `elle parle à sa voisine et elle lui offre du thé.` — singular `à sa voisine` becomes `lui`. - sentence 3, subject change: `ils vont au marché le samedi et ils y achètent des légumes.` — `y` still replaces the place. - sentence 3, detail change: `nous allons à la boulangerie le dimanche et nous y achetons du pain.` — `à la boulangerie` is also a place, so it becomes `y`. - sentence 4, subject change: `nous voulons trois pommes, alors nous en prenons trois.` — keep the number after the verb. - sentence 4, detail change: `il veut deux poires, alors il en prend deux.` — `en` replaces the noun, not the number. sentence 6 without pronouns: `nous ne buvons pas de lait, mais nous achetons du lait pour les enfants.` — removing `en` means you must say the noun phrase again. make it personal — sample answers: 1. `je bois du café le matin.` — partitive article for an unspecified amount. 2. `j'achète le pain et je le mets dans mon sac.` — direct object pronoun before the verb. 3. `je téléphone à ma mère et je lui raconte ma journée.` — indirect object pronoun for **to my mother**. 4. `je vais au marché et j'y achète des tomates.` — `y` replaces the place. 5. `je veux trois croissants et j'en prends trois.` — `en` replaces the noun in a quantity phrase; the number remains. ## mini-dialogue: at the market **vendeur :** `bonjour, vous prenez quelque chose à boire ?` — hello, are you taking something to drink? **claire :** `oui, je prends du jus d'orange, et mon frère prend de l'eau.` — yes, i am taking some orange juice, and my brother is taking some water. **vendeur :** `très bien. vous voulez aussi des pommes ?` — very good. do you also want apples? **claire :** `oui, j'en prends quatre, s'il vous plaît.` — yes, i am taking four of them, please. **vendeur :** `et ce fromage, vous le connaissez ?` — and this cheese, do you know it? **claire :** `non, mais ma mère le prend souvent et elle en parle beaucoup.` — no, but my mother buys it often and talks about it a lot. **vendeur :** `dans ce cas, j'en donne un petit morceau à goûter.` — in that case, i can give you a small piece to taste. **claire :** `merci ! je vais y réfléchir, mais je le prends peut-être aussi.` — thank you! i am going to think about it, but i may take it too. ### guided analysis 1. find every partitive article and explain why it is partitive rather than definite or indefinite. 2. identify each pronoun in the dialogue and label it direct, indirect, `y`, or `en`. 3. explain why `j'en prends quatre` keeps the number after the verb. 4. rewrite `ma mère le prend souvent et elle en parle beaucoup` without pronouns. 5. change claire's first answer from `je` to `nous`. 1. partitives in the dialogue: `du jus d'orange`, `de l'eau`, and `des pommes` mean an unspecified amount or some items, not a specific identified juice, water, or apples. `du café` does not appear in this dialogue; the drink is juice. 2. pronouns: `j'en prends quatre` uses `en` for the apples; `vous le connaissez` uses direct `le` for `ce fromage`; `ma mère le prend` uses direct `le` for the cheese; `elle en parle` uses `en` for talking about it; `j'en donne un petit morceau` uses `en` for the cheese; `je vais y réfléchir` uses `y` for thinking about that choice or matter; `je le prends` uses direct `le` for the cheese. 3. `j'en prends quatre` keeps `quatre` because `en` replaces the noun idea, not the number. french keeps the quantity after the verb. 4. without pronouns: `ma mère prend ce fromage souvent et elle parle beaucoup de ce fromage.` this is repetitive, which is why french normally uses `le` and `en`. 5. with `nous`: `oui, nous prenons du jus d'orange, et notre frère prend de l'eau.` the verb becomes `prenons`, and `mon frère` changes to `notre frère` if claire is speaking for a group. ## connected reading: un dîner simple chez moi `le soir, je prépare souvent un dîner simple. je prends des légumes dans le frigo et je les coupe pendant que l'eau chauffe. ensuite, je fais du riz ou je prends du pain si je manque de temps. je bois rarement du soda, alors j'en achète peu ; je préfère le thé, et j'en bois presque tous les jours. quand des amis viennent chez moi, je leur montre la recette et je l'explique étape par étape. à la fin du repas, s'il reste du dessert, nous en mangeons encore un peu.` — in the evening, i often prepare a simple dinner. i take vegetables from the fridge and cut them while the water heats up. next, i make rice or take some bread if i am short on time. i rarely drink soda, so i buy little of it; i prefer tea, and i drink some almost every day. when friends come to my place, i show them the recipe and explain it step by step. at the end of the meal, if there is dessert left, we eat a little more of it. ### reading practice 1. list all the partitive or quantity expressions in the reading. 2. find every pronoun before the verb and label its type. 3. rewrite the first three sentences without pronouns if possible. 4. change the reading from `je`/`nous` to `elle`/`elles` where it makes sense. 5. write three true sentences about your own eating or shopping habits using the reading as a model. 1. partitive or quantity expressions include `des légumes`, `du riz`, `du pain`, `du soda`, `peu`, `le thé` as a general preference rather than partitive, `du dessert`, and `un peu` with dessert. the phrase `des amis` is indefinite plural people, not a food partitive, but it uses the same visible form `des`. 2. pronouns before the verb: `les` in `je les coupe` is direct and replaces `des légumes`; `en` in `j'en achète peu` replaces soda; `en` in `j'en bois presque tous les jours` replaces tea; `leur` in `je leur montre` is indirect for the friends; `l'` in `je l'explique` is direct for `la recette`; `en` in `nous en mangeons encore un peu` replaces dessert. 3. without pronouns where possible: `le soir, je prépare souvent un dîner simple. je prends des légumes dans le frigo et je coupe les légumes pendant que l'eau chauffe. ensuite, je fais du riz ou je prends du pain si je manque de temps.` only the second sentence had a pronoun to expand. 4. changed to `elle`/`elles`: `le soir, elle prépare souvent un dîner simple. elle prend des légumes dans le frigo et elle les coupe pendant que l'eau chauffe. ensuite, elle fait du riz ou elle prend du pain si elle manque de temps. elle boit rarement du soda, alors elle en achète peu ; elle préfère le thé, et elle en boit presque tous les jours. quand des amies viennent chez elle, elle leur montre la recette et elle l'explique étape par étape. à la fin du repas, s'il reste du dessert, elles en mangent encore un peu.` 5. answers vary. a model set: `je bois du thé le matin.` `j'achète des légumes et je les coupe le soir.` `je vais au marché et j'y prends du pain.` mark yours wrong if a food noun has no article or if a pronoun stays after the verb. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. **dropping the article before food or drink.** say `je bois de l'eau`, not `*je bois eau`. 2. **using `le` or `la` where the meaning is partitive.** `je prends du pain` means some bread, not a specific bread. 3. **leaving the pronoun after the verb.** french says `je le prends`, `je lui parle`, `j'y vais`, `j'en veux`. 4. **confusing direct and indirect objects.** `je vois paul` -> `je le vois`, but `je parle à paul` -> `je lui parle`. 5. **forgetting `de` after negation or quantity expressions.** `je ne bois pas de café`, `beaucoup de lait`. 6. **using `y` for people.** usually `y` replaces place or `à + thing`, not `à + person`. 7. **forgetting to keep the number after `en`.** `j'en prends trois`, not `*j'en trois prends`. ## chapter reference sheet ### `prendre` family and `boire` | pronoun | `prendre` | `apprendre` | `comprendre` | `boire` | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `prends` | `apprends` | `comprends` | `bois` | | `tu` | `prends` | `apprends` | `comprends` | `bois` | | `il/elle/on` | `prend` | `apprend` | `comprend` | `boit` | | `nous` | `prenons` | `apprenons` | `comprenons` | `buvons` | | `vous` | `prenez` | `apprenez` | `comprenez` | `buvez` | | `ils/elles` | `prennent` | `apprennent` | `comprennent` | `boivent` | ### article contrast | use | form | example | |---|---|---| | part of a mass / some | `du`, `de la`, `de l'`, `des` | `du riz`, `de la soupe`, `de l'eau`, `des fruits` | | definite, specific | `le`, `la`, `l'`, `les` | `le riz sur la table` | | indefinite countable item | `un`, `une`, `des` | `un café`, `une pomme`, `des biscuits` | | after negation/quantity in many cases | `de`, `d'` | `pas de lait`, `beaucoup d'eau` | ### one-pronoun chart | function | pronouns | |---|---| | direct object | `me`, `te`, `le/la/l'`, `nous`, `vous`, `les` | | indirect object | `me`, `te`, `lui`, `nous`, `vous`, `leur` | | place / `à + thing` | `y` | | `de + thing` / quantity | `en` | ### core habits - identify the full noun phrase before replacing it with a pronoun. - ask whether the verb takes its object directly or through `à`. - keep one pronoun before the conjugated verb. - with `en`, keep the number or quantity word after the verb. ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a meal, a market trip, or your weekly food routine. include: - one sentence with `prendre` or `boire`; - one sentence with `apprendre` or `comprendre`; - at least three partitive expressions; - one direct object pronoun, one indirect object pronoun, one `y`, and one `en`; - one sentence with `en` plus a number or quantity expression; - one negative sentence that correctly changes the article to `de` when needed. after writing: 1. circle every conjugated verb; 2. underline every article; 3. box each pronoun before the verb; 4. label each boxed pronoun as direct, indirect, `y`, or `en`; 5. check every food and drink noun for the right article choice; 6. read the paragraph aloud twice and rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary. here is a model paragraph that meets every requirement: `le samedi, je vais au marché et j'y achète des légumes. je prends du pain et je le mets dans mon sac. je bois de l'eau pendant que je marche. à la maison, je comprends la recette, mais je l'explique aussi à ma sœur. je lui montre les tomates et je lui donne un couteau. nous préparons de la soupe et du riz. je ne mets pas de lait dans la soupe. elle veut trois pommes, alors j'en coupe trois.` how it satisfies the brief: `prends` and `bois` appear; `comprends` appears; the partitives are `des légumes`, `du pain`, `de l'eau`, `de la soupe`, and `du riz`; `le` and `l'` are direct object pronouns; `lui` is indirect; `y` replaces the market; `en` appears with the number `trois`; and `pas de lait` shows the negative `de` rule. mark yourself wrong if you have: - a food or drink noun with no article; - `du`, `de la`, or `des` after a simple negative where `de` is needed; - `lui` or `leur` replacing a direct object; - a direct object pronoun after the verb; - `en` without the number or quantity that should stay after the verb. "},{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 9 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-exercises\">Chapter 9 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Je mange le gâteau.</li>\n<li>Nous parlons à Marie.</li>\n<li>Ils vont au parc.</li>\n<li>Elle achète trois pommes.</li>\n<li>I drink some water.</li>\n<li>I do not drink milk.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>These answers match the answer key. The explanations show what each transformation is testing.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le mange.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le gâteau</code> is a direct object, so it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>. Put the pronoun before the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous lui parlons.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Marie</code> is <strong>à + person</strong>, so it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>. Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler à</code> takes an indirect object.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils y vont.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au parc</code> is a place, so it becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>. The pronoun goes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vont</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle en achète trois.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois pommes</code> is a quantity phrase. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> for the noun and keep the number after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois de l'eau.</code> — water is an unspecified amount, so use the partitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne bois pas de lait.</code> — after a simple negative, the partitive becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>. The trap is writing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas du lait</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>prendre and boire</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le métro.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>partitive articles</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du pain</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>direct and indirect objects</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le vois.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>y and en</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'y vais.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary by design. A correct answer must clearly show the named structure, use the right article or pronoun, and put object pronouns before the conjugated verb.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prendre and boire: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous prenons le bus et nous buvons de l'eau.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code> are both conjugated for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Partitive articles: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète de la soupe pour le dîner.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code> marks an unspecified amount of a feminine noun.</li>\n<li>Direct and indirect objects: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois Paul et je lui parle.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul</code> is direct after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code>, but indirect after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler à</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais au marché et j'en achète deux.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code> could become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces the noun in a quantity phrase.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if the pronoun stays after the verb in a normal statement, if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> is used for a direct object, or if a negative partitive remains <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du café au marché.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous prenons du café au marché.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenons</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne prends pas de café au marché.</code> — the partitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que je prends du café au marché ?</code> — statement order stays after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du thé au marché.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check that every pronoun goes before the conjugated verb, and that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> replaces places while <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces <strong>de + thing</strong> or a quantity phrase.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le métro chaque matin.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code> has a silent final <strong>s</strong>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le métro</code> is a specific object, not a partitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils boivent de l'eau pendant le cours.</code> — Listen for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'</code> before the vowel sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eau</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne bois pas de lait.</code> — The negative changes the partitive to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne le prends pas.</code> — The direct object pronoun stays before the verb, inside the negative frame.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lui téléphone tous les soirs.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> is indirect because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphoner à</code> takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> before a person.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il en veut trois.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> comes before the verb, and the number <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois</code> stays after it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct first for the article, then for pronoun position, then for silent endings. A misplaced pronoun changes the grammar even when the meaning is guessable.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a grocery or café dialogue using two partitives, two direct objects, one indirect object, y, and en.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model café dialogue:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonjour, je voudrais du café et de la soupe.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous prenez le croissant ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, je le prends.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et la salade ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je la prends aussi.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous parlez au serveur ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, je lui demande l'addition.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous allez souvent dans ce café ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, j'y vais le samedi.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous voulez deux cafés ? — Oui, j'en veux deux.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: include two partitives, two direct object pronouns, one indirect object pronoun, one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, and one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>. Mark it wrong if a pronoun appears after the verb in an ordinary sentence, or if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> loses its number in a quantity phrase.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Prendre and boire.</strong> These are irregular present-tense verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je prends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous prenons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je bois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous buvons</code>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le train et je bois de l'eau.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Partitive articles.</strong> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> for an unspecified amount, but use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> after many negatives and quantity expressions. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne mange pas de pain.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Direct and indirect object pronouns.</strong> Direct objects become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>; many <strong>à + person</strong> objects become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lui parle et je l'écoute.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Y and en.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Y</code> often replaces a place or <strong>à + thing</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces <strong>de + thing</strong> or the noun in a quantity phrase. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'y vais et j'en achète deux.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you can explain the rule but cannot produce a new example, redo the exercise tomorrow. Production is the goal.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 9 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. je mange le gâteau. 2. nous parlons à marie. 3. ils vont au parc. 4. elle achète trois pommes. 5. i drink some water. 6. i do not drink milk. these answers match the answer key. the explanations show what each transformation is testing. 1. `je le mange.` — `le gâteau` is a direct object, so it becomes `le`. put the pronoun before the conjugated verb. 2. `nous lui parlons.` — `à marie` is **à + person**, so it becomes `lui`. do not use `la`; `parler à` takes an indirect object. 3. `ils y vont.` — `au parc` is a place, so it becomes `y`. the pronoun goes before `vont`. 4. `elle en achète trois.` — `trois pommes` is a quantity phrase. use `en` for the noun and keep the number after the verb. 5. `je bois de l'eau.` — water is an unspecified amount, so use the partitive `de l'` before a vowel sound. 6. `je ne bois pas de lait.` — after a simple negative, the partitive becomes `de`. the trap is writing `pas du lait`. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **prendre and boire**. use `je prends le métro.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **partitive articles**. use `du pain` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **direct and indirect objects**. use `je le vois.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **y and en**. use `j'y vais.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary by design. a correct answer must clearly show the named structure, use the right article or pronoun, and put object pronouns before the conjugated verb. sample answers: 1. prendre and boire: `nous prenons le bus et nous buvons de l'eau.` — `prendre` and `boire` are both conjugated for `nous`. 2. partitive articles: `j'achète de la soupe pour le dîner.` — `de la` marks an unspecified amount of a feminine noun. 3. direct and indirect objects: `je vois paul et je lui parle.` — `paul` is direct after `voir`, but indirect after `parler à`. 4. `y` and `en`: `je vais au marché et j'en achète deux.` — `au marché` could become `y`; `en` replaces the noun in a quantity phrase. mark yourself wrong if the pronoun stays after the verb in a normal statement, if `lui` or `leur` is used for a direct object, or if a negative partitive remains `du`, `de la`, or `des` after `pas`. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `je prends du café au marché.` 1. change the subject: `nous prenons du café au marché.` — `prendre` changes to `prenons` with `nous`. 2. make it negative: `je ne prends pas de café au marché.` — the partitive `du` becomes `de` after `pas`. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que je prends du café au marché ?` — statement order stays after `est-ce que`. 4. change one meaningful detail: `je prends du thé au marché.` check that every pronoun goes before the conjugated verb, and that `y` replaces places while `en` replaces **de + thing** or a quantity phrase. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `je prends le métro chaque matin.` — `prends` has a silent final **s**, and `le métro` is a specific object, not a partitive. 2. `ils boivent de l'eau pendant le cours.` — listen for `de l'` before the vowel sound in `eau`. 3. `je ne bois pas de lait.` — the negative changes the partitive to `de`. 4. `je ne le prends pas.` — the direct object pronoun stays before the verb, inside the negative frame. 5. `je lui téléphone tous les soirs.` — `lui` is indirect because `téléphoner à` takes `à` before a person. 6. `il en veut trois.` — `en` comes before the verb, and the number `trois` stays after it. correct first for the article, then for pronoun position, then for silent endings. a misplaced pronoun changes the grammar even when the meaning is guessable. ## e. personal writing write a grocery or café dialogue using two partitives, two direct objects, one indirect object, y, and en. answers vary. a model café dialogue: 1. `bonjour, je voudrais du café et de la soupe.` 2. `vous prenez le croissant ?` 3. `oui, je le prends.` 4. `et la salade ?` 5. `je la prends aussi.` 6. `vous parlez au serveur ?` 7. `oui, je lui demande l'addition.` 8. `vous allez souvent dans ce café ?` 9. `oui, j'y vais le samedi.` 10. `vous voulez deux cafés ? — oui, j'en veux deux.` self-check: include two partitives, two direct object pronouns, one indirect object pronoun, one `y`, and one `en`. mark it wrong if a pronoun appears after the verb in an ordinary sentence, or if `en` loses its number in a quantity phrase. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **prendre and boire.** these are irregular present-tense verbs: `je prends`, `nous prenons`, `je bois`, `nous buvons`. example: `je prends le train et je bois de l'eau.` 2. **partitive articles.** use `du`, `de la`, `de l'`, or `des` for an unspecified amount, but use `de` after many negatives and quantity expressions. example: `je ne mange pas de pain.` 3. **direct and indirect object pronouns.** direct objects become `le`, `la`, `l'`, or `les`; many **à + person** objects become `lui` or `leur`. example: `je lui parle et je l'écoute.` 4. **y and en.** `y` often replaces a place or **à + thing**; `en` replaces **de + thing** or the noun in a quantity phrase. example: `j'y vais et j'en achète deux.` if you can explain the rule but cannot produce a new example, redo the exercise tomorrow. production is the goal. "},{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 9 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-answer-key\">Chapter 9 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le mange.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous lui parlons.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils y vont.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle en achète trois.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois de l'eau.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne bois pas de lait.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prendre and boire:</strong> Practice these verbs in meals, transportation, and daily choices.</li>\n<li><strong>Partitive articles:</strong> Use du, de la, de l', and des for an unspecified quantity. After many negatives and quantity expressions, use de.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct and indirect objects:</strong> Place object pronouns before the conjugated verb: le, la, les for direct objects; lui and leur for many à + person objects.</li>\n<li><strong>Y and en:</strong> Y often replaces à + thing or a place. En often replaces de + thing or a quantity phrase.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 9 answer key ## section a 1. `je le mange.` 2. `nous lui parlons.` 3. `ils y vont.` 4. `elle en achète trois.` 5. `je bois de l'eau.` 6. `je ne bois pas de lait.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **prendre and boire:** practice these verbs in meals, transportation, and daily choices. - **partitive articles:** use du, de la, de l', and des for an unspecified quantity. after many negatives and quantity expressions, use de. - **direct and indirect objects:** place object pronouns before the conjugated verb: le, la, les for direct objects; lui and leur for many à + person objects. - **y and en:** y often replaces à + thing or a place. en often replaces de + thing or a quantity phrase. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 9 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 9 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Prendre and boire</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Partitive articles</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Direct and indirect objects</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Y and en</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 9 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **prendre and boire**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **partitive articles** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **direct and indirect objects**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **y and en**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":10,"slug":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs","title":"Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs","documents":[{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/README.md","title":"Chapter 10: Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-and-adverbs\">Chapter 10: Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use possessive and demonstrative adjectives/pronouns.</li>\n<li>Compare adjectives, nouns, and verbs.</li>\n<li>Form superlatives.</li>\n<li>Form and place common adverbs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Possessives</li>\n<li>Demonstratives</li>\n<li>Comparisons</li>\n<li>Adverbs</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 10: possessives, demonstratives, comparisons, and adverbs this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - use possessive and demonstrative adjectives/pronouns. - compare adjectives, nouns, and verbs. - form superlatives. - form and place common adverbs. ## lesson sequence 1. possessives 2. demonstratives 3. comparisons 4. adverbs ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 10 Lessons: Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-lessons-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-and-adverbs\">Chapter 10 Lessons: Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs</h1>\n<p>This chapter helps you talk about ownership, point things out precisely, and compare people, objects, and actions with more nuance. By the end, you should be able to say not only <strong>my book</strong> and <strong>that one</strong>, but also <strong>mine is better</strong>, <strong>ours is less expensive</strong>, and <strong>she explains more clearly than I do</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>choose the correct possessive adjective for every person;</li>\n<li>distinguish possessive adjectives from possessive pronouns;</li>\n<li>use demonstrative adjectives and demonstrative pronouns naturally;</li>\n<li>compare adjectives, nouns, verbs, and adverbs with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aussi</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant</code>;</li>\n<li>form superlatives such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le plus pratique</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la moins chère</code>;</li>\n<li>avoid the common mix-up between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code>, and between <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pire</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus mal</code>;</li>\n<li>form many adverbs with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code> and place them naturally;</li>\n<li>combine these structures in original speech and writing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> correctly;</li>\n<li>make adjectives agree: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petits</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petites</code>;</li>\n<li>conjugate common present-tense verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code>;</li>\n<li>recognize that French often uses stressed pronouns after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous</code>.</li>\n<p>If those skills still feel slow, review them briefly first.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test yourself properly before you add this chapter's details. Answer these five, then check the rule behind each one.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How do you say <strong>my address</strong>? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon adresse</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">adresse</code> is feminine, but it begins with a vowel sound, so French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code>.</li>\n<li>Complete: Paul cherche ___ clé. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sa clé</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clé</code> is feminine singular, so the possessive adjective is feminine; Paul's gender does not control it.</li>\n<li>How do you say <strong>these apartments</strong>? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces appartements</code>. Plural nouns use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code> for both masculine and feminine, even before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li>Translate: Marie is more patient than me. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est plus patiente que moi.</code> The adjective agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie</code>, and after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> you use the stressed pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>.</li>\n<li>Translate: They speak more clearly than we do. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils parlent plus clairement que nous.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clairement</code> is an adverb, so it does not agree with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review articles, adjective agreement, common present-tense verbs, and stressed pronouns before continuing. This chapter makes those choices happen inside longer comparison sentences.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Say every French example aloud. When you practice comparison patterns, memorize the whole frame: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus...que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins de...que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le plus...</code>, not just the key word.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-possessive-adjectives\">Unit 1: Possessive adjectives</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A possessive adjective tells whose thing you are talking about. In French, it agrees with the <strong>thing possessed</strong>, not with the owner.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma sœur</code> — my sister</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon frère</code> — my brother</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes cousins</code> — my cousins</li>\n<p>A man can say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma sœur</code>, and a woman can say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon frère</code>. The noun controls the form.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"full-possessive-adjective-chart\">Full possessive adjective chart</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Owner</th>\n<th>Masculine singular</th>\n<th>Feminine singular</th>\n<th>Plural</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes</code></td>\n<td>my</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ton</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ta</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tes</code></td>\n<td>your</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sa</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ses</code></td>\n<td>his, her, its</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nos</code></td>\n<td>our</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">votre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">votre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vos</code></td>\n<td>your</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs</code></td>\n<td>their</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"feminine-noun-before-a-vowel-sound\">Feminine noun before a vowel sound</h3>\n<p>With a feminine singular noun beginning with a vowel sound or mute <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code>, French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ton</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ta</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sa</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Standard feminine form</th>\n<th>Before vowel sound</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon amie</code></td>\n<td>my female friend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ta</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ton</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ton adresse</code></td>\n<td>your address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sa</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son histoire</code></td>\n<td>his/her story</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The noun is still feminine.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon amie est française.</code> — my female friend is French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son habitude est étrange.</code> — his/her habit is strange.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon bureau est près de la fenêtre.</code> — My desk is near the window.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma montre est dans mon sac.</code> — My watch is in my bag.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Notre professeur connaît vos parents.</code> — Our teacher knows your parents.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Leurs enfants font leurs devoirs après le dîner.</code> — Their children do their homework after dinner.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Listen for liaison in phrases such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes amis</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vos enfants</code>. In speech, that linked sound often helps you hear the plural.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> choose the possessive by the gender of the owner.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul aime sa voiture.</code> — Paul likes his car.</li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie aime son frère.</code> — Marie likes her brother.</li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Marie aime sa frère.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace the owner in each phrase: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon livre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ta chaise</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ses clés</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre rue</code>.</li>\n<li>Say one phrase with a feminine noun before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li>Write four short sentences about objects in your home using four different possessive adjectives.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Model answers — yours can differ if the agreement is correct.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Owner changes:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon livre</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ton livre</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">livre</code> is masculine singular, so the form changes by owner, not by English <strong>my/your</strong> alone.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ta chaise</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma chaise</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaise</code> is feminine singular, so both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ta</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code> are possible with different owners.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ses clés</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nos clés</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clés</code> is plural, so use a plural possessive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre rue</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur rue</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rue</code> is singular, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> stays singular even if the owners are plural.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Feminine noun before a vowel sound: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon amie</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code>, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amie</code> begins with a vowel sound.</li>\n<li>Four home sentences:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon bureau est petit.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bureau</code> is masculine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma cuisine est claire.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cuisine</code> is feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes clés sont sur la table.</code> — plural possessed noun, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Notre appartement est calme.</code> — one apartment possessed by us, so singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The main trap is agreement: possessive adjectives agree with the thing possessed, not with the owner.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-possessive-pronouns\">Unit 2: Possessive pronouns</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A possessive pronoun replaces <strong>noun + possessive adjective</strong>. Instead of repeating the noun, French uses forms like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la tienne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les leurs</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon appartement est petit, mais le sien est lumineux.</code> — My apartment is small, but his/hers is bright.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici nos billets. Où sont les vôtres ?</code> — Here are our tickets. Where are yours?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"important-rule\">Important rule</h3>\n<p>Possessive pronouns normally come with a definite article.</p>\n<h3 id=\"full-possessive-pronoun-chart\">Full possessive pronoun chart</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Owner</th>\n<th>Masculine singular</th>\n<th>Feminine singular</th>\n<th>Masculine plural</th>\n<th>Feminine plural</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les miens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les miennes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le tien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la tienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les tiens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les tiennes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le sien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la sienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les siens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les siennes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nôtre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nôtre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nôtres</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nôtres</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vôtre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la vôtre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vôtres</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vôtres</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les leurs</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les leurs</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-choose-the-form\">How to choose the form</h3>\n<p>The possessive pronoun agrees with the <strong>thing possessed</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma veste</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne</code> — my jacket → mine</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes livres</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les miens</code> — my books → mine</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nos valises</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nôtres</code> — our suitcases → ours</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-patterns\">Common patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Full phrase</th>\n<th>Shorter version</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon téléphone</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code></td>\n<td>mine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ta table</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la tienne</code></td>\n<td>yours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ses dossiers</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les siens</code></td>\n<td>his/hers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre voiture</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nôtre</code></td>\n<td>ours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vos billets</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vôtres</code></td>\n<td>yours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs idées</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les leurs</code></td>\n<td>theirs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon appartement est plus petit que celui de ma sœur, mais le mien est plus calme.</code> — My apartment is smaller than my sister's, but mine is quieter.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ta réponse est claire, mais la sienne est encore plus précise.</code> — Your answer is clear, but his/hers is even more precise.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère votre plan au nôtre.</code> — I prefer your plan to ours.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces lunettes sont les miennes, pas les tiennes.</code> — These glasses are mine, not yours.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Singular and plural pairs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vôtre</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vôtres</code> are distinguished mainly by the article. Train your ear to notice it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not omit the article.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*mien</code></li>\n<p>Also do not choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code> just because the owner is masculine. The missing noun controls the form.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace the repeated noun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici ma clé. Où est ta clé ?</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici la mienne. Où est la tienne ?</code></li>\n<li>Transform these phrases into possessive pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre train</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vos places</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs idées</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon journal</code>.</li>\n<li>Write three comparison sentences using two possessive pronouns.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici la mienne. Où est la tienne ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clé</code> is feminine singular, so the pronouns are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la tienne</code>. The article is part of the possessive pronoun.</li>\n<li>Possessive pronouns:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre train</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nôtre</code> — masculine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vos places</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vôtres</code> — plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs idées</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les leurs</code> — plural; no accent in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs</code>, but the article still shows number.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon journal</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code> — masculine singular.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Comparison examples:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La mienne est plus grande que la tienne.</code> — both pronouns replace a feminine singular noun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le nôtre est moins rapide que le leur.</code> — both replace masculine singular nouns such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">train</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les vôtres sont aussi utiles que les leurs.</code> — both replace plural nouns.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not repeat the noun after a possessive pronoun: say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne clé</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-demonstrative-adjectives-and-demonstrative-pronouns\">Unit 3: Demonstrative adjectives and demonstrative pronouns</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Demonstratives point something out: <strong>this</strong>, <strong>that</strong>, <strong>these</strong>, <strong>those</strong>. French has one set before nouns and another set that replaces nouns.</p>\n<h3 id=\"demonstrative-adjectives\">Demonstrative adjectives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code></td>\n<td>masculine singular before consonant</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce quartier</code></td>\n<td>this/that neighborhood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code></td>\n<td>masculine singular before vowel sound or mute <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">h</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet hôtel</code></td>\n<td>this/that hotel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette</code></td>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette rue</code></td>\n<td>this/that street</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code></td>\n<td>all plurals</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces immeubles</code></td>\n<td>these/those buildings</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce / cet / cette / ces</code> can mean either <strong>this/these</strong> or <strong>that/those</strong>. To be more precise, French often adds <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ci</code> for “this/these here” and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-là</code> for “that/those there.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce livre-ci</code> — this book here</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce livre-là</code> — that book there</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces chaussures-ci</code> — these shoes here</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces chaussures-là</code> — those shoes there</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"demonstrative-pronouns\">Demonstrative pronouns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Gender and number</th>\n<th>Basic form</th>\n<th>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ci</code></th>\n<th>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-là</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-là</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle-ci</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle-là</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux-ci</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux-là</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celles-ci</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celles-là</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Demonstrative pronouns are often followed by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ci</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-là</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de + noun</code>, or a relative clause.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci / celui-là</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends celui-ci.</code></td>\n<td>I'll take this one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui de ...</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui de Paul</code></td>\n<td>Paul's one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle qui ...</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle qui est sur la table</code></td>\n<td>the one that is on the table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux de ...</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux de mes parents</code></td>\n<td>my parents' ones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celles qui ...</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celles qui brillent</code></td>\n<td>the ones that shine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette voiture roule moins rapidement que la nôtre.</code> — This car goes less quickly than ours.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais cet homme, mais pas celui qui parle avec lui.</code> — I know this man, but not the one speaking with him.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces enfants parlent aussi clairement que leurs parents.</code> — These children speak as clearly as their parents.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu préfères cette table-ci ou celle-là ?</code> — Do you prefer this table here or that one there?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cet</code> is pronounced with a clear final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t</code> before a vowel sound: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet ami</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet arbre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet hôtel</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code> before a masculine noun beginning with a vowel sound.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet appartement</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*ce appartement</code></li>\n<p>Also remember: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code> = mine, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code> = this one.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Point to four objects around you and label them with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code>.</li>\n<li>Replace the noun in each phrase: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce sac</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette photo</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces journaux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet exercice</code>.</li>\n<li>Write two short contrast sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ci</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-là</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Object labels:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce livre</code> — masculine singular before a consonant sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet ordinateur</code> — masculine singular before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette table</code> — feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces stylos</code> — plural.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Demonstrative pronouns:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce sac</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code> — masculine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette photo</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle-ci</code> — feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces journaux</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux-ci</code> — masculine plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet exercice</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code> — masculine singular; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code> was only used because of the vowel sound.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Contrast sentences:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Celui-ci est pratique, mais celui-là est plus joli.</code> — both replace masculine singular nouns.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Celle-ci est claire, mais celle-là est trop sombre.</code> — both replace feminine singular nouns.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code> before a noun. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celles</code> when the noun is gone.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-comparisons-and-superlatives\">Unit 4: Comparisons and superlatives</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French uses different comparison patterns depending on whether you are comparing an adjective, a noun, a verb, or an adverb. A superlative picks out the highest or lowest point inside a group.</p>\n<h3 id=\"comparing-adjectives-and-adverbs\">Comparing adjectives and adverbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>more ... than</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus + adjective/adverb + que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus rapide que</code></td>\n<td>faster than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>less ... than</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins + adjective/adverb + que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins lentement que</code></td>\n<td>less slowly than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>as ... as</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aussi + adjective/adverb + que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aussi clairement que</code></td>\n<td>as clearly as</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce quartier est plus calme que le centre-ville.</code> — This neighborhood is quieter than downtown.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur travaille moins rapidement que moi le matin.</code> — My sister works less quickly than I do in the morning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il parle aussi poliment que son collègue.</code> — He speaks as politely as his coworker.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"comparing-nouns\">Comparing nouns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>more ... than</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus de + noun + que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus de temps que</code></td>\n<td>more time than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>less ... than</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins de + noun + que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins d'énergie que</code></td>\n<td>less energy than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>as much/many ... as</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant de + noun + que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant de livres que</code></td>\n<td>as many books as</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons plus de place que vous.</code> — We have more space than you do.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle boit moins de café que son frère.</code> — She drinks less coffee than her brother.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'achète autant de fruits que mes parents.</code> — I buy as much fruit as my parents do.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"comparing-verbs\">Comparing verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>more than</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verb + plus que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travaille plus que</code></td>\n<td>works more than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>less than</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verb + moins que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sort moins que</code></td>\n<td>goes out less than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>as much/as often as</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verb + autant que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lit autant que</code></td>\n<td>reads as much as</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je cuisine plus que mon colocataire.</code> — I cook more than my roommate.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu dors autant que moi pendant le week-end.</code> — You sleep as much as I do during the weekend.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"superlatives\">Superlatives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the most ...</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le / la / les plus + adjective</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la plus pratique</code></td>\n<td>the most practical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the least ...</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le / la / les moins + adjective</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les moins chers</code></td>\n<td>the least expensive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the most ... with a noun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir / prendre / faire + le plus de + noun</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir le plus de temps</code></td>\n<td>to have the most time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the least ... with a noun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir / prendre / faire + le moins de + noun</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre le moins de place</code></td>\n<td>to take up the least space</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est la rue la plus tranquille du quartier.</code> — It's the quietest street in the neighborhood.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est Clara qui a le plus d'expérience.</code> — Clara is the one who has the most experience.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est cette valise qui prend le moins de place.</code> — This suitcase is the one that takes up the least space.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"irregular-comparison-forms\">Irregular comparison forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Base word</th>\n<th>Better/worse form</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon / bonne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleure</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleurs</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleures</code></td>\n<td>adjective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code></td>\n<td>adverb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mauvais / mauvaise</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pire</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus mauvais</code></td>\n<td>adjective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mal</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus mal</code></td>\n<td>adverb</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> with a noun.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce restaurant a un meilleur menu.</code> — This restaurant has a better menu.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est la meilleure solution.</code> — It's the best solution.</li>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> with a verb or adverb.</p>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle cuisine mieux que moi.</code> — She cooks better than I do.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Aujourd'hui, je me sens mieux.</code> — Today, I feel better.</li>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pire</code> with a noun or adjective function.</p>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est la pire excuse de la journée.</code> — That's the worst excuse of the day.</li>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus mal</code> with a verb or adverb function.</p>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il dort plus mal en été.</code> — He sleeps worse in summer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je chante plus mal quand je suis fatigué.</code> — I sing worse when I'm tired.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Try to hear <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus...que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins de...que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le plus...</code> as complete sound patterns. That rhythm matters more than worrying about every final consonant.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not forget <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> in noun comparisons: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus de patience</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*plus patience</code>. Also do not say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*plus bon</code> when French normally wants <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Complete these frames with your own ideas: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus ... que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins de ... que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verb + autant que</code>.</li>\n<li>Compare two people you know with one adjective, one noun, and one verb.</li>\n<li>Write one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pire</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus mal</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Completed frames:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma chambre est plus calme que la cuisine.</code> — adjective comparison: <strong>plus + adjective + que</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai moins de livres que mon frère.</code> — noun comparison: use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille autant que ma sœur.</code> — verb comparison: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant</code> comes after the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Comparing two people:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Adjective: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est plus patiente que Luc.</code> — the adjective agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie</code>.</li>\n<li>Noun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie a autant de devoirs que Luc.</code> — with nouns, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant de</code>.</li>\n<li>Verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie lit plus que Luc.</code> — with verbs, put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus</code> after the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Special forms:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce café est meilleur que celui-là.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> describes a noun; it behaves like an adjective.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu parles mieux que moi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> describes how the verb is done.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette solution est pire que l'autre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pire</code> means worse as a quality.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il écrit plus mal aujourd'hui.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus mal</code> describes the action of writing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The common trap is using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> for a noun. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> for a better thing and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> for doing something better.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-forming-and-placing-adverbs\">Unit 5: Forming and placing adverbs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Many adverbs are built from adjectives. They answer questions like <strong>how?</strong> or <strong>in what way?</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-basic-ment-pattern\">The basic <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code> pattern</h3>\n<p>A common rule is: start from the <strong>feminine adjective form</strong>, then add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Adjective</th>\n<th>Feminine form</th>\n<th>Adverb</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rapide</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rapide</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rapidement</code></td>\n<td>quickly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code></td>\n<td>fortunately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clair</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">claire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clairement</code></td>\n<td>clearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">poli</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">polie</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">poliment</code></td>\n<td>politely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">douce</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doucement</code></td>\n<td>gently</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prudent</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prudente</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prudemment</code></td>\n<td>carefully</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"very-common-adverbs-not-built-with-ment\">Very common adverbs not built with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Adverb</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code></td>\n<td>well</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mal</code></td>\n<td>badly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vite</code></td>\n<td>quickly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souvent</code></td>\n<td>often</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toujours</code></td>\n<td>always</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjà</code></td>\n<td>already</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tard</code></td>\n<td>late</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tôt</code></td>\n<td>early</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"placement-in-simple-sentences\">Placement in simple sentences</h3>\n<p>Many adverbs of manner come after the verb or after the object.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle clairement.</code> — She speaks clearly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous écoutons attentivement le guide.</code> — We listen carefully to the guide.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils répondent poliment aux questions.</code> — They answer the questions politely.</li>\n<p>Short frequent adverbs often stay close to the conjugated verb.</p>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille souvent ici.</code> — I often work here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous comprenons bien cette règle.</code> — We understand this rule well.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu arrives toujours en avance.</code> — You always arrive early.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"comparison-review-with-adverbs\">Comparison review with adverbs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle plus doucement que moi.</code> — She speaks more softly than I do.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous conduisez moins vite qu'avant.</code> — You drive less fast than before.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils révisent aussi sérieusement que leurs amis.</code> — They review as seriously as their friends.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dans la classe, Aïcha répond le mieux.</code> — In the class, Aïcha answers best.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Many <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code> adverbs are long. Say them in syllables: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clai-re-ment</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heu-reu-se-ment</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pru-dem-ment</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not automatically build the adverb from the masculine adjective form.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*heureuxment</code></li>\n<p>French also does not always place the adverb exactly where English does, so learn common whole phrases such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler clairement</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre poliment</code>.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Turn these adjectives into adverbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code>.</li>\n<li>Add a suitable adverb to each verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écouter</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre</code>.</li>\n<li>Write three sentences about your study habits using at least two <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code> adverbs and one short frequent adverb.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Adverbs:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lent</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lentement</code> — use the feminine base <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lente</code>, then add the ending.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code> — feminine <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code> gives <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieux</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieusement</code> — feminine <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieuse</code> gives <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieusement</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calmement</code> — when the adjective already ends in written <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code>, add the ending directly.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Suitable verb phrases:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler clairement</code> — how someone speaks.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écouter attentivement</code> — how someone listens.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre patiemment</code> — how someone waits.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondre poliment</code> — how someone answers.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Study-habit sentences:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'écoute attentivement les exemples.</code> — adverb after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je révise régulièrement le soir.</code> — an adverb in the ending from an adjective.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille souvent à la bibliothèque.</code> — short frequent adverb after the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Check that the adverb describes the action, not the noun. If you need to describe a noun, use an adjective instead.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine the chapter's main structures in useful French. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon appartement est plus petit que celui de ma sœur, mais le mien est plus calme.</code> — My apartment is smaller than my sister's, but mine is quieter.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette voiture roule moins rapidement que la nôtre.</code> — This car travels less quickly than ours.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces enfants parlent aussi clairement que leurs parents.</code> — These children speak as clearly as their parents.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Notre famille passe davantage de temps ensemble et choisit plus souvent ce restaurant-là.</code> — Our family spends more time together and chooses that restaurant there more often.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cet exercice est le plus difficile, mais celui-ci explique la règle le mieux.</code> — This exercise is the most difficult, but this one explains the rule the best.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère vos idées aux leurs, surtout celle qui résout le problème plus simplement.</code> — I prefer your ideas to theirs, especially the one that solves the problem more simply.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>The sentences are already printed, so check the version you wrote from memory against the features that are easy to lose.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Possessives and their accents.</strong> Sentence 1 needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code>; sentence 2 needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nôtre</code>. The circumflex in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nôtre</code> matters because the possessive pronoun is not the same written form as the adjective <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Demonstratives.</strong> Sentence 2 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette</code>, sentence 3 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces</code>, sentence 5 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cet</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code>, and sentence 6 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code>. Make sure you did not write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce exercice</code>; before a vowel sound it is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet exercice</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Comparison frames.</strong> Check the whole frame, not just the comparison word: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus petit que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins rapidement que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aussi clairement que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le plus difficile</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mieux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus simplement</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Silent plural endings.</strong> In sentence 3, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces enfants parlent</code> sounds close to singular speech; the written plural still needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">enfants</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlent</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Adverb spelling.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rapidement</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clairement</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">simplement</code> end the same way, but they come from adjective forms. Do not add agreement to them.</li>\n<li><strong>Accents and spacing.</strong> Keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sœur</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfère</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">résout</code>, and the space before the question mark in any question you create from these models.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Score each sentence for the structure it was meant to test. If one sentence loses both the pronoun and the comparison frame, rewrite that sentence from memory before moving on.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence above:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every possessive or demonstrative form;</li>\n<li>underline the comparison or superlative frame;</li>\n<li>identify whether the short substitute is a possessive pronoun or a demonstrative pronoun.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the owner, the people compared, or the group;</li>\n<li>change one detail such as the noun, adjective, or adverb.</li>\n<p>Check every agreement change carefully.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using one possessive adjective, one possessive pronoun, one demonstrative form, and one comparison with an adverb.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Notice and mark:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon appartement est plus petit que celui de ma sœur, mais le mien est plus calme.</code> — possessives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code>; comparison frames: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus petit que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus calme</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code> is a possessive pronoun, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui</code> is a demonstrative pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette voiture roule moins rapidement que la nôtre.</code> — demonstrative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette</code>; comparison frame: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins rapidement que</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nôtre</code> is a possessive pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces enfants parlent aussi clairement que leurs parents.</code> — demonstrative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code>; possessive: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs</code>; comparison frame: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aussi clairement que</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Notre famille passe davantage de temps ensemble et choisit plus souvent ce restaurant-là.</code> — possessives/demonstratives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce restaurant-là</code>; comparison ideas: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">davantage de temps</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus souvent</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cet exercice est le plus difficile, mais celui-ci explique la règle le mieux.</code> — demonstratives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code>; superlatives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le plus difficile</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mieux</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code> is a demonstrative pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère vos idées aux leurs, surtout celle qui résout le problème plus simplement.</code> — possessives/demonstratives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vos</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code>; comparison frame: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus simplement</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs</code> is a possessive pronoun after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code> is a demonstrative pronoun.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence — sample answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sentence 1, owner change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ton appartement est plus petit que celui de ton frère, mais le tien est plus calme.</code> — possessives change with the owner and the noun.</li>\n<li>Sentence 1, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon studio est plus lumineux que celui de ma sœur, mais le mien est moins calme.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code> still replaces a masculine singular noun.</li>\n<li>Sentence 2, group change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces voitures roulent moins rapidement que les nôtres.</code> — plural noun, plural pronoun.</li>\n<li>Sentence 2, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette voiture roule plus prudemment que la nôtre.</code> — adverb comparison changes, but the pronoun stays feminine singular.</li>\n<li>Sentence 3, group change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces étudiantes parlent aussi clairement que leur professeur.</code> — possessive changes because there is one professor.</li>\n<li>Sentence 3, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces enfants lisent aussi lentement que leurs parents.</code> — the adverb changes the action.</li>\n<li>Sentence 4, owner change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Votre famille passe davantage de temps ensemble et choisit plus souvent ce café-là.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">votre</code> agrees with singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">famille</code>.</li>\n<li>Sentence 4, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Notre famille passe moins de temps ensemble et choisit moins souvent ce restaurant-là.</code> — noun comparison uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins de</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Make it personal — sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon bureau est petit.</code> — possessive adjective before a noun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le mien est près de la fenêtre.</code> — possessive pronoun replacing the noun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette chaise est plus confortable que celle-là.</code> — demonstrative adjective plus demonstrative pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'étudie plus efficacement le matin que le soir.</code> — adverb comparison after the verb.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-choosing-a-study-space\">Mini-dialogue: choosing a study space</h2>\n<p><strong>Lina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu préfères cette bibliothèque-ci ou celle près du parc ?</code> — Do you prefer this library here or the one near the park? <strong>Samir :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère celle près du parc. Son espace de travail est plus lumineux que celui-ci.</code> — I prefer the one near the park. Its work area is brighter than this one. <strong>Lina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, mais la nôtre est plus calme le matin.</code> — Yes, but ours is quieter in the morning. <strong>Samir :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est vrai. Pourtant, leurs chaises sont meilleures que les nôtres.</code> — That's true. Still, their chairs are better than ours. <strong>Lina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et on y travaille mieux ?</code> — And do we work better there? <strong>Samir :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, j'y travaille plus efficacement, mais toi, tu te concentres mieux ici.</code> — I work more efficiently there, but you concentrate better here. <strong>Lina :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Alors, prenons cette salle-là aujourd'hui et celle-ci demain.</code> — Then let's take that room there today and this one here tomorrow. <strong>Samir :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Bonne idée. De toutes les options, ce sont celles qui ferment le plus tard.</code> — Good idea. Of all the options, those are the ones that close the latest.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find two demonstrative pronouns and explain what noun each one replaces.</li>\n<li>Find two possessive pronouns and state the missing noun.</li>\n<li>Identify one regular comparative and one irregular comparative.</li>\n<li>Why does Samir say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> in line 5 but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleures</code> in line 4?</li>\n<li>Rewrite line 7 as a singular informal command with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Two demonstrative pronouns:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle près du parc</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bibliothèque</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">espace de travail</code>. Later, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle-ci</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">salle</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celles qui ferment le plus tard</code> refers to the feminine plural options.</li>\n<li><strong>Two possessive pronouns:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nôtre</code> means our library or study space, so the missing noun is feminine singular. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nôtres</code> means our chairs, so the missing noun is plural.</li>\n<li><strong>Regular comparative:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Son espace de travail est plus lumineux que celui-ci.</code> This is the normal adjective frame <strong>plus + adjective + que</strong>. <strong>Irregular comparative:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs chaises sont meilleures que les nôtres</code>, where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleures</code> is the adjective form of better.</li>\n<li>Samir says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleures</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaises</code> because he is describing a noun; the adjective agrees feminine plural. Lina says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on y travaille mieux</code> because better describes the action of working, so French uses the adverb.</li>\n<li>Singular informal command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Prends cette salle-là aujourd'hui et celle-ci demain.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Prendre</code> is not a regular -er command, so the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command keeps its final written <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The main choice in this dialogue is always the missing noun: possessive and demonstrative pronouns agree with the thing they replace, not with the speaker.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-two-ways-to-study\">Connected reading: two ways to study</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon frère et moi avons un emploi du temps très différent. Le sien commence toujours tôt, tandis que le mien devient plus productif en soirée. Le matin, il travaille plus rapidement que moi et il organise ses notes plus soigneusement. Moi, je relis plus lentement, mais je retiens mieux les détails. Quand nous avons autant de devoirs l'un que l'autre, celui qui planifie le plus calmement finit souvent le premier. En général, sa méthode est la plus régulière, mais la mienne est parfois la plus créative.</code> — My brother and I have very different schedules. His starts early, while mine becomes more productive in the evening. In the morning, he works more quickly than I do and organizes his notes more carefully. I reread more slowly, but I retain details better. When we have as much homework as each other, the one who plans most calmly often finishes first. In general, his method is the most consistent, but mine is sometimes the most creative.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List every possessive form in the paragraph and classify each one as an adjective or pronoun.</li>\n<li>Find one noun comparison and three adverb comparisons.</li>\n<li>Explain why the text says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le sien</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la sienne</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the paragraph so that the two people are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma sœur et moi</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon frère et moi</code>.</li>\n<li>Write three true sentences about your own study habits using the reading as a model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Possessive forms:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon</code> is a possessive adjective with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">frère</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le sien</code> is a possessive pronoun; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code> is a possessive pronoun; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ses</code> is a possessive adjective with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notes</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sa</code> is a possessive adjective with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">méthode</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne</code> is a possessive pronoun replacing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">méthode</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Comparisons:</strong> the noun comparison is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant de devoirs l'un que l'autre</code>, where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant de</code> is followed by the noun. The adverb comparisons are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus rapidement que moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus soigneusement</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus lentement</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code>, and the superlative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le plus calmement</code>. Any three of those count.</li>\n<li>The text uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le sien</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code> because the noun they replace is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">emploi du temps</code>, which is masculine singular. A possessive pronoun agrees with the thing owned, never with the owner — which is why the last sentence switches to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne</code>, agreeing with the feminine <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">méthode</code>.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma sœur et moi</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur et moi avons un emploi du temps très différent. Le sien commence toujours tôt, tandis que le mien devient plus productif en soirée. Le matin, elle travaille plus rapidement que moi et elle organise ses notes plus soigneusement. Moi, je relis plus lentement, mais je retiens mieux les détails. Quand nous avons autant de devoirs l'une que l'autre, celle qui planifie le plus calmement finit souvent la première. En général, sa méthode est la plus régulière, mais la mienne est parfois la plus créative.</code> Notice what does <strong>not</strong> change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le sien</code> stays masculine because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">emploi du temps</code> is still the thing owned, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ses notes</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sa méthode</code> stay the same for the same reason. Only the words describing the person change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'un</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'une</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la première</code>.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. A model set: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma routine commence plus tard que celle de mon frère.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille plus lentement le matin, mais je retiens mieux les exemples.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma méthode est moins régulière que la sienne, mais elle est plus créative.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if a possessive pronoun does not match the missing noun, if a noun comparison is missing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, or if you use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> where the sentence needs the adverb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Choosing a possessive according to the owner's gender instead of the noun's gender.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that feminine nouns before a vowel sound use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ton</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon amie</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*ma amie</code>.</li>\n<li>Omitting the article before a possessive pronoun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*mien</code>.</li>\n<li>Mixing up possessive pronouns and demonstrative pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nôtre</code> is not the same as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui-ci</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> in noun comparisons: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus de patience</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins d'argent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant de temps</code>.</li>\n<li>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> where French needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pire</code> where French needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus mal</code>.</li>\n<li>Building <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code> adverbs from the wrong adjective form.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"possessive-adjectives\">Possessive adjectives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Owner</th>\n<th>Masc. sg.</th>\n<th>Fem. sg.</th>\n<th>Plural</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ton</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ta</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sa</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ses</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">notre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nos</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">votre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">votre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vos</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leurs</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"possessive-pronouns\">Possessive pronouns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Owner</th>\n<th>Masc. sg.</th>\n<th>Fem. sg.</th>\n<th>Masc. pl.</th>\n<th>Fem. pl.</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la mienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les miens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les miennes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le tien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la tienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les tiens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les tiennes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le sien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la sienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les siens</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les siennes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nôtre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nôtre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nôtres</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nôtres</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le vôtre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la vôtre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vôtres</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vôtres</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les leurs</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les leurs</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"demonstratives\">Demonstratives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Masculine singular</th>\n<th>Feminine singular</th>\n<th>Plural</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>adjective</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce / cet</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pronoun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux / celles</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"comparison-patterns\">Comparison patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>adjective / adverb</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus / moins / aussi + word + que</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>noun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus de / moins de / autant de + noun + que</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>verb</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verb + plus / moins / autant + que</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>adjective superlative</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le / la / les plus / moins + adjective</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>adverb superlative</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le plus / le moins + adverb</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"high-value-irregular-forms\">High-value irregular forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Idea</th>\n<th>Adjective use</th>\n<th>Adverb use</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>better / best</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>worse / worst</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus mal</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"adverb-formation-reminders\">Adverb formation reminders</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>very often: feminine adjective form + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clair</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clairement</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code></li>\n<li>common independent forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mal</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vite</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souvent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toujours</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences comparing two places, two people, or two routines. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least three possessive adjectives;</li>\n<li>at least two possessive pronouns;</li>\n<li>at least two demonstrative forms;</li>\n<li>one adjective comparison;</li>\n<li>one noun comparison;</li>\n<li>one verb or adverb comparison;</li>\n<li>one superlative;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code>.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every possessive form;</li>\n<li>underline every demonstrative form;</li>\n<li>box every comparison frame (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus...que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moins de...que</code>, and so on);</li>\n<li>check whether each possessive agrees with the possessed noun;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary, so compare your paragraph with the checklist. Here is one complete model:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma routine du matin est plus calme que celle de mon frère. Son appartement a moins de bruit que le mien, mais mon bureau reçoit plus de lumière que le sien. Cette cuisine-ci est petite, mais cette table-là est la plus pratique de la pièce. Nous avons autant de livres que nos voisins, mais nous travaillons plus efficacement qu'eux. Leur méthode est bonne, pourtant la nôtre marche mieux le soir. Ces notes sont plus claires que les leurs. Je choisis ce cahier-ci parce qu'il est le meilleur pour réviser. Ma sœur explique plus simplement que moi, et j'écoute attentivement ses conseils.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: possessive adjectives include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nos</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ses</code>; possessive pronouns include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le mien</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le sien</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la nôtre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les leurs</code>; demonstrative forms include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette cuisine-ci</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette table-là</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce cahier-ci</code>; the paragraph has adjective comparisons, noun comparisons, adverb comparisons, superlatives, and both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if any of these appear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a possessive chosen by the owner's gender instead of the noun, such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sa appartement</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code> before a masculine singular noun beginning with a vowel sound, such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce appartement</code>;</li>\n<li>a noun comparison without <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, such as <strong>plus bruit que</strong>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> before a noun or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> after a verb;</li>\n<li>an adverb such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clairement</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">simplement</code> with plural agreement added.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 10 lessons: possessives, demonstratives, comparisons, and adverbs this chapter helps you talk about ownership, point things out precisely, and compare people, objects, and actions with more nuance. by the end, you should be able to say not only **my book** and **that one**, but also **mine is better**, **ours is less expensive**, and **she explains more clearly than i do**. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. choose the correct possessive adjective for every person; 2. distinguish possessive adjectives from possessive pronouns; 3. use demonstrative adjectives and demonstrative pronouns naturally; 4. compare adjectives, nouns, verbs, and adverbs with `plus`, `moins`, `aussi`, and `autant`; 5. form superlatives such as `le plus pratique` and `la moins chère`; 6. avoid the common mix-up between `meilleur` and `mieux`, and between `pire` and `plus mal`; 7. form many adverbs with `-ment` and place them naturally; 8. combine these structures in original speech and writing. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - use `le`, `la`, `l'`, and `les` correctly; - make adjectives agree: `petit`, `petite`, `petits`, `petites`; - conjugate common present-tense verbs such as `être`, `avoir`, `aller`, `faire`, and `parler`; - recognize that french often uses stressed pronouns after `que`: `que moi`, `que lui`, `que nous`. if those skills still feel slow, review them briefly first. test yourself properly before you add this chapter's details. answer these five, then check the rule behind each one. 1. how do you say **my address**? — `mon adresse`. `adresse` is feminine, but it begins with a vowel sound, so french uses `mon` instead of `ma`. 2. complete: paul cherche ___ clé. — `sa clé`. `clé` is feminine singular, so the possessive adjective is feminine; paul's gender does not control it. 3. how do you say **these apartments**? — `ces appartements`. plural nouns use `ces` for both masculine and feminine, even before a vowel sound. 4. translate: marie is more patient than me. — `marie est plus patiente que moi.` the adjective agrees with `marie`, and after `que` you use the stressed pronoun `moi`. 5. translate: they speak more clearly than we do. — `ils parlent plus clairement que nous.` `clairement` is an adverb, so it does not agree with `ils`. if you missed two or more, review articles, adjective agreement, common present-tense verbs, and stressed pronouns before continuing. this chapter makes those choices happen inside longer comparison sentences. ## how to study this chapter say every french example aloud. when you practice comparison patterns, memorize the whole frame: `plus...que`, `moins de...que`, `le plus...`, not just the key word. ## unit 1: possessive adjectives ### core idea a possessive adjective tells whose thing you are talking about. in french, it agrees with the **thing possessed**, not with the owner. - `ma sœur` — my sister - `mon frère` — my brother - `mes cousins` — my cousins a man can say `ma sœur`, and a woman can say `mon frère`. the noun controls the form. ### full possessive adjective chart | owner | masculine singular | feminine singular | plural | english | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `mon` | `ma` | `mes` | my | | `tu` | `ton` | `ta` | `tes` | your | | `il / elle / on` | `son` | `sa` | `ses` | his, her, its | | `nous` | `notre` | `notre` | `nos` | our | | `vous` | `votre` | `votre` | `vos` | your | | `ils / elles` | `leur` | `leur` | `leurs` | their | ### feminine noun before a vowel sound with a feminine singular noun beginning with a vowel sound or mute `h`, french uses `mon`, `ton`, or `son` instead of `ma`, `ta`, or `sa`. | standard feminine form | before vowel sound | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `ma` | `mon` | `mon amie` | my female friend | | `ta` | `ton` | `ton adresse` | your address | | `sa` | `son` | `son histoire` | his/her story | the noun is still feminine. - `mon amie est française.` — my female friend is french. - `son habitude est étrange.` — his/her habit is strange. ### examples in context - `mon bureau est près de la fenêtre.` — my desk is near the window. - `ma montre est dans mon sac.` — my watch is in my bag. - `notre professeur connaît vos parents.` — our teacher knows your parents. - `leurs enfants font leurs devoirs après le dîner.` — their children do their homework after dinner. ### pronunciation note listen for liaison in phrases such as `mes amis` and `vos enfants`. in speech, that linked sound often helps you hear the plural. ### common mistake for english speakers do **not** choose the possessive by the gender of the owner. - correct: `paul aime sa voiture.` — paul likes his car. - correct: `marie aime son frère.` — marie likes her brother. - incorrect: `*marie aime sa frère.` ### sentence-building practice 1. replace the owner in each phrase: `mon livre`, `ta chaise`, `ses clés`, `notre rue`. 2. say one phrase with a feminine noun before a vowel sound. 3. write four short sentences about objects in your home using four different possessive adjectives. model answers — yours can differ if the agreement is correct. 1. owner changes: - `mon livre` -> `ton livre` — `livre` is masculine singular, so the form changes by owner, not by english **my/your** alone. - `ta chaise` -> `ma chaise` — `chaise` is feminine singular, so both `ta` and `ma` are possible with different owners. - `ses clés` -> `nos clés` — `clés` is plural, so use a plural possessive. - `notre rue` -> `leur rue` — `rue` is singular, so `leur` stays singular even if the owners are plural. 2. feminine noun before a vowel sound: `mon amie` — use `mon`, not `ma`, because `amie` begins with a vowel sound. 3. four home sentences: - `mon bureau est petit.` — `bureau` is masculine singular. - `ma cuisine est claire.` — `cuisine` is feminine singular. - `mes clés sont sur la table.` — plural possessed noun, so `mes`. - `notre appartement est calme.` — one apartment possessed by us, so singular `notre`. the main trap is agreement: possessive adjectives agree with the thing possessed, not with the owner. ## unit 2: possessive pronouns ### core idea a possessive pronoun replaces **noun + possessive adjective**. instead of repeating the noun, french uses forms like `le mien`, `la tienne`, `les leurs`. - `mon appartement est petit, mais le sien est lumineux.` — my apartment is small, but his/hers is bright. - `voici nos billets. où sont les vôtres ?` — here are our tickets. where are yours? ### important rule possessive pronouns normally come with a definite article. ### full possessive pronoun chart | owner | masculine singular | feminine singular | masculine plural | feminine plural | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `le mien` | `la mienne` | `les miens` | `les miennes` | | `tu` | `le tien` | `la tienne` | `les tiens` | `les tiennes` | | `il / elle / on` | `le sien` | `la sienne` | `les siens` | `les siennes` | | `nous` | `le nôtre` | `la nôtre` | `les nôtres` | `les nôtres` | | `vous` | `le vôtre` | `la vôtre` | `les vôtres` | `les vôtres` | | `ils / elles` | `le leur` | `la leur` | `les leurs` | `les leurs` | ### how to choose the form the possessive pronoun agrees with the **thing possessed**. - `ma veste` -> `la mienne` — my jacket -> mine - `mes livres` -> `les miens` — my books -> mine - `nos valises` -> `les nôtres` — our suitcases -> ours ### common patterns | full phrase | shorter version | english | |---|---|---| | `mon téléphone` | `le mien` | mine | | `ta table` | `la tienne` | yours | | `ses dossiers` | `les siens` | his/hers | | `notre voiture` | `la nôtre` | ours | | `vos billets` | `les vôtres` | yours | | `leurs idées` | `les leurs` | theirs | ### examples in context - `mon appartement est plus petit que celui de ma sœur, mais le mien est plus calme.` — my apartment is smaller than my sister's, but mine is quieter. - `ta réponse est claire, mais la sienne est encore plus précise.` — your answer is clear, but his/hers is even more precise. - `je préfère votre plan au nôtre.` — i prefer your plan to ours. - `ces lunettes sont les miennes, pas les tiennes.` — these glasses are mine, not yours. ### pronunciation note singular and plural pairs such as `le vôtre` and `les vôtres` are distinguished mainly by the article. train your ear to notice it. ### common mistake for english speakers do not omit the article. - correct: `le mien` - incorrect: `*mien` also do not choose `le mien` just because the owner is masculine. the missing noun controls the form. ### sentence-building practice 1. replace the repeated noun: `voici ma clé. où est ta clé ?` -> `voici la mienne. où est la tienne ?` 2. transform these phrases into possessive pronouns: `notre train`, `vos places`, `leurs idées`, `mon journal`. 3. write three comparison sentences using two possessive pronouns. 1. `voici la mienne. où est la tienne ?` — `clé` is feminine singular, so the pronouns are `la mienne` and `la tienne`. the article is part of the possessive pronoun. 2. possessive pronouns: - `notre train` -> `le nôtre` — masculine singular. - `vos places` -> `les vôtres` — plural. - `leurs idées` -> `les leurs` — plural; no accent in `leurs`, but the article still shows number. - `mon journal` -> `le mien` — masculine singular. 3. comparison examples: - `la mienne est plus grande que la tienne.` — both pronouns replace a feminine singular noun. - `le nôtre est moins rapide que le leur.` — both replace masculine singular nouns such as `train`. - `les vôtres sont aussi utiles que les leurs.` — both replace plural nouns. do not repeat the noun after a possessive pronoun: say `la mienne`, not `la mienne clé`. ## unit 3: demonstrative adjectives and demonstrative pronouns ### core idea demonstratives point something out: **this**, **that**, **these**, **those**. french has one set before nouns and another set that replaces nouns. ### demonstrative adjectives | form | use | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `ce` | masculine singular before consonant | `ce quartier` | this/that neighborhood | | `cet` | masculine singular before vowel sound or mute `h` | `cet hôtel` | this/that hotel | | `cette` | feminine singular | `cette rue` | this/that street | | `ces` | all plurals | `ces immeubles` | these/those buildings | french `ce / cet / cette / ces` can mean either **this/these** or **that/those**. to be more precise, french often adds `-ci` for “this/these here” and `-là` for “that/those there.” - `ce livre-ci` — this book here - `ce livre-là` — that book there - `ces chaussures-ci` — these shoes here - `ces chaussures-là` — those shoes there ### demonstrative pronouns | gender and number | basic form | with `-ci` | with `-là` | |---|---|---|---| | masculine singular | `celui` | `celui-ci` | `celui-là` | | feminine singular | `celle` | `celle-ci` | `celle-là` | | masculine plural | `ceux` | `ceux-ci` | `ceux-là` | | feminine plural | `celles` | `celles-ci` | `celles-là` | demonstrative pronouns are often followed by `-ci`, `-là`, `de + noun`, or a relative clause. | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---| | `celui-ci / celui-là` | `je prends celui-ci.` | i'll take this one. | | `celui de ...` | `celui de paul` | paul's one | | `celle qui ...` | `celle qui est sur la table` | the one that is on the table | | `ceux de ...` | `ceux de mes parents` | my parents' ones | | `celles qui ...` | `celles qui brillent` | the ones that shine | ### examples in context - `cette voiture roule moins rapidement que la nôtre.` — this car goes less quickly than ours. - `je connais cet homme, mais pas celui qui parle avec lui.` — i know this man, but not the one speaking with him. - `ces enfants parlent aussi clairement que leurs parents.` — these children speak as clearly as their parents. - `tu préfères cette table-ci ou celle-là ?` — do you prefer this table here or that one there? ### pronunciation note `cet` is pronounced with a clear final `t` before a vowel sound: `cet ami`, `cet arbre`, `cet hôtel`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use `ce` before a masculine noun beginning with a vowel sound. - correct: `cet appartement` - incorrect: `*ce appartement` also remember: `le mien` = mine, but `celui-ci` = this one. ### sentence-building practice 1. point to four objects around you and label them with `ce`, `cet`, `cette`, or `ces`. 2. replace the noun in each phrase: `ce sac`, `cette photo`, `ces journaux`, `cet exercice`. 3. write two short contrast sentences with `-ci` and `-là`. 1. object labels: - `ce livre` — masculine singular before a consonant sound. - `cet ordinateur` — masculine singular before a vowel sound. - `cette table` — feminine singular. - `ces stylos` — plural. 2. demonstrative pronouns: - `ce sac` -> `celui-ci` — masculine singular. - `cette photo` -> `celle-ci` — feminine singular. - `ces journaux` -> `ceux-ci` — masculine plural. - `cet exercice` -> `celui-ci` — masculine singular; `cet` was only used because of the vowel sound. 3. contrast sentences: - `celui-ci est pratique, mais celui-là est plus joli.` — both replace masculine singular nouns. - `celle-ci est claire, mais celle-là est trop sombre.` — both replace feminine singular nouns. use `ce`, `cet`, `cette`, and `ces` before a noun. use `celui`, `celle`, `ceux`, and `celles` when the noun is gone. ## unit 4: comparisons and superlatives ### core idea french uses different comparison patterns depending on whether you are comparing an adjective, a noun, a verb, or an adverb. a superlative picks out the highest or lowest point inside a group. ### comparing adjectives and adverbs | meaning | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | more ... than | `plus + adjective/adverb + que` | `plus rapide que` | faster than | | less ... than | `moins + adjective/adverb + que` | `moins lentement que` | less slowly than | | as ... as | `aussi + adjective/adverb + que` | `aussi clairement que` | as clearly as | - `ce quartier est plus calme que le centre-ville.` — this neighborhood is quieter than downtown. - `ma sœur travaille moins rapidement que moi le matin.` — my sister works less quickly than i do in the morning. - `il parle aussi poliment que son collègue.` — he speaks as politely as his coworker. ### comparing nouns | meaning | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | more ... than | `plus de + noun + que` | `plus de temps que` | more time than | | less ... than | `moins de + noun + que` | `moins d'énergie que` | less energy than | | as much/many ... as | `autant de + noun + que` | `autant de livres que` | as many books as | - `nous avons plus de place que vous.` — we have more space than you do. - `elle boit moins de café que son frère.` — she drinks less coffee than her brother. - `j'achète autant de fruits que mes parents.` — i buy as much fruit as my parents do. ### comparing verbs | meaning | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | more than | `verb + plus que` | `travaille plus que` | works more than | | less than | `verb + moins que` | `sort moins que` | goes out less than | | as much/as often as | `verb + autant que` | `lit autant que` | reads as much as | - `je cuisine plus que mon colocataire.` — i cook more than my roommate. - `tu dors autant que moi pendant le week-end.` — you sleep as much as i do during the weekend. ### superlatives | meaning | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | the most ... | `le / la / les plus + adjective` | `la plus pratique` | the most practical | | the least ... | `le / la / les moins + adjective` | `les moins chers` | the least expensive | | the most ... with a noun | `avoir / prendre / faire + le plus de + noun` | `avoir le plus de temps` | to have the most time | | the least ... with a noun | `avoir / prendre / faire + le moins de + noun` | `prendre le moins de place` | to take up the least space | - `c'est la rue la plus tranquille du quartier.` — it's the quietest street in the neighborhood. - `c'est clara qui a le plus d'expérience.` — clara is the one who has the most experience. - `c'est cette valise qui prend le moins de place.` — this suitcase is the one that takes up the least space. ### irregular comparison forms | base word | better/worse form | use | |---|---|---| | `bon / bonne` | `meilleur`, `meilleure`, `meilleurs`, `meilleures` | adjective | | `bien` | `mieux` | adverb | | `mauvais / mauvaise` | `pire` or `plus mauvais` | adjective | | `mal` | `plus mal` | adverb | use `meilleur` with a noun. - `ce restaurant a un meilleur menu.` — this restaurant has a better menu. - `c'est la meilleure solution.` — it's the best solution. use `mieux` with a verb or adverb. - `elle cuisine mieux que moi.` — she cooks better than i do. - `aujourd'hui, je me sens mieux.` — today, i feel better. use `pire` with a noun or adjective function. - `c'est la pire excuse de la journée.` — that's the worst excuse of the day. use `plus mal` with a verb or adverb function. - `il dort plus mal en été.` — he sleeps worse in summer. - `je chante plus mal quand je suis fatigué.` — i sing worse when i'm tired. ### pronunciation note try to hear `plus...que`, `moins de...que`, and `le plus...` as complete sound patterns. that rhythm matters more than worrying about every final consonant. ### common mistake for english speakers do not forget `de` in noun comparisons: `plus de patience`, not `*plus patience`. also do not say `*plus bon` when french normally wants `meilleur`. ### sentence-building practice 1. complete these frames with your own ideas: `plus ... que`, `moins de ... que`, `verb + autant que`. 2. compare two people you know with one adjective, one noun, and one verb. 3. write one sentence with `meilleur`, one with `mieux`, one with `pire`, and one with `plus mal`. 1. completed frames: - `ma chambre est plus calme que la cuisine.` — adjective comparison: **plus + adjective + que**. - `j'ai moins de livres que mon frère.` — noun comparison: use `de` after `moins`. - `je travaille autant que ma sœur.` — verb comparison: `autant` comes after the verb. 2. comparing two people: - adjective: `marie est plus patiente que luc.` — the adjective agrees with `marie`. - noun: `marie a autant de devoirs que luc.` — with nouns, use `autant de`. - verb: `marie lit plus que luc.` — with verbs, put `plus` after the verb. 3. special forms: - `ce café est meilleur que celui-là.` — `meilleur` describes a noun; it behaves like an adjective. - `tu parles mieux que moi.` — `mieux` describes how the verb is done. - `cette solution est pire que l'autre.` — `pire` means worse as a quality. - `il écrit plus mal aujourd'hui.` — `plus mal` describes the action of writing. the common trap is using `mieux` for a noun. use `meilleur` for a better thing and `mieux` for doing something better. ## unit 5: forming and placing adverbs ### core idea many adverbs are built from adjectives. they answer questions like **how?** or **in what way?** ### the basic `-ment` pattern a common rule is: start from the **feminine adjective form**, then add `-ment`. | adjective | feminine form | adverb | english | |---|---|---|---| | `rapide` | `rapide` | `rapidement` | quickly | | `heureux` | `heureuse` | `heureusement` | fortunately | | `clair` | `claire` | `clairement` | clearly | | `poli` | `polie` | `poliment` | politely | | `doux` | `douce` | `doucement` | gently | | `prudent` | `prudente` | `prudemment` | carefully | ### very common adverbs not built with `-ment` | adverb | english | |---|---| | `bien` | well | | `mal` | badly | | `vite` | quickly | | `souvent` | often | | `toujours` | always | | `déjà` | already | | `tard` | late | | `tôt` | early | ### placement in simple sentences many adverbs of manner come after the verb or after the object. - `elle parle clairement.` — she speaks clearly. - `nous écoutons attentivement le guide.` — we listen carefully to the guide. - `ils répondent poliment aux questions.` — they answer the questions politely. short frequent adverbs often stay close to the conjugated verb. - `je travaille souvent ici.` — i often work here. - `nous comprenons bien cette règle.` — we understand this rule well. - `tu arrives toujours en avance.` — you always arrive early. ### comparison review with adverbs - `elle parle plus doucement que moi.` — she speaks more softly than i do. - `vous conduisez moins vite qu'avant.` — you drive less fast than before. - `ils révisent aussi sérieusement que leurs amis.` — they review as seriously as their friends. - `dans la classe, aïcha répond le mieux.` — in the class, aïcha answers best. ### pronunciation note many `-ment` adverbs are long. say them in syllables: `clai-re-ment`, `heu-reu-se-ment`, `pru-dem-ment`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not automatically build the adverb from the masculine adjective form. - correct: `heureuse` -> `heureusement` - incorrect: `*heureuxment` french also does not always place the adverb exactly where english does, so learn common whole phrases such as `parler clairement` and `répondre poliment`. ### sentence-building practice 1. turn these adjectives into adverbs: `lent`, `heureux`, `sérieux`, `calme`. 2. add a suitable adverb to each verb: `parler`, `écouter`, `attendre`, `répondre`. 3. write three sentences about your study habits using at least two `-ment` adverbs and one short frequent adverb. 1. adverbs: - `lent` -> `lentement` — use the feminine base `lente`, then add the ending. - `heureux` -> `heureusement` — feminine `heureuse` gives `heureusement`. - `sérieux` -> `sérieusement` — feminine `sérieuse` gives `sérieusement`. - `calme` -> `calmement` — when the adjective already ends in written `e`, add the ending directly. 2. suitable verb phrases: - `parler clairement` — how someone speaks. - `écouter attentivement` — how someone listens. - `attendre patiemment` — how someone waits. - `répondre poliment` — how someone answers. 3. study-habit sentences: - `j'écoute attentivement les exemples.` — adverb after the verb. - `je révise régulièrement le soir.` — an adverb in the ending from an adjective. - `je travaille souvent à la bibliothèque.` — short frequent adverb after the verb. check that the adverb describes the action, not the noun. if you need to describe a noun, use an adjective instead. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine the chapter's main structures in useful french. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `mon appartement est plus petit que celui de ma sœur, mais le mien est plus calme.` — my apartment is smaller than my sister's, but mine is quieter. 2. `cette voiture roule moins rapidement que la nôtre.` — this car travels less quickly than ours. 3. `ces enfants parlent aussi clairement que leurs parents.` — these children speak as clearly as their parents. 4. `notre famille passe davantage de temps ensemble et choisit plus souvent ce restaurant-là.` — our family spends more time together and chooses that restaurant there more often. 5. `cet exercice est le plus difficile, mais celui-ci explique la règle le mieux.` — this exercise is the most difficult, but this one explains the rule the best. 6. `je préfère vos idées aux leurs, surtout celle qui résout le problème plus simplement.` — i prefer your ideas to theirs, especially the one that solves the problem more simply. the sentences are already printed, so check the version you wrote from memory against the features that are easy to lose. - **possessives and their accents.** sentence 1 needs `mon`, `ma`, and `le mien`; sentence 2 needs `la nôtre`. the circumflex in `nôtre` matters because the possessive pronoun is not the same written form as the adjective `notre`. - **demonstratives.** sentence 2 has `cette`, sentence 3 has `ces`, sentence 5 has `cet` and `celui-ci`, and sentence 6 has `celle`. make sure you did not write `ce exercice`; before a vowel sound it is `cet exercice`. - **comparison frames.** check the whole frame, not just the comparison word: `plus petit que`, `moins rapidement que`, `aussi clairement que`, `le plus difficile`, `le mieux`, `plus simplement`. - **silent plural endings.** in sentence 3, `ces enfants parlent` sounds close to singular speech; the written plural still needs `ces`, `enfants`, and `parlent`. - **adverb spelling.** `rapidement`, `clairement`, and `simplement` end the same way, but they come from adjective forms. do not add agreement to them. - **accents and spacing.** keep `sœur`, `préfère`, `résout`, and the space before the question mark in any question you create from these models. score each sentence for the structure it was meant to test. if one sentence loses both the pronoun and the comparison frame, rewrite that sentence from memory before moving on. ### notice and mark for each sentence above: 1. circle every possessive or demonstrative form; 2. underline the comparison or superlative frame; 3. identify whether the short substitute is a possessive pronoun or a demonstrative pronoun. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the owner, the people compared, or the group; 2. change one detail such as the noun, adjective, or adverb. check every agreement change carefully. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using one possessive adjective, one possessive pronoun, one demonstrative form, and one comparison with an adverb. notice and mark: 1. `mon appartement est plus petit que celui de ma sœur, mais le mien est plus calme.` — possessives: `mon`, `ma`, `le mien`; comparison frames: `plus petit que`, `plus calme`; `le mien` is a possessive pronoun, and `celui` is a demonstrative pronoun. 2. `cette voiture roule moins rapidement que la nôtre.` — demonstrative: `cette`; comparison frame: `moins rapidement que`; `la nôtre` is a possessive pronoun. 3. `ces enfants parlent aussi clairement que leurs parents.` — demonstrative: `ces`; possessive: `leurs`; comparison frame: `aussi clairement que`. 4. `notre famille passe davantage de temps ensemble et choisit plus souvent ce restaurant-là.` — possessives/demonstratives: `notre`, `ce restaurant-là`; comparison ideas: `davantage de temps`, `plus souvent`. 5. `cet exercice est le plus difficile, mais celui-ci explique la règle le mieux.` — demonstratives: `cet`, `celui-ci`; superlatives: `le plus difficile`, `le mieux`; `celui-ci` is a demonstrative pronoun. 6. `je préfère vos idées aux leurs, surtout celle qui résout le problème plus simplement.` — possessives/demonstratives: `vos`, `leurs`, `celle`; comparison frame: `plus simplement`; `leurs` is a possessive pronoun after `aux`, and `celle` is a demonstrative pronoun. change the sentence — sample answers: - sentence 1, owner change: `ton appartement est plus petit que celui de ton frère, mais le tien est plus calme.` — possessives change with the owner and the noun. - sentence 1, detail change: `mon studio est plus lumineux que celui de ma sœur, mais le mien est moins calme.` — `le mien` still replaces a masculine singular noun. - sentence 2, group change: `ces voitures roulent moins rapidement que les nôtres.` — plural noun, plural pronoun. - sentence 2, detail change: `cette voiture roule plus prudemment que la nôtre.` — adverb comparison changes, but the pronoun stays feminine singular. - sentence 3, group change: `ces étudiantes parlent aussi clairement que leur professeur.` — possessive changes because there is one professor. - sentence 3, detail change: `ces enfants lisent aussi lentement que leurs parents.` — the adverb changes the action. - sentence 4, owner change: `votre famille passe davantage de temps ensemble et choisit plus souvent ce café-là.` — `votre` agrees with singular `famille`. - sentence 4, detail change: `notre famille passe moins de temps ensemble et choisit moins souvent ce restaurant-là.` — noun comparison uses `moins de`. make it personal — sample answers: 1. `mon bureau est petit.` — possessive adjective before a noun. 2. `le mien est près de la fenêtre.` — possessive pronoun replacing the noun. 3. `cette chaise est plus confortable que celle-là.` — demonstrative adjective plus demonstrative pronoun. 4. `j'étudie plus efficacement le matin que le soir.` — adverb comparison after the verb. ## mini-dialogue: choosing a study space **lina :** `tu préfères cette bibliothèque-ci ou celle près du parc ?` — do you prefer this library here or the one near the park? **samir :** `je préfère celle près du parc. son espace de travail est plus lumineux que celui-ci.` — i prefer the one near the park. its work area is brighter than this one. **lina :** `oui, mais la nôtre est plus calme le matin.` — yes, but ours is quieter in the morning. **samir :** `c'est vrai. pourtant, leurs chaises sont meilleures que les nôtres.` — that's true. still, their chairs are better than ours. **lina :** `et on y travaille mieux ?` — and do we work better there? **samir :** `moi, j'y travaille plus efficacement, mais toi, tu te concentres mieux ici.` — i work more efficiently there, but you concentrate better here. **lina :** `alors, prenons cette salle-là aujourd'hui et celle-ci demain.` — then let's take that room there today and this one here tomorrow. **samir :** `bonne idée. de toutes les options, ce sont celles qui ferment le plus tard.` — good idea. of all the options, those are the ones that close the latest. ### guided analysis 1. find two demonstrative pronouns and explain what noun each one replaces. 2. find two possessive pronouns and state the missing noun. 3. identify one regular comparative and one irregular comparative. 4. why does samir say `mieux` in line 5 but `meilleures` in line 4? 5. rewrite line 7 as a singular informal command with `prendre`. 1. **two demonstrative pronouns:** `celle près du parc` replaces `bibliothèque`, and `celui-ci` replaces `espace de travail`. later, `celle-ci` replaces `salle`, and `celles qui ferment le plus tard` refers to the feminine plural options. 2. **two possessive pronouns:** `la nôtre` means our library or study space, so the missing noun is feminine singular. `les nôtres` means our chairs, so the missing noun is plural. 3. **regular comparative:** `son espace de travail est plus lumineux que celui-ci.` this is the normal adjective frame **plus + adjective + que**. **irregular comparative:** `leurs chaises sont meilleures que les nôtres`, where `meilleures` is the adjective form of better. 4. samir says `meilleures` for `chaises` because he is describing a noun; the adjective agrees feminine plural. lina says `mieux` in `on y travaille mieux` because better describes the action of working, so french uses the adverb. 5. singular informal command: `prends cette salle-là aujourd'hui et celle-ci demain.` `prendre` is not a regular -er command, so the `tu` command keeps its final written `s`. the main choice in this dialogue is always the missing noun: possessive and demonstrative pronouns agree with the thing they replace, not with the speaker. ## connected reading: two ways to study `mon frère et moi avons un emploi du temps très différent. le sien commence toujours tôt, tandis que le mien devient plus productif en soirée. le matin, il travaille plus rapidement que moi et il organise ses notes plus soigneusement. moi, je relis plus lentement, mais je retiens mieux les détails. quand nous avons autant de devoirs l'un que l'autre, celui qui planifie le plus calmement finit souvent le premier. en général, sa méthode est la plus régulière, mais la mienne est parfois la plus créative.` — my brother and i have very different schedules. his starts early, while mine becomes more productive in the evening. in the morning, he works more quickly than i do and organizes his notes more carefully. i reread more slowly, but i retain details better. when we have as much homework as each other, the one who plans most calmly often finishes first. in general, his method is the most consistent, but mine is sometimes the most creative. ### reading practice 1. list every possessive form in the paragraph and classify each one as an adjective or pronoun. 2. find one noun comparison and three adverb comparisons. 3. explain why the text says `le sien` and `le mien`, not `la sienne` and `la mienne`. 4. rewrite the paragraph so that the two people are `ma sœur et moi` instead of `mon frère et moi`. 5. write three true sentences about your own study habits using the reading as a model. 1. **possessive forms:** `mon` is a possessive adjective with `frère`; `le sien` is a possessive pronoun; `le mien` is a possessive pronoun; `ses` is a possessive adjective with `notes`; `sa` is a possessive adjective with `méthode`; `la mienne` is a possessive pronoun replacing `méthode`. 2. **comparisons:** the noun comparison is `autant de devoirs l'un que l'autre`, where `autant de` is followed by the noun. the adverb comparisons are `plus rapidement que moi`, `plus soigneusement`, `plus lentement`, `mieux`, and the superlative `le plus calmement`. any three of those count. 3. the text uses `le sien` and `le mien` because the noun they replace is `emploi du temps`, which is masculine singular. a possessive pronoun agrees with the thing owned, never with the owner — which is why the last sentence switches to `la mienne`, agreeing with the feminine `méthode`. 4. with `ma sœur et moi`: `ma sœur et moi avons un emploi du temps très différent. le sien commence toujours tôt, tandis que le mien devient plus productif en soirée. le matin, elle travaille plus rapidement que moi et elle organise ses notes plus soigneusement. moi, je relis plus lentement, mais je retiens mieux les détails. quand nous avons autant de devoirs l'une que l'autre, celle qui planifie le plus calmement finit souvent la première. en général, sa méthode est la plus régulière, mais la mienne est parfois la plus créative.` notice what does **not** change: `le sien` stays masculine because `emploi du temps` is still the thing owned, and `ses notes` and `sa méthode` stay the same for the same reason. only the words describing the person change: `il` to `elle`, `l'un` to `l'une`, `celui` to `celle`, `le premier` to `la première`. 5. answers vary. a model set: `ma routine commence plus tard que celle de mon frère.` `je travaille plus lentement le matin, mais je retiens mieux les exemples.` `ma méthode est moins régulière que la sienne, mais elle est plus créative.` mark yourself wrong if a possessive pronoun does not match the missing noun, if a noun comparison is missing `de`, or if you use `meilleur` where the sentence needs the adverb `mieux`. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. choosing a possessive according to the owner's gender instead of the noun's gender. 2. forgetting that feminine nouns before a vowel sound use `mon`, `ton`, or `son`: `mon amie`, not `*ma amie`. 3. omitting the article before a possessive pronoun: `le mien`, not `*mien`. 4. mixing up possessive pronouns and demonstrative pronouns: `le nôtre` is not the same as `celui-ci`. 5. forgetting `de` in noun comparisons: `plus de patience`, `moins d'argent`, `autant de temps`. 6. using `meilleur` where french needs `mieux`, or `pire` where french needs `plus mal`. 7. building `-ment` adverbs from the wrong adjective form. ## chapter reference sheet ### possessive adjectives | owner | masc. sg. | fem. sg. | plural | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `mon` | `ma` | `mes` | | `tu` | `ton` | `ta` | `tes` | | `il / elle / on` | `son` | `sa` | `ses` | | `nous` | `notre` | `notre` | `nos` | | `vous` | `votre` | `votre` | `vos` | | `ils / elles` | `leur` | `leur` | `leurs` | ### possessive pronouns | owner | masc. sg. | fem. sg. | masc. pl. | fem. pl. | |---|---|---|---|---| | `je` | `le mien` | `la mienne` | `les miens` | `les miennes` | | `tu` | `le tien` | `la tienne` | `les tiens` | `les tiennes` | | `il / elle / on` | `le sien` | `la sienne` | `les siens` | `les siennes` | | `nous` | `le nôtre` | `la nôtre` | `les nôtres` | `les nôtres` | | `vous` | `le vôtre` | `la vôtre` | `les vôtres` | `les vôtres` | | `ils / elles` | `le leur` | `la leur` | `les leurs` | `les leurs` | ### demonstratives | function | masculine singular | feminine singular | plural | |---|---|---|---| | adjective | `ce / cet` | `cette` | `ces` | | pronoun | `celui` | `celle` | `ceux / celles` | ### comparison patterns | type | pattern | |---|---| | adjective / adverb | `plus / moins / aussi + word + que` | | noun | `plus de / moins de / autant de + noun + que` | | verb | `verb + plus / moins / autant + que` | | adjective superlative | `le / la / les plus / moins + adjective` | | adverb superlative | `le plus / le moins + adverb` | ### high-value irregular forms | idea | adjective use | adverb use | |---|---|---| | better / best | `meilleur` | `mieux` | | worse / worst | `pire` | `plus mal` | ### adverb formation reminders - very often: feminine adjective form + `-ment` - `clair` -> `clairement` - `heureux` -> `heureusement` - common independent forms: `bien`, `mal`, `vite`, `souvent`, `toujours` ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences comparing two places, two people, or two routines. include: - at least three possessive adjectives; - at least two possessive pronouns; - at least two demonstrative forms; - one adjective comparison; - one noun comparison; - one verb or adverb comparison; - one superlative; - one sentence with `meilleur` or `mieux`. after writing: 1. circle every possessive form; 2. underline every demonstrative form; 3. box every comparison frame (`plus...que`, `moins de...que`, and so on); 4. check whether each possessive agrees with the possessed noun; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary, so compare your paragraph with the checklist. here is one complete model: `ma routine du matin est plus calme que celle de mon frère. son appartement a moins de bruit que le mien, mais mon bureau reçoit plus de lumière que le sien. cette cuisine-ci est petite, mais cette table-là est la plus pratique de la pièce. nous avons autant de livres que nos voisins, mais nous travaillons plus efficacement qu'eux. leur méthode est bonne, pourtant la nôtre marche mieux le soir. ces notes sont plus claires que les leurs. je choisis ce cahier-ci parce qu'il est le meilleur pour réviser. ma sœur explique plus simplement que moi, et j'écoute attentivement ses conseils.` how it satisfies the brief: possessive adjectives include `ma`, `mon`, `son`, `nos`, `leur`, and `ses`; possessive pronouns include `le mien`, `le sien`, `la nôtre`, and `les leurs`; demonstrative forms include `celle`, `cette cuisine-ci`, `cette table-là`, `ces`, and `ce cahier-ci`; the paragraph has adjective comparisons, noun comparisons, adverb comparisons, superlatives, and both `mieux` and `meilleur`. mark yourself wrong if any of these appear: - a possessive chosen by the owner's gender instead of the noun, such as `sa appartement`; - `ce` before a masculine singular noun beginning with a vowel sound, such as `ce appartement`; - a noun comparison without `de`, such as **plus bruit que**; - `mieux` before a noun or `meilleur` after a verb; - an adverb such as `clairement` or `simplement` with plural agreement added. "},{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 10 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-exercises\">Chapter 10 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>my female friend</li>\n<li>this tree</li>\n<li>those houses</li>\n<li>Marie is taller than Luc.</li>\n<li>We have as much time.</li>\n<li>heureuse → adverb</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>These answers match the answer key.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon amie</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amie</code> is feminine, but French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code> before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet arbre</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arbre</code> is masculine singular and begins with a vowel sound, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces maisons</code> — plural demonstrative adjective for both masculine and feminine nouns is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est plus grande que Luc.</code> — adjective comparison uses <strong>plus + adjective + que</strong>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grande</code> agrees with Marie.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons autant de temps.</code> — with a noun, the structure is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant de</code>, not just <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">autant</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code> — form the adverb from the feminine adjective <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code>, then add the ending.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>possessives</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon frère</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>demonstratives</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce livre</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>comparisons</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est plus rapide que moi.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>adverbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lentement</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct answer must clearly show the named structure and keep agreement with the noun being described or replaced.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Possessives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur cherche son téléphone.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sœur</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">son</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphone</code>.</li>\n<li>Demonstratives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette chaise est plus confortable que ce banc.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette</code> is feminine singular, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code> masculine singular.</li>\n<li>Comparisons: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon frère est moins patient que moi.</code> — adjective comparison with <strong>moins ... que</strong>.</li>\n<li>Adverbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle répond calmement.</code> — the adverb describes how she answers.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if a possessive agrees with the owner instead of the noun, if you use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code> before a vowel sound where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code> is needed, or if a noun comparison is missing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette voiture est plus rapide que la mienne.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces voitures sont plus rapides que la mienne.</code> — demonstrative, verb, and adjective all change for plural.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette voiture n'est pas plus rapide que la mienne.</code> — <strong>ne ... pas</strong> wraps the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que cette voiture est plus rapide que la mienne ?</code></li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette voiture est moins chère que la mienne.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check agreement every time. Demonstratives and possessive pronouns must match the noun they point to or replace.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon bureau est près de la fenêtre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bureau</code> is masculine, so the possessive is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">près</code> needs its accent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma montre est dans mon sac.</code> — two possessed nouns, two genders: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma montre</code> but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon sac</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu préfères cette table-ci ou celle-là ?</code> — listen for the accent in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préfères</code>, the contrast <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code>, and the space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce restaurant a un meilleur menu.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">meilleur</code> describes the noun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">menu</code>; do not write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mieux</code> here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle clairement.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clairement</code> is an adverb from the feminine adjective form, not an adjective agreeing with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère vos idées aux leurs, surtout celle qui résout le problème plus simplement.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux leurs</code> includes the article inside <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code> replaces a feminine singular noun.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct in separate passes. First recover the words, then check agreement, then check accents and the comparison or pronoun form.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Compare two family members or two cities in eight sentences using possessives, demonstratives, and three comparison patterns.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model comparison paragraph:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur habite à Lyon, et mon frère habite à Nantes.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Son appartement est plus grand que le sien.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette ville est moins chère que celle-là.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lyon a plus de musées que Nantes.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nantes a autant de cafés que Lyon.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur travaille plus souvent chez elle.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon frère parle plus rapidement qu'elle.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère son quartier au leur.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: include possessives, demonstratives, and three comparison patterns. Mark it wrong if an adjective does not agree, if a noun comparison lacks <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, or if a pronoun such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le sien</code> does not match the noun it replaces.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Possessives.</strong> Possessive adjectives agree with the possessed noun, not the owner. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie cherche son livre et sa clé.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Demonstratives.</strong> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces</code> before nouns; use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">celles</code> when the noun is replaced. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette chaise est confortable, mais celle-ci est meilleure.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Comparisons.</strong> Use <strong>plus/moins/aussi + adjective or adverb + que</strong>, <strong>plus/moins/autant de + noun</strong>, and <strong>verb + plus/moins/autant</strong>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lis autant que toi.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Adverbs.</strong> Many adverbs come from the feminine adjective plus the ending. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle clairement.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If your example uses the rule but has agreement wrong, the answer is not correct yet. Agreement is part of the structure.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 10 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. my female friend 2. this tree 3. those houses 4. marie is taller than luc. 5. we have as much time. 6. heureuse -> adverb these answers match the answer key. 1. `mon amie` — `amie` is feminine, but french uses `mon` before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel sound. 2. `cet arbre` — `arbre` is masculine singular and begins with a vowel sound, so use `cet`, not `ce`. 3. `ces maisons` — plural demonstrative adjective for both masculine and feminine nouns is `ces`. 4. `marie est plus grande que luc.` — adjective comparison uses **plus + adjective + que**. `grande` agrees with marie. 5. `nous avons autant de temps.` — with a noun, the structure is `autant de`, not just `autant`. 6. `heureusement` — form the adverb from the feminine adjective `heureuse`, then add the ending. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **possessives**. use `mon frère` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **demonstratives**. use `ce livre` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **comparisons**. use `elle est plus rapide que moi.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **adverbs**. use `lentement` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct answer must clearly show the named structure and keep agreement with the noun being described or replaced. sample answers: 1. possessives: `ma sœur cherche son téléphone.` — `ma` agrees with `sœur`; `son` agrees with `téléphone`. 2. demonstratives: `cette chaise est plus confortable que ce banc.` — `cette` is feminine singular, `ce` masculine singular. 3. comparisons: `mon frère est moins patient que moi.` — adjective comparison with **moins ... que**. 4. adverbs: `elle répond calmement.` — the adverb describes how she answers. mark yourself wrong if a possessive agrees with the owner instead of the noun, if you use `ce` before a vowel sound where `cet` is needed, or if a noun comparison is missing `de`. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `cette voiture est plus rapide que la mienne.` 1. change the subject: `ces voitures sont plus rapides que la mienne.` — demonstrative, verb, and adjective all change for plural. 2. make it negative: `cette voiture n'est pas plus rapide que la mienne.` — **ne ... pas** wraps the conjugated verb. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que cette voiture est plus rapide que la mienne ?` 4. change one meaningful detail: `cette voiture est moins chère que la mienne.` check agreement every time. demonstratives and possessive pronouns must match the noun they point to or replace. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `mon bureau est près de la fenêtre.` — `bureau` is masculine, so the possessive is `mon`; `près` needs its accent. 2. `ma montre est dans mon sac.` — two possessed nouns, two genders: `ma montre` but `mon sac`. 3. `tu préfères cette table-ci ou celle-là ?` — listen for the accent in `préfères`, the contrast `cette` / `celle`, and the space before `?`. 4. `ce restaurant a un meilleur menu.` — `meilleur` describes the noun `menu`; do not write `mieux` here. 5. `elle parle clairement.` — `clairement` is an adverb from the feminine adjective form, not an adjective agreeing with `elle`. 6. `je préfère vos idées aux leurs, surtout celle qui résout le problème plus simplement.` — `aux leurs` includes the article inside `aux`, and `celle` replaces a feminine singular noun. correct in separate passes. first recover the words, then check agreement, then check accents and the comparison or pronoun form. ## e. personal writing compare two family members or two cities in eight sentences using possessives, demonstratives, and three comparison patterns. answers vary. a model comparison paragraph: 1. `ma sœur habite à lyon, et mon frère habite à nantes.` 2. `son appartement est plus grand que le sien.` 3. `cette ville est moins chère que celle-là.` 4. `lyon a plus de musées que nantes.` 5. `nantes a autant de cafés que lyon.` 6. `ma sœur travaille plus souvent chez elle.` 7. `mon frère parle plus rapidement qu'elle.` 8. `je préfère son quartier au leur.` self-check: include possessives, demonstratives, and three comparison patterns. mark it wrong if an adjective does not agree, if a noun comparison lacks `de`, or if a pronoun such as `le sien` does not match the noun it replaces. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **possessives.** possessive adjectives agree with the possessed noun, not the owner. example: `marie cherche son livre et sa clé.` 2. **demonstratives.** use `ce`, `cet`, `cette`, and `ces` before nouns; use `celui`, `celle`, `ceux`, and `celles` when the noun is replaced. example: `cette chaise est confortable, mais celle-ci est meilleure.` 3. **comparisons.** use **plus/moins/aussi + adjective or adverb + que**, **plus/moins/autant de + noun**, and **verb + plus/moins/autant**. example: `je lis autant que toi.` 4. **adverbs.** many adverbs come from the feminine adjective plus the ending. example: `elle parle clairement.` if your example uses the rule but has agreement wrong, the answer is not correct yet. agreement is part of the structure. "},{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 10 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-answer-key\">Chapter 10 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon amie</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet arbre</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces maisons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est plus grande que Luc.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons autant de temps.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Possessives:</strong> Possessive adjectives agree with the possessed noun, not the owner. Use mon before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><strong>Demonstratives:</strong> Use ce, cet, cette, and ces before nouns. Demonstrative pronouns replace nouns: celui, celle, ceux, celles.</li>\n<li><strong>Comparisons:</strong> Use plus...que, moins...que, and aussi...que. Compare nouns with plus de/moins de/autant de, and verbs with plus/moins/autant.</li>\n<li><strong>Adverbs:</strong> Many adverbs derive from the feminine adjective plus -ment. Their placement depends on the tense and length.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 10 answer key ## section a 1. `mon amie` 2. `cet arbre` 3. `ces maisons` 4. `marie est plus grande que luc.` 5. `nous avons autant de temps.` 6. `heureusement` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **possessives:** possessive adjectives agree with the possessed noun, not the owner. use mon before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel sound. - **demonstratives:** use ce, cet, cette, and ces before nouns. demonstrative pronouns replace nouns: celui, celle, ceux, celles. - **comparisons:** use plus...que, moins...que, and aussi...que. compare nouns with plus de/moins de/autant de, and verbs with plus/moins/autant. - **adverbs:** many adverbs derive from the feminine adjective plus -ment. their placement depends on the tense and length. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 10 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 10 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Possessives</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Demonstratives</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Comparisons</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Adverbs</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 10 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **possessives**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **demonstratives** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **comparisons**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **adverbs**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":11,"slug":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative","title":"Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative","documents":[{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/README.md","title":"Chapter 11: Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-stressed-pronouns-and-the-imperative\">Chapter 11: Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use expanded affirmative and negative expressions.</li>\n<li>Choose stressed pronouns.</li>\n<li>Form affirmative and negative commands.</li>\n<li>Place object pronouns with commands.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Expanded negatives</li>\n<li>Stressed pronouns</li>\n<li>Imperative forms</li>\n<li>Commands with pronouns</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 11: affirmatives, negatives, stressed pronouns, and the imperative this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - use expanded affirmative and negative expressions. - choose stressed pronouns. - form affirmative and negative commands. - place object pronouns with commands. ## lesson sequence 1. expanded negatives 2. stressed pronouns 3. imperative forms 4. commands with pronouns ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 11 Lessons: Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-lessons-affirmatives-negatives-stressed-pronouns-and-the-imperative\">Chapter 11 Lessons: Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative</h1>\n<p>This chapter makes your French more interactive. You will learn how to agree, disagree, contradict a negative question, add emphasis with stressed pronouns, and give clear commands such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne pars pas</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>answer positively and negatively with more variety than simple <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">non</code>;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> to contradict a negative question or statement;</li>\n<li>use stressed pronouns after prepositions, for emphasis, in comparisons, and as short answers;</li>\n<li>form the imperative with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>;</li>\n<li>recognize the special imperative forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>;</li>\n<li>apply the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> rule in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command, including the exception before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>;</li>\n<li>form negative commands correctly;</li>\n<li>place object pronouns correctly in affirmative and negative commands.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>conjugate common present-tense verbs with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>;</li>\n<li>form ordinary negation with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>;</li>\n<li>recognize direct and indirect object pronouns from Chapter 9, because command word order builds directly on that chapter;</li>\n<li>understand comparison phrases such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus que moi</code>, because stressed pronouns appear there too.</li>\n<p>If any of those still feel weak, review them before going on.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test the prerequisite skills actively. Answer these five, then read the rule after each answer.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Give the present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu parles</code>. The subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> takes a silent final written <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> in the present tense.</li>\n<li>Now give the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle !</code> Regular -er verbs drop that final written <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> in the ordinary <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command.</li>\n<li>Make <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois Paul</code> negative. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vois pas Paul.</code> The negative frame surrounds the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie</code> in <strong>with Marie</strong>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec elle</code>. After a preposition, French uses a stressed pronoun, not a subject pronoun.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne le livre !</code> to a command with a pronoun. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le !</code> In an affirmative command, the object pronoun follows the verb and is linked with a hyphen.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review ordinary negation, object pronouns, and present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> forms first. This chapter turns those pieces into commands, where word order matters.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Read every example aloud with energy. Commands are easier to remember when you hear them as complete speech chunks: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attends !</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne bougez pas !</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-saying-yes-no-and-stronger-negatives\">Unit 1: Saying yes, no, and stronger negatives</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French has several ways to agree, disagree, or answer a negative question. Learn them as conversation tools, not as isolated vocabulary words.</p>\n<h3 id=\"core-answer-words\">Core answer words</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>French answer</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>simple yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code></td>\n<td>agreeing with a positive statement or question</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>simple no</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">non</code></td>\n<td>rejecting a statement or question</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>yes to contradict a negative</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code></td>\n<td>correcting a negative question or statement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>me too</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi aussi</code></td>\n<td>agreeing with a positive statement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>me neither</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi non plus</code></td>\n<td>agreeing with a negative statement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>not at all</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas du tout</code></td>\n<td>stronger no</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"si-the-important-contradiction-word\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>: the important contradiction word</h3>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> when the other person has used a negative idea and you want to contradict it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'aimes pas ça ? — Si, j'aime ça.</code> — You don't like that? — Yes, I do like it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous n'êtes pas prêts ? — Si, nous sommes prêts.</code> — You aren't ready? — Yes, we are ready.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il ne vient jamais ? — Si, il vient le samedi.</code> — He never comes? — Yes, he does come on Saturdays.</li>\n<p>If you answer with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> here, it can sound confusing.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"echo-agreement-and-disagreement\">Echo agreement and disagreement</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aime ce café. — Moi aussi.</code> — I like this café. — Me too.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne sortons pas ce soir. — Moi non plus.</code> — We are not going out tonight. — Me neither.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'a plus faim. — Eux non plus.</code> — She isn't hungry anymore. — Neither are they.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"other-useful-negative-patterns\">Other useful negative patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>never</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...jamais</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne mange jamais ici.</code></td>\n<td>I never eat here.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no longer / not anymore</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...plus</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il ne travaille plus ici.</code></td>\n<td>He no longer works here.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nothing</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...rien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'avons rien.</code></td>\n<td>We have nothing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nobody</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...personne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vois personne.</code></td>\n<td>I see nobody.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>not yet</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas encore</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'est pas encore là.</code></td>\n<td>She isn't here yet.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.</code> — As for me, I don't see anyone in the garden.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le dimanche.</code> — As for them, they never work on Sundays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'as pas fini ? — Si, j'ai fini.</code> — Haven't you finished? — Yes, I have.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends un dessert. — Moi aussi.</code> — I'm having dessert. — Me too.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'avons plus de pain. — Nous non plus.</code> — We don't have bread anymore. — Neither do we.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In fast speech, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi aussi</code> often flows as one chunk, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> is short but important. Train yourself to hear the logic of the answer, not just the single word.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> to contradict a negative question. French wants <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu ne viens pas ? — Si, je viens.</code></li>\n<li>confusing: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu ne viens pas ? — Oui...</code></li>\n<p>Also remember <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi non plus</code> matches a negative statement, not a positive one.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Answer these with one or two words: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu aimes le chocolat ?</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'aimes pas le chocolat ?</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous partons demain.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne partons pas demain.</code></li>\n<li>Rewrite one positive sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi aussi</code> and one negative sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi non plus</code>.</li>\n<li>Write four short answers using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas du tout</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jamais</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Short answers:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu aimes le chocolat ?</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui.</code> — an ordinary positive question takes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> for yes.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'aimes pas le chocolat ?</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si.</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> to contradict a negative question.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous partons demain.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi aussi.</code> — agree with a positive statement.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne partons pas demain.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi non plus.</code> — agree with a negative statement.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Rewrites:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous partons demain. Moi aussi.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi aussi</code> means <strong>me too</strong> for a positive idea.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne partons pas demain. Moi non plus.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi non plus</code> means <strong>me neither</strong> for a negative idea.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Four short answers:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si, j'aime le chocolat.</code> — contradicts a negative question.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pas du tout.</code> — a strong negative answer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Jamais.</code> — short answer meaning never.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Plus.</code> — short answer meaning no longer / not anymore.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The main trap is answering a negative question with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code>. If you mean <strong>yes, actually I do</strong>, French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-stressed-pronouns\">Unit 2: Stressed pronouns</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Stressed pronouns are the stronger forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>. They do not replace subject pronouns in ordinary sentences. Instead, they appear in special positions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"full-chart\">Full chart</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject pronoun</th>\n<th>Stressed pronoun</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code></td>\n<td>me / I</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code></td>\n<td>you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code></td>\n<td>him / he</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n<td>her / she</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td>us / we</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td>you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux</code></td>\n<td>them / they</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code></td>\n<td>them / they</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"main-uses\">Main uses</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>after a preposition</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec moi</code></td>\n<td>with me</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>for emphasis</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lui, il comprend vite.</code></td>\n<td>He, he understands quickly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in comparisons</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus que toi</code></td>\n<td>more than you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>alone as a short answer</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui veut venir ? — Moi.</code></td>\n<td>Who wants to come? — Me.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in a compound subject</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Toi et moi, nous commençons.</code></td>\n<td>You and I are starting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'est</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est elle.</code></td>\n<td>It's her.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le professeur parle avec nous après le cours.</code> — The teacher speaks with us after class.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je préfère travailler tôt.</code> — As for me, I prefer to work early.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle court plus vite que lui.</code> — She runs faster than he does.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui apporte les boissons ? — Eux.</code> — Who is bringing the drinks? — They are.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lui et moi, nous partageons le même bureau.</code> — He and I share the same desk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Eux</code> is short and rounded. Do not pronounce it like English “yooks.” Also notice that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> keep their stressed forms after prepositions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour elle</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use ordinary subject pronouns after prepositions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour eux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez elle</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*avec je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*pour ils</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*chez elle</code> is correct only because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> is both forms</li>\n<p>Also do not replace every subject pronoun with a stressed pronoun. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi travaille</code> is incorrect; say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je travaille</code> if you want emphasis.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace the noun phrase with a stressed pronoun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec Marie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour Paul et moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez les voisins</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sans toi</code>.</li>\n<li>Build one example for each use in the table above.</li>\n<li>Write three short comparisons using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que lui</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Replacements:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec Marie</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec elle</code> — after a preposition, use the stressed form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour Paul et moi</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pour nous</code> — Paul and I together become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez les voisins</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chez eux</code> — masculine or mixed plural people become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sans toi</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sans toi</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code> is already the stressed form.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Uses from the table:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>After a preposition: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille avec lui.</code></li>\n<li>Emphasis: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je préfère rester ici.</code></li>\n<li>Comparison: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle plus vite que nous.</code></li>\n<li>Short answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qui vient ? — Moi.</code></li>\n<li>Compound subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Toi et moi, nous commençons.</code></li>\n<li>After <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">c'est</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est elle.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Comparisons:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles plus que moi.</code> — stressed pronoun after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle chante moins fort que lui.</code> — use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code>, after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils arrivent plus tôt que nous.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> is both the subject and stressed form.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not use subject pronouns after prepositions or after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> in comparisons. Say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec moi</code>, not an English-style subject form.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-forming-the-imperative\">Unit 3: Forming the imperative</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The imperative gives commands, advice, invitations, and instructions. French uses only the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> forms, and the subject pronoun is normally omitted.</p>\n<h3 id=\"regular-imperative-forms\">Regular imperative forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> command</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrez</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-special-er-rule\">The special <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> rule</h3>\n<p>In the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> form of regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verbs, the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> disappears.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle plus lentement.</code> — Speak more slowly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde le tableau.</code> — Look at the board.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ferme la porte.</code> — Close the door.</li>\n<p>But before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> reappears for sound flow.</p>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Penses-y.</code> — Think about it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parles-en à ton frère.</code> — Talk to your brother about it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vas-y.</code> — Go ahead / Go there.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"irregular-imperative-forms\">Irregular imperative forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> command</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aie</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sache</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuillons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuillez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"uses-of-the-imperative\">Uses of the imperative</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attends ici.</code> — Wait here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Finissons ce travail maintenant.</code> — Let's finish this work now.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Soyez prudents.</code> — Be careful.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Veuillez entrer.</code> — Please come in.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Sachons écouter.</code> — Let us know how to listen / Let's learn to listen.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Allons-y</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vas-y</code> are common sound chunks. Practice them aloud until they come out smoothly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not include the subject pronoun in an ordinary command.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Fermez la fenêtre.</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Vous fermez la fenêtre.</code> when you mean “Close the window.”</li>\n<p>Also remember that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code> is correct for the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> form of a regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> verb, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*parles</code>, unless <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> follows.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Turn these infinitives into three commands each: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écouter</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code>.</li>\n<li>Write one command with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, and one polite instruction with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>.</li>\n<li>Create two <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> commands: one regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> form without <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Commands:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écouter</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoute !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoutons !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoutez !</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Choisis !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Choisissons !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Choisissez !</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attends !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attendons !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attendez !</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>Irregular and polite commands:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Sois patient !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> has an irregular command form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Aie confiance !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> also has an irregular command form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Veuillez entrer !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuillez</code> is the polite command form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Two <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> commands:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ferme la porte !</code> — regular -er <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command drops the final letter found in the present-tense spelling.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vas-y !</code> — before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command keeps the final sound-linking letter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The trap is writing the subject pronoun. A command is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoute !</code>, not a normal statement with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-negative-commands\">Unit 4: Negative commands</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A negative command surrounds the imperative verb with the negative frame, just as in an ordinary sentence.</p>\n<h3 id=\"basic-pattern\">Basic pattern</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>do not ...</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + imperative + pas</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne parle pas.</code></td>\n<td>Don't speak.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>never ...</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + imperative + jamais</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'arrivez jamais en retard.</code></td>\n<td>Never arrive late.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no longer ...</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + imperative + plus</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'en demandez plus.</code></td>\n<td>Don't ask for any more.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>say nothing</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + imperative + rien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne dites rien.</code></td>\n<td>Don't say anything.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>wait for nobody</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + imperative + personne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'attends personne ici.</code></td>\n<td>Wait for nobody here.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laissez rien sur la table !</code> — Don't leave anything on the table!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne bouge pas.</code> — Don't move.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'oublions pas les clés.</code> — Let's not forget the keys.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne parlez plus pendant l'exercice.</code> — Don't talk anymore during the exercise.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'écrivez jamais votre mot de passe ici.</code> — Never write your password here.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Before a vowel sound, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'attendez pas</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'oublie rien</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'écoutez plus</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not switch to the infinitive after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code>. French still uses the imperative form.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne prends pas ça.</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Ne prendre pas ça.</code></li>\n<p>Also place the negative words around the verb, not after the whole sentence.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Turn these into negative commands: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle plus vite.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attendez ici.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Finissons ce chapitre.</code></li>\n<li>Write one negative command with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jamais</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rien</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus</code>.</li>\n<li>Say each one aloud with strong intonation.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Negative commands:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle plus vite.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne parle pas plus vite.</code> — <strong>ne ... pas</strong> wraps the command verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attendez ici.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'attendez pas ici.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> shortens to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before the vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Finissons ce chapitre.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne finissons pas ce chapitre.</code> — the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> command form stays the same inside the negative frame.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>More negative commands:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne travaille jamais le dimanche !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jamais</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laisse rien ici !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rien</code> comes after the verb in this command.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne parle plus !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus</code> means not anymore.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>When you say them aloud, make the negative frame clear. In writing, do not drop <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> at this stage.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-commands-with-object-pronouns\">Unit 5: Commands with object pronouns</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Pronouns move in commands. In an affirmative command, they come <strong>after</strong> the verb and are linked with hyphens. In a negative command, they return <strong>before</strong> the verb in the normal order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"affirmative-commands-pronouns-after-the-verb\">Affirmative commands: pronouns after the verb</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code> after the verb.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Order after an affirmative command</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>verb + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-le / -la / -les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>verb + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-moi / -toi / -lui / -nous / -vous / -leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-moi ce livre !</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>verb + direct object + indirect object</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>verb + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-y</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-en</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vas-y !</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parles-en !</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code> — Look at it / him!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.</code> — Give me the hammer and then bring it to him afterward.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Envoyez-nous les photos !</code> — Send us the photos!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Prenez-en un.</code> — Take one of them / Take one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"negative-commands-pronouns-before-the-verb\">Negative commands: pronouns before the verb</h3>\n<p>In a negative command, pronouns go back before the verb, in the familiar sentence order.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Order before a negative command</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + me / te / nous / vous + verb + pas</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me parle pas.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + le / la / les + verb + pas</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne les ouvre pas.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + me / te / nous / vous + le / la / les + verb + pas</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me le donne pas.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne + lui / leur / y / en + verb + pas</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne leur écrivez pas.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'y va pas.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'en prends pas.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le regardez pas !</code> — Don't look at it / him!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me le donne pas !</code> — Don't give it to me!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne leur téléphonez pas maintenant.</code> — Don't call them now.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'y allez pas seuls.</code> — Don't go there alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"quick-contrast-table\">Quick contrast table</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Affirmative command</th>\n<th>Negative command</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le livre.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me donne pas le livre.</code></td>\n<td>Give me the book. / Don't give me the book.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regardez-le.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le regardez pas.</code></td>\n<td>Look at it. / Don't look at it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parles-en à ta sœur.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'en parle pas à ta sœur.</code></td>\n<td>Talk to your sister about it. / Don't talk to your sister about it.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Hyphens matter in writing because they show the command structure clearly: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donne-moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regardez-le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apporte-le-lui</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not keep pronouns before the verb in affirmative commands.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Me le donne !</code></li>\n<p>And in negative commands, switch back to normal order.</p>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me le donne pas !</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Ne donne-le-moi pas !</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Change these to affirmative commands with pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu donnes le stylo à moi.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous regardez le film.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu parles de ce problème.</code></li>\n<li>Turn each of those into a negative command.</li>\n<li>Write two new pairs of your own: one affirmative command and its negative version.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Affirmative commands:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu donnes le stylo à moi.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code> — in an affirmative command, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens; direct object <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> comes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous regardez le film.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regardez-le !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le film</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> after the command.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu parles de ce problème.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parles-en !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> replaces the <strong>de + thing</strong> phrase, and the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command keeps the linking letter before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Negative commands:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me le donne pas !</code> — in a negative command, pronouns return before the verb in normal order.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le regardez pas !</code> — <strong>ne ... pas</strong> surrounds the pronoun plus verb chunk.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">N'en parle pas !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> stays before the verb; the command form is now <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Your own pairs can vary. Models:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Aide-moi !</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne m'aide pas !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> after the affirmative command becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> before the negative command.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoutez-les !</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne les écoutez pas !</code> — direct object pronoun after the verb in the affirmative, before the verb in the negative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The big rule is position: after the verb in affirmative commands, before the verb in negative commands.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine the chapter's main structures in realistic French. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.</code> — As for me, I don't see anyone in the garden.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laissez rien sur la table !</code> — Don't leave anything on the table!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.</code> — Give me the hammer and then bring it to him afterward.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le dimanche.</code> — As for them, they never work on Sundays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'es pas prêt ? — Si, attends-moi deux minutes.</code> — You're not ready? — Yes, I am; wait for me two minutes.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec nous, soyez patients et ne nous coupez pas la parole.</code> — With us, be patient and don't interrupt us.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Use the printed sentences as a memory check. After writing them from memory, look for these specific traps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Negative frames.</strong> Sentence 1 uses <strong>ne ... personne</strong>, sentence 2 uses <strong>ne ... rien</strong>, sentence 4 uses <strong>ne ... jamais</strong>, and sentence 6 uses <strong>ne ... pas</strong> around the command <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">coupez</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stressed pronouns.</strong> Sentence 1 starts with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi</code>, sentence 4 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Eux</code>, and sentence 6 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec nous</code>. These are not ordinary subject pronouns; they are stressed forms used for emphasis or after a preposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Affirmative command pronouns.</strong> In sentence 3, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apporte-le-lui</code> put pronouns after the command with hyphens. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> comes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Negative command pronouns.</strong> In sentence 6, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne nous coupez pas</code> puts <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> before the verb because the command is negative.</li>\n<li><strong>The answer word <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>.</strong> Sentence 5 uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si</code> because it contradicts the negative question <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'es pas prêt ?</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Accents and punctuation.</strong> Keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prêt</code>, the hyphens in command-pronoun groups, and the space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">!</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If your meaning is correct but your pronoun is on the wrong side of the command verb, count the sentence wrong. Pronoun placement is the main skill here.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence above:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle the stressed pronoun or command form;</li>\n<li>underline the negative frame if there is one;</li>\n<li>identify where each object pronoun stands: before the verb or after it.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the person or number;</li>\n<li>change one important detail such as the place, object, or time expression.</li>\n<p>Make every necessary change to pronouns, agreement, and command form.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one stressed pronoun after a preposition;</li>\n<li>one answer with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi aussi</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi non plus</code>;</li>\n<li>one affirmative command;</li>\n<li>one negative command with a pronoun.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Notice and mark:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> is a stressed pronoun for emphasis; the negative frame is <strong>ne ... personne</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laissez rien sur la table !</code> — negative command; the negative frame is <strong>ne ... rien</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.</code> — command forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apporte</code>; object pronouns stand after the affirmative commands. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apporte-le-lui</code>, direct <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> comes before indirect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le dimanche.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux</code> is a stressed pronoun for emphasis; the negative frame is <strong>ne ... jamais</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'es pas prêt ? — Si, attends-moi deux minutes.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> contradicts a negative question; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> stands after the affirmative command <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec nous, soyez patients et ne nous coupez pas la parole.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> is stressed after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyez</code> is a command; in the negative command, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> stands before the verb.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence — sample answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sentence 1, person change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lui, il ne voit personne dans le jardin.</code> — stressed pronoun and subject both change.</li>\n<li>Sentence 1, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je ne vois personne dans la rue.</code> — same negative frame, new place.</li>\n<li>Sentence 2, person change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laissons rien sur la table !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> command form.</li>\n<li>Sentence 2, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laissez rien dans le sac !</code> — same <strong>ne ... rien</strong> frame.</li>\n<li>Sentence 3, person change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-moi le marteau et apportez-le-lui ensuite.</code> — both commands become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> forms.</li>\n<li>Sentence 3, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le livre et apporte-le-lui ensuite.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> still works for a masculine singular object.</li>\n<li>Sentence 4, person change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle, elle ne travaille jamais le dimanche.</code> — stressed pronoun and verb change to singular.</li>\n<li>Sentence 4, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le soir.</code> — same negative frame, new time expression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Make it personal — sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je révise avec elle après le cours.</code> — stressed pronoun after a preposition.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'es pas fatigué ? — Si, un peu.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> contradicts a negative question.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoute cette phrase !</code> — affirmative command with no subject pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me téléphone pas ce soir !</code> — negative command with the object pronoun before the verb.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-before-leaving-the-apartment\">Mini-dialogue: before leaving the apartment</h2>\n<p><strong>Nadia :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'as pas mes clés ?</code> — You don't have my keys? <strong>Julien :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si, elles sont avec moi.</code> — Yes, I do; they're with me. <strong>Nadia :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Alors, donne-les-moi, s'il te plaît.</code> — Then give them to me, please. <strong>Julien :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tiens. Et prends aussi ton chargeur.</code> — Here. And take your charger too. <strong>Nadia :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je suis prête, mais eux, ils ne sont pas encore en bas.</code> — I'm ready, but they aren't downstairs yet. <strong>Julien :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne les attends pas trop longtemps.</code> — Don't wait for them too long. <strong>Nadia :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">D'accord. Toi et moi, on part maintenant ?</code> — Okay. You and I, are we leaving now? <strong>Julien :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, allons-y, mais n'oublie rien cette fois.</code> — Yes, let's go, but don't forget anything this time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the use of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> and explain why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> would be weaker or less precise here.</li>\n<li>Identify three stressed pronouns and explain their function.</li>\n<li>Find one affirmative command with a pronoun and one negative command.</li>\n<li>Why does Nadia say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donne-les-moi</code> and not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*me les donne</code>?</li>\n<li>Rewrite line 8 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si</code> appears in Julien's answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si, elles sont avec moi.</code> Nadia's question is negative, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'as pas mes clés ?</code>, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clearly means <strong>yes, I do</strong>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui</code> would be less precise because it does not specifically contradict the negative wording.</li>\n<li>Three stressed pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec moi</code> is after a preposition; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je suis prête</code> marks emphasis; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux, ils ne sont pas encore en bas</code> also marks emphasis. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Toi et moi</code> is another stressed-pronoun use in a compound subject.</li>\n<li>Affirmative command with a pronoun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donne-les-moi</code>. Negative command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne les attends pas trop longtemps.</code> The last line also has the negative command <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'oublie rien</code>.</li>\n<li>Nadia says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donne-les-moi</code> because affirmative command pronouns follow the verb with hyphens. The direct object <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> comes before the indirect object <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>; before an affirmative command, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> after the verb.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, allons-y, mais n'oubliez rien cette fois.</code> The group invitation <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons-y</code> can stay the same; the direct command to the other person becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'oubliez</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The dialogue contrasts the two command orders: after the verb in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donne-les-moi</code>, before the verb in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne les attends pas</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-classroom-instructions-on-the-first-day\">Connected reading: classroom instructions on the first day</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le premier jour du cours, notre professeure parle très clairement. D'abord, elle dit : « Avec moi, soyez honnêtes et posez des questions. » Puis elle ajoute : « Si vous ne comprenez pas, dites-le tout de suite ; n'attendez pas la fin. » Moi aussi, j'aime cette règle, parce que les étudiants timides ne parlent pas encore beaucoup. La professeure leur dit souvent : « Vous et moi, nous sommes ici pour apprendre ; ne vous comparez pas aux autres. » Personne ne rit, et personne ne se sent exclu.</code> — On the first day of class, our teacher speaks very clearly. First, she says, “With me, be honest and ask questions.” Then she adds, “If you don't understand, say it right away; don't wait until the end.” I like that rule too, because shy students do not talk much yet. The teacher often tells them, “You and I are here to learn; don't compare yourselves to others.” Nobody laughs, and nobody feels excluded.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find every imperative form in the paragraph and identify whether it is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n<li>Find one stressed pronoun after a preposition, one in a compound subject, and one echo-answer expression.</li>\n<li>Identify the negative expressions in the paragraph.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the teacher's instructions so she is talking to one close friend instead of a class.</li>\n<li>Write three true classroom or work rules of your own using the model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Imperative forms:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyez</code> is a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">posez</code> is a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dites-le</code> is a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command with an object pronoun after it; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'attendez pas</code> is a negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne vous comparez pas</code> is also a negative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command.</li>\n<li><strong>Stressed pronouns:</strong> after a preposition, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec moi</code>; in a compound subject, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous et moi</code>; echo-answer expression, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi aussi</code>. Each one uses a stressed form because ordinary subject pronouns do not fit those positions.</li>\n<li><strong>Negative expressions:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous ne comprenez pas</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'attendez pas</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne parlent pas encore</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne vous comparez pas</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Personne ne rit</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">personne ne se sent exclu</code>. Notice that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">personne</code> can stand before the verb and still pairs with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code>.</li>\n<li>To one close friend: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec moi, sois honnête et pose des questions. Si tu ne comprends pas, dis-le tout de suite ; n'attends pas la fin. Toi et moi, nous sommes ici pour apprendre ; ne te compare pas aux autres.</code> The -er commands <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pose</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">compare</code> drop the final written <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. Model rules: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec moi, sois patient.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si tu ne comprends pas, demande-le tout de suite.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laisse rien sur le bureau.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you leave a subject pronoun inside a command or if a negative command keeps the pronoun after the verb.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> to contradict a negative question.</li>\n<li>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi aussi</code> after a negative sentence; French wants <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi non plus</code>.</li>\n<li>Using subject pronouns after prepositions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec moi</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*avec je</code>.</li>\n<li>Leaving the subject pronoun inside a command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Fermez la porte</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Vous fermez la porte</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> commands drop final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*parles</code>, unless <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> follows.</li>\n<li>Leaving object pronouns before the verb in affirmative commands.</li>\n<li>Keeping affirmative command order inside a negative command.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"yes-no-and-stronger-answer-patterns\">Yes/no and stronger answer patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">non</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>yes, in contradiction to a negative</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>me too</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi aussi</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>me neither</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi non plus</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>not at all</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas du tout</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-negative-frames\">Common negative frames</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>not</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>never</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...jamais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no longer</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...plus</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nothing</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...rien</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nobody</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...personne</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>not yet</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas encore</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"stressed-pronouns\">Stressed pronouns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Stressed</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-imperative-forms\">Core imperative forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finis</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aie</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sache</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuillons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuillez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">va</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"pronouns-in-commands\">Pronouns in commands</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Position</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>affirmative command</td>\n<td>after the verb, with hyphens: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donne-le-moi</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negative command</td>\n<td>before the verb, normal order: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne me le donne pas</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> in affirmative command</td>\n<td>become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n<td>keep or add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vas-y</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parles-en</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph or mini-scene of 8-12 sentences in which one person gives instructions and another person reacts. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one answer with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>;</li>\n<li>one use of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi aussi</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi non plus</code>;</li>\n<li>at least four stressed pronouns in different functions;</li>\n<li>three affirmative commands;</li>\n<li>three negative commands;</li>\n<li>at least two commands with object pronouns.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every stressed pronoun;</li>\n<li>underline each negative frame;</li>\n<li>box every imperative verb;</li>\n<li>check whether your pronouns are after the verb in affirmative commands and before the verb in negative commands;</li>\n<li>read the whole passage aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is a model mini-scene that satisfies every bullet:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Camille dit : « Avec moi, soyez calmes et ouvrez vos cahiers ! » Elle ajoute : « Lisez cette consigne, puis donnez-la-moi ! » Hugo répond : « Je ne comprends pas encore. » Camille demande : « Tu n'as pas ton stylo ? » Hugo dit : « Si, il est avec elle. » Camille dit : « Alors, prends-le et ne le perds pas ! » Elle dit au groupe : « Ne parlez jamais pendant l'écoute et ne me coupez pas la parole ! » Hugo répond : « Moi aussi, je veux réussir. » Camille conclut : « Toi et eux, travaillez ensemble ; pour nous, c'est important. »</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si</code> contradicts a negative question; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi aussi</code> is the echo answer; stressed pronouns include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>; affirmative commands include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donnez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travaillez</code>; negative commands include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne le perds pas</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne parlez jamais</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne me coupez pas</code>; command-pronoun examples include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donnez-la-moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends-le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne le perds pas</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne me coupez pas</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if any of these appear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> where the answer contradicts a negative question;</li>\n<li>a subject pronoun inside a command, such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous ouvrez</code> when you mean <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrez</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> after an affirmative command instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>;</li>\n<li>an affirmative command pronoun before the verb;</li>\n<li>a negative command pronoun after the verb.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 11 lessons: affirmatives, negatives, stressed pronouns, and the imperative this chapter makes your french more interactive. you will learn how to agree, disagree, contradict a negative question, add emphasis with stressed pronouns, and give clear commands such as `attends`, `ne pars pas`, and `donne-le-moi`. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. answer positively and negatively with more variety than simple `oui` and `non`; 2. use `si` to contradict a negative question or statement; 3. use stressed pronouns after prepositions, for emphasis, in comparisons, and as short answers; 4. form the imperative with `tu`, `nous`, and `vous`; 5. recognize the special imperative forms of `être`, `avoir`, `savoir`, and `vouloir`; 6. apply the `-er` rule in the `tu` command, including the exception before `y` and `en`; 7. form negative commands correctly; 8. place object pronouns correctly in affirmative and negative commands. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - conjugate common present-tense verbs with `tu`, `nous`, and `vous`; - form ordinary negation with `ne...pas`; - recognize direct and indirect object pronouns from chapter 9, because command word order builds directly on that chapter; - understand comparison phrases such as `plus que moi`, because stressed pronouns appear there too. if any of those still feel weak, review them before going on. test the prerequisite skills actively. answer these five, then read the rule after each answer. 1. give the present-tense `tu` form of `parler`. — `tu parles`. the subject `tu` takes a silent final written `s` in the present tense. 2. now give the `tu` command for `parler`. — `parle !` regular -er verbs drop that final written `s` in the ordinary `tu` command. 3. make `je vois paul` negative. — `je ne vois pas paul.` the negative frame surrounds the conjugated verb. 4. replace `marie` in **with marie**. — `avec elle`. after a preposition, french uses a stressed pronoun, not a subject pronoun. 5. change `donne le livre !` to a command with a pronoun. — `donne-le !` in an affirmative command, the object pronoun follows the verb and is linked with a hyphen. if you missed two or more, review ordinary negation, object pronouns, and present-tense `tu`, `nous`, and `vous` forms first. this chapter turns those pieces into commands, where word order matters. ## how to study this chapter read every example aloud with energy. commands are easier to remember when you hear them as complete speech chunks: `attends !`, `ne bougez pas !`, `donne-le-moi !`. ## unit 1: saying yes, no, and stronger negatives ### core idea french has several ways to agree, disagree, or answer a negative question. learn them as conversation tools, not as isolated vocabulary words. ### core answer words | situation | french answer | use | |---|---|---| | simple yes | `oui` | agreeing with a positive statement or question | | simple no | `non` | rejecting a statement or question | | yes to contradict a negative | `si` | correcting a negative question or statement | | me too | `moi aussi` | agreeing with a positive statement | | me neither | `moi non plus` | agreeing with a negative statement | | not at all | `pas du tout` | stronger no | ### `si`: the important contradiction word use `si` when the other person has used a negative idea and you want to contradict it. - `tu n'aimes pas ça ? — si, j'aime ça.` — you don't like that? — yes, i do like it. - `vous n'êtes pas prêts ? — si, nous sommes prêts.` — you aren't ready? — yes, we are ready. - `il ne vient jamais ? — si, il vient le samedi.` — he never comes? — yes, he does come on saturdays. if you answer with `oui` here, it can sound confusing. ### echo agreement and disagreement - `j'aime ce café. — moi aussi.` — i like this café. — me too. - `nous ne sortons pas ce soir. — moi non plus.` — we are not going out tonight. — me neither. - `elle n'a plus faim. — eux non plus.` — she isn't hungry anymore. — neither are they. ### other useful negative patterns | meaning | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | never | `ne...jamais` | `je ne mange jamais ici.` | i never eat here. | | no longer / not anymore | `ne...plus` | `il ne travaille plus ici.` | he no longer works here. | | nothing | `ne...rien` | `nous n'avons rien.` | we have nothing. | | nobody | `ne...personne` | `je ne vois personne.` | i see nobody. | | not yet | `ne...pas encore` | `elle n'est pas encore là.` | she isn't here yet. | ### examples in context - `moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.` — as for me, i don't see anyone in the garden. - `eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le dimanche.` — as for them, they never work on sundays. - `tu n'as pas fini ? — si, j'ai fini.` — haven't you finished? — yes, i have. - `je prends un dessert. — moi aussi.` — i'm having dessert. — me too. - `nous n'avons plus de pain. — nous non plus.` — we don't have bread anymore. — neither do we. ### pronunciation note in fast speech, `moi aussi` often flows as one chunk, and `si` is short but important. train yourself to hear the logic of the answer, not just the single word. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use `oui` to contradict a negative question. french wants `si`. - correct: `tu ne viens pas ? — si, je viens.` - confusing: `tu ne viens pas ? — oui...` also remember `moi non plus` matches a negative statement, not a positive one. ### sentence-building practice 1. answer these with one or two words: `tu aimes le chocolat ?`, `tu n'aimes pas le chocolat ?`, `nous partons demain.`, `nous ne partons pas demain.` 2. rewrite one positive sentence with `moi aussi` and one negative sentence with `moi non plus`. 3. write four short answers using `si`, `pas du tout`, `jamais`, and `plus`. 1. short answers: - `tu aimes le chocolat ?` -> `oui.` — an ordinary positive question takes `oui` for yes. - `tu n'aimes pas le chocolat ?` -> `si.` — use `si` to contradict a negative question. - `nous partons demain.` -> `moi aussi.` — agree with a positive statement. - `nous ne partons pas demain.` -> `moi non plus.` — agree with a negative statement. 2. rewrites: - `nous partons demain. moi aussi.` — `moi aussi` means **me too** for a positive idea. - `nous ne partons pas demain. moi non plus.` — `moi non plus` means **me neither** for a negative idea. 3. four short answers: - `si, j'aime le chocolat.` — contradicts a negative question. - `pas du tout.` — a strong negative answer. - `jamais.` — short answer meaning never. - `plus.` — short answer meaning no longer / not anymore. the main trap is answering a negative question with `oui`. if you mean **yes, actually i do**, french uses `si`. ## unit 2: stressed pronouns ### core idea stressed pronouns are the stronger forms: `moi`, `toi`, `lui`, `elle`, `nous`, `vous`, `eux`, `elles`. they do not replace subject pronouns in ordinary sentences. instead, they appear in special positions. ### full chart | subject pronoun | stressed pronoun | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `moi` | me / i | | `tu` | `toi` | you | | `il` | `lui` | him / he | | `elle` | `elle` | her / she | | `nous` | `nous` | us / we | | `vous` | `vous` | you | | `ils` | `eux` | them / they | | `elles` | `elles` | them / they | ### main uses | use | example | english | |---|---|---| | after a preposition | `avec moi` | with me | | for emphasis | `lui, il comprend vite.` | he, he understands quickly. | | in comparisons | `plus que toi` | more than you | | alone as a short answer | `qui veut venir ? — moi.` | who wants to come? — me. | | in a compound subject | `toi et moi, nous commençons.` | you and i are starting. | | after `c'est` | `c'est elle.` | it's her. | ### examples in context - `le professeur parle avec nous après le cours.` — the teacher speaks with us after class. - `moi, je préfère travailler tôt.` — as for me, i prefer to work early. - `elle court plus vite que lui.` — she runs faster than he does. - `qui apporte les boissons ? — eux.` — who is bringing the drinks? — they are. - `lui et moi, nous partageons le même bureau.` — he and i share the same desk. ### pronunciation note `eux` is short and rounded. do not pronounce it like english “yooks.” also notice that `lui` and `elle` keep their stressed forms after prepositions: `avec lui`, `pour elle`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use ordinary subject pronouns after prepositions. - correct: `avec moi`, `pour eux`, `chez elle` - incorrect: `*avec je`, `*pour ils`, `*chez elle` is correct only because `elle` is both forms also do not replace every subject pronoun with a stressed pronoun. `moi travaille` is incorrect; say `moi, je travaille` if you want emphasis. ### sentence-building practice 1. replace the noun phrase with a stressed pronoun: `avec marie`, `pour paul et moi`, `chez les voisins`, `sans toi`. 2. build one example for each use in the table above. 3. write three short comparisons using `que moi`, `que lui`, or `que nous`. 1. replacements: - `avec marie` -> `avec elle` — after a preposition, use the stressed form. - `pour paul et moi` -> `pour nous` — paul and i together become `nous`. - `chez les voisins` -> `chez eux` — masculine or mixed plural people become `eux`. - `sans toi` -> `sans toi` — `toi` is already the stressed form. 2. uses from the table: - after a preposition: `je travaille avec lui.` - emphasis: `moi, je préfère rester ici.` - comparison: `elle parle plus vite que nous.` - short answer: `qui vient ? — moi.` - compound subject: `toi et moi, nous commençons.` - after `c'est`: `c'est elle.` 3. comparisons: - `tu travailles plus que moi.` — stressed pronoun after `que`. - `elle chante moins fort que lui.` — use `lui`, not `il`, after `que`. - `ils arrivent plus tôt que nous.` — `nous` is both the subject and stressed form. do not use subject pronouns after prepositions or after `que` in comparisons. say `avec moi`, not an english-style subject form. ## unit 3: forming the imperative ### core idea the imperative gives commands, advice, invitations, and instructions. french uses only the `tu`, `nous`, and `vous` forms, and the subject pronoun is normally omitted. ### regular imperative forms | infinitive | `tu` command | `nous` command | `vous` command | |---|---|---|---| | `parler` | `parle` | `parlons` | `parlez` | | `finir` | `finis` | `finissons` | `finissez` | | `attendre` | `attends` | `attendons` | `attendez` | | `ouvrir` | `ouvre` | `ouvrons` | `ouvrez` | ### the special `-er` rule in the `tu` form of regular `-er` verbs, the final `-s` disappears. - `parle plus lentement.` — speak more slowly. - `regarde le tableau.` — look at the board. - `ferme la porte.` — close the door. but before `y` or `en`, that `-s` reappears for sound flow. - `penses-y.` — think about it. - `parles-en à ton frère.` — talk to your brother about it. - `vas-y.` — go ahead / go there. ### irregular imperative forms | infinitive | `tu` command | `nous` command | `vous` command | |---|---|---|---| | `être` | `sois` | `soyons` | `soyez` | | `avoir` | `aie` | `ayons` | `ayez` | | `savoir` | `sache` | `sachons` | `sachez` | | `vouloir` | `veuille` | `veuillons` | `veuillez` | | `aller` | `va` | `allons` | `allez` | ### uses of the imperative - `attends ici.` — wait here. - `finissons ce travail maintenant.` — let's finish this work now. - `soyez prudents.` — be careful. - `veuillez entrer.` — please come in. - `sachons écouter.` — let us know how to listen / let's learn to listen. ### pronunciation note `allons-y` and `vas-y` are common sound chunks. practice them aloud until they come out smoothly. ### common mistake for english speakers do not include the subject pronoun in an ordinary command. - correct: `fermez la fenêtre.` - incorrect: `*vous fermez la fenêtre.` when you mean “close the window.” also remember that `parle` is correct for the `tu` form of a regular `-er` verb, not `*parles`, unless `y` or `en` follows. ### sentence-building practice 1. turn these infinitives into three commands each: `écouter`, `choisir`, `attendre`. 2. write one command with `être`, one with `avoir`, and one polite instruction with `vouloir`. 3. create two `tu` commands: one regular `-er` form without `-s`, and one with `y` or `en`. 1. commands: | infinitive | `tu` | `nous` | `vous` | |---|---|---|---| | `écouter` | `écoute !` | `écoutons !` | `écoutez !` | | `choisir` | `choisis !` | `choisissons !` | `choisissez !` | | `attendre` | `attends !` | `attendons !` | `attendez !` | 2. irregular and polite commands: - `sois patient !` — `être` has an irregular command form. - `aie confiance !` — `avoir` also has an irregular command form. - `veuillez entrer !` — `veuillez` is the polite command form of `vouloir`. 3. two `tu` commands: - `ferme la porte !` — regular -er `tu` command drops the final letter found in the present-tense spelling. - `vas-y !` — before `y`, the `tu` command keeps the final sound-linking letter. the trap is writing the subject pronoun. a command is `écoute !`, not a normal statement with `tu`. ## unit 4: negative commands ### core idea a negative command surrounds the imperative verb with the negative frame, just as in an ordinary sentence. ### basic pattern | meaning | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | do not ... | `ne + imperative + pas` | `ne parle pas.` | don't speak. | | never ... | `ne + imperative + jamais` | `n'arrivez jamais en retard.` | never arrive late. | | no longer ... | `ne + imperative + plus` | `n'en demandez plus.` | don't ask for any more. | | say nothing | `ne + imperative + rien` | `ne dites rien.` | don't say anything. | | wait for nobody | `ne + imperative + personne` | `n'attends personne ici.` | wait for nobody here. | ### examples in context - `ne laissez rien sur la table !` — don't leave anything on the table! - `ne bouge pas.` — don't move. - `n'oublions pas les clés.` — let's not forget the keys. - `ne parlez plus pendant l'exercice.` — don't talk anymore during the exercise. - `n'écrivez jamais votre mot de passe ici.` — never write your password here. ### pronunciation note before a vowel sound, `ne` becomes `n'`: `n'attendez pas`, `n'oublie rien`, `n'écoutez plus`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not switch to the infinitive after `ne`. french still uses the imperative form. - correct: `ne prends pas ça.` - incorrect: `*ne prendre pas ça.` also place the negative words around the verb, not after the whole sentence. ### sentence-building practice 1. turn these into negative commands: `parle plus vite.`, `attendez ici.`, `finissons ce chapitre.` 2. write one negative command with `jamais`, one with `rien`, and one with `plus`. 3. say each one aloud with strong intonation. 1. negative commands: - `parle plus vite.` -> `ne parle pas plus vite.` — **ne ... pas** wraps the command verb. - `attendez ici.` -> `n'attendez pas ici.` — `ne` shortens to `n'` before the vowel sound. - `finissons ce chapitre.` -> `ne finissons pas ce chapitre.` — the `nous` command form stays the same inside the negative frame. 2. more negative commands: - `ne travaille jamais le dimanche !` — `jamais` replaces `pas`. - `ne laisse rien ici !` — `rien` comes after the verb in this command. - `ne parle plus !` — `plus` means not anymore. 3. when you say them aloud, make the negative frame clear. in writing, do not drop `ne` at this stage. ## unit 5: commands with object pronouns ### core idea pronouns move in commands. in an affirmative command, they come **after** the verb and are linked with hyphens. in a negative command, they return **before** the verb in the normal order. ### affirmative commands: pronouns after the verb `me` and `te` become `moi` and `toi` after the verb. | order after an affirmative command | example | |---|---| | verb + `-le / -la / -les` | `regarde-le !` | | verb + `-moi / -toi / -lui / -nous / -vous / -leur` | `donnez-moi ce livre !` | | verb + direct object + indirect object | `donne-le-moi !` | | verb + `-y` / `-en` | `vas-y !`, `parles-en !` | examples: - `regarde-le !` — look at it / him! - `donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.` — give me the hammer and then bring it to him afterward. - `envoyez-nous les photos !` — send us the photos! - `prenez-en un.` — take one of them / take one. ### negative commands: pronouns before the verb in a negative command, pronouns go back before the verb, in the familiar sentence order. | order before a negative command | example | |---|---| | `ne + me / te / nous / vous + verb + pas` | `ne me parle pas.` | | `ne + le / la / les + verb + pas` | `ne les ouvre pas.` | | `ne + me / te / nous / vous + le / la / les + verb + pas` | `ne me le donne pas.` | | `ne + lui / leur / y / en + verb + pas` | `ne leur écrivez pas.`, `n'y va pas.`, `n'en prends pas.` | examples: - `ne le regardez pas !` — don't look at it / him! - `ne me le donne pas !` — don't give it to me! - `ne leur téléphonez pas maintenant.` — don't call them now. - `n'y allez pas seuls.` — don't go there alone. ### quick contrast table | affirmative command | negative command | english | |---|---|---| | `donne-moi le livre.` | `ne me donne pas le livre.` | give me the book. / don't give me the book. | | `regardez-le.` | `ne le regardez pas.` | look at it. / don't look at it. | | `parles-en à ta sœur.` | `n'en parle pas à ta sœur.` | talk to your sister about it. / don't talk to your sister about it. | ### pronunciation note hyphens matter in writing because they show the command structure clearly: `donne-moi`, `regardez-le`, `apporte-le-lui`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not keep pronouns before the verb in affirmative commands. - correct: `donne-le-moi !` - incorrect: `*me le donne !` and in negative commands, switch back to normal order. - correct: `ne me le donne pas !` - incorrect: `*ne donne-le-moi pas !` ### sentence-building practice 1. change these to affirmative commands with pronouns: `tu donnes le stylo à moi.`, `vous regardez le film.`, `tu parles de ce problème.` 2. turn each of those into a negative command. 3. write two new pairs of your own: one affirmative command and its negative version. 1. affirmative commands: - `tu donnes le stylo à moi.` -> `donne-le-moi !` — in an affirmative command, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens; direct object `le` comes before `moi`. - `vous regardez le film.` -> `regardez-le !` — `le film` becomes `le` after the command. - `tu parles de ce problème.` -> `parles-en !` — `en` replaces the **de + thing** phrase, and the `tu` command keeps the linking letter before `en`. 2. negative commands: - `ne me le donne pas !` — in a negative command, pronouns return before the verb in normal order. - `ne le regardez pas !` — **ne ... pas** surrounds the pronoun plus verb chunk. - `n'en parle pas !` — `en` stays before the verb; the command form is now `parle`. 3. your own pairs can vary. models: - `aide-moi !` / `ne m'aide pas !` — `moi` after the affirmative command becomes `me` before the negative command. - `écoutez-les !` / `ne les écoutez pas !` — direct object pronoun after the verb in the affirmative, before the verb in the negative. the big rule is position: after the verb in affirmative commands, before the verb in negative commands. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine the chapter's main structures in realistic french. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.` — as for me, i don't see anyone in the garden. 2. `ne laissez rien sur la table !` — don't leave anything on the table! 3. `donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.` — give me the hammer and then bring it to him afterward. 4. `eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le dimanche.` — as for them, they never work on sundays. 5. `tu n'es pas prêt ? — si, attends-moi deux minutes.` — you're not ready? — yes, i am; wait for me two minutes. 6. `avec nous, soyez patients et ne nous coupez pas la parole.` — with us, be patient and don't interrupt us. use the printed sentences as a memory check. after writing them from memory, look for these specific traps. - **negative frames.** sentence 1 uses **ne ... personne**, sentence 2 uses **ne ... rien**, sentence 4 uses **ne ... jamais**, and sentence 6 uses **ne ... pas** around the command `coupez`. - **stressed pronouns.** sentence 1 starts with `moi`, sentence 4 with `eux`, and sentence 6 with `avec nous`. these are not ordinary subject pronouns; they are stressed forms used for emphasis or after a preposition. - **affirmative command pronouns.** in sentence 3, `donne-moi` and `apporte-le-lui` put pronouns after the command with hyphens. `le` comes before `lui`. - **negative command pronouns.** in sentence 6, `ne nous coupez pas` puts `nous` before the verb because the command is negative. - **the answer word `si`.** sentence 5 uses `si` because it contradicts the negative question `tu n'es pas prêt ?`. - **accents and punctuation.** keep `prêt`, the hyphens in command-pronoun groups, and the space before `?` and `!`. if your meaning is correct but your pronoun is on the wrong side of the command verb, count the sentence wrong. pronoun placement is the main skill here. ### notice and mark for each sentence above: 1. circle the stressed pronoun or command form; 2. underline the negative frame if there is one; 3. identify where each object pronoun stands: before the verb or after it. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the person or number; 2. change one important detail such as the place, object, or time expression. make every necessary change to pronouns, agreement, and command form. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using: - one stressed pronoun after a preposition; - one answer with `si`, `moi aussi`, or `moi non plus`; - one affirmative command; - one negative command with a pronoun. notice and mark: 1. `moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.` — `moi` is a stressed pronoun for emphasis; the negative frame is **ne ... personne**. 2. `ne laissez rien sur la table !` — negative command; the negative frame is **ne ... rien**. 3. `donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.` — command forms: `donne`, `apporte`; object pronouns stand after the affirmative commands. in `apporte-le-lui`, direct `le` comes before indirect `lui`. 4. `eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le dimanche.` — `eux` is a stressed pronoun for emphasis; the negative frame is **ne ... jamais**. 5. `tu n'es pas prêt ? — si, attends-moi deux minutes.` — `si` contradicts a negative question; `moi` stands after the affirmative command `attends`. 6. `avec nous, soyez patients et ne nous coupez pas la parole.` — `nous` is stressed after `avec`; `soyez` is a command; in the negative command, `nous` stands before the verb. change the sentence — sample answers: - sentence 1, person change: `lui, il ne voit personne dans le jardin.` — stressed pronoun and subject both change. - sentence 1, detail change: `moi, je ne vois personne dans la rue.` — same negative frame, new place. - sentence 2, person change: `ne laissons rien sur la table !` — `nous` command form. - sentence 2, detail change: `ne laissez rien dans le sac !` — same **ne ... rien** frame. - sentence 3, person change: `donnez-moi le marteau et apportez-le-lui ensuite.` — both commands become `vous` forms. - sentence 3, detail change: `donne-moi le livre et apporte-le-lui ensuite.` — `le` still works for a masculine singular object. - sentence 4, person change: `elle, elle ne travaille jamais le dimanche.` — stressed pronoun and verb change to singular. - sentence 4, detail change: `eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le soir.` — same negative frame, new time expression. make it personal — sample answers: 1. `je révise avec elle après le cours.` — stressed pronoun after a preposition. 2. `tu n'es pas fatigué ? — si, un peu.` — `si` contradicts a negative question. 3. `écoute cette phrase !` — affirmative command with no subject pronoun. 4. `ne me téléphone pas ce soir !` — negative command with the object pronoun before the verb. ## mini-dialogue: before leaving the apartment **nadia :** `tu n'as pas mes clés ?` — you don't have my keys? **julien :** `si, elles sont avec moi.` — yes, i do; they're with me. **nadia :** `alors, donne-les-moi, s'il te plaît.` — then give them to me, please. **julien :** `tiens. et prends aussi ton chargeur.` — here. and take your charger too. **nadia :** `moi, je suis prête, mais eux, ils ne sont pas encore en bas.` — i'm ready, but they aren't downstairs yet. **julien :** `ne les attends pas trop longtemps.` — don't wait for them too long. **nadia :** `d'accord. toi et moi, on part maintenant ?` — okay. you and i, are we leaving now? **julien :** `oui, allons-y, mais n'oublie rien cette fois.` — yes, let's go, but don't forget anything this time. ### guided analysis 1. find the use of `si` and explain why `oui` would be weaker or less precise here. 2. identify three stressed pronouns and explain their function. 3. find one affirmative command with a pronoun and one negative command. 4. why does nadia say `donne-les-moi` and not `*me les donne`? 5. rewrite line 8 with `vous` instead of `tu`. 1. `si` appears in julien's answer: `si, elles sont avec moi.` nadia's question is negative, `tu n'as pas mes clés ?`, so `si` clearly means **yes, i do**. `oui` would be less precise because it does not specifically contradict the negative wording. 2. three stressed pronouns: `moi` in `avec moi` is after a preposition; `moi` in `moi, je suis prête` marks emphasis; `eux` in `eux, ils ne sont pas encore en bas` also marks emphasis. `toi et moi` is another stressed-pronoun use in a compound subject. 3. affirmative command with a pronoun: `donne-les-moi`. negative command: `ne les attends pas trop longtemps.` the last line also has the negative command `n'oublie rien`. 4. nadia says `donne-les-moi` because affirmative command pronouns follow the verb with hyphens. the direct object `les` comes before the indirect object `moi`; before an affirmative command, `me` changes to `moi` after the verb. 5. with `vous` instead of `tu`: `oui, allons-y, mais n'oubliez rien cette fois.` the group invitation `allons-y` can stay the same; the direct command to the other person becomes `n'oubliez`. the dialogue contrasts the two command orders: after the verb in `donne-les-moi`, before the verb in `ne les attends pas`. ## connected reading: classroom instructions on the first day `le premier jour du cours, notre professeure parle très clairement. d'abord, elle dit : « avec moi, soyez honnêtes et posez des questions. » puis elle ajoute : « si vous ne comprenez pas, dites-le tout de suite ; n'attendez pas la fin. » moi aussi, j'aime cette règle, parce que les étudiants timides ne parlent pas encore beaucoup. la professeure leur dit souvent : « vous et moi, nous sommes ici pour apprendre ; ne vous comparez pas aux autres. » personne ne rit, et personne ne se sent exclu.` — on the first day of class, our teacher speaks very clearly. first, she says, “with me, be honest and ask questions.” then she adds, “if you don't understand, say it right away; don't wait until the end.” i like that rule too, because shy students do not talk much yet. the teacher often tells them, “you and i are here to learn; don't compare yourselves to others.” nobody laughs, and nobody feels excluded. ### reading practice 1. find every imperative form in the paragraph and identify whether it is `tu`, `nous`, or `vous`. 2. find one stressed pronoun after a preposition, one in a compound subject, and one echo-answer expression. 3. identify the negative expressions in the paragraph. 4. rewrite the teacher's instructions so she is talking to one close friend instead of a class. 5. write three true classroom or work rules of your own using the model. 1. **imperative forms:** `soyez` is a `vous` command; `posez` is a `vous` command; `dites-le` is a `vous` command with an object pronoun after it; `n'attendez pas` is a negative `vous` command; `ne vous comparez pas` is also a negative `vous` command. 2. **stressed pronouns:** after a preposition, `avec moi`; in a compound subject, `vous et moi`; echo-answer expression, `moi aussi`. each one uses a stressed form because ordinary subject pronouns do not fit those positions. 3. **negative expressions:** `vous ne comprenez pas`, `n'attendez pas`, `ne parlent pas encore`, `ne vous comparez pas`, `personne ne rit`, and `personne ne se sent exclu`. notice that `personne` can stand before the verb and still pairs with `ne`. 4. to one close friend: `avec moi, sois honnête et pose des questions. si tu ne comprends pas, dis-le tout de suite ; n'attends pas la fin. toi et moi, nous sommes ici pour apprendre ; ne te compare pas aux autres.` the -er commands `pose` and `compare` drop the final written `s` in the `tu` command. 5. answers vary. model rules: `avec moi, sois patient.` `si tu ne comprends pas, demande-le tout de suite.` `ne laisse rien sur le bureau.` mark yourself wrong if you leave a subject pronoun inside a command or if a negative command keeps the pronoun after the verb. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. using `oui` instead of `si` to contradict a negative question. 2. using `moi aussi` after a negative sentence; french wants `moi non plus`. 3. using subject pronouns after prepositions: `avec moi`, not `*avec je`. 4. leaving the subject pronoun inside a command: `fermez la porte`, not `*vous fermez la porte`. 5. forgetting that regular `-er` `tu` commands drop final `-s`: `parle`, not `*parles`, unless `y` or `en` follows. 6. leaving object pronouns before the verb in affirmative commands. 7. keeping affirmative command order inside a negative command. ## chapter reference sheet ### yes/no and stronger answer patterns | function | form | |---|---| | yes | `oui` | | no | `non` | | yes, in contradiction to a negative | `si` | | me too | `moi aussi` | | me neither | `moi non plus` | | not at all | `pas du tout` | ### common negative frames | meaning | pattern | |---|---| | not | `ne...pas` | | never | `ne...jamais` | | no longer | `ne...plus` | | nothing | `ne...rien` | | nobody | `ne...personne` | | not yet | `ne...pas encore` | ### stressed pronouns | subject | stressed | |---|---| | `je` | `moi` | | `tu` | `toi` | | `il` | `lui` | | `elle` | `elle` | | `nous` | `nous` | | `vous` | `vous` | | `ils` | `eux` | | `elles` | `elles` | ### core imperative forms | verb | `tu` | `nous` | `vous` | |---|---|---|---| | `parler` | `parle` | `parlons` | `parlez` | | `finir` | `finis` | `finissons` | `finissez` | | `attendre` | `attends` | `attendons` | `attendez` | | `être` | `sois` | `soyons` | `soyez` | | `avoir` | `aie` | `ayons` | `ayez` | | `savoir` | `sache` | `sachons` | `sachez` | | `vouloir` | `veuille` | `veuillons` | `veuillez` | | `aller` | `va` | `allons` | `allez` | ### pronouns in commands | situation | position | |---|---| | affirmative command | after the verb, with hyphens: `donne-le-moi` | | negative command | before the verb, normal order: `ne me le donne pas` | | `me` / `te` in affirmative command | become `moi` / `toi` | | regular `-er` `tu` command before `y` / `en` | keep or add `-s`: `vas-y`, `parles-en` | ## final production assignment write a paragraph or mini-scene of 8-12 sentences in which one person gives instructions and another person reacts. include: - one answer with `si`; - one use of `moi aussi` or `moi non plus`; - at least four stressed pronouns in different functions; - three affirmative commands; - three negative commands; - at least two commands with object pronouns. after writing: 1. circle every stressed pronoun; 2. underline each negative frame; 3. box every imperative verb; 4. check whether your pronouns are after the verb in affirmative commands and before the verb in negative commands; 5. read the whole passage aloud twice; 6. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary. here is a model mini-scene that satisfies every bullet: `camille dit : « avec moi, soyez calmes et ouvrez vos cahiers ! » elle ajoute : « lisez cette consigne, puis donnez-la-moi ! » hugo répond : « je ne comprends pas encore. » camille demande : « tu n'as pas ton stylo ? » hugo dit : « si, il est avec elle. » camille dit : « alors, prends-le et ne le perds pas ! » elle dit au groupe : « ne parlez jamais pendant l'écoute et ne me coupez pas la parole ! » hugo répond : « moi aussi, je veux réussir. » camille conclut : « toi et eux, travaillez ensemble ; pour nous, c'est important. »` how it satisfies the brief: `si` contradicts a negative question; `moi aussi` is the echo answer; stressed pronouns include `moi`, `elle`, `toi`, `eux`, and `nous`; affirmative commands include `soyez`, `ouvrez`, `lisez`, `donnez`, `prends`, and `travaillez`; negative commands include `ne le perds pas`, `ne parlez jamais`, and `ne me coupez pas`; command-pronoun examples include `donnez-la-moi`, `prends-le`, `ne le perds pas`, and `ne me coupez pas`. mark yourself wrong if any of these appear: - `oui` where the answer contradicts a negative question; - a subject pronoun inside a command, such as `vous ouvrez` when you mean `ouvrez`; - `me` or `te` after an affirmative command instead of `moi` or `toi`; - an affirmative command pronoun before the verb; - a negative command pronoun after the verb. "},{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 11 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-exercises\">Chapter 11 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>You never speak.</li>\n<li>with her</li>\n<li>You (tu) listen.</li>\n<li>We finish.</li>\n<li>Look at it.</li>\n<li>Do not give it to him.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>These answers match the answer key.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous ne parlez jamais.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jamais</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> and means never. The answer key chooses polite or plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> for <strong>you</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec elle</code> — after a preposition, use the stressed pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>, not a subject form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoute !</code> — a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command for a regular -er verb drops the final letter found in the present-tense spelling and has no subject pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Finissons !</code> — the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> command uses the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> verb form without the subject pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code> — in an affirmative command, the object pronoun follows the verb with a hyphen.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le lui donne pas !</code> — in a negative command, pronouns go before the verb; direct <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> comes before indirect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> follows the verb.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>expanded negatives</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vois personne.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>stressed pronouns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec moi</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>imperative forms</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle !</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>commands with pronouns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly show the named structure and place command pronouns correctly.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Expanded negatives: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vois rien dans le sac.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rien</code> replaces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> and comes after the verb here.</li>\n<li>Stressed pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je travaille avec eux.</code> — after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code>, use the stressed form.</li>\n<li>Imperative forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Fermez la porte !</code> — command form with no subject pronoun.</li>\n<li>Commands with pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code> — affirmative command pronouns come after the verb with hyphens.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you answer a negative question with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oui</code> when <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> is needed, if a command includes a subject pronoun, or if a negative command leaves the pronoun after the verb.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le livre.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-moi le livre.</code> — switch to the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command form.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me donne pas le livre.</code> — in a negative command, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> returns before the verb.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu me donnes le livre ?</code> — this is no longer a command, so normal pronoun order returns.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le cahier.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check command type first. Affirmative commands put pronouns after the verb; negative commands put them before the verb.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'aimes pas ça ? — Si, j'aime ça.</code> — the answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> because the question is negative.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi</code> is a stressed pronoun, and the negative frame is <strong>ne ... personne</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le professeur parle avec nous après le cours.</code> — after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> is the stressed pronoun form.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle plus lentement.</code> — the regular -er <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command drops the final written <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laissez rien sur la table !</code> — negative command with <strong>ne ... rien</strong>; keep the space before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">!</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.</code> — affirmative command pronouns come after the verb with hyphens, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> comes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct once for vocabulary, once for command forms, once for pronoun placement, and once for accents and punctuation. Do not fix everything in one pass.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write house or study instructions containing four affirmative commands, four negative commands, and three stressed pronouns.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Model instructions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ouvre ton cahier !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écris la date !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regardez le tableau !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi ton stylo !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne parle pas trop vite !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laissez rien sur la table !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne me téléphone pas maintenant !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le lui donne pas !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Travaille avec elle.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pour moi, c'est clair.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Eux, ils préfèrent étudier le soir.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: include four affirmative commands, four negative commands, and three stressed pronouns. Mark it wrong if a command has a subject pronoun, if <strong>ne ... pas</strong> is in the wrong place, or if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> does not become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>/<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code> after an affirmative command.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Expanded negatives.</strong> French has negative pairs such as <strong>ne ... jamais</strong>, <strong>ne ... rien</strong>, <strong>ne ... personne</strong>, and <strong>ne ... plus</strong>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vois personne.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Stressed pronouns.</strong> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eux</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code> after prepositions, for emphasis, in comparisons, and in short answers. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je viens avec eux.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Imperative forms.</strong> Commands use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> verb forms without subject pronouns. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoutez !</code></li>\n<li><strong>Commands with pronouns.</strong> Affirmative command pronouns follow the verb; negative command pronouns come before it. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le regarde pas !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you can recite the rule but put the pronoun on the wrong side of the verb, redo the command section. Placement is the point of the chapter.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 11 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. you never speak. 2. with her 3. you (tu) listen. 4. we finish. 5. look at it. 6. do not give it to him. these answers match the answer key. 1. `vous ne parlez jamais.` — `jamais` replaces `pas` and means never. the answer key chooses polite or plural `vous` for **you**. 2. `avec elle` — after a preposition, use the stressed pronoun `elle`, not a subject form. 3. `écoute !` — a `tu` command for a regular -er verb drops the final letter found in the present-tense spelling and has no subject pronoun. 4. `finissons !` — the `nous` command uses the `nous` verb form without the subject pronoun. 5. `regarde-le !` — in an affirmative command, the object pronoun follows the verb with a hyphen. 6. `ne le lui donne pas !` — in a negative command, pronouns go before the verb; direct `le` comes before indirect `lui`, and `pas` follows the verb. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **expanded negatives**. use `je ne vois personne.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **stressed pronouns**. use `avec moi` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **imperative forms**. use `parle !` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **commands with pronouns**. use `regarde-le !` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly show the named structure and place command pronouns correctly. sample answers: 1. expanded negatives: `je ne vois rien dans le sac.` — `rien` replaces `pas` and comes after the verb here. 2. stressed pronouns: `je travaille avec eux.` — after `avec`, use the stressed form. 3. imperative forms: `fermez la porte !` — command form with no subject pronoun. 4. commands with pronouns: `donne-le-moi !` — affirmative command pronouns come after the verb with hyphens. mark yourself wrong if you answer a negative question with `oui` when `si` is needed, if a command includes a subject pronoun, or if a negative command leaves the pronoun after the verb. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `donne-moi le livre.` 1. change the subject: `donnez-moi le livre.` — switch to the `vous` command form. 2. make it negative: `ne me donne pas le livre.` — in a negative command, `me` returns before the verb. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que tu me donnes le livre ?` — this is no longer a command, so normal pronoun order returns. 4. change one meaningful detail: `donne-moi le cahier.` check command type first. affirmative commands put pronouns after the verb; negative commands put them before the verb. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `tu n'aimes pas ça ? — si, j'aime ça.` — the answer is `si` because the question is negative. 2. `moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.` — `moi` is a stressed pronoun, and the negative frame is **ne ... personne**. 3. `le professeur parle avec nous après le cours.` — after `avec`, `nous` is the stressed pronoun form. 4. `parle plus lentement.` — the regular -er `tu` command drops the final written `s`. 5. `ne laissez rien sur la table !` — negative command with **ne ... rien**; keep the space before `!`. 6. `donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.` — affirmative command pronouns come after the verb with hyphens, and `le` comes before `lui`. correct once for vocabulary, once for command forms, once for pronoun placement, and once for accents and punctuation. do not fix everything in one pass. ## e. personal writing write house or study instructions containing four affirmative commands, four negative commands, and three stressed pronouns. answers vary. model instructions: 1. `ouvre ton cahier !` 2. `écris la date !` 3. `regardez le tableau !` 4. `donne-moi ton stylo !` 5. `ne parle pas trop vite !` 6. `ne laissez rien sur la table !` 7. `ne me téléphone pas maintenant !` 8. `ne le lui donne pas !` 9. `travaille avec elle.` 10. `pour moi, c'est clair.` 11. `eux, ils préfèrent étudier le soir.` self-check: include four affirmative commands, four negative commands, and three stressed pronouns. mark it wrong if a command has a subject pronoun, if **ne ... pas** is in the wrong place, or if `me`/`te` does not become `moi`/`toi` after an affirmative command. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **expanded negatives.** french has negative pairs such as **ne ... jamais**, **ne ... rien**, **ne ... personne**, and **ne ... plus**. example: `je ne vois personne.` 2. **stressed pronouns.** use `moi`, `toi`, `lui`, `elle`, `nous`, `vous`, `eux`, and `elles` after prepositions, for emphasis, in comparisons, and in short answers. example: `je viens avec eux.` 3. **imperative forms.** commands use `tu`, `nous`, or `vous` verb forms without subject pronouns. example: `écoutez !` 4. **commands with pronouns.** affirmative command pronouns follow the verb; negative command pronouns come before it. example: `regarde-le !` / `ne le regarde pas !` if you can recite the rule but put the pronoun on the wrong side of the verb, redo the command section. placement is the point of the chapter. "},{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 11 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-answer-key\">Chapter 11 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous ne parlez jamais.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec elle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoute !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Finissons !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le lui donne pas !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expanded negatives:</strong> Pairs include toujours/ne...jamais, déjà/ne...pas encore, quelqu'un/ne...personne, quelque chose/ne...rien, and encore/ne...plus.</li>\n<li><strong>Stressed pronouns:</strong> Forms are moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles. Use them after prepositions, for emphasis, comparisons, and short answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Imperative forms:</strong> Commands use tu, nous, and vous forms without subject pronouns. Regular -er tu commands normally drop final -s.</li>\n<li><strong>Commands with pronouns:</strong> In affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens and their order changes. In negative commands, they return before the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 11 answer key ## section a 1. `vous ne parlez jamais.` 2. `avec elle` 3. `écoute !` 4. `finissons !` 5. `regarde-le !` 6. `ne le lui donne pas !` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **expanded negatives:** pairs include toujours/ne...jamais, déjà/ne...pas encore, quelqu'un/ne...personne, quelque chose/ne...rien, and encore/ne...plus. - **stressed pronouns:** forms are moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles. use them after prepositions, for emphasis, comparisons, and short answers. - **imperative forms:** commands use tu, nous, and vous forms without subject pronouns. regular -er tu commands normally drop final -s. - **commands with pronouns:** in affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens and their order changes. in negative commands, they return before the verb. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 11 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 11 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Expanded negatives</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Stressed pronouns</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Imperative forms</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Commands with pronouns</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 11 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **expanded negatives**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **stressed pronouns** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **imperative forms**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **commands with pronouns**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":12,"slug":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle","title":"Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle","documents":[{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/README.md","title":"Chapter 12: Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-and-the-present-participle\">Chapter 12: Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate reflexive and reciprocal verbs.</li>\n<li>Form negatives, questions, and commands with pronominal verbs.</li>\n<li>Use body-part constructions.</li>\n<li>Form and use the present participle.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Reflexive verbs</li>\n<li>Questions, negatives, commands</li>\n<li>Body parts and reciprocal meaning</li>\n<li>Present participle</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 12: pronominal verbs and the present participle this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate reflexive and reciprocal verbs. - form negatives, questions, and commands with pronominal verbs. - use body-part constructions. - form and use the present participle. ## lesson sequence 1. reflexive verbs 2. questions, negatives, commands 3. body parts and reciprocal meaning 4. present participle ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 12 Lessons: Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-lessons-pronominal-verbs-and-the-present-participle\">Chapter 12 Lessons: Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle</h1>\n<p>This chapter gives you two very useful tools. First, you will learn pronominal verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lever</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se souvenir de</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'appeler</code>, which are everywhere in everyday French. Then you will learn the present participle, which helps you connect actions smoothly: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.</code></p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>recognize what a pronominal verb is and conjugate common ones in the present;</li>\n<li>place reflexive pronouns correctly with conjugated verbs and with infinitives;</li>\n<li>form negative, interrogative, and imperative patterns with pronominal verbs;</li>\n<li>talk naturally about body parts with reflexive verbs;</li>\n<li>recognize reciprocal meaning such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous parlons</code>;</li>\n<li>use important idiomatic pronominal verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se souvenir de</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'en aller</code>;</li>\n<li>distinguish non-pronominal and pronominal verb pairs with different meanings;</li>\n<li>form and use the present participle, including <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étant</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayant</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachant</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>conjugate regular present-tense verbs with all six persons;</li>\n<li>form basic negation with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>;</li>\n<li>understand command word order from Chapter 11, because affirmative and negative reflexive commands behave differently;</li>\n<li>use definite articles confidently, because body-part expressions depend on them.</li>\n<p>If those skills still feel uncertain, review them briefly first.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Check that the Chapter 11 machinery is still available before adding reflexive pronouns.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se laver</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me lave</code>. A pronominal verb needs both the subject and the matching reflexive pronoun.</li>\n<li>Make <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me repose</code> negative. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne me repose pas.</code> The negative frame surrounds the reflexive pronoun plus verb.</li>\n<li>Say <strong>Get up!</strong> to one close person. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code> In an affirmative reflexive command, the pronoun moves after the verb and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>.</li>\n<li>Say <strong>Do not worry!</strong> to one close person. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne t'inquiète pas !</code> In a negative command, the reflexive pronoun stays before the verb, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> elides to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li>Translate: I wash my hands. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave les mains.</code> With body parts, French normally uses the definite article because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> already shows whose hands they are.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review negation, command order, and definite articles before continuing. Pronominal verbs make pronoun placement non-negotiable.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Say the pronoun and the verb together every time: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me lève</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous dépêchons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle va se reposer</code>. For the present participle, memorize the sound pattern <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code> and connect it to full phrases such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en travaillant</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en lisant</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-what-a-pronominal-verb-is\">Unit 1: What a pronominal verb is</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A pronominal verb appears with a reflexive-form pronoun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code>. Sometimes the action comes back to the subject (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me lave</code>), and sometimes the pronominal form has a special meaning (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me souviens</code>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"reflexive-pronouns\">Reflexive pronouns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Reflexive pronoun</th>\n<th>Before vowel sound</th>\n<th>English idea</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m'</code></td>\n<td>myself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t'</code></td>\n<td>yourself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'</code></td>\n<td>himself / herself / itself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>ourselves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>yourself / yourselves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'</code></td>\n<td>themselves</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"model-conjugation-se-lever\">Model conjugation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lever</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me lève</code></td>\n<td>get up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te lèves</code></td>\n<td>get up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lève</code></td>\n<td>gets up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous levons</code></td>\n<td>get up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous levez</code></td>\n<td>get up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lèvent</code></td>\n<td>get up</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.</code> — I get up early and get ready quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te couches tard le vendredi.</code> — You go to bed late on Fridays.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous reposons après le déjeuner.</code> — We rest after lunch.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se maquillent avant de sortir.</code> — They put on makeup before going out.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Before a vowel sound, the reflexive pronoun contracts: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je m'habille</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu t'arrêtes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle s'appelle</code>. Train yourself to hear the pronoun as part of the verb chunk.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not forget either half of the structure.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève.</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Je lève.</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Me lève.</code></li>\n<p>You need both the subject pronoun and the reflexive pronoun.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se laver</code> orally with all six persons.</li>\n<li>Write three sentences about your daily routine with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lever</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se coucher</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se reposer</code>.</li>\n<li>Say one example with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m'</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t'</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se laver</code>:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me lave</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu te laves</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il se lave</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous lavons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous vous lavez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils se lavent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The reflexive pronoun changes with the subject. Do not drop it: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je lave</code> means I wash something else.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Routine sentences:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève à sept heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> matches <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous reposons après le déjeuner.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous</code> is normal: subject plus reflexive pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle se couche tôt le dimanche.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> matches <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Elision examples:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'habille vite.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m'</code> before a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu t'arrêtes ici.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t'</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle s'appelle Lina.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-pronominal-verbs-in-negatives-questions-infinitives-and-commands\">Unit 2: Pronominal verbs in negatives, questions, infinitives, and commands</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The reflexive pronoun changes position depending on the structure. With a conjugated verb, it usually stands right before the verb. With an infinitive, it goes before the infinitive. In affirmative commands, it moves after the verb and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"structure-overview-with-se-lever\">Structure overview with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lever</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>affirmative statement</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tôt.</code></td>\n<td>I get up early.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negative statement</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne me lève pas tôt.</code></td>\n<td>I don't get up early.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>yes/no question</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te lèves tôt ?</code></td>\n<td>Do you get up early?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code> question</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous vous levez tôt ?</code></td>\n<td>Do you get up early?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>with an infinitive</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais me lever.</code></td>\n<td>I am going to get up.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>affirmative command</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code></td>\n<td>Get up!</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negative command</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne te lève pas !</code></td>\n<td>Don't get up!</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"pronominal-verbs-with-infinitives\">Pronominal verbs with infinitives</h3>\n<p>The reflexive pronoun stays directly before the infinitive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais me coucher tôt ce soir.</code> — I am going to go to bed early tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons nous asseoir ici.</code> — We want to sit down here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle doit se dépêcher.</code> — She has to hurry.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu peux te reposer maintenant.</code> — You can rest now.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"questions-with-pronominal-verbs\">Questions with pronominal verbs</h3>\n<p>At this level, the easiest question patterns are intonation or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te sens mieux aujourd'hui ?</code> — Do you feel better today?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce qu'ils se connaissent ?</code> — Do they know each other?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce que vous vous inquiétez ?</code> — Why are you worrying?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"imperative-forms-of-a-pronominal-verb\">Imperative forms of a pronominal verb</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Person</th>\n<th>Affirmative command</th>\n<th>Negative command</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne te lève pas !</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Levons-nous !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne nous levons pas !</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Levez-vous !</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne vous levez pas !</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>In affirmative commands, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code> after the verb.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne se couche pas avant minuit.</code> — She doesn't go to bed before midnight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous vous levez tôt demain ?</code> — Are you getting up early tomorrow?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons nous promener après le dîner.</code> — We are going to take a walk after dinner.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi !</code> — Hurry up!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne vous inquiétez pas.</code> — Don't worry.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The hyphen in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lève-toi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dépêchons-nous</code> matters in writing. In speech, practice these as single rhythmic groups.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not put the reflexive pronoun after the infinitive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais me lever.</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Je vais lever me.</code></li>\n<p>And do not keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> in an affirmative command.</p>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Lève-te !</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Turn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me repose</code> into a negative sentence, a question, and a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais...</code> sentence.</li>\n<li>Give three commands with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se dépêcher</code>, one for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, one for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and one for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te couches tard.</code> as a negative command and then as a future-near sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Forms from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me repose</code>:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne me repose pas.</code> — <strong>ne ... pas</strong> wraps the reflexive pronoun plus verb.</li>\n<li>Question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que je me repose ?</code> — the reflexive pronoun stays before the verb.</li>\n<li>Near future: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais me reposer.</code> — with an infinitive, the reflexive pronoun goes before the infinitive.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Commands with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se dépêcher</code>:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi !</code> — affirmative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code> after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêchons-nous !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> stays <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> after the command.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêchez-vous !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> stays <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> after the command.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te couches tard.</code>:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Negative command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne te couche pas tard !</code> — in a negative command, the reflexive pronoun returns before the verb.</li>\n<li>Near future: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu vas te coucher tard.</code> — the reflexive pronoun sits before the infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">coucher</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-body-parts-and-reciprocal-meaning\">Unit 3: Body parts and reciprocal meaning</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>With reflexive verbs and body parts, French usually uses the definite article, not the possessive adjective, because the owner is already obvious from the reflexive pronoun. With plural subjects, some pronominal verbs express actions people do to each other.</p>\n<h3 id=\"body-parts-use-the-definite-article\">Body parts: use the definite article</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>French pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se + verb + le / la / les + body part</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave les mains.</code></td>\n<td>I wash my hands.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se + verb + le / la / les + body part</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle se brosse les dents.</code></td>\n<td>She brushes her teeth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se + verb + le / la / les + body part</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous cassons la voix.</code></td>\n<td>We strain our voices.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.</code> — The children wash their hands before dinner.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me brosse les cheveux rapidement.</code> — I brush my hair quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il se lave le visage à l'eau froide.</code> — He washes his face with cold water.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"reciprocal-meaning\">Reciprocal meaning</h3>\n<p>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>, a pronominal verb can mean “each other” or “one another.”</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se parler</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons souvent.</code></td>\n<td>We talk to each other often.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'écrire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils s'écrivent chaque semaine.</code></td>\n<td>They write to each other every week.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se téléphoner</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous vous téléphonez le soir.</code></td>\n<td>You call each other in the evening.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se voir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se voient demain.</code></td>\n<td>They are seeing each other tomorrow.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons en marchant vers le travail.</code> — We talk to each other while walking to work.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se comprennent bien.</code> — They understand each other well.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous vous retrouvez après le cours ?</code> — Are you meeting up after class?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Listen carefully to repeated pronoun shapes such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous vous</code>. They look heavy on the page but become natural with repetition.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not say <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Je me lave mes mains</code> in ordinary French. French normally says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave les mains.</code> Also remember that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous parlons</code> means “we speak to each other,” not “we speak ourselves.”</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace the body part: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les mains</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les dents</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le visage</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les yeux</code>.</li>\n<li>Write three reciprocal sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn one reciprocal sentence into a question and one into a negative sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Body-part examples:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave les mains.</code> — French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, not a possessive adjective, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> already shows whose hands.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me brosse les dents.</code> — same definite-article pattern.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave le visage.</code> — singular body part, singular article.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me frotte les yeux.</code> — plural body part, plural article.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Reciprocal sentences:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons souvent.</code> — with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, this can mean we talk to each other.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous vous écrivez chaque semaine.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous vous</code> is subject plus reciprocal pronoun.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se téléphonent le soir.</code> — plural subject makes the <strong>each other</strong> meaning natural.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Question and negative:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous vous écrivez chaque semaine ?</code> — the reflexive pronoun stays before the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils ne se téléphonent pas le soir.</code> — <strong>ne ... pas</strong> wraps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se téléphonent</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-idiomatic-pronominal-verbs-and-meaning-contrasts\">Unit 4: Idiomatic pronominal verbs and meaning contrasts</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Some pronominal verbs are not simple “self” actions. They have an idiomatic meaning you should learn as a whole expression. In other cases, adding the pronominal form changes the meaning of the base verb.</p>\n<h3 id=\"high-frequency-idiomatic-pronominal-verbs\">High-frequency idiomatic pronominal verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se souvenir de</code></td>\n<td>to remember</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me souviens de son nom.</code></td>\n<td>I remember his name.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'entendre avec</code></td>\n<td>to get along with</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle s'entend bien avec ses collègues.</code></td>\n<td>She gets along well with her coworkers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se dépêcher</code></td>\n<td>to hurry</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous dépêchons.</code></td>\n<td>We are hurrying.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se rendre compte de</code></td>\n<td>to realize</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te rends compte du problème ?</code></td>\n<td>Do you realize the problem?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'en aller</code></td>\n<td>to go away / leave</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils s'en vont tôt.</code></td>\n<td>They leave early.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me souviens de cette adresse.</code> — I remember this address.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On s'entend bien avec nos voisins.</code> — We get along well with our neighbors.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêchez-vous, le train arrive.</code> — Hurry up, the train is arriving.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle se rend compte de son erreur.</code> — She realizes her mistake.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Après le dîner, ils s'en vont sans bruit.</code> — After dinner, they leave quietly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"non-pronominal-vs-pronominal-pairs\">Non-pronominal vs. pronominal pairs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Non-pronominal verb</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Pronominal verb</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler quelqu'un</code></td>\n<td>to call someone</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'appeler</code></td>\n<td>to be called / named</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réveiller quelqu'un</code></td>\n<td>to wake someone</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se réveiller</code></td>\n<td>to wake up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amuser quelqu'un</code></td>\n<td>to amuse someone</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'amuser</code></td>\n<td>to have fun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">coucher quelqu'un</code></td>\n<td>to put someone to bed</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se coucher</code></td>\n<td>to go to bed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'appelle mon frère, mais je m'appelle Karim.</code> — I call my brother, but my name is Karim.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le bruit réveille les enfants, puis ils se réveillent en colère.</code> — The noise wakes the children, then they wake up angry.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette histoire amuse Léa, et Léa s'amuse beaucoup.</code> — This story amuses Léa, and Léa has a lot of fun.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'en aller</code>, the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> create a compact sound group. Practice <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je m'en vais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous en allons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez-vous-en</code> separately.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not translate every pronominal verb word for word. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me souviens de</code> means “I remember,” not “I remember myself.” Also do not forget the prepositions built into certain expressions: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se souvenir de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'entendre avec</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se rendre compte de</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Write one sentence with each of these: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se souvenir de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'entendre avec</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se dépêcher</code>.</li>\n<li>Contrast the meaning of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'appeler</code> in two short sentences.</li>\n<li>Write one question with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se rendre compte de</code> and one answer with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'en aller</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Idiomatic pronominal verbs:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me souviens de cette chanson.</code> — remember the built-in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle s'entend bien avec sa colocataire.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code> belongs to the expression.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous dépêchons parce que le train arrive.</code> — the reflexive pronoun matches <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Meaning contrast:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'appelle mon frère.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appeler</code> means to call someone.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'appelle Nadia.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'appeler</code> means to be named.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Question and answer:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te rends compte du problème ?</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se rendre compte de</code> keeps its <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>, here combined with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, je m'en vais maintenant.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'en aller</code> has both the reflexive pronoun and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>; keep the cluster together before the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The trap is translating the reflexive word by word. Learn each idiomatic verb as a whole expression.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-forming-and-using-the-present-participle\">Unit 5: Forming and using the present participle</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The present participle usually comes from the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> present-tense stem minus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code>, plus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code>. It often expresses a simultaneous action, especially with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"formation-rule\">Formation rule</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form</th>\n<th>Remove <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></th>\n<th>Add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code></th>\n<th>Present participle</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parl-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finiss-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissant</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous prenons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pren-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prenant</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous écrivons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écriv-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrivant</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous faisons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fais-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisant</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-important-irregular-forms\">The three important irregular forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Present participle</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étant</code></td>\n<td>being</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayant</code></td>\n<td>having</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachant</code></td>\n<td>knowing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"use-1-with-en-for-simultaneous-action-or-means\">Use 1: with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> for simultaneous action or means</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En marchant, elle téléphone.</code> — While walking, she is talking on the phone.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.</code> — By studying every day, she makes progress.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous révisons en écoutant de la musique douce.</code> — We review while listening to soft music.</li>\n<p>A helpful beginner rule: the subject of the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + participle</code> phrase is normally the same as the subject of the main verb.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"use-2-standing-alone-in-a-shorter-descriptive-clause\">Use 2: standing alone in a shorter descriptive clause</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Connaissant déjà la réponse, il n'a rien dit.</code> — Knowing the answer already, he said nothing.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Arrivant trop tard, nous avons raté le début.</code> — Arriving too late, we missed the beginning.</li>\n<p>This style is common in careful written French.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"use-3-as-an-adjective\">Use 3: as an adjective</h3>\n<p>When an <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code> form behaves like an adjective, it agrees like an adjective.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une histoire amusante</code> — an amusing story</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des enfants souriants</code> — smiling children</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une remarque étonnante</code> — a surprising remark</li>\n<p>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, however, the participle itself does <strong>not</strong> agree.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"examples-in-context\">Examples in context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons en préparant le dîner.</code> — We talk to each other while preparing dinner.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ayant très faim, il se sert rapidement.</code> — Being very hungry / As he is very hungry, he serves himself quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est une solution rassurante.</code> — It's a reassuring solution.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Sachant qu'elle se lève tôt, nous partons avant dix heures.</code> — Knowing that she gets up early, we leave before ten o'clock.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The ending <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ant</code> has a nasal sound. Practice <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissant</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étant</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayant</code> slowly and clearly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not form the present participle directly from the infinitive. Start from the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous faisons</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisant</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*faireant</code>. Also do not add agreement after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en étudiant</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*en étudiante</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Form the present participle of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</li>\n<li>Write two sentences with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + present participle</code> about your study habits.</li>\n<li>Turn one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + participle</code> sentence into a sentence with a full clause starting with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pendant que</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Present participles:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisir</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">choisissant</code> — from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous choisissons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">buvant</code> — from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous buvons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisant</code> — from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous lisons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisant</code> — from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous faisons</code>; this spelling is irregular-looking but standard.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étant</code> — one of the special irregular forms.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Study-habit sentences:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'apprends mieux en écoutant les dialogues.</code> — same subject does both actions.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je mémorise les mots en écrivant des exemples.</code> — the phrase with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> explains the method.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Full-clause version:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je mémorise les mots pendant que j'écris des exemples.</code> — the full clause has its own subject and conjugated verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not add agreement to the participle after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>: write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en écrivant</code>, not an adjective form.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine the chapter's main structures in natural French. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.</code> — I get up early and get ready quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.</code> — The children wash their hands before dinner.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons en marchant vers le travail.</code> — We talk to each other while walking to work.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.</code> — By studying every day, she makes progress.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais me coucher plus tôt, parce que je me rends compte de ma fatigue.</code> — I am going to go to bed earlier because I realize I am tired.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi, mais ne t'inquiète pas : nous nous en allons dans cinq minutes.</code> — Hurry up, but don't worry: we are leaving in five minutes.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>After writing the sentences from memory, check the pieces that are easiest to misplace.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reflexive pronouns.</strong> Sentence 1 needs both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me lève</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me prépare</code>; sentence 2 needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lavent</code>; sentence 5 needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me coucher</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me rends compte</code>; sentence 6 needs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t'inquiète</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous en allons</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Body-part article.</strong> Sentence 2 is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les mains</code>, not a possessive adjective, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lavent</code> already shows whose hands.</li>\n<li><strong>Present participles.</strong> Sentence 3 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en marchant</code>; sentence 4 has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En étudiant</code>. The participle after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> does not agree with the subject.</li>\n<li><strong>Commands.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi</code> puts the pronoun after the affirmative command; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne t'inquiète pas</code> puts it before the verb in the negative command.</li>\n<li><strong>Idiomatic cluster.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous en allons</code> belongs together: subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, reflexive pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, then the verb.</li>\n<li><strong>Accents and punctuation.</strong> Keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lève</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dîner</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">t'inquiète</code>, and the colon in sentence 6 if you are copying the full model.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Count a sentence wrong if the reflexive pronoun is missing, even when the main verb is spelled correctly. The pronoun is part of the verb structure in this chapter.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence above:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle the reflexive pronoun or participle;</li>\n<li>underline the conjugated verb that controls it;</li>\n<li>identify whether the verb is reflexive, reciprocal, idiomatic, or a participle structure.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject and person;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the body part, time expression, or activity.</li>\n<p>Check the reflexive pronoun and every agreement change.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one daily-routine pronominal verb;</li>\n<li>one body-part expression;</li>\n<li>one idiomatic pronominal verb;</li>\n<li>one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + present participle</code> phrase.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Notice and mark:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me lève</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me prépare</code> are reflexive; the controlling verbs are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lève</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prépare</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lavent</code> is reflexive with a body part; French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les mains</code>, not a possessive adjective.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons en marchant vers le travail.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code> is reciprocal, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en marchant</code> is a present-participle structure.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiant</code> is the participle; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code> is the main conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais me coucher plus tôt, parce que je me rends compte de ma fatigue.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me coucher</code> is a pronominal infinitive, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me rends compte de</code> is idiomatic.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi, mais ne t'inquiète pas : nous nous en allons dans cinq minutes.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi</code> is an affirmative pronominal command; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne t'inquiète pas</code> is a negative pronominal command; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous en allons</code> is idiomatic <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'en aller</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Change the sentence — sample answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sentence 1, subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous levons tôt et nous nous préparons rapidement.</code> — both reflexive pronouns and verbs change.</li>\n<li>Sentence 1, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tard et je me prépare lentement.</code> — same structure, new details.</li>\n<li>Sentence 2, subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle se lave les mains avant le dîner.</code> — subject and verb change, but the body part keeps the definite article.</li>\n<li>Sentence 2, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants se brossent les dents avant le dîner.</code> — new body part, same article rule.</li>\n<li>Sentence 3, subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se parlent en marchant vers le travail.</code> — plural subject keeps reciprocal meaning.</li>\n<li>Sentence 3, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons en attendant le bus.</code> — new participle phrase.</li>\n<li>Sentence 4, subject change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En étudiant chaque jour, nous faisons des progrès.</code> — the main subject changes, and the verb changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faisons</code>.</li>\n<li>Sentence 4, detail change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En lisant chaque soir, elle fait des progrès.</code> — new participle from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Make it personal — sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me réveille à sept heures.</code> — daily-routine pronominal verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave le visage.</code> — body part with definite article.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me souviens de mes devoirs.</code> — idiomatic pronominal verb with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'apprends le vocabulaire en marchant.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> plus present participle for simultaneous action.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-getting-ready-for-class\">Mini-dialogue: getting ready for class</h2>\n<p><strong>Inès :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te prépares déjà ?</code> — Are you getting ready already? <strong>Mathis :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, je vais m'habiller maintenant, mais je ne me sens pas très bien.</code> — Yes, I'm going to get dressed now, but I don't feel very well. <strong>Inès :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi un peu, sinon nous allons nous inquiéter.</code> — Hurry up a little, otherwise we are going to worry. <strong>Mathis :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne t'inquiète pas. En buvant un café, je vais me réveiller.</code> — Don't worry. By drinking a coffee, I'm going to wake up. <strong>Inès :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te souviens de la salle du cours ?</code> — Do you remember the classroom? <strong>Mathis :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, et je m'entends bien avec les étudiants de ce groupe.</code> — Yes, and I get along well with the students in that group. <strong>Inès :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Alors, levons-nous et allons-y.</code> — Then let's get up and go. <strong>Mathis :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">D'accord, mais attends-moi en fermant la porte.</code> — Okay, but wait for me while closing the door.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find one pronominal verb in the near future (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller + infinitive</code>) and explain the pronoun position.</li>\n<li>Find one affirmative reflexive command and one negative reflexive command.</li>\n<li>Which line contains an idiomatic pronominal verb with a preposition?</li>\n<li>Which line contains <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + present participle</code>, and what relationship between the two actions does it show?</li>\n<li>Rewrite line 7 so that Inès is speaking to one person formally.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Near-future pronominal verbs include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais m'habiller</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous allons nous inquiéter</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais me réveiller</code>. In each case, the reflexive pronoun stands directly before the infinitive, not after it.</li>\n<li>Affirmative reflexive command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi un peu</code>. Negative reflexive command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne t'inquiète pas.</code> Line 7 also has the affirmative command <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">levons-nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Idiomatic pronominal verbs with prepositions appear in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te souviens de la salle du cours ?</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je m'entends bien avec les étudiants de ce groupe.</code> The prepositions <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec</code> are part of the expressions.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En buvant un café, je vais me réveiller</code> shows method: drinking coffee is how Mathis expects to wake up. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attends-moi en fermant la porte</code> shows a simultaneous action: wait for me while closing the door.</li>\n<li>Formal command to one person: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Alors, levez-vous et allez-y.</code> The reflexive command uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> after the verb, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> gives <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allez-y</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The main contrast is position: before the infinitive in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vais m'habiller</code>, after the verb in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi</code>, and before the verb in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne t'inquiète pas</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-my-evening-routine-at-the-residence-hall\">Connected reading: my evening routine at the residence hall</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, je me détends un peu après le dîner, puis je vais me laver le visage et me brosser les dents. Mes colocataires et moi nous nous racontons notre journée en rangeant la cuisine. Quand l'un de nous se sent stressé, les autres essaient de le calmer en parlant doucement. Je me souviens toujours de préparer mes affaires avant de me coucher, parce que je me rends vite compte de mes oublis le matin. Si nous nous couchons trop tard, nous nous réveillons tous plus mal le lendemain. En travaillant régulièrement, pourtant, nous nous sentons beaucoup plus tranquilles.</code> — In the evening, I relax a little after dinner, then I go wash my face and brush my teeth. My roommates and I tell each other about our day while putting away the kitchen. When one of us feels stressed, the others try to calm him or her by speaking softly. I always remember to prepare my things before going to bed, because I quickly realize my oversights in the morning. If we go to bed too late, we all wake up feeling worse the next day. By working regularly, however, we feel much calmer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List the daily-routine pronominal verbs in the paragraph.</li>\n<li>Find one body-part expression, one reciprocal verb, and one idiomatic pronominal verb.</li>\n<li>Find the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + present participle</code> phrases and explain what each one adds.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the paragraph with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> in the first three sentences.</li>\n<li>Write three true sentences about your own evening routine using the model.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Daily-routine pronominal verbs:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me détends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais me laver</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me brosser</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me coucher</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous couchons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous réveillons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous sentons</code>. The infinitive forms still keep the reflexive pronoun before the infinitive.</li>\n<li><strong>Body-part expressions:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me laver le visage</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me brosser les dents</code>. <strong>Reciprocal verb:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous racontons notre journée</code>. <strong>Idiomatic pronominal verbs:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me souviens de</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me rends compte de</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Present-participle phrases:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en rangeant la cuisine</code> tells what is happening at the same time as the roommates talk; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en parlant doucement</code> explains how the others try to calm the stressed person; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En travaillant régulièrement</code> explains the method or condition that makes them feel calmer.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, elle se détend un peu après le dîner, puis elle va se laver le visage et se brosser les dents. Ses colocataires et elle se racontent leur journée en rangeant la cuisine. Quand l'une d'elles se sent stressée, les autres essaient de la calmer en parlant doucement.</code> The body parts still use definite articles, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">stressée</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'une</code>.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. A model set: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, je me repose après le dîner.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me brosse les dents en écoutant une leçon.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me souviens de préparer mon sac avant de me coucher.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if the reflexive pronoun is missing, if a body part takes a possessive adjective, or if an <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + participle</code> phrase gets adjective agreement.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forgetting the reflexive pronoun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me lève</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je lève</code>.</li>\n<li>Putting the reflexive pronoun after the infinitive: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais me coucher</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je vais coucher me</code>.</li>\n<li>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code> in an affirmative reflexive command.</li>\n<li>Using a possessive adjective with body parts when French normally prefers the definite article.</li>\n<li>Translating idiomatic pronominal verbs word for word.</li>\n<li>Mixing up a base verb and its pronominal partner: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'appelle</code> is not the same as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je m'appelle</code>.</li>\n<li>Forming the present participle from the infinitive instead of the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> stem.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"reflexive-pronouns\">Reflexive pronouns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Reflexive pronoun</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me / m'</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te / t'</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se / s'</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se / s'</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"model-present-tense-se-lever\">Model present tense: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lever</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me lève</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te lèves</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lève</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous levons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous levez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lèvent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"reflexive-commands\">Reflexive commands</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Person</th>\n<th>Affirmative</th>\n<th>Negative</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lève-toi</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne te lève pas</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">levons-nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne nous levons pas</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">levez-vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne vous levez pas</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"body-part-pattern\">Body-part pattern</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se + verb + le / la / les + body part</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave les mains.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reciprocal plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"idiomatic-pronominal-verbs\">Idiomatic pronominal verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se souvenir de</code></td>\n<td>to remember</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'entendre avec</code></td>\n<td>to get along with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se dépêcher</code></td>\n<td>to hurry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se rendre compte de</code></td>\n<td>to realize</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'en aller</code></td>\n<td>to go away / leave</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"present-participle-quick-reference\">Present participle quick reference</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Rule or form</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> stem - <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ons</code> + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ant</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en travaillant</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>irregular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étant</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayant</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachant</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>adjective use</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une histoire amusante</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about your daily routine, your relationships with other people, or your study habits. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least four pronominal verbs;</li>\n<li>one negative pronominal sentence;</li>\n<li>one question with a pronominal verb;</li>\n<li>one affirmative or negative reflexive command;</li>\n<li>one body-part expression with a definite article;</li>\n<li>one reciprocal verb;</li>\n<li>one idiomatic pronominal verb with its preposition;</li>\n<li>one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + present participle</code> phrase.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every reflexive pronoun;</li>\n<li>underline every conjugated verb;</li>\n<li>box the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en + present participle</code> phrase;</li>\n<li>check whether your infinitive constructions place the reflexive pronoun before the infinitive;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is one complete model:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le matin, je me réveille à sept heures. Je me lave le visage et je me brosse les dents. Je ne me couche pas tard pendant la semaine. Tu te prépares aussi tôt ? Dépêche-toi, parce que le cours commence bientôt ! Mes amis et moi nous nous parlons avant la classe. Je me souviens de la salle et je me rends compte de l'heure. En marchant vers l'école, nous révisons les verbes. Après le cours, nous nous reposons dix minutes.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: pronominal verbs include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me réveille</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me lave</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me brosse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne me couche pas</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu te prépares</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous parlons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me souviens de</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me rends compte de</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous reposons</code>; the negative pronominal sentence is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne me couche pas</code>; the question is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu te prépares aussi tôt ?</code>; the command is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi</code>; the body-part expression is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le visage</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les dents</code>; the reciprocal verb is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous parlons</code>; the idiomatic verbs keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>; and the participle phrase is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En marchant vers l'école</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if any of these appear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a pronominal verb without its reflexive pronoun;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vais coucher me</code> or any reflexive pronoun after an infinitive;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-te</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi</code>;</li>\n<li>a body-part phrase such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes dents</code> where the reflexive pronoun already shows possession;</li>\n<li>agreement added to the participle after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 12 lessons: pronominal verbs and the present participle this chapter gives you two very useful tools. first, you will learn pronominal verbs such as `se lever`, `se souvenir de`, and `s'appeler`, which are everywhere in everyday french. then you will learn the present participle, which helps you connect actions smoothly: `en étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.` ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. recognize what a pronominal verb is and conjugate common ones in the present; 2. place reflexive pronouns correctly with conjugated verbs and with infinitives; 3. form negative, interrogative, and imperative patterns with pronominal verbs; 4. talk naturally about body parts with reflexive verbs; 5. recognize reciprocal meaning such as `nous nous parlons`; 6. use important idiomatic pronominal verbs such as `se souvenir de` and `s'en aller`; 7. distinguish non-pronominal and pronominal verb pairs with different meanings; 8. form and use the present participle, including `étant`, `ayant`, and `sachant`. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - conjugate regular present-tense verbs with all six persons; - form basic negation with `ne...pas`; - understand command word order from chapter 11, because affirmative and negative reflexive commands behave differently; - use definite articles confidently, because body-part expressions depend on them. if those skills still feel uncertain, review them briefly first. check that the chapter 11 machinery is still available before adding reflexive pronouns. 1. conjugate `se laver` with `je`. — `je me lave`. a pronominal verb needs both the subject and the matching reflexive pronoun. 2. make `je me repose` negative. — `je ne me repose pas.` the negative frame surrounds the reflexive pronoun plus verb. 3. say **get up!** to one close person. — `lève-toi !` in an affirmative reflexive command, the pronoun moves after the verb and `te` becomes `toi`. 4. say **do not worry!** to one close person. — `ne t'inquiète pas !` in a negative command, the reflexive pronoun stays before the verb, and `te` elides to `t'` before a vowel sound. 5. translate: i wash my hands. — `je me lave les mains.` with body parts, french normally uses the definite article because `me` already shows whose hands they are. if you missed two or more, review negation, command order, and definite articles before continuing. pronominal verbs make pronoun placement non-negotiable. ## how to study this chapter say the pronoun and the verb together every time: `je me lève`, `nous nous dépêchons`, `elle va se reposer`. for the present participle, memorize the sound pattern `-ant` and connect it to full phrases such as `en travaillant` and `en lisant`. ## unit 1: what a pronominal verb is ### core idea a pronominal verb appears with a reflexive-form pronoun: `me`, `te`, `se`, `nous`, `vous`, `se`. sometimes the action comes back to the subject (`je me lave`), and sometimes the pronominal form has a special meaning (`je me souviens`). ### reflexive pronouns | subject | reflexive pronoun | before vowel sound | english idea | |---|---|---|---| | `je` | `me` | `m'` | myself | | `tu` | `te` | `t'` | yourself | | `il / elle / on` | `se` | `s'` | himself / herself / itself | | `nous` | `nous` | — | ourselves | | `vous` | `vous` | — | yourself / yourselves | | `ils / elles` | `se` | `s'` | themselves | ### model conjugation: `se lever` | subject | form | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `me lève` | get up | | `tu` | `te lèves` | get up | | `il / elle / on` | `se lève` | gets up | | `nous` | `nous levons` | get up | | `vous` | `vous levez` | get up | | `ils / elles` | `se lèvent` | get up | ### examples in context - `je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.` — i get up early and get ready quickly. - `tu te couches tard le vendredi.` — you go to bed late on fridays. - `nous nous reposons après le déjeuner.` — we rest after lunch. - `elles se maquillent avant de sortir.` — they put on makeup before going out. ### pronunciation note before a vowel sound, the reflexive pronoun contracts: `je m'habille`, `tu t'arrêtes`, `elle s'appelle`. train yourself to hear the pronoun as part of the verb chunk. ### common mistake for english speakers do not forget either half of the structure. - correct: `je me lève.` - incorrect: `*je lève.` - incorrect: `*me lève.` you need both the subject pronoun and the reflexive pronoun. ### sentence-building practice 1. conjugate `se laver` orally with all six persons. 2. write three sentences about your daily routine with `se lever`, `se coucher`, or `se reposer`. 3. say one example with `m'`, one with `t'`, and one with `s'`. 1. `se laver`: | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `je me lave` | | `tu` | `tu te laves` | | `il / elle / on` | `il se lave` | | `nous` | `nous nous lavons` | | `vous` | `vous vous lavez` | | `ils / elles` | `ils se lavent` | the reflexive pronoun changes with the subject. do not drop it: `je lave` means i wash something else. 2. routine sentences: - `je me lève à sept heures.` — `me` matches `je`. - `nous nous reposons après le déjeuner.` — `nous nous` is normal: subject plus reflexive pronoun. - `elle se couche tôt le dimanche.` — `se` matches `elle`. 3. elision examples: - `je m'habille vite.` — `me` becomes `m'` before a vowel sound. - `tu t'arrêtes ici.` — `te` becomes `t'`. - `elle s'appelle lina.` — `se` becomes `s'`. ## unit 2: pronominal verbs in negatives, questions, infinitives, and commands ### core idea the reflexive pronoun changes position depending on the structure. with a conjugated verb, it usually stands right before the verb. with an infinitive, it goes before the infinitive. in affirmative commands, it moves after the verb and `te` becomes `toi`. ### structure overview with `se lever` | structure | french | english | |---|---|---| | affirmative statement | `je me lève tôt.` | i get up early. | | negative statement | `je ne me lève pas tôt.` | i don't get up early. | | yes/no question | `tu te lèves tôt ?` | do you get up early? | | `est-ce que` question | `est-ce que vous vous levez tôt ?` | do you get up early? | | with an infinitive | `je vais me lever.` | i am going to get up. | | affirmative command | `lève-toi !` | get up! | | negative command | `ne te lève pas !` | don't get up! | ### pronominal verbs with infinitives the reflexive pronoun stays directly before the infinitive. - `je vais me coucher tôt ce soir.` — i am going to go to bed early tonight. - `nous voulons nous asseoir ici.` — we want to sit down here. - `elle doit se dépêcher.` — she has to hurry. - `tu peux te reposer maintenant.` — you can rest now. ### questions with pronominal verbs at this level, the easiest question patterns are intonation or `est-ce que`. - `tu te sens mieux aujourd'hui ?` — do you feel better today? - `est-ce qu'ils se connaissent ?` — do they know each other? - `pourquoi est-ce que vous vous inquiétez ?` — why are you worrying? ### imperative forms of a pronominal verb | person | affirmative command | negative command | |---|---|---| | `tu` | `lève-toi !` | `ne te lève pas !` | | `nous` | `levons-nous !` | `ne nous levons pas !` | | `vous` | `levez-vous !` | `ne vous levez pas !` | in affirmative commands, `te` becomes `toi` after the verb. ### examples in context - `elle ne se couche pas avant minuit.` — she doesn't go to bed before midnight. - `vous vous levez tôt demain ?` — are you getting up early tomorrow? - `nous allons nous promener après le dîner.` — we are going to take a walk after dinner. - `dépêche-toi !` — hurry up! - `ne vous inquiétez pas.` — don't worry. ### pronunciation note the hyphen in `lève-toi` and `dépêchons-nous` matters in writing. in speech, practice these as single rhythmic groups. ### common mistake for english speakers do not put the reflexive pronoun after the infinitive. - correct: `je vais me lever.` - incorrect: `*je vais lever me.` and do not keep `te` in an affirmative command. - correct: `lève-toi !` - incorrect: `*lève-te !` ### sentence-building practice 1. turn `je me repose` into a negative sentence, a question, and a `je vais...` sentence. 2. give three commands with `se dépêcher`, one for `tu`, one for `nous`, and one for `vous`. 3. rewrite `tu te couches tard.` as a negative command and then as a future-near sentence. 1. forms from `je me repose`: - negative: `je ne me repose pas.` — **ne ... pas** wraps the reflexive pronoun plus verb. - question: `est-ce que je me repose ?` — the reflexive pronoun stays before the verb. - near future: `je vais me reposer.` — with an infinitive, the reflexive pronoun goes before the infinitive. 2. commands with `se dépêcher`: - `dépêche-toi !` — affirmative `tu` command: `te` becomes `toi` after the verb. - `dépêchons-nous !` — `nous` stays `nous` after the command. - `dépêchez-vous !` — `vous` stays `vous` after the command. 3. `tu te couches tard.`: - negative command: `ne te couche pas tard !` — in a negative command, the reflexive pronoun returns before the verb. - near future: `tu vas te coucher tard.` — the reflexive pronoun sits before the infinitive `coucher`. ## unit 3: body parts and reciprocal meaning ### core idea with reflexive verbs and body parts, french usually uses the definite article, not the possessive adjective, because the owner is already obvious from the reflexive pronoun. with plural subjects, some pronominal verbs express actions people do to each other. ### body parts: use the definite article | french pattern | example | english | |---|---|---| | `se + verb + le / la / les + body part` | `je me lave les mains.` | i wash my hands. | | `se + verb + le / la / les + body part` | `elle se brosse les dents.` | she brushes her teeth. | | `se + verb + le / la / les + body part` | `nous nous cassons la voix.` | we strain our voices. | examples: - `les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.` — the children wash their hands before dinner. - `je me brosse les cheveux rapidement.` — i brush my hair quickly. - `il se lave le visage à l'eau froide.` — he washes his face with cold water. ### reciprocal meaning with `nous`, `vous`, `ils`, or `elles`, a pronominal verb can mean “each other” or “one another.” | verb | example | english | |---|---|---| | `se parler` | `nous nous parlons souvent.` | we talk to each other often. | | `s'écrire` | `ils s'écrivent chaque semaine.` | they write to each other every week. | | `se téléphoner` | `vous vous téléphonez le soir.` | you call each other in the evening. | | `se voir` | `elles se voient demain.` | they are seeing each other tomorrow. | examples: - `nous nous parlons en marchant vers le travail.` — we talk to each other while walking to work. - `ils se comprennent bien.` — they understand each other well. - `vous vous retrouvez après le cours ?` — are you meeting up after class? ### pronunciation note listen carefully to repeated pronoun shapes such as `nous nous` and `vous vous`. they look heavy on the page but become natural with repetition. ### common mistake for english speakers do not say `*je me lave mes mains` in ordinary french. french normally says `je me lave les mains.` also remember that `nous nous parlons` means “we speak to each other,” not “we speak ourselves.” ### sentence-building practice 1. replace the body part: `les mains`, `les dents`, `le visage`, `les yeux`. 2. write three reciprocal sentences with `nous`, `vous`, or `ils`. 3. turn one reciprocal sentence into a question and one into a negative sentence. 1. body-part examples: - `je me lave les mains.` — french uses `les`, not a possessive adjective, because `me` already shows whose hands. - `je me brosse les dents.` — same definite-article pattern. - `je me lave le visage.` — singular body part, singular article. - `je me frotte les yeux.` — plural body part, plural article. 2. reciprocal sentences: - `nous nous parlons souvent.` — with `nous`, this can mean we talk to each other. - `vous vous écrivez chaque semaine.` — `vous vous` is subject plus reciprocal pronoun. - `ils se téléphonent le soir.` — plural subject makes the **each other** meaning natural. 3. question and negative: - `est-ce que vous vous écrivez chaque semaine ?` — the reflexive pronoun stays before the verb. - `ils ne se téléphonent pas le soir.` — **ne ... pas** wraps `se téléphonent`. ## unit 4: idiomatic pronominal verbs and meaning contrasts ### core idea some pronominal verbs are not simple “self” actions. they have an idiomatic meaning you should learn as a whole expression. in other cases, adding the pronominal form changes the meaning of the base verb. ### high-frequency idiomatic pronominal verbs | verb | meaning | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `se souvenir de` | to remember | `je me souviens de son nom.` | i remember his name. | | `s'entendre avec` | to get along with | `elle s'entend bien avec ses collègues.` | she gets along well with her coworkers. | | `se dépêcher` | to hurry | `nous nous dépêchons.` | we are hurrying. | | `se rendre compte de` | to realize | `tu te rends compte du problème ?` | do you realize the problem? | | `s'en aller` | to go away / leave | `ils s'en vont tôt.` | they leave early. | ### examples in context - `je me souviens de cette adresse.` — i remember this address. - `on s'entend bien avec nos voisins.` — we get along well with our neighbors. - `dépêchez-vous, le train arrive.` — hurry up, the train is arriving. - `elle se rend compte de son erreur.` — she realizes her mistake. - `après le dîner, ils s'en vont sans bruit.` — after dinner, they leave quietly. ### non-pronominal vs. pronominal pairs | non-pronominal verb | meaning | pronominal verb | meaning | |---|---|---|---| | `appeler quelqu'un` | to call someone | `s'appeler` | to be called / named | | `réveiller quelqu'un` | to wake someone | `se réveiller` | to wake up | | `amuser quelqu'un` | to amuse someone | `s'amuser` | to have fun | | `coucher quelqu'un` | to put someone to bed | `se coucher` | to go to bed | examples: - `j'appelle mon frère, mais je m'appelle karim.` — i call my brother, but my name is karim. - `le bruit réveille les enfants, puis ils se réveillent en colère.` — the noise wakes the children, then they wake up angry. - `cette histoire amuse léa, et léa s'amuse beaucoup.` — this story amuses léa, and léa has a lot of fun. ### pronunciation note in `s'en aller`, the `s'` and `en` create a compact sound group. practice `je m'en vais`, `nous nous en allons`, `allez-vous-en` separately. ### common mistake for english speakers do not translate every pronominal verb word for word. `je me souviens de` means “i remember,” not “i remember myself.” also do not forget the prepositions built into certain expressions: `se souvenir de`, `s'entendre avec`, `se rendre compte de`. ### sentence-building practice 1. write one sentence with each of these: `se souvenir de`, `s'entendre avec`, `se dépêcher`. 2. contrast the meaning of `appeler` and `s'appeler` in two short sentences. 3. write one question with `se rendre compte de` and one answer with `s'en aller`. 1. idiomatic pronominal verbs: - `je me souviens de cette chanson.` — remember the built-in `de`. - `elle s'entend bien avec sa colocataire.` — `avec` belongs to the expression. - `nous nous dépêchons parce que le train arrive.` — the reflexive pronoun matches `nous`. 2. meaning contrast: - `j'appelle mon frère.` — `appeler` means to call someone. - `je m'appelle nadia.` — `s'appeler` means to be named. 3. question and answer: - `tu te rends compte du problème ?` — `se rendre compte de` keeps its `de`, here combined with `le` as `du`. - `oui, je m'en vais maintenant.` — `s'en aller` has both the reflexive pronoun and `en`; keep the cluster together before the verb. the trap is translating the reflexive word by word. learn each idiomatic verb as a whole expression. ## unit 5: forming and using the present participle ### core idea the present participle usually comes from the `nous` present-tense stem minus `-ons`, plus `-ant`. it often expresses a simultaneous action, especially with `en`. ### formation rule | `nous` form | remove `-ons` | add `-ant` | present participle | |---|---|---|---| | `nous parlons` | `parl-` | `-ant` | `parlant` | | `nous finissons` | `finiss-` | `-ant` | `finissant` | | `nous prenons` | `pren-` | `-ant` | `prenant` | | `nous écrivons` | `écriv-` | `-ant` | `écrivant` | | `nous faisons` | `fais-` | `-ant` | `faisant` | ### the three important irregular forms | infinitive | present participle | english | |---|---|---| | `être` | `étant` | being | | `avoir` | `ayant` | having | | `savoir` | `sachant` | knowing | ### use 1: with `en` for simultaneous action or means - `en marchant, elle téléphone.` — while walking, she is talking on the phone. - `en étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.` — by studying every day, she makes progress. - `nous révisons en écoutant de la musique douce.` — we review while listening to soft music. a helpful beginner rule: the subject of the `en + participle` phrase is normally the same as the subject of the main verb. ### use 2: standing alone in a shorter descriptive clause - `connaissant déjà la réponse, il n'a rien dit.` — knowing the answer already, he said nothing. - `arrivant trop tard, nous avons raté le début.` — arriving too late, we missed the beginning. this style is common in careful written french. ### use 3: as an adjective when an `-ant` form behaves like an adjective, it agrees like an adjective. - `une histoire amusante` — an amusing story - `des enfants souriants` — smiling children - `une remarque étonnante` — a surprising remark with `en`, however, the participle itself does **not** agree. ### examples in context - `nous nous parlons en préparant le dîner.` — we talk to each other while preparing dinner. - `ayant très faim, il se sert rapidement.` — being very hungry / as he is very hungry, he serves himself quickly. - `c'est une solution rassurante.` — it's a reassuring solution. - `sachant qu'elle se lève tôt, nous partons avant dix heures.` — knowing that she gets up early, we leave before ten o'clock. ### pronunciation note the ending `-ant` has a nasal sound. practice `parlant`, `finissant`, `étant`, `ayant` slowly and clearly. ### common mistake for english speakers do not form the present participle directly from the infinitive. start from the `nous` form: `nous faisons` -> `faisant`, not `*faireant`. also do not add agreement after `en`: `en étudiant`, not `*en étudiante`. ### sentence-building practice 1. form the present participle of `choisir`, `boire`, `lire`, `faire`, and `être`. 2. write two sentences with `en + present participle` about your study habits. 3. turn one `en + participle` sentence into a sentence with a full clause starting with `pendant que` or `quand`. 1. present participles: - `choisir` -> `choisissant` — from `nous choisissons`. - `boire` -> `buvant` — from `nous buvons`. - `lire` -> `lisant` — from `nous lisons`. - `faire` -> `faisant` — from `nous faisons`; this spelling is irregular-looking but standard. - `être` -> `étant` — one of the special irregular forms. 2. study-habit sentences: - `j'apprends mieux en écoutant les dialogues.` — same subject does both actions. - `je mémorise les mots en écrivant des exemples.` — the phrase with `en` explains the method. 3. full-clause version: - `je mémorise les mots pendant que j'écris des exemples.` — the full clause has its own subject and conjugated verb. do not add agreement to the participle after `en`: write `en écrivant`, not an adjective form. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine the chapter's main structures in natural french. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.` — i get up early and get ready quickly. 2. `les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.` — the children wash their hands before dinner. 3. `nous nous parlons en marchant vers le travail.` — we talk to each other while walking to work. 4. `en étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.` — by studying every day, she makes progress. 5. `je vais me coucher plus tôt, parce que je me rends compte de ma fatigue.` — i am going to go to bed earlier because i realize i am tired. 6. `dépêche-toi, mais ne t'inquiète pas : nous nous en allons dans cinq minutes.` — hurry up, but don't worry: we are leaving in five minutes. after writing the sentences from memory, check the pieces that are easiest to misplace. - **reflexive pronouns.** sentence 1 needs both `me lève` and `me prépare`; sentence 2 needs `se lavent`; sentence 5 needs `me coucher` and `me rends compte`; sentence 6 needs `t'inquiète` and `nous nous en allons`. - **body-part article.** sentence 2 is `les mains`, not a possessive adjective, because `se lavent` already shows whose hands. - **present participles.** sentence 3 has `en marchant`; sentence 4 has `en étudiant`. the participle after `en` does not agree with the subject. - **commands.** `dépêche-toi` puts the pronoun after the affirmative command; `ne t'inquiète pas` puts it before the verb in the negative command. - **idiomatic cluster.** `nous nous en allons` belongs together: subject `nous`, reflexive pronoun `nous`, `en`, then the verb. - **accents and punctuation.** keep `lève`, `dîner`, `t'inquiète`, and the colon in sentence 6 if you are copying the full model. count a sentence wrong if the reflexive pronoun is missing, even when the main verb is spelled correctly. the pronoun is part of the verb structure in this chapter. ### notice and mark for each sentence above: 1. circle the reflexive pronoun or participle; 2. underline the conjugated verb that controls it; 3. identify whether the verb is reflexive, reciprocal, idiomatic, or a participle structure. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject and person; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the body part, time expression, or activity. check the reflexive pronoun and every agreement change. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using: - one daily-routine pronominal verb; - one body-part expression; - one idiomatic pronominal verb; - one `en + present participle` phrase. notice and mark: 1. `je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.` — `me lève` and `me prépare` are reflexive; the controlling verbs are `lève` and `prépare`. 2. `les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.` — `se lavent` is reflexive with a body part; french uses `les mains`, not a possessive adjective. 3. `nous nous parlons en marchant vers le travail.` — `nous parlons` is reciprocal, and `en marchant` is a present-participle structure. 4. `en étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.` — `étudiant` is the participle; `fait` is the main conjugated verb. 5. `je vais me coucher plus tôt, parce que je me rends compte de ma fatigue.` — `me coucher` is a pronominal infinitive, and `me rends compte de` is idiomatic. 6. `dépêche-toi, mais ne t'inquiète pas : nous nous en allons dans cinq minutes.` — `dépêche-toi` is an affirmative pronominal command; `ne t'inquiète pas` is a negative pronominal command; `nous nous en allons` is idiomatic `s'en aller`. change the sentence — sample answers: - sentence 1, subject change: `nous nous levons tôt et nous nous préparons rapidement.` — both reflexive pronouns and verbs change. - sentence 1, detail change: `je me lève tard et je me prépare lentement.` — same structure, new details. - sentence 2, subject change: `elle se lave les mains avant le dîner.` — subject and verb change, but the body part keeps the definite article. - sentence 2, detail change: `les enfants se brossent les dents avant le dîner.` — new body part, same article rule. - sentence 3, subject change: `ils se parlent en marchant vers le travail.` — plural subject keeps reciprocal meaning. - sentence 3, detail change: `nous nous parlons en attendant le bus.` — new participle phrase. - sentence 4, subject change: `en étudiant chaque jour, nous faisons des progrès.` — the main subject changes, and the verb changes to `faisons`. - sentence 4, detail change: `en lisant chaque soir, elle fait des progrès.` — new participle from `lire`. make it personal — sample answers: 1. `je me réveille à sept heures.` — daily-routine pronominal verb. 2. `je me lave le visage.` — body part with definite article. 3. `je me souviens de mes devoirs.` — idiomatic pronominal verb with `de`. 4. `j'apprends le vocabulaire en marchant.` — `en` plus present participle for simultaneous action. ## mini-dialogue: getting ready for class **inès :** `tu te prépares déjà ?` — are you getting ready already? **mathis :** `oui, je vais m'habiller maintenant, mais je ne me sens pas très bien.` — yes, i'm going to get dressed now, but i don't feel very well. **inès :** `dépêche-toi un peu, sinon nous allons nous inquiéter.` — hurry up a little, otherwise we are going to worry. **mathis :** `ne t'inquiète pas. en buvant un café, je vais me réveiller.` — don't worry. by drinking a coffee, i'm going to wake up. **inès :** `tu te souviens de la salle du cours ?` — do you remember the classroom? **mathis :** `oui, et je m'entends bien avec les étudiants de ce groupe.` — yes, and i get along well with the students in that group. **inès :** `alors, levons-nous et allons-y.` — then let's get up and go. **mathis :** `d'accord, mais attends-moi en fermant la porte.` — okay, but wait for me while closing the door. ### guided analysis 1. find one pronominal verb in the near future (`aller + infinitive`) and explain the pronoun position. 2. find one affirmative reflexive command and one negative reflexive command. 3. which line contains an idiomatic pronominal verb with a preposition? 4. which line contains `en + present participle`, and what relationship between the two actions does it show? 5. rewrite line 7 so that inès is speaking to one person formally. 1. near-future pronominal verbs include `je vais m'habiller`, `nous allons nous inquiéter`, and `je vais me réveiller`. in each case, the reflexive pronoun stands directly before the infinitive, not after it. 2. affirmative reflexive command: `dépêche-toi un peu`. negative reflexive command: `ne t'inquiète pas.` line 7 also has the affirmative command `levons-nous`. 3. idiomatic pronominal verbs with prepositions appear in `tu te souviens de la salle du cours ?` and `je m'entends bien avec les étudiants de ce groupe.` the prepositions `de` and `avec` are part of the expressions. 4. `en buvant un café, je vais me réveiller` shows method: drinking coffee is how mathis expects to wake up. `attends-moi en fermant la porte` shows a simultaneous action: wait for me while closing the door. 5. formal command to one person: `alors, levez-vous et allez-y.` the reflexive command uses `vous` after the verb, and `aller` gives `allez-y`. the main contrast is position: before the infinitive in `vais m'habiller`, after the verb in `dépêche-toi`, and before the verb in `ne t'inquiète pas`. ## connected reading: my evening routine at the residence hall `le soir, je me détends un peu après le dîner, puis je vais me laver le visage et me brosser les dents. mes colocataires et moi nous nous racontons notre journée en rangeant la cuisine. quand l'un de nous se sent stressé, les autres essaient de le calmer en parlant doucement. je me souviens toujours de préparer mes affaires avant de me coucher, parce que je me rends vite compte de mes oublis le matin. si nous nous couchons trop tard, nous nous réveillons tous plus mal le lendemain. en travaillant régulièrement, pourtant, nous nous sentons beaucoup plus tranquilles.` — in the evening, i relax a little after dinner, then i go wash my face and brush my teeth. my roommates and i tell each other about our day while putting away the kitchen. when one of us feels stressed, the others try to calm him or her by speaking softly. i always remember to prepare my things before going to bed, because i quickly realize my oversights in the morning. if we go to bed too late, we all wake up feeling worse the next day. by working regularly, however, we feel much calmer. ### reading practice 1. list the daily-routine pronominal verbs in the paragraph. 2. find one body-part expression, one reciprocal verb, and one idiomatic pronominal verb. 3. find the `en + present participle` phrases and explain what each one adds. 4. rewrite the paragraph with `elle` instead of `je` in the first three sentences. 5. write three true sentences about your own evening routine using the model. 1. **daily-routine pronominal verbs:** `je me détends`, `je vais me laver`, `me brosser`, `me coucher`, `nous nous couchons`, `nous nous réveillons`, and `nous nous sentons`. the infinitive forms still keep the reflexive pronoun before the infinitive. 2. **body-part expressions:** `me laver le visage` and `me brosser les dents`. **reciprocal verb:** `nous nous racontons notre journée`. **idiomatic pronominal verbs:** `je me souviens de` and `je me rends compte de`. 3. **present-participle phrases:** `en rangeant la cuisine` tells what is happening at the same time as the roommates talk; `en parlant doucement` explains how the others try to calm the stressed person; `en travaillant régulièrement` explains the method or condition that makes them feel calmer. 4. with `elle`: `le soir, elle se détend un peu après le dîner, puis elle va se laver le visage et se brosser les dents. ses colocataires et elle se racontent leur journée en rangeant la cuisine. quand l'une d'elles se sent stressée, les autres essaient de la calmer en parlant doucement.` the body parts still use definite articles, and `stressée` agrees with `l'une`. 5. answers vary. a model set: `le soir, je me repose après le dîner.` `je me brosse les dents en écoutant une leçon.` `je me souviens de préparer mon sac avant de me coucher.` mark yourself wrong if the reflexive pronoun is missing, if a body part takes a possessive adjective, or if an `en + participle` phrase gets adjective agreement. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. forgetting the reflexive pronoun: `je me lève`, not `*je lève`. 2. putting the reflexive pronoun after the infinitive: `je vais me coucher`, not `*je vais coucher me`. 3. using `te` instead of `toi` in an affirmative reflexive command. 4. using a possessive adjective with body parts when french normally prefers the definite article. 5. translating idiomatic pronominal verbs word for word. 6. mixing up a base verb and its pronominal partner: `j'appelle` is not the same as `je m'appelle`. 7. forming the present participle from the infinitive instead of the `nous` stem. ## chapter reference sheet ### reflexive pronouns | subject | reflexive pronoun | |---|---| | `je` | `me / m'` | | `tu` | `te / t'` | | `il / elle / on` | `se / s'` | | `nous` | `nous` | | `vous` | `vous` | | `ils / elles` | `se / s'` | ### model present tense: `se lever` | subject | form | |---|---| | `je` | `me lève` | | `tu` | `te lèves` | | `il / elle / on` | `se lève` | | `nous` | `nous levons` | | `vous` | `vous levez` | | `ils / elles` | `se lèvent` | ### reflexive commands | person | affirmative | negative | |---|---|---| | `tu` | `lève-toi` | `ne te lève pas` | | `nous` | `levons-nous` | `ne nous levons pas` | | `vous` | `levez-vous` | `ne vous levez pas` | ### body-part pattern | pattern | example | |---|---| | `se + verb + le / la / les + body part` | `je me lave les mains.` | | reciprocal plural | `nous nous parlons.` | ### idiomatic pronominal verbs | verb | meaning | |---|---| | `se souvenir de` | to remember | | `s'entendre avec` | to get along with | | `se dépêcher` | to hurry | | `se rendre compte de` | to realize | | `s'en aller` | to go away / leave | ### present participle quick reference | rule or form | example | |---|---| | `nous` stem - `ons` + `ant` | `nous parlons` -> `parlant` | | with `en` | `en travaillant` | | irregular | `étant`, `ayant`, `sachant` | | adjective use | `une histoire amusante` | ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about your daily routine, your relationships with other people, or your study habits. include: - at least four pronominal verbs; - one negative pronominal sentence; - one question with a pronominal verb; - one affirmative or negative reflexive command; - one body-part expression with a definite article; - one reciprocal verb; - one idiomatic pronominal verb with its preposition; - one `en + present participle` phrase. after writing: 1. circle every reflexive pronoun; 2. underline every conjugated verb; 3. box the `en + present participle` phrase; 4. check whether your infinitive constructions place the reflexive pronoun before the infinitive; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary. here is one complete model: `le matin, je me réveille à sept heures. je me lave le visage et je me brosse les dents. je ne me couche pas tard pendant la semaine. tu te prépares aussi tôt ? dépêche-toi, parce que le cours commence bientôt ! mes amis et moi nous nous parlons avant la classe. je me souviens de la salle et je me rends compte de l'heure. en marchant vers l'école, nous révisons les verbes. après le cours, nous nous reposons dix minutes.` how it satisfies the brief: pronominal verbs include `je me réveille`, `je me lave`, `je me brosse`, `je ne me couche pas`, `tu te prépares`, `nous nous parlons`, `je me souviens de`, `je me rends compte de`, and `nous nous reposons`; the negative pronominal sentence is `je ne me couche pas`; the question is `tu te prépares aussi tôt ?`; the command is `dépêche-toi`; the body-part expression is `le visage` and `les dents`; the reciprocal verb is `nous nous parlons`; the idiomatic verbs keep `de`; and the participle phrase is `en marchant vers l'école`. mark yourself wrong if any of these appear: - a pronominal verb without its reflexive pronoun; - `je vais coucher me` or any reflexive pronoun after an infinitive; - `dépêche-te` instead of `dépêche-toi`; - a body-part phrase such as `mes dents` where the reflexive pronoun already shows possession; - agreement added to the participle after `en`. "},{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 12 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-exercises\">Chapter 12 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I wake up.</li>\n<li>We do not get dressed.</li>\n<li>Get up! (tu)</li>\n<li>Wash your hands! (vous)</li>\n<li>nous parlons → participle</li>\n<li>while studying</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>These answers match the answer key.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me réveille.</code> — pronominal verbs need both the subject <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> and the matching reflexive pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne nous habillons pas.</code> — <strong>ne ... pas</strong> wraps the reflexive pronoun plus verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne nous habillons pas</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code> — in an affirmative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> command, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code> after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lavez-vous les mains !</code> — affirmative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> command; French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les mains</code>, not a possessive adjective.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code> — form the participle from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code>: remove the ending and add the participle ending.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en étudiant</code> — the structure with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> plus a participle means while studying or by studying.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>reflexive verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>questions, negatives, commands</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne se couche pas.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>body parts and reciprocal meaning</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave les mains.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>present participle</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct answer must show the named structure and keep the reflexive pronoun with the correct subject.</p>\n<p>Sample answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Reflexive verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me couche tôt.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> matches <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li>Questions, negatives, commands: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne te lève pas !</code> — negative command with the reflexive pronoun before the verb.</li>\n<li>Body parts and reciprocal meaning: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous lavons les mains.</code> — definite article for the body part.</li>\n<li>Present participle: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je révise en écoutant la leçon.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> plus participle shows a simultaneous action or method.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if the reflexive pronoun is missing, if it stays after an infinitive, if an affirmative command uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>, or if a body part uses a possessive adjective where the reflexive pronoun already shows the owner.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Worked example, starting from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tôt.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous levons tôt.</code> — both the subject and the reflexive pronoun change.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne me lève pas tôt.</code> — <strong>ne ... pas</strong> wraps the reflexive pronoun plus verb.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu te lèves tôt ?</code> — the reflexive pronoun stays before the verb.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me couche tard.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check the reflexive pronoun first. A pronominal verb is incomplete if the subject changes but the reflexive pronoun does not.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.</code> — two reflexive pronouns with one subject; do not drop the second <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais me coucher plus tôt, parce que je me rends compte de ma fatigue.</code> — the reflexive pronoun stands before the infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">coucher</code>, and the idiom keeps <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.</code> — body part with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, not a possessive adjective.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dépêche-toi !</code> — affirmative reflexive command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code> after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons en préparant le dîner.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous</code> is normal, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en préparant</code> has no agreement.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.</code> — the participle comes from the verb stem and keeps the accent in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiant</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct separately for vocabulary, reflexive pronoun placement, command form, and accents. A missing reflexive pronoun changes the grammar, not just the spelling.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write your morning routine in eight sentences and add two simultaneous actions with en + present participle.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model routine:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me réveille à sept heures.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tout de suite.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave le visage.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me brosse les dents.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je m'habille rapidement.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me souviens de mes devoirs.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je révise en buvant du café.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je marche vers le cours en écoutant un dialogue.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons avant la classe.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne m'inquiète pas.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: eight routine sentences plus two <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> plus present-participle phrases. Mark it wrong if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> is missing, if a body part uses a possessive adjective, or if the participle after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> gets adjective agreement.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Reflexive verbs.</strong> Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> with the matching subject. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me prépare.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Questions, negatives, commands.</strong> The reflexive pronoun normally stays before the verb; in affirmative commands it moves after the verb, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne te lève pas !</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code></li>\n<li><strong>Body parts and reciprocal meaning.</strong> Body parts usually take the definite article, and plural pronominal verbs can mean each other. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons et nous nous lavons les mains.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Present participle.</strong> Form it from the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> stem plus the participle ending; with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code>, it often shows simultaneous action or method. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'apprends en écrivant.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If your example has the right verb but the wrong reflexive pronoun, count it wrong. The pronoun is not optional.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 12 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i wake up. 2. we do not get dressed. 3. get up! (tu) 4. wash your hands! (vous) 5. nous parlons -> participle 6. while studying these answers match the answer key. 1. `je me réveille.` — pronominal verbs need both the subject `je` and the matching reflexive pronoun `me`. 2. `nous ne nous habillons pas.` — **ne ... pas** wraps the reflexive pronoun plus verb: `ne nous habillons pas`. 3. `lève-toi !` — in an affirmative `tu` command, `te` becomes `toi` after the verb. 4. `lavez-vous les mains !` — affirmative `vous` command; french uses `les mains`, not a possessive adjective. 5. `parlant` — form the participle from `nous parlons`: remove the ending and add the participle ending. 6. `en étudiant` — the structure with `en` plus a participle means while studying or by studying. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **reflexive verbs**. use `je me lève.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **questions, negatives, commands**. use `elle ne se couche pas.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **body parts and reciprocal meaning**. use `je me lave les mains.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **present participle**. use `parlant` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct answer must show the named structure and keep the reflexive pronoun with the correct subject. sample answers: 1. reflexive verbs: `je me couche tôt.` — `me` matches `je`. 2. questions, negatives, commands: `ne te lève pas !` — negative command with the reflexive pronoun before the verb. 3. body parts and reciprocal meaning: `nous nous lavons les mains.` — definite article for the body part. 4. present participle: `je révise en écoutant la leçon.` — `en` plus participle shows a simultaneous action or method. mark yourself wrong if the reflexive pronoun is missing, if it stays after an infinitive, if an affirmative command uses `te` instead of `toi`, or if a body part uses a possessive adjective where the reflexive pronoun already shows the owner. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. worked example, starting from `je me lève tôt.` 1. change the subject: `nous nous levons tôt.` — both the subject and the reflexive pronoun change. 2. make it negative: `je ne me lève pas tôt.` — **ne ... pas** wraps the reflexive pronoun plus verb. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que tu te lèves tôt ?` — the reflexive pronoun stays before the verb. 4. change one meaningful detail: `je me couche tard.` check the reflexive pronoun first. a pronominal verb is incomplete if the subject changes but the reflexive pronoun does not. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.` — two reflexive pronouns with one subject; do not drop the second `me`. 2. `je vais me coucher plus tôt, parce que je me rends compte de ma fatigue.` — the reflexive pronoun stands before the infinitive `coucher`, and the idiom keeps `de`. 3. `les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.` — body part with `les`, not a possessive adjective. 4. `dépêche-toi !` — affirmative reflexive command: `te` becomes `toi` after the verb. 5. `nous nous parlons en préparant le dîner.` — `nous nous` is normal, and `en préparant` has no agreement. 6. `en étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.` — the participle comes from the verb stem and keeps the accent in `étudiant`. correct separately for vocabulary, reflexive pronoun placement, command form, and accents. a missing reflexive pronoun changes the grammar, not just the spelling. ## e. personal writing write your morning routine in eight sentences and add two simultaneous actions with en + present participle. answers vary. a model routine: 1. `je me réveille à sept heures.` 2. `je me lève tout de suite.` 3. `je me lave le visage.` 4. `je me brosse les dents.` 5. `je m'habille rapidement.` 6. `je me souviens de mes devoirs.` 7. `je révise en buvant du café.` 8. `je marche vers le cours en écoutant un dialogue.` 9. `nous nous parlons avant la classe.` 10. `je ne m'inquiète pas.` self-check: eight routine sentences plus two `en` plus present-participle phrases. mark it wrong if `me`, `te`, or `se` is missing, if a body part uses a possessive adjective, or if the participle after `en` gets adjective agreement. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **reflexive verbs.** use `me`, `te`, `se`, `nous`, `vous`, or `se` with the matching subject. example: `je me prépare.` 2. **questions, negatives, commands.** the reflexive pronoun normally stays before the verb; in affirmative commands it moves after the verb, and `te` becomes `toi`. example: `ne te lève pas !` / `lève-toi !` 3. **body parts and reciprocal meaning.** body parts usually take the definite article, and plural pronominal verbs can mean each other. example: `nous nous parlons et nous nous lavons les mains.` 4. **present participle.** form it from the `nous` stem plus the participle ending; with `en`, it often shows simultaneous action or method. example: `j'apprends en écrivant.` if your example has the right verb but the wrong reflexive pronoun, count it wrong. the pronoun is not optional. "},{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 12 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-answer-key\">Chapter 12 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me réveille.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne nous habillons pas.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lavez-vous les mains !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en étudiant</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reflexive verbs:</strong> Use me, te, se, nous, vous, se before the conjugated verb when the subject acts on itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Questions, negatives, commands:</strong> Keep the reflexive pronoun before the verb in negatives and most questions. In affirmative commands, use toi/nous/vous after the verb.</li>\n<li><strong>Body parts and reciprocal meaning:</strong> French often uses the definite article for body parts when possession is clear. Plural subjects may produce reciprocal meaning.</li>\n<li><strong>Present participle:</strong> Usually form it from the nous stem minus -ons plus -ant. It can express simultaneous action, often with en.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 12 answer key ## section a 1. `je me réveille.` 2. `nous ne nous habillons pas.` 3. `lève-toi !` 4. `lavez-vous les mains !` 5. `parlant` 6. `en étudiant` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **reflexive verbs:** use me, te, se, nous, vous, se before the conjugated verb when the subject acts on itself. - **questions, negatives, commands:** keep the reflexive pronoun before the verb in negatives and most questions. in affirmative commands, use toi/nous/vous after the verb. - **body parts and reciprocal meaning:** french often uses the definite article for body parts when possession is clear. plural subjects may produce reciprocal meaning. - **present participle:** usually form it from the nous stem minus -ons plus -ant. it can express simultaneous action, often with en. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 12 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 12 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Reflexive verbs</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Questions, negatives, commands</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Body parts and reciprocal meaning</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Present participle</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 12 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **reflexive verbs**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **questions, negatives, commands** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **body parts and reciprocal meaning**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **present participle**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":13,"slug":"chapter-13-passe-compose","title":"The Passé Composé","documents":[{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/README.md","title":"Chapter 13: The Passé Composé","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-the-pass-compos\">Chapter 13: The Passé Composé</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Form the passé composé with avoir and être.</li>\n<li>Use regular and common irregular past participles.</li>\n<li>Place negatives, questions, and adverbs.</li>\n<li>Handle basic past-participle agreement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Avoir + past participle</li>\n<li>Irregular participles</li>\n<li>Être verbs</li>\n<li>Placement and agreement</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 13: the passé composé this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - form the passé composé with avoir and être. - use regular and common irregular past participles. - place negatives, questions, and adverbs. - handle basic past-participle agreement. ## lesson sequence 1. avoir + past participle 2. irregular participles 3. être verbs 4. placement and agreement ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 13 Lessons: The Passé Composé","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-lessons-the-pass-compos\">Chapter 13 Lessons: The Passé Composé</h1>\n<p>The passé composé is the first major French past tense you can use to tell what happened, what someone did, and what changed. In this chapter, you will learn how to build it accurately, choose the correct auxiliary, place negatives and short adverbs correctly, and manage the agreement patterns that make this tense feel tricky at first but highly usable once the system becomes clear.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>explain when the passé composé is the right tense for a completed past event;</li>\n<li>form the passé composé with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> plus a past participle;</li>\n<li>recognize and produce regular and common irregular past participles;</li>\n<li>place negation, short adverbs, and common question patterns correctly around the auxiliary;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> with movement/change-of-state verbs and make the participle agree with the subject;</li>\n<li>use pronominal verbs in the passé composé and understand when agreement does and does not happen;</li>\n<li>make agreement with a preceding direct object pronoun, as in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai écrites.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already do the following comfortably:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>conjugate present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> without hesitation;</li>\n<li>recognize a past participle in English (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finished</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">seen</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">taken</code>) as a verb form, not a full tense by itself;</li>\n<li>identify direct object pronouns such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>;</li>\n<li>recognize subject agreement from earlier chapters (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigué</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatiguée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigués</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatiguées</code>).</li>\n<p>If any of those feel uncertain, review them first. The passé composé depends on all four skills at once.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test yourself with production, not recognition. Answer these five, then check the rule after each one.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Give <strong>avoir</strong> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <strong>être</strong> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles sont</code>. The auxiliary must agree with the subject before you even think about the participle.</li>\n<li>What is the past participle of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code> ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fini</code>. Regular -ir verbs change to -i in the passé composé.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les photos</code> with a direct object pronoun. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>. A plural direct object becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, and if it comes before the auxiliary it may control agreement.</li>\n<li>Write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigué</code> for a feminine plural group. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatiguées</code>. Add -e for feminine and -s for plural; both endings are usually silent.</li>\n<li>Correct the tense form: <strong>j'ai parler</strong>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai parlé</code>. After the auxiliary, French needs a past participle, not an infinitive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review Chapters 11-13 basics before continuing. This chapter stacks auxiliary choice, participle form, pronoun placement, and agreement in the same sentence.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Memorize one pattern at a time. Read every French example aloud, then cover the French and rebuild it from the English cue. When you make an agreement mistake, stop and ask yourself <strong>why</strong> the agreement happened before you continue.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-what-the-pass-compos-expresses-and-how-to-build-it-with-avoir\">Unit 1: What the passé composé expresses and how to build it with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The passé composé usually presents a past action as a completed whole: something happened, finished, or moved the story forward.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai terminé mes devoirs.</code> — I finished my homework.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a choisi un dessert.</code> — She chose a dessert.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons attendu le bus.</code> — We waited for the bus.</li>\n<p>In English, this tense may translate as <strong>did</strong>, <strong>have done</strong>, or sometimes just the simple past, depending on context.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"structure\">Structure</h3>\n<p>For most French verbs, the pattern is: <strong>subject + present tense of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> + past participle</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Part of the structure</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>subject</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td>I</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>auxiliary</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code></td>\n<td>have</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>past participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code></td>\n<td>spoken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>complete tense</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai parlé</code></td>\n<td>I spoke / I have spoken</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-parler-in-the-pass-compos\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> in the passé composé</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai parlé</code></td>\n<td>I spoke / I have spoken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu as parlé</code></td>\n<td>you spoke</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il a parlé</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle a parlé</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on a parlé</code></td>\n<td>he/she/we spoke</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons parlé</code></td>\n<td>we spoke</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez parlé</code></td>\n<td>you spoke</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils ont parlé</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles ont parlé</code></td>\n<td>they spoke</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"regular-past-participles\">Regular past participles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive type</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Example infinitive</th>\n<th>Past participle</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code></td>\n<td>replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-é</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code></td>\n<td>spoken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code></td>\n<td>replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-i</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fini</code></td>\n<td>finished</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code></td>\n<td>replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-u</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendu</code></td>\n<td>sold</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"more-model-pairs\">More model pairs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Past participle</th>\n<th>Example sentence</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chanter</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chanté</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons chanté dans la voiture.</code> — We sang in the car.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussi</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as réussi l'examen.</code> — You passed the exam.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils ont attendu devant le café.</code> — They waited in front of the café.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In normal speech, many written agreement endings are silent. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlés</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlées</code> are all pronounced the same. That means you often have to think grammatically even when your ear does not hear a difference.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> put the infinitive after the auxiliary.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*j'ai parler</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai parlé</code></li>\n<p>The second verb must be a <strong>past participle</strong>, not an infinitive.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai parlé</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>.</li>\n<li>Build three new sentences with these cues: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir / le projet</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre / la voiture</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regarder / un film</code>.</li>\n<li>Answer aloud: Which part tells you the time? Which part tells you the lexical meaning?</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons parlé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous avez parlé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles ont parlé</code> — the auxiliary changes with the subject, but the past participle <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code> stays the same because this is an <strong>avoir</strong> verb with no preceding direct object.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai fini le projet.</code> — regular -ir verbs form the participle in -i, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fini</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons vendu la voiture.</code> — regular -re verbs form the participle in -u, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendu</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a regardé un film.</code> — regular -er verbs form the participle in -é, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regarder</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regardé</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The present-tense auxiliary tells you the time frame: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>. The past participle carries the lexical meaning: speaking, finishing, selling, watching.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-high-frequency-irregular-past-participles\">Unit 2: High-frequency irregular past participles</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Many of the most useful French verbs have irregular past participles. Learn them as vocabulary chunks, the same way you memorize an article with a noun.</p>\n<h3 id=\"essential-irregular-participles\">Essential irregular participles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Past participle</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eu</code></td>\n<td>had</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bu</code></td>\n<td>drank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaître</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connu</code></td>\n<td>known / met</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">courir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">couru</code></td>\n<td>run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">croire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cru</code></td>\n<td>believed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dû</code></td>\n<td>had to / owed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code></td>\n<td>said / told</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrit</code></td>\n<td>written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">été</code></td>\n<td>been</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code></td>\n<td>done / made</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lu</code></td>\n<td>read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mis</code></td>\n<td>put</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offrir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">offert</code></td>\n<td>offered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvert</code></td>\n<td>opened</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pu</code></td>\n<td>been able to</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pris</code></td>\n<td>taken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçu</code></td>\n<td>received</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">su</code></td>\n<td>known / found out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code></td>\n<td>seen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulu</code></td>\n<td>wanted</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"learn-them-in-real-sentences\">Learn them in real sentences</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a fait du sport après le travail.</code> — He exercised after work.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai lu le message dans le train.</code> — I read the message on the train.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles ont pris le dernier métro.</code> — They took the last subway.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons écrit à notre professeur.</code> — We wrote to our teacher.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as ouvert la fenêtre trop tôt.</code> — You opened the window too early.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On a vu ce film l'année dernière.</code> — We saw that movie last year.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"useful-meaning-notes\">Useful meaning notes</h3>\n<p>Some irregular verbs change meaning depending on context.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai su la nouvelle</code> — I found out the news</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai connu ce quartier</code> — I knew / was familiar with this neighborhood</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai pu venir</code> — I was able to come</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The final consonant of many past participles is silent: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mis</code>. Do not try to pronounce every written letter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not invent a regular pattern where French has an irregular one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*j'ai prendu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*elle a ouvré</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*nous avons fais</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai pris</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle a ouvert</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons fait</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the past participle of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn these cues into full sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous / écrire / un courriel</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je / voir / tes clés</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / dire / la vérité</code>.</li>\n<li>Say three verbs from the table without looking, then use each in a complete sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Past participles: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçu</code>. These are irregular, so memorize them as complete verb facts, not as endings to calculate.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons écrit un courriel.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> has the irregular participle <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrit</code>; the auxiliary is <strong>avoir</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vu tes clés.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code>. No agreement is needed because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tes clés</code> comes after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils ont dit la vérité.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ont</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> as the auxiliary.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your three extra verbs, answers vary. A good set would be <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai lu le message.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a pris le bus.</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons fait le dîner.</code> Mark yourself wrong if you invented a regular participle such as <em>prendu</em> or <em>fais</em>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-negation-questions-and-adverb-placement-in-the-pass-compos\">Unit 3: Negation, questions, and adverb placement in the passé composé</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The auxiliary is the center of the tense. Negation and short adverbs usually attach to it, not to the past participle.</p>\n<h3 id=\"negation-pattern\">Negation pattern</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before a vowel sound) goes before the auxiliary, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> goes after it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Affirmative</th>\n<th>Negative</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai mangé.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas mangé.</code></td>\n<td>I did not eat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a fini.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'a pas fini.</code></td>\n<td>She did not finish.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons répondu.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'avons pas répondu.</code></td>\n<td>We did not answer.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"short-adverbs\">Short adverbs</h3>\n<p>Very common short adverbs often go between the auxiliary and the past participle.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>auxiliary + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a bien travaillé.</code></td>\n<td>He worked well.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>auxiliary + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjà</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as déjà vu ce musée.</code></td>\n<td>You have already seen this museum.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>auxiliary + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souvent</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons souvent parlé de ce projet.</code></td>\n<td>We often talked about this project.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Longer adverbial expressions usually come later in the sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a travaillé avec beaucoup d'énergie.</code> — She worked with a lot of energy.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai parlé très lentement.</code> — I spoke very slowly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"questions-in-the-pass-compos\">Questions in the passé composé</h3>\n<p>You can ask questions in the same three ways you already know in the present tense.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Style</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>intonation</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as fini ?</code></td>\n<td>Did you finish?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu as fini ?</code></td>\n<td>Did you finish?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inversion</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">As-tu fini ?</code></td>\n<td>Did you finish?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>More examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce que vous avez changé d'avis ?</code> — Why did you change your mind?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où ont-elles garé la voiture ?</code> — Where did they park the car?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand as-tu lu ce roman ?</code> — When did you read that novel?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In inversion, the auxiliary and subject pronoun form one sound group: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">As-tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">A-t-il</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ont-elles</code>. Read them as linked units, not as separate isolated words.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not place <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> after the past participle.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Je n'ai mangé pas.</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas mangé.</code></li>\n<p>Also avoid placing short adverbs after the participle when French normally prefers them between auxiliary and participle.</p>\n<li>more natural: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai déjà vu ce film.</code></li>\n<li>less natural for beginners: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vu déjà ce film.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Make these sentences negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a répondu.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons compris.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as écrit la carte.</code></li>\n<li>Add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjà</code>, then <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code>, to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai fait mes exercices.</code></li>\n<li>Ask a question three ways with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous / finir / le rapport</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'a pas répondu.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'</code> before the vowel sound in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> goes after the auxiliary.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'avons pas compris.</code> — the negative wraps the auxiliary <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code>, not the participle <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">compris</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu n'as pas écrit la carte.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> still stays between auxiliary and participle.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai déjà fait mes exercices.</code> — short adverbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjà</code> usually go between the auxiliary and the participle.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai bien fait mes exercices.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code> goes in the same slot: after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>, before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous avez fini le rapport ?</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous avez fini le rapport ?</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avez-vous fini le rapport ?</code> — all three ask the same yes/no question. In inversion, only the auxiliary moves before the subject pronoun.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-the-pass-compos-with-tre\">Unit 4: The passé composé with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>A smaller group of verbs uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> in the passé composé. These verbs usually express movement, arrival/departure, or a change of state.</p>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-arriver-in-the-pass-compos\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code> in the passé composé</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je suis arrivé(e)</code></td>\n<td>I arrived</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu es arrivé(e)</code></td>\n<td>you arrived</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est arrivé</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle est arrivée</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on est arrivé(s)</code></td>\n<td>he/she/we arrived</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous sommes arrivé(e)s</code></td>\n<td>we arrived</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous êtes arrivé(e)(s)</code></td>\n<td>you arrived</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils sont arrivés</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles sont arrivées</code></td>\n<td>they arrived</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-common-tre-verb-group\">The common <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> verb group</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></td>\n<td>to go</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">naître</code></td>\n<td>to be born</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code></td>\n<td>to arrive</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partir</code></td>\n<td>to leave</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">descendre</code></td>\n<td>to go down</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">passer</code></td>\n<td>to pass / spend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devenir</code></td>\n<td>to become</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rentrer</code></td>\n<td>to return home</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">entrer</code></td>\n<td>to enter</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rester</code></td>\n<td>to stay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">monter</code></td>\n<td>to go up</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">retourner</code></td>\n<td>to return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mourir</code></td>\n<td>to die</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code></td>\n<td>to go out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code></td>\n<td>to come</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tomber</code></td>\n<td>to fall</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>You will often see this group taught as a memory set rather than as a deep semantic rule. That is practical advice: memorize the common verbs as a family.</p>\n<h3 id=\"agreement-with-the-subject\">Agreement with the subject</h3>\n<p>With non-pronominal <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> verbs, the past participle agrees with the subject in gender and number.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est arrivée à huit heures.</code> — Marie arrived at eight o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils sont partis très tôt.</code> — They left very early.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes sœurs sont restées à la maison.</code> — My sisters stayed at home.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Nora sont venus ensemble.</code> — Paul and Nora came together.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"spoken-french-reminder\">Spoken French reminder</h3>\n<p>The written endings in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivés</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivées</code> are usually not pronounced differently. The grammar is visible mainly in writing, so check it deliberately.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Two frequent problems appear here:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>choosing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> for these verbs;</li>\n<li>forgetting written agreement.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*elle a arrivée</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle est arrivée</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sortir</code> into the passé composé with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est arrivé tard.</code> into a sentence about two women.</li>\n<li>Build three true or invented sentences about where people went yesterday.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes allés.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes venus.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes sortis.</code> — these are movement verbs from the <strong>être</strong> group, so the auxiliary is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code>. If the group is entirely female, write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allées</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venues</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sorties</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles sont arrivées tard.</code> — with non-pronominal <strong>être</strong> verbs, the participle agrees with the subject. Two women require feminine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivées</code>.</li>\n<li>Answers vary. Three possible sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Hier, je suis allé au marché.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes parents sont partis tôt.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nora est rentrée à minuit.</code> Each one needs <strong>être</strong> because the verb is in the movement/change group, and each participle must match its subject.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-pronominal-verbs-in-the-pass-compos\">Unit 5: Pronominal verbs in the passé composé</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>All pronominal verbs use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> in the passé composé.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lever</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me suis levé(e)</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'appeler</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle s'est appelée...</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se coucher</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous sommes couché(e)s</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-se-lever-in-the-pass-compos\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se lever</code> in the passé composé</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me suis levé(e)</code></td>\n<td>I got up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu t'es levé(e)</code></td>\n<td>you got up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il s'est levé</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle s'est levée</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on s'est levé(s)</code></td>\n<td>he/she/we got up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous sommes levé(e)s</code></td>\n<td>we got up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous vous êtes levé(e)(s)</code></td>\n<td>you got up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils se sont levés</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles se sont levées</code></td>\n<td>they got up</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"basic-reflexive-agreement\">Basic reflexive agreement</h3>\n<p>When the reflexive pronoun functions as the direct object, the past participle usually agrees.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle s'est levée tôt.</code> — She got up early.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous sommes habillés rapidement.</code> — We got dressed quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont vus à la gare.</code> — They saw each other at the station.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"when-there-is-no-agreement\">When there is <strong>no</strong> agreement</h3>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> agree the participle when the reflexive pronoun is indirect, or when another direct object comes after the verb.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Why there is no agreement</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont parlé.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler à quelqu'un</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> = indirect object</td>\n<td>They spoke to each other.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle s'est lavé les mains.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les mains</code> is the direct object; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> is indirect</td>\n<td>She washed her hands.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous sommes téléphoné hier.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphoner à quelqu'un</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> = indirect object</td>\n<td>We phoned each other yesterday.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"a-useful-comparison\">A useful comparison</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se sont regardées.</code> — They looked at one another. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> = direct object → agreement)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se sont parlé.</code> — They spoke to one another. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> = indirect object → no agreement)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me suis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu t'es</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il s'est</code> each form a tight spoken group. Practice them aloud as chunks so that the written apostrophes stop feeling intimidating.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not assume that every pronominal verb automatically agrees in every sentence. With pronominal verbs, agreement depends on the grammatical role of the reflexive pronoun.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se coucher</code> in the passé composé with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>.</li>\n<li>Decide whether agreement is needed: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se voir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se parler</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se téléphoner</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se retrouver</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle s'est lavé les mains.</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> and then with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me suis couché(e).</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous sommes couché(e)s.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se sont couchées.</code> — pronominal verbs use <strong>être</strong> in the passé composé. Add the written agreement that matches the subject when the reflexive pronoun is direct.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se voir</code> needs agreement, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> is direct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se sont vues.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se parler</code> does not agree, because the idea is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler à quelqu'un</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se sont parlé.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se téléphoner</code> does not agree, because the idea is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">téléphoner à quelqu'un</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se sont téléphoné.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se retrouver</code> usually agrees, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> is direct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se sont retrouvées.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont lavé les mains.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous sommes lavé les mains.</code> — no subject agreement here because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les mains</code> is the direct object and it comes after the verb.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-6-agreement-with-a-preceding-direct-object-pronoun\">Unit 6: Agreement with a preceding direct object pronoun</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, the past participle normally does <strong>not</strong> agree. But if a <strong>direct object pronoun</strong> comes before the auxiliary, the past participle agrees with that pronoun.</p>\n<h3 id=\"basic-contrast\">Basic contrast</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai écrit la lettre.</code> — I wrote the letter. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la lettre</code> comes after → no visible agreement trigger)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je l'ai écrite.</code> — I wrote it. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> = <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la lettre</code>, feminine singular → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrite</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pattern-table\">Pattern table</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun before the auxiliary</th>\n<th>Hidden noun</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Agreement</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je l'ai lu.</code></td>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la lettre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je l'ai écrite.</code></td>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les billets</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous les avons pris.</code></td>\n<td>masculine plural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les photos</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai prises.</code></td>\n<td>feminine plural</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"more-examples\">More examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les lettres ? Je les ai écrites hier soir.</code> — The letters? I wrote them last night.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les exercices ? Tu les as faits tout seul.</code> — The exercises? You did them by yourself.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La chanson ? Nous l'avons entendue à la radio.</code> — The song? We heard it on the radio.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les photos ? Je les ai prises pendant le voyage.</code> — The photos? I took them during the trip.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"a-pronominal-extension\">A pronominal extension</h3>\n<p>This same logic can show up with pronominal verbs when a direct object pronoun comes before the auxiliary.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les mains ? Elle se les est lavées.</code> — Her hands? She washed them.</li>\n<p>Here, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> is the direct object and it comes before the auxiliary, so the participle agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les mains</code>.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not agree with a preceding <strong>indirect</strong> object pronoun.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lui ai parlé.</code> — I spoke to him/her. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> is indirect → no agreement)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai vus.</code> — I saw them. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> is direct → agreement)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace the noun with a pronoun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vu les étudiantes.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ___ ai ___</code>.</li>\n<li>Replace the noun with a pronoun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons pris les clés.</code></li>\n<li>Decide whether agreement happens: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je leur ai écrit.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai écrites.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont parlé.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont vus.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vu les étudiantes.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai vues.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> replaces a feminine plural direct object and comes before the auxiliary, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vues</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons pris les clés.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous les avons prises.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les clés</code> is feminine plural; the preceding direct object pronoun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> triggers <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prises</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je leur ai écrit.</code> — no agreement: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> is indirect.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai écrites.</code> — agreement: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> is direct and feminine plural.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont parlé.</code> — no agreement: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> is indirect with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler à quelqu'un</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont vus.</code> — agreement: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> is direct with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir quelqu'un</code>; masculine or mixed plural gives <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vus</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>These sentences combine the major patterns of the chapter. Read each sentence aloud twice, then cover it and rebuild it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Hier, j'ai préparé le dîner et j'ai regardé un film.</code> — Yesterday, I prepared dinner and watched a movie.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est arrivée à huit heures et elle est repartie à minuit.</code> — Marie arrived at eight and left again at midnight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne nous sommes pas levés tôt ce matin.</code> — We did not get up early this morning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les photos ? Je les ai prises pendant le voyage.</code> — The photos? I took them during the trip.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as déjà lu le message, mais tu n'as pas encore répondu.</code> — You have already read the message, but you have not answered yet.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont parlé après la réunion, puis ils sont rentrés ensemble.</code> — They spoke to each other after the meeting, then they went home together.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>After you rebuild the sentences from memory, compare your version with the printed model. Do not rely on sound alone; many of the important endings are silent.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Auxiliary choice.</strong> Sentences 1, 4, and 5 use <strong>avoir</strong>; sentence 2 uses <strong>être</strong>; sentence 3 uses pronominal <strong>être</strong>; sentence 6 uses both pronominal <strong>être</strong> and non-pronominal <strong>être</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Negation and adverbs.</strong> In sentence 3, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne nous sommes pas levés</code> wraps the auxiliary with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code>. In sentence 5, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjà</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas encore</code> sit around the auxiliary-participle structure.</li>\n<li><strong>Agreement you cannot hear.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">levés</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prises</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rentrés</code> all need written endings for a reason, even when speech hides them.</li>\n<li><strong>Pronoun placement.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai prises</code> puts <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> before the auxiliary, so agreement is required. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont parlé</code> has no agreement because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> is indirect with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler à quelqu'un</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Accents and apostrophes.</strong> Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjà</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réunion</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">n'as</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai</code> exactly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Give yourself credit only when the auxiliary, participle, and any agreement reason are all correct.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle the auxiliary;</li>\n<li>underline the past participle;</li>\n<li>label the auxiliary <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>;</li>\n<li>if there is agreement, draw an arrow to the word or pronoun that controls it.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Auxiliary</th>\n<th>Past participle</th>\n<th>Agreement reason</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préparé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regardé</code></td>\n<td>no agreement; both objects follow the verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">repartie</code></td>\n<td><strong>être</strong> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie</code>, feminine singular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">levés</code></td>\n<td>pronominal <strong>être</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> is masculine or mixed plural here</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prises</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> = <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les photos</code>, feminine plural direct object before the auxiliary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">répondu</code></td>\n<td>no agreement; no preceding direct object controls the participle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rentrés</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code> has no agreement because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code> is indirect; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rentrés</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The key habit is to ask <strong>what controls the participle?</strong> With <strong>avoir</strong>, usually nothing controls it unless a direct object comes before. With non-pronominal <strong>être</strong>, the subject controls it.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one important detail such as the object, time, or destination.</li>\n<p>As you rewrite, check auxiliary choice, pronoun form, word order, and agreement.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>One worked set of rewrites. Yours may use different details, but the agreement logic must stay visible.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Hier, nous avons préparé le dîner et nous avons regardé un film.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Hier, j'ai préparé le déjeuner et j'ai regardé une série.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes amies sont arrivées à huit heures et elles sont reparties à minuit.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est arrivée à neuf heures et elle est repartie après le dîner.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne s'est pas levée tôt ce matin.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne nous sommes pas couchés tard hier soir.</code></li>\n<li>Subject/object: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les lettres ? Nous les avons écrites pendant le voyage.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les clés ? Je les ai prises avant de sortir.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check every new sentence in this order: choose <strong>avoir</strong> or <strong>être</strong>, place any pronoun before the auxiliary, then decide whether the participle needs a written ending.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using these four chapter patterns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> passé composé sentence;</li>\n<li>one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> passé composé sentence;</li>\n<li>one negative pronominal sentence;</li>\n<li>one sentence with a preceding direct object pronoun.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Avoir:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai préparé le dîner.</code> — a completed action with an object after the verb, so no agreement.</li>\n<li><strong>Être:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis allé au marché.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code> uses <strong>être</strong> and agrees with the subject; write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allée</code> if the speaker is feminine.</li>\n<li><strong>Negative pronominal:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne me suis pas couché tôt.</code> — the negative surrounds <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code>, and the reflexive pronoun stays before the auxiliary.</li>\n<li><strong>Preceding direct object:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les messages ? Je les ai lus.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> comes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lu</code> agrees with masculine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">messages</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you used an infinitive after the auxiliary, put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> after the participle, or forgot agreement controlled by <strong>être</strong> or by a preceding direct object.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-after-a-long-saturday\">Mini-dialogue: after a long Saturday</h2>\n<p><strong>Camille :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Alors, tu as passé une bonne journée ?</code> — So, did you have a good day? <strong>Nassim :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, mais elle a été très longue.</code> — Yes, but it was very long. <strong>Camille :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Qu'est-ce que tu as fait ce matin ?</code> — What did you do this morning? <strong>Nassim :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me suis levé tard, puis j'ai préparé le dîner pour ce soir.</code> — I got up late, then I prepared dinner for this evening. <strong>Camille :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et les courses ?</code> — And the shopping? <strong>Nassim :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai faites après le déjeuner.</code> — I did it after lunch. <strong>Camille :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tes amis sont arrivés à l'heure ?</code> — Did your friends arrive on time? <strong>Nassim :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, ils sont arrivés vers huit heures, et on a bien parlé jusqu'à minuit.</code> — Yes, they arrived around eight o'clock, and we talked a lot until midnight.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find one passé composé with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, and one pronominal form.</li>\n<li>Explain why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faites</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai faites</code> agrees.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils sont arrivés</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie</code> and then with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes sœurs</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On a bien parlé jusqu'à minuit.</code> into a negative sentence.</li>\n<li>Ask <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as passé une bonne journée ?</code> again using inversion.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>With être / pronominal être:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me suis levé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tes amis sont arrivés</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils sont arrivés</code>. <strong>With avoir:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu as passé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle a été</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu as fait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai préparé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai faites</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on a bien parlé</code>. The pronominal form is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me suis levé tard</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faites</code> agrees because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> refers to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les courses</code>, a feminine plural direct object placed before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>. With a noun after the verb, there would be no agreement: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai fait les courses</code>.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est arrivée vers huit heures.</code> With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes sœurs</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes sœurs sont arrivées vers huit heures.</code> The participle agrees with the subject because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code> uses <strong>être</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On n'a pas bien parlé jusqu'à minuit.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> surrounds the auxiliary <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code> stays before the participle.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">As-tu passé une bonne journée ?</code> — inversion moves only the auxiliary before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, and the hyphen is required.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-un-dimanche-qui-a-bien-commenc\">Connected reading: un dimanche qui a bien commencé</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dimanche matin, je me suis réveillé avant tout le monde, mais je ne me suis pas levé tout de suite. J'ai ouvert la fenêtre et j'ai vu un ciel très clair. Ma sœur est descendue quelques minutes plus tard, et nous avons préparé le petit déjeuner ensemble. Mes parents sont partis tôt pour le marché, alors je leur ai écrit un message. Vers dix heures, mes cousins sont arrivés sans prévenir, mais nous les avons accueillis avec plaisir. Finalement, on a passé toute la journée à parler, à manger et à rire.</code> — On Sunday morning, I woke up before everyone else, but I did not get up right away. I opened the window and saw a very clear sky. My sister came downstairs a few minutes later, and we prepared breakfast together. My parents left early for the market, so I wrote them a message. Around ten o'clock, my cousins arrived without warning, but we welcomed them gladly. In the end, we spent the whole day talking, eating, and laughing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List every passé composé form in the paragraph and sort it by auxiliary.</li>\n<li>Find one negative form and explain where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> is placed.</li>\n<li>Find one sentence with a preceding pronoun and explain whether it is direct or indirect.</li>\n<li>Change the paragraph so the narrator is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li>Write three follow-up sentences telling what happened in the evening.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Avoir forms:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai ouvert</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai vu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons préparé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je leur ai écrit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous les avons accueillis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on a passé</code>. <strong>Être forms:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je me suis réveillé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne me suis pas levé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur est descendue</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes parents sont partis</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes cousins sont arrivés</code>.</li>\n<li>Negative form: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne me suis pas levé</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> comes before the reflexive pronoun and auxiliary group, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> follows <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je leur ai écrit</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>, an indirect object pronoun, so there is no agreement. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous les avons accueillis</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, a direct object pronoun referring to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes cousins</code>; the participle agrees masculine plural.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dimanche matin, nous nous sommes réveillés avant tout le monde, mais nous ne nous sommes pas levés tout de suite. Nous avons ouvert la fenêtre et nous avons vu un ciel très clair.</code> Continue the pattern with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous avons préparé</code> if the narrator joins the breakfast preparation.</li>\n<li>Follow-up sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, nous avons rangé la cuisine. Mes cousins sont restés encore une heure. Je me suis couché très tard.</code> Each sentence moves the story forward, so the passé composé is the natural tense.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Writing an infinitive after the auxiliary: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*j'ai parler</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai parlé</code>.</li>\n<li>Using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> with common <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> surrounds the auxiliary, not the participle.</li>\n<li>Assuming that all pronominal verbs always agree automatically.</li>\n<li>Forgetting agreement after a preceding direct object pronoun: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les lettres ? Je les ai écrites.</code></li>\n<li>Agreeing after an indirect object pronoun such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>, where no agreement happens.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"the-two-auxiliaries-in-the-present-tense\">The two auxiliaries in the present tense</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">es</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">êtes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ont</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"regular-past-participles\">Regular past participles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive ending</th>\n<th>Participle ending</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-é</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-i</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fini</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-u</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendu</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-irregular-past-participles\">Common irregular past participles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Participle</th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Participle</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pris</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lu</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mettre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mis</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvrir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ouvert</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eu</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">été</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">reçu</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"quick-guide-to-agreement\">Quick guide to agreement</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Agreement?</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> + noun after verb</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai écrit la lettre.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> + preceding direct object pronoun</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je l'ai écrite.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>non-pronominal <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> verb</td>\n<td>yes, with subject</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pronominal verb with direct-object <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont vus.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pronominal verb with indirect-object <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont parlé.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pronominal verb with direct object after verb</td>\n<td>usually no</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle s'est lavé les mains.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"word-order-reminders\">Word-order reminders</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code> + auxiliary + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pas</code> + participle</li>\n<li>short adverb: auxiliary + <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjà/bien/souvent</code> + participle</li>\n<li>question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as fini ?</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu as fini ?</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">As-tu fini ?</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences telling what happened yesterday or during a recent outing. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least four passé composé forms with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>;</li>\n<li>at least two passé composé forms with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>;</li>\n<li>at least one pronominal verb in the passé composé;</li>\n<li>one negative sentence;</li>\n<li>one sentence with a short adverb such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">déjà</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souvent</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence with a preceding direct object pronoun;</li>\n<li>at least one feminine agreement in writing.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every auxiliary;</li>\n<li>underline every past participle;</li>\n<li>label each sentence <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>;</li>\n<li>check every agreement and write the reason in the margin;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any corrected sentence from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is a model paragraph that satisfies every requirement:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Hier, j'ai quitté la maison vers neuf heures. J'ai pris le bus et j'ai lu mes messages pendant le trajet. Ma sœur est arrivée au café avant moi, alors je me suis dépêché. Nous avons commandé deux cafés et nous avons bien parlé de nos projets. Je n'ai pas regardé mon téléphone pendant une heure. Les photos du menu ? Je les ai prises pour ma mère. Après le déjeuner, nous sommes allés au marché. Une vendeuse m'a donné une recette, et je l'ai gardée dans mon sac.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: the <strong>avoir</strong> forms include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai quitté</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai pris</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai lu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons commandé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons parlé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai regardé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai prises</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a donné</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai gardée</code>; the <strong>être</strong> forms are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est arrivée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis dépêché</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes allés</code>; the negative is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas regardé</code>; the short adverb is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bien</code>; the preceding direct object pronouns are <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je les ai prises</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je l'ai gardée</code>; feminine agreement appears in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prises</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gardée</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>an <strong>être</strong> verb uses <strong>avoir</strong>, as in <strong>elle a arrivée</strong>;</li>\n<li>a past participle is still an infinitive, as in <strong>j'ai prendre</strong>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> does not surround the auxiliary;</li>\n<li>a preceding direct object such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les photos</code> fails to control agreement;</li>\n<li>an indirect object pronoun such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> incorrectly triggers agreement.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 13 lessons: the passé composé the passé composé is the first major french past tense you can use to tell what happened, what someone did, and what changed. in this chapter, you will learn how to build it accurately, choose the correct auxiliary, place negatives and short adverbs correctly, and manage the agreement patterns that make this tense feel tricky at first but highly usable once the system becomes clear. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. explain when the passé composé is the right tense for a completed past event; 2. form the passé composé with `avoir` plus a past participle; 3. recognize and produce regular and common irregular past participles; 4. place negation, short adverbs, and common question patterns correctly around the auxiliary; 5. use `être` with movement/change-of-state verbs and make the participle agree with the subject; 6. use pronominal verbs in the passé composé and understand when agreement does and does not happen; 7. make agreement with a preceding direct object pronoun, as in `je les ai écrites.` ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already do the following comfortably: - conjugate present-tense `avoir` and `être` without hesitation; - recognize a past participle in english (`finished`, `seen`, `taken`) as a verb form, not a full tense by itself; - identify direct object pronouns such as `le`, `la`, `l'`, and `les`; - recognize subject agreement from earlier chapters (`fatigué`, `fatiguée`, `fatigués`, `fatiguées`). if any of those feel uncertain, review them first. the passé composé depends on all four skills at once. test yourself with production, not recognition. answer these five, then check the rule after each one. 1. give **avoir** for `nous` and **être** for `elles`. — `nous avons`, `elles sont`. the auxiliary must agree with the subject before you even think about the participle. 2. what is the past participle of `finir` ? — `fini`. regular -ir verbs change to -i in the passé composé. 3. replace `les photos` with a direct object pronoun. — `les`. a plural direct object becomes `les`, and if it comes before the auxiliary it may control agreement. 4. write `fatigué` for a feminine plural group. — `fatiguées`. add -e for feminine and -s for plural; both endings are usually silent. 5. correct the tense form: **j'ai parler**. — `j'ai parlé`. after the auxiliary, french needs a past participle, not an infinitive. if you missed two or more, review chapters 11-13 basics before continuing. this chapter stacks auxiliary choice, participle form, pronoun placement, and agreement in the same sentence. ## how to study this chapter memorize one pattern at a time. read every french example aloud, then cover the french and rebuild it from the english cue. when you make an agreement mistake, stop and ask yourself **why** the agreement happened before you continue. ## unit 1: what the passé composé expresses and how to build it with `avoir` ### core idea the passé composé usually presents a past action as a completed whole: something happened, finished, or moved the story forward. - `j'ai terminé mes devoirs.` — i finished my homework. - `elle a choisi un dessert.` — she chose a dessert. - `nous avons attendu le bus.` — we waited for the bus. in english, this tense may translate as **did**, **have done**, or sometimes just the simple past, depending on context. ### structure for most french verbs, the pattern is: **subject + present tense of `avoir` + past participle** | part of the structure | example | meaning | |---|---|---| | subject | `je` | i | | auxiliary | `ai` | have | | past participle | `parlé` | spoken | | complete tense | `j'ai parlé` | i spoke / i have spoken | ### full model: `parler` in the passé composé | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `j'ai parlé` | i spoke / i have spoken | | `tu` | `tu as parlé` | you spoke | | `il/elle/on` | `il a parlé` / `elle a parlé` / `on a parlé` | he/she/we spoke | | `nous` | `nous avons parlé` | we spoke | | `vous` | `vous avez parlé` | you spoke | | `ils/elles` | `ils ont parlé` / `elles ont parlé` | they spoke | ### regular past participles | infinitive type | rule | example infinitive | past participle | english | |---|---|---|---|---| | `-er` | replace `-er` with `-é` | `parler` | `parlé` | spoken | | regular `-ir` | replace `-ir` with `-i` | `finir` | `fini` | finished | | regular `-re` | replace `-re` with `-u` | `vendre` | `vendu` | sold | ### more model pairs | infinitive | past participle | example sentence | |---|---|---| | `chanter` | `chanté` | `nous avons chanté dans la voiture.` — we sang in the car. | | `réussir` | `réussi` | `tu as réussi l'examen.` — you passed the exam. | | `attendre` | `attendu` | `ils ont attendu devant le café.` — they waited in front of the café. | ### pronunciation note in normal speech, many written agreement endings are silent. `parlé`, `parlée`, `parlés`, and `parlées` are all pronounced the same. that means you often have to think grammatically even when your ear does not hear a difference. ### common mistake for english speakers do **not** put the infinitive after the auxiliary. - incorrect: `*j'ai parler` - correct: `j'ai parlé` the second verb must be a **past participle**, not an infinitive. ### sentence-building practice 1. change `j'ai parlé` to `nous`, `vous`, and `elles`. 2. build three new sentences with these cues: `finir / le projet`, `vendre / la voiture`, `regarder / un film`. 3. answer aloud: which part tells you the time? which part tells you the lexical meaning? 1. `nous avons parlé`, `vous avez parlé`, `elles ont parlé` — the auxiliary changes with the subject, but the past participle `parlé` stays the same because this is an **avoir** verb with no preceding direct object. 2. `j'ai fini le projet.` — regular -ir verbs form the participle in -i, so `finir` becomes `fini`. 3. `nous avons vendu la voiture.` — regular -re verbs form the participle in -u, so `vendre` becomes `vendu`. 4. `elle a regardé un film.` — regular -er verbs form the participle in -é, so `regarder` becomes `regardé`. the present-tense auxiliary tells you the time frame: `ai`, `avons`, `a`. the past participle carries the lexical meaning: speaking, finishing, selling, watching. ## unit 2: high-frequency irregular past participles ### core idea many of the most useful french verbs have irregular past participles. learn them as vocabulary chunks, the same way you memorize an article with a noun. ### essential irregular participles | infinitive | past participle | english | |---|---|---| | `avoir` | `eu` | had | | `boire` | `bu` | drank | | `connaître` | `connu` | known / met | | `courir` | `couru` | run | | `croire` | `cru` | believed | | `devoir` | `dû` | had to / owed | | `dire` | `dit` | said / told | | `écrire` | `écrit` | written | | `être` | `été` | been | | `faire` | `fait` | done / made | | `lire` | `lu` | read | | `mettre` | `mis` | put | | `offrir` | `offert` | offered | | `ouvrir` | `ouvert` | opened | | `pouvoir` | `pu` | been able to | | `prendre` | `pris` | taken | | `recevoir` | `reçu` | received | | `savoir` | `su` | known / found out | | `voir` | `vu` | seen | | `vouloir` | `voulu` | wanted | ### learn them in real sentences - `il a fait du sport après le travail.` — he exercised after work. - `j'ai lu le message dans le train.` — i read the message on the train. - `elles ont pris le dernier métro.` — they took the last subway. - `nous avons écrit à notre professeur.` — we wrote to our teacher. - `tu as ouvert la fenêtre trop tôt.` — you opened the window too early. - `on a vu ce film l'année dernière.` — we saw that movie last year. ### useful meaning notes some irregular verbs change meaning depending on context. - `j'ai su la nouvelle` — i found out the news - `j'ai connu ce quartier` — i knew / was familiar with this neighborhood - `j'ai pu venir` — i was able to come ### pronunciation note the final consonant of many past participles is silent: `lu`, `vu`, `dit`, `fait`, `mis`. do not try to pronounce every written letter. ### common mistake for english speakers do not invent a regular pattern where french has an irregular one. - incorrect: `*j'ai prendu`, `*elle a ouvré`, `*nous avons fais` - correct: `j'ai pris`, `elle a ouvert`, `nous avons fait` ### sentence-building practice 1. write the past participle of `faire`, `voir`, `dire`, `boire`, `mettre`, and `recevoir`. 2. turn these cues into full sentences: `nous / écrire / un courriel`, `je / voir / tes clés`, `ils / dire / la vérité`. 3. say three verbs from the table without looking, then use each in a complete sentence. 1. past participles: `fait`, `vu`, `dit`, `bu`, `mis`, `reçu`. these are irregular, so memorize them as complete verb facts, not as endings to calculate. 2. `nous avons écrit un courriel.` — `écrire` has the irregular participle `écrit`; the auxiliary is **avoir**. 3. `j'ai vu tes clés.` — `voir` becomes `vu`. no agreement is needed because `tes clés` comes after the verb. 4. `ils ont dit la vérité.` — `dire` becomes `dit`; `ont` agrees with `ils` as the auxiliary. for your three extra verbs, answers vary. a good set would be `j'ai lu le message.`, `elle a pris le bus.`, and `nous avons fait le dîner.` mark yourself wrong if you invented a regular participle such as *prendu* or *fais*. ## unit 3: negation, questions, and adverb placement in the passé composé ### core idea the auxiliary is the center of the tense. negation and short adverbs usually attach to it, not to the past participle. ### negation pattern `ne` (`n'` before a vowel sound) goes before the auxiliary, and `pas` goes after it. | affirmative | negative | english | |---|---|---| | `j'ai mangé.` | `je n'ai pas mangé.` | i did not eat. | | `elle a fini.` | `elle n'a pas fini.` | she did not finish. | | `nous avons répondu.` | `nous n'avons pas répondu.` | we did not answer. | ### short adverbs very common short adverbs often go between the auxiliary and the past participle. | pattern | example | english | |---|---|---| | auxiliary + `bien` + participle | `il a bien travaillé.` | he worked well. | | auxiliary + `déjà` + participle | `tu as déjà vu ce musée.` | you have already seen this museum. | | auxiliary + `souvent` + participle | `nous avons souvent parlé de ce projet.` | we often talked about this project. | longer adverbial expressions usually come later in the sentence. - `elle a travaillé avec beaucoup d'énergie.` — she worked with a lot of energy. - `j'ai parlé très lentement.` — i spoke very slowly. ### questions in the passé composé you can ask questions in the same three ways you already know in the present tense. | style | french | english | |---|---|---| | intonation | `tu as fini ?` | did you finish? | | `est-ce que` | `est-ce que tu as fini ?` | did you finish? | | inversion | `as-tu fini ?` | did you finish? | more examples: - `pourquoi est-ce que vous avez changé d'avis ?` — why did you change your mind? - `où ont-elles garé la voiture ?` — where did they park the car? - `quand as-tu lu ce roman ?` — when did you read that novel? ### pronunciation note in inversion, the auxiliary and subject pronoun form one sound group: `as-tu`, `a-t-il`, `ont-elles`. read them as linked units, not as separate isolated words. ### common mistake for english speakers do not place `pas` after the past participle. - incorrect: `*je n'ai mangé pas.` - correct: `je n'ai pas mangé.` also avoid placing short adverbs after the participle when french normally prefers them between auxiliary and participle. - more natural: `j'ai déjà vu ce film.` - less natural for beginners: `j'ai vu déjà ce film.` ### sentence-building practice 1. make these sentences negative: `elle a répondu.`, `nous avons compris.`, `tu as écrit la carte.` 2. add `déjà`, then `bien`, to `j'ai fait mes exercices.` 3. ask a question three ways with `vous / finir / le rapport`. 1. `elle n'a pas répondu.` — `ne` becomes `n'` before the vowel sound in `a`, and `pas` goes after the auxiliary. 2. `nous n'avons pas compris.` — the negative wraps the auxiliary `avons`, not the participle `compris`. 3. `tu n'as pas écrit la carte.` — `pas` still stays between auxiliary and participle. 4. `j'ai déjà fait mes exercices.` — short adverbs such as `déjà` usually go between the auxiliary and the participle. 5. `j'ai bien fait mes exercices.` — `bien` goes in the same slot: after `ai`, before `fait`. 6. `vous avez fini le rapport ?` / `est-ce que vous avez fini le rapport ?` / `avez-vous fini le rapport ?` — all three ask the same yes/no question. in inversion, only the auxiliary moves before the subject pronoun. ## unit 4: the passé composé with `être` ### core idea a smaller group of verbs uses `être` instead of `avoir` in the passé composé. these verbs usually express movement, arrival/departure, or a change of state. ### full model: `arriver` in the passé composé | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `je suis arrivé(e)` | i arrived | | `tu` | `tu es arrivé(e)` | you arrived | | `il/elle/on` | `il est arrivé` / `elle est arrivée` / `on est arrivé(s)` | he/she/we arrived | | `nous` | `nous sommes arrivé(e)s` | we arrived | | `vous` | `vous êtes arrivé(e)(s)` | you arrived | | `ils/elles` | `ils sont arrivés` / `elles sont arrivées` | they arrived | ### the common `être` verb group | verb | meaning | verb | meaning | |---|---|---|---| | `aller` | to go | `naître` | to be born | | `arriver` | to arrive | `partir` | to leave | | `descendre` | to go down | `passer` | to pass / spend | | `devenir` | to become | `rentrer` | to return home | | `entrer` | to enter | `rester` | to stay | | `monter` | to go up | `retourner` | to return | | `mourir` | to die | `sortir` | to go out | | `venir` | to come | `tomber` | to fall | you will often see this group taught as a memory set rather than as a deep semantic rule. that is practical advice: memorize the common verbs as a family. ### agreement with the subject with non-pronominal `être` verbs, the past participle agrees with the subject in gender and number. - `marie est arrivée à huit heures.` — marie arrived at eight o'clock. - `ils sont partis très tôt.` — they left very early. - `mes sœurs sont restées à la maison.` — my sisters stayed at home. - `paul et nora sont venus ensemble.` — paul and nora came together. ### spoken french reminder the written endings in `arrivé`, `arrivée`, `arrivés`, and `arrivées` are usually not pronounced differently. the grammar is visible mainly in writing, so check it deliberately. ### common mistake for english speakers two frequent problems appear here: 1. choosing `avoir` instead of `être` for these verbs; 2. forgetting written agreement. - incorrect: `*elle a arrivée` - correct: `elle est arrivée` ### sentence-building practice 1. put `aller`, `venir`, and `sortir` into the passé composé with `nous`. 2. change `il est arrivé tard.` into a sentence about two women. 3. build three true or invented sentences about where people went yesterday. 1. `nous sommes allés.`, `nous sommes venus.`, `nous sommes sortis.` — these are movement verbs from the **être** group, so the auxiliary is `sommes`. if the group is entirely female, write `allées`, `venues`, and `sorties`. 2. `elles sont arrivées tard.` — with non-pronominal **être** verbs, the participle agrees with the subject. two women require feminine plural `arrivées`. 3. answers vary. three possible sentences: `hier, je suis allé au marché.`, `mes parents sont partis tôt.`, `nora est rentrée à minuit.` each one needs **être** because the verb is in the movement/change group, and each participle must match its subject. ## unit 5: pronominal verbs in the passé composé ### core idea all pronominal verbs use `être` in the passé composé. - `se lever` -> `je me suis levé(e)` - `s'appeler` -> `elle s'est appelée...` - `se coucher` -> `nous nous sommes couché(e)s` ### full model: `se lever` in the passé composé | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `je me suis levé(e)` | i got up | | `tu` | `tu t'es levé(e)` | you got up | | `il/elle/on` | `il s'est levé` / `elle s'est levée` / `on s'est levé(s)` | he/she/we got up | | `nous` | `nous nous sommes levé(e)s` | we got up | | `vous` | `vous vous êtes levé(e)(s)` | you got up | | `ils/elles` | `ils se sont levés` / `elles se sont levées` | they got up | ### basic reflexive agreement when the reflexive pronoun functions as the direct object, the past participle usually agrees. - `elle s'est levée tôt.` — she got up early. - `nous nous sommes habillés rapidement.` — we got dressed quickly. - `ils se sont vus à la gare.` — they saw each other at the station. ### when there is **no** agreement do **not** agree the participle when the reflexive pronoun is indirect, or when another direct object comes after the verb. | sentence | why there is no agreement | english | |---|---|---| | `ils se sont parlé.` | `parler à quelqu'un`: `se` = indirect object | they spoke to each other. | | `elle s'est lavé les mains.` | `les mains` is the direct object; `se` is indirect | she washed her hands. | | `nous nous sommes téléphoné hier.` | `téléphoner à quelqu'un`: `se` = indirect object | we phoned each other yesterday. | ### a useful comparison - `elles se sont regardées.` — they looked at one another. (`se` = direct object -> agreement) - `elles se sont parlé.` — they spoke to one another. (`se` = indirect object -> no agreement) ### pronunciation note `je me suis`, `tu t'es`, and `il s'est` each form a tight spoken group. practice them aloud as chunks so that the written apostrophes stop feeling intimidating. ### common mistake for english speakers do not assume that every pronominal verb automatically agrees in every sentence. with pronominal verbs, agreement depends on the grammatical role of the reflexive pronoun. ### sentence-building practice 1. put `se coucher` in the passé composé with `je`, `nous`, and `elles`. 2. decide whether agreement is needed: `se voir`, `se parler`, `se téléphoner`, `se retrouver`. 3. rewrite `elle s'est lavé les mains.` with `ils` and then with `nous`. 1. `je me suis couché(e).`, `nous nous sommes couché(e)s.`, `elles se sont couchées.` — pronominal verbs use **être** in the passé composé. add the written agreement that matches the subject when the reflexive pronoun is direct. 2. `se voir` needs agreement, because `se` is direct: `elles se sont vues.` 3. `se parler` does not agree, because the idea is `parler à quelqu'un`: `elles se sont parlé.` 4. `se téléphoner` does not agree, because the idea is `téléphoner à quelqu'un`: `elles se sont téléphoné.` 5. `se retrouver` usually agrees, because `se` is direct: `elles se sont retrouvées.` 6. `ils se sont lavé les mains.` / `nous nous sommes lavé les mains.` — no subject agreement here because `les mains` is the direct object and it comes after the verb. ## unit 6: agreement with a preceding direct object pronoun ### core idea with `avoir`, the past participle normally does **not** agree. but if a **direct object pronoun** comes before the auxiliary, the past participle agrees with that pronoun. ### basic contrast - `j'ai écrit la lettre.` — i wrote the letter. (`la lettre` comes after -> no visible agreement trigger) - `je l'ai écrite.` — i wrote it. (`l'` = `la lettre`, feminine singular -> `écrite`) ### pattern table | pronoun before the auxiliary | hidden noun | example | agreement | |---|---|---|---| | `l'` | `le livre` | `je l'ai lu.` | masculine singular | | `l'` | `la lettre` | `je l'ai écrite.` | feminine singular | | `les` | `les billets` | `nous les avons pris.` | masculine plural | | `les` | `les photos` | `je les ai prises.` | feminine plural | ### more examples - `les lettres ? je les ai écrites hier soir.` — the letters? i wrote them last night. - `les exercices ? tu les as faits tout seul.` — the exercises? you did them by yourself. - `la chanson ? nous l'avons entendue à la radio.` — the song? we heard it on the radio. - `les photos ? je les ai prises pendant le voyage.` — the photos? i took them during the trip. ### a pronominal extension this same logic can show up with pronominal verbs when a direct object pronoun comes before the auxiliary. - `les mains ? elle se les est lavées.` — her hands? she washed them. here, `les` is the direct object and it comes before the auxiliary, so the participle agrees with `les mains`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not agree with a preceding **indirect** object pronoun. - `je lui ai parlé.` — i spoke to him/her. (`lui` is indirect -> no agreement) - `je les ai vus.` — i saw them. (`les` is direct -> agreement) ### sentence-building practice 1. replace the noun with a pronoun: `j'ai vu les étudiantes.` -> `je ___ ai ___`. 2. replace the noun with a pronoun: `nous avons pris les clés.` 3. decide whether agreement happens: `je leur ai écrit.` / `je les ai écrites.` / `ils se sont parlé.` / `ils se sont vus.` 1. `j'ai vu les étudiantes.` → `je les ai vues.` — `les` replaces a feminine plural direct object and comes before the auxiliary, so `vu` becomes `vues`. 2. `nous avons pris les clés.` → `nous les avons prises.` — `les clés` is feminine plural; the preceding direct object pronoun `les` triggers `prises`. 3. `je leur ai écrit.` — no agreement: `leur` is indirect. 4. `je les ai écrites.` — agreement: `les` is direct and feminine plural. 5. `ils se sont parlé.` — no agreement: `se` is indirect with `parler à quelqu'un`. 6. `ils se sont vus.` — agreement: `se` is direct with `voir quelqu'un`; masculine or mixed plural gives `vus`. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice these sentences combine the major patterns of the chapter. read each sentence aloud twice, then cover it and rebuild it from memory. 1. `hier, j'ai préparé le dîner et j'ai regardé un film.` — yesterday, i prepared dinner and watched a movie. 2. `marie est arrivée à huit heures et elle est repartie à minuit.` — marie arrived at eight and left again at midnight. 3. `nous ne nous sommes pas levés tôt ce matin.` — we did not get up early this morning. 4. `les photos ? je les ai prises pendant le voyage.` — the photos? i took them during the trip. 5. `tu as déjà lu le message, mais tu n'as pas encore répondu.` — you have already read the message, but you have not answered yet. 6. `ils se sont parlé après la réunion, puis ils sont rentrés ensemble.` — they spoke to each other after the meeting, then they went home together. after you rebuild the sentences from memory, compare your version with the printed model. do not rely on sound alone; many of the important endings are silent. - **auxiliary choice.** sentences 1, 4, and 5 use **avoir**; sentence 2 uses **être**; sentence 3 uses pronominal **être**; sentence 6 uses both pronominal **être** and non-pronominal **être**. - **negation and adverbs.** in sentence 3, `ne nous sommes pas levés` wraps the auxiliary with `ne...pas`. in sentence 5, `déjà` and `pas encore` sit around the auxiliary-participle structure. - **agreement you cannot hear.** `arrivée`, `levés`, `prises`, and `rentrés` all need written endings for a reason, even when speech hides them. - **pronoun placement.** `je les ai prises` puts `les` before the auxiliary, so agreement is required. `ils se sont parlé` has no agreement because `se` is indirect with `parler à quelqu'un`. - **accents and apostrophes.** check `déjà`, `réunion`, `n'as`, and `j'ai` exactly. give yourself credit only when the auxiliary, participle, and any agreement reason are all correct. ### notice and mark for each sentence: 1. circle the auxiliary; 2. underline the past participle; 3. label the auxiliary `avoir` or `être`; 4. if there is agreement, draw an arrow to the word or pronoun that controls it. | # | auxiliary | past participle | agreement reason | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `ai`, `ai` | `préparé`, `regardé` | no agreement; both objects follow the verb | | 2 | `est`, `est` | `arrivée`, `repartie` | **être** agrees with `marie`, feminine singular | | 3 | `sommes` | `levés` | pronominal **être**; `nous` is masculine or mixed plural here | | 4 | `ai` | `prises` | `les` = `les photos`, feminine plural direct object before the auxiliary | | 5 | `as`, `as` | `lu`, `répondu` | no agreement; no preceding direct object controls the participle | | 6 | `sont`, `sont` | `parlé`, `rentrés` | `parlé` has no agreement because `se` is indirect; `rentrés` agrees with `ils` | the key habit is to ask **what controls the participle?** with **avoir**, usually nothing controls it unless a direct object comes before. with non-pronominal **être**, the subject controls it. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one important detail such as the object, time, or destination. as you rewrite, check auxiliary choice, pronoun form, word order, and agreement. one worked set of rewrites. yours may use different details, but the agreement logic must stay visible. 1. subject: `hier, nous avons préparé le dîner et nous avons regardé un film.` detail: `hier, j'ai préparé le déjeuner et j'ai regardé une série.` 2. subject: `mes amies sont arrivées à huit heures et elles sont reparties à minuit.` detail: `marie est arrivée à neuf heures et elle est repartie après le dîner.` 3. subject: `elle ne s'est pas levée tôt ce matin.` detail: `nous ne nous sommes pas couchés tard hier soir.` 4. subject/object: `les lettres ? nous les avons écrites pendant le voyage.` detail: `les clés ? je les ai prises avant de sortir.` check every new sentence in this order: choose **avoir** or **être**, place any pronoun before the auxiliary, then decide whether the participle needs a written ending. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using these four chapter patterns: - one `avoir` passé composé sentence; - one `être` passé composé sentence; - one negative pronominal sentence; - one sentence with a preceding direct object pronoun. answers vary. a model set: - **avoir:** `j'ai préparé le dîner.` — a completed action with an object after the verb, so no agreement. - **être:** `je suis allé au marché.` — `aller` uses **être** and agrees with the subject; write `allée` if the speaker is feminine. - **negative pronominal:** `je ne me suis pas couché tôt.` — the negative surrounds `suis`, and the reflexive pronoun stays before the auxiliary. - **preceding direct object:** `les messages ? je les ai lus.` — `les` comes before `ai`, so `lu` agrees with masculine plural `messages`. mark yourself wrong if you used an infinitive after the auxiliary, put `pas` after the participle, or forgot agreement controlled by **être** or by a preceding direct object. ## mini-dialogue: after a long saturday **camille :** `alors, tu as passé une bonne journée ?` — so, did you have a good day? **nassim :** `oui, mais elle a été très longue.` — yes, but it was very long. **camille :** `qu'est-ce que tu as fait ce matin ?` — what did you do this morning? **nassim :** `je me suis levé tard, puis j'ai préparé le dîner pour ce soir.` — i got up late, then i prepared dinner for this evening. **camille :** `et les courses ?` — and the shopping? **nassim :** `je les ai faites après le déjeuner.` — i did it after lunch. **camille :** `tes amis sont arrivés à l'heure ?` — did your friends arrive on time? **nassim :** `oui, ils sont arrivés vers huit heures, et on a bien parlé jusqu'à minuit.` — yes, they arrived around eight o'clock, and we talked a lot until midnight. ### guided analysis 1. find one passé composé with `être`, one with `avoir`, and one pronominal form. 2. explain why `faites` in `je les ai faites` agrees. 3. rewrite `ils sont arrivés` with `marie` and then with `mes sœurs`. 4. turn `on a bien parlé jusqu'à minuit.` into a negative sentence. 5. ask `tu as passé une bonne journée ?` again using inversion. 1. **with être / pronominal être:** `je me suis levé`, `tes amis sont arrivés`, `ils sont arrivés`. **with avoir:** `tu as passé`, `elle a été`, `tu as fait`, `j'ai préparé`, `je les ai faites`, `on a bien parlé`. the pronominal form is `je me suis levé tard`. 2. `faites` agrees because `les` refers to `les courses`, a feminine plural direct object placed before `ai`. with a noun after the verb, there would be no agreement: `j'ai fait les courses`. 3. with `marie`: `marie est arrivée vers huit heures.` with `mes sœurs`: `mes sœurs sont arrivées vers huit heures.` the participle agrees with the subject because `arriver` uses **être**. 4. `on n'a pas bien parlé jusqu'à minuit.` — `ne...pas` surrounds the auxiliary `a`; `bien` stays before the participle. 5. `as-tu passé une bonne journée ?` — inversion moves only the auxiliary before `tu`, and the hyphen is required. ## connected reading: un dimanche qui a bien commencé `dimanche matin, je me suis réveillé avant tout le monde, mais je ne me suis pas levé tout de suite. j'ai ouvert la fenêtre et j'ai vu un ciel très clair. ma sœur est descendue quelques minutes plus tard, et nous avons préparé le petit déjeuner ensemble. mes parents sont partis tôt pour le marché, alors je leur ai écrit un message. vers dix heures, mes cousins sont arrivés sans prévenir, mais nous les avons accueillis avec plaisir. finalement, on a passé toute la journée à parler, à manger et à rire.` — on sunday morning, i woke up before everyone else, but i did not get up right away. i opened the window and saw a very clear sky. my sister came downstairs a few minutes later, and we prepared breakfast together. my parents left early for the market, so i wrote them a message. around ten o'clock, my cousins arrived without warning, but we welcomed them gladly. in the end, we spent the whole day talking, eating, and laughing. ### reading practice 1. list every passé composé form in the paragraph and sort it by auxiliary. 2. find one negative form and explain where `ne...pas` is placed. 3. find one sentence with a preceding pronoun and explain whether it is direct or indirect. 4. change the paragraph so the narrator is `nous` instead of `je`. 5. write three follow-up sentences telling what happened in the evening. 1. **avoir forms:** `j'ai ouvert`, `j'ai vu`, `nous avons préparé`, `je leur ai écrit`, `nous les avons accueillis`, `on a passé`. **être forms:** `je me suis réveillé`, `je ne me suis pas levé`, `ma sœur est descendue`, `mes parents sont partis`, `mes cousins sont arrivés`. 2. negative form: `je ne me suis pas levé`. `ne` comes before the reflexive pronoun and auxiliary group, and `pas` follows `suis`. 3. `je leur ai écrit` has `leur`, an indirect object pronoun, so there is no agreement. `nous les avons accueillis` has `les`, a direct object pronoun referring to `mes cousins`; the participle agrees masculine plural. 4. with `nous`: `dimanche matin, nous nous sommes réveillés avant tout le monde, mais nous ne nous sommes pas levés tout de suite. nous avons ouvert la fenêtre et nous avons vu un ciel très clair.` continue the pattern with `nous avons préparé` if the narrator joins the breakfast preparation. 5. follow-up sentences: `le soir, nous avons rangé la cuisine. mes cousins sont restés encore une heure. je me suis couché très tard.` each sentence moves the story forward, so the passé composé is the natural tense. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. writing an infinitive after the auxiliary: `*j'ai parler` instead of `j'ai parlé`. 2. using `avoir` with common `être` verbs such as `aller`, `venir`, or `arriver`. 3. forgetting that `ne...pas` surrounds the auxiliary, not the participle. 4. assuming that all pronominal verbs always agree automatically. 5. forgetting agreement after a preceding direct object pronoun: `les lettres ? je les ai écrites.` 6. agreeing after an indirect object pronoun such as `lui` or `leur`, where no agreement happens. ## chapter reference sheet ### the two auxiliaries in the present tense | pronoun | `avoir` | `être` | |---|---|---| | `je` | `ai` | `suis` | | `tu` | `as` | `es` | | `il/elle/on` | `a` | `est` | | `nous` | `avons` | `sommes` | | `vous` | `avez` | `êtes` | | `ils/elles` | `ont` | `sont` | ### regular past participles | infinitive ending | participle ending | example | |---|---|---| | `-er` | `-é` | `parler` -> `parlé` | | regular `-ir` | `-i` | `finir` -> `fini` | | regular `-re` | `-u` | `vendre` -> `vendu` | ### common irregular past participles | verb | participle | verb | participle | |---|---|---|---| | `faire` | `fait` | `voir` | `vu` | | `prendre` | `pris` | `lire` | `lu` | | `écrire` | `écrit` | `dire` | `dit` | | `boire` | `bu` | `mettre` | `mis` | | `ouvrir` | `ouvert` | `avoir` | `eu` | | `être` | `été` | `recevoir` | `reçu` | ### quick guide to agreement | situation | agreement? | example | |---|---|---| | `avoir` + noun after verb | no | `j'ai écrit la lettre.` | | `avoir` + preceding direct object pronoun | yes | `je l'ai écrite.` | | non-pronominal `être` verb | yes, with subject | `elle est arrivée.` | | pronominal verb with direct-object `se` | yes | `ils se sont vus.` | | pronominal verb with indirect-object `se` | no | `ils se sont parlé.` | | pronominal verb with direct object after verb | usually no | `elle s'est lavé les mains.` | ### word-order reminders - negative: `ne` + auxiliary + `pas` + participle - short adverb: auxiliary + `déjà/bien/souvent` + participle - question: `tu as fini ?` / `est-ce que tu as fini ?` / `as-tu fini ?` ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences telling what happened yesterday or during a recent outing. include: - at least four passé composé forms with `avoir`; - at least two passé composé forms with `être`; - at least one pronominal verb in the passé composé; - one negative sentence; - one sentence with a short adverb such as `déjà`, `bien`, or `souvent`; - one sentence with a preceding direct object pronoun; - at least one feminine agreement in writing. after writing: 1. circle every auxiliary; 2. underline every past participle; 3. label each sentence `avoir` or `être`; 4. check every agreement and write the reason in the margin; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any corrected sentence from memory. answers vary. here is a model paragraph that satisfies every requirement: `hier, j'ai quitté la maison vers neuf heures. j'ai pris le bus et j'ai lu mes messages pendant le trajet. ma sœur est arrivée au café avant moi, alors je me suis dépêché. nous avons commandé deux cafés et nous avons bien parlé de nos projets. je n'ai pas regardé mon téléphone pendant une heure. les photos du menu ? je les ai prises pour ma mère. après le déjeuner, nous sommes allés au marché. une vendeuse m'a donné une recette, et je l'ai gardée dans mon sac.` how it satisfies the brief: the **avoir** forms include `ai quitté`, `ai pris`, `ai lu`, `avons commandé`, `avons parlé`, `ai regardé`, `ai prises`, `a donné`, `ai gardée`; the **être** forms are `est arrivée`, `suis dépêché`, and `sommes allés`; the negative is `je n'ai pas regardé`; the short adverb is `bien`; the preceding direct object pronouns are `les` in `je les ai prises` and `l'` in `je l'ai gardée`; feminine agreement appears in `arrivée`, `prises`, and `gardée`. mark yourself wrong if: - an **être** verb uses **avoir**, as in **elle a arrivée**; - a past participle is still an infinitive, as in **j'ai prendre**; - `ne...pas` does not surround the auxiliary; - a preceding direct object such as `les photos` fails to control agreement; - an indirect object pronoun such as `lui` or `leur` incorrectly triggers agreement. "},{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 13 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-exercises\">Chapter 13 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Je parle.</li>\n<li>Nous finissons.</li>\n<li>Elle arrive.</li>\n<li>Ils se lèvent.</li>\n<li>I did not see it.</li>\n<li>The letters? I wrote them.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai parlé.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> is a regular -er verb, so the participle is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlé</code>, and the auxiliary is <strong>avoir</strong>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons fini.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code> forms the regular participle <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fini</code>, with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code> is an <strong>être</strong> verb, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivée</code> agrees with feminine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont levés.</code> — Pronominal verbs use <strong>être</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">levés</code> agrees with masculine or mixed plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne l'ai pas vu.</code> — The negative surrounds the auxiliary, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code> is treated as masculine singular here, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code> has no visible agreement.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les lettres ? Je les ai écrites.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> is a preceding direct object pronoun referring to feminine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les lettres</code>, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrit</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrites</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>avoir + past participle</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai parlé.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>irregular participles</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a fait du sport.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>être verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>placement and agreement</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas mangé.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct answer must show the named structure and change the model sentence's content.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Avoir + past participle:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons vendu la voiture.</code> — <strong>avoir</strong> plus regular participle <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendu</code>; no agreement because the object follows.</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular participle:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a pris le métro.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prendre</code> has the irregular participle <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pris</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Être verbs:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes amies sont parties tôt.</code> — <strong>être</strong> verb with subject agreement: feminine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parties</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Placement and agreement:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les photos ? Je les ai prises.</code> — the preceding direct object <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> refers to feminine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">photos</code>, so the participle agrees.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you used an infinitive after the auxiliary, chose <strong>avoir</strong> for an <strong>être</strong> verb, or missed required agreement.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Worked example from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée à huit heures.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils sont arrivés à huit heures.</code> — <strong>être</strong> stays the auxiliary, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivés</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'est pas arrivée à huit heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne...pas</code> surrounds the auxiliary <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce qu'elle est arrivée à huit heures ?</code> — the auxiliary remains after the subject in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est-ce que</code> form.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée à midi.</code> — tense and agreement stay the same; only the time changes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own choices, check auxiliary choice first, then pronoun placement, then agreement.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai terminé mes devoirs.</code> — The trap is the accent in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">terminé</code>; the second verb is a past participle, not an infinitive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons écrit à notre professeur.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrit</code> ends with a silent consonant, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> has an accent.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas mangé.</code> — The negative surrounds <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai</code>; do not write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai mangé pas</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est arrivée à huit heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivée</code> has feminine agreement with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à</code> appears before the time.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle s'est lavé les mains.</code> — There is no agreement on <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lavé</code> because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les mains</code> is the direct object and comes after the verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les photos ? Je les ai prises pendant le voyage.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prises</code> agrees with the preceding direct object <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, referring to feminine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">photos</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct in passes: first recover the words, then the auxiliary, then agreement, then accents and apostrophes.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a ten-sentence account of yesterday using six avoir verbs, two être verbs, one pronominal verb, and one negative.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model ten-sentence account:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Hier, j'ai préparé le petit déjeuner.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai lu mes messages dans le train.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vu ma voisine au marché.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur est arrivée vers midi.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes sortis après le déjeuner.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me suis couché tard.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas regardé la télévision.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons mangé au restaurant.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai écrit à un ami.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai fini mes exercices avant minuit.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: at least six <strong>avoir</strong> forms, two <strong>être</strong> forms, one pronominal verb, and one negative. Agreement is required with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est arrivée</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes sortis</code>; change the endings if your subjects are different.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Avoir + past participle.</strong> Most verbs use present-tense <strong>avoir</strong> plus a past participle. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai fini le travail.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Irregular participles.</strong> Common verbs must be memorized: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pris</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrit</code>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a pris le bus.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Être verbs.</strong> Movement/change verbs and pronominal verbs use <strong>être</strong>, and the participle usually agrees with the subject. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils sont partis tôt.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Placement and agreement.</strong> Negation surrounds the auxiliary, and a preceding direct object pronoun can trigger agreement. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les clés ? Je les ai prises.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you can explain the rule but cannot create a new French example, review the unit before moving on.</p></div></details>","search":"# chapter 13 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. je parle. 2. nous finissons. 3. elle arrive. 4. ils se lèvent. 5. i did not see it. 6. the letters? i wrote them. 1. `j'ai parlé.` — `parler` is a regular -er verb, so the participle is `parlé`, and the auxiliary is **avoir**. 2. `nous avons fini.` — `finir` forms the regular participle `fini`, with `avons` for `nous`. 3. `elle est arrivée.` — `arriver` is an **être** verb, and `arrivée` agrees with feminine singular `elle`. 4. `ils se sont levés.` — pronominal verbs use **être**; `levés` agrees with masculine or mixed plural `ils`. 5. `je ne l'ai pas vu.` — the negative surrounds the auxiliary, and `l'` before `ai` is treated as masculine singular here, so `vu` has no visible agreement. 6. `les lettres ? je les ai écrites.` — `les` is a preceding direct object pronoun referring to feminine plural `les lettres`, so `écrit` becomes `écrites`. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **avoir + past participle**. use `j'ai parlé.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **irregular participles**. use `il a fait du sport.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **être verbs**. use `elle est arrivée.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **placement and agreement**. use `je n'ai pas mangé.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct answer must show the named structure and change the model sentence's content. 1. **avoir + past participle:** `nous avons vendu la voiture.` — **avoir** plus regular participle `vendu`; no agreement because the object follows. 2. **irregular participle:** `elle a pris le métro.` — `prendre` has the irregular participle `pris`. 3. **être verbs:** `mes amies sont parties tôt.` — **être** verb with subject agreement: feminine plural `parties`. 4. **placement and agreement:** `les photos ? je les ai prises.` — the preceding direct object `les` refers to feminine plural `photos`, so the participle agrees. mark yourself wrong if you used an infinitive after the auxiliary, chose **avoir** for an **être** verb, or missed required agreement. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. answers vary. worked example from `elle est arrivée à huit heures.` 1. change the subject: `ils sont arrivés à huit heures.` — **être** stays the auxiliary, and `arrivés` agrees with `ils`. 2. make it negative: `elle n'est pas arrivée à huit heures.` — `ne...pas` surrounds the auxiliary `est`. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce qu'elle est arrivée à huit heures ?` — the auxiliary remains after the subject in the `est-ce que` form. 4. change one meaningful detail: `elle est arrivée à midi.` — tense and agreement stay the same; only the time changes. for your own choices, check auxiliary choice first, then pronoun placement, then agreement. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `j'ai terminé mes devoirs.` — the trap is the accent in `terminé`; the second verb is a past participle, not an infinitive. 2. `nous avons écrit à notre professeur.` — `écrit` ends with a silent consonant, and `à` has an accent. 3. `je n'ai pas mangé.` — the negative surrounds `ai`; do not write `je n'ai mangé pas`. 4. `marie est arrivée à huit heures.` — `arrivée` has feminine agreement with `marie`, and `à` appears before the time. 5. `elle s'est lavé les mains.` — there is no agreement on `lavé` because `les mains` is the direct object and comes after the verb. 6. `les photos ? je les ai prises pendant le voyage.` — `prises` agrees with the preceding direct object `les`, referring to feminine plural `photos`. correct in passes: first recover the words, then the auxiliary, then agreement, then accents and apostrophes. ## e. personal writing write a ten-sentence account of yesterday using six avoir verbs, two être verbs, one pronominal verb, and one negative. answers vary. a model ten-sentence account: 1. `hier, j'ai préparé le petit déjeuner.` 2. `j'ai lu mes messages dans le train.` 3. `j'ai vu ma voisine au marché.` 4. `ma sœur est arrivée vers midi.` 5. `nous sommes sortis après le déjeuner.` 6. `je me suis couché tard.` 7. `je n'ai pas regardé la télévision.` 8. `nous avons mangé au restaurant.` 9. `j'ai écrit à un ami.` 10. `j'ai fini mes exercices avant minuit.` self-check: at least six **avoir** forms, two **être** forms, one pronominal verb, and one negative. agreement is required with `est arrivée` and `sommes sortis`; change the endings if your subjects are different. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **avoir + past participle.** most verbs use present-tense **avoir** plus a past participle. example: `j'ai fini le travail.` 2. **irregular participles.** common verbs must be memorized: `fait`, `pris`, `vu`, `lu`, `écrit`. example: `elle a pris le bus.` 3. **être verbs.** movement/change verbs and pronominal verbs use **être**, and the participle usually agrees with the subject. example: `ils sont partis tôt.` 4. **placement and agreement.** negation surrounds the auxiliary, and a preceding direct object pronoun can trigger agreement. example: `les clés ? je les ai prises.` if you can explain the rule but cannot create a new french example, review the unit before moving on. "},{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 13 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-answer-key\">Chapter 13 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai parlé.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons fini.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont levés.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne l'ai pas vu.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les lettres ? Je les ai écrites.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Avoir + past participle:</strong> Most verbs use present-tense avoir plus a past participle. Regular endings are -é for -er, -i for regular -ir, and -u for regular -re.</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular participles:</strong> Learn frequent forms as verb chunks: faire/fait, prendre/pris, voir/vu, lire/lu, écrire/écrit, boire/bu, avoir/eu, être/été.</li>\n<li><strong>Être verbs:</strong> A smaller movement/change group and pronominal verbs use être. The participle generally agrees with the subject.</li>\n<li><strong>Placement and agreement:</strong> Negation surrounds the auxiliary. Short adverbs often follow it. A preceding direct-object pronoun may trigger agreement with an avoir verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 13 answer key ## section a 1. `j'ai parlé.` 2. `nous avons fini.` 3. `elle est arrivée.` 4. `ils se sont levés.` 5. `je ne l'ai pas vu.` 6. `les lettres ? je les ai écrites.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **avoir + past participle:** most verbs use present-tense avoir plus a past participle. regular endings are -é for -er, -i for regular -ir, and -u for regular -re. - **irregular participles:** learn frequent forms as verb chunks: faire/fait, prendre/pris, voir/vu, lire/lu, écrire/écrit, boire/bu, avoir/eu, être/été. - **être verbs:** a smaller movement/change group and pronominal verbs use être. the participle generally agrees with the subject. - **placement and agreement:** negation surrounds the auxiliary. short adverbs often follow it. a preceding direct-object pronoun may trigger agreement with an avoir verb. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 13 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 13 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Avoir + past participle</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Irregular participles</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Être verbs</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Placement and agreement</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 13 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **avoir + past participle**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **irregular participles** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **être verbs**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **placement and agreement**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":14,"slug":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns","title":"The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns","documents":[{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/README.md","title":"Chapter 14: The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-the-imparfait-past-narration-and-double-object-pronouns\">Chapter 14: The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Form and use the imparfait.</li>\n<li>Contrast background and completed events.</li>\n<li>Combine passé composé and imparfait.</li>\n<li>Order double object pronouns.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forming the imparfait</li>\n<li>Choosing the past tense</li>\n<li>Past narration</li>\n<li>Double object pronouns</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 14: the imparfait, past narration, and double object pronouns this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - form and use the imparfait. - contrast background and completed events. - combine passé composé and imparfait. - order double object pronouns. ## lesson sequence 1. forming the imparfait 2. choosing the past tense 3. past narration 4. double object pronouns ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 14 Lessons: The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-lessons-the-imparfait-past-narration-and-double-object-pronouns\">Chapter 14 Lessons: The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This chapter gives you the second major past tense and shows you how French speakers combine it with the passé composé to tell stories naturally. You will also learn how to stack two object pronouns in the correct order, first in ordinary sentences and then in affirmative commands, where the order changes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>form the imparfait from the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> stem and recognize the special stem <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ét-</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>;</li>\n<li>use the imparfait for background description, habitual past action, ongoing action, and past mental or emotional states;</li>\n<li>contrast the imparfait with the passé composé in narration;</li>\n<li>interpret and produce <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lorsque</code> sentences that combine both past tenses;</li>\n<li>place two object pronouns before a conjugated verb in the correct order;</li>\n<li>change that pronoun order correctly in affirmative imperative commands.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>form the passé composé with both <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> from Chapter 13;</li>\n<li>recognize direct object pronouns such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> and indirect object pronouns such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>;</li>\n<li>distinguish subject agreement from object pronoun agreement in writing;</li>\n<li>conjugate present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> forms accurately, because the imparfait stem comes from them.</li>\n<p>If Chapter 13 still feels unstable, review it first. The narration contrast in this chapter only makes sense when your passé composé is automatic.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test the prerequisites by producing forms, not by nodding at the list.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code> in the passé composé with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles sont arrivées</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code> uses <strong>être</strong>, and the participle agrees feminine plural.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à Paul</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je donne le livre à Paul</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code> Direct <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> comes before indirect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>.</li>\n<li>Why does <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les ai vues</code> have -es ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> is a preceding direct object referring to something feminine plural. With <strong>avoir</strong>, that is the agreement trigger.</li>\n<li>Give the present <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code>. The imparfait stem will be finiss-.</li>\n<li>Build the imparfait of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous étions</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> uses the special stem <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ét-</code>, not the present form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review passé composé agreement and object pronouns before continuing. This chapter assumes you can see what each pronoun is doing.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Do not memorize the imparfait as a translation such as “was doing.” Learn the form, then ask what the sentence is doing in context: setting the scene, describing a habit, or showing an action already in progress when something else happened.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-forming-the-imparfait\">Unit 1: Forming the imparfait</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Take the present-tense <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form, remove <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code>, and add the endings <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-aient</code>. The one major irregular verb is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, which uses the stem <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ét-</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-endings\">The endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>Pronunciation note</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code></td>\n<td>sounds like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eh</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code></td>\n<td>sounds like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eh</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ait</code></td>\n<td>sounds like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eh</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ions</code></td>\n<td>distinct spoken ending</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iez</code></td>\n<td>distinct spoken ending</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-aient</code></td>\n<td>sounds like <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">eh</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"building-the-stem\">Building the stem</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Present <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form</th>\n<th>Stem</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parl-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finiss-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu finissais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous vendons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vend-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle vendait</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous sommes</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ét-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous étions</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-parler\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code></td>\n<td>I was speaking / I used to speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu parlais</code></td>\n<td>you were speaking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parlait</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle parlait</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on parlait</code></td>\n<td>he/she/we was speaking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlions</code></td>\n<td>we were speaking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous parliez</code></td>\n<td>you were speaking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlaient</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles parlaient</code></td>\n<td>they were speaking</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-tre\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'étais</code></td>\n<td>I was</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu étais</code></td>\n<td>you were</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il était</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle était</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on était</code></td>\n<td>he/she/we was</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous étions</code></td>\n<td>we were</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous étiez</code></td>\n<td>you were</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils étaient</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles étaient</code></td>\n<td>they were</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu parlais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parlait</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlaient</code> all end with the same sound. In speech, the contrast usually appears only in the subject pronoun. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlions</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous parliez</code> sound clearly different.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not build the imparfait from the infinitive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je parlerais</code> for “I was speaking”</li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code></li>\n<p>Also remember that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlerais</code> is a <strong>conditional</strong> form you will study in Chapter 15.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>.</li>\n<li>Write the full six forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> in the imparfait from memory.</li>\n<li>Say aloud which three persons share the same spoken ending in most verbs.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je finissais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles finissaient</code> — the present <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code>, so the imparfait stem is finiss-.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vendais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous vendions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles vendaient</code> — the present <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous vendons</code>, so the stem is vend-.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'étais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu étais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il était</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous étions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous étiez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils étaient</code> — <strong>être</strong> is the major irregular stem: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ét-</code>.</li>\n<li>In most verbs, the rows <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il / elle / on</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / elles</code> share the same spoken ending. The spelling changes, so writing forces you to check the subject.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-what-the-imparfait-means\">Unit 2: What the imparfait means</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The imparfait usually presents a past action or state as ongoing, repeated, descriptive, or unfinished from the speaker's viewpoint.</p>\n<h3 id=\"four-high-frequency-uses\">Four high-frequency uses</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>background description</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait et les rues étaient presque vides.</code></td>\n<td>It was raining and the streets were almost empty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>habitual past</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitions à Lyon à cette époque.</code></td>\n<td>We lived / used to live in Lyon at that time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ongoing action</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais dans ma chambre.</code></td>\n<td>I was reading in my room.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>state of mind or body</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle avait peur, mais elle voulait essayer.</code></td>\n<td>She was afraid, but she wanted to try.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"more-examples\">More examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand j'étais petit, je passais tous mes étés chez mes grands-parents.</code> — When I was little, I spent all my summers at my grandparents' house.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le professeur expliquait la règle très calmement.</code> — The teacher was explaining the rule very calmly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous pensions déjà à nos vacances.</code> — We were already thinking about our vacation.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous aviez souvent faim après le cours.</code> — You were often hungry after class.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"a-useful-translation-warning\">A useful translation warning</h3>\n<p>The imparfait can mean <strong>was doing</strong>, <strong>used to do</strong>, or simply <strong>did</strong> in the sense of an ongoing background situation. Do not force one English label onto every example.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Because several spoken forms sound identical, time words and context matter a lot: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque été</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souvent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">à cette époque</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pendant que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> often help you hear the intended meaning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>English speakers often choose the passé composé for every past sentence. French uses the imparfait when the point is the setting, the habit, or the ongoing state rather than the completed event.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Turn these cues into full imparfait sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il pleuvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous habiter à Paris</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je lire dans ma chambre</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles être fatiguées</code>.</li>\n<li>Write one sentence about a childhood habit and one about yesterday's weather.</li>\n<li>Decide which meaning is best in English: “was doing,” “used to do,” or simple descriptive past.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait.</code> — weather is background description, so the imparfait fits.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitions à Paris.</code> — living somewhere at that time is an ongoing past situation, not a single completed event.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais dans ma chambre.</code> — the action is in progress, so use the imparfait.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles étaient fatiguées.</code> — a physical state in the past normally uses the imparfait; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatiguées</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles</code>.</li>\n<li>Childhood habit: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand j'étais petit, je jouais souvent dehors.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">souvent</code> and the childhood frame make it habitual.</li>\n<li>Yesterday's weather: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Hier, il faisait froid.</code> — weather sets the scene, so the imparfait is natural.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The best English labels are: weather/background = simple descriptive past, habits = used to do, and actions in progress = was doing.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-imparfait-and-pass-compos-in-narration\">Unit 3: Imparfait and passé composé in narration</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The imparfait usually sets the scene; the passé composé usually tells what happened next, what interrupted the scene, or what moved the story forward.</p>\n<h3 id=\"contrast-table\">Contrast table</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Function in the story</th>\n<th>Preferred tense</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>background, weather, description</td>\n<td>imparfait</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La salle était calme.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>repeated or habitual situation</td>\n<td>imparfait</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous arrivions toujours tôt.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>completed event</td>\n<td>passé composé</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le concert a commencé.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>interruption or new event</td>\n<td>passé composé</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le téléphone a sonné.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"scene-event-pattern\">Scene + event pattern</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais dans ma chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.</code> — I was reading in my room when the telephone rang.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitions à Paris lorsque nous avons rencontré Léa.</code> — We were living in Paris when we met Léa.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faisait beau, puis un vent froid est arrivé.</code> — The weather was nice, then a cold wind arrived.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pendant que je préparais le café, les invités sont entrés.</code> — While I was making coffee, the guests came in.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"two-questions-to-ask-yourself\">Two questions to ask yourself</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Is this the background or the main event?</li>\n<li>Is the action presented as ongoing/habitual, or as completed and bounded?</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"a-mini-story\">A mini-story</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le restaurant était plein et tout le monde parlait assez fort. Je cherchais une table près de la fenêtre quand un serveur m'a appelé. Il m'a montré une place tranquille, et je me suis assis tout de suite.</code> — The restaurant was full and everyone was speaking rather loudly. I was looking for a table near the window when a waiter called me. He showed me a quiet seat, and I sat down right away.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use the imparfait just because the English sentence contains “was.” In a sentence such as “The concert was great,” the choice still depends on what the speaker wants to emphasize. Context, not English wording, decides the tense.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Complete the pair: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dormais quand...</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous marchions lorsque...</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faisait froid, puis...</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le téléphone a sonné</code> as background information and rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais</code> as the interrupting event.</li>\n<li>Write a three-sentence story using at least one imparfait and two passé composé forms.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dormais quand le téléphone a sonné.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dormais</code> is the ongoing background; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a sonné</code> is the completed interruption.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous marchions lorsque la pluie a commencé.</code> — walking was already in progress; the rain beginning moves the story forward.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faisait froid, puis nous sommes rentrés.</code> — weather is scene-setting; going home is the completed event.</li>\n<li>Background version: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le téléphone sonnait dans le salon.</code> — now the ringing is treated as ongoing background noise.</li>\n<li>Interrupting-event version: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai lu la note quand Marie est entrée.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai lu</code> presents the reading as a completed event.</li>\n<li>Three-sentence story: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La rue était calme. Je cherchais mon téléphone quand Paul m'a appelé. Nous avons parlé pendant dix minutes.</code> Check that the scene uses the imparfait and the new events use the passé composé.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-double-object-pronouns-before-a-conjugated-verb\">Unit 4: Double object pronouns before a conjugated verb</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>When a sentence has two object pronouns, French uses a fixed order. The whole pronoun block comes before the conjugated verb; in compound tenses, that means before the auxiliary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"standard-order-before-the-verb\">Standard order before the verb</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Position 1</th>\n<th>Position 2</th>\n<th>Position 3</th>\n<th>Position 4</th>\n<th>Position 5</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"reading-the-order-as-a-frame\">Reading the order as a frame</h3>\n<p>Think of it as a lane system:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>first lane: people close to the verb (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">se</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>)</li>\n<li>second lane: direct objects (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>)</li>\n<li>third lane: indirect objects (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>)</li>\n<li>fourth and fifth lanes: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code>, then <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code> — I give it to him/her.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous les leur montrions.</code> — We were showing them to them.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle m'y conduit.</code> — She takes me there.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils t'en parlent souvent.</code> — They often talk to you about it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul voulait les billets, alors je les lui ai donnés.</code> — Paul wanted the tickets, so I gave them to him.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"with-the-pass-compos\">With the passé composé</h3>\n<p>Pronouns still come before the auxiliary.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les lui ai donnés.</code> — I gave them to him.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous l'y avons mise.</code> — We put it there.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In fast speech, these clusters become one rhythm group: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je-le-lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous-les-leur</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je-les-lui-ai</code>. Practice slowly first, then speed up without dropping the order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>English allows more flexible object order in paraphrase, but French pronoun order is fixed. Do not translate word for word from English.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Je lui le donne.</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace the nouns with pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je donne le livre à Marie.</code></li>\n<li>Replace the nouns with pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous montrions les photos aux voisins.</code></li>\n<li>Put the pronouns into a compound tense: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as envoyé la lettre à tes parents.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je donne le livre à Marie.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> is the direct object, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> is the indirect object; direct object pronouns come before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous montrions les photos aux voisins.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous les leur montrions.</code> — the whole pronoun group goes before the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as envoyé la lettre à tes parents.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu la leur as envoyée.</code> — pronouns go before the auxiliary in a compound tense. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> is a preceding direct object referring to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la lettre</code>, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyé</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyée</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-double-pronouns-with-the-affirmative-imperative\">Unit 5: Double pronouns with the affirmative imperative</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Affirmative commands use a different order: the pronouns come <strong>after</strong> the verb, joined by hyphens. In addition, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code> become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"order-after-an-affirmative-imperative\">Order after an affirmative imperative</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>direct object</th>\n<th>indirect object / stressed form</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>command form</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-examples\">Core examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code> — Give it to me!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montrez-les-lui !</code> — Show them to him/her!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mets-y la chaise !</code> — Put the chair there!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mets-y-en un peu !</code> — Put a little of it there!</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"compare-ordinary-order-and-imperative-order\">Compare ordinary order and imperative order</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Ordinary statement</th>\n<th>Affirmative command</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu me le donnes.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code></td>\n<td>Give it to me!</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous le leur montrez.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montrez-le-leur !</code></td>\n<td>Show it to them!</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu y en mets.</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mets-y-en !</code></td>\n<td>Put some there!</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not keep the ordinary pre-verb order after an affirmative imperative.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Le-moi donne !</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code></li>\n<p>Also remember the form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toi</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">te</code>.</p>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Change these statements into commands: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu me le passes.</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous les lui envoyez.</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu y en mets.</code></li>\n<li>Change these commands back into ordinary statements.</li>\n<li>Write three classroom or household commands using double pronouns.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu me le passes.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Passe-le-moi !</code> — in an affirmative command, pronouns go after the verb and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous les lui envoyez.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Envoyez-les-lui !</code> — after an affirmative imperative, the direct object comes before the indirect object.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu y en mets.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mets-y-en !</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code> comes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> after the command.</li>\n<li>Back to statements: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu me le passes.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous les lui envoyez.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu y en mets.</code> The ordinary sentence order returns to pronouns before the verb.</li>\n<li>Sample commands: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-les-moi !</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montre-le-lui !</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mets-y-en !</code> Mark yourself wrong if you wrote the ordinary pre-verb order after the command.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read these aloud twice, then cover them and rebuild them from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait et les rues étaient presque vides.</code> — It was raining and the streets were almost empty.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais dans ma chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.</code> — I was reading in my room when the telephone rang.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitions à Paris lorsque nous avons rencontré Léa.</code> — We were living in Paris when we met Léa.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul voulait les billets, alors je les lui ai donnés.</code> — Paul wanted the tickets, so I gave them to him.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La serveuse nous apportait toujours du thé, mais ce soir-là elle nous a apporté du café.</code> — The waitress always used to bring us tea, but that evening she brought us coffee.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Prends-les-moi tout de suite et mets-les-y !</code> — Take them for me right now and put them there!</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>When you rewrite from memory, check the contrast between what was going on and what happened. Also check whether pronouns stayed in the correct order.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Imparfait endings.</strong> Sentences 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 contain forms such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pleuvait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habitions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulait</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apportait</code>. Several sound alike, so the subject decides the spelling.</li>\n<li><strong>Passé composé events.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a sonné</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons rencontré</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai donnés</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a apporté</code> move the story forward as completed events.</li>\n<li><strong>Double pronouns.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je les lui ai donnés</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> before the auxiliary. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Prends-les-moi</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets-les-y</code> put pronouns after an affirmative command.</li>\n<li><strong>Agreement.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donnés</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les billets</code>, masculine plural, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> is a preceding direct object. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apporté</code> does not agree with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> is indirect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A correct copy is not enough: be able to explain why each tense and pronoun order was chosen.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every verb in the past tense or command form;</li>\n<li>label each past tense as imparfait or passé composé;</li>\n<li>box any pronoun cluster and identify the order.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Verb forms</th>\n<th>Labels</th>\n<th>Pronoun cluster</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pleuvait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étaient</code></td>\n<td>imparfait, imparfait</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a sonné</code></td>\n<td>imparfait, passé composé</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habitions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons rencontré</code></td>\n<td>imparfait, passé composé</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai donnés</code></td>\n<td>imparfait, passé composé</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les lui</code> = direct object before indirect object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">apportait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a apporté</code></td>\n<td>imparfait, passé composé</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> is an indirect object pronoun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Prends</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets</code></td>\n<td>affirmative commands</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les-moi</code>, then <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les-y</code> after the command</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Sentence 4 is the key double-pronoun model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> comes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, and because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> refers to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les billets</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donnés</code> has masculine plural agreement.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one detail such as the location, object, or weather.</li>\n<p>Then rewrite sentence 6 as an ordinary statement instead of a command.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>One worked set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il neigeait et les rues étaient presque vides.</code> Subject/detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La place était calme et les rues étaient presque vides.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous lisions dans notre chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais dans le salon quand Marie est entrée.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle habitait à Paris lorsqu'elle a rencontré Léa.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitions à Lyon lorsque nous avons rencontré Samir.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Nora voulaient les billets, alors je les leur ai donnés.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul voulait les clés, alors je les lui ai données.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sentence 6 as an ordinary statement: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu me les prends tout de suite et tu les y mets.</code> In ordinary statements, pronouns return before the verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me les</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">prends</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les y</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mets</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one background description in the imparfait;</li>\n<li>one interruption sentence with both past tenses;</li>\n<li>one sentence with two object pronouns before the verb;</li>\n<li>one affirmative command with two pronouns.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Background description: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La cuisine était calme et la fenêtre était ouverte.</code> — description takes the imparfait.</li>\n<li>Interruption: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préparais le café quand mon frère est arrivé.</code> — ongoing action in the imparfait, completed arrival in the passé composé.</li>\n<li>Two pronouns before the verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je les lui montre demain.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> direct object comes before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> indirect object.</li>\n<li>Affirmative command: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montre-les-lui !</code> — after a positive command, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if a sudden event is in the imparfait, if background description is in the passé composé without a reason, or if you write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui les</code> before the verb.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-after-the-museum-visit\">Mini-dialogue: after the museum visit</h2>\n<p><strong>Élise :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Alors, comment était la visite ?</code> — So, how was the visit? <strong>Romain :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le musée était calme au début, et peu de gens regardaient les tableaux.</code> — The museum was quiet at first, and few people were looking at the paintings. <strong>Élise :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et puis ?</code> — And then? <strong>Romain :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais une explication quand le guide m'a appelé.</code> — I was reading an explanation when the guide called me. <strong>Élise :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi ?</code> — Why? <strong>Romain :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parce qu'il avait des billets supplémentaires, et il me les a donnés.</code> — Because he had extra tickets, and he gave them to me. <strong>Élise :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu les as montrés à Clara ?</code> — Did you show them to Clara? <strong>Romain :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, et je les lui ai passés tout de suite.</code> — Yes, and I handed them to her right away.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find two imparfait forms and explain why the imparfait fits.</li>\n<li>Find two passé composé forms and explain what event each one advances.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je les lui ai passés</code> with a present-tense version.</li>\n<li>Turn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il me les a donnés</code> into an affirmative imperative addressed to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the first answer as if the museum had been crowded and noisy.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Imparfait forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">était</code> describes the visit; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">regardaient</code> describes what people were doing; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisais</code> is an action in progress; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avait</code> gives background possession. The imparfait fits because these are scene-setting or ongoing.</li>\n<li>Passé composé forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m'a appelé</code> is the event that interrupts the reading; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a donnés</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">as montrés</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai passés</code> are completed actions with the tickets.</li>\n<li>Present tense: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je les lui passe</code>. The pronoun order stays <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> then <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, before the conjugated verb.</li>\n<li>Command to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-les-moi !</code> In an affirmative imperative, the pronouns come after the verb; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>.</li>\n<li>Crowded/noisy version: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le musée était plein au début, et beaucoup de gens parlaient fort et regardaient les tableaux.</code> Description stays in the imparfait because it sets the scene.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-une-soir-e-qui-a-chang-de-rythme\">Connected reading: une soirée qui a changé de rythme</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Chaque vendredi, nous dînions chez ma tante et nous restions longtemps à table. Un soir, tout semblait normal: les enfants jouaient dans le salon, les adultes parlaient de la semaine, et la radio passait doucement en arrière-plan. Je coupais le pain quand la lumière s'est éteinte dans tout l'appartement. Ma tante a cherché des bougies, mon oncle nous en a apporté, et mes cousins les lui ont allumées avec beaucoup de soin. Après quelques minutes, l'électricité est revenue, mais la soirée n'avait plus le même calme.</code> — Every Friday, we used to have dinner at my aunt's house and stay at the table for a long time. One evening, everything seemed normal: the children were playing in the living room, the adults were talking about the week, and the radio was playing softly in the background. I was cutting the bread when the lights went out in the whole apartment. My aunt looked for candles, my uncle brought us some, and my cousins lit them for her very carefully. After a few minutes, the electricity came back, but the evening no longer had the same calm.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List the imparfait forms and explain the role each one plays.</li>\n<li>List the passé composé forms and explain which event each one introduces.</li>\n<li>Find two sentences with object pronouns and identify whether the pronouns are direct or indirect.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> in the first sentence and rewrite it correctly.</li>\n<li>Write three new sentences continuing the story after the electricity returned.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Imparfait forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dînions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">restions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">semblait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jouaient</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlaient</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">passait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">coupais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avait</code>. They describe habits, background, or an action already in progress.</li>\n<li>Passé composé forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s'est éteinte</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a cherché</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a apporté</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ont allumées</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est revenue</code>. Each one introduces a new event in the sequence.</li>\n<li>Object pronouns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous en a apporté</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> as the indirect recipient and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code> for an indefinite quantity of candles. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les lui ont allumées</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> as the direct object and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> as the indirect object; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allumées</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, referring to feminine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bougies</code>.</li>\n<li>First sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Chaque vendredi, je dînais chez ma tante et je restais longtemps à table.</code> The habitual past remains imparfait.</li>\n<li>Continuation: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons ri dans le noir pendant quelques minutes. Ma tante racontait une histoire quand la lumière est revenue encore une fois. Ensuite, mes cousins nous ont montré les bougies et nous les leur avons rendues.</code> The new events use passé composé; the ongoing storytelling uses imparfait.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Building the imparfait from the infinitive instead of the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> stem.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the irregular stem <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ét-</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>.</li>\n<li>Using the passé composé for background weather, description, or habitual action when the imparfait is needed.</li>\n<li>Using the imparfait for the sudden event that interrupts the scene.</li>\n<li>Reversing the order of double object pronouns before the verb: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je lui le donne</code>.</li>\n<li>Keeping the normal pronoun order after an affirmative imperative instead of changing to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"imparfait-endings\">Imparfait endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ait</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ions</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-aient</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"building-the-imparfait\">Building the imparfait</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>start with the present <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>remove <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>add imparfait endings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>special case</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ét-</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"narration-quick-guide\">Narration quick guide</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>If the sentence does this...</th>\n<th>Use...</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>describes weather, people, setting</td>\n<td>imparfait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>expresses habit or repeated action</td>\n<td>imparfait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>shows action already in progress</td>\n<td>imparfait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tells the new event that happened</td>\n<td>passé composé</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"double-pronoun-order-before-the-verb\">Double pronoun order before the verb</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n<th>5</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me/te/se/nous/vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le/la/les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui/leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"double-pronoun-order-in-the-affirmative-imperative\">Double pronoun order in the affirmative imperative</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>after the verb</th>\n<th>after the verb</th>\n<th>after the verb</th>\n<th>after the verb</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le/la/les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi/toi/lui/nous/vous/leur</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">y</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a memorable past scene. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least four imparfait forms;</li>\n<li>at least three passé composé forms;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lorsque</code> combining the two past tenses;</li>\n<li>one habitual past sentence;</li>\n<li>one sentence with two object pronouns before the verb;</li>\n<li>one affirmative imperative with pronouns, addressed to a real or imagined person.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every past-tense verb and label it <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">IMP</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">PC</code>;</li>\n<li>underline the sentence that sets the scene;</li>\n<li>box any pronoun cluster and check the order;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any sentence whose tense choice was wrong.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is a model paragraph that meets the checklist:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce soir-là, la gare était pleine et les voyageurs semblaient nerveux. Il pleuvait très fort, et nous attendions depuis une heure. Quand le train est enfin arrivé, tout le monde a couru vers le quai. Ma sœur cherchait nos billets, mais je les lui avais déjà donnés. Un employé nous a crié : « Montrez-les-moi ! » Nous avons trouvé nos places, et le wagon était presque vide. D'habitude, je lisais pendant les trajets, mais ce soir-là j'ai dormi tout de suite.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: the imparfait forms include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">était</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">semblaient</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pleuvait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cherchait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">était</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisais</code>; the passé composé forms include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est arrivé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a couru</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a crié</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons trouvé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ai dormi</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand le train est enfin arrivé</code> combines the tenses in narration; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">D'habitude, je lisais</code> is habitual past; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je les lui avais déjà donnés</code> has two object pronouns before the auxiliary; and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montrez-les-moi !</code> is an affirmative imperative with pronouns.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>background weather or description is treated as a completed event without a reason;</li>\n<li>the interrupting event after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> stays in the imparfait;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> appears after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code> before a verb;</li>\n<li>an affirmative command keeps ordinary pre-verb pronoun order;</li>\n<li>a preceding direct object pronoun fails to trigger agreement where it should.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 14 lessons: the imparfait, past narration, and double object pronouns this chapter gives you the second major past tense and shows you how french speakers combine it with the passé composé to tell stories naturally. you will also learn how to stack two object pronouns in the correct order, first in ordinary sentences and then in affirmative commands, where the order changes. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. form the imparfait from the `nous` stem and recognize the special stem `ét-` for `être`; 2. use the imparfait for background description, habitual past action, ongoing action, and past mental or emotional states; 3. contrast the imparfait with the passé composé in narration; 4. interpret and produce `quand` and `lorsque` sentences that combine both past tenses; 5. place two object pronouns before a conjugated verb in the correct order; 6. change that pronoun order correctly in affirmative imperative commands. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - form the passé composé with both `avoir` and `être` from chapter 13; - recognize direct object pronouns such as `le`, `la`, `les` and indirect object pronouns such as `lui`, `leur`; - distinguish subject agreement from object pronoun agreement in writing; - conjugate present-tense `nous` forms accurately, because the imparfait stem comes from them. if chapter 13 still feels unstable, review it first. the narration contrast in this chapter only makes sense when your passé composé is automatic. test the prerequisites by producing forms, not by nodding at the list. 1. put `arriver` in the passé composé with `elles`. — `elles sont arrivées`. `arriver` uses **être**, and the participle agrees feminine plural. 2. replace `le livre` and `à paul` in `je donne le livre à paul`. — `je le lui donne.` direct `le` comes before indirect `lui`. 3. why does `je les ai vues` have -es ? — `les` is a preceding direct object referring to something feminine plural. with **avoir**, that is the agreement trigger. 4. give the present `nous` form of `finir`. — `nous finissons`. the imparfait stem will be finiss-. 5. build the imparfait of `être` with `nous`. — `nous étions`. `être` uses the special stem `ét-`, not the present form `sommes`. if you missed two or more, review passé composé agreement and object pronouns before continuing. this chapter assumes you can see what each pronoun is doing. ## how to study this chapter do not memorize the imparfait as a translation such as “was doing.” learn the form, then ask what the sentence is doing in context: setting the scene, describing a habit, or showing an action already in progress when something else happened. ## unit 1: forming the imparfait ### core idea take the present-tense `nous` form, remove `-ons`, and add the endings `-ais`, `-ais`, `-ait`, `-ions`, `-iez`, `-aient`. the one major irregular verb is `être`, which uses the stem `ét-`. ### the endings | pronoun | ending | pronunciation note | |---|---|---| | `je` | `-ais` | sounds like `eh` | | `tu` | `-ais` | sounds like `eh` | | `il/elle/on` | `-ait` | sounds like `eh` | | `nous` | `-ions` | distinct spoken ending | | `vous` | `-iez` | distinct spoken ending | | `ils/elles` | `-aient` | sounds like `eh` | ### building the stem | verb | present `nous` form | stem | example | |---|---|---|---| | `parler` | `nous parlons` | `parl-` | `je parlais` | | `finir` | `nous finissons` | `finiss-` | `tu finissais` | | `vendre` | `nous vendons` | `vend-` | `elle vendait` | | `être` | `nous sommes` | `ét-` | `nous étions` | ### full model: `parler` | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `je parlais` | i was speaking / i used to speak | | `tu` | `tu parlais` | you were speaking | | `il/elle/on` | `il parlait` / `elle parlait` / `on parlait` | he/she/we was speaking | | `nous` | `nous parlions` | we were speaking | | `vous` | `vous parliez` | you were speaking | | `ils/elles` | `ils parlaient` / `elles parlaient` | they were speaking | ### full model: `être` | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `j'étais` | i was | | `tu` | `tu étais` | you were | | `il/elle/on` | `il était` / `elle était` / `on était` | he/she/we was | | `nous` | `nous étions` | we were | | `vous` | `vous étiez` | you were | | `ils/elles` | `ils étaient` / `elles étaient` | they were | ### pronunciation note `je parlais`, `tu parlais`, `il parlait`, and `ils parlaient` all end with the same sound. in speech, the contrast usually appears only in the subject pronoun. `nous parlions` and `vous parliez` sound clearly different. ### common mistake for english speakers do not build the imparfait from the infinitive. - incorrect: `*je parlerais` for “i was speaking” - correct: `je parlais` also remember that `je parlerais` is a **conditional** form you will study in chapter 15. ### sentence-building practice 1. conjugate `finir` and `vendre` for `je`, `nous`, and `elles`. 2. write the full six forms of `être` in the imparfait from memory. 3. say aloud which three persons share the same spoken ending in most verbs. 1. `je finissais`, `nous finissions`, `elles finissaient` — the present `nous` form is `nous finissons`, so the imparfait stem is finiss-. 2. `je vendais`, `nous vendions`, `elles vendaient` — the present `nous` form is `nous vendons`, so the stem is vend-. 3. `j'étais`, `tu étais`, `il était`, `nous étions`, `vous étiez`, `ils étaient` — **être** is the major irregular stem: `ét-`. 4. in most verbs, the rows `je`, `tu`, `il / elle / on`, and `ils / elles` share the same spoken ending. the spelling changes, so writing forces you to check the subject. ## unit 2: what the imparfait means ### core idea the imparfait usually presents a past action or state as ongoing, repeated, descriptive, or unfinished from the speaker's viewpoint. ### four high-frequency uses | use | french example | english | |---|---|---| | background description | `il pleuvait et les rues étaient presque vides.` | it was raining and the streets were almost empty. | | habitual past | `nous habitions à lyon à cette époque.` | we lived / used to live in lyon at that time. | | ongoing action | `je lisais dans ma chambre.` | i was reading in my room. | | state of mind or body | `elle avait peur, mais elle voulait essayer.` | she was afraid, but she wanted to try. | ### more examples - `quand j'étais petit, je passais tous mes étés chez mes grands-parents.` — when i was little, i spent all my summers at my grandparents' house. - `le professeur expliquait la règle très calmement.` — the teacher was explaining the rule very calmly. - `nous pensions déjà à nos vacances.` — we were already thinking about our vacation. - `vous aviez souvent faim après le cours.` — you were often hungry after class. ### a useful translation warning the imparfait can mean **was doing**, **used to do**, or simply **did** in the sense of an ongoing background situation. do not force one english label onto every example. ### pronunciation note because several spoken forms sound identical, time words and context matter a lot: `chaque été`, `souvent`, `à cette époque`, `pendant que`, and `quand` often help you hear the intended meaning. ### common mistake for english speakers english speakers often choose the passé composé for every past sentence. french uses the imparfait when the point is the setting, the habit, or the ongoing state rather than the completed event. ### sentence-building practice 1. turn these cues into full imparfait sentences: `il pleuvoir`, `nous habiter à paris`, `je lire dans ma chambre`, `elles être fatiguées`. 2. write one sentence about a childhood habit and one about yesterday's weather. 3. decide which meaning is best in english: “was doing,” “used to do,” or simple descriptive past. 1. `il pleuvait.` — weather is background description, so the imparfait fits. 2. `nous habitions à paris.` — living somewhere at that time is an ongoing past situation, not a single completed event. 3. `je lisais dans ma chambre.` — the action is in progress, so use the imparfait. 4. `elles étaient fatiguées.` — a physical state in the past normally uses the imparfait; `fatiguées` agrees with `elles`. 5. childhood habit: `quand j'étais petit, je jouais souvent dehors.` — `souvent` and the childhood frame make it habitual. 6. yesterday's weather: `hier, il faisait froid.` — weather sets the scene, so the imparfait is natural. the best english labels are: weather/background = simple descriptive past, habits = used to do, and actions in progress = was doing. ## unit 3: imparfait and passé composé in narration ### core idea the imparfait usually sets the scene; the passé composé usually tells what happened next, what interrupted the scene, or what moved the story forward. ### contrast table | function in the story | preferred tense | example | |---|---|---| | background, weather, description | imparfait | `la salle était calme.` | | repeated or habitual situation | imparfait | `nous arrivions toujours tôt.` | | completed event | passé composé | `le concert a commencé.` | | interruption or new event | passé composé | `le téléphone a sonné.` | ### scene + event pattern - `je lisais dans ma chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.` — i was reading in my room when the telephone rang. - `nous habitions à paris lorsque nous avons rencontré léa.` — we were living in paris when we met léa. - `il faisait beau, puis un vent froid est arrivé.` — the weather was nice, then a cold wind arrived. - `pendant que je préparais le café, les invités sont entrés.` — while i was making coffee, the guests came in. ### two questions to ask yourself 1. is this the background or the main event? 2. is the action presented as ongoing/habitual, or as completed and bounded? ### a mini-story `le restaurant était plein et tout le monde parlait assez fort. je cherchais une table près de la fenêtre quand un serveur m'a appelé. il m'a montré une place tranquille, et je me suis assis tout de suite.` — the restaurant was full and everyone was speaking rather loudly. i was looking for a table near the window when a waiter called me. he showed me a quiet seat, and i sat down right away. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use the imparfait just because the english sentence contains “was.” in a sentence such as “the concert was great,” the choice still depends on what the speaker wants to emphasize. context, not english wording, decides the tense. ### sentence-building practice 1. complete the pair: `je dormais quand...`; `nous marchions lorsque...`; `il faisait froid, puis...`. 2. rewrite `le téléphone a sonné` as background information and rewrite `je lisais` as the interrupting event. 3. write a three-sentence story using at least one imparfait and two passé composé forms. 1. `je dormais quand le téléphone a sonné.` — `dormais` is the ongoing background; `a sonné` is the completed interruption. 2. `nous marchions lorsque la pluie a commencé.` — walking was already in progress; the rain beginning moves the story forward. 3. `il faisait froid, puis nous sommes rentrés.` — weather is scene-setting; going home is the completed event. 4. background version: `le téléphone sonnait dans le salon.` — now the ringing is treated as ongoing background noise. 5. interrupting-event version: `j'ai lu la note quand marie est entrée.` — `j'ai lu` presents the reading as a completed event. 6. three-sentence story: `la rue était calme. je cherchais mon téléphone quand paul m'a appelé. nous avons parlé pendant dix minutes.` check that the scene uses the imparfait and the new events use the passé composé. ## unit 4: double object pronouns before a conjugated verb ### core idea when a sentence has two object pronouns, french uses a fixed order. the whole pronoun block comes before the conjugated verb; in compound tenses, that means before the auxiliary. ### standard order before the verb | position 1 | position 2 | position 3 | position 4 | position 5 | |---|---|---|---|---| | `me`, `te`, `se`, `nous`, `vous` | `le`, `la`, `les` | `lui`, `leur` | `y` | `en` | ### reading the order as a frame think of it as a lane system: - first lane: people close to the verb (`me`, `te`, `se`, `nous`, `vous`) - second lane: direct objects (`le`, `la`, `les`) - third lane: indirect objects (`lui`, `leur`) - fourth and fifth lanes: `y`, then `en` ### examples - `je le lui donne.` — i give it to him/her. - `nous les leur montrions.` — we were showing them to them. - `elle m'y conduit.` — she takes me there. - `ils t'en parlent souvent.` — they often talk to you about it. - `paul voulait les billets, alors je les lui ai donnés.` — paul wanted the tickets, so i gave them to him. ### with the passé composé pronouns still come before the auxiliary. - `je les lui ai donnés.` — i gave them to him. - `nous l'y avons mise.` — we put it there. ### pronunciation note in fast speech, these clusters become one rhythm group: `je-le-lui`, `nous-les-leur`, `je-les-lui-ai`. practice slowly first, then speed up without dropping the order. ### common mistake for english speakers english allows more flexible object order in paraphrase, but french pronoun order is fixed. do not translate word for word from english. - incorrect: `*je lui le donne.` - correct: `je le lui donne.` ### sentence-building practice 1. replace the nouns with pronouns: `je donne le livre à marie.` 2. replace the nouns with pronouns: `nous montrions les photos aux voisins.` 3. put the pronouns into a compound tense: `tu as envoyé la lettre à tes parents.` 1. `je donne le livre à marie.` → `je le lui donne.` — `le` is the direct object, and `lui` is the indirect object; direct object pronouns come before `lui` / `leur`. 2. `nous montrions les photos aux voisins.` → `nous les leur montrions.` — the whole pronoun group goes before the conjugated verb. 3. `tu as envoyé la lettre à tes parents.` → `tu la leur as envoyée.` — pronouns go before the auxiliary in a compound tense. `la` is a preceding direct object referring to `la lettre`, so `envoyé` becomes `envoyée`. ## unit 5: double pronouns with the affirmative imperative ### core idea affirmative commands use a different order: the pronouns come **after** the verb, joined by hyphens. in addition, `me` and `te` become `moi` and `toi`. ### order after an affirmative imperative | verb | direct object | indirect object / stressed form | `y` | `en` | |---|---|---|---|---| | command form | `le`, `la`, `les` | `moi`, `toi`, `lui`, `nous`, `vous`, `leur` | `y` | `en` | ### core examples - `donne-le-moi !` — give it to me! - `montrez-les-lui !` — show them to him/her! - `mets-y la chaise !` — put the chair there! - `mets-y-en un peu !` — put a little of it there! ### compare ordinary order and imperative order | ordinary statement | affirmative command | english | |---|---|---| | `tu me le donnes.` | `donne-le-moi !` | give it to me! | | `vous le leur montrez.` | `montrez-le-leur !` | show it to them! | | `tu y en mets.` | `mets-y-en !` | put some there! | ### common mistake for english speakers do not keep the ordinary pre-verb order after an affirmative imperative. - incorrect: `*le-moi donne !` - correct: `donne-le-moi !` also remember the form `moi` / `toi`, not `me` / `te`. ### sentence-building practice 1. change these statements into commands: `tu me le passes.`; `vous les lui envoyez.`; `tu y en mets.` 2. change these commands back into ordinary statements. 3. write three classroom or household commands using double pronouns. 1. `tu me le passes.` → `passe-le-moi !` — in an affirmative command, pronouns go after the verb and `me` becomes `moi`. 2. `vous les lui envoyez.` → `envoyez-les-lui !` — after an affirmative imperative, the direct object comes before the indirect object. 3. `tu y en mets.` → `mets-y-en !` — `y` comes before `en` after the command. 4. back to statements: `tu me le passes.`, `vous les lui envoyez.`, `tu y en mets.` the ordinary sentence order returns to pronouns before the verb. 5. sample commands: `donnez-les-moi !`, `montre-le-lui !`, `mets-y-en !` mark yourself wrong if you wrote the ordinary pre-verb order after the command. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice read these aloud twice, then cover them and rebuild them from memory. 1. `il pleuvait et les rues étaient presque vides.` — it was raining and the streets were almost empty. 2. `je lisais dans ma chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.` — i was reading in my room when the telephone rang. 3. `nous habitions à paris lorsque nous avons rencontré léa.` — we were living in paris when we met léa. 4. `paul voulait les billets, alors je les lui ai donnés.` — paul wanted the tickets, so i gave them to him. 5. `la serveuse nous apportait toujours du thé, mais ce soir-là elle nous a apporté du café.` — the waitress always used to bring us tea, but that evening she brought us coffee. 6. `prends-les-moi tout de suite et mets-les-y !` — take them for me right now and put them there! when you rewrite from memory, check the contrast between what was going on and what happened. also check whether pronouns stayed in the correct order. - **imparfait endings.** sentences 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 contain forms such as `pleuvait`, `lisais`, `habitions`, `voulait`, and `apportait`. several sound alike, so the subject decides the spelling. - **passé composé events.** `a sonné`, `avons rencontré`, `ai donnés`, and `a apporté` move the story forward as completed events. - **double pronouns.** `je les lui ai donnés` has `les` before `lui` before the auxiliary. `prends-les-moi` and `mets-les-y` put pronouns after an affirmative command. - **agreement.** `donnés` agrees with `les billets`, masculine plural, because `les` is a preceding direct object. `apporté` does not agree with `nous`, because `nous` is indirect. a correct copy is not enough: be able to explain why each tense and pronoun order was chosen. ### notice and mark for each sentence: 1. circle every verb in the past tense or command form; 2. label each past tense as imparfait or passé composé; 3. box any pronoun cluster and identify the order. | # | verb forms | labels | pronoun cluster | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `pleuvait`, `étaient` | imparfait, imparfait | — | | 2 | `lisais`, `a sonné` | imparfait, passé composé | — | | 3 | `habitions`, `avons rencontré` | imparfait, passé composé | — | | 4 | `voulait`, `ai donnés` | imparfait, passé composé | `les lui` = direct object before indirect object | | 5 | `apportait`, `a apporté` | imparfait, passé composé | `nous` is an indirect object pronoun | | 6 | `prends`, `mets` | affirmative commands | `les-moi`, then `les-y` after the command | sentence 4 is the key double-pronoun model: `les` comes before `lui`, and because `les` refers to `les billets`, `donnés` has masculine plural agreement. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one detail such as the location, object, or weather. then rewrite sentence 6 as an ordinary statement instead of a command. one worked set: 1. detail: `il neigeait et les rues étaient presque vides.` subject/detail: `la place était calme et les rues étaient presque vides.` 2. subject: `nous lisions dans notre chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.` detail: `je lisais dans le salon quand marie est entrée.` 3. subject: `elle habitait à paris lorsqu'elle a rencontré léa.` detail: `nous habitions à lyon lorsque nous avons rencontré samir.` 4. subject: `paul et nora voulaient les billets, alors je les leur ai donnés.` detail: `paul voulait les clés, alors je les lui ai données.` sentence 6 as an ordinary statement: `tu me les prends tout de suite et tu les y mets.` in ordinary statements, pronouns return before the verb: `me les` before `prends`, and `les y` before `mets`. ### make it personal write four new sentences: - one background description in the imparfait; - one interruption sentence with both past tenses; - one sentence with two object pronouns before the verb; - one affirmative command with two pronouns. answers vary. a model set: 1. background description: `la cuisine était calme et la fenêtre était ouverte.` — description takes the imparfait. 2. interruption: `je préparais le café quand mon frère est arrivé.` — ongoing action in the imparfait, completed arrival in the passé composé. 3. two pronouns before the verb: `je les lui montre demain.` — `les` direct object comes before `lui` indirect object. 4. affirmative command: `montre-les-lui !` — after a positive command, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens. mark yourself wrong if a sudden event is in the imparfait, if background description is in the passé composé without a reason, or if you write `lui les` before the verb. ## mini-dialogue: after the museum visit **élise :** `alors, comment était la visite ?` — so, how was the visit? **romain :** `le musée était calme au début, et peu de gens regardaient les tableaux.` — the museum was quiet at first, and few people were looking at the paintings. **élise :** `et puis ?` — and then? **romain :** `je lisais une explication quand le guide m'a appelé.` — i was reading an explanation when the guide called me. **élise :** `pourquoi ?` — why? **romain :** `parce qu'il avait des billets supplémentaires, et il me les a donnés.` — because he had extra tickets, and he gave them to me. **élise :** `tu les as montrés à clara ?` — did you show them to clara? **romain :** `oui, et je les lui ai passés tout de suite.` — yes, and i handed them to her right away. ### guided analysis 1. find two imparfait forms and explain why the imparfait fits. 2. find two passé composé forms and explain what event each one advances. 3. replace `je les lui ai passés` with a present-tense version. 4. turn `il me les a donnés` into an affirmative imperative addressed to `tu`. 5. rewrite the first answer as if the museum had been crowded and noisy. 1. imparfait forms: `était` describes the visit; `regardaient` describes what people were doing; `lisais` is an action in progress; `avait` gives background possession. the imparfait fits because these are scene-setting or ongoing. 2. passé composé forms: `m'a appelé` is the event that interrupts the reading; `a donnés`, `as montrés`, and `ai passés` are completed actions with the tickets. 3. present tense: `je les lui passe`. the pronoun order stays `les` then `lui`, before the conjugated verb. 4. command to `tu`: `donne-les-moi !` in an affirmative imperative, the pronouns come after the verb; `me` becomes `moi`. 5. crowded/noisy version: `le musée était plein au début, et beaucoup de gens parlaient fort et regardaient les tableaux.` description stays in the imparfait because it sets the scene. ## connected reading: une soirée qui a changé de rythme `chaque vendredi, nous dînions chez ma tante et nous restions longtemps à table. un soir, tout semblait normal: les enfants jouaient dans le salon, les adultes parlaient de la semaine, et la radio passait doucement en arrière-plan. je coupais le pain quand la lumière s'est éteinte dans tout l'appartement. ma tante a cherché des bougies, mon oncle nous en a apporté, et mes cousins les lui ont allumées avec beaucoup de soin. après quelques minutes, l'électricité est revenue, mais la soirée n'avait plus le même calme.` — every friday, we used to have dinner at my aunt's house and stay at the table for a long time. one evening, everything seemed normal: the children were playing in the living room, the adults were talking about the week, and the radio was playing softly in the background. i was cutting the bread when the lights went out in the whole apartment. my aunt looked for candles, my uncle brought us some, and my cousins lit them for her very carefully. after a few minutes, the electricity came back, but the evening no longer had the same calm. ### reading practice 1. list the imparfait forms and explain the role each one plays. 2. list the passé composé forms and explain which event each one introduces. 3. find two sentences with object pronouns and identify whether the pronouns are direct or indirect. 4. change `nous` to `je` in the first sentence and rewrite it correctly. 5. write three new sentences continuing the story after the electricity returned. 1. imparfait forms: `dînions`, `restions`, `semblait`, `jouaient`, `parlaient`, `passait`, `coupais`, `avait`. they describe habits, background, or an action already in progress. 2. passé composé forms: `s'est éteinte`, `a cherché`, `a apporté`, `ont allumées`, `est revenue`. each one introduces a new event in the sequence. 3. object pronouns: `nous en a apporté` has `nous` as the indirect recipient and `en` for an indefinite quantity of candles. `les lui ont allumées` has `les` as the direct object and `lui` as the indirect object; `allumées` agrees with `les`, referring to feminine plural `bougies`. 4. first sentence with `je`: `chaque vendredi, je dînais chez ma tante et je restais longtemps à table.` the habitual past remains imparfait. 5. continuation: `nous avons ri dans le noir pendant quelques minutes. ma tante racontait une histoire quand la lumière est revenue encore une fois. ensuite, mes cousins nous ont montré les bougies et nous les leur avons rendues.` the new events use passé composé; the ongoing storytelling uses imparfait. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. building the imparfait from the infinitive instead of the `nous` stem. 2. forgetting the irregular stem `ét-` for `être`. 3. using the passé composé for background weather, description, or habitual action when the imparfait is needed. 4. using the imparfait for the sudden event that interrupts the scene. 5. reversing the order of double object pronouns before the verb: `*je lui le donne`. 6. keeping the normal pronoun order after an affirmative imperative instead of changing to `donne-le-moi !` ## chapter reference sheet ### imparfait endings | pronoun | ending | |---|---| | `je` | `-ais` | | `tu` | `-ais` | | `il/elle/on` | `-ait` | | `nous` | `-ions` | | `vous` | `-iez` | | `ils/elles` | `-aient` | ### building the imparfait | step | action | |---|---| | 1 | start with the present `nous` form | | 2 | remove `-ons` | | 3 | add imparfait endings | | special case | `être` uses `ét-` | ### narration quick guide | if the sentence does this... | use... | |---|---| | describes weather, people, setting | imparfait | | expresses habit or repeated action | imparfait | | shows action already in progress | imparfait | | tells the new event that happened | passé composé | ### double pronoun order before the verb | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | |---|---|---|---|---| | `me/te/se/nous/vous` | `le/la/les` | `lui/leur` | `y` | `en` | ### double pronoun order in the affirmative imperative | after the verb | after the verb | after the verb | after the verb | |---|---|---|---| | `le/la/les` | `moi/toi/lui/nous/vous/leur` | `y` | `en` | ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about a memorable past scene. include: - at least four imparfait forms; - at least three passé composé forms; - one sentence with `quand` or `lorsque` combining the two past tenses; - one habitual past sentence; - one sentence with two object pronouns before the verb; - one affirmative imperative with pronouns, addressed to a real or imagined person. after writing: 1. circle every past-tense verb and label it `imp` or `pc`; 2. underline the sentence that sets the scene; 3. box any pronoun cluster and check the order; 4. read the paragraph aloud twice; 5. rewrite any sentence whose tense choice was wrong. answers vary. here is a model paragraph that meets the checklist: `ce soir-là, la gare était pleine et les voyageurs semblaient nerveux. il pleuvait très fort, et nous attendions depuis une heure. quand le train est enfin arrivé, tout le monde a couru vers le quai. ma sœur cherchait nos billets, mais je les lui avais déjà donnés. un employé nous a crié : « montrez-les-moi ! » nous avons trouvé nos places, et le wagon était presque vide. d'habitude, je lisais pendant les trajets, mais ce soir-là j'ai dormi tout de suite.` how it satisfies the brief: the imparfait forms include `était`, `semblaient`, `pleuvait`, `attendions`, `cherchait`, `était`, `lisais`; the passé composé forms include `est arrivé`, `a couru`, `a crié`, `avons trouvé`, `ai dormi`; `quand le train est enfin arrivé` combines the tenses in narration; `d'habitude, je lisais` is habitual past; `je les lui avais déjà donnés` has two object pronouns before the auxiliary; and `montrez-les-moi !` is an affirmative imperative with pronouns. mark yourself wrong if: - background weather or description is treated as a completed event without a reason; - the interrupting event after `quand` stays in the imparfait; - `le`, `la`, or `les` appears after `lui` or `leur` before a verb; - an affirmative command keeps ordinary pre-verb pronoun order; - a preceding direct object pronoun fails to trigger agreement where it should. "},{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 14 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-exercises\">Chapter 14 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>nous parlons → imparfait, je</li>\n<li>nous finissons → imparfait, ils</li>\n<li>It was raining.</li>\n<li>I was reading when he arrived.</li>\n<li>Je donne le livre à Paul.</li>\n<li>Give it to me!</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code> — Start from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code>, remove -ons, add -ais.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils finissaient</code> — Start from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code>, so the stem is finiss-, then add -aient.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait.</code> — Weather/background description normally uses the imparfait.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais quand il est arrivé.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisais</code> is the ongoing action; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est arrivé</code> is the completed interruption.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> is the direct object and comes before indirect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-le-moi !</code> — In an affirmative command, pronouns follow the verb; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">me</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">moi</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>forming the imparfait</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>choosing the past tense</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>past narration</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dormais quand Marie a appelé.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>double object pronouns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct answer must clearly show the named structure and change the model sentence's content.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Forming the imparfait:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous vendions des livres.</code> — from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous vendons</code>, stem vend-, ending -ions.</li>\n<li><strong>Choosing the past tense:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La salle était calme.</code> — description/background takes the imparfait.</li>\n<li><strong>Past narration:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je marchais quand la pluie a commencé.</code> — ongoing scene in the imparfait, new event in the passé composé.</li>\n<li><strong>Double object pronouns:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous les lui envoyons.</code> — direct <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> comes before indirect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you used a conditional form such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlerais</code> for the imparfait, or if you reversed <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le / la / les</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui / leur</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Worked example from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais quand le téléphone a sonné.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous lisions quand le téléphone a sonné.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisais</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisions</code> for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne lisais pas quand le téléphone a sonné.</code> — the negative wraps the imparfait verb.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que tu lisais quand le téléphone a sonné ?</code> — the past-tense contrast stays the same.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais quand Marie est entrée.</code> — the interruption is now a different passé composé event.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own sentence, ask: which part is background or ongoing, and which part is the completed event?</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait et les rues étaient presque vides.</code> — Both verbs are imparfait; the silent endings make subject agreement easy to miss.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais dans ma chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.</code> — The contrast is the trap: ongoing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lisais</code>, sudden event <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">a sonné</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitions à Paris lorsque nous avons rencontré Léa.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">habitions</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avons</code> both include the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> sound, but they belong to different tenses.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul voulait les billets, alors je les lui ai donnés.</code> — The cluster <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les lui</code> must stay in that order, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donnés</code> agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les billets</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La serveuse nous apportait toujours du thé.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> is unstressed and easy to drop; it is the indirect recipient.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Prends-les-moi tout de suite et mets-les-y !</code> — Positive commands put pronouns after the verb with hyphens.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Listen once for the whole sentence, then again for the verb endings and pronoun clusters. Correct tense choice before you correct accents.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a short travel memory: four background sentences in the imparfait and four completed events in the passé composé.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model travel memory:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faisait chaud ce jour-là.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous marchions près de la rivière.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les rues étaient pleines de touristes.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voulais prendre des photos.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Puis nous avons trouvé un petit café.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le serveur nous a apporté de l'eau.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai commandé une tarte.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes rentrés à l'hôtel très tard.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: the first four sentences give background, description, or state, so they use the imparfait. The last four move the story forward, so they use the passé composé.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Forming the imparfait.</strong> Use the present <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> form, remove -ons, and add imparfait endings. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissions</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Choosing the past tense.</strong> Use the imparfait for background, habits, description, and ongoing states; use the passé composé for completed events. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait quand je suis sorti.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Past narration.</strong> A common story pattern is scene in the imparfait, event in the passé composé. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dormais quand elle a appelé.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Double object pronouns.</strong> Before a verb, the fixed order puts <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> before <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lui</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">leur</code>. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code> In affirmative commands, the pronouns follow: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-le-moi !</code></li>\n</ol></div></details>","search":"# chapter 14 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. nous parlons -> imparfait, je 2. nous finissons -> imparfait, ils 3. it was raining. 4. i was reading when he arrived. 5. je donne le livre à paul. 6. give it to me! 1. `je parlais` — start from `nous parlons`, remove -ons, add -ais. 2. `ils finissaient` — start from `nous finissons`, so the stem is finiss-, then add -aient. 3. `il pleuvait.` — weather/background description normally uses the imparfait. 4. `je lisais quand il est arrivé.` — `lisais` is the ongoing action; `est arrivé` is the completed interruption. 5. `je le lui donne.` — `le` is the direct object and comes before indirect `lui`. 6. `donnez-le-moi !` — in an affirmative command, pronouns follow the verb; `me` becomes `moi`. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **forming the imparfait**. use `je parlais` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **choosing the past tense**. use `il pleuvait.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **past narration**. use `je dormais quand marie a appelé.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **double object pronouns**. use `je le lui donne.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct answer must clearly show the named structure and change the model sentence's content. 1. **forming the imparfait:** `nous vendions des livres.` — from `nous vendons`, stem vend-, ending -ions. 2. **choosing the past tense:** `la salle était calme.` — description/background takes the imparfait. 3. **past narration:** `je marchais quand la pluie a commencé.` — ongoing scene in the imparfait, new event in the passé composé. 4. **double object pronouns:** `nous les lui envoyons.` — direct `les` comes before indirect `lui`. mark yourself wrong if you used a conditional form such as `je parlerais` for the imparfait, or if you reversed `le / la / les` and `lui / leur`. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. answers vary. worked example from `je lisais quand le téléphone a sonné.` 1. change the subject: `nous lisions quand le téléphone a sonné.` — `lisais` changes to `lisions` for `nous`. 2. make it negative: `je ne lisais pas quand le téléphone a sonné.` — the negative wraps the imparfait verb. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que tu lisais quand le téléphone a sonné ?` — the past-tense contrast stays the same. 4. change one meaningful detail: `je lisais quand marie est entrée.` — the interruption is now a different passé composé event. for your own sentence, ask: which part is background or ongoing, and which part is the completed event? ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `il pleuvait et les rues étaient presque vides.` — both verbs are imparfait; the silent endings make subject agreement easy to miss. 2. `je lisais dans ma chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.` — the contrast is the trap: ongoing `lisais`, sudden event `a sonné`. 3. `nous habitions à paris lorsque nous avons rencontré léa.` — `habitions` and `avons` both include the `nous` sound, but they belong to different tenses. 4. `paul voulait les billets, alors je les lui ai donnés.` — the cluster `les lui` must stay in that order, and `donnés` agrees with `les billets`. 5. `la serveuse nous apportait toujours du thé.` — `nous` is unstressed and easy to drop; it is the indirect recipient. 6. `prends-les-moi tout de suite et mets-les-y !` — positive commands put pronouns after the verb with hyphens. listen once for the whole sentence, then again for the verb endings and pronoun clusters. correct tense choice before you correct accents. ## e. personal writing write a short travel memory: four background sentences in the imparfait and four completed events in the passé composé. answers vary. a model travel memory: 1. `il faisait chaud ce jour-là.` 2. `nous marchions près de la rivière.` 3. `les rues étaient pleines de touristes.` 4. `je voulais prendre des photos.` 5. `puis nous avons trouvé un petit café.` 6. `le serveur nous a apporté de l'eau.` 7. `j'ai commandé une tarte.` 8. `nous sommes rentrés à l'hôtel très tard.` self-check: the first four sentences give background, description, or state, so they use the imparfait. the last four move the story forward, so they use the passé composé. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **forming the imparfait.** use the present `nous` form, remove -ons, and add imparfait endings. example: `nous finissions`. 2. **choosing the past tense.** use the imparfait for background, habits, description, and ongoing states; use the passé composé for completed events. example: `il pleuvait quand je suis sorti.` 3. **past narration.** a common story pattern is scene in the imparfait, event in the passé composé. example: `je dormais quand elle a appelé.` 4. **double object pronouns.** before a verb, the fixed order puts `le`, `la`, `les` before `lui`, `leur`. example: `je le lui donne.` in affirmative commands, the pronouns follow: `donne-le-moi !` "},{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 14 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-answer-key\">Chapter 14 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils finissaient</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais quand il est arrivé.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-le-moi !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forming the imparfait:</strong> Take the present nous form, remove -ons, and add -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient. Être uses ét-.</li>\n<li><strong>Choosing the past tense:</strong> Use the imparfait for background, description, habit, and ongoing action; use the passé composé for bounded completed events.</li>\n<li><strong>Past narration:</strong> Set the scene with the imparfait and move events forward with the passé composé.</li>\n<li><strong>Double object pronouns:</strong> Pronoun order is fixed before ordinary verbs. With affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb in a different order.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 14 answer key ## section a 1. `je parlais` 2. `ils finissaient` 3. `il pleuvait.` 4. `je lisais quand il est arrivé.` 5. `je le lui donne.` 6. `donnez-le-moi !` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **forming the imparfait:** take the present nous form, remove -ons, and add -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient. être uses ét-. - **choosing the past tense:** use the imparfait for background, description, habit, and ongoing action; use the passé composé for bounded completed events. - **past narration:** set the scene with the imparfait and move events forward with the passé composé. - **double object pronouns:** pronoun order is fixed before ordinary verbs. with affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb in a different order. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 14 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 14 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Forming the imparfait</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Choosing the past tense</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Past narration</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Double object pronouns</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 14 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **forming the imparfait**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **choosing the past tense** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **past narration**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **double object pronouns**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":15,"slug":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites","title":"Future, Conditional, Pluperfect, and Indefinites","documents":[{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/README.md","title":"Chapter 15: Future, Conditional, Pluperfect, and Indefinites","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-future-conditional-pluperfect-and-indefinites\">Chapter 15: Future, Conditional, Pluperfect, and Indefinites</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Form the future and conditional.</li>\n<li>Use common irregular stems.</li>\n<li>Form the pluperfect and past conditional.</li>\n<li>Use tout and other indefinite words.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Future tense</li>\n<li>Conditional</li>\n<li>Earlier past and unreal past</li>\n<li>Tout and indefinites</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 15: future, conditional, pluperfect, and indefinites this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - form the future and conditional. - use common irregular stems. - form the pluperfect and past conditional. - use tout and other indefinite words. ## lesson sequence 1. future tense 2. conditional 3. earlier past and unreal past 4. tout and indefinites ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 15 Lessons: The Future, the Conditional, Earlier Past, and Indefinites","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-lessons-the-future-the-conditional-earlier-past-and-indefinites\">Chapter 15 Lessons: The Future, the Conditional, Earlier Past, and Indefinites</h1>\n<p>This chapter moves you forward and sideways in time. You will learn how French talks about future events, imagined results, unreal past alternatives, and broad ideas such as “all,” “some,” “several,” and “each.” The chapter also gives you the classic <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> patterns that connect real possibilities, present hypotheticals, and regrets about the past.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>form the future tense with regular and irregular stems;</li>\n<li>use the future after time expressions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lorsque</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que</code>;</li>\n<li>form and use the conditional for politeness, wishes, advice, and hypothetical results;</li>\n<li>build the pluperfect and the past conditional;</li>\n<li>choose the correct tense pattern in the three standard <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>-clause sequences;</li>\n<li>use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> and several common indefinite adjectives and pronouns accurately.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>form the imparfait from Chapter 14, because the conditional uses the same endings;</li>\n<li>form the passé composé and recognize past participles from Chapter 13, because the pluperfect and past conditional depend on them;</li>\n<li>identify the difference between a main clause and a subordinate clause;</li>\n<li>recognize agreement in gender and number, since <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> changes form.</li>\n<p>If you cannot produce <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai fini</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle est arrivée</code> quickly, review those first.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Check the prerequisites with these five quick productions.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Give the imparfait of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlions</code>. The conditional will reuse endings from this family.</li>\n<li>Put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code> in the passé composé with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai fini</code>. The pluperfect and past conditional still need past participles.</li>\n<li>Put <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriver</code> in the passé composé with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle est arrivée</code>. <strong>Être</strong> agreement continues in compound tenses.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais le temps, je voyagerais</code>, which clause gives the condition? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais le temps</code>. The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause sets the condition; the other clause gives the result.</li>\n<li>Choose the right form: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ les maisons</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes les maisons</code>. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maisons</code> is feminine plural, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review the imparfait, passé composé, and agreement before continuing. Chapter 15 combines all three.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Learn the future and the conditional together, because they share the same stems. Then study the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> patterns as fixed sentence frames. When you practice, say the whole two-clause sentence aloud rather than memorizing only isolated verb forms.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-forming-the-future-tense\">Unit 1: Forming the future tense</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>For most verbs, the future uses the infinitive as the stem plus the endings <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-as</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ont</code>. For <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs, remove the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> first.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-endings\">The endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ai</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-as</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-a</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ont</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-parler\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlerai</code></td>\n<td>I will speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu parleras</code></td>\n<td>you will speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parlera</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle parlera</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on parlera</code></td>\n<td>he/she/we will speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlerons</code></td>\n<td>we will speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous parlerez</code></td>\n<td>you will speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parleront</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles parleront</code></td>\n<td>they will speak</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"three-model-stems\">Three model stems</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb type</th>\n<th>Future stem</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-er</code></td>\n<td>infinitive</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlerai</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ir</code></td>\n<td>infinitive</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finirons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code></td>\n<td>infinitive minus final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle vendra</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"more-examples\">More examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous irons à la banque et nous parlerons au directeur.</code> — Tomorrow, we will go to the bank and speak to the manager.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu finiras ce chapitre ce soir.</code> — You will finish this chapter tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">On prendra le train de six heures.</code> — We will take the six o'clock train.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>The future endings are usually clear in speech, especially in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> forms. Listen for the linking consonants in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils auront</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles iront</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlerons</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use the present tense automatically for future meaning just because English sometimes does. French certainly can use the present for near-future context in conversation, but the regular future is the safe default when you are learning planned or predicted future events.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code> in the future for all six persons.</li>\n<li>Build three sentences with these cues: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">demain / nous / visiter le musée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce soir / elle / écrire à sa sœur</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la semaine prochaine / ils / vendre leur voiture</code>.</li>\n<li>Say the six future endings from memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Future of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je finirai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu finiras</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il finira</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finirons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous finirez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils finiront</code> — regular -ir verbs keep the infinitive as the future stem.</li>\n<li>Future of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je vendrai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu vendras</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle vendra</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous vendrons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous vendrez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils vendront</code> — -re verbs drop the final -e before the future endings.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous visiterons le musée.</code> — future ending -ons with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce soir, elle écrira à sa sœur.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">écrire</code> uses the future stem écrir-, plus -a for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La semaine prochaine, ils vendront leur voiture.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code> drops final -e, then takes -ont for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>.</li>\n<li>The six endings are -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont. The stem says which verb; the ending says who will do it.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-irregular-future-stems-and-future-time-clauses\">Unit 2: Irregular future stems and future-time clauses</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Many common verbs have irregular future stems, but the endings stay regular. French also uses the future in time clauses introduced by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lorsque</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que</code> when the meaning is future.</p>\n<h3 id=\"essential-irregular-future-stems\">Essential irregular future stems</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Infinitive</th>\n<th>Future stem</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ir-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'irai</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aur-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous aurons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devr-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu devras</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">enverr-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle enverra</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ser-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous serez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fer-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on fera</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourr-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils pourront</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">saur-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je saurai</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viendr-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles viendront</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verr-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu verras</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudr-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je voudrai</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"learn-them-in-sentences\">Learn them in sentences</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle sera prête à huit heures.</code> — She will be ready at eight o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous aurons plus de temps samedi.</code> — We will have more time on Saturday.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils viendront après le travail.</code> — They will come after work.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu verras la différence demain.</code> — You will see the difference tomorrow.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"future-after-quand-lorsque-d-s-que\">Future after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lorsque</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que</code></h3>\n<p>English says “When I arrive, I will call you,” with a present-tense verb in the time clause. French uses the future in <strong>both</strong> clauses when the meaning is future.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>English idea</th>\n<th>French structure</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>when + future time</td>\n<td>future + future</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand j'arriverai, je t'appellerai.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>as soon as + future time</td>\n<td>future + future</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dès que nous finirons, nous sortirons.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>when + future time</td>\n<td>future + future</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lorsque vous aurez le temps, vous viendrez.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not write <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Quand j'arrive, je t'appellerai</code> when you mean a future arrival. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand j'arriverai, je t'appellerai.</code></p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code> in the future for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>.</li>\n<li>Complete these frames: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand je...</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lorsque nous...</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dès qu'elle...</code>.</li>\n<li>Rewrite three English prompts in French: “When we arrive, we will eat.” “As soon as he knows, he will tell me.” “When you see Paul, you will give him the file.”</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je serai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous serons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils seront</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aurai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous aurons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils auront</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'irai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous irons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils iront</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je viendrai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous viendrons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils viendront</code> — the stems are irregular, but the future endings are regular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand je finirai, je t'appellerai.</code> — future meaning after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> takes the future in French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lorsque nous arriverons, nous mangerons.</code> — both actions are future, so both verbs are future.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dès qu'elle saura la réponse, elle nous le dira.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> uses saur-; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dire</code> uses dir- in the future.</li>\n<li>“When we arrive, we will eat.” → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand nous arriverons, nous mangerons.</code></li>\n<li>“As soon as he knows, he will tell me.” → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Dès qu'il saura, il me le dira.</code></li>\n<li>“When you see Paul, you will give him the file.” → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand tu verras Paul, tu lui donneras le dossier.</code></li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-forming-and-using-the-conditional\">Unit 3: Forming and using the conditional</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The conditional uses the <strong>future stem</strong> plus the <strong>imparfait endings</strong>. It often expresses politeness, desire, advice, or a hypothetical result.</p>\n<h3 id=\"endings-of-the-conditional\">Endings of the conditional</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ait</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ions</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-aient</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-parler\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlerais</code></td>\n<td>I would speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu parlerais</code></td>\n<td>you would speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il parlerait</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle parlerait</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on parlerait</code></td>\n<td>he/she/we would speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlerions</code></td>\n<td>we would speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous parleriez</code></td>\n<td>you would speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parleraient</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles parleraient</code></td>\n<td>they would speak</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"main-uses\">Main uses</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>politeness</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.</code></td>\n<td>I would like to open an account, please.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>wish</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aimerais vivre près de la mer.</code></td>\n<td>I would like to live near the sea.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>advice</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais dormir plus tôt.</code></td>\n<td>You should sleep earlier.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hypothetical result</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais dans tous ces pays.</code></td>\n<td>If I had more time, I would travel in all these countries.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"stem-sharing-reminder\">Stem-sharing reminder</h3>\n<p>Because the conditional uses the same stems as the future, the following pairs go together:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je serai</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je serais</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous aurons</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous aurions</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils viendront</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils viendraient</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>In modern French, future and conditional forms can sound very close, especially outside the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> forms. Context usually tells you whether the meaning is future or hypothetical.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not mix future endings and conditional endings.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je voudrai un café</code> when making a polite request</li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je voudrais un café</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the conditional forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code> for all six persons.</li>\n<li>Turn these prompts into polite requests: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir / fermer la fenêtre</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir / parler au directeur</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer / essayer ce dessert</code>.</li>\n<li>Give three pieces of advice beginning with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais...</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous devriez...</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je serais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu serais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il serait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous serions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous seriez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils seraient</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aurais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu aurais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle aurait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous aurions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous auriez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles auraient</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ferais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu ferais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">on ferait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous ferions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous feriez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils feraient</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je viendrais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu viendrais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle viendrait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous viendrions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous viendriez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils viendraient</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourriez-vous fermer la fenêtre ?</code> — conditional of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code> makes the request polite.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais parler au directeur.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrais</code> is the polite conditional; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrai</code> would mean future.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aimerais essayer ce dessert.</code> — conditional of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code> expresses a wish.</li>\n<li>Advice examples: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais dormir plus tôt.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous devriez appeler le médecin.</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais relire la consigne.</code> Advice uses conditional forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-the-pluperfect-and-the-past-conditional\">Unit 4: The pluperfect and the past conditional</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The pluperfect (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plus-que-parfait</code>) places one past action before another past moment. The past conditional expresses what would have happened under different past circumstances.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pluperfect-structure\">Pluperfect structure</h3>\n<p><strong>imparfait of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> + past participle</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Auxiliary in the imparfait</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'avais</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'avais fini.</code></td>\n<td>I had finished.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle était</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle était partie.</code></td>\n<td>She had left.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous étions</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous étions levés.</code></td>\n<td>We had gotten up.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"past-conditional-structure\">Past conditional structure</h3>\n<p><strong>conditional of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> + past participle</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Auxiliary in the conditional</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aurais</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aurais accepté.</code></td>\n<td>I would have accepted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle serait</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle serait venue.</code></td>\n<td>She would have come.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous nous serions</code> + participle</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous serions couchés plus tôt.</code></td>\n<td>We would have gone to bed earlier.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"in-context\">In context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle avait déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.</code> — She had already finished when we arrived.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils étaient partis avant la pluie.</code> — They had left before the rain.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec plus d'argent, nous aurions loué un appartement plus grand.</code> — With more money, we would have rented a bigger apartment.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Sans ton message, je serais resté à la maison.</code> — Without your message, I would have stayed home.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"agreement-reminder\">Agreement reminder</h3>\n<p>The same passé composé agreement rules still apply when the auxiliary is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> or when a preceding direct object pronoun triggers agreement.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not confuse <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'avais fini</code> (had finished) with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai fini</code> (finished / have finished). The pluperfect needs the <strong>imparfait</strong> auxiliary, not the present-tense auxiliary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Build one pluperfect sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, and one pronominal pluperfect sentence.</li>\n<li>Turn these cues into past conditional sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous / venir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle / accepter</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils / se lever plus tôt</code>.</li>\n<li>Pair one pluperfect sentence with one passé composé sentence to show sequence in the past.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'avais fini le travail.</code> — pluperfect with the imparfait auxiliary <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avais</code> plus the participle <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fini</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle était partie avant midi.</code> — pluperfect with <strong>être</strong>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">partie</code> agrees with feminine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous étions levés tôt.</code> — pronominal pluperfect uses <strong>être</strong>, and the participle agrees with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> when the reflexive pronoun is direct.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous serions venus.</code> — past conditional with conditional <strong>être</strong> plus participle; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venus</code> agrees with masculine or mixed plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle aurait accepté.</code> — past conditional with conditional <strong>avoir</strong>; no agreement because there is no preceding direct object.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se seraient levés plus tôt.</code> — pronominal past conditional uses <strong>être</strong> and agreement with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>.</li>\n<li>Sequence pair: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'avais déjà préparé le dîner quand mes amis sont arrivés.</code> The pluperfect action happened first; the passé composé event happened next in the story.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-the-three-standard-si-patterns\">Unit 5: The three standard <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> patterns</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>French <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clauses follow stable tense patterns. Learn them as complete sentence frames.</p>\n<h3 id=\"essential-si-clause-table\">Essential <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>-clause table</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause</th>\n<th>Main clause</th>\n<th>Main meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + present</td>\n<td>present / future / imperative</td>\n<td>real possibility or general truth</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si tu viens, nous dînerons ensemble.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + imparfait</td>\n<td>conditional</td>\n<td>present or future hypothetical</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + pluperfect</td>\n<td>past conditional</td>\n<td>unreal past result / regret</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si nous avions réservé plus tôt, nous aurions eu de meilleures places.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"three-model-sentences\">Three model sentences</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si tu as faim, prends une pomme.</code> — If you are hungry, have an apple.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si elle trouve le dossier, elle nous appellera.</code> — If she finds the file, she will call us.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si je gagnais à la loterie, j'achèterais une petite maison.</code> — If I won the lottery, I would buy a small house.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si vous étiez venus plus tôt, vous auriez vu le début du film.</code> — If you had come earlier, you would have seen the beginning of the movie.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"critical-warning\">Critical warning</h3>\n<p>Never put the future or the conditional directly after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> in these standard patterns.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Si j'aurai le temps...</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'ai le temps...</code></li>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Si j'aurais le temps...</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais le temps...</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Finish three real-possibility sentences starting with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'ai...</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si nous finissons...</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si vous voyez Paul...</code>.</li>\n<li>Finish three hypothetical sentences starting with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'étais...</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si tu pouvais...</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si elles habitaient ici...</code>.</li>\n<li>Finish two regret sentences starting with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si nous avions su...</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si je m'étais réveillé plus tôt...</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'ai le temps, je t'appellerai.</code> — real possibility: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + present, then future.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si nous finissons tôt, nous sortirons.</code> — still a real future possibility, so the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause stays present.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si vous voyez Paul, donnez-lui le dossier.</code> — real possibility plus imperative is allowed.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'étais riche, je voyagerais.</code> — present/future hypothetical: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + imparfait, then conditional.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si tu pouvais venir, nous dînerions ensemble.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvais</code> is imparfait; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dînerions</code> is conditional.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si elles habitaient ici, elles prendraient le métro.</code> — hypothetical condition plus hypothetical result.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si nous avions su, nous serions venus plus tôt.</code> — unreal past: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + pluperfect, then past conditional.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si je m'étais réveillé plus tôt, j'aurais pris le train.</code> — the missed condition is pluperfect; the missed result is past conditional.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Never put a future or conditional form immediately after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> in these standard patterns.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-6-tout-and-other-common-indefinite-words\">Unit 6: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tout</code> and other common indefinite words</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Indefinites help you speak about quantity or identity without naming exact numbers or exact people: all, every, some, several, none, each.</p>\n<h3 id=\"tout-as-adjective-or-pronoun\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tout</code> as adjective or pronoun</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code></td>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout le quartier</code></td>\n<td>the whole neighborhood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute</code></td>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute la journée</code></td>\n<td>the whole day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous</code></td>\n<td>masculine plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous les clients</code></td>\n<td>all the customers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes</code></td>\n<td>feminine plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes les réponses</code></td>\n<td>all the answers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"tout-in-sentences\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tout</code> in sentences</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tout le monde viendra demain.</code> — Everyone will come tomorrow.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Toute la famille sera là.</code> — The whole family will be there.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tous partiraient plus tôt si le train arrivait à l'heure.</code> — Everyone would leave earlier if the train arrived on time.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles ont lu toutes les consignes.</code> — They read all the instructions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"other-useful-indefinites\">Other useful indefinites</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code></td>\n<td>adjective, singular noun after it</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque étudiant choisira un sujet</code></td>\n<td>each student will choose a topic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs</code></td>\n<td>adjective or pronoun, plural meaning</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs sont déjà partis</code></td>\n<td>several have already left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">certains / certaines</code></td>\n<td>adjective or pronoun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">certaines préféreraient attendre</code></td>\n<td>some would prefer to wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques</code></td>\n<td>adjective, plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques amis viendront</code></td>\n<td>a few friends will come</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aucun / aucune</code></td>\n<td>adjective, usually with negation</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aucune solution ne marcherait</code></td>\n<td>no solution would work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chacun / chacune</code></td>\n<td>pronoun</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chacun aura son billet</code></td>\n<td>each person will have his or her ticket</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Remember that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code> is singular in form, even though it refers to many people one by one: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque étudiant aura</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*chaque étudiants auront</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Complete with the right form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ la ville</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ les jours</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ mes amies</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ le repas</code>.</li>\n<li>Write one future sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code>, one conditional sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">certains</code>, and one negative sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aucun</code>.</li>\n<li>Replace a specific quantity with an indefinite word: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trois amis viendront</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques amis viendront</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute la ville</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ville</code> is feminine singular, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous les jours</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jours</code> is masculine plural, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes mes amies</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amies</code> is feminine plural, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout le repas</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">repas</code> is masculine singular, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Chaque étudiant recevra un courriel.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code> is followed by a singular noun and a singular verb.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Certains préféreraient attendre.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">certains</code> is plural and the conditional expresses preference.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Aucune réponse ne serait parfaite.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aucun / aucune</code> normally appears with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponse</code> is feminine singular.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Trois amis viendront.</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quelques amis viendront.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques</code> gives an indefinite small quantity and stays with a plural noun.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud twice, then cover it and rebuild it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous irons à la banque et nous parlerons au directeur.</code> — Tomorrow, we will go to the bank and speak to the manager.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.</code> — I would like to open an account, please.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais dans tous ces pays.</code> — If I had more time, I would travel in all these countries.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle avait déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.</code> — She had already finished when we arrived.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand tu verras Paul, tu lui donneras toutes les informations.</code> — When you see Paul, you will give him all the information.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si nous avions réservé plus tôt, nous aurions eu quelques places près de la fenêtre.</code> — If we had reserved earlier, we would have had a few seats near the window.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>After writing from memory, check stems and tense patterns before small spelling details.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Future stems.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">irons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlerons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verras</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donneras</code> are future forms. Sentence 5 uses future in both clauses because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> refers to future time.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditional forms.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrais</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyagerais</code> use future stems plus imparfait endings. Do not confuse <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrais</code> with future <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrai</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Earlier past.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avait terminé</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avions réservé</code> are pluperfect forms: imparfait auxiliary plus participle.</li>\n<li><strong>Unreal past result.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aurions eu</code> is past conditional: conditional auxiliary plus participle.</li>\n<li><strong>Indefinites.</strong> Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous ces pays</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes les informations</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques places</code>. The form must match the noun or the quantity idea.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong for any future or conditional form placed directly after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> in these standard patterns.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle the tense-bearing verb;</li>\n<li>label it future, conditional, pluperfect, past conditional, or passé composé;</li>\n<li>box the indefinite word if there is one.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Tense-bearing verb(s)</th>\n<th>Label</th>\n<th>Indefinite</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">irons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlerons</code></td>\n<td>future, future</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrais</code></td>\n<td>conditional</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voyagerais</code></td>\n<td>imparfait in the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause, conditional result</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous ces pays</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avait terminé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sommes arrivés</code></td>\n<td>pluperfect, passé composé</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verras</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">donneras</code></td>\n<td>future, future</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes les informations</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avions réservé</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aurions eu</code></td>\n<td>pluperfect, past conditional</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques places</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The most important pattern is sentence 6: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + pluperfect gives an unreal past condition, and the result uses the past conditional. Do not write a conditional auxiliary immediately after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, or condition.</li>\n<p>Then rewrite sentence 3 as a real possibility and sentence 6 as present advice instead of regret.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>One worked set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, elle ira à la banque et elle parlera au directeur.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous irons à la gare et nous parlerons à l'employé.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voudrions ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais réserver une table, s'il vous plaît.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si nous avions plus de temps, nous voyagerions dans tous ces pays.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais plus d'argent, j'achèterais tous ces livres.</code></li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles avaient déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.</code> Detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle avait déjà préparé le dîner quand ses amis sont arrivés.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sentence 3 as a real possibility: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'ai plus de temps, je voyagerai dans tous ces pays.</code> Sentence 6 as present advice instead of regret: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous devriez réserver plus tôt pour avoir quelques places près de la fenêtre.</code></p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one with the future;</li>\n<li>one with the conditional;</li>\n<li>one <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> sentence;</li>\n<li>one sentence using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aucun</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chacun</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Future: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, je travaillerai à la maison.</code> — future stem plus future ending.</li>\n<li>Conditional: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aimerais visiter Montréal.</code> — conditional of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aimer</code> expresses a wish.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si</code> sentence: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais plus de temps, je lirais tous les soirs.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + imparfait, then conditional.</li>\n<li>Indefinite: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Chaque ami recevra un message.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code> is followed by a singular noun and singular verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Check your own four for the big errors: no future or conditional directly after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>, correct irregular stems, and agreement in forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-planning-a-move\">Mini-dialogue: planning a move</h2>\n<p><strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Alors, quand est-ce que tu déménageras ?</code> — So, when will you move? <strong>Yanis :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je partirai probablement en octobre, dès que je signerai le nouveau bail.</code> — I will probably leave in October, as soon as I sign the new lease. <strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu vivras seul ?</code> — Will you live alone? <strong>Yanis :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, mais j'aimerais inviter quelques amis le premier soir.</code> — Yes, but I would like to invite a few friends on the first evening. <strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Et si tu trouvais un appartement plus près du centre ?</code> — And what if you found an apartment closer to downtown? <strong>Yanis :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce serait pratique, mais ce serait aussi plus cher.</code> — That would be practical, but it would also be more expensive. <strong>Nora :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu avais déjà visité ce quartier avant ?</code> — Had you already visited that neighborhood before? <strong>Yanis :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui. Si je ne l'avais pas aimé, je ne l'aurais jamais choisi.</code> — Yes. If I had not liked it, I would never have chosen it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find one future form, one conditional form, one pluperfect form, and one past conditional form.</li>\n<li>Explain why both verbs in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que je signerai le nouveau bail, je partirai probablement en octobre</code> are in the future.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques amis</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute la famille</code> and make every necessary agreement change.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce serait pratique</code> as direct present-tense advice to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n<li>Create one more <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> sentence that Nora could say.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Future form: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu déménageras</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je partirai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je signerai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu vivras</code>. Conditional form: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aimerais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">trouvais</code> is imparfait in a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">serait</code> is conditional. Pluperfect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu avais déjà visité</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne l'avais pas aimé</code>. Past conditional: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne l'aurais jamais choisi</code>.</li>\n<li>In future-time clauses with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que</code>, French uses the future in both clauses: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que je signerai le nouveau bail, je partirai probablement en octobre</code>. English often uses present after “as soon as,” but French does not in this pattern.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aimerais inviter toute la famille le premier soir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques amis</code> becomes feminine singular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute la famille</code>. No participle agreement is involved because this is an infinitive construction.</li>\n<li>Direct advice: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais chercher un appartement plus près du centre.</code> The conditional of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code> gives advice.</li>\n<li>Nora could add: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si tu avais plus d'argent, tu choisirais un appartement près du centre.</code> This is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + imparfait with a conditional result.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-un-projet-pour-l-ann-e-prochaine\">Connected reading: un projet pour l'année prochaine</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'année prochaine, je prendrai une semaine de vacances en avril et je visiterai la Bretagne avec ma sœur. Nous irons en train, et dès que nous arriverons, nous louerons des vélos pour explorer les villages voisins. J'aimerais voir la mer tous les matins, mais je voudrais aussi passer du temps dans les petites librairies du centre. Si le temps était mauvais, nous changerions peut-être notre programme, mais nous trouverions quand même quelque chose d'intéressant à faire. J'avais déjà regardé plusieurs hôtels l'hiver dernier, mais aucun ne convenait vraiment à notre budget. Si j'avais réservé plus tôt, j'aurais sans doute trouvé une meilleure offre.</code> — Next year, I will take a week of vacation in April and I will visit Brittany with my sister. We will go by train, and as soon as we arrive, we will rent bicycles to explore the neighboring villages. I would like to see the sea every morning, but I would also like to spend time in the small bookstores downtown. If the weather were bad, we might change our plan, but we would still find something interesting to do. I had already looked at several hotels last winter, but none of them really suited our budget. If I had booked earlier, I probably would have found a better deal.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List the future forms and explain why each one is future rather than present.</li>\n<li>Find the four conditional forms. Say which two depend on a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause, and what the other two are doing instead.</li>\n<li>Find the pluperfect and the past conditional.</li>\n<li>List every indefinite word in the passage and explain what it refers to.</li>\n<li>Write three follow-up sentences about what the narrator will do after arriving.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Future forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je prendrai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je visiterai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous irons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous arriverons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous louerons</code>. They refer to planned future actions, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que nous arriverons</code> is future because the arrival is still in the future.</li>\n<li>Conditional forms: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aimerais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je voudrais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous changerions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous trouverions</code>. Only the last two depend on a condition — the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si le temps était mauvais</code>, which pairs an imperfect <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause with a conditional main clause. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aimerais</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je voudrais</code> depend on nothing: the conditional is being used to soften a wish, which is why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je voudrais</code> sounds polite where <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je veux</code> sounds blunt.</li>\n<li>Pluperfect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'avais déjà regardé plusieurs hôtels</code>. Past conditional: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aurais sans doute trouvé une meilleure offre</code>.</li>\n<li>Indefinites: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous les matins</code> means every morning; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs hôtels</code> means several hotels; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aucun</code> refers to no hotel; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelque chose</code> means something indefinite; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une meilleure offre</code> is one nonspecific offer.</li>\n<li>Follow-up sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand nous arriverons, nous déposerons nos sacs à l'hôtel. Ensuite, nous marcherons près de la mer. Si nous avons assez d'énergie, nous visiterons une petite librairie.</code> Future-time <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> and real <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> possibilities use future or present according to the standard patterns.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forgetting to drop the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-re</code> verbs: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle vendra</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*elle vendera</code>.</li>\n<li>Using the present after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lorsque</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que</code> when the meaning is future.</li>\n<li>Using future endings instead of conditional endings in polite requests: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je voudrais</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*je voudrai</code>, for “I would like.”</li>\n<li>Putting future or conditional directly after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> in the standard patterns.</li>\n<li>Confusing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'avais fini</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai fini</code>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting agreement and number with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute la journée</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous les livres</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes les tables</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"future-endings\">Future endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ai</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-as</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-a</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ons</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ont</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"conditional-endings\">Conditional endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ais</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ait</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ions</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-aient</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"irregular-future-conditional-stems\">Irregular future/conditional stems</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Stem</th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Stem</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ser-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">verr-</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aur-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viendr-</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ir-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">envoyer</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">enverr-</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fer-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudr-</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourr-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devr-</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">saur-</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-si-patterns\">The three <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause</th>\n<th>Main clause</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + present</td>\n<td>present / future / imperative</td>\n<td>real possibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + imparfait</td>\n<td>conditional</td>\n<td>present/future hypothetical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> + pluperfect</td>\n<td>past conditional</td>\n<td>unreal past result</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"indefinite-quick-guide\">Indefinite quick guide</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Core meaning</th>\n<th>Grammar note</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout/toute/tous/toutes</code></td>\n<td>all / whole</td>\n<td>agrees in gender and number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code></td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>singular noun after it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs</code></td>\n<td>several</td>\n<td>plural meaning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">certains/certaines</code></td>\n<td>some / certain ones</td>\n<td>agrees in gender and number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques</code></td>\n<td>a few / some</td>\n<td>plural noun after it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aucun/aucune</code></td>\n<td>no / not any</td>\n<td>usually with negation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chacun/chacune</code></td>\n<td>each one</td>\n<td>pronoun</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about your plans for a future trip, project, or life change. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least three future forms;</li>\n<li>at least two conditional forms;</li>\n<li>one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lorsque</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que</code> followed by a future-time clause;</li>\n<li>one pluperfect sentence;</li>\n<li>one past conditional sentence;</li>\n<li>one correct <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause;</li>\n<li>at least two indefinite words, one of which must be a form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every future stem;</li>\n<li>underline every imparfait ending used in a conditional or pluperfect auxiliary;</li>\n<li>box the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause and check the tense pattern;</li>\n<li>check agreement of every indefinite word;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any sentence whose tense pattern was wrong.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is a model paragraph that satisfies the brief:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'année prochaine, je commencerai un nouveau projet et je travaillerai avec plusieurs collègues. Quand nous aurons le calendrier final, nous réserverons toutes les salles nécessaires. J'aimerais organiser une première réunion en avril, et mes collègues voudraient inviter quelques partenaires. J'avais déjà préparé une liste l'année dernière, mais elle était trop vague. Si j'avais gardé mes anciennes notes, j'aurais gagné du temps. Si le budget augmente, nous achèterons aussi de nouveaux logiciels. Chaque personne recevra un rôle précis. Ce serait plus simple avec un assistant, mais nous ferons le travail ensemble.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: future forms include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commencerai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">travaillerai</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aurons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réserverons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">achèterons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">recevra</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ferons</code>; conditionals include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aimerais</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudraient</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">serait</code>; the future-time clause is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand nous aurons...</code>; the pluperfect is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'avais déjà préparé</code>; the past conditional is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'aurais gagné</code>; the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clauses follow correct patterns; and the indefinites include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quelques</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a future-time <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">dès que</code> clause uses the present by English habit;</li>\n<li>a future or conditional appears immediately after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> fails to agree with its noun;</li>\n<li>a past conditional uses a present auxiliary instead of a conditional auxiliary;</li>\n<li>a conditional request accidentally uses future <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrai</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrais</code>.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 15 lessons: the future, the conditional, earlier past, and indefinites this chapter moves you forward and sideways in time. you will learn how french talks about future events, imagined results, unreal past alternatives, and broad ideas such as “all,” “some,” “several,” and “each.” the chapter also gives you the classic `si` patterns that connect real possibilities, present hypotheticals, and regrets about the past. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. form the future tense with regular and irregular stems; 2. use the future after time expressions such as `quand`, `lorsque`, and `dès que`; 3. form and use the conditional for politeness, wishes, advice, and hypothetical results; 4. build the pluperfect and the past conditional; 5. choose the correct tense pattern in the three standard `si`-clause sequences; 6. use `tout` and several common indefinite adjectives and pronouns accurately. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - form the imparfait from chapter 14, because the conditional uses the same endings; - form the passé composé and recognize past participles from chapter 13, because the pluperfect and past conditional depend on them; - identify the difference between a main clause and a subordinate clause; - recognize agreement in gender and number, since `tout` changes form. if you cannot produce `nous parlions`, `j'ai fini`, and `elle est arrivée` quickly, review those first. check the prerequisites with these five quick productions. 1. give the imparfait of `parler` with `nous`. — `nous parlions`. the conditional will reuse endings from this family. 2. put `finir` in the passé composé with `j'`. — `j'ai fini`. the pluperfect and past conditional still need past participles. 3. put `arriver` in the passé composé with `elle`. — `elle est arrivée`. **être** agreement continues in compound tenses. 4. in `si j'avais le temps, je voyagerais`, which clause gives the condition? — `si j'avais le temps`. the `si` clause sets the condition; the other clause gives the result. 5. choose the right form: `___ les maisons`. — `toutes les maisons`. `maisons` is feminine plural, so `tout` becomes `toutes`. if you missed two or more, review the imparfait, passé composé, and agreement before continuing. chapter 15 combines all three. ## how to study this chapter learn the future and the conditional together, because they share the same stems. then study the `si` patterns as fixed sentence frames. when you practice, say the whole two-clause sentence aloud rather than memorizing only isolated verb forms. ## unit 1: forming the future tense ### core idea for most verbs, the future uses the infinitive as the stem plus the endings `-ai`, `-as`, `-a`, `-ons`, `-ez`, `-ont`. for `-re` verbs, remove the final `-e` first. ### the endings | pronoun | ending | |---|---| | `je` | `-ai` | | `tu` | `-as` | | `il/elle/on` | `-a` | | `nous` | `-ons` | | `vous` | `-ez` | | `ils/elles` | `-ont` | ### full model: `parler` | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `je parlerai` | i will speak | | `tu` | `tu parleras` | you will speak | | `il/elle/on` | `il parlera` / `elle parlera` / `on parlera` | he/she/we will speak | | `nous` | `nous parlerons` | we will speak | | `vous` | `vous parlerez` | you will speak | | `ils/elles` | `ils parleront` / `elles parleront` | they will speak | ### three model stems | verb type | future stem | example | |---|---|---| | `-er` | infinitive | `parler` -> `je parlerai` | | `-ir` | infinitive | `finir` -> `nous finirons` | | `-re` | infinitive minus final `-e` | `vendre` -> `elle vendra` | ### more examples - `demain, nous irons à la banque et nous parlerons au directeur.` — tomorrow, we will go to the bank and speak to the manager. - `tu finiras ce chapitre ce soir.` — you will finish this chapter tonight. - `on prendra le train de six heures.` — we will take the six o'clock train. ### pronunciation note the future endings are usually clear in speech, especially in `nous` and `vous` forms. listen for the linking consonants in `ils auront`, `elles iront`, and `nous parlerons`. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use the present tense automatically for future meaning just because english sometimes does. french certainly can use the present for near-future context in conversation, but the regular future is the safe default when you are learning planned or predicted future events. ### sentence-building practice 1. conjugate `finir` and `vendre` in the future for all six persons. 2. build three sentences with these cues: `demain / nous / visiter le musée`, `ce soir / elle / écrire à sa sœur`, `la semaine prochaine / ils / vendre leur voiture`. 3. say the six future endings from memory. 1. future of `finir`: `je finirai`, `tu finiras`, `il finira`, `nous finirons`, `vous finirez`, `ils finiront` — regular -ir verbs keep the infinitive as the future stem. 2. future of `vendre`: `je vendrai`, `tu vendras`, `elle vendra`, `nous vendrons`, `vous vendrez`, `ils vendront` — -re verbs drop the final -e before the future endings. 3. `demain, nous visiterons le musée.` — future ending -ons with `nous`. 4. `ce soir, elle écrira à sa sœur.` — `écrire` uses the future stem écrir-, plus -a for `elle`. 5. `la semaine prochaine, ils vendront leur voiture.` — `vendre` drops final -e, then takes -ont for `ils`. 6. the six endings are -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont. the stem says which verb; the ending says who will do it. ## unit 2: irregular future stems and future-time clauses ### core idea many common verbs have irregular future stems, but the endings stay regular. french also uses the future in time clauses introduced by `quand`, `lorsque`, and `dès que` when the meaning is future. ### essential irregular future stems | infinitive | future stem | example | |---|---|---| | `aller` | `ir-` | `j'irai` | | `avoir` | `aur-` | `nous aurons` | | `devoir` | `devr-` | `tu devras` | | `envoyer` | `enverr-` | `elle enverra` | | `être` | `ser-` | `vous serez` | | `faire` | `fer-` | `on fera` | | `pouvoir` | `pourr-` | `ils pourront` | | `savoir` | `saur-` | `je saurai` | | `venir` | `viendr-` | `elles viendront` | | `voir` | `verr-` | `tu verras` | | `vouloir` | `voudr-` | `je voudrai` | ### learn them in sentences - `elle sera prête à huit heures.` — she will be ready at eight o'clock. - `nous aurons plus de temps samedi.` — we will have more time on saturday. - `ils viendront après le travail.` — they will come after work. - `tu verras la différence demain.` — you will see the difference tomorrow. ### future after `quand`, `lorsque`, `dès que` english says “when i arrive, i will call you,” with a present-tense verb in the time clause. french uses the future in **both** clauses when the meaning is future. | english idea | french structure | example | |---|---|---| | when + future time | future + future | `quand j'arriverai, je t'appellerai.` | | as soon as + future time | future + future | `dès que nous finirons, nous sortirons.` | | when + future time | future + future | `lorsque vous aurez le temps, vous viendrez.` | ### common mistake for english speakers do not write `*quand j'arrive, je t'appellerai` when you mean a future arrival. use `quand j'arriverai, je t'appellerai.` ### sentence-building practice 1. conjugate `être`, `avoir`, `aller`, and `venir` in the future for `je`, `nous`, and `ils`. 2. complete these frames: `quand je...`, `lorsque nous...`, `dès qu'elle...`. 3. rewrite three english prompts in french: “when we arrive, we will eat.” “as soon as he knows, he will tell me.” “when you see paul, you will give him the file.” 1. `je serai`, `nous serons`, `ils seront`; `j'aurai`, `nous aurons`, `ils auront`; `j'irai`, `nous irons`, `ils iront`; `je viendrai`, `nous viendrons`, `ils viendront` — the stems are irregular, but the future endings are regular. 2. `quand je finirai, je t'appellerai.` — future meaning after `quand` takes the future in french. 3. `lorsque nous arriverons, nous mangerons.` — both actions are future, so both verbs are future. 4. `dès qu'elle saura la réponse, elle nous le dira.` — `savoir` uses saur-; `dire` uses dir- in the future. 5. “when we arrive, we will eat.” → `quand nous arriverons, nous mangerons.` 6. “as soon as he knows, he will tell me.” → `dès qu'il saura, il me le dira.` 7. “when you see paul, you will give him the file.” → `quand tu verras paul, tu lui donneras le dossier.` ## unit 3: forming and using the conditional ### core idea the conditional uses the **future stem** plus the **imparfait endings**. it often expresses politeness, desire, advice, or a hypothetical result. ### endings of the conditional | pronoun | ending | |---|---| | `je` | `-ais` | | `tu` | `-ais` | | `il/elle/on` | `-ait` | | `nous` | `-ions` | | `vous` | `-iez` | | `ils/elles` | `-aient` | ### full model: `parler` | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `je` | `je parlerais` | i would speak | | `tu` | `tu parlerais` | you would speak | | `il/elle/on` | `il parlerait` / `elle parlerait` / `on parlerait` | he/she/we would speak | | `nous` | `nous parlerions` | we would speak | | `vous` | `vous parleriez` | you would speak | | `ils/elles` | `ils parleraient` / `elles parleraient` | they would speak | ### main uses | use | french example | english | |---|---|---| | politeness | `je voudrais ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.` | i would like to open an account, please. | | wish | `j'aimerais vivre près de la mer.` | i would like to live near the sea. | | advice | `tu devrais dormir plus tôt.` | you should sleep earlier. | | hypothetical result | `si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais dans tous ces pays.` | if i had more time, i would travel in all these countries. | ### stem-sharing reminder because the conditional uses the same stems as the future, the following pairs go together: - `je serai` / `je serais` - `nous aurons` / `nous aurions` - `ils viendront` / `ils viendraient` ### pronunciation note in modern french, future and conditional forms can sound very close, especially outside the `nous` and `vous` forms. context usually tells you whether the meaning is future or hypothetical. ### common mistake for english speakers do not mix future endings and conditional endings. - incorrect: `*je voudrai un café` when making a polite request - correct: `je voudrais un café` ### sentence-building practice 1. write the conditional forms of `être`, `avoir`, `faire`, and `venir` for all six persons. 2. turn these prompts into polite requests: `pouvoir / fermer la fenêtre`; `vouloir / parler au directeur`; `aimer / essayer ce dessert`. 3. give three pieces of advice beginning with `tu devrais...` or `vous devriez...`. 1. `je serais`, `tu serais`, `il serait`, `nous serions`, `vous seriez`, `ils seraient`. 2. `j'aurais`, `tu aurais`, `elle aurait`, `nous aurions`, `vous auriez`, `elles auraient`. 3. `je ferais`, `tu ferais`, `on ferait`, `nous ferions`, `vous feriez`, `ils feraient`. 4. `je viendrais`, `tu viendrais`, `elle viendrait`, `nous viendrions`, `vous viendriez`, `ils viendraient`. 5. `pourriez-vous fermer la fenêtre ?` — conditional of `pouvoir` makes the request polite. 6. `je voudrais parler au directeur.` — `voudrais` is the polite conditional; `voudrai` would mean future. 7. `j'aimerais essayer ce dessert.` — conditional of `aimer` expresses a wish. 8. advice examples: `tu devrais dormir plus tôt.`, `vous devriez appeler le médecin.`, `tu devrais relire la consigne.` advice uses conditional forms of `devoir`. ## unit 4: the pluperfect and the past conditional ### core idea the pluperfect (`plus-que-parfait`) places one past action before another past moment. the past conditional expresses what would have happened under different past circumstances. ### pluperfect structure **imparfait of `avoir` or `être` + past participle** | auxiliary in the imparfait | example | english | |---|---|---| | `j'avais` + participle | `j'avais fini.` | i had finished. | | `elle était` + participle | `elle était partie.` | she had left. | | `nous nous étions` + participle | `nous nous étions levés.` | we had gotten up. | ### past conditional structure **conditional of `avoir` or `être` + past participle** | auxiliary in the conditional | example | english | |---|---|---| | `j'aurais` + participle | `j'aurais accepté.` | i would have accepted. | | `elle serait` + participle | `elle serait venue.` | she would have come. | | `nous nous serions` + participle | `nous nous serions couchés plus tôt.` | we would have gone to bed earlier. | ### in context - `elle avait déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.` — she had already finished when we arrived. - `ils étaient partis avant la pluie.` — they had left before the rain. - `avec plus d'argent, nous aurions loué un appartement plus grand.` — with more money, we would have rented a bigger apartment. - `sans ton message, je serais resté à la maison.` — without your message, i would have stayed home. ### agreement reminder the same passé composé agreement rules still apply when the auxiliary is `être` or when a preceding direct object pronoun triggers agreement. ### common mistake for english speakers do not confuse `j'avais fini` (had finished) with `j'ai fini` (finished / have finished). the pluperfect needs the **imparfait** auxiliary, not the present-tense auxiliary. ### sentence-building practice 1. build one pluperfect sentence with `avoir`, one with `être`, and one pronominal pluperfect sentence. 2. turn these cues into past conditional sentences: `nous / venir`, `elle / accepter`, `ils / se lever plus tôt`. 3. pair one pluperfect sentence with one passé composé sentence to show sequence in the past. 1. `j'avais fini le travail.` — pluperfect with the imparfait auxiliary `avais` plus the participle `fini`. 2. `elle était partie avant midi.` — pluperfect with **être**; `partie` agrees with feminine singular `elle`. 3. `nous nous étions levés tôt.` — pronominal pluperfect uses **être**, and the participle agrees with `nous` when the reflexive pronoun is direct. 4. `nous serions venus.` — past conditional with conditional **être** plus participle; `venus` agrees with masculine or mixed plural `nous`. 5. `elle aurait accepté.` — past conditional with conditional **avoir**; no agreement because there is no preceding direct object. 6. `ils se seraient levés plus tôt.` — pronominal past conditional uses **être** and agreement with `ils`. 7. sequence pair: `j'avais déjà préparé le dîner quand mes amis sont arrivés.` the pluperfect action happened first; the passé composé event happened next in the story. ## unit 5: the three standard `si` patterns ### core idea french `si` clauses follow stable tense patterns. learn them as complete sentence frames. ### essential `si`-clause table | `si` clause | main clause | main meaning | example | |---|---|---|---| | `si` + present | present / future / imperative | real possibility or general truth | `si tu viens, nous dînerons ensemble.` | | `si` + imparfait | conditional | present or future hypothetical | `si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais.` | | `si` + pluperfect | past conditional | unreal past result / regret | `si nous avions réservé plus tôt, nous aurions eu de meilleures places.` | ### three model sentences - `si tu as faim, prends une pomme.` — if you are hungry, have an apple. - `si elle trouve le dossier, elle nous appellera.` — if she finds the file, she will call us. - `si je gagnais à la loterie, j'achèterais une petite maison.` — if i won the lottery, i would buy a small house. - `si vous étiez venus plus tôt, vous auriez vu le début du film.` — if you had come earlier, you would have seen the beginning of the movie. ### critical warning never put the future or the conditional directly after `si` in these standard patterns. - incorrect: `*si j'aurai le temps...` - correct: `si j'ai le temps...` - incorrect: `*si j'aurais le temps...` - correct: `si j'avais le temps...` ### sentence-building practice 1. finish three real-possibility sentences starting with `si j'ai...`, `si nous finissons...`, `si vous voyez paul...`. 2. finish three hypothetical sentences starting with `si j'étais...`, `si tu pouvais...`, `si elles habitaient ici...`. 3. finish two regret sentences starting with `si nous avions su...`, `si je m'étais réveillé plus tôt...`. 1. `si j'ai le temps, je t'appellerai.` — real possibility: `si` + present, then future. 2. `si nous finissons tôt, nous sortirons.` — still a real future possibility, so the `si` clause stays present. 3. `si vous voyez paul, donnez-lui le dossier.` — real possibility plus imperative is allowed. 4. `si j'étais riche, je voyagerais.` — present/future hypothetical: `si` + imparfait, then conditional. 5. `si tu pouvais venir, nous dînerions ensemble.` — `pouvais` is imparfait; `dînerions` is conditional. 6. `si elles habitaient ici, elles prendraient le métro.` — hypothetical condition plus hypothetical result. 7. `si nous avions su, nous serions venus plus tôt.` — unreal past: `si` + pluperfect, then past conditional. 8. `si je m'étais réveillé plus tôt, j'aurais pris le train.` — the missed condition is pluperfect; the missed result is past conditional. never put a future or conditional form immediately after `si` in these standard patterns. ## unit 6: `tout` and other common indefinite words ### core idea indefinites help you speak about quantity or identity without naming exact numbers or exact people: all, every, some, several, none, each. ### `tout` as adjective or pronoun | form | typical use | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `tout` | masculine singular | `tout le quartier` | the whole neighborhood | | `toute` | feminine singular | `toute la journée` | the whole day | | `tous` | masculine plural | `tous les clients` | all the customers | | `toutes` | feminine plural | `toutes les réponses` | all the answers | ### `tout` in sentences - `tout le monde viendra demain.` — everyone will come tomorrow. - `toute la famille sera là.` — the whole family will be there. - `tous partiraient plus tôt si le train arrivait à l'heure.` — everyone would leave earlier if the train arrived on time. - `elles ont lu toutes les consignes.` — they read all the instructions. ### other useful indefinites | word | function | example | english | |---|---|---|---| | `chaque` | adjective, singular noun after it | `chaque étudiant choisira un sujet` | each student will choose a topic | | `plusieurs` | adjective or pronoun, plural meaning | `plusieurs sont déjà partis` | several have already left | | `certains / certaines` | adjective or pronoun | `certaines préféreraient attendre` | some would prefer to wait | | `quelques` | adjective, plural | `quelques amis viendront` | a few friends will come | | `aucun / aucune` | adjective, usually with negation | `aucune solution ne marcherait` | no solution would work | | `chacun / chacune` | pronoun | `chacun aura son billet` | each person will have his or her ticket | ### common mistake for english speakers remember that `chaque` is singular in form, even though it refers to many people one by one: `chaque étudiant aura`, not `*chaque étudiants auront`. ### sentence-building practice 1. complete with the right form of `tout`: `___ la ville`, `___ les jours`, `___ mes amies`, `___ le repas`. 2. write one future sentence with `chaque`, one conditional sentence with `certains`, and one negative sentence with `aucun`. 3. replace a specific quantity with an indefinite word: `trois amis viendront` -> `quelques amis viendront`. 1. `toute la ville` — `ville` is feminine singular, so use `toute`. 2. `tous les jours` — `jours` is masculine plural, so use `tous`. 3. `toutes mes amies` — `amies` is feminine plural, so use `toutes`. 4. `tout le repas` — `repas` is masculine singular, so use `tout`. 5. `chaque étudiant recevra un courriel.` — `chaque` is followed by a singular noun and a singular verb. 6. `certains préféreraient attendre.` — `certains` is plural and the conditional expresses preference. 7. `aucune réponse ne serait parfaite.` — `aucun / aucune` normally appears with `ne`; `réponse` is feminine singular. 8. `trois amis viendront.` → `quelques amis viendront.` — `quelques` gives an indefinite small quantity and stays with a plural noun. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice read each sentence aloud twice, then cover it and rebuild it from memory. 1. `demain, nous irons à la banque et nous parlerons au directeur.` — tomorrow, we will go to the bank and speak to the manager. 2. `je voudrais ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.` — i would like to open an account, please. 3. `si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais dans tous ces pays.` — if i had more time, i would travel in all these countries. 4. `elle avait déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.` — she had already finished when we arrived. 5. `quand tu verras paul, tu lui donneras toutes les informations.` — when you see paul, you will give him all the information. 6. `si nous avions réservé plus tôt, nous aurions eu quelques places près de la fenêtre.` — if we had reserved earlier, we would have had a few seats near the window. after writing from memory, check stems and tense patterns before small spelling details. - **future stems.** `irons`, `parlerons`, `verras`, and `donneras` are future forms. sentence 5 uses future in both clauses because `quand` refers to future time. - **conditional forms.** `voudrais` and `voyagerais` use future stems plus imparfait endings. do not confuse `voudrais` with future `voudrai`. - **earlier past.** `avait terminé` and `avions réservé` are pluperfect forms: imparfait auxiliary plus participle. - **unreal past result.** `aurions eu` is past conditional: conditional auxiliary plus participle. - **indefinites.** check `tous ces pays`, `toutes les informations`, and `quelques places`. the form must match the noun or the quantity idea. mark yourself wrong for any future or conditional form placed directly after `si` in these standard patterns. ### notice and mark for each sentence: 1. circle the tense-bearing verb; 2. label it future, conditional, pluperfect, past conditional, or passé composé; 3. box the indefinite word if there is one. | # | tense-bearing verb(s) | label | indefinite | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `irons`, `parlerons` | future, future | — | | 2 | `voudrais` | conditional | — | | 3 | `avais`, `voyagerais` | imparfait in the `si` clause, conditional result | `tous ces pays` | | 4 | `avait terminé`, `sommes arrivés` | pluperfect, passé composé | — | | 5 | `verras`, `donneras` | future, future | `toutes les informations` | | 6 | `avions réservé`, `aurions eu` | pluperfect, past conditional | `quelques places` | the most important pattern is sentence 6: `si` + pluperfect gives an unreal past condition, and the result uses the past conditional. do not write a conditional auxiliary immediately after `si`. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, or condition. then rewrite sentence 3 as a real possibility and sentence 6 as present advice instead of regret. one worked set: 1. subject: `demain, elle ira à la banque et elle parlera au directeur.` detail: `demain, nous irons à la gare et nous parlerons à l'employé.` 2. subject: `nous voudrions ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.` detail: `je voudrais réserver une table, s'il vous plaît.` 3. subject: `si nous avions plus de temps, nous voyagerions dans tous ces pays.` detail: `si j'avais plus d'argent, j'achèterais tous ces livres.` 4. subject: `elles avaient déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.` detail: `elle avait déjà préparé le dîner quand ses amis sont arrivés.` sentence 3 as a real possibility: `si j'ai plus de temps, je voyagerai dans tous ces pays.` sentence 6 as present advice instead of regret: `vous devriez réserver plus tôt pour avoir quelques places près de la fenêtre.` ### make it personal write four new sentences: - one with the future; - one with the conditional; - one `si` sentence; - one sentence using `tout`, `chaque`, `plusieurs`, `aucun`, or `chacun`. answers vary. a model set: - future: `demain, je travaillerai à la maison.` — future stem plus future ending. - conditional: `j'aimerais visiter montréal.` — conditional of `aimer` expresses a wish. - `si` sentence: `si j'avais plus de temps, je lirais tous les soirs.` — `si` + imparfait, then conditional. - indefinite: `chaque ami recevra un message.` — `chaque` is followed by a singular noun and singular verb. check your own four for the big errors: no future or conditional directly after `si`, correct irregular stems, and agreement in forms of `tout`. ## mini-dialogue: planning a move **nora :** `alors, quand est-ce que tu déménageras ?` — so, when will you move? **yanis :** `je partirai probablement en octobre, dès que je signerai le nouveau bail.` — i will probably leave in october, as soon as i sign the new lease. **nora :** `tu vivras seul ?` — will you live alone? **yanis :** `oui, mais j'aimerais inviter quelques amis le premier soir.` — yes, but i would like to invite a few friends on the first evening. **nora :** `et si tu trouvais un appartement plus près du centre ?` — and what if you found an apartment closer to downtown? **yanis :** `ce serait pratique, mais ce serait aussi plus cher.` — that would be practical, but it would also be more expensive. **nora :** `tu avais déjà visité ce quartier avant ?` — had you already visited that neighborhood before? **yanis :** `oui. si je ne l'avais pas aimé, je ne l'aurais jamais choisi.` — yes. if i had not liked it, i would never have chosen it. ### guided analysis 1. find one future form, one conditional form, one pluperfect form, and one past conditional form. 2. explain why both verbs in `dès que je signerai le nouveau bail, je partirai probablement en octobre` are in the future. 3. replace `quelques amis` with `toute la famille` and make every necessary agreement change. 4. rewrite `ce serait pratique` as direct present-tense advice to `tu`. 5. create one more `si` sentence that nora could say. 1. future form: `tu déménageras`, `je partirai`, `je signerai`, `tu vivras`. conditional form: `j'aimerais`, `trouvais` is imparfait in a `si` clause, and `serait` is conditional. pluperfect: `tu avais déjà visité`, `je ne l'avais pas aimé`. past conditional: `je ne l'aurais jamais choisi`. 2. in future-time clauses with `dès que`, french uses the future in both clauses: `dès que je signerai le nouveau bail, je partirai probablement en octobre`. english often uses present after “as soon as,” but french does not in this pattern. 3. `j'aimerais inviter toute la famille le premier soir.` — `quelques amis` becomes feminine singular `toute la famille`. no participle agreement is involved because this is an infinitive construction. 4. direct advice: `tu devrais chercher un appartement plus près du centre.` the conditional of `devoir` gives advice. 5. nora could add: `si tu avais plus d'argent, tu choisirais un appartement près du centre.` this is `si` + imparfait with a conditional result. ## connected reading: un projet pour l'année prochaine `l'année prochaine, je prendrai une semaine de vacances en avril et je visiterai la bretagne avec ma sœur. nous irons en train, et dès que nous arriverons, nous louerons des vélos pour explorer les villages voisins. j'aimerais voir la mer tous les matins, mais je voudrais aussi passer du temps dans les petites librairies du centre. si le temps était mauvais, nous changerions peut-être notre programme, mais nous trouverions quand même quelque chose d'intéressant à faire. j'avais déjà regardé plusieurs hôtels l'hiver dernier, mais aucun ne convenait vraiment à notre budget. si j'avais réservé plus tôt, j'aurais sans doute trouvé une meilleure offre.` — next year, i will take a week of vacation in april and i will visit brittany with my sister. we will go by train, and as soon as we arrive, we will rent bicycles to explore the neighboring villages. i would like to see the sea every morning, but i would also like to spend time in the small bookstores downtown. if the weather were bad, we might change our plan, but we would still find something interesting to do. i had already looked at several hotels last winter, but none of them really suited our budget. if i had booked earlier, i probably would have found a better deal. ### reading practice 1. list the future forms and explain why each one is future rather than present. 2. find the four conditional forms. say which two depend on a `si` clause, and what the other two are doing instead. 3. find the pluperfect and the past conditional. 4. list every indefinite word in the passage and explain what it refers to. 5. write three follow-up sentences about what the narrator will do after arriving. 1. future forms: `je prendrai`, `je visiterai`, `nous irons`, `nous arriverons`, `nous louerons`. they refer to planned future actions, and `dès que nous arriverons` is future because the arrival is still in the future. 2. conditional forms: `j'aimerais`, `je voudrais`, `nous changerions`, `nous trouverions`. only the last two depend on a condition — the `si` clause `si le temps était mauvais`, which pairs an imperfect `si` clause with a conditional main clause. `j'aimerais` and `je voudrais` depend on nothing: the conditional is being used to soften a wish, which is why `je voudrais` sounds polite where `je veux` sounds blunt. 3. pluperfect: `j'avais déjà regardé plusieurs hôtels`. past conditional: `j'aurais sans doute trouvé une meilleure offre`. 4. indefinites: `tous les matins` means every morning; `plusieurs hôtels` means several hotels; `aucun` refers to no hotel; `quelque chose` means something indefinite; `une meilleure offre` is one nonspecific offer. 5. follow-up sentences: `quand nous arriverons, nous déposerons nos sacs à l'hôtel. ensuite, nous marcherons près de la mer. si nous avons assez d'énergie, nous visiterons une petite librairie.` future-time `quand` and real `si` possibilities use future or present according to the standard patterns. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. forgetting to drop the final `-e` in `-re` verbs: `elle vendra`, not `*elle vendera`. 2. using the present after `quand`, `lorsque`, or `dès que` when the meaning is future. 3. using future endings instead of conditional endings in polite requests: `je voudrais`, not `*je voudrai`, for “i would like.” 4. putting future or conditional directly after `si` in the standard patterns. 5. confusing `j'avais fini` with `j'ai fini`. 6. forgetting agreement and number with `tout`: `toute la journée`, `tous les livres`, `toutes les tables`. ## chapter reference sheet ### future endings | pronoun | ending | |---|---| | `je` | `-ai` | | `tu` | `-as` | | `il/elle/on` | `-a` | | `nous` | `-ons` | | `vous` | `-ez` | | `ils/elles` | `-ont` | ### conditional endings | pronoun | ending | |---|---| | `je` | `-ais` | | `tu` | `-ais` | | `il/elle/on` | `-ait` | | `nous` | `-ions` | | `vous` | `-iez` | | `ils/elles` | `-aient` | ### irregular future/conditional stems | verb | stem | verb | stem | |---|---|---|---| | `être` | `ser-` | `voir` | `verr-` | | `avoir` | `aur-` | `venir` | `viendr-` | | `aller` | `ir-` | `envoyer` | `enverr-` | | `faire` | `fer-` | `vouloir` | `voudr-` | | `pouvoir` | `pourr-` | `devoir` | `devr-` | | `savoir` | `saur-` | | | ### the three `si` patterns | `si` clause | main clause | meaning | |---|---|---| | `si` + present | present / future / imperative | real possibility | | `si` + imparfait | conditional | present/future hypothetical | | `si` + pluperfect | past conditional | unreal past result | ### indefinite quick guide | word | core meaning | grammar note | |---|---|---| | `tout/toute/tous/toutes` | all / whole | agrees in gender and number | | `chaque` | each | singular noun after it | | `plusieurs` | several | plural meaning | | `certains/certaines` | some / certain ones | agrees in gender and number | | `quelques` | a few / some | plural noun after it | | `aucun/aucune` | no / not any | usually with negation | | `chacun/chacune` | each one | pronoun | ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences about your plans for a future trip, project, or life change. include: - at least three future forms; - at least two conditional forms; - one sentence with `quand`, `lorsque`, or `dès que` followed by a future-time clause; - one pluperfect sentence; - one past conditional sentence; - one correct `si` clause; - at least two indefinite words, one of which must be a form of `tout`. after writing: 1. circle every future stem; 2. underline every imparfait ending used in a conditional or pluperfect auxiliary; 3. box the `si` clause and check the tense pattern; 4. check agreement of every indefinite word; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any sentence whose tense pattern was wrong. answers vary. here is a model paragraph that satisfies the brief: `l'année prochaine, je commencerai un nouveau projet et je travaillerai avec plusieurs collègues. quand nous aurons le calendrier final, nous réserverons toutes les salles nécessaires. j'aimerais organiser une première réunion en avril, et mes collègues voudraient inviter quelques partenaires. j'avais déjà préparé une liste l'année dernière, mais elle était trop vague. si j'avais gardé mes anciennes notes, j'aurais gagné du temps. si le budget augmente, nous achèterons aussi de nouveaux logiciels. chaque personne recevra un rôle précis. ce serait plus simple avec un assistant, mais nous ferons le travail ensemble.` how it satisfies the brief: future forms include `commencerai`, `travaillerai`, `aurons`, `réserverons`, `achèterons`, `recevra`, `ferons`; conditionals include `j'aimerais`, `voudraient`, and `serait`; the future-time clause is `quand nous aurons...`; the pluperfect is `j'avais déjà préparé`; the past conditional is `j'aurais gagné`; the `si` clauses follow correct patterns; and the indefinites include `plusieurs`, `toutes`, `quelques`, and `chaque`. mark yourself wrong if: - a future-time `quand` or `dès que` clause uses the present by english habit; - a future or conditional appears immediately after `si`; - `tout` fails to agree with its noun; - a past conditional uses a present auxiliary instead of a conditional auxiliary; - a conditional request accidentally uses future `voudrai` instead of `voudrais`. "},{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 15 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-exercises\">Chapter 15 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I will speak.</li>\n<li>She will come.</li>\n<li>I would like...</li>\n<li>You should leave.</li>\n<li>We had finished.</li>\n<li>all the houses</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parlerai.</code> — Regular future uses the infinitive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code> plus -ai for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle viendra.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code> has the irregular future stem viendr-, plus -a for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais...</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrais</code> is conditional and is the polite form for “I would like.”</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais partir.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devrais</code> is conditional of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">devoir</code>, used for advice.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avions fini.</code> — The pluperfect uses the imparfait auxiliary <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avions</code> plus participle <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fini</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes les maisons</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maisons</code> is feminine plural, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>future tense</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parlerai.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>conditional</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un café.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>earlier past and unreal past</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'avais fini.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>tout and indefinites</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute la journée</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct answer must show the named structure and change the model sentence's content.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Future tense:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous finirons le chapitre demain.</code> — future stem plus -ons for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditional:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais une table près de la fenêtre.</code> — polite conditional request.</li>\n<li><strong>Earlier past and unreal past:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais réservé plus tôt, j'aurais trouvé une place.</code> — pluperfect condition plus past conditional result.</li>\n<li><strong>Tout and indefinites:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tous les étudiants recevront un message.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous</code> agrees with masculine plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">étudiants</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you put a future or conditional directly after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code>, or if <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> does not agree with the noun.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Worked example from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous irons à la banque.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, elle ira à la banque.</code> — irregular future stem ir-, ending -a for <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle</code>.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous n'irons pas à la banque.</code> — the negative wraps the future verb.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que nous irons à la banque demain ?</code> — the future form stays <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">irons</code>.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous irons au musée.</code> — the destination changes, but the future tense does not.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own sentences, check the stem first, then the ending, then any <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> pattern or indefinite agreement.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous irons à la banque et nous parlerons au directeur.</code> — The trap is the irregular future stem <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ir-</code> beside regular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlerons</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand j'arriverai, je t'appellerai.</code> — French uses the future in both halves when <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quand</code> points to future time.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrais</code> is conditional for politeness; future <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voudrai</code> would change the meaning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle avait déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avait terminé</code> is pluperfect, earlier than the passé composé arrival.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais dans tous ces pays.</code> — The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause uses the imparfait, not the conditional.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Toutes les réponses sont importantes.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes</code> must be feminine plural to agree with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponses</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct in passes: first identify the tense frame, then check the stem, then the ending, then agreement in the indefinite word.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write future plans and a hypothetical alternative: five future sentences, four conditional sentences, and one past-conditional sentence.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model response:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, je partirai tôt.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prendrai le train.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous visiterons un musée.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes amis viendront avec moi.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le soir, nous dînerons près de la gare.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'aimerais rester plus longtemps.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous pourrions louer une voiture.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais réserver maintenant.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ce serait plus simple avec plus de temps.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Avec une autre date, j'aurais choisi un hôtel moins cher.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: five future forms, four conditional forms, and one past conditional form. If you use a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> clause, keep future and conditional out of the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">si</code> half.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Future tense.</strong> Use the infinitive or irregular future stem plus future endings. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle viendra demain.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Conditional.</strong> Use the future stem plus imparfait endings for politeness, wishes, advice, or hypothetical results. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un café.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Earlier past and unreal past.</strong> The pluperfect uses an imparfait auxiliary; the past conditional uses a conditional auxiliary. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais su, je serais venu.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Tout and indefinites.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code> agrees as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tout</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes</code>; words such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chaque</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aucun</code> also have number rules. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Toutes les maisons sont grandes.</code></li>\n</ol></div></details>","search":"# chapter 15 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i will speak. 2. she will come. 3. i would like... 4. you should leave. 5. we had finished. 6. all the houses 1. `je parlerai.` — regular future uses the infinitive `parler` plus -ai for `je`. 2. `elle viendra.` — `venir` has the irregular future stem viendr-, plus -a for `elle`. 3. `je voudrais...` — `voudrais` is conditional and is the polite form for “i would like.” 4. `tu devrais partir.` — `devrais` is conditional of `devoir`, used for advice. 5. `nous avions fini.` — the pluperfect uses the imparfait auxiliary `avions` plus participle `fini`. 6. `toutes les maisons` — `maisons` is feminine plural, so `tout` becomes `toutes`. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **future tense**. use `je parlerai.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **conditional**. use `je voudrais un café.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **earlier past and unreal past**. use `j'avais fini.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **tout and indefinites**. use `toute la journée` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct answer must show the named structure and change the model sentence's content. 1. **future tense:** `nous finirons le chapitre demain.` — future stem plus -ons for `nous`. 2. **conditional:** `je voudrais une table près de la fenêtre.` — polite conditional request. 3. **earlier past and unreal past:** `si j'avais réservé plus tôt, j'aurais trouvé une place.` — pluperfect condition plus past conditional result. 4. **tout and indefinites:** `tous les étudiants recevront un message.` — `tous` agrees with masculine plural `étudiants`. mark yourself wrong if you put a future or conditional directly after `si`, or if `tout` does not agree with the noun. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. answers vary. worked example from `demain, nous irons à la banque.` 1. change the subject: `demain, elle ira à la banque.` — irregular future stem ir-, ending -a for `elle`. 2. make it negative: `demain, nous n'irons pas à la banque.` — the negative wraps the future verb. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce que nous irons à la banque demain ?` — the future form stays `irons`. 4. change one meaningful detail: `demain, nous irons au musée.` — the destination changes, but the future tense does not. for your own sentences, check the stem first, then the ending, then any `si` pattern or indefinite agreement. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `demain, nous irons à la banque et nous parlerons au directeur.` — the trap is the irregular future stem `ir-` beside regular `parlerons`. 2. `quand j'arriverai, je t'appellerai.` — french uses the future in both halves when `quand` points to future time. 3. `je voudrais ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.` — `voudrais` is conditional for politeness; future `voudrai` would change the meaning. 4. `elle avait déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.` — `avait terminé` is pluperfect, earlier than the passé composé arrival. 5. `si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais dans tous ces pays.` — the `si` clause uses the imparfait, not the conditional. 6. `toutes les réponses sont importantes.` — `toutes` must be feminine plural to agree with `réponses`. correct in passes: first identify the tense frame, then check the stem, then the ending, then agreement in the indefinite word. ## e. personal writing write future plans and a hypothetical alternative: five future sentences, four conditional sentences, and one past-conditional sentence. answers vary. a model response: 1. `demain, je partirai tôt.` 2. `je prendrai le train.` 3. `nous visiterons un musée.` 4. `mes amis viendront avec moi.` 5. `le soir, nous dînerons près de la gare.` 6. `j'aimerais rester plus longtemps.` 7. `nous pourrions louer une voiture.` 8. `tu devrais réserver maintenant.` 9. `ce serait plus simple avec plus de temps.` 10. `avec une autre date, j'aurais choisi un hôtel moins cher.` self-check: five future forms, four conditional forms, and one past conditional form. if you use a `si` clause, keep future and conditional out of the `si` half. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **future tense.** use the infinitive or irregular future stem plus future endings. example: `elle viendra demain.` 2. **conditional.** use the future stem plus imparfait endings for politeness, wishes, advice, or hypothetical results. example: `je voudrais un café.` 3. **earlier past and unreal past.** the pluperfect uses an imparfait auxiliary; the past conditional uses a conditional auxiliary. example: `si j'avais su, je serais venu.` 4. **tout and indefinites.** `tout` agrees as `tout`, `toute`, `tous`, or `toutes`; words such as `chaque`, `plusieurs`, and `aucun` also have number rules. example: `toutes les maisons sont grandes.` "},{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 15 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-answer-key\">Chapter 15 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parlerai.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle viendra.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais...</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais partir.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avions fini.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes les maisons</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Future tense:</strong> Add -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont to the infinitive or irregular future stem.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditional:</strong> Use the future stem plus imparfait endings. It expresses politeness, wishes, advice, and hypothetical results.</li>\n<li><strong>Earlier past and unreal past:</strong> The pluperfect uses the imparfait of avoir/être plus a participle. The past conditional uses the conditional auxiliary plus a participle.</li>\n<li><strong>Tout and indefinites:</strong> Tout changes form as an adjective or pronoun: tout, toute, tous, toutes. Other useful indefinite forms include chaque, plusieurs, certains, aucun, and chacun.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 15 answer key ## section a 1. `je parlerai.` 2. `elle viendra.` 3. `je voudrais...` 4. `tu devrais partir.` 5. `nous avions fini.` 6. `toutes les maisons` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **future tense:** add -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont to the infinitive or irregular future stem. - **conditional:** use the future stem plus imparfait endings. it expresses politeness, wishes, advice, and hypothetical results. - **earlier past and unreal past:** the pluperfect uses the imparfait of avoir/être plus a participle. the past conditional uses the conditional auxiliary plus a participle. - **tout and indefinites:** tout changes form as an adjective or pronoun: tout, toute, tous, toutes. other useful indefinite forms include chaque, plusieurs, certains, aucun, and chacun. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 15 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 15 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Future tense</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Conditional</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Earlier past and unreal past</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Tout and indefinites</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 15 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **future tense**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **conditional** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **earlier past and unreal past**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **tout and indefinites**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":16,"slug":"chapter-16-subjunctive","title":"The Present Subjunctive","documents":[{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/README.md","title":"Chapter 16: The Present Subjunctive","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-the-present-subjunctive\">Chapter 16: The Present Subjunctive</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Form regular and common irregular subjunctives.</li>\n<li>Recognize triggers involving necessity and judgment.</li>\n<li>Use the subjunctive after volition, emotion, and doubt.</li>\n<li>Contrast indicative and subjunctive meaning.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forming the subjunctive</li>\n<li>Important irregulars</li>\n<li>Necessity and judgment</li>\n<li>Volition, emotion, and doubt</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 16: the present subjunctive this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - form regular and common irregular subjunctives. - recognize triggers involving necessity and judgment. - use the subjunctive after volition, emotion, and doubt. - contrast indicative and subjunctive meaning. ## lesson sequence 1. forming the subjunctive 2. important irregulars 3. necessity and judgment 4. volition, emotion, and doubt ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 16 Lessons: The Present Subjunctive","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-lessons-the-present-subjunctive\">Chapter 16 Lessons: The Present Subjunctive</h1>\n<p>The subjunctive is not another simple timeline tense like the future or the imparfait. It is a mood: a verb form used in certain dependent clauses after triggers involving necessity, judgment, desire, emotion, uncertainty, or doubt. In this chapter, you will learn how to form it, when it appears, and how to avoid the most frequent English-speaker mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>You will learn to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>explain what the subjunctive is and where it usually appears;</li>\n<li>form regular subjunctive verbs from the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> present-tense stem;</li>\n<li>handle stem-changing and irregular subjunctive forms, including <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code>;</li>\n<li>use the subjunctive after impersonal expressions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est important que</code>;</li>\n<li>use the subjunctive after expressions of volition, emotion, and doubt when the subject changes;</li>\n<li>contrast subjunctive-triggering uncertainty with indicative-triggering certainty.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-quick-check\">Prerequisite quick check</h2>\n<p>Before beginning, make sure you can already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>conjugate the present tense of common verbs, especially <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> forms, because regular subjunctive stems come from them;</li>\n<li>recognize dependent clauses introduced by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>;</li>\n<li>identify whether two verbs in a sentence have the same subject or different subjects;</li>\n<li>distinguish the imparfait stem from the present stem, because some <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> subjunctive forms look like imperfect-family forms.</li>\n<p>If you still hesitate with present-tense forms such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils viennent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils boivent</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils prennent</code>, review them now. They matter directly in this chapter.</p>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Test the prerequisites before you add a new mood.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Give the present <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> form of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils viennent</code>. The regular subjunctive-style stem for singular forms is often found by removing -ent.</li>\n<li>Find the dependent clause in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu partes</code>. — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu partes</code>. A dependent clause begins with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> and has its own subject and verb.</li>\n<li>Do the subjects change in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux partir</code> ? — No; both ideas belong to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>. French normally uses the infinitive, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> + subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Do the subjects change in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu partes</code> ? — Yes: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code> wants, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code> leaves. That change makes the subjunctive clause natural.</li>\n<li>Which looks like an imparfait-family ending: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous parlions</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code> ? — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous parlions</code>. The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> subjunctive endings resemble imparfait endings, so do not confuse the stem rules.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you missed two or more, review present stems and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> clauses first. Subjunctive errors usually begin before the subjunctive verb itself.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-this-chapter\">How to study this chapter</h2>\n<p>Always learn the trigger and the verb together: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que + subjunctive</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je doute que + subjunctive</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pense que + indicative</code>. When you practice, ask two questions first: “What is the trigger?” and “Did the subject change?”</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-what-the-subjunctive-is-and-how-regular-forms-are-built\">Unit 1: What the subjunctive is and how regular forms are built</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>The subjunctive most often appears in a dependent clause introduced by <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, after a trigger in the main clause. It is especially common when the main clause expresses necessity, will, emotion, or uncertainty, and when the subject changes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.</code> — You must arrive before nine o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses.</code> — I want you to succeed.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes contents qu'elle soit ici.</code> — We are happy that she is here.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"regular-formation-rule\">Regular formation rule</h3>\n<p>Start from the third-person plural present form, remove <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code>, and add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-endings\">The endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ions</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-parler\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parler</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code></td>\n<td>that I speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parles</code></td>\n<td>that you speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parle</code></td>\n<td>that he/she/we speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlions</code></td>\n<td>that we speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parliez</code></td>\n<td>that you speak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlent</code></td>\n<td>that they speak</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"another-regular-model-finir\">Another regular model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finir</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finisse</code></td>\n<td>that I finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finisses</code></td>\n<td>that you finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finisse</code></td>\n<td>that he/she/we finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissions</code></td>\n<td>that we finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissiez</code></td>\n<td>that you finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissent</code></td>\n<td>that they finish</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"same-subject-vs-different-subject\">Same subject vs different subject</h3>\n<p>French often prefers an infinitive instead of a subjunctive clause when the subject stays the same.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux partir.</code> — I want to leave.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu partes.</code> — I want you to leave.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h3>\n<p>Many subjunctive singular forms sound like present-tense forms. The difference often matters more in writing and in sentence structure than in sound alone.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use the subjunctive just because there is a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> clause. The trigger matters.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais qu'il vient.</code> — I know he is coming. (indicative, certainty)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux qu'il vienne.</code> — I want him to come. (subjunctive, volition)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Build the subjunctive of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">attendre</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vendre</code> for all six persons.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux partir</code> to a sentence with a different subject.</li>\n<li>In each sentence, identify the trigger and the subject of both clauses.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que j'attende</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu attendes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il attende</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous attendions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que vous attendiez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils attendent</code> — start from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils attendent</code>, remove -ent, then add subjunctive endings.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je vende</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu vendes</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle vende</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous vendions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que vous vendiez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elles vendent</code> — the stem is vend-, from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils vendent</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu partes.</code> — changing from same subject to different subject makes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> + subjunctive natural. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux partir.</code> stays infinitive because the subject is still <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li>In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu partes</code>, the trigger is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je veux que</code>; the main-clause subject is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>, and the dependent-clause subject is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-stem-changing-and-irregular-subjunctive-forms\">Unit 2: Stem-changing and irregular subjunctive forms</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Many verbs still follow the regular endings, but the stem may change. Some very common verbs are completely irregular and must be memorized as full sets.</p>\n<h3 id=\"stem-changing-pattern-two-stems-in-one-paradigm\">Stem-changing pattern: two stems in one paradigm</h3>\n<p>For many verbs, the singular forms and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code> come from the third-person plural present stem, while <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> line up with the stem you already know from the present/imparfait family.</p>\n<h3 id=\"full-model-venir\">Full model: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vienne</code></td>\n<td>that I come</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viennes</code></td>\n<td>that you come</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vienne</code></td>\n<td>that he/she/we come</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venions</code></td>\n<td>that we come</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veniez</code></td>\n<td>that you come</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viennent</code></td>\n<td>that they come</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"another-useful-pattern-boire\">Another useful pattern: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je boive</code> — that I drink</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu boives</code> — that you drink</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il boive</code> — that he drinks</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous buvions</code> — that we drink</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que vous buviez</code> — that you drink</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils boivent</code> — that they drink</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"core-irregular-verbs\">Core irregular verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sois</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soit</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soyez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soient</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aie</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aies</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ait</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayons</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aient</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ailles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">allions</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">alliez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aillent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fasse</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fasses</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fasse</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fassions</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fassiez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fassent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puisse</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puisses</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puisse</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puissions</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puissiez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puissent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sache</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">saches</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sache</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachions</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachiez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sachent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuilles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voulions</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouliez</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuillent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"fixed-one-form-verbs\">Fixed one-form verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">falloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il faille</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je regrette qu'il faille partir si tôt.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pleuvoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il pleuve</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute qu'il pleuve demain.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not assume that the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> subjunctive forms always use the same stem as the singular forms. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Que nous venions</code> does not match <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils viennent</code> letter for letter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the six subjunctive forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> from memory.</li>\n<li>Conjugate <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code> in the subjunctive with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code>.</li>\n<li>Build three clauses beginning with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que...</code>, each using a different irregular subjunctive form.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Être:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je sois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu sois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il soit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous soyons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que vous soyez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elles soient</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoir:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que j'aie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu aies</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle ait</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous ayons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que vous ayez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils aient</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous venions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils viennent</code> — the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> stem differs from the singular and plural third-person stem.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous buvions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils boivent</code> — the stem changes between buv- and boiv-.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous fassions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils fassent</code> — irregular stem fass-.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que je sois prêt.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut qu'elle aille au bureau.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que nous puissions répondre.</code> Each sentence has an impersonal trigger and an irregular subjunctive form.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-impersonal-expressions-of-necessity-and-judgment\">Unit 3: Impersonal expressions of necessity and judgment</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Many impersonal expressions trigger the subjunctive because they present an action as necessary, desirable, important, possible, or regrettable rather than as a plain fact.</p>\n<h3 id=\"high-frequency-triggers\">High-frequency triggers</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Trigger</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code></td>\n<td>it is necessary that / must</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est important que</code></td>\n<td>it is important that</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est important que tu comprennes cette règle.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vaut mieux que</code></td>\n<td>it is better that</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il vaut mieux que nous partions maintenant.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est possible que</code></td>\n<td>it is possible that</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible qu'elle sache déjà la réponse.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est dommage que</code></td>\n<td>it is a shame that</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est dommage qu'ils soient absents.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"more-examples\">More examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que mes amis puissent venir à la fête.</code> — My friends have to be able to come to the party.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il vaut mieux que vous preniez le train plus tôt.</code> — It is better that you take the earlier train.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible qu'on ait oublié un détail.</code> — It is possible that we forgot a detail.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"meaning-note\">Meaning note</h3>\n<p>These expressions do not state a fact directly; they frame the second clause as evaluated, necessary, or uncertain. That is why the subjunctive appears.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not replace the subjunctive with an infinitive after these impersonal triggers when the clause has its own subject.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>incorrect: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">*Il faut vous arrivez avant neuf heures.</code></li>\n<li>correct: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Finish three sentences beginning with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que...</code>.</li>\n<li>Write one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est important que</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vaut mieux que</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est possible que</code>.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible qu'elle soit en retard.</code> into a sentence of certainty and decide whether the subjunctive remains.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que tu arrives à l'heure.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code> expresses necessity, so use the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que nous finissions ce travail.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissions</code> is the subjunctive form after the trigger.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut qu'ils soient prudents.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> is irregular: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils soient</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est important que vous compreniez la règle.</code> — judgment of importance triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il vaut mieux que nous partions maintenant.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vaut mieux que</code> also triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible qu'elle sache la réponse.</code> — possibility is not presented as certain fact, so use the subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Certainty version: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est certain qu'elle est en retard.</code> The subjunctive does not remain, because certainty presents the clause as a fact and uses the indicative.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-volition-emotion-and-doubt\">Unit 4: Volition, emotion, and doubt</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Personal expressions of desire, emotion, fear, and doubt usually trigger the subjunctive when the subject of the second clause is different.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-trigger-groups\">Common trigger groups</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Trigger</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>volition</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu viennes.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>preference</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous préférons que vous restiez ici.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>emotion</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être content(e) que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes heureux qu'elle soit ici avec nous.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fear</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir peur que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai peur qu'il oublie l'adresse.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>doubt</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">douter que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute qu'ils puissent finir aujourd'hui.</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negative opinion</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne pas penser que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne pas croire que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'il connaisse la réponse.</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"more-examples\">More examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que mes amis puissent venir à la fête.</code> — I want my friends to be able to come to the party.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est contente que nous soyons là.</code> — She is happy that we are here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils doutent que le projet avance assez vite.</code> — They doubt that the project is moving fast enough.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai peur que vous soyez déjà partis.</code> — I am afraid that you have already left.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"same-subject-reminder\">Same-subject reminder</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux réussir.</code> — I want to succeed. (same subject → infinitive)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que mon frère réussisse.</code> — I want my brother to succeed. (different subject → subjunctive)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Do not use the indicative after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne pense pas que</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je doute que</code> in standard careful French.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>standard: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'il vienne.</code></li>\n<li>standard: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute qu'il soit prêt.</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Write one sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir que</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être content que</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir peur que</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">douter que</code>.</li>\n<li>Change each sentence so the second clause has a different subject.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que je réussisse</code> in the more natural infinitive pattern.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu viennes demain.</code> — desire plus a different subject triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes contents qu'elle soit ici.</code> — emotion about someone else's situation triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai peur qu'il oublie l'adresse.</code> — fear triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute qu'ils puissent finir aujourd'hui.</code> — doubt triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Same-subject contrast: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux réussir.</code> is more natural than <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que je réussisse</code>, because French normally uses the infinitive when the subject does not change.</li>\n<li>If you change the dependent subject, the subjunctive returns: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que mon frère réussisse.</code></li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"unit-5-certainty-vs-uncertainty-indicative-or-subjunctive\">Unit 5: Certainty vs uncertainty — indicative or subjunctive?</h2>\n<h3 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h3>\n<p>Some verbs change mood according to meaning. A statement of certainty usually takes the indicative; a statement of doubt, denial, or uncertainty often triggers the subjunctive.</p>\n<h3 id=\"key-contrasts\">Key contrasts</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Certainty / assertion</th>\n<th>Mood</th>\n<th>Doubt / negation</th>\n<th>Mood</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense qu'il vient.</code></td>\n<td>indicative</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'il vienne.</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je crois qu'elle a raison.</code></td>\n<td>indicative</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne crois pas qu'elle ait raison.</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est certain qu'ils sont là.</code></td>\n<td>indicative</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'est pas certain qu'ils soient là.</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais que tu peux le faire.</code></td>\n<td>indicative</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute que tu puisses le faire.</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"practical-rule\">Practical rule</h3>\n<p>Ask yourself whether the main clause presents the second clause as a fact or as uncertain / desired / judged. If it is presented as a fact, the indicative is usually right. If not, the subjunctive often appears.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistake-for-english-speakers\">Common mistake for English speakers</h3>\n<p>Because English rarely changes the verb form in these clauses, learners often miss the French contrast entirely. Memorize the pairs as opposites: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pense que</code> vs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne pense pas que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je crois que</code> vs <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne crois pas que</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sentence-building-practice\">Sentence-building practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Turn these certainties into doubts: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense qu'il comprend.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous croyons qu'elle viendra.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est certain qu'ils sont prêts.</code></li>\n<li>Turn these doubts back into certainties.</li>\n<li>Write one pair of your own: affirmative certainty + negative doubt.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'il comprenne.</code> — negative opinion triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne croyons pas qu'elle vienne.</code> — the future idea is still expressed with the present subjunctive after doubt or negation.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'est pas certain qu'ils soient prêts.</code> — uncertainty triggers the subjunctive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soient</code>.</li>\n<li>Back to certainty: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense qu'il comprend.</code> — affirmative certainty uses the indicative.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous croyons qu'elle viendra.</code> — belief/assertion uses the indicative, and the future form is possible here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est certain qu'ils sont prêts.</code> — certainty uses indicative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sont</code>.</li>\n<li>Your own pair could be: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais qu'elle a raison.</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute qu'elle ait raison.</code> The first presents a fact; the second presents doubt, so the mood changes.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"cumulative-sentence-usage-practice\">Cumulative sentence-usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud twice, then cover it and rebuild it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.</code> — You must arrive before nine o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que mes amis puissent venir à la fête.</code> — I want my friends to be able to come to the party.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes heureux qu'elle soit ici avec nous.</code> — We are happy that she is here with us.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'il connaisse la réponse.</code> — I do not think that he knows the answer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible que nous ayons oublié un document.</code> — It is possible that we forgot a document.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je crois qu'elle comprend la situation, mais je doute qu'elle accepte ce plan.</code> — I think she understands the situation, but I doubt that she will accept this plan.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>When you rebuild these sentences, mark the trigger first. The verb form only makes sense after you know whether the main clause presents the second clause as fact, desire, emotion, or uncertainty.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Necessity and possibility.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible que</code> trigger the subjunctive: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriviez</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayons oublié</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Desire and emotion.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes heureux que</code> trigger the subjunctive: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puissent</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soit</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Negative opinion and doubt.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas que</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je doute que</code> trigger the subjunctive: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaisse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">accepte</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Certainty.</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je crois que</code> presents the clause as believed fact, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprend</code> is indicative.</li>\n<li><strong>Accents and apostrophes.</strong> Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayons</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponse</code> exactly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A correct answer must label the mood from the trigger, not from English translation alone.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>circle the trigger expression;</li>\n<li>underline the verb in the dependent clause;</li>\n<li>label the dependent clause <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">SUBJ</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">IND</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Trigger</th>\n<th>Dependent verb</th>\n<th>Mood</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriviez</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puissent</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes heureux que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soit</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">connaisse</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayons oublié</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6a</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je crois que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">comprend</code></td>\n<td>indicative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6b</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je doute que</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">accepte</code></td>\n<td>subjunctive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Sentence 6 is the contrast to memorize: affirmative belief uses the indicative, but doubt uses the subjunctive. The word <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> alone does not decide the mood.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject of the main clause or dependent clause;</li>\n<li>change the trigger so that the mood also changes if necessary.</li>\n<p>Then turn sentence 6 into two sentences that both use the indicative.</p>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>One worked set:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que tu arrives avant neuf heures.</code> Trigger change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais que vous arrivez avant neuf heures.</code> The certainty trigger changes the mood to indicative.</li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que mon ami puisse venir à la fête.</code> Trigger change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense que mes amis pourront venir à la fête.</code> Belief takes the indicative, here future <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pourront</code>.</li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes heureux que vous soyez ici avec nous.</code> Trigger change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous savons qu'elle est ici avec nous.</code> Certainty uses indicative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">est</code>.</li>\n<li>Subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'ils connaissent la réponse.</code> Trigger change: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense qu'il connaît la réponse.</code> Affirmative opinion normally uses the indicative.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sentence 6 as two indicative sentences: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je crois qu'elle comprend la situation. Je suis sûr qu'elle acceptera ce plan.</code> Certainty presents both clauses as facts or predictions, so the subjunctive disappears.</p></div></details>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one with an impersonal trigger;</li>\n<li>one with desire or preference;</li>\n<li>one with emotion;</li>\n<li>one with certainty contrasted against doubt.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impersonal trigger: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que je dorme plus tôt.</code> — necessity triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Desire or preference: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère que mes amis viennent samedi.</code> — preference plus a different subject triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Emotion: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis content que tu sois là.</code> — emotion about another subject triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Certainty contrasted with doubt: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais qu'elle comprend, mais je doute qu'elle accepte.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sais que</code> takes the indicative; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doute que</code> takes the subjunctive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you use the subjunctive after a certainty trigger, or if you keep <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> + subjunctive when the same subject should use an infinitive.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"mini-dialogue-before-the-exam\">Mini-dialogue: before the exam</h2>\n<p><strong>Maya :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que nous arrivions un peu en avance demain.</code> — We need to arrive a little early tomorrow. <strong>Jonas :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Oui, et il est important que nous ayons tous nos papiers.</code> — Yes, and it is important that we have all our documents. <strong>Maya :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu crois que le professeur sera déjà là ?</code> — Do you think the teacher will already be there? <strong>Jonas :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense qu'il sera là, mais je ne crois pas qu'il commence exactement à huit heures.</code> — I think he will be there, but I do not think he will start exactly at eight o'clock. <strong>Maya :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu me rappelles ce soir de préparer ma carte d'identité.</code> — I want you to remind me tonight to prepare my ID card. <strong>Jonas :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">D'accord, mais j'ai peur que tu oublies quand même.</code> — Okay, but I'm afraid you will forget anyway. <strong>Maya :</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ça m'étonnerait, parce que je suis contente que tu sois aussi organisé.</code> — That would surprise me, because I am glad that you are so organized.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guided-analysis\">Guided analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Find three subjunctive forms and one indicative form after a certainty trigger.</li>\n<li>Explain why <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il sera là</code> is indicative but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il commence</code> is subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Rewrite <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu me rappelles</code> so that the subject stays the same and the infinitive becomes possible.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">j'ai peur que tu oublies</code> into a sentence with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pense que</code> and explain the mood change.</li>\n<li>Add one more line using <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vaut mieux que</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Subjunctive forms include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arrivions</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayons</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">commence</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rappelles</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">oublies</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sois</code>. An indicative form after a certainty trigger is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il sera</code> in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense qu'il sera là</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il sera là</code> is indicative because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense que</code> presents the idea as a belief or assertion. <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il commence</code> is subjunctive because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne crois pas que</code> is negative opinion and does not present the start time as certain.</li>\n<li>Same subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux me rappeler ce soir de préparer ma carte d'identité.</code> With the same subject, French normally uses an infinitive construction instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> + subjunctive.</li>\n<li>With certainty: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pense que tu oublieras quand même.</code> The mood changes to indicative because <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pense que</code> asserts a likely fact; the future is natural because the forgetting is later.</li>\n<li>Added line: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il vaut mieux que nous préparions nos sacs ce soir.</code> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il vaut mieux que</code> is a judgment trigger, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préparions</code> is subjunctive.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"connected-reading-les-conseils-d-une-s-ur-a-n-e\">Connected reading: les conseils d'une sœur aînée</h2>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur veut que je prenne mon année universitaire plus au sérieux. Elle insiste pour que je fasse un vrai planning et pour que je choisisse mes priorités plus clairement. Il est important, selon elle, que je sois moins dispersé et que j'aie un espace de travail plus calme. Je sais qu'elle a de bonnes intentions, et je crois qu'elle comprend assez bien mes difficultés. Pourtant, il est possible qu'elle sous-estime le temps que prennent mes trajets quotidiens. Je suis quand même reconnaissant qu'elle m'encourage, même quand elle doute que je change mes habitudes très vite.</code> — My sister wants me to take my university year more seriously. She insists that I make a real schedule and choose my priorities more clearly. According to her, it is important that I be less scattered and have a quieter workspace. I know that she has good intentions, and I think that she understands my difficulties fairly well. Yet it is possible that she underestimates how much time my daily commutes take. I am still grateful that she encourages me, even when she doubts that I will change my habits very quickly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-practice\">Reading practice</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>List the subjunctive triggers in the paragraph.</li>\n<li>Separate the subjunctive clauses from the indicative clauses.</li>\n<li>Find one sentence of certainty and one of uncertainty.</li>\n<li>Rewrite the first two sentences so that the narrator talks about <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> instead of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</li>\n<li>Write three follow-up sentences: one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code>, one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pense que</code>, and one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je doute que</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li>Triggers: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veut que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">insiste pour que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est important que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">suis reconnaissant que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">doute que</code>.</li>\n<li>Subjunctive clauses: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je prenne</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je fasse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je choisisse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je sois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que j'aie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle sous-estime</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle m'encourage</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je change</code>. Indicative clauses: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle a</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle comprend</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que prennent mes trajets quotidiens</code> inside the factual noun phrase about commute time.</li>\n<li>Certainty: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais qu'elle a de bonnes intentions</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je crois qu'elle comprend assez bien mes difficultés</code>. Uncertainty: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est possible qu'elle sous-estime...</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle doute que je change...</code>.</li>\n<li>With <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ma sœur veut que nous prenions notre année universitaire plus au sérieux. Elle insiste pour que nous fassions un vrai planning et pour que nous choisissions nos priorités plus clairement.</code> The subject in the dependent clause changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code>, so the subjunctive forms change too.</li>\n<li>Follow-up: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que je fasse un planning réaliste. Je pense que ma sœur a raison. Je doute que je change tout en une semaine.</code> Necessity and doubt take the subjunctive; affirmative belief takes the indicative.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes-to-avoid\">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forming the subjunctive from the infinitive instead of the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> present stem.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the special <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> stems in verbs such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">boire</code>.</li>\n<li>Using the subjunctive after every <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> clause, even after certainty expressions such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je sais que</code>.</li>\n<li>Using the indicative after <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je doute que</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je ne pense pas que</code>.</li>\n<li>Keeping a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> clause when the subject stays the same and an infinitive is more natural.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that some forms are highly irregular: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aille</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fasse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puisse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sache</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuille</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-reference-sheet\">Chapter reference sheet</h2>\n<h3 id=\"regular-subjunctive-endings\">Regular subjunctive endings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pronoun</th>\n<th>Ending</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il/elle/on</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ions</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-iez</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"formation-shortcut\">Formation shortcut</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>take the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils/elles</code> present form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>remove <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>add subjunctive endings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>note</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous</code> / <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> may show a different stem in some verbs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"high-frequency-subjunctive-triggers\">High-frequency subjunctive triggers</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Triggers</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>necessity / judgment</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est important que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vaut mieux que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est possible que</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>volition</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">préférer que</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>emotion</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être content(e) que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être heureux(-se) que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir peur que</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>doubt / negation</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">douter que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne pas penser que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ne pas croire que</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>certainty (indicative instead)</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pense que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je crois que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je sais que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est certain que</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"core-irregular-forms\">Core irregular forms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Key forms</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sois, sois, soit, soyons, soyez, soient</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aie, aies, ait, ayons, ayez, aient</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aller</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aille, ailles, aille, allions, alliez, aillent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">faire</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fasse, fasses, fasse, fassions, fassiez, fassent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puisse, puisses, puisse, puissions, puissiez, puissent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sache, saches, sache, sachions, sachiez, sachent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vouloir</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuille, veuilles, veuille, voulions, vouliez, veuillent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>special one-form verbs</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il faille</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il pleuve</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"final-production-assignment\">Final production assignment</h2>\n<p>Write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences giving advice, hopes, reactions, and doubts about a real situation in your life. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least three different subjunctive triggers;</li>\n<li>one impersonal expression such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vaut mieux que</code>;</li>\n<li>one sentence of desire or preference;</li>\n<li>one sentence of emotion;</li>\n<li>one sentence of doubt or negative opinion;</li>\n<li>one contrasting sentence of certainty that uses the indicative instead;</li>\n<li>one example where the same-subject version uses an infinitive.</li>\n<p>After writing:</p>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>circle every trigger expression;</li>\n<li>underline every verb in a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code> clause;</li>\n<li>label each dependent clause <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">SUBJ</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">IND</code>;</li>\n<li>check whether the subjects change where a subjunctive clause appears;</li>\n<li>read the paragraph aloud twice;</li>\n<li>rewrite any clause whose mood choice was wrong.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Here is a model paragraph that satisfies the brief:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette semaine, il faut que je prépare un entretien important. Il vaut mieux que je dorme assez et que je relise mes notes. Je veux que mon ami me pose des questions difficiles. Je suis content qu'il soit disponible ce soir. Je doute que je connaisse toutes les réponses, mais je pense que je peux expliquer mon parcours clairement. Je ne crois pas que l'entretien soit facile. Je veux réussir, donc je vais pratiquer encore une heure. Si mon ami est libre, je préfère qu'il me rappelle demain matin.</code></p>\n<p>How it satisfies the brief: the triggers include <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vaut mieux que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis content que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne crois pas que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je préfère que</code>; the certainty sentence is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pense que je peux...</code>, with indicative <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">peux</code>; and the same-subject infinitive is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux réussir</code>, because the subject stays <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je</code>.</p>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a certainty trigger such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je sais que</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je pense que</code> takes the subjunctive without a negative or doubt meaning;</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code> is followed by an indicative form;</li>\n<li>the same subject uses an awkward <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je...</code> clause where an infinitive is natural;</li>\n<li>an irregular form such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soit</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fasse</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puisse</code> is replaced by a regular-looking form;</li>\n<li>you choose the mood from English wording instead of from the French trigger.</li>\n</ul></div></details>","search":"# chapter 16 lessons: the present subjunctive the subjunctive is not another simple timeline tense like the future or the imparfait. it is a mood: a verb form used in certain dependent clauses after triggers involving necessity, judgment, desire, emotion, uncertainty, or doubt. in this chapter, you will learn how to form it, when it appears, and how to avoid the most frequent english-speaker mistakes. ## learning objectives you will learn to: 1. explain what the subjunctive is and where it usually appears; 2. form regular subjunctive verbs from the `ils` present-tense stem; 3. handle stem-changing and irregular subjunctive forms, including `être`, `avoir`, `aller`, `faire`, `pouvoir`, `savoir`, and `vouloir`; 4. use the subjunctive after impersonal expressions such as `il faut que` and `il est important que`; 5. use the subjunctive after expressions of volition, emotion, and doubt when the subject changes; 6. contrast subjunctive-triggering uncertainty with indicative-triggering certainty. ## prerequisite quick check before beginning, make sure you can already: - conjugate the present tense of common verbs, especially `ils` forms, because regular subjunctive stems come from them; - recognize dependent clauses introduced by `que`; - identify whether two verbs in a sentence have the same subject or different subjects; - distinguish the imparfait stem from the present stem, because some `nous` and `vous` subjunctive forms look like imperfect-family forms. if you still hesitate with present-tense forms such as `ils viennent`, `ils boivent`, or `ils prennent`, review them now. they matter directly in this chapter. test the prerequisites before you add a new mood. 1. give the present `ils` form of `venir`. — `ils viennent`. the regular subjunctive-style stem for singular forms is often found by removing -ent. 2. find the dependent clause in `je veux que tu partes`. — `que tu partes`. a dependent clause begins with `que` and has its own subject and verb. 3. do the subjects change in `je veux partir` ? — no; both ideas belong to `je`. french normally uses the infinitive, not `que` + subjunctive. 4. do the subjects change in `je veux que tu partes` ? — yes: `je` wants, `tu` leaves. that change makes the subjunctive clause natural. 5. which looks like an imparfait-family ending: `que nous parlions` or `que je parle` ? — `que nous parlions`. the `nous` and `vous` subjunctive endings resemble imparfait endings, so do not confuse the stem rules. if you missed two or more, review present stems and `que` clauses first. subjunctive errors usually begin before the subjunctive verb itself. ## how to study this chapter always learn the trigger and the verb together: `il faut que + subjunctive`, `je doute que + subjunctive`, `je pense que + indicative`. when you practice, ask two questions first: “what is the trigger?” and “did the subject change?” ## unit 1: what the subjunctive is and how regular forms are built ### core idea the subjunctive most often appears in a dependent clause introduced by `que`, after a trigger in the main clause. it is especially common when the main clause expresses necessity, will, emotion, or uncertainty, and when the subject changes. - `il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.` — you must arrive before nine o'clock. - `je veux que tu réussisses.` — i want you to succeed. - `nous sommes contents qu'elle soit ici.` — we are happy that she is here. ### regular formation rule start from the third-person plural present form, remove `-ent`, and add `-e`, `-es`, `-e`, `-ions`, `-iez`, `-ent`. ### the endings | pronoun | ending | |---|---| | `je` | `-e` | | `tu` | `-es` | | `il/elle/on` | `-e` | | `nous` | `-ions` | | `vous` | `-iez` | | `ils/elles` | `-ent` | ### full model: `parler` | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `que je` | `parle` | that i speak | | `que tu` | `parles` | that you speak | | `qu'il/elle/on` | `parle` | that he/she/we speak | | `que nous` | `parlions` | that we speak | | `que vous` | `parliez` | that you speak | | `qu'ils/elles` | `parlent` | that they speak | ### another regular model: `finir` | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `que je` | `finisse` | that i finish | | `que tu` | `finisses` | that you finish | | `qu'il/elle/on` | `finisse` | that he/she/we finish | | `que nous` | `finissions` | that we finish | | `que vous` | `finissiez` | that you finish | | `qu'ils/elles` | `finissent` | that they finish | ### same subject vs different subject french often prefers an infinitive instead of a subjunctive clause when the subject stays the same. - `je veux partir.` — i want to leave. - `je veux que tu partes.` — i want you to leave. ### pronunciation note many subjunctive singular forms sound like present-tense forms. the difference often matters more in writing and in sentence structure than in sound alone. ### common mistake for english speakers do not use the subjunctive just because there is a `que` clause. the trigger matters. - `je sais qu'il vient.` — i know he is coming. (indicative, certainty) - `je veux qu'il vienne.` — i want him to come. (subjunctive, volition) ### sentence-building practice 1. build the subjunctive of `attendre` and `vendre` for all six persons. 2. change `je veux partir` to a sentence with a different subject. 3. in each sentence, identify the trigger and the subject of both clauses. 1. `que j'attende`, `que tu attendes`, `qu'il attende`, `que nous attendions`, `que vous attendiez`, `qu'ils attendent` — start from `ils attendent`, remove -ent, then add subjunctive endings. 2. `que je vende`, `que tu vendes`, `qu'elle vende`, `que nous vendions`, `que vous vendiez`, `qu'elles vendent` — the stem is vend-, from `ils vendent`. 3. `je veux que tu partes.` — changing from same subject to different subject makes `que` + subjunctive natural. `je veux partir.` stays infinitive because the subject is still `je`. 4. in `je veux que tu partes`, the trigger is `je veux que`; the main-clause subject is `je`, and the dependent-clause subject is `tu`. ## unit 2: stem-changing and irregular subjunctive forms ### core idea many verbs still follow the regular endings, but the stem may change. some very common verbs are completely irregular and must be memorized as full sets. ### stem-changing pattern: two stems in one paradigm for many verbs, the singular forms and `ils/elles` come from the third-person plural present stem, while `nous` and `vous` line up with the stem you already know from the present/imparfait family. ### full model: `venir` | pronoun | french | english | |---|---|---| | `que je` | `vienne` | that i come | | `que tu` | `viennes` | that you come | | `qu'il/elle/on` | `vienne` | that he/she/we come | | `que nous` | `venions` | that we come | | `que vous` | `veniez` | that you come | | `qu'ils/elles` | `viennent` | that they come | ### another useful pattern: `boire` - `que je boive` — that i drink - `que tu boives` — that you drink - `qu'il boive` — that he drinks - `que nous buvions` — that we drink - `que vous buviez` — that you drink - `qu'ils boivent` — that they drink ### core irregular verbs | verb | `je` | `tu` | `il/elle/on` | `nous` | `vous` | `ils/elles` | |---|---|---|---|---|---|---| | `être` | `sois` | `sois` | `soit` | `soyons` | `soyez` | `soient` | | `avoir` | `aie` | `aies` | `ait` | `ayons` | `ayez` | `aient` | | `aller` | `aille` | `ailles` | `aille` | `allions` | `alliez` | `aillent` | | `faire` | `fasse` | `fasses` | `fasse` | `fassions` | `fassiez` | `fassent` | | `pouvoir` | `puisse` | `puisses` | `puisse` | `puissions` | `puissiez` | `puissent` | | `savoir` | `sache` | `saches` | `sache` | `sachions` | `sachiez` | `sachent` | | `vouloir` | `veuille` | `veuilles` | `veuille` | `voulions` | `vouliez` | `veuillent` | ### fixed one-form verbs | verb | form | example | |---|---|---| | `falloir` | `qu'il faille` | `je regrette qu'il faille partir si tôt.` | | `pleuvoir` | `qu'il pleuve` | `je doute qu'il pleuve demain.` | ### common mistake for english speakers do not assume that the `nous` and `vous` subjunctive forms always use the same stem as the singular forms. `que nous venions` does not match `qu'ils viennent` letter for letter. ### sentence-building practice 1. write the six subjunctive forms of `être` and `avoir` from memory. 2. conjugate `venir`, `boire`, and `faire` in the subjunctive with `nous` and `ils`. 3. build three clauses beginning with `il faut que...`, each using a different irregular subjunctive form. 1. **être:** `que je sois`, `que tu sois`, `qu'il soit`, `que nous soyons`, `que vous soyez`, `qu'elles soient`. 2. **avoir:** `que j'aie`, `que tu aies`, `qu'elle ait`, `que nous ayons`, `que vous ayez`, `qu'ils aient`. 3. `venir`: `que nous venions`, `qu'ils viennent` — the `nous` stem differs from the singular and plural third-person stem. 4. `boire`: `que nous buvions`, `qu'ils boivent` — the stem changes between buv- and boiv-. 5. `faire`: `que nous fassions`, `qu'ils fassent` — irregular stem fass-. 6. `il faut que je sois prêt.` / `il faut qu'elle aille au bureau.` / `il faut que nous puissions répondre.` each sentence has an impersonal trigger and an irregular subjunctive form. ## unit 3: impersonal expressions of necessity and judgment ### core idea many impersonal expressions trigger the subjunctive because they present an action as necessary, desirable, important, possible, or regrettable rather than as a plain fact. ### high-frequency triggers | trigger | meaning | example | |---|---|---| | `il faut que` | it is necessary that / must | `il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.` | | `il est important que` | it is important that | `il est important que tu comprennes cette règle.` | | `il vaut mieux que` | it is better that | `il vaut mieux que nous partions maintenant.` | | `il est possible que` | it is possible that | `il est possible qu'elle sache déjà la réponse.` | | `il est dommage que` | it is a shame that | `il est dommage qu'ils soient absents.` | ### more examples - `il faut que mes amis puissent venir à la fête.` — my friends have to be able to come to the party. - `il vaut mieux que vous preniez le train plus tôt.` — it is better that you take the earlier train. - `il est possible qu'on ait oublié un détail.` — it is possible that we forgot a detail. ### meaning note these expressions do not state a fact directly; they frame the second clause as evaluated, necessary, or uncertain. that is why the subjunctive appears. ### common mistake for english speakers do not replace the subjunctive with an infinitive after these impersonal triggers when the clause has its own subject. - incorrect: `*il faut vous arrivez avant neuf heures.` - correct: `il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.` ### sentence-building practice 1. finish three sentences beginning with `il faut que...`. 2. write one sentence with `il est important que`, one with `il vaut mieux que`, and one with `il est possible que`. 3. change `il est possible qu'elle soit en retard.` into a sentence of certainty and decide whether the subjunctive remains. 1. `il faut que tu arrives à l'heure.` — `il faut que` expresses necessity, so use the subjunctive. 2. `il faut que nous finissions ce travail.` — `finissions` is the subjunctive form after the trigger. 3. `il faut qu'ils soient prudents.` — `être` is irregular: `qu'ils soient`. 4. `il est important que vous compreniez la règle.` — judgment of importance triggers the subjunctive. 5. `il vaut mieux que nous partions maintenant.` — `il vaut mieux que` also triggers the subjunctive. 6. `il est possible qu'elle sache la réponse.` — possibility is not presented as certain fact, so use the subjunctive. 7. certainty version: `il est certain qu'elle est en retard.` the subjunctive does not remain, because certainty presents the clause as a fact and uses the indicative. ## unit 4: volition, emotion, and doubt ### core idea personal expressions of desire, emotion, fear, and doubt usually trigger the subjunctive when the subject of the second clause is different. ### common trigger groups | group | trigger | example | |---|---|---| | volition | `vouloir que` | `je veux que tu viennes.` | | preference | `préférer que` | `nous préférons que vous restiez ici.` | | emotion | `être content(e) que` | `nous sommes heureux qu'elle soit ici avec nous.` | | fear | `avoir peur que` | `j'ai peur qu'il oublie l'adresse.` | | doubt | `douter que` | `je doute qu'ils puissent finir aujourd'hui.` | | negative opinion | `ne pas penser que`, `ne pas croire que` | `je ne pense pas qu'il connaisse la réponse.` | ### more examples - `je veux que mes amis puissent venir à la fête.` — i want my friends to be able to come to the party. - `elle est contente que nous soyons là.` — she is happy that we are here. - `ils doutent que le projet avance assez vite.` — they doubt that the project is moving fast enough. - `j'ai peur que vous soyez déjà partis.` — i am afraid that you have already left. ### same-subject reminder - `je veux réussir.` — i want to succeed. (same subject -> infinitive) - `je veux que mon frère réussisse.` — i want my brother to succeed. (different subject -> subjunctive) ### common mistake for english speakers do not use the indicative after `je ne pense pas que` or `je doute que` in standard careful french. - standard: `je ne pense pas qu'il vienne.` - standard: `je doute qu'il soit prêt.` ### sentence-building practice 1. write one sentence with `vouloir que`, one with `être content que`, one with `avoir peur que`, and one with `douter que`. 2. change each sentence so the second clause has a different subject. 3. rewrite `je veux que je réussisse` in the more natural infinitive pattern. 1. `je veux que tu viennes demain.` — desire plus a different subject triggers the subjunctive. 2. `nous sommes contents qu'elle soit ici.` — emotion about someone else's situation triggers the subjunctive. 3. `j'ai peur qu'il oublie l'adresse.` — fear triggers the subjunctive. 4. `je doute qu'ils puissent finir aujourd'hui.` — doubt triggers the subjunctive. 5. same-subject contrast: `je veux réussir.` is more natural than `je veux que je réussisse`, because french normally uses the infinitive when the subject does not change. 6. if you change the dependent subject, the subjunctive returns: `je veux que mon frère réussisse.` ## unit 5: certainty vs uncertainty — indicative or subjunctive? ### core idea some verbs change mood according to meaning. a statement of certainty usually takes the indicative; a statement of doubt, denial, or uncertainty often triggers the subjunctive. ### key contrasts | certainty / assertion | mood | doubt / negation | mood | |---|---|---|---| | `je pense qu'il vient.` | indicative | `je ne pense pas qu'il vienne.` | subjunctive | | `je crois qu'elle a raison.` | indicative | `je ne crois pas qu'elle ait raison.` | subjunctive | | `il est certain qu'ils sont là.` | indicative | `il n'est pas certain qu'ils soient là.` | subjunctive | | `je sais que tu peux le faire.` | indicative | `je doute que tu puisses le faire.` | subjunctive | ### practical rule ask yourself whether the main clause presents the second clause as a fact or as uncertain / desired / judged. if it is presented as a fact, the indicative is usually right. if not, the subjunctive often appears. ### common mistake for english speakers because english rarely changes the verb form in these clauses, learners often miss the french contrast entirely. memorize the pairs as opposites: `je pense que` vs `je ne pense pas que`, `je crois que` vs `je ne crois pas que`. ### sentence-building practice 1. turn these certainties into doubts: `je pense qu'il comprend.` `nous croyons qu'elle viendra.` `il est certain qu'ils sont prêts.` 2. turn these doubts back into certainties. 3. write one pair of your own: affirmative certainty + negative doubt. 1. `je ne pense pas qu'il comprenne.` — negative opinion triggers the subjunctive. 2. `nous ne croyons pas qu'elle vienne.` — the future idea is still expressed with the present subjunctive after doubt or negation. 3. `il n'est pas certain qu'ils soient prêts.` — uncertainty triggers the subjunctive; `être` becomes `soient`. 4. back to certainty: `je pense qu'il comprend.` — affirmative certainty uses the indicative. 5. `nous croyons qu'elle viendra.` — belief/assertion uses the indicative, and the future form is possible here. 6. `il est certain qu'ils sont prêts.` — certainty uses indicative `sont`. 7. your own pair could be: `je sais qu'elle a raison.` / `je doute qu'elle ait raison.` the first presents a fact; the second presents doubt, so the mood changes. ## cumulative sentence-usage practice read each sentence aloud twice, then cover it and rebuild it from memory. 1. `il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.` — you must arrive before nine o'clock. 2. `je veux que mes amis puissent venir à la fête.` — i want my friends to be able to come to the party. 3. `nous sommes heureux qu'elle soit ici avec nous.` — we are happy that she is here with us. 4. `je ne pense pas qu'il connaisse la réponse.` — i do not think that he knows the answer. 5. `il est possible que nous ayons oublié un document.` — it is possible that we forgot a document. 6. `je crois qu'elle comprend la situation, mais je doute qu'elle accepte ce plan.` — i think she understands the situation, but i doubt that she will accept this plan. when you rebuild these sentences, mark the trigger first. the verb form only makes sense after you know whether the main clause presents the second clause as fact, desire, emotion, or uncertainty. - **necessity and possibility.** `il faut que` and `il est possible que` trigger the subjunctive: `arriviez`, `ayons oublié`. - **desire and emotion.** `je veux que` and `nous sommes heureux que` trigger the subjunctive: `puissent`, `soit`. - **negative opinion and doubt.** `je ne pense pas que` and `je doute que` trigger the subjunctive: `connaisse`, `accepte`. - **certainty.** `je crois que` presents the clause as believed fact, so `comprend` is indicative. - **accents and apostrophes.** check `qu'elle`, `qu'il`, `ayons`, and `réponse` exactly. a correct answer must label the mood from the trigger, not from english translation alone. ### notice and mark for each sentence: 1. circle the trigger expression; 2. underline the verb in the dependent clause; 3. label the dependent clause `subj` or `ind`. | # | trigger | dependent verb | mood | |---|---|---|---| | 1 | `il faut que` | `arriviez` | subjunctive | | 2 | `je veux que` | `puissent` | subjunctive | | 3 | `nous sommes heureux que` | `soit` | subjunctive | | 4 | `je ne pense pas que` | `connaisse` | subjunctive | | 5 | `il est possible que` | `ayons oublié` | subjunctive | | 6a | `je crois que` | `comprend` | indicative | | 6b | `je doute que` | `accepte` | subjunctive | sentence 6 is the contrast to memorize: affirmative belief uses the indicative, but doubt uses the subjunctive. the word `que` alone does not decide the mood. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-4 twice each: 1. change the subject of the main clause or dependent clause; 2. change the trigger so that the mood also changes if necessary. then turn sentence 6 into two sentences that both use the indicative. one worked set: 1. subject: `il faut que tu arrives avant neuf heures.` trigger change: `je sais que vous arrivez avant neuf heures.` the certainty trigger changes the mood to indicative. 2. subject: `je veux que mon ami puisse venir à la fête.` trigger change: `je pense que mes amis pourront venir à la fête.` belief takes the indicative, here future `pourront`. 3. subject: `nous sommes heureux que vous soyez ici avec nous.` trigger change: `nous savons qu'elle est ici avec nous.` certainty uses indicative `est`. 4. subject: `je ne pense pas qu'ils connaissent la réponse.` trigger change: `je pense qu'il connaît la réponse.` affirmative opinion normally uses the indicative. sentence 6 as two indicative sentences: `je crois qu'elle comprend la situation. je suis sûr qu'elle acceptera ce plan.` certainty presents both clauses as facts or predictions, so the subjunctive disappears. ### make it personal write four new sentences: - one with an impersonal trigger; - one with desire or preference; - one with emotion; - one with certainty contrasted against doubt. answers vary. a model set: - impersonal trigger: `il faut que je dorme plus tôt.` — necessity triggers the subjunctive. - desire or preference: `je préfère que mes amis viennent samedi.` — preference plus a different subject triggers the subjunctive. - emotion: `je suis content que tu sois là.` — emotion about another subject triggers the subjunctive. - certainty contrasted with doubt: `je sais qu'elle comprend, mais je doute qu'elle accepte.` — `sais que` takes the indicative; `doute que` takes the subjunctive. mark yourself wrong if you use the subjunctive after a certainty trigger, or if you keep `que` + subjunctive when the same subject should use an infinitive. ## mini-dialogue: before the exam **maya :** `il faut que nous arrivions un peu en avance demain.` — we need to arrive a little early tomorrow. **jonas :** `oui, et il est important que nous ayons tous nos papiers.` — yes, and it is important that we have all our documents. **maya :** `tu crois que le professeur sera déjà là ?` — do you think the teacher will already be there? **jonas :** `je pense qu'il sera là, mais je ne crois pas qu'il commence exactement à huit heures.` — i think he will be there, but i do not think he will start exactly at eight o'clock. **maya :** `je veux que tu me rappelles ce soir de préparer ma carte d'identité.` — i want you to remind me tonight to prepare my id card. **jonas :** `d'accord, mais j'ai peur que tu oublies quand même.` — okay, but i'm afraid you will forget anyway. **maya :** `ça m'étonnerait, parce que je suis contente que tu sois aussi organisé.` — that would surprise me, because i am glad that you are so organized. ### guided analysis 1. find three subjunctive forms and one indicative form after a certainty trigger. 2. explain why `qu'il sera là` is indicative but `qu'il commence` is subjunctive. 3. rewrite `je veux que tu me rappelles` so that the subject stays the same and the infinitive becomes possible. 4. change `j'ai peur que tu oublies` into a sentence with `je pense que` and explain the mood change. 5. add one more line using `il vaut mieux que`. 1. subjunctive forms include `arrivions`, `ayons`, `commence`, `rappelles`, `oublies`, and `sois`. an indicative form after a certainty trigger is `il sera` in `je pense qu'il sera là`. 2. `qu'il sera là` is indicative because `je pense que` presents the idea as a belief or assertion. `qu'il commence` is subjunctive because `je ne crois pas que` is negative opinion and does not present the start time as certain. 3. same subject: `je veux me rappeler ce soir de préparer ma carte d'identité.` with the same subject, french normally uses an infinitive construction instead of `que` + subjunctive. 4. with certainty: `je pense que tu oublieras quand même.` the mood changes to indicative because `je pense que` asserts a likely fact; the future is natural because the forgetting is later. 5. added line: `il vaut mieux que nous préparions nos sacs ce soir.` `il vaut mieux que` is a judgment trigger, so `préparions` is subjunctive. ## connected reading: les conseils d'une sœur aînée `ma sœur veut que je prenne mon année universitaire plus au sérieux. elle insiste pour que je fasse un vrai planning et pour que je choisisse mes priorités plus clairement. il est important, selon elle, que je sois moins dispersé et que j'aie un espace de travail plus calme. je sais qu'elle a de bonnes intentions, et je crois qu'elle comprend assez bien mes difficultés. pourtant, il est possible qu'elle sous-estime le temps que prennent mes trajets quotidiens. je suis quand même reconnaissant qu'elle m'encourage, même quand elle doute que je change mes habitudes très vite.` — my sister wants me to take my university year more seriously. she insists that i make a real schedule and choose my priorities more clearly. according to her, it is important that i be less scattered and have a quieter workspace. i know that she has good intentions, and i think that she understands my difficulties fairly well. yet it is possible that she underestimates how much time my daily commutes take. i am still grateful that she encourages me, even when she doubts that i will change my habits very quickly. ### reading practice 1. list the subjunctive triggers in the paragraph. 2. separate the subjunctive clauses from the indicative clauses. 3. find one sentence of certainty and one of uncertainty. 4. rewrite the first two sentences so that the narrator talks about `nous` instead of `je`. 5. write three follow-up sentences: one with `il faut que`, one with `je pense que`, and one with `je doute que`. 1. triggers: `veut que`, `insiste pour que`, `il est important que`, `il est possible que`, `suis reconnaissant que`, `doute que`. 2. subjunctive clauses: `que je prenne`, `que je fasse`, `que je choisisse`, `que je sois`, `que j'aie`, `qu'elle sous-estime`, `qu'elle m'encourage`, `que je change`. indicative clauses: `qu'elle a`, `qu'elle comprend`, and `que prennent mes trajets quotidiens` inside the factual noun phrase about commute time. 3. certainty: `je sais qu'elle a de bonnes intentions` and `je crois qu'elle comprend assez bien mes difficultés`. uncertainty: `il est possible qu'elle sous-estime...` and `elle doute que je change...`. 4. with `nous`: `ma sœur veut que nous prenions notre année universitaire plus au sérieux. elle insiste pour que nous fassions un vrai planning et pour que nous choisissions nos priorités plus clairement.` the subject in the dependent clause changes to `nous`, so the subjunctive forms change too. 5. follow-up: `il faut que je fasse un planning réaliste. je pense que ma sœur a raison. je doute que je change tout en une semaine.` necessity and doubt take the subjunctive; affirmative belief takes the indicative. ## common mistakes to avoid 1. forming the subjunctive from the infinitive instead of the `ils` present stem. 2. forgetting the special `nous` and `vous` stems in verbs such as `venir` and `boire`. 3. using the subjunctive after every `que` clause, even after certainty expressions such as `je sais que`. 4. using the indicative after `il faut que`, `je doute que`, or `je ne pense pas que`. 5. keeping a `que` clause when the subject stays the same and an infinitive is more natural. 6. forgetting that some forms are highly irregular: `sois`, `aie`, `aille`, `fasse`, `puisse`, `sache`, `veuille`. ## chapter reference sheet ### regular subjunctive endings | pronoun | ending | |---|---| | `je` | `-e` | | `tu` | `-es` | | `il/elle/on` | `-e` | | `nous` | `-ions` | | `vous` | `-iez` | | `ils/elles` | `-ent` | ### formation shortcut | step | action | |---|---| | 1 | take the `ils/elles` present form | | 2 | remove `-ent` | | 3 | add subjunctive endings | | note | `nous` / `vous` may show a different stem in some verbs | ### high-frequency subjunctive triggers | type | triggers | |---|---| | necessity / judgment | `il faut que`, `il est important que`, `il vaut mieux que`, `il est possible que` | | volition | `vouloir que`, `préférer que` | | emotion | `être content(e) que`, `être heureux(-se) que`, `avoir peur que` | | doubt / negation | `douter que`, `ne pas penser que`, `ne pas croire que` | | certainty (indicative instead) | `je pense que`, `je crois que`, `je sais que`, `il est certain que` | ### core irregular forms | verb | key forms | |---|---| | `être` | `sois, sois, soit, soyons, soyez, soient` | | `avoir` | `aie, aies, ait, ayons, ayez, aient` | | `aller` | `aille, ailles, aille, allions, alliez, aillent` | | `faire` | `fasse, fasses, fasse, fassions, fassiez, fassent` | | `pouvoir` | `puisse, puisses, puisse, puissions, puissiez, puissent` | | `savoir` | `sache, saches, sache, sachions, sachiez, sachent` | | `vouloir` | `veuille, veuilles, veuille, voulions, vouliez, veuillent` | | special one-form verbs | `qu'il faille`, `qu'il pleuve` | ## final production assignment write a paragraph of 8-12 sentences giving advice, hopes, reactions, and doubts about a real situation in your life. include: - at least three different subjunctive triggers; - one impersonal expression such as `il faut que` or `il vaut mieux que`; - one sentence of desire or preference; - one sentence of emotion; - one sentence of doubt or negative opinion; - one contrasting sentence of certainty that uses the indicative instead; - one example where the same-subject version uses an infinitive. after writing: 1. circle every trigger expression; 2. underline every verb in a `que` clause; 3. label each dependent clause `subj` or `ind`; 4. check whether the subjects change where a subjunctive clause appears; 5. read the paragraph aloud twice; 6. rewrite any clause whose mood choice was wrong. answers vary. here is a model paragraph that satisfies the brief: `cette semaine, il faut que je prépare un entretien important. il vaut mieux que je dorme assez et que je relise mes notes. je veux que mon ami me pose des questions difficiles. je suis content qu'il soit disponible ce soir. je doute que je connaisse toutes les réponses, mais je pense que je peux expliquer mon parcours clairement. je ne crois pas que l'entretien soit facile. je veux réussir, donc je vais pratiquer encore une heure. si mon ami est libre, je préfère qu'il me rappelle demain matin.` how it satisfies the brief: the triggers include `il faut que`, `il vaut mieux que`, `je veux que`, `je suis content que`, `je doute que`, `je ne crois pas que`, and `je préfère que`; the certainty sentence is `je pense que je peux...`, with indicative `peux`; and the same-subject infinitive is `je veux réussir`, because the subject stays `je`. mark yourself wrong if: - a certainty trigger such as `je sais que` or `je pense que` takes the subjunctive without a negative or doubt meaning; - `il faut que` is followed by an indicative form; - the same subject uses an awkward `que je...` clause where an infinitive is natural; - an irregular form such as `soit`, `aie`, `fasse`, or `puisse` is replaced by a regular-looking form; - you choose the mood from english wording instead of from the french trigger. "},{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 16 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-exercises\">Chapter 16 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>parler, que je</li>\n<li>finir, que nous</li>\n<li>être, qu'il</li>\n<li>Il faut / vous venir</li>\n<li>Je veux / tu réussir</li>\n<li>I am happy that she is here.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code> — Regular subjunctive from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils parlent</code>: stem parl- plus -e.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous finissions</code> — From <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils finissent</code>, stem finiss- plus -ions.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il soit</code> — <strong>Être</strong> is irregular in the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous veniez.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code> triggers the subjunctive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code> has <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veniez</code> with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses.</code> — Desire plus a different subject triggers the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis content qu'elle soit ici.</code> — Emotion triggers the subjunctive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soit</code>.</li>\n</ol></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>forming the subjunctive</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>important irregulars</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je sois</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>necessity and judgment</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous veniez.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>volition, emotion, and doubt</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A correct answer must show the named structure and change the model sentence's content.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Forming the subjunctive:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu vendes</code> — from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils vendent</code>, stem vend-, ending -es.</li>\n<li><strong>Important irregulars:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous ayons le temps</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avoir</code> is irregular: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ayons</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Necessity and judgment:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est important que vous compreniez.</code> — judgment trigger plus subjunctive.</li>\n<li><strong>Volition, emotion, and doubt:</strong> <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute qu'ils puissent venir.</code> — doubt triggers the subjunctive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">pouvoir</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puissent</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mark yourself wrong if you used the subjunctive after a certainty trigger such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je sais que</code>, or if the same subject should have used an infinitive instead.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. Worked example from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous veniez avant neuf heures.</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the subject: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que tu viennes avant neuf heures.</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code> changes from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veniez</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">viennes</code>.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il ne faut pas que vous veniez avant neuf heures.</code> — the trigger becomes negative, but it still takes the subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Turn it into a question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce qu'il faut que vous veniez avant neuf heures ?</code> — the dependent verb remains subjunctive.</li>\n<li>Change one meaningful detail: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous veniez après le déjeuner.</code> — the time changes; the trigger and mood stay the same.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For your own versions, identify the trigger before choosing the verb form.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. Then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.</code> — The trap is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">arriviez</code>, a subjunctive <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous</code> form that sounds close to other -iez forms.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses.</code> — Desire plus a different subject triggers the subjunctive; the double <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> belongs to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réussisses</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes contents qu'elle soit ici.</code> — Emotion triggers the subjunctive, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code> becomes irregular <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">soit</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est possible qu'elle sache déjà la réponse.</code> — Possibility triggers the subjunctive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">savoir</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sache</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'il vienne.</code> — Negative opinion triggers the subjunctive; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">venir</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vienne</code>.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais qu'il vient.</code> — Certainty uses the indicative, so this is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vient</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vienne</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correct by trigger first. If the trigger is wrong, the verb form will usually be wrong too.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write eight recommendations for a friend using il faut que, je veux que, il est important que, and je suis content que.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><p>Answers vary. A model set of eight recommendations:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que tu dormes plus tôt.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que tu boives plus d'eau.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses ton examen.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu prennes une vraie pause.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est important que tu sois honnête avec toi-même.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est important que tu fasses un planning simple.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis content que tu demandes de l'aide.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis content que tu veuilles progresser.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Self-check: each sentence has a trigger plus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que</code>, a subject in the dependent clause, and a subjunctive verb. If the subject is the same as the main clause subject, an infinitive may be more natural.</p></div></details>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>\n<details class=\"answer-block\"><summary>Show answers</summary><div class=\"answer-body\"><ol>\n<li><strong>Forming the subjunctive.</strong> Many verbs start from the <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils</code> present stem, remove -ent, and add subjunctive endings. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Important irregulars.</strong> Forms such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sois</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aie</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aille</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fasse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">puisse</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sache</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">veuille</code> must be memorized. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut qu'elle soit ici.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Necessity and judgment.</strong> Triggers such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il faut que</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il est important que</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il vaut mieux que</code> usually take the subjunctive. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que nous partions.</code></li>\n<li><strong>Volition, emotion, and doubt.</strong> Desire, emotion, fear, doubt, and negative opinion often take the subjunctive when the subject changes. Example: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je doute qu'il vienne.</code> Certainty triggers such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je sais que</code> use the indicative instead.</li>\n</ol></div></details>","search":"# chapter 16 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. parler, que je 2. finir, que nous 3. être, qu'il 4. il faut / vous venir 5. je veux / tu réussir 6. i am happy that she is here. 1. `que je parle` — regular subjunctive from `ils parlent`: stem parl- plus -e. 2. `que nous finissions` — from `ils finissent`, stem finiss- plus -ions. 3. `qu'il soit` — **être** is irregular in the subjunctive. 4. `il faut que vous veniez.` — `il faut que` triggers the subjunctive; `venir` has `veniez` with `vous`. 5. `je veux que tu réussisses.` — desire plus a different subject triggers the subjunctive. 6. `je suis content qu'elle soit ici.` — emotion triggers the subjunctive; `être` becomes `soit`. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **forming the subjunctive**. use `que je parle` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **important irregulars**. use `que je sois` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **necessity and judgment**. use `il faut que vous veniez.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **volition, emotion, and doubt**. use `je veux que tu réussisses.` only as a structural model; change its content. answers vary. a correct answer must show the named structure and change the model sentence's content. 1. **forming the subjunctive:** `que tu vendes` — from `ils vendent`, stem vend-, ending -es. 2. **important irregulars:** `que nous ayons le temps` — `avoir` is irregular: `ayons`. 3. **necessity and judgment:** `il est important que vous compreniez.` — judgment trigger plus subjunctive. 4. **volition, emotion, and doubt:** `je doute qu'ils puissent venir.` — doubt triggers the subjunctive; `pouvoir` becomes `puissent`. mark yourself wrong if you used the subjunctive after a certainty trigger such as `je sais que`, or if the same subject should have used an infinitive instead. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. answers vary. worked example from `il faut que vous veniez avant neuf heures.` 1. change the subject: `il faut que tu viennes avant neuf heures.` — `venir` changes from `veniez` to `viennes`. 2. make it negative: `il ne faut pas que vous veniez avant neuf heures.` — the trigger becomes negative, but it still takes the subjunctive. 3. turn it into a question: `est-ce qu'il faut que vous veniez avant neuf heures ?` — the dependent verb remains subjunctive. 4. change one meaningful detail: `il faut que vous veniez après le déjeuner.` — the time changes; the trigger and mood stay the same. for your own versions, identify the trigger before choosing the verb form. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. do the dictation first, using this chapter's own examples as your audio. then open the answers to check: they list six sentences worth dictating and the trap each one sets. six sentences from this chapter that make good dictation material, and the trap each one sets: 1. `il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.` — the trap is `arriviez`, a subjunctive `vous` form that sounds close to other -iez forms. 2. `je veux que tu réussisses.` — desire plus a different subject triggers the subjunctive; the double `s` belongs to `réussisses`. 3. `nous sommes contents qu'elle soit ici.` — emotion triggers the subjunctive, and `être` becomes irregular `soit`. 4. `il est possible qu'elle sache déjà la réponse.` — possibility triggers the subjunctive; `savoir` becomes `sache`. 5. `je ne pense pas qu'il vienne.` — negative opinion triggers the subjunctive; `venir` becomes `vienne`. 6. `je sais qu'il vient.` — certainty uses the indicative, so this is `vient`, not `vienne`. correct by trigger first. if the trigger is wrong, the verb form will usually be wrong too. ## e. personal writing write eight recommendations for a friend using il faut que, je veux que, il est important que, and je suis content que. answers vary. a model set of eight recommendations: 1. `il faut que tu dormes plus tôt.` 2. `il faut que tu boives plus d'eau.` 3. `je veux que tu réussisses ton examen.` 4. `je veux que tu prennes une vraie pause.` 5. `il est important que tu sois honnête avec toi-même.` 6. `il est important que tu fasses un planning simple.` 7. `je suis content que tu demandes de l'aide.` 8. `je suis content que tu veuilles progresser.` self-check: each sentence has a trigger plus `que`, a subject in the dependent clause, and a subjunctive verb. if the subject is the same as the main clause subject, an infinitive may be more natural. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. 1. **forming the subjunctive.** many verbs start from the `ils` present stem, remove -ent, and add subjunctive endings. example: `que je parle`. 2. **important irregulars.** forms such as `sois`, `aie`, `aille`, `fasse`, `puisse`, `sache`, and `veuille` must be memorized. example: `il faut qu'elle soit ici.` 3. **necessity and judgment.** triggers such as `il faut que`, `il est important que`, and `il vaut mieux que` usually take the subjunctive. example: `il faut que nous partions.` 4. **volition, emotion, and doubt.** desire, emotion, fear, doubt, and negative opinion often take the subjunctive when the subject changes. example: `je doute qu'il vienne.` certainty triggers such as `je sais que` use the indicative instead. "},{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 16 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-answer-key\">Chapter 16 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous finissions</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il soit</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous veniez.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis content qu'elle soit ici.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forming the subjunctive:</strong> For many verbs, start from the ils present stem, remove -ent, and add -e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent.</li>\n<li><strong>Important irregulars:</strong> Master être, avoir, aller, faire, pouvoir, savoir, vouloir, and common stem-changing forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Necessity and judgment:</strong> Impersonal expressions such as il faut que, il est important que, and il vaut mieux que commonly trigger the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><strong>Volition, emotion, and doubt:</strong> A change of subject after vouloir, préférer, être content, avoir peur, douter, or a negative opinion often leads to que + subjunctive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 16 answer key ## section a 1. `que je parle` 2. `que nous finissions` 3. `qu'il soit` 4. `il faut que vous veniez.` 5. `je veux que tu réussisses.` 6. `je suis content qu'elle soit ici.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **forming the subjunctive:** for many verbs, start from the ils present stem, remove -ent, and add -e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent. - **important irregulars:** master être, avoir, aller, faire, pouvoir, savoir, vouloir, and common stem-changing forms. - **necessity and judgment:** impersonal expressions such as il faut que, il est important que, and il vaut mieux que commonly trigger the subjunctive. - **volition, emotion, and doubt:** a change of subject after vouloir, préférer, être content, avoir peur, douter, or a negative opinion often leads to que + subjunctive. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 16 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 16 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Forming the subjunctive</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Important irregulars</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Necessity and judgment</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Volition, emotion, and doubt</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 16 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **forming the subjunctive**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **important irregulars** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **necessity and judgment**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **volition, emotion, and doubt**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]}]};
